    <node id="Q-Learning">
      <data key="d0">Q-Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Q-Learning (QL) is a foundational, model-free reinforcement learning algorithm developed by Christopher J.C.H. Watkins and Peter Dayan, with foundational work published in 1992 following earlier development in 1989. It is primarily designed to learn an action-value function, often denoted as Q(s,a), which quantifies the expected utility or desirability of taking a specific action within a given state. This algorithm aims to find an optimal action-value function and, consequently, an optimal policy for any given finite Markov Decision Process, guiding an agent on what action to take under various circumstances.

As an off-policy, temporal-difference control algorithm, Q-Learning uses a recursive definition of return for incremental updates. It iteratively estimates the optimal action-value function through a temporal-difference update rule, typically employing the Q-Learning Equation for learning and updating Q-values. This process involves bootstrapping estimates of future returns and is characterized by its use of the Bellman optimality equation in its loss function. Unlike on-policy methods such as SARSA, Q-Learning specifically contrasts by using the maximum Q-value for the next state, rather than the Q-value of the actually taken action.

Q-Learning is an online learning algorithm that maintains a balance between exploration and exploitation. While it commonly uses a greedy policy, repeatedly selecting actions with the highest Q-values, it can also incorporate exploration strategies like the $\epsilon$-greedy approach. Traditionally, Q-tables are used to store and evaluate Q-values for state-action pairs. However, for larger-scale applications, the traditional state-action table can be replaced by neural networks, turning it into a Deep Reinforcement Learning algorithm. When implemented with neural networks, actions are stacked, and weights are updated based on action-value estimates, often utilizing the Mean Squared Error (MSE) or its variants for updates.

Q-Learning is a value-based method in Reinforcement Learning, which means it estimates action values to determine optimal actions, contrasting with Policy Gradient methods that directly optimize the policy. It relies on sequences of state-action pairs and Q-value estimates to update Q-function estimates. Beyond general reinforcement learning, Q-Learning has been used as an optimization method, such as in the context of topology optimization for generating optimal C-core actuator designs. It can also function as a component responsible for processing stored experiences from an experience buffer. The algorithm's update rule, similar to weight update rules in other value-based methods, approximates optimal policies, making it a robust and versatile tool in the field of artificial intelligence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66f8a9943508aa952a9845858b8d3374&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d057a6f40f6119ac37674e31ce09f40a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1cc3c9a8202b9032fe06ba473d7c76d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ceb1ac6e54fec53c601d8b38f2b338d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c2c4cc1ff144256a7c660b31a82de7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11cc1c9381d2b807e25d94ba609ca1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e3e1dd6f2a21d7997b5fa281b76b709&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca0b752852f034cec7766c7e6e92af67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12274febd6bccf438d9cbc6fce42afe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb28e5c877704a09cf7eb4cbeb6522fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f509e07c140db54396c0dd6295e0be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
