    <node id="Machine Learning">
      <data key="d0">Machine Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Machine Learning (ML) is a rapidly developing field within artificial intelligence focused on teaching computers to learn from data. It encompasses various methods used for predictive tasks, such as performance analysis and surrogate modeling, particularly relevant in applications like predicting the performance of electric machines. These methods often rely on experimental or Finite Element model data. ML has opened new avenues for pattern recognition and curve fitting in complex problems.

Within Machine Learning, topics such as Transfer Learning are well-studied. It also finds application in the design and analysis of low-frequency electromagnetic devices. Furthermore, "Machine Learning" refers to a scientific journal that has published influential works, including "Reinforcement learning with replacing eligibility traces," "Learning to predict by the methods of temporal differences," and the foundational "Q-learning" paper in 1992.&lt;SEP&gt;Machine Learning refers to approaches being researched for faster prediction of efficiency maps, as their generation is computationally expensive.&lt;SEP&gt;Machine learning is the application domain where this equation is used, specifically for evaluating classification models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-efb0bf44fcf7466c35982fd3bb8ae7ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1e3ef3a96efc33efd9047031ab15a09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05d9b6a613299c6224fd2160377cbc33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2e27a9a79daf5a813690efa01766eca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74dead31275ed48a2a31d5ae16d58cb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a37e88914ecd388d79917c1c24f82fc2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f509e07c140db54396c0dd6295e0be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-588116b5242516d003d1e70d48a7f42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-172d2c57a19dd33adf49cb4619a788cc</data>
