      <data key="d0">Re-examination of Synchronous Machine Modeling Techniques for Electromagnetic Transient Simulations</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">This paper re-examines techniques for modeling synchronous machines in electromagnetic transient simulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2f509e07c140db54396c0dd6295e0be1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285216</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ON/OFF TO">
      <data key="d0">ON/OFF TO</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">ON/OFF TO is a topology optimization methodology that is compared to Sequence-based TO in terms of function calls and computational efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;ON/OFF Topology Optimization, a method used with GA and GA+ filters.&lt;SEP&gt;A Topology Optimization method used with GA and GA+ filters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285216</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sequence-based TO">
      <data key="d0">Sequence-based TO</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Sequence-based TO (SeqTO-v1) is a topology optimization method that enforces material connectivity, leading to manufacturable designs and reduced function calls compared to ON/OFF TO. It is efficient due to reduced dimensionality and avoids evaluating designs with floating or isolated material.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285216</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Function Calls">
      <data key="d0">Function Calls</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Function calls (FE) are operations whose number is limited by the controller in the design optimization setup.&lt;SEP&gt;Function calls are a measure of computational cost in optimization procedures. Sequence-based TO significantly reduces the number of function calls compared to ON/OFF TO.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285216</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Computational Efficiency">
      <data key="d0">Computational Efficiency</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Computational efficiency is a measure of how effectively an algorithm uses computational resources. Sequence-based TO offers improved computational efficiency due to reduced dimensionality and enforced connectivity.&lt;SEP&gt;Computational Efficiency is a metric used to compare different RL based algorithms and optimization methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Computational Efficiency refers to the measure of the simulations required by different optimization methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Computational efficiency is a factor considered in the comparison of different algorithms for topology optimization, related to the number of simulation calls.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0d5c4a49fbb5bedfe6eb2f200271d99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289025</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Table 4.8">
      <data key="d0">Table 4.8</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Table 4.8 is a data source referenced in the text that verifies the computational efficiency of Sequence-based TO by showing a reduction in function calls.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 4.8 presents data related to the number of function calls, demonstrating the efficiency of Sequence-based TO.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 4.8 is a comparative table titled "Comparison of On/Off TO with Seq based TO using GA &amp; TD-learning", presenting a quantitative comparison of computational efficiency and optimal design achievement across different topology optimization methodologies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0d5c4a49fbb5bedfe6eb2f200271d99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289018</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TD-based Learning">
      <data key="d0">TD-based Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">TD-based Learning is a learning technique that, when applied to Sequence-based TO, requires fewer FE evaluations than GA for optimal design generation. It offers interpretability in the design process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285218</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IEEE">
      <data key="d0">IEEE</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The IEEE, or Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers, is a professional association primarily focused on electrical and electronics engineering. As a prominent organization, IEEE functions as a publisher of various journals, transactions, and conference proceedings. These publications cover a broad range of topics, including the "Design Process of Advanced Reluctance Machines for Electric Vehicle Applications," published through its VPPC conference, and "IEEE Transactions on Magnetics."

Beyond its publishing activities, IEEE is also heavily involved in organizing and hosting numerous events and conferences. Examples include the 2015 IEEE Energy Conversion Congress and Exposition (ECCE), the 2011 4th International Conference on Power Electronics Systems and Applications, and the 2016 IEEE Conference on Electromagnetic Field Computation (CEFC), whose proceedings it also published. The organization is a key entity in disseminating research and fostering collaboration within the electrical and power engineering fields.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e02179750b3f9b10c33f013ef970dc4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2e27a9a79daf5a813690efa01766eca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-366a439420ac0344c697dc0460ffa8aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5f63a01dc7031890756e399578cff0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f509e07c140db54396c0dd6295e0be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-588116b5242516d003d1e70d48a7f42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56168e4e7d535f0d2b13e88db7d37f2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71a776c7100b2c1d65439d9079530532&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-403239ec5550f149ced3802b6c18600b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285218</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-core Design Problem">
      <data key="d0">C-core Design Problem</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The C-core design problem is a specific engineering problem used to test the effectiveness of optimization algorithms with varying complexities (linear and non-linear material). It has a larger design space compared to SynRM.&lt;SEP&gt;A C-core Design Problem is a practical application domain where Reinforcement Learning can be used to optimize the geometry of a C-core subject to material constraints.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289119</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SynRM">
      <data key="d0">SynRM</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">SynRM is an abbreviation for Synchronous Reluctance Motor.&lt;SEP&gt;SynRM stands for Synchronous Reluctance Motors, which are subjects of topology optimization using GA and TD-learning.&lt;SEP&gt;A SynRM (Synchronous Reluctance Motor) produces reluctance torque through a salient rotor structure and is an active field of research for performance improvement.&lt;SEP&gt;SynRM (Synchronous Reluctance Machine) is an engineering design problem with a relatively smaller design space (5x5 = 25 elements) used for topology optimization.&lt;SEP&gt;SynRM is a design scenario used for comparing topology optimization methodologies, achieving an optimal value of '3.4N-m'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0d5c4a49fbb5bedfe6eb2f200271d99</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289026</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FE Evaluations">
      <data key="d0">FE Evaluations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">FE evaluations (Finite Element evaluations) are a measure of computational cost, particularly in the context of GA and TD-based learning for topology optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285218</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Controller">
      <data key="d0">Controller</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The "Controller" is a multi-faceted component predominantly featured in the context of sequence-based Topology Optimization (TO) methods, particularly in the design of electromagnetic (EM) actuator cores and rotor flux barriers. It functions as an agent or process that determines the distribution of material within a discretized design space. In some contexts, it is described as a 3x3 block of cells, visually represented by a red square outline with a black central cell, which moves within the design domain. This movement signifies state transitions or actions in a process akin to the Markov Decision Process (MDP framework.

The Controller's primary responsibility is to interpret the current material distribution and make rational decisions or actions to optimize material distribution, often with the goal of limiting function calls and using a limited amount of material. It is responsible for defining the sequence of movements that leads to an optimized core for an EM actuator. Specifically, it can introduce "air" into the discretized design space, influencing elements such as the rotor's flux barrier. The Controller can place combinations of items in a single step using both normal and 'soft' actions and its actions align with the MDP framework, where it receives a 'state' from the environment, makes an 'action', and transitions to a next 'state'.

In the Deep Q-Learning application for C-core design, a 3x3 controller is referenced as defining the environment for optimization. It is sometimes depicted as a light grey square cell labeled "Controller" within the design domain or a single black square within a grid, whose movement indicates an action. The Controller is also an entity within the SeqTO-v2 environment capable of executing actions from an evolved action space to manipulate design. While primarily a component in optimization processes, "Controller" can also be used as an overarching term referring to the system being designed, with connections to Coil elements and pointer areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ad46225ae0d5f3835a9bda1ed8158d48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31fcb301590d668517d7e9583a508d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a0921c09c7af3fdaeac2a38ff90bbb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51ad9a81904dfb8134371112a0be9fa9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6ab3c95a34435be519e28c9335ef7bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-906a75be5f88ad37713839d335d1720c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1b166c346deb9e14c2fe057bc33cdbf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d610c2b7090b4469e2b4b784c49c5807&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26404cee26f7623e6d969e264821542f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678d567f82303d2bd1928259fd243036&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e55225b1f173db3033115aeae970db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288904</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Random Seed">
      <data key="d0">Random Seed</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A random seed is a variable used to initiate pseudo-random number generation, serving as the sole difference in multiple test runs.&lt;SEP&gt;A random seed is a factor that introduces variability between independent runs of algorithms, ensuring comparable scientific results.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f237e58ddc202b94dc8ea5b3ef7e3145</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TD-algorithm">
      <data key="d0">TD-algorithm</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The TD-algorithm is a learning algorithm whose transitional progress is shown in Figure 4.31, demonstrating its learning process until convergence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285220</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.31">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.31</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.31 ((a), (b), (c)) illustrates the transitional progress of learning with the TD-algorithm for C-core (linear and non-linear material) and SynRM.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 4.31 is a visual representation titled "Training Rewards vs Episodes," presented as an image with a specific file path.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dacf515b824228b17645db0ff98e2fee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289442</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FE Solver">
      <data key="d0">FE Solver</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A FE Solver is a tool used for electromagnetic analyses, specifically MAGNET v2020.2 in this study, though other EM solvers with an interface to the optimization algorithm would suffice.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285220</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MAGNET v2020.2">
      <data key="d0">MAGNET v2020.2</data>
      <data key="d1">software</data>
      <data key="d2">MAGNET v2020.2 is a specific software used for Finite Element Analysis (FEA).&lt;SEP&gt;A specific version of the FEA software package MAGNET, used for simulations and containing a material library.&lt;SEP&gt;MAGNET v2020.2 is a specific FE solver used for electromagnetic analyses in this study.&lt;SEP&gt;MAGNET v2020.2 is the software used for the transient 2D Finite Element simulation.&lt;SEP&gt;MAGNET v2020.2 is the software used for the Finite Element simulation that generated the magnetic field distribution plot in Panel (b).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e320c3afc0456b527186e2755a2ac4af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b6f3bea48fce928809f6ddf7db422c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b56c95146eb3d0e8591ffefd6ab679ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75365c11d58d9301541e75f2ba5f03ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289045</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electromagnetic Analyses">
      <data key="d0">Electromagnetic Analyses</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Electromagnetic analyses are performed using an FE solver to evaluate desired performance criteria for problem geometries.&lt;SEP&gt;Electromagnetic analyses are performed using a FE solver, providing data like force magnitude and field distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285220</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GA">
      <data key="d0">GA</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">GA, or Genetic Algorithm, is a widely used optimization method employed to find optimal solutions across various applications. It is frequently utilized in conjunction with specific methodologies like SeqTO-v1 (Sequence-based Topology Optimization) for different case studies and design problems. Additionally, GA is applied to SeqTO (Sequence-based Topology Optimization) for design problems and is a component of the ON/OFF TO (On-Off Topology Optimization) methodology.

The Genetic Algorithm plays a role in identifying optimal geometry, determining optimal B distribution for excitation scenarios, and in topology optimization for Synchronous Reluctance Machines (SynRM). It is also used to obtain magnetic flux density distribution and overall optimal design solutions. Researchers often compare GA with other optimization approaches, such as TD-based learning, A2C (Advantage Actor-Critic), and other Reinforcement Learning (RL) based algorithms, particularly in the context of ON/OFF TO and optimal geometry identification.&lt;SEP&gt;GA, likely Genetic Algorithm, is a method discussed for topology optimization of the C-core.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic Algorithm (GA) is an optimization algorithm used for determining optimal C-core geometry, resulting in a force of 82.10 and 195,000 FE Calls.&lt;SEP&gt;GA (Genetic Algorithm) is an optimization algorithm used to determine optimal geometry and B distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;GA (Genetic Algorithm) is an optimization method depicted in Figure 5.33 (caption a) for determining optimal geometry and B distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;GA is an optimization algorithm used to determine optimal geometry and B distribution for the excitation scenario.&lt;SEP&gt;GA is an optimization method used to determine optimal geometry and B distribution for the excitation scenario.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1757cd493f052e1f9528255eb34ea59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a5c42907185fecc94ee85f1b33ec380&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2785b4e150ada39f3077e573325eeb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11edf0b881aae28423c1b0d33893b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4124ebf92b84af2f8bbaecff99882b47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-081d5d2ff62504474fc8532dd817fb39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12274febd6bccf438d9cbc6fce42afe1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289028</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QL">
      <data key="d0">QL</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">QL, also known as Q-Learning, is a reinforcement learning method primarily used for optimization. This algorithm is frequently implemented in conjunction with SeqTO-v1, and its applicability has been demonstrated in various contexts, including comparisons with other optimization algorithms like Genetic Algorithms (GA).

QL is utilized to achieve optimal design solutions and determine optimal geometries. Specific applications include obtaining magnetic flux density distribution, identifying optimal C-core geometry, and generating optimal B distribution. In practical scenarios, QL has been shown to achieve precise placements, such as positioning a thin strip close to an air gap, often within a 1x1 controller setup. When applied to C-core optimization using SeqTO-v1, QL has been instrumental in determining optimal C-core geometry, resulting in a force of 82.2 N and requiring 105,000 Finite Element (FE) calls.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a5c42907185fecc94ee85f1b33ec380&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2785b4e150ada39f3077e573325eeb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11edf0b881aae28423c1b0d33893b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-081d5d2ff62504474fc8532dd817fb39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12274febd6bccf438d9cbc6fce42afe1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Island/Stray Material">
      <data key="d0">Island/Stray Material</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">"Island/Stray material" refers to disconnected or isolated material in a design, which SeqTO aims to avoid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285222</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Engineering Domains">
      <data key="d0">Engineering Domains</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Engineering domains are fields where design methodologies like SeqTO can be applied, beyond electromagnetics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285222</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Reinforcement Learning Models">
      <data key="d0">Deep Reinforcement Learning Models</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) models are generalizable algorithms that apply to high-dimensionality problems, often leveraging Deep Neural Networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285222</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Neural Networks">
      <data key="d0">Deep Neural Networks</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep Neural Networks (DNNs) are a type of method that have gained popularity in computational electromagnetics for providing accurate solutions faster than physics-based simulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep Neural Networks (DNN) are complex computational models used in Deep Reinforcement Learning to handle high-dimensionality problems and facilitate knowledge transfer.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep Neural Networks or DNNs are neural networks for which uncertainty analysis is performed using Bayesian learning.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep Neural Networks (DNNs) are a type of artificial intelligence used to process motor geometries as inputs.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep Neural Networks (DNNs) are used as surrogates for FE analysis in Stages 1 and 3 of the process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f6cebd0187b0c1924a3e6491e8ebe8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-540fc8a4d14fea776a933c31f85916ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289230</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 5">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 5</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 5, titled "Intelligent Agents for Topology Optimization," investigates the application of intelligent agents within the field of Topology Optimization (TO), specifically utilizing Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) algorithms. This chapter explores a novel technique for artificial intelligence to interact with the electromagnetic environment and discusses advanced topics, particularly in the context of DRL. It also explores stochastic policies and the integration of manufacturability into TO.

Furthermore, Chapter 5 outlines future extensions and modifications to the TO methodology. It proposes extending the discussed TO methodology to address generalization to unseen design problems, ensuring its applicability to a broader range of scenarios. The chapter also indicates the use of other state spaces, specifically referencing figures 4.15 and 4.16, in conjunction with advanced algorithms in Reinforcement Learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7b76c5d19e7bcbbd5f11b71588e066f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51ad9a81904dfb8134371112a0be9fa9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3676b77d4e3057ccce502057364cbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75365c11d58d9301541e75f2ba5f03ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289134</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intelligent Agents for Topology Optimization">
      <data key="d0">Intelligent Agents for Topology Optimization</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Intelligent Agents for Topology Optimization is a concept exploring the application of machine learning algorithms, Deep RL, and policy gradients in TO.&lt;SEP&gt;Intelligent Agents for Topology Optimization is a concept explored in Chapter 5, focusing on machines that can learn and solve tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285222</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topology Optimization">
      <data key="d0">Topology Optimization</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Topology Optimization (TO) is a computational and mathematical design optimization methodology fundamentally concerned with optimizing the distribution of material within a defined design space. Its primary goal is to maximize the performance of a system or component, often without imposing restrictions on the final shape or topology, and frequently aims to minimize material usage while satisfying performance criteria. This process can transform the material distribution problem into a search for a controller's movement sequence or a general movement sequence.

TO is a significant topic across numerous fields and is considered a method under intelligent generative design for creating flexible and complex shapes. It involves modifying an object's internal structure, connectivity, and the presence of holes by adding or removing material. Key methods within TO include homogenization, density-based, boundary-based techniques, and discrete methods such as ON/OFF or binary discrete approaches, with a specific preference for distinct material interfaces. While its theoretical foundations originated in the mechanical domain, its importance has grown significantly in manufacturing, particularly with the advent of Additive Manufacturing.

Applications of Topology Optimization are diverse, spanning product design, the optimization of electric machines like C-core and Synchronous Reluctance Motors (SynRM)—including their rotor structures and flux barrier topology—customizing automotive structural components, and designing concrete beams and aircraft/aerospace structures. Specific examples include the GE aircraft bracket and the optimization of C-core electromagnets to maximize force on an armature. The method can involve determining the presence or absence of material in each cell of a design domain to achieve an optimal design, and for the SynRM, it is used to improve average torque.

Computational efficiency in Topology Optimization can be enhanced through the integration of artificial intelligence (AI) and deep learning (DL), allowing AI to interact with the electromagnetic environment and leveraging machine learning (ML) algorithms to approximate non-linear functions and reduce computational costs. Deep reinforcement learning (DRL) is also being applied to TO, drawing from its applications in robotics and computer games, where it is used to train intelligent agents for sequential refinement of designs. Research in this field involves both single and multi-objective tasks, with ongoing application of deep learning and Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs). The use of genetic algorithms, such as the SeqTO-v1 method, has also been noted in optimizing SynRM designs. The method is sometimes described by the equation $\pi(s)=a$ to determine material distribution in a design domain, and is relevant for imaged discrete design domains.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-efb0bf44fcf7466c35982fd3bb8ae7ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb6925d3d6ed02adeec99edaa647d90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a37e88914ecd388d79917c1c24f82fc2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-588116b5242516d003d1e70d48a7f42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e55225b1f173db3033115aeae970db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3639a9f481dea165d73df49859233cf4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-403239ec5550f149ced3802b6c18600b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d608386f6581f290b37ee1f8bb75e40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75365c11d58d9301541e75f2ba5f03ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1757cd493f052e1f9528255eb34ea59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f3d168f1b6a8020e0606ef52cd19390&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-610e0748724855c847b8fd20690e22db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0d5c4a49fbb5bedfe6eb2f200271d99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abb64932da4baf92322bc61ecf0df44f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f2486e70169df5ba045508cbd9276f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e02179750b3f9b10c33f013ef970dc4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad46225ae0d5f3835a9bda1ed8158d48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2e27a9a79daf5a813690efa01766eca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1474f4155ebb5657d8ca7f3c0d29b49&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66f6813286ff2495396939f94deff0c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f5a390cc7143789eccd25f5f3623694&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8803108c64e008ff20b50a70b77f8af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a93df544481c46867db2b00e4b597341&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d40236fe707c1436e66df73317598df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c232c16db2cb946ab34105a48ebff79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678d567f82303d2bd1928259fd243036&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26404cee26f7623e6d969e264821542f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288759</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Turing 1950">
      <data key="d0">Turing 1950</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Turing 1950 is a reference to a work or concept by Turing from 1950, related to the idea of intelligent machines.</data>
