      <data key="d5">1760285224</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Law of Effects">
      <data key="d0">Law of Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The 'Law of Effects' is a psychology principle associated with animal behavior, which states that responses leading to satisfying situations are more likely to recur, while discomforting ones are less likely.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285224</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thorndike 1913">
      <data key="d0">Thorndike 1913</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Thorndike 1913 is a reference to a work or concept by Edward Thorndike from 1913, related to the 'Law of Effects' and the introduction of 'reinforcement'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285224</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Edward Thorndike">
      <data key="d0">Edward Thorndike</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Edward Thorndike introduced the concept of 'reinforcement' in psychology and is associated with the 'Law of Effects'.&lt;SEP&gt;Edward Thorndike is credited with introducing the concept of 'reinforcement' into the psychological principle of learning and advancing the 'Law of effects'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285224</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gray 2011">
      <data key="d0">Gray 2011</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Gray 2011 is a reference to a work or concept by Gray from 2011, related to the 'Law of Effects'.&lt;SEP&gt;Gray 2011 is a publication that describes the 'Law of effects' as responses producing satisfying situations becoming more likely.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285224</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ON/OFF TO Methodology">
      <data key="d0">ON/OFF TO Methodology</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The ON/OFF TO Methodology is a Topology Optimization technique compared with SeqTO-v1.&lt;SEP&gt;The ON/OFF TO methodology, also known as ON/OFF method, is popular due to its convenient implementation and integration with commercial FEA software, and it is well-designed to exploit global solutions with the help of evolutionary algorithms.&lt;SEP&gt;ON/OFF TO methodology is a topology optimization method against which Sequence-based TO is compared for efficiency and performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285224</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sequence-Based TO">
      <data key="d0">Sequence-Based TO</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Sequence-based TO (SeqTO-v1) is a topology optimization method that enforces material connectivity, leading to manufacturable designs and computational efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;Sequence-based TO is a proposed methodology capable of providing optimal or near-optimal solutions for an electromagnetic device.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285226</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-Core Design Problem">
      <data key="d0">C-Core Design Problem</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The C-core design problem is a specific engineering design challenge used for testing topology optimization algorithms, having 288 elements in its design space.&lt;SEP&gt;A C-Core design problem is an application domain for the equation, specifically an episodic task related to optimal electromagnetic core design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289122</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reinforcement Learning">
      <data key="d0">Reinforcement Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Reinforcement Learning (RL) is a subfield of machine learning and a category of Artificial Intelligence (AI) research methodology. It focuses on how an intelligent agent learns to make sequential decisions by interacting with an environment to maximize a notion of cumulative reward, also known as the "return." This learning process primarily occurs through trial and error, where the agent utilizes feedback from its actions and experiences to improve over time.

RL is a general framework for sequential decision-making, particularly relevant in problems that can be represented as Markov Decision Processes (MDPs), where the goal is to learn a policy that maximizes future rewards. Concepts such as value functions, the Q-function, and the Advantage function are fundamental to this field. The "Law of effects" is closely related to the underlying principles of RL.

RL algorithms are fundamentally based on Dynamic Programming principles. In some applications, RL is considered a heuristic tree search-based algorithm and is one of several statistical learning methods. Practical applications include research and practice within smart grids and its use in Topology Optimization (TO) for design, where it can be applied with different state representations and sequence-based environments. When coupled with neural networks, it forms the basis of Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL). Strategies like the $\epsilon$-greedy exploration policy are fundamental within RL, particularly in Q-learning. RL also provides the context for models like the Actor-Critic and involves adjusting weights to improve action-value prediction.&lt;SEP&gt;Reinforcement Learning is a field of machine learning concerned with how intelligent agents ought to take actions in an environment to maximize the notion of cumulative reward.&lt;SEP&gt;The equation is indicative of a reinforcement learning algorithm, specifically a Q-learning or actor-critic update rule.&lt;SEP&gt;The equation is a fundamental component of various reinforcement learning algorithms, specifically policy gradient methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66f8a9943508aa952a9845858b8d3374&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-906a75be5f88ad37713839d335d1720c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1cc3c9a8202b9032fe06ba473d7c76d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ceb1ac6e54fec53c601d8b38f2b338d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c2c4cc1ff144256a7c660b31a82de7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b76c5d19e7bcbbd5f11b71588e066f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70c2a865bc92e157d3426a926d7ddd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-808c974f7871ba6b7b5d18f72afc85a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-294177d434050aa2bd996039510efe9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb622c9ce0600f3539c3ed7199e0c0ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58674fc78b8e0f9b48c3a573d49dd684&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e5324f5a16fd6822c647598c8e749b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a7d0fc487b66b91a1d8bae6610798e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-610e0748724855c847b8fd20690e22db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e02179750b3f9b10c33f013ef970dc4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb28e5c877704a09cf7eb4cbeb6522fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb650db56653b515d55eb1ef7514257&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad46225ae0d5f3835a9bda1ed8158d48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d164a774e2261a7031fb600ad6568887&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1474f4155ebb5657d8ca7f3c0d29b49&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e90fd88a3c123b4352d139141e81b16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9b48425c0c345b3be14ed824c6579b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a93df544481c46867db2b00e4b597341&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TD-Based Learning">
      <data key="d0">TD-Based Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">TD-based learning is a learning method that requires fewer FE evaluations for optimal designs compared to GA in certain scenarios, especially when applied with SeqTO.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285226</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Computation Cost">
      <data key="d0">Computation Cost</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Computation cost, presented in Table 4.8, is calculated by the average performance over many independent runs for scientific comparison.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285226</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electromagnetics">
      <data key="d0">Electromagnetics</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A field where finding suitable applications for state-of-the-art ML techniques is challenging.&lt;SEP&gt;Electromagnetics is a field in which extending the current state of the art is explored by new AI interactions with environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Electromagnetics is an engineering discipline involved in the complex design optimization process of electric machines, especially when considering environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Electromagnetics is a field where distributing materials to optimize performance is a significant topic.&lt;SEP&gt;Electromagnetics is a field of study where the SeqTO methodology can be applied, alongside other engineering domains.&lt;SEP&gt;The study of electromagnetic forces and phenomena.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f3d168f1b6a8020e0606ef52cd19390&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75365c11d58d9301541e75f2ba5f03ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285226</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Reinforcement Learning">
      <data key="d0">Deep Reinforcement Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) is a specialized subfield of machine learning that integrates the principles of Reinforcement Learning (RL) with Deep Learning (DL), often leveraging deep neural networks. This combination allows DRL algorithms to address complex problems, particularly those involving high-dimensional state and action spaces, by using neural networks as powerful function approximators for tasks such as policy parameterization.

DRL algorithms find practical applications in various domains, notably in Topology Optimization (TO), where they are employed to train intelligent agents. These agents can generate manufacturable solutions without extensive iterative analysis and are also utilized in conjunction with sequence-based TO for sequential decision-making processes. Key algorithms within DRL include Q-learning, Monte Carlo, and Policy Gradients. The field has notably been applied to tasks like playing Atari games, where concepts like the Advantage function, as referenced with Mnih et al. (2016), are engaged. Essentially, Deep Reinforcement Learning applies deep neural networks to solve problems typically encountered in Reinforcement Learning contexts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-294177d434050aa2bd996039510efe9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-588116b5242516d003d1e70d48a7f42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289062</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Supervised Learning">
      <data key="d0">Supervised Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Supervised Learning is a fundamental machine learning paradigm and a category within AI research methodology, characterized as a statistical learning method. This approach involves a model learning from labeled data, which means it requires both domain-specific input data and corresponding output labels. The core principle is to learn a function that maps an input to an output based on these example input-output pairs, inferring this function from a set of labeled training examples. The presence of true labels provides immediate feedback, allowing for a clear incentive for improvement and facilitating the reduction of error over training epochs, as depicted by a training curve that starts with a high error and gradually decreases to a low error value.

This paradigm is frequently employed in the development of Deep Learning (DL) surrogate models, involving deliberate data collection and problem description to inform model architecture. It is also used for training new layers within neural networks and fine-tuning existing ones. Many applications, especially in areas like Topology Optimization (TO), extensively utilize supervised learning due to its reliance on static, pre-computed training data. Furthermore, supervised learning finds analogy with the value estimation problem, particularly when framed as a regression task, where the squared difference is a common loss function, such as using Mean Squared Error (MSE) for value estimation in the context of Reinforcement Learning (RL) problems.

Despite its widespread use, Supervised Learning faces several challenges and limitations. These include the significant computational resources required for data generation, especially when dealing with limited sample data, which can constrain the model's ability to learn effectively and potentially lead to a lack of generalizability. Its dependence on static and pre-computed training data also distinguishes it from online learning approaches like Reinforcement Learning, where data generation is dynamic. While it functions as a surrogate model, it is not inherently an adaptive optimizer. The results and challenges of supervised learning are often discussed in the context of prediction, and it is a field where concepts such as knowledge transfer are explored, with some research extending it to incorporate methodologies like Bayesian learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-248a0b72513cd485685d4b3e3864e01e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76b69dc7913a240aef52b550d176029b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e320c3afc0456b527186e2755a2ac4af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-679159f51a7b3c245fdff9193fd1ef89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff6410a69aa370e2358cfa5bfe581796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75365c11d58d9301541e75f2ba5f03ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-610e0748724855c847b8fd20690e22db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f6cebd0187b0c1924a3e6491e8ebe8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bea7680cdfeea039ec6cf21875ea0c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a93df544481c46867db2b00e4b597341&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-540fc8a4d14fea776a933c31f85916ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3676b77d4e3057ccce502057364cbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Annual CO2 Emission">
      <data key="d0">Annual CO2 Emission</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Data representing the annual carbon dioxide emissions.&lt;SEP&gt;Annual CO2 Emission is data on carbon dioxide emissions, referenced in Figure 3.1a and attributed to Friedlingstein et al. (2019).&lt;SEP&gt;Annual CO2 emission refers to the global release of carbon dioxide into the atmosphere, which is shown to be increasing dramatically over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1360ac2945668789a7cee8bd946888f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fac0a146871deda05d5134b0c361666</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289349</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Global Land-Sea Temperature Increase">
      <data key="d0">Global Land-Sea Temperature Increase</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Data indicating the global land-sea temperature increase relative to the 1961-1990 average temperature.&lt;SEP&gt;Global Land-Sea Temperature Increase is data on temperature changes relative to a baseline period, referenced in Figure 3.1b and attributed to Friedlingstein et al. (2019).&lt;SEP&gt;Global land-sea temperature increase is the rise in average temperature relative to the 1961-1990 baseline, demonstrating a clear upward trend after 1970.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1360ac2945668789a7cee8bd946888f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fac0a146871deda05d5134b0c361666</data>
