    <node id="Maximum-Torque-Per-Volt">
      <data key="d0">Maximum-Torque-Per-Volt</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">This is a control strategy used in Stage 2 to find optimal excitation conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285255</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Excitation Conditions">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Excitation Conditions</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">These are conditions found using various control strategies in Stage 2, including optimal current and angle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285255</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Motor Speed">
      <data key="d0">Motor Speed</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Motor speed is a parameter used in Stage 2 to find optimal excitation conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285256</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electromagnetic Torque">
      <data key="d0">Electromagnetic Torque</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Electromagnetic torque is proportional to state variables such as flux, current, and speed.&lt;SEP&gt;Electromagnetic torque is an electromagnetic quantity calculated from magnetic vector potentials after Finite Element analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Values computed in FE simulations during Stage 3 of the efficiency map calculation procedure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05d9b6a613299c6224fd2160377cbc33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285256</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Efficiency">
      <data key="d0">Efficiency</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A measure of how effectively resources are used.&lt;SEP&gt;A performance parameter for electric machines, indicating how well energy is converted.&lt;SEP&gt;A performance parameter of an electric motor drive.&lt;SEP&gt;Efficiency is a performance characteristic that an optimization task aims to increase.&lt;SEP&gt;Efficiency is a type of performance map.&lt;SEP&gt;Values computed in FE simulations during Stage 3 of the efficiency map calculation procedure.&lt;SEP&gt;Efficiency is a key metric calculated for electric motors and is part of the performance maps.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75365c11d58d9301541e75f2ba5f03ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289416</data>
    </node>
    <node id="N-T-Efficiency Points">
      <data key="d0">N-T-Efficiency Points</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">These points, comprising motor speed, electromagnetic torque, and efficiency, are post-processed and interpolated to generate an efficiency map.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285257</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Variations Dataset">
      <data key="d0">Design Variations Dataset</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A large dataset of design variations, images, and efficiency maps is required to predict efficiency maps for motor geometries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285257</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Motor Geometric Images">
      <data key="d0">Motor Geometric Images</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">These images are part of the dataset needed for predicting efficiency maps.&lt;SEP&gt;Motor geometric images are generated using the reference design parameters and are required for training deep learning models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288799</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Latin Hypercube Sampling">
      <data key="d0">Latin Hypercube Sampling</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Latin Hypercube Sampling is a statistical method primarily used for generating and populating design spaces. This technique aims to create an unbiased representation of a population by systematically varying parameters within specified bounds, commonly by ±50% from nominal values. It is cited as being used to generate datasets for diverse applications, including the training of neural networks, often by varying multiple parameters, such as X1 through X12. Furthermore, Latin Hypercube Sampling is specifically mentioned in the context of generating design space samples for parameterized motors, including PM motors, with specific instances of generating 2900 or 3200 samples. The method was notably referenced by McKay, Beckman, and Conover in 2000.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f6cebd0187b0c1924a3e6491e8ebe8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f476972defaf212918b90b259aa69f03&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-679159f51a7b3c245fdff9193fd1ef89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e98180b8e135e08cf842f2a94ed38a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28be27ccbb95fc691bf7d1ab7fac43a1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288813</data>
    </node>
    <node id="McKay, Beckman, and Conover 2000">
      <data key="d0">McKay, Beckman, and Conover 2000</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">This reference describes the Latin Hypercube sampling method used.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285258</data>
    </node>
    <node id="24-Slot 4-Pole Parameterized IPM Motor">
      <data key="d0">24-Slot 4-Pole Parameterized IPM Motor</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">This is a specific type of motor design whose samples are used in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific configuration of an IPM motor with 24 slots and 4 poles, whose geometry is varied to create samples for neural network training.&lt;SEP&gt;This is the specific type of motor for which the reference design parameters are defined.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reference Design Parameters">
      <data key="d0">Reference Design Parameters</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">These parameters, shown in Table 3.1, had a variation of ±50% set on them.&lt;SEP&gt;The Reference Design Parameters are the baseline specifications for the motor design around which variations are applied to create a larger dataset.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28be27ccbb95fc691bf7d1ab7fac43a1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288889</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Training Set">
      <data key="d0">Training Set</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">2500 randomly partitioned samples for training.&lt;SEP&gt;The training set is a portion of the generated samples used to train models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-679159f51a7b3c245fdff9193fd1ef89</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285259</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Validation Set">
      <data key="d0">Validation Set</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">200 randomly partitioned samples for validation.&lt;SEP&gt;The validation set is a portion of the generated samples used to validate models during training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-679159f51a7b3c245fdff9193fd1ef89</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285259</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Test Sets">
      <data key="d0">Test Sets</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">200 randomly partitioned samples for testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285259</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FE Computations">
      <data key="d0">FE Computations</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Detailed finite element computations, known for their high cost in generating efficiency maps.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285260</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Space">
      <data key="d0">Design Space</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The "Design Space" is an abstract or vast area that an agent or controller explores to find optimal designs, particularly in the context of material distribution and magnetic field analysis for electrical components, such as permanent magnet (IPM) motors. It defines the boundaries within which the controller's pointer can operate and material blocks can be positioned, encompassing all possible configurations, parameters, and variations of a motor's dimensions, often created by perturbing reference design parameters.

This space provides information about the magnetic field distribution in a state representation and serves as the context for the Discrete Design Domain and the state space. It can be populated using geometrical and excitation parameters, frequently through sampling methods like Latin Hypercube sampling. For instance, a specific example details a design space of 2900 samples of a 24-slot 4-pole parameterized IPM motor, populated via Latin Hypercube sampling. The area where material distribution is optimized can also be discretized, for example, into 288 square cells.

Visual representations of the design space include a large grid of white squares with black outlines, representing the area for design optimization in a half C-core visualization, or the area within the inner cavity of an L-shaped core extending upwards to a dashed black line, filled with a grid of white squares. Fundamentally, it functions as a grid-based environment representing valid areas for an electrical component's design, bounded by elements like 'COIL' and 'ARMATURE' blocks, within which the controller operates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca13e3ef7d4bf929b84d6b30574e3485&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f6cebd0187b0c1924a3e6491e8ebe8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0534c4408a52c608a5a24ccf9951f5f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51ad9a81904dfb8134371112a0be9fa9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1b166c346deb9e14c2fe057bc33cdbf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678d567f82303d2bd1928259fd243036&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-997630dd5ed7d24f34fabba54a0e27c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28be27ccbb95fc691bf7d1ab7fac43a1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288801</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DL Models">
      <data key="d0">DL Models</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep Learning models developed and validated by the author for various aspects of the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep Learning models used in the chapter, trained to predict quantitative quality like magnetic field distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep Learning models are targeted as candidates for replacing FE simulations in Stages 1 and 3 due to the high cost of FE computations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e320c3afc0456b527186e2755a2ac4af</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285260</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Efficiency Map">
      <data key="d0">Efficiency Map</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An Efficiency Map is a specialized type of performance map that serves as a visual representation of varying efficiency levels across different operational regions of a system, particularly a motor. This map is highly useful for applications such as vehicle dynamic simulations. It is typically generated in Stage 4 of a process through the post-processing and interpolation of N-T-Efficiency points.

Machine learning models, including Deep Learning (DL) models, can be explored for generating or leveraging Efficiency Maps. Such maps can be learned by machine learning models, which may also act as surrogates for them, and can be visualized with associated error or uncertainty distributions. The creation of an Efficiency Map can involve predictions from both networks within a system, indicating the system's overall efficiency. Representations of efficiency, initially presented on a 2D map, can be subsequently converted into a 1D sequence. The smooth regions within an Efficiency Map are particularly important for mechanisms such as the attention mechanism in machine learning. Furthermore, these maps, which represent the performance of a single motor drive topology, can also be efficiently produced using GPUs and probabilistic methods, often requiring the simulation of numerous operating points.&lt;SEP&gt;An efficiency map is a two-dimensional plot for an electric motor, displaying motor efficiency as a function of speed and torque, used for performance analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;The Efficiency Map is the final output of the deep learning model, representing the predicted efficiency of the electric motor.&lt;SEP&gt;Efficiency Map is a type of performance map for motor drives, which is hinted at in the surrounding content.&lt;SEP&gt;The Efficiency Map is the final output of the calculation process, derived from the Torque and Efficiency Calculation. This is stage S4 of the process.&lt;SEP&gt;An Efficiency Map is a visual representation, typically a contour plot, showing operational efficiency across different parameters like torque and speed. It serves as both an input and output in the described neural network.&lt;SEP&gt;Efficiency Map is a concept referenced in the title of Figure 3.22, indicating the output of the comparison, specifically for IPM to PM.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c85d4390c7f5cda3bb7e9cc87cbcec0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76b69dc7913a240aef52b550d176029b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bea7680cdfeea039ec6cf21875ea0c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640da4d848d6502c665bfc0ea66dce13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-679159f51a7b3c245fdff9193fd1ef89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f237e58ddc202b94dc8ea5b3ef7e3145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-540fc8a4d14fea776a933c31f85916ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-172d2c57a19dd33adf49cb4619a788cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288858</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANN-CNN Architecture">
      <data key="d0">ANN-CNN Architecture</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The first DL model is based on an Artificial Neural Network-Convolutional Neural Network architecture, serving as a surrogate for Stage 1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285261</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flux Linkage Calculation">
      <data key="d0">Flux Linkage Calculation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">This is the function of Stage 1, for which the ANN-CNN architecture acts as a surrogate.&lt;SEP&gt;Flux Linkage Calculation is the first stage of the efficiency map process, involving FEA Simulations to obtain dq flux linkages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288912</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Torque and Efficiency Calculation">
      <data key="d0">Torque and Efficiency Calculation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">This is the function of Stage 3, for which a CNN-RNN architecture learns the behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Torque and Efficiency Calculation is the third stage of the efficiency map process, using FE simulations to compute electromagnetic torque and efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288913</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CNN-RNN Architecture">
      <data key="d0">CNN-RNN Architecture</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A second DL model, discussed later, based on a combined CNN-Recurrent Neural Network architecture, learns the behavior of Stage 3.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285261</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feedforward Network">
      <data key="d0">Feedforward Network</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The terms feedforward network and ANN are used interchangeably in this work.&lt;SEP&gt;A Feedforward Network is a type of artificial neural network that needs modification for external knowledge transfer tasks to match input dimensions.&lt;SEP&gt;Feedforward Network is a green-colored rectangular box in the neural network, receiving "Geometrical Information" and feeding into "LSTM #1."&lt;SEP&gt;A type of artificial neural network where connections between the nodes do not form a cycle; it's a component of the neural network architecture shown.&lt;SEP&gt;A Feedforward Network is a type of artificial neural network architecture used in some of the surveyed papers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f237e58ddc202b94dc8ea5b3ef7e3145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3676b77d4e3057ccce502057364cbae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288724</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Modular Network Approach">
      <data key="d0">Modular Network Approach</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A neural network approach for performance map prediction described in section 3.4.1.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific approach to designing and implementing neural networks, discussed in sections 3.4.1 and 3.5.1.&lt;SEP&gt;This approach involves a model (in Figure 3.7) to predict dq flux linkages.&lt;SEP&gt;The modular network approach describes a network structure with symmetrical encoding and decoding sections, along with a supplementary network for value conversion.&lt;SEP&gt;A description of the neural network architecture used in Figure 3.7, where disparate inputs are processed by specialized networks and combined.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-efb0bf44fcf7466c35982fd3bb8ae7ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17fa5f6a446284811fca7ceb6337e9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76b69dc7913a240aef52b550d176029b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CAD Geometry">
      <data key="d0">CAD Geometry</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">CAD Geometry is a representation used for input data, combined with information on material distribution and excitations.&lt;SEP&gt;Parameterized CAD geometry is used as input to a CNN to build a design space.&lt;SEP&gt;One of the inputs used by the supplementary network to convert normalized values to absolute values.&lt;SEP&gt;Information about the computer-aided design geometry included in the 2D structured input for the end-to-end network.&lt;SEP&gt;CAD geometry refers to the motor cross-section represented as a pixelated image, used as input for the CNN.&lt;SEP&gt;CAD geometry is an input for the CNN model, parameterized to allow for dimensional changes to build a design space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76b69dc7913a240aef52b550d176029b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17fa5f6a446284811fca7ceb6337e9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e320c3afc0456b527186e2755a2ac4af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288799</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Material Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Material Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Material distribution refers to the arrangement of materials within a specified design domain, which is a critical aspect in various optimization problems, particularly in topology optimization (TO). This arrangement dictates how different materials, such as solid and void, electrical steel and air, or iron and air barriers (flux barriers), are spread or located. In some contexts, it is represented as a pixelated image or an arrangement of grey filled cells (material) and white empty cells (void) within a grid, often forming shapes like E or C core magnet-like structures.

Material distribution serves as a key input or a component of the state representation in end-to-end networks and reinforcement learning frameworks. For instance, it can be fed as a pixelated image into a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) or constitute the first channel of state representation alongside spatial location, current magnitude, and coil turns. The optimization of this distribution, often driven by agent actions in TO, aims to achieve specific performance goals, such as in the design of electromagnetic (EM) actuators or C-core electromagnetic actuators, where the arrangement of electrical steel and air within discretized cells is crucial. The distribution can also be determined by the values of a level set function or depicted by filled grey cells in a discrete design domain. One specific illustrated method shows that a "Controller" might leave a "trail" of material, representing this distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb6925d3d6ed02adeec99edaa647d90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0691c9f7251ff59c05b1bc277ea93e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0534c4408a52c608a5a24ccf9951f5f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d610c2b7090b4469e2b4b784c49c5807&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26404cee26f7623e6d969e264821542f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288758</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Current Excitation">
      <data key="d0">Current Excitation</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Represented as a 1-D vector, current excitation is fed to the first feedforward network in parallel with CAD geometry.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285263</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hidden Layers">
      <data key="d0">Hidden Layers</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Hidden layers are components of neural networks, with their number often defining whether a network is considered "deep".&lt;SEP&gt;Information passes through a series of hidden layers in both ANN and CNN components.&lt;SEP&gt;Layers within a neural network containing real-valued weights that transform the input state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1e3ef3a96efc33efd9047031ab15a09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285263</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Predicted DQ Flux Linkage Values">
      <data key="d0">Predicted DQ Flux Linkage Values</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">These values are used to calculate optimal control values in Stage 2.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285263</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Control Values">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Control Values</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Calculated from the flux linkage map, these values are used as input for the Stage 3 surrogate model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285263</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pixelated Input of CAD Geometry">
      <data key="d0">Pixelated Input of CAD Geometry</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Used as input for the Stage 3 surrogate model to predict efficiency values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285263</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Model Architecture">
      <data key="d0">Model Architecture</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The design of the model used to address supervised learning problems and its requirements are discussed.&lt;SEP&gt;A model architecture suited to handle variable input sequence length, designed to be independent of the order of the sequence for performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285263</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Efficiency Map Calculation">
      <data key="d0">Efficiency Map Calculation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A procedure based on Mohammadi and Lowther 2017 used for calculating efficiency maps, involving multiple stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285265</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mohammadi And Lowther 2017">
      <data key="d0">Mohammadi And Lowther 2017</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A publication from 2017 by Mohammadi and Lowther that is referenced in the context of reduced prediction time for performance maps.&lt;SEP&gt;A reference publication that forms the basis for the efficiency map calculation procedure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285265</data>
    </node>
    <node id="McKay, Beckman, And Conover 2000">
      <data key="d0">McKay, Beckman, And Conover 2000</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A reference publication cited for Latin Hypercube sampling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285265</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Artificial Neural Network-Convolutional Neural Network (ANN-CNN) Architecture">
      <data key="d0">Artificial Neural Network-Convolutional Neural Network (ANN-CNN) Architecture</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of neural network architecture used as a surrogate for 'Flux Linkage Calculation' in Stage 1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285266</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Combined CNN-Recurrent Neural Network (RNN) Architecture">
      <data key="d0">Combined CNN-Recurrent Neural Network (RNN) Architecture</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of neural network architecture used to learn the behavior of 'Torque and Efficiency Calculation' in Stage 3.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285266</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANN">
      <data key="d0">ANN</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">ANN, or Artificial Neural Network, is a computational model inspired by biological neural networks.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of neural network, used interchangeably with feedforward network in this work.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91f06583cd9261910571520c3ff2088e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285266</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MDP Dynamics">
      <data key="d0">MDP Dynamics</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">MDP dynamics refers to the rules and consequences within the Markov Decision Process, which the agent learns to exploit, including penalties for moving out of the domain.&lt;SEP&gt;The agent learns to exploit the MDP (Markov Decision Process) dynamics where it receives a penalty for moving out of the domain, but can move back to the origin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-12274febd6bccf438d9cbc6fce42afe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285266</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Saturation">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Saturation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnetic saturation refers to the state where magnetic materials limit the effectiveness of analytical methods for field analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Regions of high magnetic saturation in a geometry that a trained human eye can identify.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic saturation refers to regions in a geometry where magnetic fields are highly concentrated.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic saturation is a phenomenon that severely affects design, especially when magnetic material is added where it is dominant for cases with an excitation of 500 Amp-Turns and beyond.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05d9b6a613299c6224fd2160377cbc33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12274febd6bccf438d9cbc6fce42afe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75365c11d58d9301541e75f2ba5f03ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285266</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-core Excitation Scenario 3">
      <data key="d0">C-core Excitation Scenario 3</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific scenario for C-core excitation with a coil current of 1.5A and coil turns of 500.&lt;SEP&gt;C-core Excitation Scenario 3 is a specific context for the optimization, indicating particular excitation conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-12274febd6bccf438d9cbc6fce42afe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f2486e70169df5ba045508cbd9276f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289152</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coil Current">
      <data key="d0">Coil Current</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Coil Current is an electrical parameter, measured in Amperes (A), that represents the electric current flowing through a coil. This parameter is consistently identified as a variable in experimental scenarios, particularly within the context of electromagnetic actuators, where it directly influences their operation and generated force.

Across various experimental setups and excitation scenarios, the Coil Current's value has been specified with some variation. In several instances, including C-core Excitation Scenario 3, Figure 5.4, and other surrounding content related to excitation, the Coil Current is precisely set at 1.5 Amperes. Another described instance indicates a Coil Current of 1.0 A for an electromagnetic actuator excitation scenario. Additionally, one experimental setup considers a varying Coil Current that changes from 5A to 15A, highlighting its use as a dynamic variable in certain contexts. Overall, Coil Current is a fundamental component of C-core excitation scenarios and electromagnetic actuator operation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5f031035d088ffa5bcc3e370fe22c958&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c88cccaef550c4d3cde6f591d2fe5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b984e4831b11f20f84bb8669cd7396b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6504e2cf679305513217b8548174a68b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4124ebf92b84af2f8bbaecff99882b47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-081d5d2ff62504474fc8532dd817fb39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12274febd6bccf438d9cbc6fce42afe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f2486e70169df5ba045508cbd9276f</data>
