    <node id="Stochastic Policy">
      <data key="d0">Stochastic Policy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Stochastic Policy is a fundamental concept in reinforcement learning that defines how an agent takes actions within an environment. Unlike a deterministic policy, which prescribes a single action for every state, a Stochastic Policy outputs a probability distribution over actions for each given state. This means that for a given state $s$, an agent following a Stochastic Policy will select actions probabilistically, with each possible action $a$ having a non-zero probability, often denoted as $\pi(a|s)$ or $\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(a|\boldsymbol{s})$, where $\boldsymbol{\theta}$ represents the policy's parameters.

This probabilistic nature is crucial for exploration in Reinforcement Learning, allowing the agent to try various actions and discover optimal strategies without getting stuck in local optima. The probability distribution can vary across different states. Algorithms like Policy Gradients, including A2C, train agents to learn these probability distributions over actions. By outputting a probability distribution, a Stochastic Policy enables exploration and can potentially lead to the discovery of multiple optimal geometries or strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4bc9886b92f8e393e3ab3d494b9db33c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12274febd6bccf438d9cbc6fce42afe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289067</data>
    </node>
