    <node id="SeqTO-v1">
      <data key="d0">SeqTO-v1</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">SeqTO-v1, which stands for Sequential Topology Optimization - version 1, is a specific and current formulation of a sequence-based Topology Optimization (TO) methodology or environment. It serves as an initial version of the SeqTO optimization method, with future modifications potentially outlined in sections such as Section 5.4. This environment is designed for use with various algorithms including Genetic Algorithm (GA) and Q-Learning (QL), and specifically functions as a testing ground for GA with different controller sizes and materials in topology optimization.

SeqTO-v1 is employed as a sequence-based controller to introduce air for flux barriers, aiming for an "optimal flux barrier topology." It is recognized for its efficiency in terms of function calls and is utilized in conjunction with Tabular Q-Learning (QL), characterized by a 1x1 pointer size. This QL methodology facilitates the strategic placement of thin strips near the air gap and offers a certain level of granularity. Notably, the number of electromagnetic simulations (FE calls) required by SeqTO-v1 is considered too extensive for effective management by function approximators like neural networks.

This methodology is used for optimizing the 3x3 controller, specifically for a C-core design domain, and allows for the creation of thinner strips near the air gap in C-core actuator designs. It is also applied in conjunction with TD-learning for Topology Optimization, particularly for Synchronous Reluctance Motor (SynRM) design, to achieve optimal geometry and magnetic field (B) distribution. SeqTO-v1 forms the basis for the QL optimization method, making it a foundational element in various topological optimization processes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a38c88cccaef550c4d3cde6f591d2fe5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51ad9a81904dfb8134371112a0be9fa9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b7b73719952876f4f9c0e6b4efe0027&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b56c95146eb3d0e8591ffefd6ab679ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a5c42907185fecc94ee85f1b33ec380&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2785b4e150ada39f3077e573325eeb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4124ebf92b84af2f8bbaecff99882b47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-081d5d2ff62504474fc8532dd817fb39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1757cd493f052e1f9528255eb34ea59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b6f3bea48fce928809f6ddf7db422c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6719c866b8100236897a8ad6ae78d11f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11edf0b881aae28423c1b0d33893b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abb64932da4baf92322bc61ecf0df44f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12274febd6bccf438d9cbc6fce42afe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f5a390cc7143789eccd25f5f3623694&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c48c238aedc178e88e075043138eb72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288759</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Section 5.5">
      <data key="d0">Section 5.5</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Section 5.5 contains explanations about the probabilistic nature of A2C and PG algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-12274febd6bccf438d9cbc6fce42afe1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285275</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Greedy Policy">
      <data key="d0">Greedy Policy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Greedy Policy is a decision-making strategy predominantly used in reinforcement learning, where an agent consistently selects the action that is expected to yield the highest immediate reward or has the highest estimated value. This policy is characterized by its deterministic nature, as the agent's action at any given state is solely determined by its current understanding of which action maximizes future rewards.

Specifically, in the context of Tabular Q-Learning (QL), a Greedy Policy is often employed for optimal material placement. It operates by selecting the action with the highest estimated Q-value (or expected Q-value) for a given state. The policy's objective is to maximize immediate reward, and its operational rule can be directly derived from an action-value function. This approach ensures that the algorithm always chooses what it perceives as the best available option at each step.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ceb1ac6e54fec53c601d8b38f2b338d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58674fc78b8e0f9b48c3a573d49dd684&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca0b752852f034cec7766c7e6e92af67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12274febd6bccf438d9cbc6fce42afe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288976</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Episode">
      <data key="d0">Episode</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An episode in reinforcement learning is a sequence of states, actions, and rewards from a starting decision to a terminal decision, used for calculating returns.&lt;SEP&gt;An episode is a sequence of actions and states from start to finish within a reinforcement learning environment.&lt;SEP&gt;An episode is a sequence of actions and states followed by a greedy policy until its end.&lt;SEP&gt;An episode refers to a sequence of steps during which an agent interacts with its environment, potentially losing a life and being reset to the origin.&lt;SEP&gt;An episode refers to a complete sequence of interactions from an initial state to a terminal state in a reinforcement learning environment.&lt;SEP&gt;A sequence of states, actions, and rewards that ends in a terminal state.&lt;SEP&gt;An episode is a sequence of actions or steps during which the force on the armature is measured and plotted in Component (b).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12274febd6bccf438d9cbc6fce42afe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Domain">
      <data key="d0">Design Domain</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Design Domain refers to the defined spatial area, often labeled with the Greek letter Omega (Ω), within which a design is created and optimized. It serves as the valid space for an agent's operations, imposing penalties if the agent moves outside of it. This domain is crucial for optimization problems as it defines the optimization variables, including constraints.

In various applications, the Design Domain is represented as a grid-based space, often discretized into cells. Examples include a 10x10 grid of cells where a Controller distributes material, a region for the optimization of a C-core shape consisting of 288 square cells, or a rectangular, gridded area approximately 19 cells wide and 12 cells high for C-core controller optimization. In Topology Optimization, each cell within the grid represents a discrete element. The material placement is optimized within this domain using different encoding techniques, with material distribution often determined by the values of a level set function (e.g., Φ&lt;0 for solid, Φ&gt;0 for void). This domain is where the C-core actuator geometry is defined and optimized, and where the optimization process for magnetic core design is conducted. The domain can be depicted as a larger area, such as a trapezoidal shape, from which a specific region is selected for discretization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb6925d3d6ed02adeec99edaa647d90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31fcb301590d668517d7e9583a508d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1474f4155ebb5657d8ca7f3c0d29b49&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d610c2b7090b4469e2b4b784c49c5807&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26404cee26f7623e6d969e264821542f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6248c24886e0bd2c4ecb6bd221bb01dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12274febd6bccf438d9cbc6fce42afe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288754</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Life">
      <data key="d0">Life</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A life represents a certain state of the agent, and losing one results in the agent being placed back at the origin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-12274febd6bccf438d9cbc6fce42afe1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285275</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Computational Cost">
      <data key="d0">Computational Cost</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The computational cost associated with training deep networks and performing iterative electromagnetic analyses.&lt;SEP&gt;Computational cost refers to the resources (e.g., time) required for iterative electromagnetic analyses, especially high for conventional optimization methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-efb0bf44fcf7466c35982fd3bb8ae7ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12274febd6bccf438d9cbc6fce42afe1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285275</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Armature">
      <data key="d0">Armature</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Armature is a critical and central component within electromagnetic (EM) actuators, particularly within the C-core design, and is also found in C-core solenoids. It is consistently described as the element that experiences force or pull from the magnetic flux, acting as the primary location where mechanical force is generated. Its function is to interact with the C-core, and it is specifically designed to maximize the force it experiences, for instance, with an excitation current of 1.0 Amps and 500 turns. The magnetic field distribution and the force it experiences are key metrics for evaluating performance, sometimes serving as elements in reward functions within a Markov Decision Process (MDP).

Visually, the Armature is typically represented as a solid, light gray, gray, or dark gray rectangular block. It is vertically oriented and often described as tall and narrow, located to the right of the Core or C-core, separated by an air gap. Specific dimensions given include 6 mm wide and 30 mm tall. It is frequently labeled "ARMATURE" with bold, black serif letters stacked vertically down its center, or in bright red letters. In diagrams, its top edge often aligns with the top dashed line of the design space, and it can be a boundary for the design. The Armature's design and function are integral to the C-core EM actuator's efficiency; magnetic flux lines are directed into it to enhance both the magnetic flux and the generated force. It also plays a crucial role in influencing the placement of magnetic materials by facing the air gap, impacting optimal design decisions and the variations in material distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5f031035d088ffa5bcc3e370fe22c958&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca13e3ef7d4bf929b84d6b30574e3485&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b984e4831b11f20f84bb8669cd7396b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51ad9a81904dfb8134371112a0be9fa9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5526ac278068ab9cbf72c11fd3817672&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a0921c09c7af3fdaeac2a38ff90bbb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1757cd493f052e1f9528255eb34ea59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31fcb301590d668517d7e9583a508d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57172dfb9b0c90234ff04b1a5519b71e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1b166c346deb9e14c2fe057bc33cdbf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d94fd82540e01e83a3a7b0febcf3d7ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abb64932da4baf92322bc61ecf0df44f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12274febd6bccf438d9cbc6fce42afe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8803108c64e008ff20b50a70b77f8af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678d567f82303d2bd1928259fd243036&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288766</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Time Required for Training">
      <data key="d0">Time Required for Training</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The time required for training refers to the long duration needed to train an agent using deep learning-based RL methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-12274febd6bccf438d9cbc6fce42afe1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285277</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Generalizability">
      <data key="d0">Generalizability</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Generalizability refers to the ability of a model, learning system, or trained agent to perform well on new, unseen data, scenarios, or conditions. This characteristic is a key aspect for all statistical learning techniques, including Deep Neural Networks (DNNs) and Deep Reinforcement Learning (RL) agents, implying their broad applicability to similar problems with ease. For function approximators, generalizability specifically means they can apply knowledge gained from a particular scenario to similar situations, which influences value estimates of statistically similar state-action pairs.

In the context of DNNs, Transfer Learning aims to enhance generalizability to reduce future training costs and improve network robustness. Similarly, for the A2C algorithm, generalizability denotes its capacity to perform effectively on unseen scenarios, a capability that is often tested using specific benchmarks like the LSEL scenarios. Overall, generalizability is vital for a successful Deep RL agent and is a fundamental focus in extending Machine Learning (ML) algorithms in various domains.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f031035d088ffa5bcc3e370fe22c958&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12274febd6bccf438d9cbc6fce42afe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a93df544481c46867db2b00e4b597341&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6504e2cf679305513217b8548174a68b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Close of Optimal Topologies">
      <data key="d0">Close of Optimal Topologies</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Close of optimal topologies are designs with 5-10% lower performance than optimal ones, which can be improved with retraining or used as starting seeds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-12274febd6bccf438d9cbc6fce42afe1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285278</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Retrainings">
      <data key="d0">Retrainings</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Retrainings are processes requiring very little computation cost that can improve sub-optimal solutions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-12274febd6bccf438d9cbc6fce42afe1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285278</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Starting Seed">
      <data key="d0">Starting Seed</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A starting seed is an initial point or solution used for a different optimization scheme.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-12274febd6bccf438d9cbc6fce42afe1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285278</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SeqTO-v1 (1x1)">
      <data key="d0">SeqTO-v1 (1x1)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">SeqTO-v1 (1x1) is a methodology that allows for placing a thin strip close to the air gap, achievable with QL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-12274febd6bccf438d9cbc6fce42afe1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285278</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AI">
      <data key="d0">AI</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">AI refers to artificial intelligence, an area whose adoption in the industry is impacted by a lack of intuitive understanding in certain applications.&lt;SEP&gt;AI, or Artificial Intelligence, is a broad field encompassing various intelligent systems and algorithms.&lt;SEP&gt;Artificial Intelligence (AI) is a broad field aiming to create intelligent machines, with DRL representing a step towards its advancement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75365c11d58d9301541e75f2ba5f03ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285278</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Networks">
      <data key="d0">Deep Networks</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep networks are a type of machine learning model that requires rich representations of knowledge to understand an environment.&lt;SEP&gt;A neural network with more than one hidden layer, characterized by multiple nonlinear layers (CAP ≥ 3).&lt;SEP&gt;Deep learning architectures that require significant amounts of simulated data for training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b1e3ef3a96efc33efd9047031ab15a09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75365c11d58d9301541e75f2ba5f03ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285279</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Optimization">
      <data key="d0">Design Optimization</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Design optimization is a task that involves information about material distribution, field distribution, excitations, boundary conditions, and other factors affecting performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Design optimization is a process aimed at improving the performance of SynRMs, particularly focusing on the shape of the rotor's barrier.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of finding the best design parameters for a given set of objectives.&lt;SEP&gt;Design Optimization is the parent node in the diagram, representing the overarching category for optimal design problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66f6813286ff2495396939f94deff0c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-588116b5242516d003d1e70d48a7f42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75365c11d58d9301541e75f2ba5f03ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289285</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electromagnetic Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Electromagnetic Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Electromagnetic analysis is a method used to generate big data for training DL-based learning algorithms.&lt;SEP&gt;A method used in the development of electric machines to study electromagnetic properties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75365c11d58d9301541e75f2ba5f03ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285279</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ML Algorithm">
      <data key="d0">ML Algorithm</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">ML algorithms are constrained by computational resources, leading to generalization issues and biases in model performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Machine Learning algorithm whose performance is dependent on the quality of the training data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-75365c11d58d9301541e75f2ba5f03ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e320c3afc0456b527186e2755a2ac4af</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285280</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DL-Based Learning Algorithm">
      <data key="d0">DL-Based Learning Algorithm</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A DL-based learning algorithm is a deep learning algorithm that requires proper training with big data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-75365c11d58d9301541e75f2ba5f03ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285280</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High Dimensional Space Representation">
      <data key="d0">High Dimensional Space Representation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A high dimensional space representation is a characteristic of a learning task involving factors like material and field distribution, excitations, and boundary conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-75365c11d58d9301541e75f2ba5f03ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285280</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Geometry Search">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Geometry Search</data>
      <data key="d1">task</data>
      <data key="d2">Optimal geometry search is a task where formulating an incentive for a learning algorithm to improve performance is troublesome.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-75365c11d58d9301541e75f2ba5f03ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285280</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 2">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 2</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A chapter in the thesis that deals with specific problems and involves an extension of supervised learning to include Bayesian learning.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 2 focuses on magnetic field prediction and introduces data-driven methodologies.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 2 deals with problems of electric devices operating at electrical power frequency (50Hz), discussing their advantages and limitations.&lt;SEP&gt;A section of content referenced in relation to Field &amp; Performance Maps Estimation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-610e0748724855c847b8fd20690e22db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75365c11d58d9301541e75f2ba5f03ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285280</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 3">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 3</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 3 focuses on efficiency prediction, where the attention mechanism algorithm was modified.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 3 explores the ability to learn from given data and discusses how knowledge learned by a neural network on one task can improve performance on another.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 3 discusses the extension of networks to predict higher-dimensional output spaces like performance maps and forms the basis for two papers.&lt;SEP&gt;The succeeding chapter that will discuss performance map prediction.&lt;SEP&gt;A section of content referenced in relation to Field &amp; Performance Maps Estimation.&lt;SEP&gt;A chapter in the source text that discusses concepts of Transfer Learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-610e0748724855c847b8fd20690e22db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75365c11d58d9301541e75f2ba5f03ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285281</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 4">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 4</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 4 discusses a novel technique for AI to interact with the electromagnetic environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 4 introduces a new sequence-based Topology Optimization method.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 4 is a section of the document that introduces Sequence-based Topology Optimization.&lt;SEP&gt;A previous chapter that discusses concepts related to manufacturability and Tabular Q-learning.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 4 is the section of a larger work where Topology Optimization and its applications are discussed.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 4 introduces the concept of manufacturability, which Chapter 5 aims to integrate into Topology Optimization.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 4 is a section of text where the state representation illustrated in Figure 5.9 is used and described.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec0d779e474774183c36eade5f33db6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d608386f6581f290b37ee1f8bb75e40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f237e58ddc202b94dc8ea5b3ef7e3145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3676b77d4e3057ccce502057364cbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75365c11d58d9301541e75f2ba5f03ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
