    <node id="Optimal Geometry">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Geometry</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Optimal geometry refers to the best structural arrangement or configuration of a design, often determined through various optimization methods for specific excitation scenarios. This optimal arrangement represents the most effective or desired shape of a given component, such as an electromagnetic actuator, a C-core, or the rotor and stator of a SynRM. It is the target or desired outcome of optimization processes, including topological optimization, and dynamic processes like those observed in Markov Decision Processes (MDPs), frequently subject to material or volume constraints.

This concept encompasses identifying the best possible design configuration. While it aims for objectives like optimal force or specific performance goals for components like C-core actuators, formulating incentives for achieving it can be challenging in unsupervised learning tasks. The optimal geometry can be visualized through various structural examples, including discrete cellular structures, L-shaped structures composed of different colored blocks, or arrangements of white, blue, and brick-red squares. It is often determined considering specific parameters, such as current (e.g., 1.0 A) and the number of turns (e.g., 250 Turns), particularly for magnetic materials. The resulting optimal geometry may resemble conventional designs, such as a C-core in topology optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a5c42907185fecc94ee85f1b33ec380&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2785b4e150ada39f3077e573325eeb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b984e4831b11f20f84bb8669cd7396b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b7b73719952876f4f9c0e6b4efe0027&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c48c238aedc178e88e075043138eb72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75365c11d58d9301541e75f2ba5f03ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11edf0b881aae28423c1b0d33893b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6248c24886e0bd2c4ecb6bd221bb01dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f2486e70169df5ba045508cbd9276f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288765</data>
    </node>
