    </node>
    <node id="Neural Network">
      <data key="d0">Neural Network</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Neural Network is a computational model inspired by the structure and function of the human brain and biological neural networks. It is widely used in machine learning, particularly in deep learning and deep reinforcement learning, for various tasks such as prediction, generalization, and discrimination. Composed of interconnected nodes organized into various layers, including feedforward, encoder-decoder, and LSTM components, it processes data through hidden layers with real-valued weights to produce an output, such as an Efficiency Map or an action value estimate.

Neural Networks serve as non-linear function approximators, often used to estimate the action-value function, denoted as Q(s, a) or $\hat{Q}(s, a, w)$, where 'w' or '$\theta$' represent its adaptable parameters (weights and biases). These weights are adjusted during the learning process, which can involve training through methods like dropout, approximating the average output at test time, or, in the case of reinforcement learning, to define the policy ($\pi$). They are particularly valuable for coping with large-scale tasks by replacing traditional state-action tables in variations of Q-learning, and can be stacked to predict action values for all possible actions.

In supervised learning, Neural Networks can be employed for Topology Optimization to predict solutions, as demonstrated in mapping boundary conditions to optimal structures. They are also used to parametrize the policy ($\pi$) in Policy Gradient algorithms. While powerful, Neural Networks can incur substantial errors if the input geometry significantly differs from the training data, emphasizing the critical role of relevant and diverse training data for accurate outcomes. An example of a specialized type is the Convolutional Neural Network (CNN). Neural Networks can also transfer knowledge from one task to improve performance on another.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1cc3c9a8202b9032fe06ba473d7c76d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-679159f51a7b3c245fdff9193fd1ef89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75365c11d58d9301541e75f2ba5f03ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-808c974f7871ba6b7b5d18f72afc85a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb28e5c877704a09cf7eb4cbeb6522fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288847</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Finite Element Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Finite Element Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Finite Element Analysis (FEA) is a numerical and computational method employed for solving problems in engineering and mathematical physics, particularly in the design and analysis of electrical machines, including electric motors and generators. As a specific example of numerical models, it represents a method for electromagnetic modeling. FEA is recognized for its ability to study complex issues and internal faults within machines, offering finer modeling and simulation resolution, which generally leads to higher accuracy compared to methods like the Magnetic Equivalent Circuit (MEC), though it is computationally intensive. Beyond direct engineering applications, FEA is also utilized in academic contexts, such as in a thesis for gathering ground truth, labels, and evaluations. This engineering simulation method is crucial where reliability and risk assessment are important, and it can produce results such as magnetic field predictions, as depicted in certain technological processes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-592244534b4a5f40cf1d7d58a26d28cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05d9b6a613299c6224fd2160377cbc33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d146c06d89d470b7db435c475826c0e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8b78525002f5dfa42398879cc72242c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b33ad32faa20d67bef064cd7c0c2009c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75365c11d58d9301541e75f2ba5f03ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-403239ec5550f149ced3802b6c18600b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288944</data>
    </node>
