    <node id="Skip Connections">
      <data key="d0">Skip Connections</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Skip connections are architectural elements in networks, shown in Figure 2.8 and 2.11 (b), that enable efficient information flow.&lt;SEP&gt;Skip Connections are black curved arrows that connect layers from the Encoder directly to corresponding layers in the Decoder, aiding in the recovery of spatial information.&lt;SEP&gt;Skip Connections are black curved arrows that bypass sections of the network, linking layers from the encoder directly to corresponding layers in the decoder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-640da4d848d6502c665bfc0ea66dce13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f41a0abc7626a2c56bb77667264c7785&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288958</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 2.8">
      <data key="d0">Figure 2.8</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 2.8 is a visual representation of skip connections in a network architecture.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 2.8 illustrates the DL model and its division into encoder and decoder sections.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 2.8 refers to an external figure mentioned on page 42 that, along with Figure 2.11, shows the Deep Learning model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a177157c3402545cf649cdb67022050a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289001</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 2.11b">
      <data key="d0">Figure 2.11b</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 2.11b is a visual representation showing skip connections in a network architecture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Encoder">
      <data key="d0">Encoder</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The encoder is a component of a network that outperforms other networks when using alternate dilated layers.&lt;SEP&gt;The encoder is one of the two parts of the DL network architecture, responsible for extracting spatially related features and creating a bottleneck layer.&lt;SEP&gt;Part of the model in sequence-to-sequence modeling, responsible for encoding input information.&lt;SEP&gt;The Encoder is the left section of the network, depicted with a purple bracket, responsible for downsampling operations.&lt;SEP&gt;The Encoder is a section of the deep learning model that processes input data into a compressed latent representation, using convolutional and max pooling layers.&lt;SEP&gt;The Encoder is a section of the neural network encompassing "LSTM #1" and "LSTM #2."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640da4d848d6502c665bfc0ea66dce13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f41a0abc7626a2c56bb77667264c7785&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288724</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Normalized RMS Error">
      <data key="d0">Normalized RMS Error</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The normalized RMS error is a metric used to measure prediction accuracy over validation data, showing improvement with dilated networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Validation Data">
      <data key="d0">Validation Data</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A portion of the dataset used to validate the DL model during training.&lt;SEP&gt;Validation data is used to test the prediction accuracy of the network, specifically for the normalized RMS error.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="K=32">
      <data key="d0">K=32</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">K=32 represents the number of kernels/filters in a convolutional layer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285318</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coil Problem">
      <data key="d0">Coil Problem</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The coil problem is one of three problems for which network accuracy levels are consistent, as shown in Figure 2.10 (a), (b), and (c).&lt;SEP&gt;A problem previously discussed, which is less complex than the transformer problem in terms of geometry, material, and field distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;The Coil Problem is one of the electromagnetic problems used to test the training curves and performance of neural network models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e98180b8e135e08cf842f2a94ed38a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8854ea1ca7b618b176856798664355f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288771</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 2.10a">
      <data key="d0">Figure 2.10a</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 2.10a illustrates the accuracy levels for networks with K=32 and K=64 for the coil problem.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285318</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 2.10b">
      <data key="d0">Figure 2.10b</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 2.10b illustrates the accuracy levels for networks with K=32 and K=64 for the coil problem.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285319</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 2.10c">
      <data key="d0">Figure 2.10c</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 2.10c illustrates the accuracy levels for networks with K=32 and K=64 for the coil problem.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285319</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dilation">
      <data key="d0">Dilation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Dilation is a network feature that enables capturing spatially far relations without increasing kernel size, important for maintaining locality-context trade-off.&lt;SEP&gt;Dilation is a technique that enables spatially far relations to be captured without increasing the kernel size, improving neural network performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8854ea1ca7b618b176856798664355f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288773</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Locality-Context Trade-Off">
      <data key="d0">Locality-Context Trade-Off</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Locality-context trade-off refers to the balance a network maintains between local and global information.&lt;SEP&gt;The locality-context trade-off describes the balance between processing local features versus understanding broader context within a neural network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8854ea1ca7b618b176856798664355f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288776</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 2.11c">
      <data key="d0">Figure 2.11c</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 2.11c shows the predicted magnetic field distribution in a coil, transformer, and IPM motor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285319</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transformer">
      <data key="d0">Transformer</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A transformer with two coils is considered a more complex problem than the coil problem, featuring M19 silicon steel and copper coils.&lt;SEP&gt;A device used as one of the three problems to establish the idea of DL for magnetic field estimation (TEAM Problem: 32 A Test-Case).&lt;SEP&gt;A transformer is an object mentioned in the study where the predicted magnetic field distribution is shown.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e320c3afc0456b527186e2755a2ac4af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285320</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IPM Motor">
      <data key="d0">IPM Motor</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The IPM Motor refers to an Interior Permanent Magnet Motor (also known as Internal Permanent Magnet motor). This motor type is a frequent subject of analysis across various contexts. Its geometry is often parametrized and illustrated, as seen in diagrams and inset technical drawings, and it is referenced in the context of geometric variations. A specific 4-Pole, 24-slot IPM motor is analyzed as a complex problem, utilizing materials such as M19, copper, and NdFeBr permanent magnets.

The IPM motor's magnetic field distribution is predicted, with its error distribution detailed in Figure 2.12. Prediction times for IPM motors show an improvement over traditional Finite Element (FE) simulations. The motor's parameters are listed in Table 2.3, and its behavior is studied using advanced computational methods. Efficiency maps, displayed through contour plots, reveal the performance of the IPM motor, with stator and rotor cutaway diagrams embedded within these plots. A sample IPM motor is used to generate an efficiency map. Furthermore, an IPM motor is specified as the source motor in a transfer learning task, and a certain level of normalized Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) was obtained for it, as mentioned in Section 3.5.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff320ec3e3a24e3c69d31630b6cba885&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee3a2fda6147e62f8093cb1da810ea6f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76b69dc7913a240aef52b550d176029b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f237e58ddc202b94dc8ea5b3ef7e3145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-997630dd5ed7d24f34fabba54a0e27c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf789c1974c43fd1722237522bebd446</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288733</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Normalized Mean Squared Error">
      <data key="d0">Normalized Mean Squared Error</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Normalized mean squared error is a metric used to quantify the difference between ground truth and prediction, ranging from 0.1% - 1% per node/pixel.&lt;SEP&gt;Normalized mean squared error is a metric for accuracy, which was improved by MC sampling.&lt;SEP&gt;A metric for accuracy that was improved by Monte Carlo sampling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285320</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prediction">
      <data key="d0">Prediction</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Prediction is a concept discussed in the text, including its introduction, literature survey, data collection, and application of neural networks and supervised learning.&lt;SEP&gt;Prediction refers to the output of the network, which is compared against ground truth.&lt;SEP&gt;Prediction refers to the output generated by a model or system, which is compared against the ground truth to calculate error.&lt;SEP&gt;Prediction refers to a contour plot displayed as part of Figure 3.14, representing efficiency maps for an IPM motor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee3a2fda6147e62f8093cb1da810ea6f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb99d0c44b7c6ee269cad3cce40b7be7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289109</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 2.12">
      <data key="d0">Figure 2.12</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 2.12 shows the error distribution for an IPM motor.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 2.12 is an image depicting the Error (Ground Truth - Prediction) distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb99d0c44b7c6ee269cad3cce40b7be7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289108</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carrier Region of the Rotor">
      <data key="d0">Carrier Region of the Rotor</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The carrier region of the rotor is a specific area within an IPM motor where prediction error is highest.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285321</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Back Iron">
      <data key="d0">Back Iron</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Back iron is a section of the IPM motor where the error distribution is highest.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285321</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nonlinear Relations">
      <data key="d0">Nonlinear Relations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Nonlinear relations were included in the study, but their presence can deteriorate network performance if excitation differs significantly from the training dataset.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285321</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Field Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Field Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnetic field distribution refers to the spatial arrangement and strength of a magnetic field within a given object, such as a coil, transformer, or Interior Permanent Magnet (IPM) motor. This distribution is a quantitative quality that can be predicted by deep learning (DL) models based on various input features and is a characteristic being predicted by deep neural networks for different topologies. The magnetic field distribution values, for instance in a C-core armature, serve as a robust indicator of performance, directly proportional to magnetic field lines. It represents the magnitude of the magnetic field in areas like the airgap or over the complete design space and can be included in alternate state representations. While a trained human eye can analyze this phenomenon in geometry to identify regions of high magnetic saturation, it is also specifically optimized for certain geometries. Magnetic field distribution is typically represented using floating-point data types within rich encoding techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic field distribution is the visualization of the strength and direction of magnetic fields within the optimized C-core geometry.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic field distribution is the pattern and intensity of a magnetic field within a specific geometry, visualized using colors and flux lines.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic Field Distribution is an example of a potentially large or continuous state/action space that would be infeasible for tabular methods.&lt;SEP&gt;The Magnetic Field Distribution visualizes the magnitude and direction of the magnetic field in a geometric configuration, indicated by a gradient color scheme and flux lines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d8330ecbeee1c4ebe7ea64bfd7fbb45b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ceb1ac6e54fec53c601d8b38f2b338d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0534c4408a52c608a5a24ccf9951f5f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e320c3afc0456b527186e2755a2ac4af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6719c866b8100236897a8ad6ae78d11f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4124ebf92b84af2f8bbaecff99882b47</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288835</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NVIDIA 1080 Ti">
      <data key="d0">NVIDIA 1080 Ti</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The NVIDIA 1080 Ti is the graphics processing unit on which the network was trained.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Learning (DL)">
      <data key="d0">Deep Learning (DL)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep learning is the method employed in this work and other studies, relying on supervised training.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep Learning is a subfield of machine learning that uses neural networks with multiple layers to learn complex patterns from data.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep Learning (DL) is a method used to obtain one of the flux linkage maps, as depicted in part (a) of Figure 3.11.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f12687a170b3ffb0757382a02803b98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289213</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tang et al. 2017">
      <data key="d0">Tang et al. 2017</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Tang et al. 2017 is a cited work that, along with the current work, employs supervised training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Supervised Training">
      <data key="d0">Supervised Training</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Supervised training is a method where prediction accuracy depends on the similarity of input data to training data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probabilistic Component">
      <data key="d0">Probabilistic Component</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A probabilistic component can be introduced into neuron weights to extend the DL model for uncertainty quantification.&lt;SEP&gt;A probabilistic component can be added to neuron weights in a Deep Learning model to extend it and provide an uncertainty measure in results.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-679159f51a7b3c245fdff9193fd1ef89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monte-Carlo (MC) Dropout">
      <data key="d0">Monte-Carlo (MC) Dropout</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Monte-Carlo (MC) dropout is a technique based on quantifying uncertainty by introducing a probabilistic component in neuron weights during runtime.&lt;SEP&gt;Monte-Carlo (MC) Dropout is an approximate Bayesian inference method for quantifying prediction confidence in deep learning tasks, such as image segmentation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289186</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gal and Ghahramani 2016">
      <data key="d0">Gal and Ghahramani 2016</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A publication by Gal and Ghahramani in 2016 that introduced Monte Carlo (MC) dropout.&lt;SEP&gt;Gal and Ghahramani 2016 is a reference cited for the Monte-Carlo (MC) dropout technique and its use in deep CNNs to get uncertainty information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285323</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monte Carlo Integration">
      <data key="d0">Monte Carlo Integration</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Monte Carlo integration is a method that gained popularity during the Manhattan Project and involves simulating a problem multiple times to average out quantities of interest.&lt;SEP&gt;A method used to approximate the posterior distribution when the true posterior is intractable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285325</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Manhattan Project">
      <data key="d0">Manhattan Project</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Manhattan Project was a historical event during which Monte Carlo integration gained popularity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285325</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Beginning of Monte Carlo Method">
      <data key="d0">The Beginning of Monte Carlo Method</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Beginning of Monte Carlo Method is a reference identifying Monte Carlo integration as one of the top 10 algorithms of the 20th century.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285325</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Uncertainty Maps">
      <data key="d0">Uncertainty Maps</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Uncertainty maps are images that indicate a model’s uncertainty for a given pixel classification.&lt;SEP&gt;Visual representations generated using dropout to measure prediction uncertainty, and also generated using Monte Carlo dropout.&lt;SEP&gt;Maps computed by retrieving 10 MC samples from the network and calculating the standard deviation over the samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Uncertainty Maps are visual representations that indicate regions where a model is less confident in its predictions, often derived from the standard deviation over Monte-Carlo samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Visual representations that indicate regions of an image where a model is confident or unsure in its predictions, particularly in image segmentation.&lt;SEP&gt;Maps generated from the standard deviation of 'y' samples from the predictive distribution, indicating regions of low confidence in predictions, useful for tasks like image segmentation.&lt;SEP&gt;Maps created by calculating the standard deviation over multiple Monte Carlo samples of y, used for uncertainty quantification.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289186</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Softmax Layer">
      <data key="d0">Softmax Layer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The softmax layer is the final layer of a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) whose output values might be misinterpreted as confidence scores.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CNN">
      <data key="d0">CNN</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">CNN, or Convolutional Neural Network, is a type of neural network.&lt;SEP&gt;CNN, or Convolutional Neural Networks, are similar to ordinary Neural Networks but make assumptions about input data structure.&lt;SEP&gt;A CNN (Convolutional Neural Network) is a type of neural network whose final softmax layer's outputs can be affected by minute perturbations.&lt;SEP&gt;Convolutional Neural Network, a type of neural network used in the DL method by Zhang et al. 2019.&lt;SEP&gt;A CNN (Convolutional Neural Network) is a type of neural network used for processing data with a grid-like topology, such as images.&lt;SEP&gt;Convolutional Neural Network, a type of Machine Learning architecture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a4b902f2347e5fea9d43e606ce167ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3676b77d4e3057ccce502057364cbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289012</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adversarial Networks">
      <data key="d0">Adversarial Networks</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Adversarial Networks have shown that minute perturbations can significantly alter softmax output, impacting the reliability of confidence scores.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Goodfellow, Shlens, and Szegedy 2014">
      <data key="d0">Goodfellow, Shlens, and Szegedy 2014</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Goodfellow, Shlens, and Szegedy 2014 is a reference detailing how minute perturbations can change softmax output in Adversarial Networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dropout">
      <data key="d0">Dropout</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Dropout is a regularization technique introduced to manage the bias-variance tradeoff of a network.&lt;SEP&gt;A regularization technique, introduced by Srivastava et al. (2014), aimed at managing the bias-variance tradeoff in neural networks. It involves a parameter (p) determining the probability of dropping out neurons during training.&lt;SEP&gt;Dropout is a technique that can be cast as an approximate Bayesian inference method over network weights, implying a Bernoulli distribution over the CNN filter's weights.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288902</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Srivastava et al. 2014">
      <data key="d0">Srivastava et al. 2014</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Srivastava et al. 2014 refers to a publication that introduced dropout as a regularization technique.&lt;SEP&gt;A publication by Srivastava et al. in 2014 that introduced the Dropout regularization technique.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285327</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bias-Variance Tradeoff">
      <data key="d0">Bias-Variance Tradeoff</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The bias-variance tradeoff is a fundamental concept in machine learning that dropout aims to address as a regularization technique.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285327</data>
    </node>
    <node id="G´eron 2019">
      <data key="d0">G´eron 2019</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">G´eron 2019 is a reference cited in the text regarding hyperparameter optimization methods.&lt;SEP&gt;A cited work that discusses the concept of a confusion matrix.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76b69dc7913a240aef52b550d176029b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285327</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 2.10">
      <data key="d0">Figure 2.10</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 2.10 presents a comparison of different network configurations and shows performance improvements. It specifically highlights results for K=32 and K=64 for the coil problem in its sub-parts (a), (b), and (c).&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 2.10 is a visual representation showing training curves with Normalized Prediction Error for different electromagnetic problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8854ea1ca7b618b176856798664355f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288769</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 2.11 (b)">
      <data key="d0">Figure 2.11 (b)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 2.11 (b) is a visual representation related to skip connections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285328</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Locality-Context Trade-off">
      <data key="d0">Locality-Context Trade-off</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The locality-context trade-off is a concept in network design that dilation helps maintain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285328</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coil">
      <data key="d0">Coil</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A Coil is an electrical component, often made of copper, that serves as a source of excitation for generating a magnetic field in various applications, particularly within electromagnet designs. It is frequently presented as a problematic example for magnetic field estimation, with its radius and center position being variable parameters.

In different contexts, the Coil's appearance and representation vary. It can be depicted as a simple copper coil inside an airbox, with its radius and center position as variable parameters. Alternatively, within a C-core electromagnet or similar design domains, the Coil is commonly represented as rectangular blocks or regions, often colored red, reddish-pink, or light red/salmon. These rectangular elements are sometimes textured, such as with a repeating circular pattern or hatched sections. They are typically positioned within the inner cavity or vertical arms of a core structure, or directly on a grid in a discrete design domain.

There can be one or two such coils in a design. For instance, some descriptions mention two red, textured rectangular regions winding around the core's arms or two red rectangular elements within a design domain. Other descriptions refer to a single coil but imply its function within a system. These coil blocks are sometimes labeled vertically as "COIL" and are intended to be avoided by a controller in certain designs. In summary, the Coil is a crucial component responsible for the electromagnetic function, generating magnetic fields through excitation, and its precise appearance and placement are context-dependent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5f031035d088ffa5bcc3e370fe22c958&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca13e3ef7d4bf929b84d6b30574e3485&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31fcb301590d668517d7e9583a508d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6dd34d919cd23617da046186d8e9bc3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b76c5d19e7bcbbd5f11b71588e066f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57172dfb9b0c90234ff04b1a5519b71e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e320c3afc0456b527186e2755a2ac4af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c48c238aedc178e88e075043138eb72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1b166c346deb9e14c2fe057bc33cdbf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678d567f82303d2bd1928259fd243036&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d94fd82540e01e83a3a7b0febcf3d7ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abb64932da4baf92322bc61ecf0df44f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a0921c09c7af3fdaeac2a38ff90bbb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288902</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prediction Error">
      <data key="d0">Prediction Error</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Prediction error refers to the difference between the model's predicted values and the ground truth, ranging from 0.1% to 1% per node/pixel.&lt;SEP&gt;Prediction Error is the difference between the predicted values and the actual ground truth values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92cc2b02ae3a119d92f0d1a7f6617885&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289158</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Section of Back Iron">
      <data key="d0">Section of Back Iron</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The section of back iron is an area within an IPM motor where prediction error is highest.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285329</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Structured Grid">
      <data key="d0">Structured Grid</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A structured grid is a method of spatial discretization used in simulations, which would be computationally expensive if made dense enough to capture minute details in this exploratory work.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285329</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Batch Size">
      <data key="d0">Batch Size</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Batch size refers to the number of samples processed together in one go during training or inference.&lt;SEP&gt;Batch Size is a parameter that defines the number of samples that will be propagated through the network one time, which is specified as 16 for the end-to-end network training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf789c1974c43fd1722237522bebd446&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epochs">
      <data key="d0">Epochs</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Epochs indicate the number of complete passes through the training dataset during the learning process.&lt;SEP&gt;Epochs refer to the number of passes through the entire training dataset during the training of machine learning models.&lt;SEP&gt;Epochs represent the number of times the entire training dataset has been passed forward and backward through the neural network, plotted on the x-axis.&lt;SEP&gt;Epochs represent the number of complete passes through the training dataset, displayed on the x-axis of the graph.&lt;SEP&gt;Epochs are represented on the x-axis of Figure 3.22, ranging from 0 to 40.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bea7680cdfeea039ec6cf21875ea0c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8854ea1ca7b618b176856798664355f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-248a0b72513cd485685d4b3e3864e01e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf789c1974c43fd1722237522bebd446</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288771</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Training Dataset">
      <data key="d0">Training Dataset</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A training dataset consisting of thousands of simulated designs is populated using FE methods.&lt;SEP&gt;The training dataset is the collection of data used to train the deep learning model.&lt;SEP&gt;The training dataset is the collection of training data used by ML models, and its coverage restricts the predictions.&lt;SEP&gt;A training dataset composed of thousands of simulated designs, which FEA is suitable for populating.&lt;SEP&gt;Training Dataset is a portion of the total samples, consisting of 30,000 samples, used to train the Deep Learning model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-592244534b4a5f40cf1d7d58a26d28cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a177157c3402545cf649cdb67022050a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d146c06d89d470b7db435c475826c0e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289000</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prediction Accuracy">
      <data key="d0">Prediction Accuracy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Prediction accuracy refers to how closely the model's output matches the actual outcome, dependent on the input's similarity to training data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285330</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probabilistic Component In Neuron Weights">
      <data key="d0">Probabilistic Component In Neuron Weights</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Introducing a probabilistic component in neuron weights is a technique to extend DL models to quantify uncertainty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285331</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regularization Technique">
      <data key="d0">Regularization Technique</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A regularization technique, such as dropout, is used to prevent overfitting and check the bias-variance tradeoff of a network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285331</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Law Of Effects">
      <data key="d0">Law Of Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A psychological principle stating that responses to satisfying experiences are more likely to recur, and discomforting ones less likely. It is associated with the behavior of animals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285331</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DRL">
      <data key="d0">DRL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) is a computational method that combines Deep Learning and Reinforcement Learning, expected to revolutionize AI by enabling autonomous systems with higher-level understanding and applied to various complex problems.&lt;SEP&gt;DRL refers to Deep Reinforcement Learning, a concept introduced for Topology Optimization (TO) in this chapter to extend generalizability.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) is a concept in artificial intelligence that combines deep learning with reinforcement learning, used for mastering complex tasks in various fields.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5f6d6c8243089f59fe677475645a52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Atari Games">
      <data key="d0">Atari Games</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of video games, often used as benchmarks for artificial intelligence and DRL applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Atari games served as an early benchmark for DRL algorithm development, showcasing the ability of algorithms to play a range of complex games.&lt;SEP&gt;Atari games are a field where Deep Reinforcement Learning is applied, exemplified by a game similar to 'Breakout'.&lt;SEP&gt;A practical use case for DQNs, demonstrating successful application of the error function in control tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;Atari games are a practical example of a domain where Deep Q-Networks are applied to learn optimal control.&lt;SEP&gt;Atari games are a practical application or use case for Policy Gradient algorithms.&lt;SEP&gt;A domain mentioned as a successful application of the Advantage Actor-Critic (A2C) algorithm, which utilizes the Advantage function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d5f6d6c8243089f59fe677475645a52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4124ebf92b84af2f8bbaecff99882b47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1cc3c9a8202b9032fe06ba473d7c76d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288868</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AlphaGo">
      <data key="d0">AlphaGo</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">AlphaGo is a single system that can play multiple video games directly from pixels, demonstrating the scalability of DRL in previously intractable problems.&lt;SEP&gt;AlphaGo is an example of a game-playing application that utilized reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289211</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AlphaGo Zero">
      <data key="d0">AlphaGo Zero</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A system related to AlphaGo, also developed using deep neural networks and reinforcement learning, capable of playing multiple video games from pixels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285332</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Robotics">
      <data key="d0">Robotics</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Robotics is a field of engineering and technology centered on the design, construction, operation, and application of robots. This field is an active area of research where modern artificial intelligence (AI) algorithms are applied. Specifically, robotics serves as an application area for reinforcement learning, as highlighted in a 2013 survey, where it is used for learning control policies.

Deep reinforcement learning (DRL) algorithms are also extensively applied in robotics to enable robots to learn control policies directly from real-world sensory inputs. This makes robotics a significant application domain for DRL, involving the control of robotic agents and fundamental weight updates for learning these policies. Furthermore, robotics is an application area for autonomous systems, illustrating its broad impact beyond just AI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d5f6d6c8243089f59fe677475645a52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2e27a9a79daf5a813690efa01766eca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4124ebf92b84af2f8bbaecff99882b47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288923</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Resource Allocation And Scheduling">
      <data key="d0">Resource Allocation And Scheduling</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An application of DRL algorithms to automatically learn how to allocate and schedule computer resources to waiting jobs, minimizing average job slowdown.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285333</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Traffic Light Controller">
      <data key="d0">Traffic Light Controller</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An application of DRL to solve the congestion problem by designing intelligent traffic light control algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285333</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Training Data">
      <data key="d0">Training Data</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Training data refers to the dataset specifically designed and utilized to train various machine learning models, primarily neural networks and deep learning algorithms. It consists of input-output pairs or collected data formatted suitably for this purpose. This data is crucial for supervised learning models, as it directly influences their ability to provide fast and precise solutions and their ultimate prediction accuracy.

The training data is a portion of a larger dataset, and its quality, coverage, and quantity can significantly impact the model's performance and limit its predictions. Generating this data often requires considerable computational resources. In some studies, the amount of training data is systematically varied to determine data sufficiency for effective model training.&lt;SEP&gt;Training data consists of input (X) and target outputs (Y) used to train a neural network and update beliefs about its weights.&lt;SEP&gt;Training data is required for supervised learning and is computationally intensive to generate for various motor configurations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38ad7ba535a3b44f510acc91b0e8d5f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f6cebd0187b0c1924a3e6491e8ebe8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e320c3afc0456b527186e2755a2ac4af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f237e58ddc202b94dc8ea5b3ef7e3145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-679159f51a7b3c245fdff9193fd1ef89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289187</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bidding And Advertisements">
      <data key="d0">Bidding And Advertisements</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A domain where DRL algorithms are applied, as mentioned in Jin et al. (2018).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285334</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Protein Folding">
      <data key="d0">Protein Folding</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The process of predicting the 3D shape a protein will fold into, which DRL has been tested on with significant results in CASP.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285334</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CASP">
      <data key="d0">CASP</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Critical Assessment of Structure Prediction (CASP) is a biennial challenge in protein-structure prediction, where DRL showed breakthrough results in drug discovery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285334</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Drug Discovery">
      <data key="d0">Drug Discovery</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Drug Discovery is a field where DRL has shown breakthroughs in applications like predicting the 3D shape of proteins, although it is not considered part of conventional RL domains.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285334</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Machine Learning Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Machine Learning Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Machine Learning (ML) algorithms, specifically feedforward neural networks (FNN), are suitable candidates for function approximation.&lt;SEP&gt;Computational methods studied extensively in Topology Optimization for their ability to approximate highly non-linear functions and reduce computational costs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-610e0748724855c847b8fd20690e22db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285335</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ML Surrogate Model">
      <data key="d0">ML Surrogate Model</data>
      <data key="d1">model</data>
      <data key="d2">A trained machine learning model capable of predicting structures based on learned patterns and behaviors from analysis of examples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285335</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yu et al. 2019">
      <data key="d0">Yu et al. 2019</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Researchers who used 100,000 optimal solutions to a mechanical compliance problem to train a neural network for predicting near-optimal designs in solid mechanics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285335</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chandrasekhar and Suresh 2020">
      <data key="d0">Chandrasekhar and Suresh 2020</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Researchers who used neural networks to represent the density parameter in the SIMP approach for Topology Optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285336</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SIMP Approach">
      <data key="d0">SIMP Approach</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Solid, Isotropic Microstructures with Penalization (SIMP) approach, used in Topology Optimization, where neural networks represented the density parameter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285336</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Banga et al. 2018">
      <data key="d0">Banga et al. 2018</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Researchers who demonstrated the potential of a 3D Encoder-Decoder based approach to convert the Topology Optimization problem into an image segmentation problem.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285336</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Image Segmentation">
      <data key="d0">Image Segmentation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Image segmentation is a purpose for which the DL network architecture resembles, focusing on dense regression instead of classification.&lt;SEP&gt;A computer vision technique that the Topology Optimization problem can be converted into using a 3D Encoder-Decoder based approach.&lt;SEP&gt;A task for which the described CNN architecture is commonly used, involving the partitioning of an image into regions or objects.&lt;SEP&gt;Image Segmentation is a computer vision task that involves partitioning an image into multiple segments or objects, where uncertainty maps are often applied.&lt;SEP&gt;Image segmentation is a computer vision task where models predict labels for each pixel in an image, for which uncertainty maps can be generated.&lt;SEP&gt;A computer vision task where a model assigns a label (e.g., pixel's class) to every pixel in an image, mentioned as an application for $y$.&lt;SEP&gt;A task in deep learning where an image is partitioned into multiple segments or objects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f41a0abc7626a2c56bb77667264c7785&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288961</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sosnovik and Oseledets 2019">
      <data key="d0">Sosnovik and Oseledets 2019</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Researchers who were credited with the first usage of Deep Learning algorithms in the field of Topology Optimization using a Deep CNN Autoencoder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285336</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep CNN Autoencoder">
      <data key="d0">Deep CNN Autoencoder</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A Deep CNN Autoencoder was an early DL algorithm used in Topology Optimization to reduce input dimensionality, extracting latent information from its hidden layers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285337</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deng, Qin, and Lu 2020">
      <data key="d0">Deng, Qin, and Lu 2020</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Researchers who criticized supervised learning methods in TO for lacking generalizability and not being true TO algorithms.&lt;SEP&gt;A research paper that criticized supervised learning as a TO algorithm and limited the use of ML algorithms to performance estimation in compliance minimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285337</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Computer Resource Management">
      <data key="d0">Computer Resource Management</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">DRL algorithms are applied to design algorithms that automatically learn to allocate and schedule computer resources to waiting jobs to minimize average job slowdown.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285337</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Traffic Light Control">
      <data key="d0">Traffic Light Control</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">DRL algorithms are used to design traffic light controllers to solve congestion problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285338</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ML Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">ML Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">ML algorithms, particularly Deep Learning, are used in Topology Optimization for their capability to approximate highly non-linear functions, predict structures, and reduce computational costs.&lt;SEP&gt;ML algorithms are a category that includes supervised learning and Deep Reinforcement Learning, utilized in various capacities within Topology Optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285338</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conditional Generative Adversarial Network">
      <data key="d0">Conditional Generative Adversarial Network</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A conditional Generative Adversarial Network (cGAN) is used in conjunction with a CNN to predict near-optimal designs of mass fraction for given boundary forces in Topology Optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285338</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Solid, Isotropic Microstructures With Penalization">
      <data key="d0">Solid, Isotropic Microstructures With Penalization</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Solid, Isotropic Microstructures with Penalization (SIMP) is an approach used in Topology Optimization, where neural networks represent the density parameter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285338</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3D Encoder-Decoder">
      <data key="d0">3D Encoder-Decoder</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A 3D Encoder-Decoder based approach converts the Topology Optimization problem into an image segmentation problem.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d320c7db870069aad6b0a37ec04c740b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285338</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thorndike 1898">
      <data key="d0">Thorndike 1898</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Thorndike 1898 refers to an early publication by Edward Thorndike where he introduced the concept of 'reinforcement'.</data>
