      <data key="d0">DRL Paradigm</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep Reinforcement Learning, a computational paradigm that combines reinforcement learning with deep learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4124ebf92b84af2f8bbaecff99882b47</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285347</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Game of Go">
      <data key="d0">Game of Go</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An abstract strategy board game, also used as a benchmark for AI and DRL.&lt;SEP&gt;The Game of Go is a strategic board game where Deep Reinforcement Learning has shown significant impact.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4124ebf92b84af2f8bbaecff99882b47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5f6d6c8243089f59fe677475645a52</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288978</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feedforward Neural Network">
      <data key="d0">Feedforward Neural Network</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of artificial neural network where connections between the nodes do not form a cycle, used for approximating value functions like Q(s,a) or V(s).&lt;SEP&gt;This is a type of neural network used to process the Current and Advance Angle Vector.&lt;SEP&gt;A Feedforward Neural Network is a type of neural network used in two stages of the architecture for processing 3D geometric parameters and then combined features.&lt;SEP&gt;A Feedforward Neural Network is an architecture designed for approximating value functions or action-value functions in reinforcement learning. It processes input from left to right, through an input layer, hidden layers, and an output layer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4124ebf92b84af2f8bbaecff99882b47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b7e7c8729e5eef7f4ff08e29b1d0014&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb622c9ce0600f3539c3ed7199e0c0ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17fa5f6a446284811fca7ceb6337e9ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288870</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Value Function">
      <data key="d0">Value Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The "Value Function" in reinforcement learning is a pivotal concept that estimates the expected return for an agent either being in a given state or taking a specific action within a given state. This function formalizes the objective of maximizing long-term rewards across all subsequent state trajectories. It is always defined in relation to a particular policy, denoted as $\pi$, and its estimation is a key component of Policy Evaluation.

The Value Function can take two primary forms: $V(s)$ for state values or $Q(s,a)$ (Q-value) for state-action pair values. In either case, it provides an estimate of the value of different actions or states, which the agent continually refines through exploration. This estimated expected return, often approximated as $G_t$, is crucial for algorithms like Temporal Difference (TD) learning, where it's used to update current estimates.

In specific implementations, such as with a TO environment (SeqTo-v1), the Q-value form of the Value Function is learned for state and action pairs. When implemented using neural networks, the Value Function (specifically $V(s)$) can be estimated by the output of a Feedforward Neural Network, representing the expected return from a state. In some contexts, it can also be the purpose of a Critic Network's output, potentially represented as a single output value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1757cd493f052e1f9528255eb34ea59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb622c9ce0600f3539c3ed7199e0c0ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5dae035c707962e5992f8ffb5b5c0c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4124ebf92b84af2f8bbaecff99882b47</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288794</data>
    </node>
