    <node id="Discrete Design Domain">
      <data key="d0">Discrete Design Domain</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Discrete Design Domain is a concept primarily used in Topology Optimization (TO) and computational modeling, where it defines a design space broken down into individual, distinct units. This domain is typically represented as a grid-based system, often a two-dimensional grid composed of uniform square cells. Within this grid, cells can either be filled with different materials or remain empty, allowing for the representation of material distribution, such as electrical coils in an electromagnetic device.

A key characteristic of a Discrete Design Domain is that the state representation can be achieved in multiple ways, with both states and actions being countable. This grid-based representation serves as the state space for various optimization problems, including those involving electromagnetic devices. Examples of this concept are visually presented in figures such as Figure 4.14, Figure 5.9, and the left portion of Figure 5.11, where filled (grey) and empty (white) cells illustrate material distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec0d779e474774183c36eade5f33db6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b76c5d19e7bcbbd5f11b71588e066f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5eebe68238bf8a33757041bc7dd3ffe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8803108c64e008ff20b50a70b77f8af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0534c4408a52c608a5a24ccf9951f5f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4124ebf92b84af2f8bbaecff99882b47</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288890</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State Space">
      <data key="d0">State Space</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The set of all possible states an agent can be in within a given environment.&lt;SEP&gt;The state space is the set of all possible states that an agent can be in within an environment.&lt;SEP&gt;State Space is a concept used in optimization problems, where the B_airgap or the Magnetic Flux Density in the design space can define it.&lt;SEP&gt;State Space represents the domain where the discrete design exists, as depicted in Figure 4.14.&lt;SEP&gt;The State Space is the representation that the discrete design domain embodies, suitable for tabular reinforcement learning algorithms.&lt;SEP&gt;State Space is a concept illustrated in Figure 5.11, where numerical data, such as magnetic field distribution, is incorporated to represent the state in the design space.</data>
