    <node id="Excitation Scenario">
      <data key="d0">Excitation Scenario</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Excitation Scenario refers to a specific set of conditions or parameters under which an experiment, simulation, or system operation is conducted. These parameters primarily define the electromagnetic characteristics for devices such as C-core solenoids, magnetic actuators, or systems subject to optimization algorithms. Key parameters that constitute an excitation scenario include the coil current and the number of coil turns, which are crucial for defining a particular test condition or operational state.

For instance, an excitation scenario can specify a coil current (e.g., 1.0 A, 1.75 A, 0.75 A, 0.25 A, 1.5 A, 1.25 A, or 2.0 A) and the number of turns (commonly 500 Turns, though 250 Turns is also mentioned). These specific values are instrumental for various analyses, including electromagnetic optimization problems, analyzing optimal geometry and magnetic field (B) distribution, and influencing the behavior of reinforcement learning algorithms. Ultimately, the Excitation Scenario encapsulates the precise operational environment for a given system or model under investigation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4b98982b6cac8028635bf8c32451b12e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a5c42907185fecc94ee85f1b33ec380&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2785b4e150ada39f3077e573325eeb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b984e4831b11f20f84bb8669cd7396b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c48c238aedc178e88e075043138eb72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11edf0b881aae28423c1b0d33893b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4124ebf92b84af2f8bbaecff99882b47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-081d5d2ff62504474fc8532dd817fb39</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289014</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Convergence Results">
      <data key="d0">Convergence Results</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The outcomes showing whether an algorithm reached a stable solution.&lt;SEP&gt;Convergence Results refer to the behavior shown in the graph where the Exponentially Average Reward stabilizes over increasing Episodes Count, despite fluctuations.&lt;SEP&gt;Convergence Results is data showing the behavior of an algorithm, specifically for 0.75 Amps and 500 Turns, where the Exponentially Average Reward is plotted against Episodes Count.&lt;SEP&gt;Convergence results refer to the outcome of a reinforcement learning algorithm, showing how an "Exponentially Average Reward" changes over "Episodes Count" for specific excitation parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4b98982b6cac8028635bf8c32451b12e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ad8c5c524179d51552952772945627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a91ba976c2a6f12065edbafc2b708ae2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4124ebf92b84af2f8bbaecff99882b47</data>
