      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285366</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Geometry and B Distribution for Excitation Scenario (1.5 A, 500 Turns)">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Geometry and B Distribution for Excitation Scenario (1.5 A, 500 Turns)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The optimized geometry and magnetic flux density distribution for an excitation scenario with 1.5 A and 500 turns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4124ebf92b84af2f8bbaecff99882b47</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285366</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sequence for Optimal Geometry for Excitation: 1.5 A, 500 Turns">
      <data key="d0">Sequence for Optimal Geometry for Excitation: 1.5 A, 500 Turns</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The step-by-step process or configuration leading to the optimal geometry for excitation conditions of 1.5 A and 500 turns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4124ebf92b84af2f8bbaecff99882b47</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Convergence Results for 1.5 Amps, 500 Turns">
      <data key="d0">Convergence Results for 1.5 Amps, 500 Turns</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The outcomes demonstrating the stabilization of an algorithm under specific excitation parameters (1.5 Amps, 500 Turns).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4124ebf92b84af2f8bbaecff99882b47</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Geometry and B Distribution for Excitation Scenario (2.0 A, 500 Turns)">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Geometry and B Distribution for Excitation Scenario (2.0 A, 500 Turns)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The optimized geometry and magnetic flux density distribution for an excitation scenario with 2.0 A and 500 turns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4124ebf92b84af2f8bbaecff99882b47</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Geometry and B Distribution for Excitation Scenario (1.75 A, 500 Turns)">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Geometry and B Distribution for Excitation Scenario (1.75 A, 500 Turns)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The optimized geometry and magnetic flux density distribution for an excitation scenario with 1.75 A and 500 turns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4124ebf92b84af2f8bbaecff99882b47</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285368</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Geometry and B Distribution for Excitation Scenario (1.25 A, 500 Turns)">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Geometry and B Distribution for Excitation Scenario (1.25 A, 500 Turns)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The optimized geometry and magnetic flux density distribution for an excitation scenario with 1.25 A and 500 turns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4124ebf92b84af2f8bbaecff99882b47</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285368</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1D Sequence">
      <data key="d0">1D Sequence</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A representation of torque-speed points or efficiency values, converted from a 2D map.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285368</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sequence-to-Sequence Modeling">
      <data key="d0">Sequence-to-Sequence Modeling</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of modeling that processes variable length input sequences to variable length output sequences.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285369</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs)">
      <data key="d0">Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of deep learning network effective for problems with variable numbers of inputs and/outputs, and used for predicting performance parameters of electric motor drives.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of neural network used in sequence-to-sequence modeling, especially with an attention mechanism to handle long-term dependencies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285369</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Attention Mechanism">
      <data key="d0">Attention Mechanism</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The attention mechanism is an algorithm whose usage was modified to better suit the task of efficiency prediction in Chapter 3.&lt;SEP&gt;A technique introduced to mitigate information loss in long sequences, allowing the model to focus on important parts of the input sequence for better prediction.&lt;SEP&gt;An Attention Mechanism is introduced in the RNN architecture to mitigate information loss when encoding long sequences, visually represented by a central horizontal grey line in Figure 3.8(a).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-610e0748724855c847b8fd20690e22db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0dd4b73a53d12770e110415d865cc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289364</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Decoder">
      <data key="d0">Decoder</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The decoder is the second part of the DL network architecture, which uses information from the encoder to semantically project discriminative features onto the original input space.&lt;SEP&gt;A component of the model, implemented as a dense time-distributed layer, that accumulates information and predicts the target sequence of efficiency values.&lt;SEP&gt;The Decoder is the right section of the network, indicated by a green bracket, responsible for upsampling operations.&lt;SEP&gt;The Decoder is a section of the deep learning model that reconstructs the output from the latent representation, using up sampling and de-convolutional layers.&lt;SEP&gt;The Decoder is a section of the neural network comprising "LSTM #3" and the "Time-Distributed Dense Layer."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640da4d848d6502c665bfc0ea66dce13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f41a0abc7626a2c56bb77667264c7785&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Excitation">
      <data key="d0">Excitation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Excitation is information included in the network input at each node.&lt;SEP&gt;One of the five inputs fed to the sequence-based model, represented by $(N^*, I_s^*, \gamma^*)$.&lt;SEP&gt;Excitation is a process or state defined by specific parameters (1.0 A, 250 Turns), which is being optimized in the presented sequence.&lt;SEP&gt;Excitation is a condition applied to the C-core, specified as 0.75 A and 500 Turns for the optimization sequence.&lt;SEP&gt;Excitation refers to the condition under which the optimal geometry for the magnetic core is determined, specifically 1.5 A and 500 Turns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68f5932d6d7a0e838c4c7ec90c8f340d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6248c24886e0bd2c4ecb6bd221bb01dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f2486e70169df5ba045508cbd9276f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288764</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Torque Values">
      <data key="d0">Torque Values</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">One of the five inputs fed to the sequence-based model, represented by $T_{em}^*$.&lt;SEP&gt;Torque values are the outputs predicted by a CNN-based performance estimator for the optimization of IPM motors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285370</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Base Speed">
      <data key="d0">Base Speed</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">One of the five inputs fed to the sequence-based model, represented by $N_{base}^*$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285370</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RNN Cell">
      <data key="d0">RNN Cell</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A component of an RNN consisting of hidden memory elements responsible for encoding long-term dependencies in the input sequence.&lt;SEP&gt;The RNN Cell is a component within the Recurrent Neural Network, responsible for encoding long-term dependencies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0dd4b73a53d12770e110415d865cc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289366</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Context Vector">
      <data key="d0">Context Vector</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A vector initialized with information related to motor geometry, encoding input information relevant for prediction, and summarizing global information for a design sample.&lt;SEP&gt;The Context Vector summarizes information from the RNN sequence, initialized with motor geometry information and combined with the attention mechanism's output.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0dd4b73a53d12770e110415d865cc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289364</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CNN (Convolutional Neural Network)">
      <data key="d0">CNN (Convolutional Neural Network)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of neural network used to provide motor geometry information to the context vector or for efficiency map prediction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285371</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Graves, Mohamed, and Hinton (2013)">
      <data key="d0">Graves, Mohamed, and Hinton (2013)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Researchers associated with the development of RNNs for encoding long-term dependencies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285371</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vaswani et al. (2017)">
      <data key="d0">Vaswani et al. (2017)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Researchers associated with the introduction of the attention mechanism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285371</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Time-Distributed Feedforward Network">
      <data key="d0">Time-Distributed Feedforward Network</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An ANN sub-network that takes global information (context vector) and predicts efficiency values, with weights shared across sequence elements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285371</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parameter Sharing">
      <data key="d0">Parameter Sharing</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A property that allows RNNs and time-distributed feedforward networks to handle variable sequence lengths by sharing weights across cells or copies of the network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285372</data>
    </node>
    <node id="End-to-End DL Model">
      <data key="d0">End-to-End DL Model</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A single deep learning model that integrates all steps from input to output, bypassing sub-models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285373</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FE-Based Efficiency Map">
      <data key="d0">FE-Based Efficiency Map</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An efficiency map generated using finite element methods, from which efficiency values are interpolated for the target.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285373</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flux Linkage Map">
      <data key="d0">Flux Linkage Map</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A component whose generation dependency is bypassed by formulating data for an end-to-end model.&lt;SEP&gt;A map constructed using predicted dq flux linkage values from the DL model, compared against maps obtained from FE analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76b69dc7913a240aef52b550d176029b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285373</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Control Strategies">
      <data key="d0">Control Strategies</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Methods used for excitation calculation, whose dependency is bypassed by formulating data for an end-to-end model.&lt;SEP&gt;Control Strategies are an input to the Excitation Calculation stage and are used to find optimal excitation conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Control Strategies are inputs that feed into the Excitation Calculation (Analytical Equations) block.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288911</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peak Values">
      <data key="d0">Peak Values</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Normalized values of speed (N*) and EM torque (Tem*) that the efficiency map relies on, predicted by a separate network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285374</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Section 3.4.1">
      <data key="d0">Section 3.4.1</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A section describing the chain of sub-models that the end-to-end network approach is compared against.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285374</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Section 2.6">
      <data key="d0">Section 2.6</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A section discussing the CNN-based network similar to the one used for efficiency map prediction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285374</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 3.8 (a)">
      <data key="d0">Figure 3.8 (a)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A figure referenced for visualizing RNNs with attention, input sequences, RNN cells, context vector initialization, and the attention mechanism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285374</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 3.8 (b)">
      <data key="d0">Figure 3.8 (b)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A figure referenced for visualizing a dense time distributed layer as a decoder and the weighting of encoder output with score value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285375</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 3.9">
      <data key="d0">Figure 3.9</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A figure referenced in the text that shows the architecture of the CNN-based network.&lt;SEP&gt;A figure referenced for showing the network architecture of the end-to-end DL model.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 3.9 is an image depicting the Network Architecture for End-to-end efficiency map prediction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-640da4d848d6502c665bfc0ea66dce13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76b69dc7913a240aef52b550d176029b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289263</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Global Information">
      <data key="d0">Global Information</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Centralized data relevant to the entire system, summarized by the context vector.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285375</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Torque-Speed Points">
      <data key="d0">Torque-Speed Points</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Data points related to torque and speed that can be represented as a 1D sequence derived from a 2D efficiency map.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285375</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2D Map">
      <data key="d0">2D Map</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A representation, such as an efficiency map, that can be converted into a 1D sequence for processing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285376</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RNNs">
      <data key="d0">RNNs</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Recurrent Neural Networks, a type of deep learning algorithm used for sequence-to-sequence modeling, particularly with an attention mechanism for handling long-term dependencies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285376</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Input Sequence">
      <data key="d0">Input Sequence</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Five inputs fed to the sequence-based model: excitation (N*, Is*, γ*), torque values (Tem*), and base speed (Nbase*).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285376</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Long-Term Dependencies">
      <data key="d0">Long-Term Dependencies</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Relationships in a sequence that span over a large separation, handled by RNNs with attention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285377</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Motor Geometry">
      <data key="d0">Motor Geometry</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Motor geometry refers to the physical design of a motor, which can significantly influence the accuracy of neural network predictions if it differs from the training set.&lt;SEP&gt;Information about the physical characteristics of a motor, provided as geometric parameters or through a CNN, used to initialize the context vector.&lt;SEP&gt;The geometric configuration of the IPM motor, including its various components and their dimensions, which are parametrized for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-679159f51a7b3c245fdff9193fd1ef89</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289310</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Geometric Parameters">
      <data key="d0">Geometric Parameters</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Values used in a variant of a sub-network, which marginally outperformed the CNN-based model.&lt;SEP&gt;Data describing the motor geometry, used as part of the input information.&lt;SEP&gt;Geometric parameters refer to the variables defining the physical dimensions and shape of the motor design, such as X1, X2, X3, X4, X5, X6, X7, X8, X9, and X10.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76b69dc7913a240aef52b550d176029b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289339</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Information Loss">
      <data key="d0">Information Loss</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A phenomenon occurring when all input data and motor geometric information is encoded into a long sequence, leading to poor performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285377</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vaswani Et Al.">
      <data key="d0">Vaswani Et Al.</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The authors of a 2017 paper credited with the introduction of the attention mechanism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285377</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Attention Layer">
      <data key="d0">Attention Layer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The part of the model where the input sequence is compared with encoder information to generate an attention score.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285378</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Attention Score">
      <data key="d0">Attention Score</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A value (e.g., a32) indicating the importance of an encoder output state for predicting a specific step in the efficiency sequence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285378</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Time Distributed Feedforward Network">
      <data key="d0">Time Distributed Feedforward Network</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A network that takes global information (context vector) and weighted encoder output to predict efficiency values.&lt;SEP&gt;A Time Distributed Feedforward Network receives combined information from the attention mechanism's output and the context vector to predict the efficiency sequence, as shown in Figure 3.8(b).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0dd4b73a53d12770e110415d865cc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289366</data>
    </node>
    <node id="End-To-End Network Approach">
      <data key="d0">End-To-End Network Approach</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An alternative approach to neural network design, discussed in sections 3.4.2 and 3.5.2.&lt;SEP&gt;The end-to-end network approach trains a single network to perform the entire task, capable of predicting efficiency maps.&lt;SEP&gt;An alternative method involving a single deep learning model to predict efficiency maps, bypassing intermediate sub-models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76b69dc7913a240aef52b550d176029b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285379</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Uniform Grid Of Efficiency Values">
      <data key="d0">Uniform Grid Of Efficiency Values</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The target for the end-to-end network, interpolated from an FE-based efficiency map.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285379</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peak Speed">
      <data key="d0">Peak Speed</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The maximum speed value (N*) in the predicted map, normalized to 1 per sample.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285379</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peak EM Torque">
      <data key="d0">Peak EM Torque</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The maximum electromagnetic torque value (Tem*) in the predicted map, normalized to 1 per sample.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285380</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CNN-Based Network">
      <data key="d0">CNN-Based Network</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) based network discussed in Section 2.6 and shown in Figure 3.9, which has symmetrical encoding and decoding sections. It predicts normalized values of speed and EM torque.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of network referenced in Section 2.6, similar to the one used for efficiency map prediction in the end-to-end approach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76b69dc7913a240aef52b550d176029b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285380</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Target 2D Data">
      <data key="d0">Target 2D Data</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Formulated data for the end-to-end network approach where peak speed and EM torque are normalized.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285380</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Graves, Mohamed, And Hinton (2013)">
      <data key="d0">Graves, Mohamed, And Hinton (2013)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Authors credited for work related to RNN cells encoding long-term dependencies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285380</data>
    </node>
    <node id="N*">
      <data key="d0">N*</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A variable representing excitation or peak speed.&lt;SEP&gt;N* is an output of the Excitation Calculation stage representing optimal conditions, and an input to the Torque and Efficiency Calculation stage.&lt;SEP&gt;N* is an output from the Excitation Calculation (Analytical Equations), representing a specific excited motor speed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288911</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Is*">
      <data key="d0">Is*</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A variable representing excitation.&lt;SEP&gt;Is* is an output from the Excitation Calculation (Analytical Equations), representing a specific excited motor current.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289416</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gamma*">
      <data key="d0">Gamma*</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A variable representing excitation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gamma* is an output from the Excitation Calculation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tem*">
      <data key="d0">Tem*</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A variable representing torque values or peak EM torque.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285381</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nbase*">
      <data key="d0">Nbase*</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A variable representing base speed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d489b649275341aa1660eded71c88c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285382</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SeqTOv2">
      <data key="d0">SeqTOv2</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">SeqTOv2 is a methodology that does not support placing a thin strip close to the air gap, which indicates inflexibility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285382</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RL Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">RL Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">RL Algorithms are reinforcement learning algorithms whose applicability will be explored in the future with an improved SeqTO environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285383</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electromagnetic Device">
      <data key="d0">Electromagnetic Device</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">An electromagnetic device is the subject for which the proposed TO technique and learning methodology provide solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;The Electromagnetic Device is a physical structure, possibly a motor or generator stator, whose cross-section is depicted in Figure 2.13 to show error and uncertainty.&lt;SEP&gt;An Electromagnetic Device, specifically a C-core, is the subject of the design domain illustrated.&lt;SEP&gt;An application domain for the described equation, specifically for a C-core actuator.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec0d779e474774183c36eade5f33db6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92cc2b02ae3a119d92f0d1a7f6617885&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288891</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Statistical Learning Methods">
      <data key="d0">Statistical Learning Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Statistical Learning Methods, including supervised, Bayesian, and reinforcement learning, were used to explore the feasibility of aiding design optimization and analysis of electric devices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285383</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electric Devices and Machines">
      <data key="d0">Electric Devices and Machines</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Electric devices and machines are the subjects of design optimization and analysis using statistical learning methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285383</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Learning Models">
      <data key="d0">Deep Learning Models</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Novel data-driven deep learning models are explored to predict solutions for Maxwell's equations and performance maps for low-frequency electromagnetic devices.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep Learning Models are models that typically have many parameters to be tuned and require substantial data for generalization.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep Learning Models are explored for other computationally expensive tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;Models that possess powerful expressive ability, which should be leveraged by retaining the pre-trained network as much as possible.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep Learning Models are modern models used to reduce the time spent in analyzing field and performance maps for low-frequency electromagnetic devices.&lt;SEP&gt;These models are trained using motor geometric images and efficiency maps to understand motor designs.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep Learning Models are targeted for replacing costly FE simulations in Stages 1 and 3 of the efficiency map calculation process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e320c3afc0456b527186e2755a2ac4af</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288799</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low-Frequency Electromagnetic Devices">
      <data key="d0">Low-Frequency Electromagnetic Devices</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">These are specific types of electromagnetic devices that are analyzed in the thesis, particularly regarding performance maps.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-frequency electromagnetic devices are specific electric devices for which Deep Learning models are used to reduce analysis time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285384</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Finite Element Method">
      <data key="d0">Finite Element Method</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Finite Element method is a conventional high-fidelity solver used for analyzing field and performance maps but demands high computation resources.&lt;SEP&gt;A numerical method described in a book by Jin and also used in the analysis of BLDC motors and hybrid stepping motors.&lt;SEP&gt;The Finite Element Method (FEM) is a computational technique for solving engineering and mathematical physics problems, often involving iterative refinement.&lt;SEP&gt;The Finite Element Method is a simulation approach implied by the discretized representation in panel (b), where the design space is divided into discrete elements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce41e8cc5ef38832503f503eebaccf56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2e27a9a79daf5a813690efa01766eca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca13e3ef7d4bf929b84d6b30574e3485</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289139</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Computational Electromagnetics">
      <data key="d0">Computational Electromagnetics</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Computational electromagnetics is a field where deep learning strategies are designed for inexpensive and accurate predictions of field distribution and performance maps of electric machines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285384</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Finite Elements Simulations">
      <data key="d0">Finite Elements Simulations</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Finite Elements simulations are used to create massive datasets, which can be expensive and time-consuming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285384</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transfer Learning">
      <data key="d0">Transfer Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Transfer Learning is a well-studied topic and machine learning technique that focuses on model-based knowledge transfer. It aims to enhance the generalizability of Deep Neural Networks (DNNs), reduce training costs, improve robustness, and decrease computational load by reducing data requirements and training time for new tasks. This is achieved by leveraging knowledge extracted from neural networks previously trained on similar domains, facilitating an information transfer strategy to handle large data requirements between comparable problems.

Transfer Learning involves transferring knowledge from a pre-trained model to a fine-tuned model for a new task. It is represented by a teal or light blue line in training curve depictions, such as Figure 3.22, demonstrating quicker initial error reduction and lower error values earlier than Supervised Learning, and achieving a low validation error faster and with less fluctuation. This methodology can be applied using Stage 3 of certain processes and is utilized to fine-tune agents like the A2C, significantly reducing computational resources needed for optimization. Different datasets, such as those for motor designs and performance maps, are essential for this technique. An experimental formulation of Transfer Learning is discussed in Section 3.7, and it is also the subject of a survey paper. Furthermore, it is employed for efficiency map prediction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f6cebd0187b0c1924a3e6491e8ebe8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bea7680cdfeea039ec6cf21875ea0c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2e27a9a79daf5a813690efa01766eca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c88cccaef550c4d3cde6f591d2fe5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-679159f51a7b3c245fdff9193fd1ef89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-248a0b72513cd485685d4b3e3864e01e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-403239ec5550f149ced3802b6c18600b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288783</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Motor Topology">
      <data key="d0">Motor Topology</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Motor topology refers to the design structure of a motor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285385</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bayesian Learning">
      <data key="d0">Bayesian Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Bayesian learning is an extension incorporated into the supervised learning task to develop an indication of algorithm confidence in prediction.&lt;SEP&gt;Bayesian learning is an approach used to address the issue of uncertainty in trained networks within the context of generating performance maps.&lt;SEP&gt;Bayesian Learning is used for uncertainty analysis in the predictions of Deep Neural Networks (DNNs).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-610e0748724855c847b8fd20690e22db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-540fc8a4d14fea776a933c31f85916ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285385</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neural Networks">
      <data key="d0">Neural Networks</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Neural Networks encompass both a type of machine learning model and an academic journal. As a machine learning construct, Neural Networks are computational models inspired by the structure of the human brain. They consist of collections of neurons connected in an acyclic graph, functioning as universal approximators and implementing a nonlinear function of states to produce action value estimates. These networks are fundamental to Deep Learning, often described in overviews of the field, and compared to simpler versions like Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs). They require extensive training data, which can be a larger dataset created from variations of reference design parameters, and may necessitate retraining when exposed to new datasets or conditions. Historically, experiments with neural networks date back to the 1940s.

Neural Networks are primarily utilized as parametrized approximate value functions, with internal parameters like connection weights often denoted as 'w' or 'θ' (as seen in Policy Gradient algorithms where θ defines the policy π). They are specific types of function approximators used to parameterize the Q-function and approximate the action-value function Q(s, a). When coupled with Reinforcement Learning, they form the basis of Deep Reinforcement Learning. They are employed in various applications, including predicting magnetic field estimation in transformers, and for knowledge transfer tasks in motor design for electrical machines. The study of Neural Networks is a significant field related to Artificial Intelligence.

Separately, "Neural Networks" also refers to an academic journal that publishes research in the field of neural networks, known for publishing significant papers, such as one on "universal approximators."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-efb0bf44fcf7466c35982fd3bb8ae7ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a37e88914ecd388d79917c1c24f82fc2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-588116b5242516d003d1e70d48a7f42c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1cc3c9a8202b9032fe06ba473d7c76d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28be27ccbb95fc691bf7d1ab7fac43a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-403239ec5550f149ced3802b6c18600b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-294177d434050aa2bd996039510efe9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb622c9ce0600f3539c3ed7199e0c0ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-193c3a719cf23865bd32e0d110afb409&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1e3ef3a96efc33efd9047031ab15a09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2e27a9a79daf5a813690efa01766eca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e98180b8e135e08cf842f2a94ed38a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288837</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SeqTO">
      <data key="d0">SeqTO</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">SeqTO is a new Topology Optimization method that generates manufacturable designs and requires significantly less computational resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285386</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monte Carlo Tree Search">
      <data key="d0">Monte Carlo Tree Search</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Monte Carlo Tree Search is a tree search-based heuristic algorithm explored within the SeqTO environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285386</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Algorithm">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Algorithm</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Genetic Algorithm (GA) is an optimization algorithm and a popular metaheuristic approach inspired by natural selection. It is primarily used for various optimization problems, including binary optimization, and is particularly effective for optimizing discrete design parameters and material distribution within a design space.

In the context of the SeqTO-v1 environment, the Genetic Algorithm is applied for topology optimization, specifically to refine geometry and achieve an optimal magnetic field distribution. This includes optimizing the geometry of electromagnets, such as a C-core solenoid, and the topological optimization of a SynRM rotor. The GA has demonstrated high performance for optimal design within SeqTO-v1, achieving a force of 55 N with a 1x1 controller.

The Genetic Algorithm serves as an optimization technique for achieving C-core topology optimization within the SeqTO-v1 environment and is used to optimize the core's material distribution. It is also mentioned for its application with different state representations in optimization problems and for comparison with other methods like Monte Carlo Tree Search and TD-learning within the SeqTO framework.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f5a390cc7143789eccd25f5f3623694&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b76c5d19e7bcbbd5f11b71588e066f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57172dfb9b0c90234ff04b1a5519b71e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b984e4831b11f20f84bb8669cd7396b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51ad9a81904dfb8134371112a0be9fa9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6719c866b8100236897a8ad6ae78d11f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b7b73719952876f4f9c0e6b4efe0027&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5526ac278068ab9cbf72c11fd3817672&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0d5c4a49fbb5bedfe6eb2f200271d99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abb64932da4baf92322bc61ecf0df44f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288841</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reinforcement Learning Agent">
      <data key="d0">Reinforcement Learning Agent</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An agent based on deep reinforcement learning capable of producing manufacturable solutions with short iterative analysis process times.&lt;SEP&gt;A Reinforcement Learning Agent, trained with the sequential nature of the SeqTO environment and NN pattern learning, can generalize to different design parameters.&lt;SEP&gt;A reinforcement learning agent is an entity, possibly an A2C agent, whose reward function's evolution is demonstrated by the graph.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-efb0bf44fcf7466c35982fd3bb8ae7ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a91ba976c2a6f12065edbafc2b708ae2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289325</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 6">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 6</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 6 is titled "Conclusion &amp; Future Work" and provides an overview of the thesis findings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
