    </node>
    <node id="Evolutionary Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Evolutionary Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of conventional optimization method that requires high computational cost for iterative electromagnetic analyses.&lt;SEP&gt;Evolutionary Algorithms are a type of optimization algorithm compared with Reinforcement Learning in Table 4.1.&lt;SEP&gt;Evolutionary Algorithms (EA) are a type of stochastic method used in solving discrete material distribution problems, offering better optimization capability, stronger robustness, and parallel searching capability.&lt;SEP&gt;A popular type of stochastic method characterized by population, fitness, and variation.&lt;SEP&gt;Evolutionary Algorithms are a type of algorithm compared to sequence-based decision-making algorithms.&lt;SEP&gt;Evolutionary algorithms are conventional optimization methods that the trained RL agent outperforms in terms of finite element analysis reductions.&lt;SEP&gt;Evolutionary Algorithms (EAs) are an algorithmic approach characterized by starting with many agents, where only strong ones survive, and learning only optimal solutions while discarding suboptimal information. They are good for problems where the loss function is non-differentiable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8f5af4d3dcb1894cb22db778a1e1b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad46225ae0d5f3835a9bda1ed8158d48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289094</data>
    </node>
