    <node id="Density-Based Methods">
      <data key="d0">Density-Based Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Density-Based Methods are a common approach to topology optimization where the design domain is parameterized in an explicit function.&lt;SEP&gt;Density-based methods are a type of topology optimization approach that optimizes an objective function by determining whether each finite element should be solid material or void, using interpolation functions where material properties are interpreted as continuous design variables.&lt;SEP&gt;Density-Based Methods are an alternative approach to topology optimization that represents material distribution using a density field, contrasting with boundary-based methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Density-based methods are topology optimization techniques that infer material properties through continuous density fields.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d40236fe707c1436e66df73317598df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Homogenization">
      <data key="d0">Homogenization</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Homogenization is a topology optimization method based on the homogenization principle, transforming the optimal shape of a structure into an optimization problem for ideal material distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285396</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discrete Methods">
      <data key="d0">Discrete Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Discrete Methods are techniques used in Topology Optimization.&lt;SEP&gt;Discrete methods, also known as ON/OFF or binary discrete methods, constitute an approach to Topology Optimization where each element in the design space is described using binary digits, representing either a particular material or a void.&lt;SEP&gt;Discrete methods, also known as the ON/OFF approach, are a type of topology optimization technique.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285396</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ON/OFF Approach">
