    <node id="Sethian">
      <data key="d0">Sethian</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Sethian is a researcher credited with developing the methodology for boundary based topology optimization in 1988 alongside Osher.&lt;SEP&gt;Sethian is a researcher who, along with Osher, developed the boundary variation methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285405</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TO">
      <data key="d0">TO</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">TO, or Topology Optimization, is a mathematical method that optimizes material distribution within a given design space.&lt;SEP&gt;TO refers to Topology Optimization, which is a significant topic in various fields concerning material distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;An unspecified task facing the challenge of reducing its computational cost.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285405</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Homogenization Methods">
      <data key="d0">Homogenization Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Homogenization Methods are an approach to topology optimization that is contrasted with boundary-based and density-based methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Homogenization methods are a category of topology optimization techniques where the optimal shape of a structure is transformed into the optimal material distribution, based on the concept of relaxation.&lt;SEP&gt;A concept related to advanced manufacturing techniques used in topology optimization and 3D printing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c232c16db2cb946ab34105a48ebff79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289250</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Boundary-Based Methods">
      <data key="d0">Boundary-Based Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Boundary-based methods are a type of topology optimization approach widely adopted, particularly in recent decades, developed by Osher and Sethian.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285405</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Díaz">
      <data key="d0">Díaz</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Díaz is a researcher associated with the field of structural engineering and the attention received by the homogenization method.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285405</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Solid Isotropic Microstructure With Penalization Method">
      <data key="d0">Solid Isotropic Microstructure With Penalization Method</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Solid Isotropic Microstructure with Penalization (SIMP) method is a typical density-based method for topology optimization, proposed by Bendsoe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285406</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electromagnetic Field">
      <data key="d0">Electromagnetic Field</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The electromagnetic field is a domain where global optimization methods and topology optimization techniques are applied.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285406</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Solution Accuracy">
      <data key="d0">Solution Accuracy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Solution accuracy refers to the precision desired in a solution, which can lead to finer meshes and slow convergence speed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285407</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Global Optimization Methods">
      <data key="d0">Global Optimization Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Global optimization methods are techniques commonly applied in the electromagnetic field to find optimal solutions by iteratively improving solutions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285407</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimization Problem">
      <data key="d0">Optimization Problem</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A problem formulated to minimize the negative magnitude of force experienced by the armature.&lt;SEP&gt;An optimization problem involves finding the optimal solution among a set of possible solutions through iterative processes.&lt;SEP&gt;The Optimization Problem is the broad framework for designing the C-core electromagnetic actuator to maximize the force experienced by the armature.&lt;SEP&gt;An Optimization Problem involves the placement or configuration of materials within a discretized space, potentially using Reinforcement Learning or Genetic Algorithms.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical problem represented by the equation aiming to minimize a function 'f(a)' subject to certain constraints.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7b76c5d19e7bcbbd5f11b71588e066f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14506e8e02aeb799dcd49d4b24c5b529&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292791</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EM Devices">
      <data key="d0">EM Devices</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">EM devices are Electromagnetic devices that have been designed using various Topology Optimization methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285407</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Structural Engineering">
      <data key="d0">Structural Engineering</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Structural engineering is a field where Bendsoe and Kikuchi's homogenization method has received a lot of attention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285407</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Shape">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Shape</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Optimal shape refers to the ideal configuration of a structure, which is transformed into an optimization problem for ideal material distribution in homogenization methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285407</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2D Design Space">
      <data key="d0">2D Design Space</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The 2D design space is a context where a microstructure is described using three design variables in the homogenization method.&lt;SEP&gt;The 2D Design Space is the context in which the three design variables (x, y, and θ) are used to describe a microstructure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb6925d3d6ed02adeec99edaa647d90b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288753</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microstructure">
      <data key="d0">Microstructure</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A microstructure can be described by design variables in the homogenization method and refers to the internal structure of materials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Relaxation">
      <data key="d0">Relaxation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Relaxation is a concept on which the homogenization method is based, making ill-posed problems well-posed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285409</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fixed Domain Of Finite Elements">
      <data key="d0">Fixed Domain Of Finite Elements</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A fixed domain of finite elements is the context in which density-based methods operate to optimize an objective function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285409</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Objective Function">
      <data key="d0">Objective Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A function to be either minimized or maximized in an optimization problem, also known as a cost function.&lt;SEP&gt;An objective function is optimized in density-based methods by determining whether each element should be solid material or void.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285409</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Interpolation Functions">
      <data key="d0">Interpolation Functions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Interpolation functions are adopted in density-based methods to interpret material properties as continuous design variables.&lt;SEP&gt;Interpolation Functions are mathematical functions used in FEM to approximate unknown values within elements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce41e8cc5ef38832503f503eebaccf56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289141</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discrete Variables">
      <data key="d0">Discrete Variables</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Discrete variables in density-based methods are transferred to continuous variables to steer the solution iteratively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285410</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Continuous Variables">
      <data key="d0">Continuous Variables</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Continuous variables are derived from discrete variables in density-based methods to allow for iterative optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285410</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimizer">
      <data key="d0">Optimizer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An optimizer is used in density-based methods to iteratively steer the solution towards a discrete solid/void topology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285411</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Penalty Methods">
      <data key="d0">Penalty Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Penalty methods are utilized in density-based methods to force the solution to crisp "0/1" or "solid/void" topologies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285411</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regularization">
      <data key="d0">Regularization</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Regularization is a technique used to prevent overfitting, employed during the model training process.&lt;SEP&gt;Regularization is a technique adopted in density-based methods to alleviate the checkerboard problem and mesh-dependency issue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76b69dc7913a240aef52b550d176029b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285411</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Filter Techniques">
      <data key="d0">Filter Techniques</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Filter techniques are adopted in density-based methods to alleviate the checkerboard problem and mesh-dependency issue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285411</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Checkerboard Problem">
      <data key="d0">Checkerboard Problem</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The checkerboard problem is an issue in density-based methods that regularization and filter techniques aim to alleviate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285412</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mesh-Dependency Issue">
      <data key="d0">Mesh-Dependency Issue</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The mesh-dependency issue is a problem in density-based methods that regularization and filter techniques aim to alleviate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285412</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Structure Optimizations">
      <data key="d0">Structure Optimizations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Structure optimizations are applications where the SIMP method gained popularity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285412</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electromagnetics Domain">
      <data key="d0">Electromagnetics Domain</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The electromagnetics domain is a field where topology optimization literature based on density-based methods has sprouted.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285412</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mechanical Domain">
      <data key="d0">Mechanical Domain</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mechanical Domain is the origin of the theoretical description of Topology Optimization, which can be traced back to Michell’s 1904 paper.&lt;SEP&gt;The mechanical domain is where Sigmund and Petersson discussed difficulties of the SIMP method.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f3d168f1b6a8020e0606ef52cd19390</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285413</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Optimal">
      <data key="d0">Local Optimal</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Local optimal refers to a type of difficulty encountered with the SIMP method.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285413</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mesh-Dependent Structures">
      <data key="d0">Mesh-Dependent Structures</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mesh-dependent structures are a difficulty of the SIMP method, where optimization results are influenced by the mesh.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285413</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Checkerboard Patterns">
      <data key="d0">Checkerboard Patterns</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Checkerboard patterns are associated with difficulties in the SIMP method.&lt;SEP&gt;Checkerboard Patterns are a limitation or issue that can affect density-based topology optimization methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Checkerboard patterns are undesirable artifacts in Topology Optimization that the described approach aims to avoid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d40236fe707c1436e66df73317598df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288987</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Initial Conditions">
      <data key="d0">Initial Conditions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Initial conditions are factors that optimized results depend on in density-based methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285414</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intermediate Density">
      <data key="d0">Intermediate Density</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Intermediate density, also referred to as grayscales material, is a typical problem with the density-based method.&lt;SEP&gt;Intermediate Density refers to a problem in topology optimization methods where material properties are not distinctly binary (solid/void), often seen in density-based approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d40236fe707c1436e66df73317598df7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grayscales Material">
      <data key="d0">Grayscales Material</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Grayscales material refers to regions of intermediate density allowed in optimal configurations of density-based methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285414</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Engineering Topology Design Problems">
      <data key="d0">Engineering Topology Design Problems</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Engineering topology design problems are contexts where penalization and filtering schemes aim to eliminate grayscales.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285414</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Penalization">
      <data key="d0">Penalization</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Penalization is a scheme used to eliminate grayscales in engineering topology design problems, acting as a form of regularization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285415</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Artificial Parameters">
      <data key="d0">Artificial Parameters</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Artificial parameters are relied upon by filtering schemes, lacking rational guidelines for their determination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285415</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Appropriate A Priori Parameter Values">
      <data key="d0">Appropriate A Priori Parameter Values</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Appropriate a priori parameter values are values for artificial parameters that lack rational guidelines for determination in filtering schemes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285416</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shape Optimization Techniques">
      <data key="d0">Shape Optimization Techniques</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Shape Optimization Techniques form the root of boundary variation methods in topology optimization.&lt;SEP&gt;Shape optimization techniques are the root of boundary variation methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285416</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Implicit Function">
      <data key="d0">Implicit Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An implicit function is the basis for boundary-based methods, differentiating them from density-based methods.&lt;SEP&gt;An implicit function, such as Φ(x,t) = c, defines a structural boundary without explicitly solving for one variable in terms of others.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31b45185f3e33b63259454671f7614ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bayesian Approach">
      <data key="d0">Bayesian Approach</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An approach that could relate two regions of high error with high uncertainty.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bayesian approach relates two regions of high error with high uncertainty and improves accuracy.&lt;SEP&gt;A Bayesian approach can relate regions of high error with high uncertainty in predictions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-679159f51a7b3c245fdff9193fd1ef89</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285416</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MC Samples">
      <data key="d0">MC Samples</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">MC samples are retrieved from a network to compute uncertainty maps and calculate standard deviation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285416</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Network">
      <data key="d0">Network</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The network is used in the Bayesian approach to retrieve MC samples.&lt;SEP&gt;The network is an entity that directly outputs the probability of selecting each action in the context of the PG algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285416</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standard Deviation">
      <data key="d0">Standard Deviation</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard deviation is calculated over MC samples to determine uncertainty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Field Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Field Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Learned from the input text in the conclusion.&lt;SEP&gt;The field distribution is learned from FEA software and can be efficiently approximated and generated for new geometries.&lt;SEP&gt;Field distribution is a performance measure required for the operation of the optimization algorithm, provided by the EM solver.&lt;SEP&gt;A distribution representing values across a given field, mentioned as an application for $y$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289396</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FEA Software">
      <data key="d0">FEA Software</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Finite Element Analysis software from which the model learns the field distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;Software used for Finite Element Analysis, which might be consulted when high prediction uncertainty is detected by the model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289279</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GPUs">
      <data key="d0">GPUs</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">GPUs (Graphics Processing Units) are a type of computation power that contribute to the growth of deep learning.&lt;SEP&gt;Graphics Processing Units that offer the advantage of parallelizability for the model, enabling faster processing.&lt;SEP&gt;GPUs (Graphics Processing Units) are hardware which, along with probabilistic methods, makes a parallelizable and robust method to produce efficiency maps feasible.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b1e3ef3a96efc33efd9047031ab15a09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f237e58ddc202b94dc8ea5b3ef7e3145</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285418</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probabilistic Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Probabilistic Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Probabilistic analysis is presented for prediction without ground truth, offering decision-making capacity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285418</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Structured CNN">
      <data key="d0">Structured CNN</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of Convolutional Neural Network that is not ideal for handling unstructured meshes used in FEA.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285419</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DL Field Estimator Model">
      <data key="d0">DL Field Estimator Model</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A Deep Learning model that can function as an initial guess for the FEA solver despite not being an ideal architecture for unstructured meshes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285419</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FEA Solver">
      <data key="d0">FEA Solver</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The FEA solver is a numerical method that can use the DL field estimator model as an initial guess.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285419</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Graph Convolutional Networks">
      <data key="d0">Graph Convolutional Networks</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Graph Convolutional Networks are more suited for handling unstructured meshes and are a potential extension for improved prediction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285419</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Unstructured Meshes">
      <data key="d0">Unstructured Meshes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Unstructured meshes are employed in FEA and are better handled by Graph Convolutional Networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285419</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Simulated Data">
      <data key="d0">Simulated Data</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Large amounts of data required for training deep networks, identified as a bottleneck.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285420</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Semi-Supervised Learning">
      <data key="d0">Semi-Supervised Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Semi-supervised learning is an approach that offers an exciting path with less dependency on labeled data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285420</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Unsupervised Learning">
      <data key="d0">Unsupervised Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An approach, similar to semi-supervised learning, that can reduce dependency on labeled data for training deep networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285421</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Network">
      <data key="d0">Deep Network</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A Deep Network is a neural network characterized by having more than one hidden layer.&lt;SEP&gt;A deep network requires a significant amount of simulated data for training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e320c3afc0456b527186e2755a2ac4af</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285421</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Performance Maps">
      <data key="d0">Performance Maps</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Higher dimensional output spaces representing performance parameters of electric motors, whose prediction time is significantly reduced using deep networks.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance maps, including efficiency and power factor maps, are computationally expensive tasks discussed later.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance maps are outputs of DNNs for motor drives, such as efficiency and power factor, generated based on motor geometries.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance Maps are visual aids used to illustrate the performance and reliability of models or simulations in the context of electric motor design.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance maps are the output of a calculation process, specifically related to efficiency and power factor for electric motors.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance maps define a motor's performance and are calculated through FE simulations, using design parameters as inputs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c85d4390c7f5cda3bb7e9cc87cbcec0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f6cebd0187b0c1924a3e6491e8ebe8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-679159f51a7b3c245fdff9193fd1ef89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289228</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Power Factor Maps">
      <data key="d0">Power Factor Maps</data>
