      <data key="d3">chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285439</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yang et al. 2015">
      <data key="d0">Yang et al. 2015</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A reference to a study related to the encouragement from the US Department of Energy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285440</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Highly Efficient Drivetrains">
      <data key="d0">Highly Efficient Drivetrains</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Drivetrains encouraged for design by the US Department of Energy for variable-speed drives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285440</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Variable-Speed Drives">
      <data key="d0">Variable-Speed Drives</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Drives for which highly efficient drivetrains are encouraged, such as those in hybrid and electric vehicles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285440</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hybrid Electric Vehicles">
      <data key="d0">Hybrid Electric Vehicles</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Vehicles that serve as an alternative to fossil fuel-based vehicles and offer environmentally friendly transportation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hybrid Electric Vehicles are a type of vehicle that uses both a conventional internal combustion engine and an electric propulsion system, for which motor efficiency maps are crucial in dynamic simulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-172d2c57a19dd33adf49cb4619a788cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electric Vehicles">
      <data key="d0">Electric Vehicles</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Vehicles that serve as an alternative to fossil fuel-based vehicles and offer environmentally friendly transportation.&lt;SEP&gt;Electric vehicles are practical applications that can benefit from enhanced motor designs resulting from this optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288996</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fossil Fuel-Based Vehicles">
      <data key="d0">Fossil Fuel-Based Vehicles</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Vehicles that HEVs serve as an alternative to.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6d4389bce8e015dd342452266c2e8a65</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285441</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feedforward Neural Networks">
      <data key="d0">Feedforward Neural Networks</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Feedforward Neural Networks (FNN) are a type of Machine Learning algorithm suitable for function approximation and handling tasks with a limited number of output values.&lt;SEP&gt;Feedforward Neural Networks are simple neural networks discussed for comparison with CNNs.&lt;SEP&gt;Feedforward Neural Networks are a type of simple neural network discussed in Section 2.5, used for comparison with CNNs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-610e0748724855c847b8fd20690e22db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285441</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CNNs">
      <data key="d0">CNNs</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">CNNs (Convolutional Neural Networks) are a type of neural network whose architecture is described, featuring convolutional, pooling, and fully connected layers.&lt;SEP&gt;Refers to three Convolutional Neural Networks for which uncertainty maps are computed using Monte Carlo dropout.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285441</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Convolutional Layer (CONV)">
      <data key="d0">Convolutional Layer (CONV)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Convolutional layer is a type of layer in CNN architecture that computes connections with local regions of the input volume using learnable filters/kernels, creating a 2-D activation map.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285442</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Filters/Kernels">
      <data key="d0">Filters/Kernels</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Filters/Kernels are learnable components within a Convolutional layer that slide across the input to compute dot products and create activation maps, designed to detect specific visual features.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285442</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2-D Activation Map/Feature Map">
      <data key="d0">2-D Activation Map/Feature Map</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A 2-D Activation Map/Feature Map is created by a filter in a Convolutional layer, representing the filter's response at different positions in the input.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285442</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pooling Layer (POOL)">
      <data key="d0">Pooling Layer (POOL)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Pooling layer is a type of layer in CNN architecture responsible for downsampling the spatial dimension of the input, aiming to reduce parameters and avoid overfitting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285442</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fully Connected Layer (FC)">
      <data key="d0">Fully Connected Layer (FC)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Fully Connected layer is a type of layer in CNN architecture where neurons have dense connections with activations in previous layers, balancing locality-context tradeoff and identifying global trends.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285443</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DL Network">
      <data key="d0">DL Network</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The DL (Deep Learning) network processes uniformly placed spatial data with related neighboring pixels, performing dense regression for tasks like image segmentation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285443</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dense Kernel">
      <data key="d0">Dense Kernel</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A dense kernel is used by a convolutional filter to group similar pixels, as shown in Figure 2.7.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285443</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dilated Filter">
      <data key="d0">Dilated Filter</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A dilated filter is a type of filter that can capture spatially distant relationships, as illustrated in Figure 2.9b.&lt;SEP&gt;A Dilated Filter is a type of convolutional filter that processes a wider, non-contiguous area of the input map by introducing gaps between its sampling points, illustrated in part (b) of Figure 2.9.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a4b902f2347e5fea9d43e606ce167ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289014</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DL Model">
      <data key="d0">DL Model</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The DL model described in Figures 2.8 and 2.11 has 32 layers, including convolutional, pooling/up-sampling, and dropout layers, and is trained using backpropagation.&lt;SEP&gt;A Deep Learning model used to predict dq flux linkage values.&lt;SEP&gt;The DL model mentioned in the text refers to the Deep Learning model whose architecture and training are detailed in Table 2.4.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a177157c3402545cf649cdb67022050a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76b69dc7913a240aef52b550d176029b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289004</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ReLU (Rectified Linear Unit)">
      <data key="d0">ReLU (Rectified Linear Unit)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">ReLU is an activation function used in the convolutional layers as part of each block in the DL model's architecture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285444</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Batch-Normalization Layer">
      <data key="d0">Batch-Normalization Layer</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A batch-normalization layer is included in each block of the DL model's architecture to normalize activations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285445</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Max-Pooling">
      <data key="d0">Max-Pooling</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Max-pooling is a technique used in the DL model, specifically a $2 \times 2$ max-pooling layer with a stride of 2, identified by a green arrow in the figures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285445</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Backpropagation">
      <data key="d0">Backpropagation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Backpropagation is a method for computing gradients of expressions through the recursive application of the chain rule.&lt;SEP&gt;Backpropagation is the algorithm used to train the DL network, aiming to minimize the RMS error.&lt;SEP&gt;A method by which gradients flow through layers during training to update network weights.&lt;SEP&gt;The algorithm used to calculate the gradient of the loss function with respect to the weights in a neural network.&lt;SEP&gt;Backpropagation is a method compatible with the update equation for training neural networks by propagating errors backward through the network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288856</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RMS Error">
      <data key="d0">RMS Error</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">RMS (Root Mean Square) error is a metric that the network aims to minimize during training between prediction and FEA field distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;RMS error is a metric referring to the Root Mean Square error, used to measure prediction accuracy, especially in the context of the validation dataset and FEA field distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a177157c3402545cf649cdb67022050a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289004</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FEA Field Distribution">
      <data key="d0">FEA Field Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">FEA (Finite Element Analysis) field distribution is the target for the DL model's prediction, from which the RMS error is calculated.&lt;SEP&gt;FEA Field Distribution refers to the finite element analysis field distribution, which the Deep Learning model aims to predict and minimize RMS error with.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a177157c3402545cf649cdb67022050a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Geometrical and Excitation Parameters">
      <data key="d0">Geometrical and Excitation Parameters</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Geometrical and excitation parameters, mentioned in Section 2.4, set limits for populating a design space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285445</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Latin Hypercube">
      <data key="d0">Latin Hypercube</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A sampling technique used to populate all samples to address issues in data representation.&lt;SEP&gt;Latin Hypercube is a sampling method used to populate a design space with geometrical and excitation parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e320c3afc0456b527186e2755a2ac4af</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285446</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dataset">
      <data key="d0">Dataset</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A collection of instances sharing common attributes, serving as input data for Deep Learning models.&lt;SEP&gt;The dataset is partitioned randomly into training, validation, and test sets.&lt;SEP&gt;The dataset is generated by varying transformer dimensions within specified bounds to train neural networks.&lt;SEP&gt;Dataset is a collection of data used for training and validation of neural network models.&lt;SEP&gt;Dataset refers to the collection of samples used for training, validating, and testing the Deep Learning model.&lt;SEP&gt;A Dataset of 3200 samples is created by varying parameters of the FSCW PM motor geometry, used for training, validation, and testing in transfer learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a177157c3402545cf649cdb67022050a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e320c3afc0456b527186e2755a2ac4af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8854ea1ca7b618b176856798664355f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e98180b8e135e08cf842f2a94ed38a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f476972defaf212918b90b259aa69f03</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288770</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Test Data">
      <data key="d0">Test Data</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A portion of the dataset used to test the final performance of the DL model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285447</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cross-Validation">
      <data key="d0">Cross-Validation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Cross-validation is a data partitioning technique, not used in this case due to high computation cost.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285447</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bootstrap-Based Validation">
      <data key="d0">Bootstrap-Based Validation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Bootstrap-based validation is a data partitioning technique, not used in this case due to high computation cost.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285447</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dense Regression">
      <data key="d0">Dense Regression</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Dense Regression is a problem area in which Machine Learning, particularly with Deep Learning, is applied.&lt;SEP&gt;Dense regression is the focus of the DL model, rather than classification, resembling architectures used for image segmentation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b1e3ef3a96efc33efd9047031ab15a09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285448</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Classification">
      <data key="d0">Classification</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Classification is less relevant for the DL model's architecture, which focuses on dense regression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285448</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Encoder Section">
      <data key="d0">Encoder Section</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The encoder section is one of two parts of the network architecture, responsible for extracting spatially related features and creating a bottleneck layer.&lt;SEP&gt;The left part of the Encode-Decoder CNN architecture, depicted with a purple bracket, responsible for downsampling the input.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f41a0abc7626a2c56bb77667264c7785</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288960</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Decoder Section">
      <data key="d0">Decoder Section</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The decoder section is the second part of the network architecture, which uses information from the encoder to semantically project discriminative features.&lt;SEP&gt;The right part of the Encode-Decoder CNN architecture, indicated by a green bracket, responsible for upsampling and reconstructing the output.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f41a0abc7626a2c56bb77667264c7785</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288960</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bottleneck Layer">
      <data key="d0">Bottleneck Layer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A bottleneck layer is created by the encoder to compress useful information from a high-dimensional input into a 'latent representation'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285448</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Latent Representation">
      <data key="d0">Latent Representation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Latent representation is the compressed, most useful information from a high-dimensional input, created by the bottleneck layer.&lt;SEP&gt;The Latent Representation is the compressed feature space that bridges the Encoder and Decoder sections of the deep learning model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-640da4d848d6502c665bfc0ea66dce13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289264</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Field Solution">
      <data key="d0">Field Solution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The field solution refers to the predicted magnetic field of new unseen geometries.&lt;SEP&gt;The field solution is what the decoder section predicts by projecting discriminative features onto the original input space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e320c3afc0456b527186e2755a2ac4af</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285449</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Model Capacity">
      <data key="d0">Model Capacity</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Model capacity refers to the network's ability to capture the complexity of the problem, achieved by selecting appropriate layers, kernel size, kernel count, and regularization parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285450</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Model Selection">
      <data key="d0">Model Selection</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Model selection involves investigating candidate models to find the best balance between model fit and complexity using methods like random search and grid search.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285450</data>
    </node>
