    <node id="2-D Activation Map">
      <data key="d0">2-D Activation Map</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A 2-D activation map, also known as a feature map, is created by a convolutional filter sliding across the input, showing the filter's response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285456</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feature Map">
      <data key="d0">Feature Map</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A feature map, also known as a 2-D activation map, indicates the response of a convolutional filter at any position.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285456</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Output Volume of a CONV Layer">
      <data key="d0">Output Volume of a CONV Layer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The output volume of a CONV layer is formed when 2-D activation maps/feature maps are stacked together.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3b0d30beebae149e306398e8c2b1b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285457</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Computational Time">
      <data key="d0">Computational Time</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Computational time refers to the time required for calculations in an optimization process, which can be reduced by methods like dimensionality reduction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285457</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dimensionality Reduction">
      <data key="d0">Dimensionality Reduction</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A method used to decrease the number of random variables under consideration, which helped in cutting down computational time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285457</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topology Optimization (TO)">
      <data key="d0">Topology Optimization (TO)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Topology Optimization (TO) is an advanced computational design method used to find the optimal distribution of material within a given design space. It is a freeshape optimization technique that can outperform template-based design optimization routines. This method aims to create efficient topologies by distributing material while satisfying specific performance requirements and constraints, often addressing manufacturability issues.

TO is inherently associated with a discrete design domain, leading to discussions regarding its state representations, particularly for such domains. It is an application domain where an equation can be used for making sequential decisions about material distribution. In practice, Topology Optimization often leverages Machine Learning (ML) algorithms, such as Reinforcement Learning (RL) or Genetic Algorithms (GA), to determine the presence or absence of material in each cell of this discrete design domain, thereby finding optimal designs.

The method focuses on optimizing material distribution for a given set of loads and constraints, with single objective tasks frequently studied. TO has been applied to specific engineering problems, such as optimizing the structure of a C-core actuator to maximize the force experienced by its armature. Furthermore, it is also utilized for optimizing the design of structures and materials, including applications to electromagnetic devices, often incorporating deep reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8803108c64e008ff20b50a70b77f8af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a93df544481c46867db2b00e4b597341&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f237e58ddc202b94dc8ea5b3ef7e3145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3676b77d4e3057ccce502057364cbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289115</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Designs">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Designs</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Optimal designs are existing structural or functional configurations that serve as training data for supervised learning methods in Topology Optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285458</data>
