    </node>
    <node id="Manufacturability">
      <data key="d0">Manufacturability</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The ease or difficulty of producing a design, identified as an important criterion to include in TO with RL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reinforcement Learning (RL)">
      <data key="d0">Reinforcement Learning (RL)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Reinforcement Learning (RL) is a subfield of machine learning and artificial intelligence focused on how intelligent agents learn to make sequential decisions by performing actions within an environment. The overarching goal for these agents is to maximize the notion of cumulative reward through their interactions. This decision-making process is characterized by actions influencing later inputs.

RL involves agents learning through a system of rewards and penalties received from the environment. The concept of "Return" is fundamental to RL, serving as a key measurement in this learning process. While primarily concerned with agents maximizing cumulative rewards, RL is also an application domain where various equations are employed. For instance, the Mean Squared Error (MSE) equation can be situated within RL, framed as a supervised learning task to approximate optimal action-value functions. Furthermore, RL is the domain for applying equations specifically for implementing algorithms such as Deep Q-Networks (DQNs) or Q-learning, focusing on value estimation and agent learning. One application even involves using state representations for discrete design domains in Topology Optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58674fc78b8e0f9b48c3a573d49dd684&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="System Dynamics">
      <data key="d0">System Dynamics</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The interactive forces within a system that cause change over time, used in DRL in the form of an MDP.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285467</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MDP">
      <data key="d0">MDP</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">MDP, or Markov Decision Process, is a mathematical framework for modeling decision making in situations where outcomes are partly random and partly under the control of a decision maker.&lt;SEP&gt;MDP (Markov Decision Process) is a framework within which the force or average torque acts as a reward function.&lt;SEP&gt;Markov Decision Process, a mathematical framework used to model decision making in situations where outcomes are partly random and partly under the control of a decision maker.&lt;SEP&gt;Markov Decision Process, a framework for modeling decision making in situations where outcomes are partly random and partly under the control of a decision maker.&lt;SEP&gt;MDP, or Markov Decision Process, is a framework for modeling decision-making where outcomes are partly random and partly under the control of a decision maker. The discussed tree diagram demonstrates the complexity of its state-action space.&lt;SEP&gt;MDP stands for Markov Decision Process, a mathematical framework for modeling decision making in situations where outcomes are partly random and partly under the control of a decision maker.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ceb1ac6e54fec53c601d8b38f2b338d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288973</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Function Approximators">
      <data key="d0">Function Approximators</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Function approximators are neural networks used in Deep Reinforcement Learning to generalize to new geometries and excitations.&lt;SEP&gt;Function Approximators are models like neural networks, linear regressors, decision trees, and SVMs that implement the function $\\hat { v } ( s , w )$ to generalize knowledge across similar states.&lt;SEP&gt;Function Approximators are computational methods discussed in the surrounding text that relate to interpreting different states and their associated rewards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5f031035d088ffa5bcc3e370fe22c958&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289117</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FE Calls">
      <data key="d0">FE Calls</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">FE Calls, or Finite Element Calls, is a key performance metric used in optimization processes. It quantifies the computational cost or efficiency by indicating the number of simulations or analyses performed using a Finite Element solver. This metric essentially counts the number of finite element evaluations or iterations required by an optimization algorithm or for a particular scenario.

FE Calls serve as a measure of the computational effort involved, representing how many finite element analyses were executed. As a quantitative metric, it is often used to compare the performance and efficiency of different optimization methods, reflecting their computational burden. These calls are sometimes presented numerically, for example, as 105,000, and may be enclosed in brackets in tables.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a38c88cccaef550c4d3cde6f591d2fe5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a5c42907185fecc94ee85f1b33ec380&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2785b4e150ada39f3077e573325eeb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b984e4831b11f20f84bb8669cd7396b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c48c238aedc178e88e075043138eb72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11edf0b881aae28423c1b0d33893b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-081d5d2ff62504474fc8532dd817fb39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288780</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EM Solver">
      <data key="d0">EM Solver</data>
      <data key="d1">tool</data>
      <data key="d2">A tool or software capable of performing electromagnetic analysis and providing accurate solutions and performance measures.&lt;SEP&gt;An EM solver is discussed as a necessary component that provides an accurate solution and performance measures like force magnitude and field distribution for an optimization algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285468</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Force Magnitude">
      <data key="d0">Force Magnitude</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Force magnitude is a performance measure required for the operation of the optimization algorithm, provided by the EM solver.&lt;SEP&gt;Force magnitude refers to the strength of the force, measured in Newtons, which is a key metric for comparison across different optimization algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a38c88cccaef550c4d3cde6f591d2fe5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288777</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Material Placement">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Material Placement</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The ideal arrangement of materials to achieve desired performance, obtained using a greedy policy in Tabular QL.&lt;SEP&gt;Optimal material placement is achieved using a greedy policy obtained through the Tabular QL algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285469</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deng, Qin, And Lu 2020">
      <data key="d0">Deng, Qin, And Lu 2020</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Deng, Qin, and Lu 2020 refers to a research paper that criticizes supervised learning methods for topology optimization and proposes limiting the use of ML algorithms to performance estimation.&lt;SEP&gt;A research paper listed in Table 5.1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3676b77d4e3057ccce502057364cbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289128</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimization Algorithm">
      <data key="d0">Optimization Algorithm</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The optimization algorithm is discussed in the work, with requirements for an EM solver to provide accurate solutions and performance measures.&lt;SEP&gt;An optimization algorithm is a procedure used to find optimal solutions; traditionally, it's distinct from methods like dimensionality reduction and the role ML plays in TO.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285469</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surrogate DL Model">
      <data key="d0">Surrogate DL Model</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A surrogate DL model is a Deep Learning model that can provide highly accurate results within the design space of its training data but performs unreliably for unseen geometries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285469</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TO Problem">
      <data key="d0">TO Problem</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A TO problem refers to a Topology Optimization problem that may require new datasets and neural networks for changes in conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285470</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Zhang Et Al. 2019">
      <data key="d0">Zhang Et Al. 2019</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Zhang et al. 2019 refers to a research paper that discusses a Deep Learning method based on CNN and GANs for Topology Optimization with generalizability.&lt;SEP&gt;A research paper mentioned in the context of generalizability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3676b77d4e3057ccce502057364cbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289130</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DL Method Based On CNN And GANs">
      <data key="d0">DL Method Based On CNN And GANs</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A DL method based on CNN and GANs is a Deep Learning approach used by Zhang et al. 2019 for Topology Optimization with generalizability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285470</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Generalizability Ability">
      <data key="d0">Generalizability Ability</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Generalizability ability is the capacity of a model to perform accurately on new, unseen data or different boundary conditions, which Zhang et al. 2019 aimed to address.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285470</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sparse Boundary Conditions">
      <data key="d0">Sparse Boundary Conditions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Sparse boundary conditions refer to limited or insufficient data points defining the boundaries of a problem, which Zhang et al. 2019 avoided mapping directly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285471</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physical Fields">
      <data key="d0">Physical Fields</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Physical fields are concepts found by Neural Networks to learn more accurate mappings between boundary conditions and optimal structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285471</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Well-Designed Input Form">
      <data key="d0">Well-Designed Input Form</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A well-designed input form is the approach taken by Zhang et al. 2019 to enable a Deep Learning model to solve Topology Optimization problems with different boundary conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285471</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Learning Methodology">
      <data key="d0">Learning Methodology</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Learning methodology refers to the approach used for training a model; in Zhang et al. 2019, it was supervised, sharing common shortcomings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285471</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inequality Constraint">
      <data key="d0">Inequality Constraint</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An inequality constraint is a true limitation, distinct from how material (volume) constraints are treated in supervised learning due to loss function minimization.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical condition that bounds the total volume or proportion of air within the rotor's design space, defined by $K1 \leq \sum_{j=1}^{n} (p_j = 0) \leq K2$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TO Process">
      <data key="d0">TO Process</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The TO process refers to the steps involved in Topology Optimization, where ML algorithms may not be directly involved in generating new topologies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Generation Of New Topologies">
      <data key="d0">Generation Of New Topologies</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Generation of new topologies refers to the creation of new structural designs, a step where some ML approaches in TO are not directly involved.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Highly Imbalanced Dataset">
      <data key="d0">Highly Imbalanced Dataset</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A highly imbalanced dataset is a training dataset with a disproportionate number of certain types of data, leading to a network that overestimates performance for poor designs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285472</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Performance Indicator">
      <data key="d0">Performance Indicator</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A performance indicator is a metric that can be overestimated for a poor design due to a network trained with a highly imbalanced dataset.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285473</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TO For Electromagnetics">
      <data key="d0">TO For Electromagnetics</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">TO for Electromagnetics refers to Topology Optimization specifically applied in the field of electromagnetism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285473</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sasaki And Igarashi 2019">
      <data key="d0">Sasaki And Igarashi 2019</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Sasaki and Igarashi 2019 refers to a research paper that introduced Topology Optimization for Electromagnetic analysis using a CNN-based performance estimator.&lt;SEP&gt;A research paper listed in Table 5.1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3676b77d4e3057ccce502057364cbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289128</data>
