    <node id="DL Methods As A Filter">
      <data key="d0">DL Methods As A Filter</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">DL methods as a filter refers to the use of deep learning to remove inferior populations at iterations of the GA process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285474</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inferior Population">
      <data key="d0">Inferior Population</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Inferior population refers to less effective designs or solutions that are removed by DL methods during the GA process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285474</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Computation Burden">
      <data key="d0">Computation Burden</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Computation burden refers to the computational cost associated with FE analysis, which DL methods aim to reduce.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285475</data>
    </node>
    <node id="On-Line Fashion">
      <data key="d0">On-Line Fashion</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">On-line fashion is a method of implementing supervised learning, where data is processed sequentially, as seen in Doi, Sasaki, and Igarashi 2019.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285475</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Off-Line Fashion">
      <data key="d0">Off-Line Fashion</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Off-line fashion is a method of implementing supervised learning, where data is processed in batches, as seen in Sasaki and Igarashi 2019.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285476</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Manufacturability From Chapter 4">
      <data key="d0">Manufacturability From Chapter 4</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Manufacturability from Chapter 4 is an important criterion to be included from the initial phase of Topology Optimization with Reinforcement Learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285476</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Q-Learning Methodology">
      <data key="d0">Deep Q-Learning Methodology</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep Q-Learning Methodology is a modification of Q-learning discussed in Chapter 4, involving the use of neural networks as function approximators and changes to the MDP.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285476</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Learning From Tabular">
      <data key="d0">Q-Learning From Tabular</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Q-learning from Tabular refers to a Q-learning methodology described in Chapter 4 Section 4.5.3, which is modified into Deep Q-Learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285477</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FEM Solver">
      <data key="d0">FEM Solver</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A FE solver is used for electromagnetic analyses in the study, providing accurate solutions and performance measures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285477</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tabular QL Algorithm">
      <data key="d0">Tabular QL Algorithm</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Tabular QL algorithm is discussed as a method from Chapter 4, Section 4.5.3, used for optimal material placement and learning the value function for states and action pairs in a TO environment.&lt;SEP&gt;The Tabular QL algorithm is a Q-learning method discussed in Chapter 4, Section 4.5.3, which aims to obtain a greedy policy for optimal material placement.&lt;SEP&gt;An algorithm whose results are matched by the QL-based agent.&lt;SEP&gt;The Tabular QL algorithm is a specific implementation of Q-learning that uses a table to store Q-values for discrete states and actions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d519cd75c14207ceeaef0cf84051a1d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ceb1ac6e54fec53c601d8b38f2b338d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288973</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ground Truth (FE Results)">
      <data key="d0">Ground Truth (FE Results)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Ground Truth (FE Results) refers to the accurate results obtained from Finite Element simulations, used to verify predictions made by neural network models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-679159f51a7b3c245fdff9193fd1ef89</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Uncertainty Measure">
      <data key="d0">Uncertainty Measure</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An uncertainty measure quantifies the confidence level of a prediction, providing an indicator of how much a user should trust the prediction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-679159f51a7b3c245fdff9193fd1ef89</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MC Dropout">
      <data key="d0">MC Dropout</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">MC dropout is a technique used to quantify uncertainty in neural network predictions by retrieving multiple samples and calculating standard deviation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-679159f51a7b3c245fdff9193fd1ef89</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Error Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Error Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The error distribution between the prediction and ground truth, shown in Figure 2.13(a).&lt;SEP&gt;The error distribution visualizes the errors over an efficiency map, normalized for better clarity.&lt;SEP&gt;Error Distribution is represented by a false-color heat map, showing the discrepancy between ground truth and prediction values.&lt;SEP&gt;Error Distribution is a visual representation of the spread and magnitude of calculation errors across a specific domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb99d0c44b7c6ee269cad3cce40b7be7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92cc2b02ae3a119d92f0d1a7f6617885&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-679159f51a7b3c245fdff9193fd1ef89</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289109</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FE Simulations">
      <data key="d0">FE Simulations</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">FE Simulations are Finite Element simulations, used as ground truth and for generating training data for models.&lt;SEP&gt;FE Simulations (Finite Element Simulations) are a method that the models can act as a surrogate to, with high accuracy.&lt;SEP&gt;FE simulations are used to generate the desired performance map in the post-processing stage, with two DNNs acting as surrogates for the analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;FE Simulations (Finite Element Simulations) are performed in each optimization run, and their number is recorded for comparison of algorithms' efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;FE Simulations (Finite Element Simulations) are used to calculate performance maps of motors based on design parameters.&lt;SEP&gt;FE Simulations refer to the number of Finite Element (FE) simulations required to reach the optimal design, representing computational cost.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f6cebd0187b0c1924a3e6491e8ebe8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f237e58ddc202b94dc8ea5b3ef7e3145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-679159f51a7b3c245fdff9193fd1ef89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0d5c4a49fbb5bedfe6eb2f200271d99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289020</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Neural Network (DNN)">
      <data key="d0">Deep Neural Network (DNN)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Deep Neural Network (DNN) is a type of neural network that can be trained from scratch but requires substantial training data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-679159f51a7b3c245fdff9193fd1ef89</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285479</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transfer Learning (TL)">
      <data key="d0">Transfer Learning (TL)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Transfer learning is a concept used to reduce computational burden by leveraging knowledge from neural networks trained on similar domains for new tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;Transfer Learning is a machine learning technique that reduces the amount of training data needed and allows reuse of models, extending applicability.&lt;SEP&gt;Transfer Learning is a technique that can be used to improve performance, particularly on test cases, by leveraging knowledge from one domain to another.&lt;SEP&gt;Transfer Learning is a concept that specifically utilizes Stage 3 of the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Transfer Learning (TL) is a machine learning concept applied in the re-training of the A2C agent to improve computational efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c88cccaef550c4d3cde6f591d2fe5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f237e58ddc202b94dc8ea5b3ef7e3145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-679159f51a7b3c245fdff9193fd1ef89</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pan and Yang (2009)">
      <data key="d0">Pan and Yang (2009)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Pan and Yang (2009) is a reference cited for the concept of transfer learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-679159f51a7b3c245fdff9193fd1ef89</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285479</data>
    </node>
