    </node>
    <node id="GRU">
      <data key="d0">GRU</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">GRU, or Gated Recurrent Unit, is a gating mechanism in recurrent neural networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285529</data>
    </node>
    <node id="G_t">
      <data key="d0">G_t</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">G_t, in the context of reinforcement learning, represents the "return" at time step t. This return is defined as the total future reward an agent expects to accumulate, or the total accumulated reward, starting from time step t until the end of an episode. It specifically encompasses the total discounted reward from time step t onwards. G_t serves as an indicator of the desirability of a particular action sequence and can function as a target for Q-value estimation. Essentially, it is a variable or quantity at time t, signifying the discounted sum of all future rewards.&lt;SEP&gt;G_t represents a variable in the equation, signifying a value at time t.&lt;SEP&gt;G_t represents a variable, likely a return or cumulative reward, defined by the sum of future rewards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-86d0a1a94798eb7b1674502b0ae73cf4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b2d0224b72ce534931ab18bf45eae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-620c2ed72be858c05e569ae1452705a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6444ddfe2f3b1042c888bb109eee4098&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f7dc16108c864fd058d1379ed9f298</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293089</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IPM">
      <data key="d0">IPM</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">IPM, or Internal Permanent Magnet, refers to a type of motor where magnets are embedded within the rotor.&lt;SEP&gt;IPM stands for Internal Permanent Magnet, likely referring to a type of motor relevant to the Efficiency Map to Power Factor Map task.&lt;SEP&gt;IPM is a concept used in the title of Figure 3.22, indicating a domain or type of efficiency map.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2bea7680cdfeea039ec6cf21875ea0c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-248a0b72513cd485685d4b3e3864e01e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289303</data>
    </node>
    <node id="l.r">
      <data key="d0">l.r</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">l.r., or learning rate, is a hyperparameter in optimization algorithms that determines the step size at each iteration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285530</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LSTM">
      <data key="d0">LSTM</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">LSTM, or Long Short-Term Memory, is a type of recurrent neural network capable of learning long-term dependencies.&lt;SEP&gt;LSTM (Long short-term memory) is an improvement in algorithms that contribute to the success of deep learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1e3ef3a96efc33efd9047031ab15a09</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285530</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MC">
      <data key="d0">MC</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">MC, or Monte Carlo, refers to a broad class of computational algorithms that rely on repeated random sampling to obtain numerical results.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285530</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEC">
      <data key="d0">MEC</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">MEC, or Magnetic Equivalent Circuits, is a method for analyzing magnetic fields.&lt;SEP&gt;MEC, or Magnetic Equivalent Circuits, is a category of electromagnetic modeling techniques, depicted as one of three main branches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b33ad32faa20d67bef064cd7c0c2009c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288940</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ML">
      <data key="d0">ML</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">ML, or Machine Learning, is a scientific discipline concerned with the design and development of algorithms that allow computers to evolve behaviors based on empirical data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285530</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSE">
      <data key="d0">MSE</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">MSE, or Mean Squared Error, is a common metric used to measure the average of the squares of the errors.&lt;SEP&gt;MSE stands for Mean Squared Error, indicated by the formula provided as a metric to quantify error.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-feffff69e0f56312f2846d9f57d0a4f7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292889</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NL">
      <data key="d0">NL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">NL, or Non-Linear, describes systems or functions that are not linear.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285532</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PG">
      <data key="d0">PG</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">PG, or Policy Gradient, is a category of reinforcement learning algorithms that optimize the policy directly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285532</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q(s, a)">
      <data key="d0">Q(s, a)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Q(s, a) represents the action-value function in reinforcement learning, giving the expected return of taking action 'a' in state 's'.&lt;SEP&gt;Q(s, a) represents the agent's current estimated values, used in exploitation.&lt;SEP&gt;Q(s, a) is the action-value function, also known as the Q-function, which quantifies the expected cumulative future reward obtained by taking action 'a' in state 's' and then following a specific policy.&lt;SEP&gt;Q(s, a) represents the estimated action-value function, often approximated using neural networks.&lt;SEP&gt;Q(s, a) is the action-value function, or Q-value function, which quantifies the expected total discounted future reward of taking action 'a' in state 's' and subsequently following an optimal policy.&lt;SEP&gt;The Q-value function, which estimates the expected cumulative future reward for taking action $a$ in state $s$ and following an optimal policy thereafter.&lt;SEP&gt;Q(s, a) is likely a Q-function in reinforcement learning, representing the quality or expected return of taking action `a` in state `s`.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-808c974f7871ba6b7b5d18f72afc85a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1757cd493f052e1f9528255eb34ea59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58674fc78b8e0f9b48c3a573d49dd684&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3c16dd3211cf28f9ec77da01f786ee6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292746</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RNN">
      <data key="d0">RNN</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">RNN, or Recurrent Neural Network, is a class of artificial neural networks where connections between nodes form a directed graph along a temporal sequence.&lt;SEP&gt;RNN, or Recurrent Neural Networks, are mentioned as following the same N-layer terminology as CNNs.&lt;SEP&gt;A Recurrent Neural Network used in one variant of a sub-network, which takes design and material distribution information from a CNN as initiation for its hidden layer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76b69dc7913a240aef52b550d176029b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285533</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TD">
      <data key="d0">TD</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">TD, or Temporal Difference, is a class of model-free reinforcement learning methods which learn by bootstrapping from the estimated value of the next state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285533</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TL">
      <data key="d0">TL</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">TL, or Transfer Learning, is a machine learning method where a model developed for a task is reused as the starting point for a model on a second task.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285533</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VDC">
      <data key="d0">VDC</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">VDC, or V-design cycle, is a product development procedure encompassing design, optimization, manufacturing, and testing stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285533</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V(s)">
      <data key="d0">V(s)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">V(s) represents the state value function in reinforcement learning, giving the expected return from state 's'.&lt;SEP&gt;V(s) represents the state's Value function, which indicates the expected return from state 's'.&lt;SEP&gt;The state-value function evaluated at the current state $s$, representing the expected cumulative discounted future reward from state $s$ onwards under the current policy.&lt;SEP&gt;V(s) represents the value of the current state (s), indicating the expected cumulative reward from that state onwards.&lt;SEP&gt;V(s) represents the state-value function, which signifies the expected return of being in state 's' and subsequently following a particular policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e90fd88a3c123b4352d139141e81b16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dae820ad2360b2e0540cedd11e75bb15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimization">
      <data key="d0">Optimization</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A process of making something as effective as possible, crucial in electric machine design.&lt;SEP&gt;Optimization is the process of iteratively adjusting system parameters to maximize an objective function, such as the performance measure $J$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288856</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electric Motor Design">
      <data key="d0">Electric Motor Design</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A rigorous, knowledge-based, and time-consuming procedure for developing electric motors.&lt;SEP&gt;Electric Motor Design is a field where the heatmaps serve as visual aids to illustrate performance and reliability of models or simulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Electric Motor Design involves defining the structural and electrical characteristics of motors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c85d4390c7f5cda3bb7e9cc87cbcec0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f6cebd0187b0c1924a3e6491e8ebe8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289225</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure">
      <data key="d0">Figure</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to an illustration showing the overview of the proposed automated design and manufacturing process for electric motor drives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a87e071d0b56bc121b18a65c5875d0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285535</data>
    </node>
