    </node>
    <node id="Boundary Based Methods">
      <data key="d0">Boundary Based Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Boundary based methods are a widely adopted approach to topology optimization, especially in recent decades, developed by Osher and Sethian in 1988, rooted in shape optimization techniques and based on an implicit function that defines the structural boundary.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285538</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Density Based Methods">
      <data key="d0">Density Based Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Density based methods are a type of topology optimization approach where the design domain is parameterized in an explicit function. These methods can be seen as translating the discrete problem of material distribution to a continuous form.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285538</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Level-Set Method">
      <data key="d0">Level-Set Method</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The level-set method is a boundary based method for topology optimization where obtained boundaries are represented by a discretized and unsmooth mesh unless alternative techniques are applied. It can generate an irregular and toothed optimized topology and is dependent on the initial state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285538</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase-Field Methods">
      <data key="d0">Phase-Field Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase-Field Methods are another technique used in conjunction with level-set methods within boundary variation methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285538</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gradient-Based Optimization Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Gradient-Based Optimization Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Gradient-based optimization algorithms are enabled by translating the discrete problem of material distribution to a continuous form, and are generally faster than stochastic optimization based discrete methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285538</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stochastic Optimization Based Discrete Methods">
      <data key="d0">Stochastic Optimization Based Discrete Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Stochastic optimization based discrete methods, such as Evolutionary Algorithms, offer better optimization capability, stronger robustness, and parallel searching capability for discrete material distribution problems, despite being slower than gradient-based approaches and having high computation demand.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285539</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Campelo">
      <data key="d0">Campelo</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Campelo is an author cited in 2008 and 2010 for research on filtering and smoothing methods to overcome issues in discrete methods for topology optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285540</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Watanabe">
      <data key="d0">Watanabe</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Watanabe is a researcher cited alongside Campelo and Igarashi for work on filtering and smoothing techniques (2008a, 2008b, 2014).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285540</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Igarashi">
      <data key="d0">Igarashi</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Igarashi is a researcher cited alongside Campelo and Watanabe for work on filtering and smoothing techniques (2008a, 2008b, 2010, 2014).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285540</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dupré">
      <data key="d0">Dupré</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Dupré is an author cited in 2014 for research on adding smoothness as an objective of the optimization process in discrete methods for topology optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285540</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ramirez">
      <data key="d0">Ramirez</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Ramirez is a researcher cited alongside Campelo and Igarashi in 2010 for work on filtering clusters and smoothing techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285540</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sato">
      <data key="d0">Sato</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Sato is a researcher cited alongside Watanabe and Igarashi in 2014 for work on filtering clusters and smoothing techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285540</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Proposed TO Methodology">
      <data key="d0">Proposed TO Methodology</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The proposed TO methodology leverages a sequence-based environment to impose connectivity on cells with the same material, ensuring the final optimized solution does not have checkerboard patterns, perforations, or floating pieces.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285540</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sequence-Based environment">
      <data key="d0">Sequence-Based environment</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A sequence-based environment is leveraged by the proposed TO methodology to impose connectivity on cells containing the same material.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285540</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heuristic Tree Search-Based Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Heuristic Tree Search-Based Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Heuristic tree search-based algorithms, such as Reinforcement Learning, are facilitated by the proposed new TO method.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285542</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-Core Actuator">
      <data key="d0">C-Core Actuator</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A C-core actuator is an electromagnetic device used in various applications, consisting of a coil, a magnetic core, and an armature. It is used as a specific example to prove the validity of a method.&lt;SEP&gt;A C-Core actuator is a test problem used to prove the validity of a new topology optimization method.&lt;SEP&gt;The C-core actuator is a specific type of EM actuator whose dimensions are chosen based on examples from scientific papers.&lt;SEP&gt;A C-core actuator is a device whose design and simulation setup are illustrated to explore topology optimization.&lt;SEP&gt;The C-core actuator is a device whose optimal geometry and magnetic flux density distribution are being analyzed.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific type of electromagnetic device for which topology optimization is being applied.&lt;SEP&gt;An electromagnetic device that is the subject of optimization through topology optimization using deep reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51ad9a81904dfb8134371112a0be9fa9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289155</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rotor of a Synchronous Reluctance Motor">
      <data key="d0">Rotor of a Synchronous Reluctance Motor</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The rotor of a Synchronous Reluctance Motor is a test problem used to validate the proposed TO methodology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285542</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sequence Based Controller">
      <data key="d0">Sequence Based Controller</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Sequence Based Controller is a method, with its operation described.&lt;SEP&gt;The sequence based controller is a proposed method.&lt;SEP&gt;A group of cells in the discretized design space that serves as a pointer and moves within the design space, leaving a trail of material. It is characterized by its size and associated material.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51ad9a81904dfb8134371112a0be9fa9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285543</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deaton">
      <data key="d0">Deaton</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Deaton is a researcher cited in 2014 regarding the implicit function definition of structural boundaries in boundary-based methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285543</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grandhi">
      <data key="d0">Grandhi</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Grandhi is a researcher cited in 2014 regarding the implicit function definition of structural boundaries in boundary-based methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285543</data>
    </node>
    <node id="A. Dupr´e">
      <data key="d0">A. Dupr´e</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A. Dupr´e is a researcher cited in 2014 regarding adding solution smoothness as an optimization objective to overcome drawbacks in discrete methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285543</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Boundary Based TO">
      <data key="d0">Boundary Based TO</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Boundary Based TO is a technique used in Topology Optimization.&lt;SEP&gt;Boundary Based TO is a topology optimization approach that uses an implicit function to define structural boundaries, developed by Osher and Sethian in 1988.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285543</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Filtering Techniques">
      <data key="d0">Filtering Techniques</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Filtering Techniques are methods employed to overcome the drawbacks of discrete optimization methods, such as floating pieces and checkerboard patterns, but can be unreliable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285543</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Smoothing Methods">
      <data key="d0">Smoothing Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Smoothing Methods are techniques used to address issues like floating pieces and checkerboard patterns in optimal solutions from discrete optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285544</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sequence-Based Environment">
      <data key="d0">Sequence-Based Environment</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A sequence-based environment is leveraged in a new topology optimization methodology to impose connectivity on cells, aiming to prevent checkerboard patterns and perforations.&lt;SEP&gt;An environment used for topology optimization discussed in a 2020 paper.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e2e27a9a79daf5a813690efa01766eca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285545</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rotor Of A Synchronous Reluctance Motor">
      <data key="d0">Rotor Of A Synchronous Reluctance Motor</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A rotor of a Synchronous Reluctance Motor is a test problem used to prove the validity of a new topology optimization method.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285545</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2D Curve">
      <data key="d0">2D Curve</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A 2D curve is represented by an intersection between a plane and a surface in boundary based methods, specific to Figure 4.7.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285545</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Zero Level Set">
      <data key="d0">Zero Level Set</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Zero Level Set of a function $\Phi$ represents the tracked boundary in boundary-based topology optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285545</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Level Set Function">
      <data key="d0">Level Set Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Level Set Function $(\Phi)$ directly determines the material distribution in the design domain in boundary-based methods.&lt;SEP&gt;The Level Set Function, denoted as $\Phi$, is an implicit function of spatial coordinates and potentially time, determining whether a point belongs to a solid or void region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d40236fe707c1436e66df73317598df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288984</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FE Solution">
      <data key="d0">FE Solution</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A finite element solution that provides a mesh structure with numerical values for local quantities.&lt;SEP&gt;FE Solution refers to the Finite Element solution, which can involve more than 10,000 evaluations in generating an optimal TO design for an electric machine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285545</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topology Optimization Problems">
      <data key="d0">Topology Optimization Problems</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Topology Optimization Problems are design challenges that various methods attempt to solve, often requiring specific parameter tuning for optimal results.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285545</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discrete Problem Of Material Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Discrete Problem Of Material Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Discrete Problem Of Material Distribution is translated into a continuous form by density-based methods to enable gradient-based optimization algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe80fce29c226462c0b0c6baffec8feb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285545</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Linear Model">
      <data key="d0">Linear Model</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Linear models are difficult to train as the number of features increases and their interactions become complicated.&lt;SEP&gt;A Linear Model is a simpler statistical model that can become difficult to train as the number of features in data increases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b1e3ef3a96efc33efd9047031ab15a09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e320c3afc0456b527186e2755a2ac4af</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285547</data>
