    </node>
    <node id="Airbox">
      <data key="d0">Airbox</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">An airbox with fixed dimensions (height = 160mm, width = 160mm) contains the coil in the problem description.&lt;SEP&gt;An airbox is the container in which the copper coil is located.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e320c3afc0456b527186e2755a2ac4af</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285555</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 4 Section 4.5.3">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 4 Section 4.5.3</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">This refers to a specific section in Chapter 4 where the Tabular QL algorithm is discussed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285555</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-value">
      <data key="d0">Q-value</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The Q-value, or value function, is learned for state and action pairs in a TO environment.&lt;SEP&gt;The Q-value is equivalent to the Action-Value Function, $Q(s, a)$, representing the expected future discounted cumulative reward (return $G_t$) from a given state-action pair under a policy.&lt;SEP&gt;The Q-value, denoted as $Q_\pi(s_t, a_t)$, is the estimated action-value function for a given state-action pair, representing the expected cumulative discounted future rewards.&lt;SEP&gt;The Q-value for a state-action pair $(s, a)$, represented as $Q(s, a) = r(s, a) + \gamma V(s')$, estimates the expected return of taking action $a$ in state $s$.&lt;SEP&gt;Q-value, represented as $Q_t(s,a)$, is a measure of the expected utility of taking a given action 'a' in a given state 's' at time 't'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0604f5005a60d070243b26410ba10b20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293112</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TO Environment (SeqTo-v1)">
      <data key="d0">TO Environment (SeqTo-v1)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Topography Optimization environment, specifically SeqTo-v1, which involves learning Q-values for states and actions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285555</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Curse of Dimensionality">
      <data key="d0">Curse of Dimensionality</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The curse of dimensionality is a problem that limits the tabular QL approach from expanding to high dimensional state and action spaces, especially with continuous states or actions.&lt;SEP&gt;The curse of dimensionality refers to various phenomena that arise when analyzing and organizing data in high-dimensional spaces, making tabular methods impractical for large or continuous state-action spaces.&lt;SEP&gt;The Curse of Dimensionality is a challenge in Reinforcement Learning that makes tabular methods infeasible for large state/action spaces, directly addressed by the function approximation equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2ceb1ac6e54fec53c601d8b38f2b338d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288974</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Function Approximation">
      <data key="d0">Function Approximation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A method introduced in this chapter to approximate action values when the tabular method is infeasible or too memory intensive.&lt;SEP&gt;Function approximation is used in Q-learning, leveraging a neural network to estimate the true, unknown action-value function instead of a tabular method.&lt;SEP&gt;Function Approximation is a method used in reinforcement learning, often employing neural networks, to estimate action-value functions when lookup tables are impractical.&lt;SEP&gt;Techniques like neural networks used to estimate complex functions, such as the Q-function, especially in environments with large state spaces.&lt;SEP&gt;Function Approximation is the method of using a neural network to estimate the action-value function, allowing the method to scale to large problems.&lt;SEP&gt;Function approximation is a technique, often using neural networks, to estimate complex functions like the Q-function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288923</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Linear Regressors">
      <data key="d0">Linear Regressors</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Linear Regressors are a class of parametrized functions used to approximate value functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Linear Regressors are a type of function approximator used to implement $\\hat { v } ( s , w )$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289441</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Decision Trees">
      <data key="d0">Decision Trees</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Decision Trees are a class of parametrized functions used to approximate value functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Decision Trees are a type of function approximator used to implement $\\hat { v } ( s , w )$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289441</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SVMs">
      <data key="d0">SVMs</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">SVMs (Support Vector Machines) are a class of parametrized functions used to approximate value functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285557</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action Value Function Predictor">
      <data key="d0">Action Value Function Predictor</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An Action Value Function Predictor, denoted as $\hat{Q}(s, a, w)$, approximates the true action-value function and is better than the tabular approach for large state and action spaces.&lt;SEP&gt;The Action Value Function Predictor ($\hat { Q } ( s , a , w )$) is another function approximated using parameterized functions, related to the state-value function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289441</data>
    </node>
    <node id="True Action-Value Function">
      <data key="d0">True Action-Value Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The True Action-Value Function, denoted as $Q_{\pi}(s, a, w)$, is approximated by the Action Value Function Predictor.&lt;SEP&gt;The true action-value function, $Q_{\pi}(s, a)$, represents the expected return when starting in state $s$, taking action $a$, and thereafter following policy $\pi$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289295</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Integer-Based Encoding">
      <data key="d0">Integer-Based Encoding</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An integer-based encoding of the design domain, as shown in Figure 5.9, is suitable for the tabular approach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.9">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.9</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.9 illustrates an integer-based encoding of the design domain useful for the tabular approach.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5.9 is an image titled "Discrete design domain as the state space" illustrating a design domain for an electromagnetic device.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec0d779e474774183c36eade5f33db6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288891</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.10">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.10</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A figure illustrating a richer encoding technique, which might include magnetic field distribution in the air gap.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5.10 is a visual representation displayed in the document, depicting a discrete design domain with the $| B | _ { a i r g a p }$ as the state space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5eebe68238bf8a33757041bc7dd3ffe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289387</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.11">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.11</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A figure illustrating a richer encoding technique, which might include the whole geometry.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5.11 is a two-part diagram illustrating a design domain and its corresponding magnetic field distribution, which serves to demonstrate an advanced state representation method for the SeqTO-v2 environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0534c4408a52c608a5a24ccf9951f5f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288886</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Floating-Point Data Type">
      <data key="d0">Floating-Point Data Type</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The data type needed to represent continuous values like magnetic field distribution, which poses memory challenges for tabular approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Linear Function Approximator">
      <data key="d0">Linear Function Approximator</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The simplest form of function approximator, typically involving a linear representation of constituent weights for the Q-value.&lt;SEP&gt;A Linear Function Approximator is a model that approximates a function using a linear combination of features, here specifically mimicking a tabular representation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-70c2a865bc92e157d3426a926d7ddd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289305</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Table 5.2">
      <data key="d0">Table 5.2</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Table 5.2 likely contains data related to Q-values and constituent weights for linear function approximators.&lt;SEP&gt;A table where each entry represents a distinct state-action pair in tabular methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 5.2, titled 'Example of a Q-table', illustrates a tabular Q-learning approach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ceb1ac6e54fec53c601d8b38f2b338d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288975</data>
    </node>
    <node id="One-Hot Encoding">
      <data key="d0">One-Hot Encoding</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A method used for $x(s_i, a_j)$ as described in G´eron (2019), which produces a vector with a single non-zero element.&lt;SEP&gt;One-hot encoding is a method used to represent categorical data as binary vectors, where only one element is non-zero, corresponding to the highest possible value for each row of the Q-table, indicating the optimal action.&lt;SEP&gt;One-Hot Encoding is a specific sparse representation of categorical data where a feature vector has a '1' at the index corresponding to a specific state-action pair and '0's elsewhere.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2ceb1ac6e54fec53c601d8b38f2b338d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70c2a865bc92e157d3426a926d7ddd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288972</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gérôn (2019)">
      <data key="d0">Gérôn (2019)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The author of a work referenced for the definition of one-hot encoding.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285560</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action Space">
      <data key="d0">Action Space</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Action Space is defined as the set of all possible actions that an agent can undertake within a particular environment or a given state. This space encompasses every available action for the agent. For example, in the SeqTO-v2 environment, the action space for the controller is specifically expanded to include eight distinct actions, such as 'Right', 'Soft Right', 'Soft Down', and 'Down'. The Action Space can be formally represented as a set, such as $(a_1, \ldots a_k)$, containing all permissible actions. A challenge associated with a complex action space is the "curse of dimensionality," which arises when the range of possible actions becomes excessively large.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-596bd8f0cbcfc00de61fa772b9317edc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1cc3c9a8202b9032fe06ba473d7c76d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0604f5005a60d070243b26410ba10b20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293115</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State">
      <data key="d0">State</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A "State," frequently denoted as $s$, $s_t$, $s_i$, or $\boldsymbol{s}$, represents the current situation or configuration of an agent within an environment at a specific moment. This concept is fundamental in reinforcement learning and is a key entity in a Markov Decision Process (MDP). A state encapsulates all relevant information about the environment, serving as a comprehensive description of the conditions an agent encounters.

The state provides crucial details such as material distribution, which enables a controller or agent to make informed decisions that influence subsequent actions and rewards. States can be either discrete (e.g., S0, S1, S2, or numerical values like 80, 81, 82) or continuous, and they can encompass complex or high-dimensional information, including magnetic field distribution or geometry. For example, in topology optimization for a C-core actuator, the state might encode details about optimal design or excitation conditions. In essence, a state can be understood as a vector or observation that describes the current situation.

Furthermore, the state $s_t$ or $S_t$ at time $t$ is frequently used as an input to the policy function, guiding the agent's decision-making process, and is a component of the Q-function, often seen with $S_{t+1}$ referring to the state at time $t+1$. The variable 's' or 'st' specifically refers to a state within a sequential decision-making process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66f8a9943508aa952a9845858b8d3374&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8823695e17798553515e243bdbfda2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-742edca401668538e3a7a9b93ec14162&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d0e0689a5d95559de772960193f788e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-feffff69e0f56312f2846d9f57d0a4f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-808c974f7871ba6b7b5d18f72afc85a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70c2a865bc92e157d3426a926d7ddd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e5324f5a16fd6822c647598c8e749b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca0b752852f034cec7766c7e6e92af67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6444ddfe2f3b1042c888bb109eee4098&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39c26f40d41eeedc15f07ee76f39c83c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4bc9886b92f8e393e3ab3d494b9db33c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293091</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced Function Approximators">
      <data key="d0">Advanced Function Approximators</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Sophisticated methods used to approximate functions, often in machine learning contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;This section focuses on methods to approximate action values with a relatively small number of parameters, building on the Tabular QL algorithm.&lt;SEP&gt;Advanced Function Approximators are techniques used in reinforcement learning to generalize across states and actions, addressing the limitations of tabular methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2ceb1ac6e54fec53c601d8b38f2b338d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288974</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TO Environment">
      <data key="d0">TO Environment</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A TO (Topology Optimization) environment, specifically SeqTo-v1, is where the value function for states and action pairs is learned using the Tabular QL approach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285560</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SeqTo-v1">
      <data key="d0">SeqTo-v1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">SeqTo-v1 is a specific type of TO environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285560</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High Dimensional State and Action Space">
      <data key="d0">High Dimensional State and Action Space</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A challenge that the tabular QL approach cannot expand to, due to the curse of dimensionality, where computing and preserving the value function for all possible state-action pairs becomes infeasible.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285560</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State-Action Pairs">
      <data key="d0">State-Action Pairs</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Pairs of states and actions for which the value function (Q-value) needs to be computed and preserved.&lt;SEP&gt;Combinations of a state and an action, which an approximator might not perform equally well on.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285562</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Memory Intensive">
      <data key="d0">Memory Intensive</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A characteristic describing the tabular method when applied to complex action spaces, making it impractical.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285562</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action Values">
      <data key="d0">Action Values</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Values that are approximated by function approximators.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285563</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parametrized Functions">
      <data key="d0">Parametrized Functions</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A popular category of function approximators where real-valued weights are adjusted to learn a representation from the state space to action values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285563</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Real-Valued Weights">
      <data key="d0">Real-Valued Weights</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Adjustable parameters in parametrized functions used to learn a representation from the state space to action values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285563</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Richer Encoding Technique">
      <data key="d0">Richer Encoding Technique</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Encoding techniques, such as those shown in Figure 5.10 or 5.11, that the tabular approach is not appropriate for.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285563</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Air Gap">
      <data key="d0">Air Gap</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Air Gap refers to a crucial physical space, typically characterized by the absence of magnetic material, that significantly influences magnetic field distribution and operation within various electromagnetic systems. This region can be specifically where magnetic field distribution, represented as |B|, is measured or observed, often appearing as a designated area like a light blue vertical bar labeled "|B| Air Gap" in diagrams.

In specific electromechanical contexts, the Air Gap is defined as the thin space separating key components. For instance, it is the space between the stator and the rotor in rotating machinery. In the case of a C-core solenoid, the Air Gap is the region between the rightmost vertical arm of the Core and the Armature, or between the C-core's vertical leg and the armature, often specified as 1 mm at its narrowest point. It can also be described as the region between the end of an L-shaped arm and a horizontal base where a strong magnetic field is observed. This gap plays a vital role in force generation and overall magnetic properties, allowing for the placement of thin strips of iron or serving as a boundary condition for magnetic field analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff320ec3e3a24e3c69d31630b6cba885&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8330ecbeee1c4ebe7ea64bfd7fbb45b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca13e3ef7d4bf929b84d6b30574e3485&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b984e4831b11f20f84bb8669cd7396b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c48c238aedc178e88e075043138eb72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d94fd82540e01e83a3a7b0febcf3d7ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288866</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Whole Geometry">
      <data key="d0">Whole Geometry</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A representation of an entire design, as illustrated in Figure 5.11.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285563</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Precision Trade-Off">
      <data key="d0">Precision Trade-Off</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The result of attempting to discretize continuous magnetic field values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285563</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parametrized Approximator">
      <data key="d0">Parametrized Approximator</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An ideal method for representing a state of the design domain that includes magnetic field distribution, offering flexibility for similar problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parameter Fitting">
      <data key="d0">Parameter Fitting</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A technique used by parametrized approximators for representing the design domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Interpolation">
      <data key="d0">Interpolation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A technique used by parametrized approximators for representing the design domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Value Function Table">
      <data key="d0">Value Function Table</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A data structure where modifying a single entity does not change values associated with other states or state-action pairs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285566</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Linear Function">
      <data key="d0">Linear Function</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The simplest form of function approximator, representing weights linearly for the Q-value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285566</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weights">
      <data key="d0">Weights</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Weights are fundamental parameters within a neural network, often denoted as $w$ or $\boldsymbol{\theta}$. They are real-valued variables that are adjusted during the learning process to tune the output of a parameterized function and minimize error. In the context of a neural network, weights are controllable variables that are part of the loss function.

These weights serve as constituent components of a linear function, where they are multiplied with features to produce a scalar value. In deep neural networks, particularly Bayesian contexts, convolutional weights are often considered random variables. When applied to policy gradient methods, weights are parameters associated with the neural network of the policy that undergo optimization. They can also represent the parameters of the estimated action-value function, which are adjusted during learning. Ultimately, weights are the crucial parameters that the learning algorithm seeks to optimize within a neural network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb282e0735521b8981f08f0cfd47613&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289191</data>
    </node>
