      <data key="d0">High-Performance Computing</data>
      <data key="d1">technology</data>
      <data key="d2">High-performance computing is used for tasks like deep learning to facilitate complex calculations and analyses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-610e0748724855c847b8fd20690e22db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285610</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prototype">
      <data key="d0">Prototype</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A manufactured model whose performance is experimentally verified in the product development process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-610e0748724855c847b8fd20690e22db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285611</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Product Development">
      <data key="d0">Product Development</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A procedure involving design, optimization, manufacturing, and testing, often following a V-design cycle.&lt;SEP&gt;Product Development refers to the overall process of creating and launching new products, in this case, electric motors, guided by methodologies like the V-Design and Manufacturing Cycle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-610e0748724855c847b8fd20690e22db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0af18e12848c44ab0d9b01471789474</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288734</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Equivalent Circuit">
      <data key="d0">Equivalent Circuit</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An equivalent circuit, or lumped parameter approach, is a method for designing electrical machines that provides fast predictions of global performance parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-610e0748724855c847b8fd20690e22db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285611</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Data Mining Tools">
      <data key="d0">Data Mining Tools</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Tools considered promising for keeping up with the demand for fast and accurate analysis of electric designs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-610e0748724855c847b8fd20690e22db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285611</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intelligent Generative Design">
      <data key="d0">Intelligent Generative Design</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Intelligent generative design, such as topology optimization, is used for flexible and complex shape design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-610e0748724855c847b8fd20690e22db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285611</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thesis">
      <data key="d0">Thesis</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A document organized into six chapters, discussing novel ideas for the design and analysis of electric devices and machines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-610e0748724855c847b8fd20690e22db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285611</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Convolutional Neural Networks">
      <data key="d0">Convolutional Neural Networks</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific architecture of neural networks suitable for certain types of data or problems.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) are efficient for handling many input-many output design and modeling problems.&lt;SEP&gt;Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) are a type of deep learning method used for efficiently handling complex design and modeling problems.&lt;SEP&gt;Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN/ConvNets) are a type of deep network, with the first successful application developed by Yann LeCun in the 1990s.&lt;SEP&gt;Convolutional Neural Networks (ConvNets or CNN) are a type of neural network with learnable weights and biases, suitable for structured input data.&lt;SEP&gt;Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) are a type of artificial neural network relevant to the context of convolution kernel visualization.&lt;SEP&gt;Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) are a type of neural network that utilizes convolutional filters for image and data processing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-efb0bf44fcf7466c35982fd3bb8ae7ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1e3ef3a96efc33efd9047031ab15a09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a4b902f2347e5fea9d43e606ce167ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d59e50443efd19bae755d63382e315bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-610e0748724855c847b8fd20690e22db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289016</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recurrent Neural Networks">
      <data key="d0">Recurrent Neural Networks</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) are efficient for handling many input-many output design and modeling problems.&lt;SEP&gt;Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) are a type of deep learning method used for efficiently handling complex design and modeling problems, especially with variable numbers of inputs and outputs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-610e0748724855c847b8fd20690e22db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285611</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Flux Density">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Flux Density</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A local quantity of an electric motor that requires hundreds or thousands of numerical values to be predicted from a mesh structure.&lt;SEP&gt;A local quantity of an electric motor that needs to be predicted, typically involving hundreds or thousands of numerical values.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical quantity whose distribution is plotted and analyzed for optimal geometry in specific excitation scenarios.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic Flux Density, denoted as |B|, is visualized using a color gradient and lines of force, representing the strength and direction of the magnetic field.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic Flux Density is a measure of the strength of the magnetic field in a region, visualized in Panel (b) using a color gradient.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic flux density is a measure of the strength of a magnetic field, represented by contour plots in the image.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic Flux Density, or B-distribution, is visually represented by color bars and plots, showing the strength of the magnetic field.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b76c5d19e7bcbbd5f11b71588e066f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b984e4831b11f20f84bb8669cd7396b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b56c95146eb3d0e8591ffefd6ab679ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-610e0748724855c847b8fd20690e22db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-081d5d2ff62504474fc8532dd817fb39</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289028</data>
    </node>
