    <node id="Section 4.3.1">
      <data key="d0">Section 4.3.1</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The section detailing the operation of the controller.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51ad9a81904dfb8134371112a0be9fa9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285627</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Section 4.4">
      <data key="d0">Section 4.4</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The section discussing Topology Optimization with SeqTO-v1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51ad9a81904dfb8134371112a0be9fa9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285627</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Section 4.4.1">
      <data key="d0">Section 4.4.1</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The section discussing Genetic Algorithm.&lt;SEP&gt;Section 4.4.1 details a prior method or information referenced for comparison and context regarding optimization objectives and GA algorithm settings.&lt;SEP&gt;Section 4.4.1 is a part of the surrounding text that describes the general settings for the Genetic Algorithm, including a 'stall generations set to 100' and 'population size of 50'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f5a390cc7143789eccd25f5f3623694&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51ad9a81904dfb8134371112a0be9fa9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289360</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Section 5.4">
      <data key="d0">Section 5.4</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A future section in Chapter 5 where SeqTO-vZ will be introduced.&lt;SEP&gt;Section 5.4 will discuss modifications to the current formulation of SeqTO-v1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51ad9a81904dfb8134371112a0be9fa9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285627</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electromagnetic Devices">
      <data key="d0">Electromagnetic Devices</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The thesis explores design and analysis for these devices using statistical learning methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Electromagnetic devices are the subjects for which field distribution is predicted using Deep Learning.&lt;SEP&gt;Devices that operate based on electromagnetic principles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-403239ec5550f149ced3802b6c18600b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285627</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Network Input">
      <data key="d0">Network Input</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The network input is formatted as a uniform structured grid containing geometric, material, and excitation information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285628</data>
    </node>
    <node id="M19 Silicon Steel Material">
      <data key="d0">M19 Silicon Steel Material</data>
      <data key="d1">material</data>
      <data key="d2">M19 silicon steel material is used for the core of the transformer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285629</data>
    </node>
    <node id="M19">
      <data key="d0">M19</data>
      <data key="d1">material</data>
      <data key="d2">M19 is a material used in the IPM motor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285629</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Permanent Magnets">
      <data key="d0">Permanent Magnets</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Permanent magnets, specifically NdFeBr, are incorporated into the IPM motor.&lt;SEP&gt;Permanent magnets are internal components of an IPM motor rotor, depicted in red/blue in the embedded cutaway diagrams.&lt;SEP&gt;Permanent magnets are components within the motor, represented by rectangular blocks colored in alternating red and blue, positioned between the inner grey rotor core and the outer grey stator material.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnets that retain their magnetic properties without an external power source, colored red and blue in the rotor of the motor cross-section to indicate poles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f476972defaf212918b90b259aa69f03</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288739</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NdFeBr">
      <data key="d0">NdFeBr</data>
      <data key="d1">material</data>
      <data key="d2">NdFeBr is the material used for the permanent magnets in the IPM motor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285629</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ML Network">
      <data key="d0">ML Network</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An ML network trained in a supervised manner can predict solutions for arbitrary inputs but lacks physics-based judgment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285629</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neurons">
      <data key="d0">Neurons</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Neurons are the basic units of Neural Networks, connected in an acyclic graph.&lt;SEP&gt;Neurons are collections of connected units in a Neural Network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca632ae39508b7c6fdf1ad04973857b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289258</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acyclic Graph">
      <data key="d0">Acyclic Graph</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An acyclic graph describes the connection structure of neurons in Neural Networks, where cycles are not allowed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285629</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fully Connected Layer">
      <data key="d0">Fully Connected Layer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A fully connected layer in a Neural Network allows outputs of neurons to become inputs to other neurons through pairwise connections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285629</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Infinite Loop">
      <data key="d0">Infinite Loop</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Infinite loops are avoided in neural networks by using acyclic graphs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285631</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hidden Layer">
      <data key="d0">Hidden Layer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A hidden layer in a Neural Network contains neurons that do not share any connections with each other within the same layer.&lt;SEP&gt;A Hidden Layer is an intermediate layer in a neural network that performs computations and passes results to the next layer without being directly exposed to input or output data.&lt;SEP&gt;The Hidden Layer is an intermediate component of the Feedforward Neural Network, located between the Input Layer and the Output Layer. It consists of 'm' hidden units (`H1, H2, Hm-1, Hm`) and processes information from the input layer through weighted connections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca632ae39508b7c6fdf1ad04973857b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb622c9ce0600f3539c3ed7199e0c0ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Artificial Neural Network">
      <data key="d0">Artificial Neural Network</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An Artificial Neural Network (ANN) is a type of neural network, an example of which is shown with two hidden layers.&lt;SEP&gt;An artificial neural network is a type of algorithm where the number of hidden units has doubled every 2-3 years due to advancements in computational technologies and DL algorithms.&lt;SEP&gt;An Artificial Neural Network (ANN) is a computational model inspired by the structure and function of biological neural networks, consisting of interconnected processing units called neurons.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca632ae39508b7c6fdf1ad04973857b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1e3ef3a96efc33efd9047031ab15a09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289258</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Activation Function">
      <data key="d0">Activation Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An activation function, or non-linearity, performs a specific fixed mathematical operation on a unique value and is described in literature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285632</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Output Layer Neurons">
      <data key="d0">Output Layer Neurons</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Output layer neurons in a Neural Network commonly do not have an activation function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285632</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parameters">
      <data key="d0">Parameters</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Parameters, specifically weights and biases, are part of the network and are updated using backpropagation.&lt;SEP&gt;Parameters ($\theta$) represent the weights associated with a neural network that defines the policy or the Q-network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-808c974f7871ba6b7b5d18f72afc85a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289377</data>
    </node>
    <node id="N-layer Neural Network">
      <data key="d0">N-layer Neural Network</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">N-layer neural network terminology excludes the input layer; a single-layer network has no hidden layers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285632</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Convolutional Layer">
      <data key="d0">Convolutional Layer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A convolutional layer (CONV) computes the connection of each neuron with the local region of the input volume and consists of learnable filters/kernels.&lt;SEP&gt;A layer in the network architecture used to capture spatial relationships.&lt;SEP&gt;A Convolutional Layer is a fundamental component of Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) that uses convolutional filters or kernels to process input data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a4b902f2347e5fea9d43e606ce167ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289012</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Network Training">
      <data key="d0">Network Training</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Network training is the process for which inputs and outputs must be generated to train Deep Learning networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285632</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radius of the Coil">
      <data key="d0">Radius of the Coil</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The radius of the coil is a parameter that changes, ranging from 3mm to 15mm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285633</data>
    </node>
    <node id="M19 Silicon Steel">
      <data key="d0">M19 Silicon Steel</data>
      <data key="d1">material</data>
      <data key="d2">M19 silicon steel is the material used for the transformer core.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285634</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Left Coil">
      <data key="d0">Left Coil</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The left coil of the transformer has a fixed current of 1A and 90 turns.&lt;SEP&gt;The Left Coil is part of the transformer windings, consisting of two orange strips, specified to have a fixed current of 1A and 90 turns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c59eba9d5ca580dfd954f743c22649a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288850</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Right Coil">
      <data key="d0">Right Coil</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The right coil of the transformer has zero current.&lt;SEP&gt;The Right Coil is part of the transformer windings, also consisting of two orange strips, specified to have zero current.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c59eba9d5ca580dfd954f743c22649a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288850</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stator Windings">
      <data key="d0">Stator Windings</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Stator windings in the IPM motor have 8 turns.&lt;SEP&gt;These are orange trapezoidal shapes arranged radially within the stator, representing stator windings or slots.&lt;SEP&gt;Stator Windings are copper-colored, teardrop-shaped slots arranged around the inner circumference of the stator.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff320ec3e3a24e3c69d31630b6cba885&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b56c95146eb3d0e8591ffefd6ab679ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289044</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Excitation Current">
      <data key="d0">Excitation Current</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The excitation current for the stator windings of the IPM motor is varied from 25A to 35A rms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285634</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Partial Model">
      <data key="d0">Partial Model</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A partial (one pole) model is used for simulations to reduce computation time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285634</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Field Estimation">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Field Estimation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A problem targeted for acceleration through the application of deep networks, particularly for low-frequency EM devices using a bitmap approach.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic field estimation is a challenge addressed in this work.&lt;SEP&gt;A process where deep learning is applied.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic Field Estimation is the process of predicting the magnetic field within transformers, achieved through training neural networks with varied dimensional data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e2e27a9a79daf5a813690efa01766eca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e98180b8e135e08cf842f2a94ed38a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1e3ef3a96efc33efd9047031ab15a09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289091</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DL Estimator">
      <data key="d0">DL Estimator</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The DL estimator is hoped to be generalized through uncertainty analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285634</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weighted Sum">
      <data key="d0">Weighted Sum</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A weighted sum of incoming inputs is computed by every node in a Neural Network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285636</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Class Scores">
      <data key="d0">Class Scores</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Class scores are arbitrary real-valued numbers often represented by the output layer in classification tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285636</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Real-Valued Target">
      <data key="d0">Real-Valued Target</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A real-valued target is what the output layer represents in regression tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285636</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biases">
      <data key="d0">Biases</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Biases are part of the network's parameters affecting the loss function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285637</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gradients">
      <data key="d0">Gradients</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Gradients are computed using backpropagation for network parameters.&lt;SEP&gt;Gradients are used in Policy Gradient algorithms to update the weights (parameters θ) of the policy by performing gradient ascent on the performance measure J(θ).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb28e5c877704a09cf7eb4cbeb6522fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289078</data>
    </node>
    <node id="N-Layer Neural Network">
      <data key="d0">N-Layer Neural Network</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An N-layer Neural Network is a terminology that does not count the input layer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285637</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Single-Layer Neural Network">
      <data key="d0">Single-Layer Neural Network</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A single-layer Neural Network describes a model with no hidden layers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285637</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Learnable Weights">
      <data key="d0">Learnable Weights</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Learnable weights are parameters in CNNs and Neural Networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285637</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Linear Function">
      <data key="d0">Non-Linear Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A non-linear function is operated on by neurons in CNNs after multiplying inputs with weights.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285637</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Input Data">
      <data key="d0">Input Data</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Input data for CNNs follows a structured pattern, often a uniform grid (e.g., images, time-series events).&lt;SEP&gt;Input data, denoted by X, is typically a matrix or tensor of training features, such as images for image segmentation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289188</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Learnable Filters/Kernels">
      <data key="d0">Learnable Filters/Kernels</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Learnable filters/kernels are part of the convolutional layer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285639</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Input (Pixel)">
      <data key="d0">Input (Pixel)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The input of the network is formatted as a uniform structured grid containing geometry, material, and excitation information at each node, also referred to as a pixel.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285639</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Geometry">
      <data key="d0">Geometry</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Geometry is information included in the network input at each node.&lt;SEP&gt;An input parameter for the 'Flux Linkage Calculation' stage.&lt;SEP&gt;Geometry is an input parameter for the Flux Linkage Calculation (FEA Simulations) in the flowchart.&lt;SEP&gt;Geometry refers to the shape and material distribution visualized in the image. It is represented by a rectangular grid with white, blue, and brick-red squares.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a11edf0b881aae28423c1b0d33893b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288909</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Material">
      <data key="d0">Material</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Material is information included in the network input at each node.&lt;SEP&gt;An input parameter for the 'Flux Linkage Calculation' stage.&lt;SEP&gt;Material is an input parameter for the Flux Linkage Calculation (FEA Simulations) in the flowchart.&lt;SEP&gt;Material is a concept whose value is defined by the given equation, taking values 0 or 1 based on the condition of $\\Phi$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03395909e238b7701943add27146739b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6cf4c742d5c3ecf6b0e985a5914f0f4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-learning">
      <data key="d0">Q-learning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A learning algorithm used in Reinforcement Learning, often in combination with neural networks in Deep Reinforcement Learning.&lt;SEP&gt;Q-learning is a model-free reinforcement learning algorithm to learn an optimal action-value function.&lt;SEP&gt;A model-free reinforcement learning algorithm to learn optimal Q-functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Q-learning is a reinforcement learning algorithm that uses function approximation to estimate action-value functions, often with neural networks.&lt;SEP&gt;Q-learning is a reinforcement learning algorithm that estimates action values, contrasted with Policy Gradient which directly optimizes the policy.&lt;SEP&gt;Q-learning is a model-free reinforcement learning algorithm to learn the value of an action in a particular state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monte Carlo">
      <data key="d0">Monte Carlo</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A learning algorithm used in Reinforcement Learning, often in combination with neural networks in Deep Reinforcement Learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285639</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Gradients">
      <data key="d0">Policy Gradients</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A learning algorithm used in Reinforcement Learning, often in combination with neural networks in Deep Reinforcement Learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285639</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Function Approximator">
      <data key="d0">Function Approximator</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A function approximator is a method used to handle high dimensionality state-space or continuous values, as discussed in Chapter 5.&lt;SEP&gt;A component that needs to satisfy criteria like generalizability and discrimination to produce a satisfactory predictor for the value function.&lt;SEP&gt;A function approximator is a model that takes inputs and outputs scalar values, whose underlying operations vary based on the chosen type (e.g., linear models, neural networks). In the context, it is used to generalize knowledge across similar states.&lt;SEP&gt;A function approximator is a component in a learning system that estimates values or policies, challenged by states with similar features but drastically different returns, as demonstrated in Figure 5.4.&lt;SEP&gt;A function approximator is a mathematical model, like a neural network, that estimates a score or preference for an action in a state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f031035d088ffa5bcc3e370fe22c958&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1757cd493f052e1f9528255eb34ea59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289066</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discrimination">
      <data key="d0">Discrimination</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The ability of a function approximator to distinguish between significantly different states by assigning different predicted values.&lt;SEP&gt;Discrimination is a property of function approximators to distinguish significantly different states, vital for a successful Deep RL agent.&lt;SEP&gt;Discrimination refers to the ability to distinguish between different states or features, which is crucial for a function approximator to avoid misinterpreting similar states with vastly different outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f031035d088ffa5bcc3e370fe22c958&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tabular Methods">
      <data key="d0">Tabular Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Methods that are good at distinguishing two states perfectly but lack generalizability, where each state-action pair is represented by a distinct entry in a table.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285641</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State-Action Pair">
      <data key="d0">State-Action Pair</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A state-action pair refers to a specific state in an environment and an action taken within that state.&lt;SEP&gt;A combination of a state and an action, fundamental in reinforcement learning for value estimation.&lt;SEP&gt;A State-Action Pair ($s, a$) consists of a specific state ($s$) in an environment and a specific action ($a$) taken by an agent in that state.&lt;SEP&gt;A state-action pair, denoted as (S_i, A_i), represents the state the agent was in and the action it took at a specific time step.&lt;SEP&gt;A state-action pair (st, at) refers to a specific state and the action taken in that state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec4e495c59f004fcbe4475de4b2bcbd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1757cd493f052e1f9528255eb34ea59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293036</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reinforcement Learning Task">
      <data key="d0">Reinforcement Learning Task</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A learning scenario where data generation is online due to constant interaction between an agent and its environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285642</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Value Function">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Value Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The true and ideal value function, denoted as Q*(s, a), which is often unknown at the beginning of training in reinforcement learning problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285642</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Temporal Difference Learning">
      <data key="d0">Temporal Difference Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Temporal Difference Learning, often abbreviated as TD Learning or TD-learning, is a fundamental reinforcement learning (RL) method that involves updating estimated value functions. This algorithm operates by learning from the difference between temporally successive predictions, making its update rule a direct implementation of a TD learning update for function approximation.

A key characteristic of Temporal Difference Learning is its reliance on bootstrapping, meaning it updates value estimates based on other estimates, particularly the estimated value of the next state. This approach combines aspects of Monte Carlo ideas with dynamic programming, enabling incremental updates using the recursive relationship of returns. As a class of model-free RL methods, TD Learning uses sequences of experiences to generate targets for updating Q-function estimates, where the target \(Q_{\pi}(s, a)\) is an estimate based on future estimated values.

Temporal Difference Learning is applied in various contexts, including being discussed as a learning algorithm that updates estimated value functions based on differences between predictions at different times. It can also be deployed as a type of tree search algorithm and is explored as a sequence-based decision-making algorithm within RL for applications like sequence-based topology optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad46225ae0d5f3835a9bda1ed8158d48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0d5c4a49fbb5bedfe6eb2f200271d99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288921</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Return (Gt)">
      <data key="d0">Return (Gt)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Return (Gt) refers to the cumulative reward obtained from a specific time step 't' onwards in an episodic task.&lt;SEP&gt;An estimate used as an approximation for the true Value function, associated with high computation cost as discussed in Chapter 4, Section 4.5.3.&lt;SEP&gt;The Return (Gt) is an estimate of the action-value function, used to update neural network weights, and can be replaced by a bootstrap target.&lt;SEP&gt;The return (Gt) is the total accumulated reward from step 't' till the end of the episode, used in Q-learning and as a way to analyze performance in PG.&lt;SEP&gt;Return is the total discounted future reward from a given time step.&lt;SEP&gt;Return (Gt) is the cumulative reward received from time step t onwards in an episode, serving as the ultimate objective to maximize in RL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb28e5c877704a09cf7eb4cbeb6522fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289078</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Learning System">
      <data key="d0">Learning System</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A system that should ideally balance generalizability, discrimination, and online learning, and its ideal form is task-dependent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285642</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Online Learning">
      <data key="d0">Online Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The ability of the learning system to continuously learn from interaction with its environment, which is characteristic of RL tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;Online Learning refers to a machine learning capability where the model continuously learns from new data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3676b77d4e3057ccce502057364cbae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289125</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trajectory">
      <data key="d0">Trajectory</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A sequence of experiences used in reinforcement learning to make updates and decrease error.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285643</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weights (W)">
      <data key="d0">Weights (W)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Parameters in a neural network that are updated to make the action value estimate closer to the return.&lt;SEP&gt;Weights (W) represent the entire set of convolutional parameters of a deep neural network, considered random variables distributed according to a probability distribution in a Bayesian context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289184</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RL Task">
      <data key="d0">RL Task</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Reinforcement Learning task describes the challenge of training an agent to make optimal decisions through trial and error.&lt;SEP&gt;An RL Task is a specific problem in Reinforcement Learning where the goal is to optimize an agent's behavior based on experience.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289297</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Agent">
      <data key="d0">Agent</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An Agent is a fundamental entity within a reinforcement learning (RL) environment, designed to interact with its surroundings and make decisions. Its primary objective is to maximize cumulative rewards by taking actions in a given state, often exploring the environment to learn state transitions and dynamics. The Agent functions as a learner, continuously optimizing its actions based on estimated values and the feedback it receives, which can be in the form of rewards or penalties. Its past behavior plays a crucial role in informing its future actions, as it seeks to exploit existing knowledge while also exploring new possibilities.

The Agent processes and trains on data collected from its interactions within various scenarios. It is the decision-making entity that follows a policy to guide its actions. In some specific contextual representations, such as those within certain diagrams or grid-based environments (e.g., Figure 5.4), the Agent might be visually represented as a small black square, sometimes with a red frame, or as the component placing data blocks.

Furthermore, within the architecture of models like the Actor-Critic model, the Agent is a primary component. In this context, it encompasses various internal nodes including the Critic, Input, Learning element, Actor, and Output, highlighting its complex internal structure in advanced RL frameworks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f031035d088ffa5bcc3e370fe22c958&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1757cd493f052e1f9528255eb34ea59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1474f4155ebb5657d8ca7f3c0d29b49&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9b48425c0c345b3be14ed824c6579b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-596bd8f0cbcfc00de61fa772b9317edc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288878</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Environment">
      <data key="d0">Environment</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The environment is a key entity in a Markov Decision Process with which the controller interacts.&lt;SEP&gt;The environment provides feedback to the agent in the form of rewards or penalties and the next transition state based on the agent's actions.&lt;SEP&gt;The context in which a reinforcement learning agent operates and interacts.&lt;SEP&gt;The setting in which an agent operates, interacts, and collects experience.&lt;SEP&gt;In the context of a Markov Decision Process, the environment provides the 'state' to a controller.&lt;SEP&gt;The Environment is a central interaction point in the Q-Learning Cycle that receives an action `a_t` and returns the next state `s_t+1` and reward `r_t`.&lt;SEP&gt;The Environment is a primary component of the Actor-Critic model, interacting with the Agent by providing input and receiving output.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9b48425c0c345b3be14ed824c6579b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6ab3c95a34435be519e28c9335ef7bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11cc1c9381d2b807e25d94ba609ca1ea</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289101</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Stationary Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Non-Stationary Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A characteristic of training data in online learning where the data's statistical properties change over time due to the agent's dynamic interaction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285644</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sample Efficient Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Sample Efficient Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Algorithms designed to learn effectively from fewer data samples, particularly relevant in Deep RL due to non-stationary data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285644</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Value Estimation Problem">
      <data key="d0">Value Estimation Problem</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A problem focused on determining the value of states or state-action pairs, which can be set up as a supervised learning task.&lt;SEP&gt;The Value Estimation Problem is the goal of estimating Q_π(S, A) using individual samples from an experience trajectory.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288950</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regression Task">
      <data key="d0">Regression Task</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of supervised learning task where the goal is to predict a continuous output value.&lt;SEP&gt;A Regression Task is a type of supervised learning task where the goal is to predict continuous values, which aligns with minimizing the squared error.&lt;SEP&gt;A Regression Task is a type of supervised learning problem where the goal is to predict a continuous output variable. The value estimation problem is presented as a form of online regression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288920</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Estimate of the Return">
      <data key="d0">Estimate of the Return</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An approximation of the total reward received from a state or action in reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285646</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.4">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.4</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A figure providing an example of states with similar features but drastically different returns.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5.4 is an image displaying three grids labeled (a), (b), and (c), illustrating a sequential process or a series of states in what appears to be a Reinforcement Learning (RL) environment. The figure shows a sudden decrease in performance with a slight change in the state representation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f031035d088ffa5bcc3e370fe22c958</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289114</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hornik, Stinchcombe, and White (1989)">
      <data key="d0">Hornik, Stinchcombe, and White (1989)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Researchers credited for work related to the generalizability-differentiability trade-off in neural networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285647</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Henderson et al. (2018)">
      <data key="d0">Henderson et al. (2018)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Researchers associated with work on sample efficient algorithms in Deep RL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9360ea49ebcfafb62140f053dfc1f70c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285647</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Field &amp; Performance Maps Estimation">
