    <node id="Ripple Torque">
      <data key="d0">Ripple Torque</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Ripple torque is a global performance parameter of electric machines that can be predicted by certain modeling methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-05d9b6a613299c6224fd2160377cbc33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Effects">
      <data key="d0">Local Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Local effects within a device, such as fields, losses, and stresses, are not typically captured by current Machine Learning models based on literature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-05d9b6a613299c6224fd2160377cbc33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Labeled Data">
      <data key="d0">Labeled Data</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Labeled data is required for training Machine Learning models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-05d9b6a613299c6224fd2160377cbc33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physics-based Modeling">
      <data key="d0">Physics-based Modeling</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Physics-based Modeling is an approach that determines the numerical method suitable for generating synthetic training data, understanding capabilities and limitations of FEA and MEC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-05d9b6a613299c6224fd2160377cbc33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Domain Discretization">
      <data key="d0">Domain Discretization</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Domain Discretization is the first step in Finite Element Analysis, involving optimizing the number of elements and unknowns for desired solution accuracy.&lt;SEP&gt;Domain Discretization is a concept related to refining analyses in FEM, involving the division of a problem domain into smaller elements.&lt;SEP&gt;Domain Discretization is a concept illustrated in the image, showing how a larger design domain is broken down into smaller, discrete units for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce41e8cc5ef38832503f503eebaccf56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb6925d3d6ed02adeec99edaa647d90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05d9b6a613299c6224fd2160377cbc33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288750</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Interpolation Functions Selection">
      <data key="d0">Interpolation Functions Selection</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Selection of Interpolation Functions is the second step in Finite Element Analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-05d9b6a613299c6224fd2160377cbc33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="System of Equations Formulation">
      <data key="d0">System of Equations Formulation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Formulation of the System of Equations is the third step in Finite Element Analysis, involving converting a continuous operator problem to a discrete problem.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-05d9b6a613299c6224fd2160377cbc33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
