    <node id="ON/OFF Methodology">
      <data key="d0">ON/OFF Methodology</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A methodology used to optimize the core of an electromagnetic (EM) actuator to maximize the force experienced by the armature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285691</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SeqTO-v1 Methodology">
      <data key="d0">SeqTO-v1 Methodology</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A methodology used to optimize the core of an electromagnetic (EM) actuator to maximize the force experienced by the armature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285691</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EM Actuator">
      <data key="d0">EM Actuator</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">An electromagnetic actuator, the core of which is optimized to maximize force on the armature.&lt;SEP&gt;An EM actuator is an electromagnetic actuator, specifically a conventional C-core type.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1757cd493f052e1f9528255eb34ea59</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285691</data>
    </node>
    <node id="x-Axis Symmetry">
      <data key="d0">x-Axis Symmetry</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A principle utilized to reduce the size of the design space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285691</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electrical Steel">
      <data key="d0">Electrical Steel</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Electrical steel is one of the materials that cells in the design space can switch to (p_i=1), and it makes up the conventional C-core shape.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrical steel (M-19 26 Ga) is a non-linear material used in the C-core topology optimization study.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrical Steel is a material property assigned to cells ($p_j=1$) in the design domain of the C-core electromagnetic actuator.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrical steel is one of the materials (p_i = 1) that can be distributed within the cells of the DESIGN SPACE for core optimization.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrical steel is a material mentioned in the context of controllers (1x1 and 3x3) and their performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1757cd493f052e1f9528255eb34ea59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57172dfb9b0c90234ff04b1a5519b71e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6719c866b8100236897a8ad6ae78d11f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Air">
      <data key="d0">Air</data>
      <data key="d1">material</data>
      <data key="d2">A material that cells in the design space can switch to, along with electrical steel.&lt;SEP&gt;Air is a material property represented by p_j=0 in the design for the C-core electromagnetic actuator.&lt;SEP&gt;Air is one of the materials (p_i = 0) that can be distributed within the cells of the DESIGN SPACE for core optimization.&lt;SEP&gt;Air is the barrier material specified, which is introduced as flux barriers in the SynRM design during topology optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57172dfb9b0c90234ff04b1a5519b71e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288946</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GA Parameters">
      <data key="d0">GA Parameters</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Parameters of a Genetic Algorithm that are dependent on the TO controller configuration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285693</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TO Controller Configuration">
      <data key="d0">TO Controller Configuration</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The setup of the Topology Optimization controller, which influences GA parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285694</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tree Search Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Tree Search Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Algorithms that can be leveraged by the Topology Optimization method.&lt;SEP&gt;Algorithms that explore possible solutions by searching through a tree structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Tree search algorithms are a type of sequence-based decision-making algorithm, such as Temporal Difference (TD) learning and Monte Carlo (MC) algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285694</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monte Carlo Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Monte Carlo Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of tree search algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285694</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dynamic Programming">
      <data key="d0">Dynamic Programming</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Algorithms related to Tree Search Algorithms that make decisions as a series of sequences (Sutton 1988).&lt;SEP&gt;Dynamic Programming (DP) is a method from which Reinforcement Learning algorithms were developed, and it shares similarities with TD Learning, such as bootstrapping.&lt;SEP&gt;Dynamic Programming principles, specifically Bellman (1957), form the basis for reinforcement learning algorithms like Q-Learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289300</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sutton 1988">
      <data key="d0">Sutton 1988</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Sutton 1988 is a reference source related to Dynamic Programming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285694</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sutton and Barto 2018">
      <data key="d0">Sutton and Barto 2018</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Sutton and Barto 2018 is a reference source about Reinforcement Learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285694</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Markov Decision Process (MDP)">
      <data key="d0">Markov Decision Process (MDP)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Markov Decision Process (MDP) is a mathematical framework fundamentally designed for modeling sequential decision-making. It operates in discrete time, converting a task into a sequential problem where outcomes are partly random and partly influenced by a decision-maker's actions. This framework is particularly central to Reinforcement Learning, providing the underlying structure for such problems.

The key components of an MDP include states (S), actions (A), transition probabilities (P), and rewards (R). An implicit assumption within an MDP is that state transitions and reward functions depend exclusively on the current state and the chosen action, maintaining independence from previous states and actions. This characteristic is often referred to as the Markov property. The MDP defines an environment where these states, actions, rewards, and transitions are established, sometimes described in the context of advanced concepts like the Advantage function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ad46225ae0d5f3835a9bda1ed8158d48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289060</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action">
      <data key="d0">Action</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">In contexts such as Markov Decision Processes and Reinforcement Learning, an "Action" represents a fundamental concept referring to a decision, move, or operation executed by a controller or agent. It is often denoted by symbols such as $a$, $u$, $a_t$, $a_j$, or $A_i$. From a given state, an agent selects an action from an available set of choices, known as the action space. This selection influences future states, transitioning the system and potentially leading to rewards. The terms $a_t$ and $a_{t+1}$ specifically refer to the action taken at time $t$ and $t+1$, respectively, which are components of the Q-function and can also serve as input to the policy function at time $t$. The Policy Gradient algorithm, for instance, can output such an action $a_i$ given a particular state $s$.

Actions are crucial for an agent to interact with and influence its environment. These can range from simple directional commands like "Soft Up," "Soft Down," "Up," or "Down," to more complex operations such as design choices or material placements in electromagnetic device optimization. The variable 'a' or 'at' consistently represents an action taken in a specific state, signifying a choice or decision made within that state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66f8a9943508aa952a9845858b8d3374&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8823695e17798553515e243bdbfda2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-742edca401668538e3a7a9b93ec14162&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d0e0689a5d95559de772960193f788e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-feffff69e0f56312f2846d9f57d0a4f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-808c974f7871ba6b7b5d18f72afc85a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70c2a865bc92e157d3426a926d7ddd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e5324f5a16fd6822c647598c8e749b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca0b752852f034cec7766c7e6e92af67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6444ddfe2f3b1042c888bb109eee4098&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39c26f40d41eeedc15f07ee76f39c83c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293091</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reward">
      <data key="d0">Reward</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Reward is a key entity in a Markov Decision Process, representing feedback from the environment based on the agent's action.&lt;SEP&gt;A reward (r_t) is a feedback signal provided by the environment, which can be positive or negative (penalty), and is a scalar quantity.&lt;SEP&gt;Based on the change in magnetic field distribution near the middle of the armature.&lt;SEP&gt;Reward is received from a state and action in the Q-Value calculation.&lt;SEP&gt;Reward (R_t) is a metric in the reinforcement learning scenario, with a specific value of 64.94 shown in the diagram headers.&lt;SEP&gt;Reward ($R_{t+1}$) is the scalar reward received at time step $t+1$ after taking action $a_t$ in state $s_t$.&lt;SEP&gt;The term 'R_t' in the equation likely refers to a reward at time t.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-feffff69e0f56312f2846d9f57d0a4f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1474f4155ebb5657d8ca7f3c0d29b49&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292889</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electromagnetic (EM) Actuator">
      <data key="d0">Electromagnetic (EM) Actuator</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The electromagnetic (EM) actuator is the device being optimized to maximize the force experienced by its armature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285696</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.9">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.9</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.9 illustrates the C-core actuator based on the examples from Park and Min 2009a and Midha et al. 2019.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285696</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eqn. 4.2">
      <data key="d0">Eqn. 4.2</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Eqn. 4.2 is the formulation of the optimization problem.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285696</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action Vector `a`">
      <data key="d0">Action Vector `a`</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The action vector `a` is a sequence of actions that governs the controller’s movements and populates the material distribution vector `p`.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285696</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Material Distribution Vector `p`">
      <data key="d0">Material Distribution Vector `p`</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The material distribution vector `p` represents the material property of each cell in the discretized design domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285696</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sequence Length `m`">
      <data key="d0">Sequence Length `m`</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The sequence length `m` is a pre-defined length of the action vector `a` and represents the controller's freedom to explore the environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285696</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Length `n` of Material Distribution Vector `p`">
      <data key="d0">Length `n` of Material Distribution Vector `p`</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The length `n` is the total number of cells in the material distribution vector `p`.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285696</data>
    </node>
    <node id="K1">
      <data key="d0">K1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">K1 is a limit defining the minimum number of switched cells permitted in the design space.&lt;SEP&gt;K1 is a positive integer constant representing the minimum allowed number of cells assigned the property $p_j=1$ (electrical steel), set to 80.&lt;SEP&gt;K1 is a problem-specific parameter defining the lower bound for the number of air cells allowed in the rotor design.&lt;SEP&gt;K1 is a lower bound constant in the inequality constraint within the equation.&lt;SEP&gt;K1 represents a lower bound in the given equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14506e8e02aeb799dcd49d4b24c5b529&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50e280b998ddabea3a4713c685bb83d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="K2">
      <data key="d0">K2</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">K2 is a limit defining the maximum number of switched cells permitted in the design space, set to 180 to enforce a volume constraint for electrical steel.&lt;SEP&gt;K2 is a positive integer constant representing the maximum allowed number of cells assigned the property $p_j=1$ (electrical steel), set to 180.&lt;SEP&gt;K2 is a problem-specific parameter defining the upper bound for the number of air cells allowed in the rotor design.&lt;SEP&gt;K2 is an upper bound constant in the inequality constraint within the equation.&lt;SEP&gt;K2 represents an upper bound in the given equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14506e8e02aeb799dcd49d4b24c5b529&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50e280b998ddabea3a4713c685bb83d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Space (2-D)">
      <data key="d0">Design Space (2-D)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The 2-D design space is the area for optimization, discretized into 288 square cells of 1 mm size, with symmetry utilized to reduce its size.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285699</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.10">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.10</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.10 shows the reduction in the size of the design space using symmetry about the x-axis.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 4.10 is an illustration titled "C - core discretized design space" that depicts a 2D grid-based diagram for an electromagnetic actuator.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57172dfb9b0c90234ff04b1a5519b71e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289073</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Algorithms (GA)">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Algorithms (GA)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">GA is an optimization algorithm whose parameters are dependent on the Topology Optimization (TO) controller configuration.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic Algorithms are used with specific state representations for optimization.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic Algorithms are algorithms that will use this image's state representation for discrete design domains in Topology Optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289143</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Temporal Difference (TD) learning">
      <data key="d0">Temporal Difference (TD) learning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">TD-learning is a tree search algorithm used in sequence-based Topology Optimization and is a type of Reinforcement Learning.&lt;SEP&gt;Temporal Difference learning is a method in Reinforcement Learning that uses bootstrapping to update value functions by replacing the reward and an estimate of the return from the next state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289210</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monte Carlo (MC) Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Monte Carlo (MC) Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">MC algorithms are a type of tree search algorithm related to Dynamic Programming, used in sequence-based Topology Optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285699</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dynamic Programming (DP)">
      <data key="d0">Dynamic Programming (DP)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Dynamic Programming is a method related to tree search algorithms like Temporal Difference and Monte Carlo algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285699</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Table 4.1">
      <data key="d0">Table 4.1</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Table 4.1 lists a comparison of Evolutionary Algorithms and sequence-based decision-making algorithms like TD-learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9268a7feb5bb249a1838bc7743ffa61</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285699</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surrogate Models">
      <data key="d0">Surrogate Models</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Surrogate models, also known as data-driven models, approximate relationships between input parameters and output characteristics, reducing the repetitive bottom-up approach of first-principle models.&lt;SEP&gt;Surrogate models are alternative methods developed to accurately evaluate and/or optimize a device.&lt;SEP&gt;Surrogate models are alternative methods used to evaluate and optimize devices, often specific to a problem and limited by the number of parameters they can handle.&lt;SEP&gt;Surrogate Models are simpler models that emulate the behavior of more complex systems, often used in ML applications for optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-592244534b4a5f40cf1d7d58a26d28cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3676b77d4e3057ccce502057364cbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1e3ef3a96efc33efd9047031ab15a09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289134</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Computer Vision">
      <data key="d0">Computer Vision</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An active field of research where modern AI algorithms are applied.&lt;SEP&gt;Computer Vision is a complex problem area where Machine Learning and Deep Learning have found application for tasks like pattern recognition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b36856e6868820792611d3fddfdfc66f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1e3ef3a96efc33efd9047031ab15a09</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285701</data>
