    <node id="Loss Function Gradient">
      <data key="d0">Loss Function Gradient</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Loss Function Gradient is used to determine error on an experience trajectory, derived from Equation 5.1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285724</data>
    </node>
    <node id="True Value Function">
      <data key="d0">True Value Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The True Value Function is approximated by the estimation of the return for making updates in the weights of a neural network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weights (w)">
      <data key="d0">Weights (w)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Weights (w) are parameters of the neural network that are updated to make the action value estimate closer to the return or another estimate of the action-value function.&lt;SEP&gt;The 'weights' (w) are learnable parameters of the function approximator, adjusted during the learning process to tune the output of the parameterized function. For neural networks, these are the connection weights between layers.&lt;SEP&gt;Weights (w) are parameters of a neural network that are adjusted during the learning process to minimize the error between the optimal and estimated action-value functions.&lt;SEP&gt;A vector representing the adaptable parameters (weights and biases) of the neural network being trained, implicitly referring to the weights of the behavior Q-network ($\hat{Q}_{beh}$).&lt;SEP&gt;Weights (w) are parameters that are updated in the equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39c26f40d41eeedc15f07ee76f39c83c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action Value Function Q(s, a)">
      <data key="d0">Action Value Function Q(s, a)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Action Value Function Q(s, a) is predicted by a neural network and its estimate is brought closer to the return (Gt) or a bootstrap target.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bootstrap Target">
      <data key="d0">Bootstrap Target</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Bootstrap Target is an estimate of the action-value function based on the next step's action-value, used as an alternative to the Return (Gt) in the error equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Learning with Neural Networks">
      <data key="d0">Q-Learning with Neural Networks</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Q-learning with neural networks is an implementation approach where actions are stacked in one neural network to predict action values for all possible actions in a state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="On-Policy Learning">
      <data key="d0">On-Policy Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A method of learning where the policy used to generate experience is the same as the policy being improved.&lt;SEP&gt;On-policy learning is a learning approach where the data collection policy is the same as the target policy.&lt;SEP&gt;On-Policy Learning is a method where the action chosen by the current policy is used to form the target for the value function update, aligning with SARSA.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of reinforcement learning where the policy used to generate behavior is the same as the policy that is being evaluated and improved.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288920</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Behaviour Q-Network">
      <data key="d0">Behaviour Q-Network</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Behaviour Q-Network is one of two neural networks used in a more complex version of Q-learning, responsible for suggesting actions while collecting experience.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Target Q-Network">
      <data key="d0">Target Q-Network</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Target Q-Network is one of two neural networks used in a more complex version of Q-learning, used to train the Behaviour Policy but does not directly interact with the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;The target Q-network is a type of neural network used to provide stable estimated action-values ($\hat{Q}_{tar}$) for the update rule.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289272</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$ \hat { Q } _ { t a r } ( s _ { t } , a _ { t } , w _ { t a r } ) $">
      <data key="d0">$ \hat { Q } _ { t a r } ( s _ { t } , a _ { t } , w _ { t a r } ) $</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">This represents the target policy in the update equation for off-policy learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$ \hat { Q } _ { b e h } ( s _ { t } , a _ { t } , w _ { b e h } ) $">
      <data key="d0">$ \hat { Q } _ { b e h } ( s _ { t } , a _ { t } , w _ { b e h } ) $</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">This represents the behaviour policy in the update equation for off-policy learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285729</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Behaviour Policy">
      <data key="d0">Behaviour Policy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Behaviour Policy is associated with the Behaviour Q-Network and suggests actions while collecting experience.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285730</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Target Policy">
      <data key="d0">Target Policy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Target Policy is associated with the Target Q-Network, does not interact with the environment directly, and is used to train the Behaviour Policy.&lt;SEP&gt;The target policy is the policy that the agent aims to learn or optimize.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289106</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Off-Policy Learning">
      <data key="d0">Off-Policy Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Off-policy learning is a method where policies used for generating experience and training are different, employing two neural networks (Behaviour Q-Network and Target Q-Network). It has shown improved performance in Q-learning training.&lt;SEP&gt;Off-policy learning is a learning approach where the data collection policy differs from the policy being learned.&lt;SEP&gt;Off-Policy Learning is a method in RL where the policy used to generate behavior (explore) is different from the policy that is evaluated and improved.&lt;SEP&gt;Off-policy learning refers to reinforcement learning methods where the agent learns from data gathered by a different policy than the one being learned.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288955</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hasselt 2010">
      <data key="d0">Hasselt 2010</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Hasselt 2010 reference is cited as a source for improved performance in training Q-learning with off-policy learning.&lt;SEP&gt;Hasselt 2010 refers to a work by Hasselt that highlights improved performance in the training of Q-learning using specific methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289270</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$ \epsilon $-Greedy">
      <data key="d0">$ \epsilon $-Greedy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">$\epsilon$-Greedy is an exploration strategy used for both off-policy and on-policy learning, emphasizing exploration at the start of training and gradually decreasing it as the agent learns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285730</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exploration">
      <data key="d0">Exploration</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Exploration is a strategy where an agent chooses random actions to gain a more accurate estimate of its value function.&lt;SEP&gt;Exploration is a concept in Q-learning that allows an agent to improve its knowledge about each action, leading to more accurate estimates of its value function.&lt;SEP&gt;Exploration is the process where an agent tries many different actions in numerous states to learn all available possibilities and find optimal material distribution to maximize reward.&lt;SEP&gt;The phase where an agent deviates from its known optimal actions to discover potentially better actions or explore unknown states.&lt;SEP&gt;Exploration is the agent's strategy of trying different actions to discover their effects in an environment.&lt;SEP&gt;The probabilistic nature of a stochastic policy is crucial for exploration in Reinforcement Learning, allowing agents to try non-optimal actions for potentially better long-term rewards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1757cd493f052e1f9528255eb34ea59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289070</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exploitation">
      <data key="d0">Exploitation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Exploitation is a strategy where an agent chooses actions predicted to yield the most reward based on current estimated values.&lt;SEP&gt;Exploitation is a concept in Q-learning where an agent uses its current estimated values ( $Q ( s , a )$ ) and chooses the greedy action to maximize reward.&lt;SEP&gt;Exploitation is the process where an agent uses learned information to maximize rewards it can receive.&lt;SEP&gt;Exploitation is the phase in reinforcement learning where an agent leverages its current knowledge to choose the best action to maximize future rewards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1757cd493f052e1f9528255eb34ea59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289103</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RL Problems">
      <data key="d0">RL Problems</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Reinforcement Learning problems, which present a significant challenge due to the trade-off between exploration and exploitation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285730</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Experience Replay">
      <data key="d0">Experience Replay</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Experience Replay is a technique used in Deep Q-learning to store and reuse past experiences.&lt;SEP&gt;Experience Replay is a method where agents store records of state transitions in a memory buffer to learn from them later, as RL tasks lack pre-generated training sets.&lt;SEP&gt;Experience Replay is a memory mechanism used in DQN but not in PG.&lt;SEP&gt;A key addition to the Q-learning procedure introduced in a variation discussed in Section 5.3.2.&lt;SEP&gt;Experience Replay is a memory mechanism used in some reinforcement learning algorithms, like DQN, which is not present in PG algorithms.&lt;SEP&gt;Experience Replay is a method used in Deep Q-Networks (DQN) where the agent stores and samples past experiences to train the Q-network.&lt;SEP&gt;A technique where past experiences are stored and later sampled to train a neural network, allowing the agent to learn from past actions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e3e1dd6f2a21d7997b5fa281b76b709&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288951</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Memory Buffer">
      <data key="d0">Memory Buffer</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A Memory Buffer is a storage mechanism used in Experience Replay to keep records of state transitions encountered by an agent.&lt;SEP&gt;Populated with samples collected following a random policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285732</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cyclic Memory Buffers">
      <data key="d0">Cyclic Memory Buffers</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Cyclic Memory Buffers are a common type of memory buffer that behave like a queue, removing the oldest entry once capacity is reached, ensuring training on new behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285732</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reservoir-Sampling-Based Memory Buffer">
      <data key="d0">Reservoir-Sampling-Based Memory Buffer</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A Reservoir-Sampling-Based Memory Buffer guarantees that each state-transition recorded has an even probability of being inserted into the buffer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285733</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DeepMind's DQN">
      <data key="d0">DeepMind's DQN</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">DeepMind's DQN (Deep Q Network) is an algorithm that combines Experience Replay, $\epsilon$-based exploration, and Q-learning, using a neural network to find an optimal policy greedy on the cumulated reward function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285733</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mnih et al. 2013">
      <data key="d0">Mnih et al. 2013</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Mnih et al. 2013 reference is cited as the source for the breakthrough success of DeepMind's DQN in applying deep learning to RL problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285733</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Network (DQN)">
      <data key="d0">Q-Network (DQN)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A Q-Network (DQN) is a neural network trained in Deep Q-learning to find an optimal policy that is greedy on the cumulated reward function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285733</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tabular Q-Learning Algorithm">
      <data key="d0">Tabular Q-Learning Algorithm</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Tabular Q-learning Algorithm is a foundational method similar to DQN, but it uses state-action tables instead of functional approximators.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285733</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Functional Approximators">
      <data key="d0">Functional Approximators</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Functional Approximators, specifically neural networks, are used in advanced Q-learning variations to replace state-action tables for large-scale tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285733</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Separate Target Network">
      <data key="d0">Separate Target Network</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A separate target network is a key addition to the Q-learning procedure in Deep Q-learning, enhancing stability and performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285735</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reward Function">
      <data key="d0">Reward Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The reward function captures the notion of short-term gain for an agent in a task.&lt;SEP&gt;The reward function is the performance criterion (objective) that is maximized or minimized in an optimization process.&lt;SEP&gt;A function whose cumulated value is used to determine the optimal policy in Deep Q-learning, where the goal is to maximize it.&lt;SEP&gt;The reward function is referred to as the increasing force on the armature, which indicates the success of the optimization sequence.&lt;SEP&gt;Reward Function, specified as '∇T', is used in the TD-learning algorithm and represents the average torque generated by the motor.&lt;SEP&gt;The reward function quantifies the immediate feedback an agent receives from the environment, influencing the learning process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b6f3bea48fce928809f6ddf7db422c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288764</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State-Action Table">
      <data key="d0">State-Action Table</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A representation used in tabular Q-learning which is replaced by advanced functional approximators like neural networks in large-scale tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285735</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Section 4.5.3">
      <data key="d0">Section 4.5.3</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to a section discussing tabular Q-learning, which is algorithmically related to DQN.&lt;SEP&gt;A section referenced in the text that discusses Policy Evaluation and Control/Policy Improvement.&lt;SEP&gt;Section 4.5.3 is a specific part of a larger text that discusses the Policy Function.&lt;SEP&gt;Section 4.5.3 is a specific section in the surrounding text where the Tabular QL algorithm is discussed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4bc9886b92f8e393e3ab3d494b9db33c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ceb1ac6e54fec53c601d8b38f2b338d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288978</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Section 4.5.2">
      <data key="d0">Section 4.5.2</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to a section that discusses epsilon-greedy exploration.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific section referenced for further details on the State-Value Function V(s).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289178</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.5">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.5</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A figure referenced to show how Q-learning with neural networks is implemented by stacking actions in one neural network.&lt;SEP&gt;The title of the image, indicating it is an illustration within a larger document, showing different methods for setting up a Q-Network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9a7d0fc487b66b91a1d8bae6610798e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288916</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.6">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.6</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A figure referenced to illustrate "Knowledge Transfer" between a behavior policy and a target policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285736</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DeepMind">
      <data key="d0">DeepMind</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The organization credited with the breakthrough success of DQN (Deep Q Network) in applying deep learning to RL problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285736</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DQN">
      <data key="d0">DQN</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">DQN (Deep Q-Network) is a Q-learning-based algorithm that uses Experience Replay memory.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep Q Network, a successful application of deep learning to RL problems, combining experience replay, epsilon-based exploration, and Q-learning to train a neural network.&lt;SEP&gt;DQN is a reinforcement learning method that utilizes 'Experience Replay' memory.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e3e1dd6f2a21d7997b5fa281b76b709&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285736</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Experience Trajectory">
      <data key="d0">Experience Trajectory</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A sequence of states, actions, and rewards encountered by an agent in an environment.&lt;SEP&gt;An experience trajectory is a sequence of interactions between an agent and its environment, represented by state-action pairs and their estimated Q-values.&lt;SEP&gt;An experience trajectory refers to a sequence of states, actions, and rewards encountered by an agent in an RL environment.&lt;SEP&gt;The sequence of actions, states, and rewards collected by an agent through interaction with its environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288950</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gradient">
      <data key="d0">Gradient</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A mathematical concept used in the determination of error on an experience trajectory, derived from the loss function.&lt;SEP&gt;The gradient is a mathematical operator ($\nabla$) that computes the vector of partial derivatives, indicating the direction of the greatest rate of increase of a scalar field.&lt;SEP&gt;The gradient ($\nabla \hat{Q}(s_t, a_t, w)$) points in the direction of the steepest increase for $\hat{Q}(s_t, a_t, w)$ in the weight space.&lt;SEP&gt;The gradient ($\nabla J(\theta_t)$) represents the direction and rate of the steepest ascent of a function $J$ with respect to $\theta$ at time $t$.&lt;SEP&gt;nabla_theta represents the gradient with respect to theta.&lt;SEP&gt;The term G_t represents a gradient or a related quantity at time t.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e5324f5a16fd6822c647598c8e749b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d0e0689a5d95559de772960193f788e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2ac9217313164d00979e7afd6541254</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293045</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eq. 5.1">
      <data key="d0">Eq. 5.1</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific equation referenced in the text related to the loss function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285739</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Return">
      <data key="d0">Return</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An estimation of the cumulative future rewards used as an approximation for the true Value function in updating neural network weights. Represented as Gt.&lt;SEP&gt;The 'Return' at time step $t$, denoted as $G_t$, is the total discounted future reward from a given state-action pair, serving as a target for the $\hat{Q}$ function.&lt;SEP&gt;Return is a concept in reinforcement learning, denoted as G_t, representing the total accumulated reward from a specific time step.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q(s,a)">
      <data key="d0">Q(s,a)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The action value function, predicted by a neural network, representing the expected return for taking action 'a' in state 's'.&lt;SEP&gt;Q(s,a) represents the Q-value, which is the expected reward of taking action 'a' in state 's' and then following the optimal policy.&lt;SEP&gt;Q(s,a) is a mathematical notation representing the expected return from state s and action a, following policy π.&lt;SEP&gt;Q(s,a) represents the action-value function, which signifies the expected return of taking action 'a' in state 's' and then following a particular policy.&lt;SEP&gt;Q(s,a) represents the action-value function in reinforcement learning, which evaluates the expected return of taking action 'a' in state 's'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7724d4c011c9981c316498400dd34c58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6444ddfe2f3b1042c888bb109eee4098&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dae820ad2360b2e0540cedd11e75bb15</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293106</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action Value Function">
      <data key="d0">Action Value Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Action Value Function, denoted as $Q _ { \pi }$, describes what happens when an agent selects a particular action and indicates the value function's contingency on the agent selecting actions according to the policy.&lt;SEP&gt;A function that estimates the value of taking a particular action in a given state, predicted by a neural network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285739</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Error Equation">
      <data key="d0">Error Equation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An equation derived when using a bootstrap target for the action-value of the next step instead of the return.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285739</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Behavior Q-Network">
      <data key="d0">Behavior Q-Network</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">One of two neural networks used in a more complex version of Q-learning, responsible for suggesting actions while collecting experience.&lt;SEP&gt;The behavior Q-network is a type of neural network used to provide estimated action-values ($\hat{Q}_{beh}$) and whose weights are primarily updated by the equation.&lt;SEP&gt;The Behavior Q-Network is a specific type of Q-network used to estimate the action-value function for the behavior policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289106</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Update Equation">
      <data key="d0">Update Equation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An equation for updating the Q-value, specifically mentioned in the context of using two neural networks (behavior and target Q-networks).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285739</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hat Q_tar(st,at,wtar)">
      <data key="d0">Hat Q_tar(st,at,wtar)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Represents the target policy in the update equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285741</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hat Q_beh(st,at,wbeh)">
      <data key="d0">Hat Q_beh(st,at,wbeh)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Represents the behavior policy in the update equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285741</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Behavior Policy">
      <data key="d0">Behavior Policy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A policy responsible for suggesting actions while collecting experience, and for influencing the target policy.&lt;SEP&gt;The behavior policy is the policy used by an agent to generate actions and interact with the environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289106</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epsilon-Greedy">
      <data key="d0">Epsilon-Greedy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Epsilon-greedy is a random process used for choosing between exploration and exploitation, based on a parameter epsilon.&lt;SEP&gt;An exploration strategy used for both off-policy and on-policy learning, emphasizing exploration at the start and gradually decreasing it.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1757cd493f052e1f9528255eb34ea59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285742</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dynamics">
      <data key="d0">Dynamics</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The patterns of interaction and behavior within an environment that an agent learns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285742</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Material Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Material Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The ideal distribution that maximizes an agent's overall reward, found through exploration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285742</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State Transitions">
      <data key="d0">State Transitions</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Records of changes from one state to another encountered by an agent, stored in a memory buffer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285742</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Q-learning">
      <data key="d0">Deep Q-learning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep Q-learning is a reinforcement learning algorithm, with its results discussed.&lt;SEP&gt;A method that combines Experience Replay, epsilon-based exploration, and the Q-learning algorithm to train a Neural Network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285742</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced Functional Approximators">
      <data key="d0">Advanced Functional Approximators</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Mechanisms like neural networks used to replace state-action tables in large-scale Q-learning tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de02f58d9cc1d404c5da9c1f45b14fa6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285742</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Equivalent Circuit Method">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Equivalent Circuit Method</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Magnetic Equivalent Circuit (MEC) method is a reduced-order finite element analysis technique that translates a geometrical description of a magnetic device into an electrical circuit description. It is useful for performance analysis and initial device sizing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-592244534b4a5f40cf1d7d58a26d28cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285744</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electric Machine">
      <data key="d0">Electric Machine</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">An electric machine is considered a quasi-stationary device where changes in current are immediately followed by changes in flux.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-592244534b4a5f40cf1d7d58a26d28cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285744</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flux Tube">
      <data key="d0">Flux Tube</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A flux tube is a geometrical space where lines of flux are perpendicular to their bases and do not cut their sides. Flux tubes form the basis of the magnetic equivalent circuit method.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-592244534b4a5f40cf1d7d58a26d28cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285744</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reluctance Network">
      <data key="d0">Reluctance Network</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A reluctance network is a representation of the magnetic circuit, whose change during motion adds to the complexity of solving equations in motor analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-592244534b4a5f40cf1d7d58a26d28cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285745</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FE Modeling">
      <data key="d0">FE Modeling</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">FE modeling refers to the process of creating models using Finite Element methods, which is computationally expensive for simulation purposes and still an active field of research.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-592244534b4a5f40cf1d7d58a26d28cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285745</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Design Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Design analysis refers to the evaluation of designs, often using methods like FEA, to ensure optimal performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-592244534b4a5f40cf1d7d58a26d28cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285745</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Real-Time Simulation Environment">
      <data key="d0">Real-Time Simulation Environment</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A real-time simulation environment is a system where simulations run with timing constraints, making computationally intensive models like FEM unsuitable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-592244534b4a5f40cf1d7d58a26d28cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285745</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3-D FEA">
      <data key="d0">3-D FEA</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">3-D FEA is a three-dimensional Finite Element Analysis method used for design testing, which is computationally very expensive due to longer analysis time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-592244534b4a5f40cf1d7d58a26d28cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285745</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Krause et al. (2013)">
      <data key="d0">Krause et al. (2013)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Krause et al. (2013) are researchers credited with contributing to the definition of the Magnetic Equivalent Circuit method.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-592244534b4a5f40cf1d7d58a26d28cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285745</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carpenter (1968)">
      <data key="d0">Carpenter (1968)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Carpenter (1968) is a researcher whose work is cited in relation to the accuracy of solutions provided by the Magnetic Equivalent Circuit method.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-592244534b4a5f40cf1d7d58a26d28cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285746</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ostovic (2012)">
      <data key="d0">Ostovic (2012)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Ostovic (2012) is a researcher cited for defining flux tubes and detailing aspects of FEA and MEC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-592244534b4a5f40cf1d7d58a26d28cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285747</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Zhao et al. (2011)">
      <data key="d0">Zhao et al. (2011)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Zhao et al. (2011) are researchers who have presented methods to consider motion in motors, specifically regarding the complexity added by changes in reluctance networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-592244534b4a5f40cf1d7d58a26d28cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285747</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Zhang et al. (2006)">
      <data key="d0">Zhang et al. (2006)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Zhang et al. (2006) are researchers who have presented methods to consider motion in motors, specifically regarding the complexity added by changes in reluctance networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-592244534b4a5f40cf1d7d58a26d28cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285747</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wang, Jatskevich, and Dommel (2007)">
      <data key="d0">Wang, Jatskevich, and Dommel (2007)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Wang, Jatskevich, and Dommel (2007) are researchers whose work supports the use of FE analysis for studying complex issues in machines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-592244534b4a5f40cf1d7d58a26d28cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285747</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tavana and Dinavahi (2016)">
      <data key="d0">Tavana and Dinavahi (2016)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Tavana and Dinavahi (2016) are researchers cited in the context of FEM models being unsuitable for real-time simulation environments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-592244534b4a5f40cf1d7d58a26d28cc</data>
