    <node id="Reward ($r_t$ )">
      <data key="d0">Reward ($r_t$ )</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Reward is a feedback mechanism provided by the environment to the agent; it can be positive for good moves or negative (penalty) for illegal/bad moves and is a scalar quantity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285804</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State ($s_t$ )">
      <data key="d0">State ($s_t$ )</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The state describes the current material distribution in the design domain, based on which the agent takes action, and is part of Markov Decision Process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285804</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action ($\boldsymbol{u}$)">
      <data key="d0">Action ($\boldsymbol{u}$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An action is a rational decision made by the controller or agent, which influences rewards and future states. The choice of action impacts immediate reward and the next state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285804</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Penalty">
      <data key="d0">Penalty</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A penalty is a negative reward given to the agent for an illegal or bad move.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285804</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State Transition">
      <data key="d0">State Transition</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The process of moving from one state ($s_t$) to another ($s_t^\prime$) based on an action, learned through exploration of the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;State Transition is the concept of moving from one state to another based on an action, as illustrated in the diagram.&lt;SEP&gt;State transition refers to the movement from an initial state (s) to a subsequent state (s'), visually demonstrated by the controller's movement from sub-image (a) to sub-image (b).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e6ab3c95a34435be519e28c9335ef7bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-906a75be5f88ad37713839d335d1720c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289098</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reward Signal ($r_t$ )">
      <data key="d0">Reward Signal ($r_t$ )</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A scalar quantity ($r_t \in \mathbb{R}$) received at each step, indicating the consequence of an action.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Terminal States">
      <data key="d0">Terminal States</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Specific states in an MDP where tasks reaching them are terminated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy">
      <data key="d0">Policy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A "Policy", often denoted as $\pi$ or $\pi(a|s)$, is a fundamental concept in Reinforcement Learning that defines how an agent behaves. It represents a mapping from a given state to either specific actions or, more commonly, to probabilities of selecting each possible action from that state. This mapping essentially dictates the agent's strategy for choosing actions.

The policy determines how an agent selects actions by providing a distribution over actions for all possible states. It can be parameterized, often by $\theta$ (e.g., $\pi_{\theta}(a|s)$ or $\pi(a_t | s_t ; \theta_t)$), where $\theta$ represents the parameters that define this mapping. This parameterized probability distribution over actions given a state is central to how an agent makes decisions and defines the agent's behavior. The true state-value function ($v_{\pi}(s)$) is defined under a specific policy.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy 'π' is a behavioral strategy that maps states to probabilities of selecting each possible action.&lt;SEP&gt;pi(a_t | s_t; theta_t) represents the policy, which is the probability of taking action a_t in state s_t given parameter theta.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb28e5c877704a09cf7eb4cbeb6522fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6444ddfe2f3b1042c888bb109eee4098&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e3e1dd6f2a21d7997b5fa281b76b709&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e5324f5a16fd6822c647598c8e749b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deterministic Policy ($\pi$ )">
      <data key="d0">Deterministic Policy ($\pi$ )</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of policy that maps each state to a single action from the action set, meaning $\pi(s)$ represents the action selected in state $s$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285806</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Episodic Task">
      <data key="d0">Episodic Task</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of task where the length of an episode is limited to allow the controller to move and place a given volume of material, ensuring sufficient exploration.&lt;SEP&gt;An Episodic Task is a type of task in Reinforcement Learning where the sequence of actions and rewards has a clear end point, characterized by a finite horizon.&lt;SEP&gt;An episodic task is a type of task in reinforcement learning that has a clear beginning and end, used to guide the learning process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289112</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Function Calls (FE)">
      <data key="d0">Function Calls (FE)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Calls made by the controller, which are intended to be limited in number.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285806</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Optimization Setup">
      <data key="d0">Design Optimization Setup</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The design optimization setup limits the length of an episode to ensure sufficient material placement and exploration of the design space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285806</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conventional Geometry">
      <data key="d0">Conventional Geometry</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A standard or traditional geometric form, used as a reference for determining the number of steps needed in an episode.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285806</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reward Formulation">
      <data key="d0">Reward Formulation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Reward formulation involves defining the dynamics of the MDP to achieve successful creation of optimal geometry under constraints, aiming to maximize agent's reward.&lt;SEP&gt;Reward Formulation refers to the process of defining what constitutes 'good' behavior at each step in a Reinforcement Learning problem, dictating the values of $R_k$.&lt;SEP&gt;Reward Formulation is the process of defining the rewards an agent receives for its actions, aimed at maximizing cumulative rewards and guiding policy learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289112</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immediate Reward">
      <data key="d0">Immediate Reward</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The reward received by the agent at the current step.&lt;SEP&gt;The immediate reward ($R_{t+1}$) is the reward received after taking an action at state $s_t$ and transitioning to state $s_{t+1}$.&lt;SEP&gt;The immediate reward, denoted as $r(s, a)$, is the scalar feedback received from the environment after taking action $a$ in state $s$. It is a direct signal from the environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288802</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cumulative Reward">
      <data key="d0">Cumulative Reward</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cumulative reward represents the sum of rewards an agent receives over an episode or a period, reflecting long-term goals.&lt;SEP&gt;Cumulative Reward is a concept closely related to 'Return', representing the sum of rewards over a period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289123</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Learning">
      <data key="d0">Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The agent's process of balancing short-term and long-term goals by maximizing rewards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285808</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Performance (Objective) Criteria">
      <data key="d0">Performance (Objective) Criteria</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The metric that is maximized or minimized, which serves as the obvious reward function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285809</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-core">
      <data key="d0">C-core</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">C-core is an object for which Topology Optimization results are presented (GA with SeqTO-v1 and TD-learning results).&lt;SEP&gt;A C-core is an object for which the force experienced by its armature or the magnetic field distribution values are used as performance indicators in the MDP.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of core being studied, with results obtained from TD-learning based on a specific design setup.&lt;SEP&gt;A C-core is an object for which excitation scenarios are considered.&lt;SEP&gt;A C-core is an example of an optimized design aim.&lt;SEP&gt;C-core is a practical application or system where the A2C algorithm is used for control with different excitation scenarios.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5526ac278068ab9cbf72c11fd3817672&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289112</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Field Lines">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Field Lines</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnetic field lines pass through the armature, and their quantity is directly proportional to the magnetic field distribution, indicating performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic Field Lines are thin black lines on the magnetic flux distribution visualization, indicating the direction and density of the magnetic field.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b7b73719952876f4f9c0e6b4efe0027&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289289</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Total Return ($G_t$ )">
      <data key="d0">Total Return ($G_t$ )</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The sum of rewards the agent will receive after a certain time, representing a measure of future accumulated rewards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285809</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Markov Property">
      <data key="d0">Markov Property</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Markov Property states that the present state is sufficient for making predictions about the future, without needing knowledge of earlier states.&lt;SEP&gt;A property stating that future states and rewards depend only on the current state and action, not on past states or actions, making the present state sufficient for prediction.&lt;SEP&gt;The Markov property states that there must not be any useful information in previous states for predicting future states.&lt;SEP&gt;The Markov Property is visually exemplified by the image, meaning the current state is sufficient to predict future states without needing a history of previous states, crucial for Markov Decision Processes (MDPs).&lt;SEP&gt;The Markov property describes a system where future states depend only on the current state, not on the sequence of events that preceded it. The image implies breaking this property by inferring 'bad actions' from previous frames.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1b166c346deb9e14c2fe057bc33cdbf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289088</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State Representation">
      <data key="d0">State Representation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The State Representation includes information about the past distribution of design domain and material distribution, crucial for decision-making without violating the Markov property.&lt;SEP&gt;State representation involves how the current material distribution and past distributions are included in the state to enable actions that depend on previous behavior without violating the Markov property.&lt;SEP&gt;The image displays a state representation for Topology Optimization within the context of Reinforcement Learning or Genetic Algorithms.&lt;SEP&gt;Refers to how data is structured to describe the current condition or configuration of a system, particularly in optimization frameworks like RL and GA.&lt;SEP&gt;State representation refers to how the current condition of an environment or system is depicted, as illustrated in Figure 5.4, where even a slight change in this representation can significantly impact performance.&lt;SEP&gt;State Representation is a discrete design domain used in Chapter 4 for the C-core problem, ideal for tabular reinforcement learning algorithms due to its integer encoding.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec0d779e474774183c36eade5f33db6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f031035d088ffa5bcc3e370fe22c958&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b76c5d19e7bcbbd5f11b71588e066f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288895</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.13">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.13</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.13 illustrates the C-core armature where magnetic field distribution values are observed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.14">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.14</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.14 illustrates a specific state representation used with Reinforcement Learning (RL) and Genetic Algorithms (GA) within this chapter.&lt;SEP&gt;A figure referenced in the text illustrating multiple ways of representing the state for a discrete design domain for Topology Optimization.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 4.14 is an image titled "Discrete design domain as the state space" which presents a two-dimensional grid used in topology optimization.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 4.14 is the context provided in surrounding text, referring to the image as a state representation for discrete design domains in Topology Optimization.&lt;SEP&gt;A figure referenced for a state representation used with RL and GA algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b76c5d19e7bcbbd5f11b71588e066f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8803108c64e008ff20b50a70b77f8af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289135</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transition State">
      <data key="d0">Transition State</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The transition state (s_{t+1} or s') is the next state the environment provides after an agent takes an action in the current state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Algorithm">
      <data key="d0">Algorithm</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The algorithm explores the environment to learn transitions between states based on actions.&lt;SEP&gt;An algorithm achieves a stable, high Exponentially Average Reward after a certain number of training episodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad8c5c524179d51552952772945627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288746</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deterministic Policy">
      <data key="d0">Deterministic Policy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A deterministic policy results when a policy maps each state to a single action.&lt;SEP&gt;A deterministic policy maps each state to a single action from the action set, often represented in a tabular format.&lt;SEP&gt;A deterministic policy is a strategy in Reinforcement Learning that prescribes a unique and specific action for every given state.&lt;SEP&gt;A deterministic policy is a policy that unequivocally prescribes a single action 'a' for a given state 's', unlike a stochastic policy.&lt;SEP&gt;A deterministic policy is a function that maps a state to a single, specific action, defining the optimal course of action in a given state.&lt;SEP&gt;A policy in reinforcement learning where the optimal action $a^*$ is chosen by maximizing the Q-function, leading to a single, predictable action for a given state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58674fc78b8e0f9b48c3a573d49dd684&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289111</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Performance Criteria">
      <data key="d0">Performance Criteria</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Performance criteria, such as objective functions, are used as reward functions to communicate desired achievements to the agent.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance criteria is another term for the performance measure J.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289371</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Return (G_t)">
      <data key="d0">Return (G_t)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Return (G_t) is the cumulative reward obtained from time step t onwards in an episodic task.&lt;SEP&gt;The return (G_t) is the sum of rewards an agent receives after a certain time, representing the total future reward from that point.&lt;SEP&gt;Return (G_t) is the total accumulated reward from a step 't' till the end of the episode, used in Q-learning and as a performance measure.&lt;SEP&gt;Return (G_t) is the total discounted reward from time t onwards, as seen in the first equation where it updates the Q-value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7545e109d55e050bca56ba6604cda3ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8823695e17798553515e243bdbfda2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292784</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Uncertainty Threshold Value">
      <data key="d0">Uncertainty Threshold Value</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A value used in segmentation to sort pixels based on the model's confidence in prediction, providing a partial but highly accurate solution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parameter (p)">
      <data key="d0">Parameter (p)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A parameter that determines the probability of dropping out any neuron from the network for an iteration during training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dataset (X,Y)">
      <data key="d0">Dataset (X,Y)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The dataset used for training a neural network, consisting of input (X) and target (Y).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285813</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thinned Networks">
      <data key="d0">Thinned Networks</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A collection of 2^K networks with extensive weight sharing that are approximated by a single network at test time when using dropout.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285813</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weight (W)">
      <data key="d0">Weight (W)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to the extensive weight sharing among thinned networks, or a point estimate for weights in a single network's approximation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weight Averaging Technique">
      <data key="d0">Weight Averaging Technique</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A technique used by a single network to approximate the average output at test time when dropout is applied.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Badrinarayanan, Handa, and Cipolla 2015">
      <data key="d0">Badrinarayanan, Handa, and Cipolla 2015</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A publication by Badrinarayanan, Handa, and Cipolla in 2015 related to weight averaging in neural networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dropout Variational Inference">
      <data key="d0">Dropout Variational Inference</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A practical approach for approximate inference in large and complex models, as described by Kendall, Badrinarayanan, and Cipolla (2015).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kendall, Badrinarayanan, and Cipolla 2015">
      <data key="d0">Kendall, Badrinarayanan, and Cipolla 2015</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A publication by Kendall, Badrinarayanan, and Cipolla in 2015, which discusses dropout variational inference and Bayesian SegNet.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Model Posterior Distribution (P(W|X,Y))">
      <data key="d0">Model Posterior Distribution (P(W|X,Y))</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A difficult-to-infer distribution in a Bayesian Neural Network, approximated by variational inference.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bayesian Neural Network (BNN)">
      <data key="d0">Bayesian Neural Network (BNN)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of neural network where inferring the model posterior distribution is a difficult task.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Variational Inference (VI)">
      <data key="d0">Variational Inference (VI)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A popular approach to approximate the posterior distribution by minimizing the Kullback-Leibler divergence.&lt;SEP&gt;Variational Inference (VI) is a method used to approximate intractable posterior distributions by minimizing the Kullback-Leibler divergence between the true posterior and a tractable distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;Variational Inference is an approximation technique used to estimate intractable posterior distributions by finding an approximate distribution that minimizes the Kullback-Leibler divergence to the true posterior.&lt;SEP&gt;Variational Inference (VI) is a method that uses the minimization of KL divergence to find an approximate posterior distribution q(W) for intractable posterior distributions in Bayesian models.&lt;SEP&gt;An approximation method for estimating the intractable posterior distribution $p(W|X, Y)$ by minimizing Kullback-Leibler divergence.&lt;SEP&gt;A technique used to learn the approximate posterior distribution Q(W) by minimizing the Kullback-Leibler divergence between Q(W) and the true posterior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288902</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kullback-Leibler Divergence">
      <data key="d0">Kullback-Leibler Divergence</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A measure of how one probability distribution is different from a second, reference probability distribution. It is minimized in Variational Inference to approximate the true posterior.&lt;SEP&gt;The Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence, denoted as KL(q || p), is a non-symmetric measure of the difference between two probability distributions q and p, quantifying the 'information loss' when q approximates p. It plays a central role in variational inference.&lt;SEP&gt;Kullback-Leibler divergence is a measure used in variational inference to quantify the difference between two probability distributions, particularly for approximating the posterior distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288900</data>
    </node>
    <node id="True Posterior (P(W|X,Y))">
      <data key="d0">True Posterior (P(W|X,Y))</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The true posterior distribution that variational inference aims to approximate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blundell et al. 2015">
      <data key="d0">Blundell et al. 2015</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A publication by Blundell et al. in 2015, which states that variational inference can almost double the number of model parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gaussian Processes">
      <data key="d0">Gaussian Processes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A concept in which dropout can be cast as approximate Bayesian inference, allowing uncertainty information extraction without additional parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monte Carlo Sampling">
      <data key="d0">Monte Carlo Sampling</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A technique used to perform dropout during the test phase, viewing it as sampling from the posterior distribution over various dropout models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bernoulli Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Bernoulli Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The distribution from which weights are essentially drawn when applying dropout to all weight layers.&lt;SEP&gt;Bernoulli Distribution is a discrete probability distribution that implicitly arises when dropout is applied, influencing the sampling of weights.&lt;SEP&gt;A probability distribution imposed over the CNN filter's weights by dropout, providing a mechanism for sampling different $W$ configurations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288902</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bayesian SegNet">
      <data key="d0">Bayesian SegNet</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A method referred to as being in line with the work extending uncertainty information to more than a visualization tool.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Input (X)">
      <data key="d0">Input (X)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The observed training data used to find the posterior distribution over convolutional weights.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Target (Y)">
      <data key="d0">Target (Y)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The observed training data used to find the posterior distribution over convolutional weights.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285818</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Evidence Term">
      <data key="d0">Evidence Term</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A term, p(X) = ∫p(X, W) × p(W) dW, that is very difficult to compute.&lt;SEP&gt;The evidence term, $P(X)$, also known as marginal likelihood or model evidence, is the probability of observing the input data X and serves as a normalizing constant. It is explicitly stated to be difficult to compute.&lt;SEP&gt;The marginal probability of observing the data, which serves as a normalization constant in Bayes' theorem. Represented by 'p(X, Y)'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289189</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Distribution q(W)">
      <data key="d0">Distribution q(W)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An approximate distribution used in Variational Inference to minimize Kullback-Leibler divergence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prediction Uncertainty">
      <data key="d0">Prediction Uncertainty</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Uncertainty in predictions induced by the uncertainty in weights when dropout is seen as an approximation to a Bayesian Network.&lt;SEP&gt;The theoretical significance of the equation, underpinning the concept of capturing uncertainty arising from model parameters for robust predictions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289277</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probability (p)">
      <data key="d0">Probability (p)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The probability with which a unit is present in Dropout; weights are multiplied by 'p' at testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CNN Filter's Weights">
      <data key="d0">CNN Filter's Weights</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The weights of a Convolutional Neural Network filter, on which a Bernoulli distribution is imposed by dropout.&lt;SEP&gt;Weights within a Convolutional Neural Network filter to which the Bernoulli distribution is applied during dropout.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289400</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monte Carlo (MC) Dropout">
      <data key="d0">Monte Carlo (MC) Dropout</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A method (Gal and Ghahramani 2016) used to compute uncertainty maps by retrieving samples and calculating standard deviation.&lt;SEP&gt;Monte Carlo (MC) Dropout is a practical implementation that approximates Bayesian inference, providing a computationally efficient way to sample from an approximate posterior distribution and obtain uncertainty measures.&lt;SEP&gt;A practical approximation technique for Monte Carlo integration, used to approximate the intractable integral by sampling from the posterior.&lt;SEP&gt;A technique viewed as performing Monte Carlo sampling from the posterior distribution over various dropout models, approximating the integral in the equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288902</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Softmax Outputs">
      <data key="d0">Softmax Outputs</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Outputs of a neural network layer over which the standard deviation is calculated to generate uncertainty maps.&lt;SEP&gt;Softmax Outputs are the results of the softmax function, often used in neural networks to represent probability distributions over classes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92cc2b02ae3a119d92f0d1a7f6617885</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289158</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Classification Accuracy">
      <data key="d0">Classification Accuracy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A measure that is related to the standard deviation of Monte Carlo samples and can be increased by Monte Carlo sampling.&lt;SEP&gt;A measure of how correctly a model predicts classes, noting that Monte Carlo sampling can improve it.&lt;SEP&gt;Classification Accuracy (CA) is a fundamental metric in binary classification tasks, quantifying the proportion of correctly classified instances out of the total.&lt;SEP&gt;Classification Accuracy is a metric used to evaluate the performance of a classification model, calculated as the ratio of correctly predicted instances (True Positives and True Negatives) to the total number of instances.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6871b523f5d81edec8c74e56e7c813&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74dead31275ed48a2a31d5ae16d58cb0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292681</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 2.13(b)">
      <data key="d0">Figure 2.13(b)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A figure showing a normalized uncertainty map, with regions of high uncertainty highlighted.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 2.13(c)">
      <data key="d0">Figure 2.13(c)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A figure showing highlighted and expanded regions of high uncertainty from Figure 2.13(b).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 2.13(a)">
      <data key="d0">Figure 2.13(a)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A figure showing the error distribution between prediction and ground truth, including circled regions of high error.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standard Deviation of Monte Carlo Samples">
      <data key="d0">Standard Deviation of Monte Carlo Samples</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Related to classification accuracy, as shown in Figure 2.13(b).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High Uncertainty Value">
      <data key="d0">High Uncertainty Value</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An indication not to rely on results and to perhaps employ generalized conventional solvers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Generalized Conventional Solvers">
      <data key="d0">Generalized Conventional Solvers</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Methods like FEA, FDM, etc., that might be employed when high uncertainty values are present.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FEA">
      <data key="d0">FEA</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to Finite Element Analysis, a generalized conventional solver that might be employed when uncertainty is high.&lt;SEP&gt;Refers to Finite Element Analysis software, mentioned in the context of informed decision-making where high uncertainty in model predictions might lead to relying on traditional solvers like FEA.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289282</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FDM">
      <data key="d0">FDM</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to Finite Difference Method, a generalized conventional solver that might be employed when uncertainty is high.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mean Squared Error">
      <data key="d0">Mean Squared Error</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A metric used to measure accuracy, which was improved by MC sampling.&lt;SEP&gt;Mean Squared Error is a common metric used to measure the average of the squares of the errors, representing the difference between predicted values and actual values, indicated to be in the range of 1-1.5% per node/point.&lt;SEP&gt;Mean Squared Error is a common loss function in supervised learning, which uses the squared difference of errors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf789c1974c43fd1722237522bebd446</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289035</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Srivastava Et Al. 2014">
      <data key="d0">Srivastava Et Al. 2014</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A publication that introduced the Dropout regularization technique.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285823</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parameter &quot;P&quot;">
      <data key="d0">Parameter "P"</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A parameter within the Dropout technique that determines the probability of dropping out any neuron from the network during an iteration of training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285823</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dataset (X, Y)">
      <data key="d0">Dataset (X, Y)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Represents the input (X) and target (Y) data used for training a neural network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Badrinarayanan, Handa, And Cipolla 2015">
      <data key="d0">Badrinarayanan, Handa, And Cipolla 2015</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A publication relevant to the weight averaging technique in neural networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kendall, Badrinarayanan, And Cipolla 2015">
      <data key="d0">Kendall, Badrinarayanan, And Cipolla 2015</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A publication discussing dropout variational inference and Bayesian SegNet, and showing the value of Monte Carlo sampling for classification accuracy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Model Posterior Distribution P(W|X,Y)">
      <data key="d0">Model Posterior Distribution P(W|X,Y)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The posterior distribution of weights given input and target data in a Bayesian Neural Network, which is difficult to infer without approximation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bayesian NN">
      <data key="d0">Bayesian NN</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of neural network where inferring the model posterior distribution is a difficult task.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Variational Inference">
      <data key="d0">Variational Inference</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A popular approach to approximate the posterior distribution in Bayesian NNs by minimizing the Kullback-Leibler divergence. It can be computationally expensive.&lt;SEP&gt;Variational Inference (VI) is a method used to approximate difficult-to-infer posterior distributions by minimizing the Kullback-Leibler divergence between the true posterior and a tractable distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;A method that uses the minimization of KL divergence to find an approximate posterior distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288906</data>
    </node>
    <node id="True Posterior P(W|X,Y)">
      <data key="d0">True Posterior P(W|X,Y)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The actual posterior distribution of weights given input and target data, which is approximated by minimizing Kullback-Leibler divergence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Evidence Term P(X)">
      <data key="d0">Evidence Term P(X)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A term represented by an integral that is computationally difficult to compute, requiring approximation of the weight distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285826</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Distribution Q(W)">
      <data key="d0">Distribution Q(W)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An approximate distribution learned in Variational Inference by minimizing the Kullback-Leibler divergence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285826</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probability P">
      <data key="d0">Probability P</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The probability with which a unit is present when dropout is applied.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285826</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bayesian Network">
      <data key="d0">Bayesian Network</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A method used in uncertainty prediction, with results discussed in section 2.9.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of network for which dropout is seen as an approximation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-efb0bf44fcf7466c35982fd3bb8ae7ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gal And Ghahramani 2016">
      <data key="d0">Gal And Ghahramani 2016</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A publication that introduced Monte Carlo (MC) dropout.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standard Weight Averaging Technique">
      <data key="d0">Standard Weight Averaging Technique</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A technique that Monte Carlo sampling has been shown to outperform in classification accuracy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Black Box In Figure 2.13(a)">
      <data key="d0">Black Box In Figure 2.13(a)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific region within Figure 2.13(a) that the Bayesian approach could relate to high uncertainty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8952c3b21b80d4712abc62f00e8c46</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Target Network">
      <data key="d0">Target Network</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The neural network being adapted or trained for the target task.&lt;SEP&gt;A separate network used in a variation of the Q-learning procedure, as discussed in Section 5.3.2.&lt;SEP&gt;A model that has undergone transfer learning and fine-tuning on a target task, adapted from a pre-trained network.&lt;SEP&gt;In a Deep Q-Network, the target network provides a stable estimate of Q-values for calculating the Temporal Difference (TD) error, with its weights ($w_{tar}$) updated less frequently or asynchronously from the behavior network's weights.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289199</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Function Approximation-Based Q-Learning">
      <data key="d0">Function Approximation-Based Q-Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A method whose setup is shown in Figure 5.6. It uses techniques like Experience Replay and a separate target network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-Core">
      <data key="d0">C-Core</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">C-core refers to a fundamental component within electromagnetic actuators, characterized by its distinctive C-shape. This core is typically made of magnetic material and serves as the main body of the actuator, enclosing a central region for an air gap. Its design is often optimized using techniques such as Topology Optimization (TO) and Deep Q-Learning, with the primary objective of maximizing the force exerted on an armature.

The C-core has been a subject of study in experiments, where its conventional geometry has been analyzed and different excitation scenarios, sometimes as many as eight, have been tabulated and evaluated using various algorithms. It is considered an example of a design that can be effectively optimized using advanced frameworks, with the reward function in such optimizations often being the force experienced by the armature. In visual representations, the C-core is frequently depicted in light gray or as gray cells, sometimes shown as being open to the right.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec0d779e474774183c36eade5f33db6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca13e3ef7d4bf929b84d6b30574e3485&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1757cd493f052e1f9528255eb34ea59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6504e2cf679305513217b8548174a68b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c48c238aedc178e88e075043138eb72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678d567f82303d2bd1928259fd243036&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a0921c09c7af3fdaeac2a38ff90bbb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288895</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Current Coil">
      <data key="d0">Current Coil</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">An electrical component with 500 turns, set at 1.0 Amps for excitation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3x3 SeqTO Environment">
      <data key="d0">3x3 SeqTO Environment</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific environment configuration with linear magnetic material for which optimal geometry is determined.&lt;SEP&gt;A 3x3 SeqTO environment is the specific setting in which the optimal C-core geometry was determined, as mentioned in Section 5.3.5.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a0921c09c7af3fdaeac2a38ff90bbb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289253</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Linear Magnetic Material">
      <data key="d0">Linear Magnetic Material</data>
      <data key="d1">component</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of material used in the 3x3 SeqTO environment to determine optimal geometry.&lt;SEP&gt;The Linear Magnetic Material is an L-shaped gray structure formed by individual gray squares within the grid, positioned between and around the COIL elements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec0d779e474774183c36eade5f33db6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288893</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GA Algorithm">
      <data key="d0">GA Algorithm</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The GA algorithm is a method used to obtain optimal geometry and achieve a higher performance value.&lt;SEP&gt;The GA (Genetic Algorithm) algorithm is a method of topological optimization used to find optimal flux barrier topologies.&lt;SEP&gt;An algorithm that produced a geometry matching the optimal one found through other methods for the 3x3 SeqTO environment.&lt;SEP&gt;The GA algorithm is a genetic algorithm used for optimization and directly optimized with 'test' case excitation values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5526ac278068ab9cbf72c11fd3817672&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tabular TD-Learning/Q-Learning Algorithm">
      <data key="d0">Tabular TD-Learning/Q-Learning Algorithm</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An algorithm that produced a geometry matching the optimal one found for the 3x3 SeqTO environment, shown in Figure 4.26a.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Random Policy">
      <data key="d0">Random Policy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Used to collect samples for populating the memory buffer, aiding in exploration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;bold&gt;_greedy Policy&lt;/bold&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;bold&gt;_greedy Policy&lt;/bold&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A policy mentioned as being aided by random sampling for exploration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QL-Based Agent">
      <data key="d0">QL-Based Agent</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The agent that begins its learning phase after sufficient random samples have been generated.&lt;SEP&gt;The QL-based agent is a reinforcement learning agent optimized for specific excitation values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FE Function Calls">
      <data key="d0">FE Function Calls</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to Finite Element function calls, of which about 20,000 are required for convergence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285831</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SeqTO-v1 Environment">
      <data key="d0">SeqTO-v1 Environment</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The SeqTO-v1 environment is a testing ground for controllers used in optimal design, particularly for linear material based controllers.&lt;SEP&gt;An earlier version of the SeqTO environment that undergoes modifications.&lt;SEP&gt;The SeqTO-v1 Environment is a context mentioned in the surrounding text, related to the comparison of controller sizes in topology optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5526ac278068ab9cbf72c11fd3817672&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca13e3ef7d4bf929b84d6b30574e3485</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289392</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bellman">
      <data key="d0">Bellman</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Bellman is a person associated with the development of RL algorithms from Dynamic Programming principles in 1957.&lt;SEP&gt;Associated with the introduction of the Markov Decision Process in 1957.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285831</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transition Probability Function P">
      <data key="d0">Transition Probability Function P</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A component of the Markov Decision Process (MDP) that is omitted because any state-action pair leads deterministically to a unique next state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285832</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 4 MDP">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 4 MDP</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to the Markov Decision Process discussed in Chapter 4, particularly Section 4.5.1, which is suitable for RL algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285832</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1x1 Controller">
      <data key="d0">1x1 Controller</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A 1x1 controller is a type of controller used on a SeqTO-vl environment.&lt;SEP&gt;A smaller controller size that would require an overwhelming number of steps and simulations with the current state representation and MDP.&lt;SEP&gt;A 1x1 controller is associated with QL and is capable of placing a thin strip of iron close to the air gap.&lt;SEP&gt;A controller used in the SeqTO-v1 with GA method for optimizing geometry in a non-linear environment, achieving higher force output compared to the 3x3 controller.&lt;SEP&gt;The 1x1 Controller is a specific configuration or type of controller for which the optimized geometry is designed, yielding higher performance and finer granularity compared to a 3x3 controller.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0691c9f7251ff59c05b1bc277ea93e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6719c866b8100236897a8ad6ae78d11f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5526ac278068ab9cbf72c11fd3817672</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288837</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SeqTO-v2 Environment">
      <data key="d0">SeqTO-v2 Environment</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A modified environment developed to mitigate issues with prior state representations and action spaces, including different ways of encoding numerical information.&lt;SEP&gt;The SeqTO-v2 Environment uses an advanced state representation method that incorporates numerical data into the state space to generate complex structures and improve Q-Learning algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0534c4408a52c608a5a24ccf9951f5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288886</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SynRM Geometry">
      <data key="d0">SynRM Geometry</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of geometry to which the changes in SeqTO-v2 are applied, for which action dimensionality remains unchanged from SeqTO-v1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285832</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multi-Channel or 3D Representation">
      <data key="d0">Multi-Channel or 3D Representation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A state representation format where information is stored in channels, described as the most complete.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285832</data>
    </node>
    <node id="First Channel">
      <data key="d0">First Channel</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Part of a multi-channel representation, consisting of material distribution, spatial location, current magnitude, and number of turns for the coil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Second Channel">
      <data key="d0">Second Channel</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Part of a multi-channel representation, consisting of the magnitude of the magnetic field distribution over the same spatial dimension as the first channel.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soft Actions">
      <data key="d0">Soft Actions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An additional set of actions introduced to increase the controller's capability in placing different block sizes.&lt;SEP&gt;Soft actions are an additional set of actions available to the controller, expanding its placement capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Soft Actions refer to a new class of actions (Soft Up, Soft Down, Soft Right, Soft Left) introduced in the evolved action space, allowing for more granular control.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289233</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TO (Topology Optimization)">
      <data key="d0">TO (Topology Optimization)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A design method used for the C-core to maximize the force on the armature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bellman 1957">
      <data key="d0">Bellman 1957</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Bellman 1957 is a reference indicating the origin of Dynamic Programming principles.&lt;SEP&gt;The person associated with the concept of the Markov Decision Process (MDP).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GA (Genetic Algorithm)">
      <data key="d0">GA (Genetic Algorithm)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">GA is a heuristic search algorithm used for topology optimization, employed on the SeqTO-v1 environment.&lt;SEP&gt;An optimization algorithm mentioned as an alternative method for obtaining optimal geometry.&lt;SEP&gt;GA, or Genetic Algorithm, is a method discussed in the context of topology optimization of the C-core.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1757cd493f052e1f9528255eb34ea59</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289163</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tabular TDlearning">
      <data key="d0">Tabular TDlearning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An algorithm that, along with GA and Q-Learning, provides a geometry that matches the optimal geometry.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Random Sampling">
      <data key="d0">Random Sampling</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A technique used to populate the memory buffer, helping with exploration initially.&lt;SEP&gt;Random sampling is a mathematical operation where an action $a$ is drawn uniformly at random from a discrete set of available actions, denoted as $a \\sim (a_1, \\ldots, a_k)$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e23213a2c2ccb19eb502826a6f80accd</data>
