    <node id="Rotor">
      <data key="d0">Rotor</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Rotor is a critical rotating component found in various types of electric motors, prominently in Synchronous Reluctance Motors (SynRM) and Interior Permanent Magnet (IPM) motors. Its fundamental function is to move to a position of least reluctance relative to the stator.

In SynRM applications, the rotor is often subject to topology optimization for design, with notable instances including developments using the SeqTO-v1 and modifications from conventional to discretized and optimized states, as illustrated in various configurations like Figure 4.28. In these motors, the rotor is typically depicted as an inner component, sometimes with a light blue-grey base material structured with a 5x5 grid for optimization purposes.

For IPM motors, the Rotor is situated beneath the stator and is generally light grey. It contains internal components, most notably permanent magnets. More generally, motor designs suggest the rotor as a darker gray, rounded element located near the center, incorporating elements like permanent magnets or magnetic barriers. Specific depictions showcase a central component with four prominent rectangular magnet sections, symmetrically arranged around a central circular hole, often colored blue and red, within the motor cross-section.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff320ec3e3a24e3c69d31630b6cba885&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2e27a9a79daf5a813690efa01766eca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b7e7c8729e5eef7f4ff08e29b1d0014&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b7b73719952876f4f9c0e6b4efe0027&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5526ac278068ab9cbf72c11fd3817672&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e55225b1f173db3033115aeae970db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288862</data>
    </node>
