    <node id="MDP Formulation">
      <data key="d0">MDP Formulation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The MDP (Markov Decision Process) formulation is a flexible and abstract framework that can be designed in various ways for a given task.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285864</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electromagnetic Problem">
      <data key="d0">Electromagnetic Problem</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An electromagnetic problem involves the accurate modeling and analysis of electric devices, which is challenging due to nonlinearities and complex geometries.&lt;SEP&gt;The Electromagnetic Problem refers to the system dynamics that need to be understood for formulating an MDP for a task.&lt;SEP&gt;The electromagnetic problem refers to the context of the C-core design optimization where this equation helps define the long-term goals.&lt;SEP&gt;An Electromagnetic Problem is suggested by the presence of coils and magnetic field measurements, which the design domain aims to optimize.&lt;SEP&gt;An electromagnetic problem is the broader context for the design optimization, hinted at by the presence of 'COIL' components.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1474f4155ebb5657d8ca7f3c0d29b49&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289123</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Value Functions">
      <data key="d0">Value Functions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Functions that estimate the "goodness" of a state or action in reinforcement learning.&lt;SEP&gt;Value Functions formalize the notion of achieving the most reward in the long term, classified into Action value function and State value function.&lt;SEP&gt;Value Functions ($Q(s,a)$ or $V(s)$) are mathematical concepts introduced later in the text (Section 4.5.2) as formalizations of achieving the most reward in future state trajectories, building upon the concept of return.&lt;SEP&gt;Value functions ($Q(s,a)$ and $V(s)$) are concepts in reinforcement learning related to evaluating an agent's actions and states.&lt;SEP&gt;Value Functions ($Q(s,a)$ and $V(s)$) are estimations or expectations of the Return ($G_t$) under specific policies. They quantify the goodness of states or state-action pairs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-906a75be5f88ad37713839d335d1720c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289123</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action Value Function Q(s,a)">
      <data key="d0">Action Value Function Q(s,a)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Action Value Function Q(s,a) measures all the rewards an agent receives until the end of an episode, starting from state 's' and taking action 'a'.&lt;SEP&gt;An estimate for the expected return Gt, related to the value of taking a specific action in a specific state, used in Temporal Difference learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289245</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State Value Function V(s)">
      <data key="d0">State Value Function V(s)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The State Value Function V(s) is the expected value of all possible future rewards across all possible actions from a given state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monte Carlo (MC) Based Methods">
      <data key="d0">Monte Carlo (MC) Based Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Monte Carlo (MC) based methods are learning algorithms that wait until the end of an episode to update the value function based on the absolute return.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bootstrapping-Based Methods">
      <data key="d0">Bootstrapping-Based Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Bootstrapping-Based Methods, such as TD-learning, learn incrementally throughout an episode by updating estimates based on other estimates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Singh and Sutton (1996)">
      <data key="d0">Singh and Sutton (1996)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A reference cited in the text regarding Monte Carlo methods and their limitations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sutton and Barto (2018)">
      <data key="d0">Sutton and Barto (2018)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A reference cited in the text regarding bootstrapping-based methods and their convergence properties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285866</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action Value Function Q(S,A)">
      <data key="d0">Action Value Function Q(S,A)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Action Value Function Q(s,a) measures all rewards an agent receives until the end of an episode, starting from state 's' and performing action 'u'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State Value Function V(S)">
      <data key="d0">State Value Function V(S)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The State Value Function V(s) represents the expected value of all possible future rewards across all possible actions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bootstrapping">
      <data key="d0">Bootstrapping</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Bootstrapping is a fundamental technique within reinforcement learning, particularly characteristic of Temporal Difference (TD) learning methods. It refers to an approach where updates to estimated values are based on other estimated values, rather than actual, observed returns. This allows for recursive and incremental updates, often enabling learning to occur mid-episode without waiting for the full sum of rewards to be observed.

Specifically, in bootstrapping, an estimate of future returns, such as the value function V(s') or Q(s', a') for the next state, is used to update current estimates of the return. This concept is embodied in equations like the Q-Learning Equation, where the update for a value estimate relies on an estimate of the value of the next state. The target for an update in bootstrapping is, therefore, based on an existing estimate rather than an actual outcome, making it a key feature that distinguishes TD learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28d95920dc2d89112c02d6c86f6d7e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289214</data>
    </node>
