    <node id="Machine Learning Approach">
      <data key="d0">Machine Learning Approach</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A machine learning approach is a potential candidate for describing performance maps, provided issues like generalization and transfer learning can be resolved.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-540fc8a4d14fea776a933c31f85916ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Markov">
      <data key="d0">Markov</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Markov is associated with the Markov property (Markov and Nagornyi 1951).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nagornyi">
      <data key="d0">Nagornyi</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Nagornyi is associated with the Markov property (Markov and Nagornyi 1951).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Silver Et Al. 2017">
      <data key="d0">Silver Et Al. 2017</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Silver et al. 2017 refers to a body of work that allows the relaxation of the Markov property.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Domain Information">
      <data key="d0">Design Domain Information</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Design domain information at an instant 't^gamma' is used as a state in a learning process, which breaks the Markov property.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coil Space">
      <data key="d0">Coil Space</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The coil space is a location the agent might try to enter, identified as a negative action.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Gradient">
      <data key="d0">Policy Gradient</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Policy Gradient is a class of algorithms in reinforcement learning, with improvements discussed.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy Gradient (PG) is a paradigm of Reinforcement Learning algorithm that learns by evaluating which action to prefer, rather than evaluating the value of each action.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy Gradient is a reinforcement learning algorithm depicted as a vertical flow with an input of state and action pair (s, a), and an output denoting the policy that gives the probability of selecting an action 'a' given a state 's' and parameters 'θ'.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy Gradient (PG) is a type of reinforcement learning algorithm that updates the weights of a neural network directly by optimizing a policy, rather than estimating action values.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy Gradient (PG) is a machine learning algorithm, specifically in reinforcement learning, that processes a given state to determine the probabilities for various actions. It is noted for skipping the Q-Value function part and directly outputting action probabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy Gradient is a method in Reinforcement Learning for optimizing the parameters of a policy function using gradient-based techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy Gradient (PG) refers to a class of reinforcement learning algorithms that optimize a parameterized policy by following the gradient of some performance measure with respect to the policy parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66f8a9943508aa952a9845858b8d3374&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d057a6f40f6119ac37674e31ce09f40a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Learning Algorithm">
      <data key="d0">Q-Learning Algorithm</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Q-Learning algorithm learns by trying to find the action-value function (Q-Value function) for each state.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific algorithm within Reinforcement Learning used by agents to balance exploration and exploitation in an environment to maximize reward signals.&lt;SEP&gt;The Q-Learning algorithm is a Reinforcement Learning technique that aims to find each state's action-value function, from which a policy is derived.&lt;SEP&gt;The equation represents a core update rule in the Q-learning algorithm, a model-free reinforcement learning technique.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-808c974f7871ba6b7b5d18f72afc85a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec4e495c59f004fcbe4475de4b2bcbd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58674fc78b8e0f9b48c3a573d49dd684&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293034</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Value Function">
      <data key="d0">Q-Value Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Q-Value function is an action-value function that the Q-Learning algorithm tries to find.&lt;SEP&gt;The Q-value function, denoted as $Q(s, a)$, represents the expected cumulative discounted reward of taking action $a$ in state $s$ and then following an optimal policy.&lt;SEP&gt;The Q-Value Function ($Q(s, a)$) quantifies the quality or expected total discounted future reward of taking action `a` in state `s`.&lt;SEP&gt;The Q-Value Function is an intermediate step in Q-Learning that Policy Gradient algorithms bypass when determining action probabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;The Q-value function, denoted as $Q(s, a)$, represents the expected return when starting in state $s$, taking action $a$, and following a particular policy thereafter. It quantifies the 'goodness' of a specific action in a specific state.&lt;SEP&gt;The Q-value function, denoted as Qπ(s, a), represents the expected return for taking action 'a' in state 's' under policy 'π'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-808c974f7871ba6b7b5d18f72afc85a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66f8a9943508aa952a9845858b8d3374&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8823695e17798553515e243bdbfda2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RL Algorithm">
      <data key="d0">RL Algorithm</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">RL (Reinforcement Learning) Algorithm is a class of algorithms that Policy Gradient belongs to.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neural Net">
      <data key="d0">Neural Net</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The neural net is a component whose weights are changed based on the "performance measure" in Policy Gradient.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy (π)">
      <data key="d0">Policy (π)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The policy (π) is what the action selection probability p(a) is derived from. It is trained stochastically in PG.&lt;SEP&gt;Pi (π) represents the agent's strategy, defining how it chooses actions given the current state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289112</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Learning Rate (α)">
      <data key="d0">Learning Rate (α)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The learning rate (α) is a parameter used in updating the weights of the policy network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parameters (θ)">
      <data key="d0">Parameters (θ)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Parameters (θ) represent the weights associated with the neural network of the policy.&lt;SEP&gt;Parameters ($\\boldsymbol{\\theta}$) are a set of values, typically weights and biases of a neural network, that define a policy in deep reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289155</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probability Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Probability Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The probability distribution over the action space determines the amount of randomness in action selection for a stochastic policy.&lt;SEP&gt;A probability distribution over possible actions given the current state, represented by the stochastic policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conditional Probability">
      <data key="d0">Conditional Probability</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Conditional probability is considered when deciding whether to explore actions in a given state, especially when two actions have comparable probabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Indicates a probability distribution of one variable given the values of other variables.&lt;SEP&gt;The equation expresses a conditional probability of 'y' given 'x', 'X', and 'Y'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-215b04d60447db6b473faf768914326f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="On-Policy Algorithm">
      <data key="d0">On-Policy Algorithm</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An on-policy algorithm learns only using state-action transitions made by the current active policy, such as PG.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.12">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.12</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.12 visually represents various combinations of item placements and the operation of actions.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5.12 is an image illustrating agent movement with the SeqTO-v2 controller, showing a sequence of actions ('Right', 'Soft Right', 'Soft Down', 'Down') within a grid-based environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-596bd8f0cbcfc00de61fa772b9317edc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288873</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Table 5.3">
      <data key="d0">Table 5.3</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Table 5.3 mentions the complete set of actions available to the controller at any step in an episode.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 5.3 is a table titled "Evolved action space of the controller (SeqTO-v2)" which lists eight distinct actions available to the controller.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289230</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Markov And Nagornyi 1951">
      <data key="d0">Markov And Nagornyi 1951</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Markov and Nagornyi 1951 is a reference to the origin or a significant work regarding the Markov property.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285878</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.13">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.13</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.13 illustrates the differentiation of an agent trying to enter the coil space as a bad action.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5.13 is an image that illustrates the possibility of a design error due to a controller moving out of its designated design space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1b166c346deb9e14c2fe057bc33cdbf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289086</data>
    </node>
    <node id="History">
      <data key="d0">History</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">History refers to past states or actions that are incorporated into the current state to address the breaking of the Markov property.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285878</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action-Value Function">
      <data key="d0">Action-Value Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Action-Value Function, also known as the Q-Value function or Q-function, is a fundamental concept in reinforcement learning that quantifies the "goodness" of taking a specific action in a given state. It mathematically expresses the expected return an agent can anticipate by starting in a particular state, taking a specific action, and subsequently following a defined policy.

This function represents the expected cumulative future reward that an agent can receive. It is commonly denoted in several ways, including $Q_\pi(s, a)$ for the true action-value under policy $\pi$, $Q(s, a)$, or $\hat{Q}(s, a)$ for its estimated form. The notation $Q_\pi(S_i, A_i)$ explicitly refers to the expected return by starting in state $S_i$, taking action $A_i$, and then following policy $\pi$.

The Action-Value Function can be estimated by various methods, including the output of a Feedforward Neural Network, or more broadly, by a neural network, often represented as $\hat{Q}(s, a, w)$, where $w$ denotes the network's weights. In actor-critic architectures, it can be estimated by the Critic network. In some contexts, it can be considered a 'score' function, $f(\boldsymbol{s}, a)$, which generates a real-valued output indicating a preference for an action when given a current state $\boldsymbol{s}$ and a potential action $a$ as inputs. This estimation process is a crucial aspect of many reinforcement learning algorithms, including Q-Learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb622c9ce0600f3539c3ed7199e0c0ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288822</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Learning Procedure">
      <data key="d0">Learning Procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The learning procedure in Q-Learning involves figuring out the quality of each possible action to select the best one.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285878</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bootstrapped Action Value">
      <data key="d0">Bootstrapped Action Value</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Bootstrapped action value ($R + Q(s', a')$) is a metric used for updates in the Q-learning algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Performance Measure (J)">
      <data key="d0">Performance Measure (J)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The performance measure (J) is a metric in Policy Gradient responsible for making changes in the neural network's weights.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rewards">
      <data key="d0">Rewards</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Rewards are accumulated by the agent in an episode and serve as a good performance measurement for maximizing overall collected rewards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neural Network Weights">
      <data key="d0">Neural Network Weights</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Neural network weights are parameters that are optimized based on the performance criteria in Policy Gradient.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probability Of Action Selection (P(a))">
      <data key="d0">Probability Of Action Selection (P(a))</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The probability of action selection (p(a)) depends on the policy and influences the gradient of the performance measure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy (Pi)">
      <data key="d0">Policy (Pi)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The policy (π) dictates the probability of action selection in a given state, specifically in Policy Gradient algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gradient Of J(Theta)">
      <data key="d0">Gradient Of J(Theta)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The gradient of J(θ) is calculated to update the weights of the neural network in Policy Gradient.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Learning Rate (Alpha)">
      <data key="d0">Learning Rate (Alpha)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The learning rate (α) is a parameter used in updating the weights of the policy network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parameters (Theta)">
      <data key="d0">Parameters (Theta)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Parameters (θ) represent the weights associated with the neural network of the policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exploration &amp; Exploitation">
      <data key="d0">Exploration &amp; Exploitation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Exploration &amp; Exploitation is a fundamental concept in reinforcement learning concerning balancing trying new actions versus using known good actions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285882</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QL/DQN">
      <data key="d0">QL/DQN</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">QL/DQN refers to Q-Learning and Deep Q-Networks, which are reinforcement learning algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285882</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probability Distribution Over The Action Space">
      <data key="d0">Probability Distribution Over The Action Space</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The probability distribution over the action space determines the amount of randomness in action selection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285882</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.15">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.15</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.15 showcases a scenario where two actions have comparable conditional probabilities, suggesting exploration.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5.15 is a visual representation titled "Policy Gradient implementation."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66f8a9943508aa952a9845858b8d3374</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289200</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epsilon Parameter">
      <data key="d0">Epsilon Parameter</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The epsilon parameter determines the rate of exploration in Q-learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285883</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.16 (A)">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.16 (A)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.16 (a) illustrates the greedy policy of the Q-Learning algorithm, always choosing the action with label two.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285883</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.16 (B)">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.16 (B)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.16 (b) shows how A2C samples all actions based on their probabilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285883</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action Labels 2 &amp; 3">
      <data key="d0">Action Labels 2 &amp; 3</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Action labels 2 &amp; 3 refer to specific actions that have comparatively high conditional probability in a given state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285883</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Improvement To Policy Gradient">
      <data key="d0">Improvement To Policy Gradient</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Improvement to Policy Gradient is a section discussing enhancements to the PG algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285883</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Experience Replay Memory">
      <data key="d0">Experience Replay Memory</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Experience Replay Memory is a component in DQN that stores and reuses past experiences for learning, which is not present in on-policy algorithms like PG.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7133594b2e19b403575b42aef1cafffb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285884</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Airbus 380">
      <data key="d0">Airbus 380</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Airbus 380 is an aircraft for which droop nose ribs were designed using Topology Optimization.&lt;SEP&gt;The Airbus 380 is an aircraft for which the design of droop nose ribs was optimized using Topology Optimization, leading to reduced weight and required mechanical performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f237e58ddc202b94dc8ea5b3ef7e3145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f3d168f1b6a8020e0606ef52cd19390</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285885</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Generative Design">
      <data key="d0">Generative Design</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Generative Design is an advanced design method, similar to Topology Optimization, that industries are increasingly employing for future products.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f3d168f1b6a8020e0606ef52cd19390</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285885</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Structural Mechanics">
      <data key="d0">Structural Mechanics</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Structural Mechanics is an engineering discipline that, along with electromagnetics and heat transfer, is investigated during the multiphysics analysis in the optimization process. This Concept is used during design optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f3d168f1b6a8020e0606ef52cd19390</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285885</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Layout Design of Trusses">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Layout Design of Trusses</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Optimal Layout Design of Trusses is a sub-field of engineering that saw renewed interest in the 1950s and 1960s, contributing to the development of Topology Optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f3d168f1b6a8020e0606ef52cd19390</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285885</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimized Material Distribution Method">
      <data key="d0">Optimized Material Distribution Method</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Optimized Material Distribution method was proposed by Dyck and Lowther in 1996 for applying Topology Optimization to electromagnetic (EM) fields.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f3d168f1b6a8020e0606ef52cd19390</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285885</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sizing Optimization">
      <data key="d0">Sizing Optimization</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Sizing optimization is a design optimization methodology where designs are parameterized by geometric variables such as thickness, width, and length.&lt;SEP&gt;A parametric optimization method where designs are parameterized by a few geometric variables like thickness, width, and length.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f3d168f1b6a8020e0606ef52cd19390&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285885</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shape Optimization">
      <data key="d0">Shape Optimization</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Shape Optimization is a design optimization methodology that focuses on optimizing the boundary of a design.&lt;SEP&gt;A design optimization method that optimizes the boundary and cross-section of structural components, leading to changes in size and cross-section.&lt;SEP&gt;A design optimization technique aimed at finding the best shape for a system or component.&lt;SEP&gt;Shape Optimization is a type of optimal design problem where the boundaries or outline of an object are modified while maintaining its overall topology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-403239ec5550f149ced3802b6c18600b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66f6813286ff2495396939f94deff0c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f3d168f1b6a8020e0606ef52cd19390&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289286</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Krog, Tucker, Rollema, et al. 2002">
      <data key="d0">Krog, Tucker, Rollema, et al. 2002</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">This work describes the design of droop nose ribs for the Airbus 380 using Topology Optimization, achieving weight reduction and mechanical performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f3d168f1b6a8020e0606ef52cd19390</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285885</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Atkinson and Ezell 2019">
      <data key="d0">Atkinson and Ezell 2019</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">This work is cited in relation to industries looking towards advanced design methods like topology optimization and generative design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f3d168f1b6a8020e0606ef52cd19390</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285887</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coop 2018">
      <data key="d0">Coop 2018</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">This work is cited in relation to industries looking towards advanced design methods like topology optimization and generative design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f3d168f1b6a8020e0606ef52cd19390</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285887</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Waide and Brunner 2011">
      <data key="d0">Waide and Brunner 2011</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">This work estimates that electrical machines use about 43%-46% of total electricity generated globally, leading to significant CO2 emissions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f3d168f1b6a8020e0606ef52cd19390</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285888</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Michell 1904">
      <data key="d0">Michell 1904</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Michell's 1904 paper is referenced as the origin of the theoretical description of topology optimization in the mechanical domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f3d168f1b6a8020e0606ef52cd19390</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285888</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cox 1958">
      <data key="d0">Cox 1958</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Cox 1958 is cited for work focused on the optimal layout design of trusses and as a significant publication in layout theory research during the 1960s and 1970s.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6f3d168f1b6a8020e0606ef52cd19390</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285888</data>
    </node>
