    <node id="The Bulletin Of Mathematical Biophysics">
      <data key="d0">The Bulletin Of Mathematical Biophysics</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">This is a journal that published the paper "A logical calculus of the ideas immanent in nervous activity."&lt;SEP&gt;The bulletin of mathematical biophysics is a publication that featured "A logical calculus of the ideas immanent in nervous activity".</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5f63a01dc7031890756e399578cff0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56168e4e7d535f0d2b13e88db7d37f2d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285926</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TD Learning">
      <data key="d0">TD Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">TD Learning (Temporal Difference Learning) is a reinforcement learning algorithm, involving Markov Decision Process, value functions, and policy functions.&lt;SEP&gt;TD Learning is a reinforcement learning method that reduces variance in return by adjusting rewards based on their importance over time, using a discount factor to stabilize learning. It learns directly from experience and combines ideas from Dynamic Programming and Monte Carlo-based learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285926</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discount Factor">
      <data key="d0">Discount Factor</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The discount factor, denoted by $\gamma$, is a parameter used in TD Learning to weigh rewards based on their importance over time, reducing the influence of future rewards on current value function estimates.&lt;SEP&gt;A discount factor is a mathematical component typically incorporated into value functions for infinite-horizon problems to weight future rewards less than immediate rewards.&lt;SEP&gt;The discount factor, denoted as gamma ($\gamma$), is a scalar value between 0 and 1 that controls the importance of future rewards relative to immediate rewards in reinforcement learning.&lt;SEP&gt;The discount factor is denoted by gamma (γ) and determines the present value of future rewards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec4e495c59f004fcbe4475de4b2bcbd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293034</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monte Carlo-based Learning">
      <data key="d0">Monte Carlo-based Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Monte Carlo-based Learning is a type of learning that, like TD learning, learns directly from the experience generated by an agent's interaction with the environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285928</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Evaluation">
      <data key="d0">Policy Evaluation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Policy Evaluation is a task in reinforcement learning used to understand an agent-environment interaction by considering the reward signal. It determines the value function for a specific policy.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy Evaluation is a process in reinforcement learning to assess the value of a given policy.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy evaluation is a step in reinforcement learning to assess the value of a policy.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy Evaluation is a process in reinforcement learning to estimate the action-value function for a given policy.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy Evaluation is a discussion point on page 102 related to how agent uses value function information to make decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy evaluation is a use case where the sequence is used to evaluate the Q-value function for a given policy π.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca0b752852f034cec7766c7e6e92af67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288955</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Control / Policy Improvement">
      <data key="d0">Control / Policy Improvement</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Control / Policy Improvement involves developing algorithms that search the space of behaviors to maximize reward signals, which means finding a policy that maximizes the value function.&lt;SEP&gt;Control / Policy Improvement is a discussion point on page 102 related to how agent uses value function information to derive an optimal policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca0b752852f034cec7766c7e6e92af67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289330</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Q(s, a) Function">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Q(s, a) Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The optimal Q(s, a) function provides the optimal policy, specifically representing the best action at every state.&lt;SEP&gt;The Optimal Q(s, a) Function represents the maximum expected future reward achievable by taking action 'a' in state 's' and acting optimally thereafter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289308</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Watkins and Dayan 1992">
      <data key="d0">Watkins and Dayan 1992</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Watkins and Dayan 1992 refers to the publication where Q-Learning was developed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Learning Rate">
      <data key="d0">Learning Rate</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Learning Rate is a crucial hyperparameter in machine learning and optimization algorithms, denoted by $\alpha$ (alpha). It is a positive scalar that plays a fundamental role in controlling the step size of parameter updates during the iterative process of minimizing a loss function. This parameter directly influences how quickly the model's policy parameters or weights adjust, thereby impacting the speed, stability, and convergence of the learning process.

In contexts such as TD-learning (Temporal Difference learning), the learning rate acts as a step-size parameter, typically with a value between 0 and 1. Its function here is to determine the magnitude of the step taken toward a new target, ultimately reducing the error in an estimate. The learning rate is often reduced or decayed during fine-tuning or re-training phases of a model to allow for more precise adjustments as the model approaches an optimal state. For instance, in a specific experiment, the learning rate was explicitly set to 0.8 within the TD-learning algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b6f3bea48fce928809f6ddf7db422c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ε-greedy Approach">
      <data key="d0">ε-greedy Approach</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The $\epsilon$-greedy approach is a stochastic rule used by Q-Learning to balance the dilemma of exploration and exploitation by allowing the agent to improve its knowledge about each action (exploration) and choose the action with the highest expected Q-Value (exploitation).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MC-Based Methods">
      <data key="d0">MC-Based Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">MC-based methods are learning approaches that, along with TD learning, learn directly from the experience generated by an agent's interaction with the environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Q Function">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Q Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Optimal Q ( s,a ) function provides the agent with the optimal action based on a policy starting in a given state and continuing until the end of an episode. Knowing it gives the optimal policy automatically.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$\epsilon$ -Greedy Approach">
      <data key="d0">$\epsilon$ -Greedy Approach</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The $\epsilon$ -greedy approach is a stochastic rule used in Q-learning to balance exploration and exploitation, where $\epsilon$ -greedy exploration helps in decision-making for the QL-based controller.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Online Learning Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Online Learning Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Q-Learning is identified as one of the major online learning algorithms, which denotes a type of algorithm that learns iteratively from data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Watkins and Dayan">
      <data key="d0">Watkins and Dayan</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Watkins and Dayan are individuals credited with developing Q-Learning in 1989.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 3.19">
      <data key="d0">Figure 3.19</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A visual representation referenced in the text, using colors to denote changes in weights.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 3.19 is a visual representation of performance data that serves as a comparison point for changes in weights.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 3.19 is an image titled: "Change in weights of each layer: Efficiency map to Power factor map (IPM to IPM)". It contains a flow diagram of a neural network and two heat map-style contour plots.&lt;SEP&gt;A visual representation used to display changes in weights of transfer-learned neural network layers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f237e58ddc202b94dc8ea5b3ef7e3145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288721</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Section 3.7.2">
      <data key="d0">Section 3.7.2</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Section 3.7.2 discusses the distribution of weight changes in network layers and forms the basis for hypotheses related to knowledge transfer.&lt;SEP&gt;A section in the document where related discussions are located.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f237e58ddc202b94dc8ea5b3ef7e3145</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Normalized RMSE">
      <data key="d0">Normalized RMSE</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Normalized Root Mean Square Error is a metric used to evaluate model performance, specifically the error per point in a pf map.&lt;SEP&gt;Normalized Root Mean Squared Error, a measure of prediction accuracy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f237e58ddc202b94dc8ea5b3ef7e3145</data>
