    </node>
    <node id="High Accuracy">
      <data key="d0">High Accuracy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">High accuracy is an attribute of the models' ability to predict efficiency maps and act as surrogates for FE simulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f237e58ddc202b94dc8ea5b3ef7e3145</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Gradient Algorithm">
      <data key="d0">Policy Gradient Algorithm</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Policy Gradient (PG) algorithm is an on-policy reinforcement learning algorithm that learns using only state-action transitions made by the current active policy.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy Gradient Algorithm is a type of algorithm in Reinforcement Learning that directly searches in the policy space using gradient-based updates to learn an optimal policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e3e1dd6f2a21d7997b5fa281b76b709</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288853</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Q-Network">
      <data key="d0">Deep Q-Network</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">DQN is a reinforcement learning method that utilizes 'Experience Replay' memory.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific Deep Reinforcement Learning architecture that utilizes deep neural networks to approximate the Q-function, often with separate behavior and target networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e3e1dd6f2a21d7997b5fa281b76b709</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289267</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Actor-Critic">
      <data key="d0">Actor-Critic</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Actor-Critic (AC) is a combined reinforcement learning method that merges Policy Gradient and value functions, consisting of two learning components: Actor and Critic.&lt;SEP&gt;Actor-Critic is an algorithm in Reinforcement Learning where an 'Actor' learns the policy and a 'Critic' evaluates chosen actions or states.&lt;SEP&gt;Actor-Critic (A2C) is a type of Reinforcement Learning algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2e3e1dd6f2a21d7997b5fa281b76b709&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289065</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Actor">
      <data key="d0">Actor</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Actor is a component within the Actor-Critic method that learns the policy, determining the behavior/action of the agent.&lt;SEP&gt;The Actor in Actor-Critic (AC) methods determines the policy.&lt;SEP&gt;The Actor is a node within the Agent that determines the agent's behavior and outputs an action 'a' to the Environment.&lt;SEP&gt;The Actor is a component of the Advantage Actor-Critic (A2C) model responsible for Policy Gradient, producing probability distributions over possible actions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27909faa34224de8c011e982c98fcef6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e3e1dd6f2a21d7997b5fa281b76b709&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9b48425c0c345b3be14ed824c6579b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289071</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Critic">
      <data key="d0">Critic</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Critic is a component within the Actor-Critic method that learns the Q-Value of each state and action pair.&lt;SEP&gt;The Critic in Actor-Critic (AC) methods evaluates chosen actions or states.&lt;SEP&gt;The Critic is a node within the Agent, located in the top-left portion, that evaluates actions or the current state and provides feedback.&lt;SEP&gt;The Critic is a component of the Advantage Actor-Critic (A2C) model responsible for Value Prediction, estimating Q-values based on states and actions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27909faa34224de8c011e982c98fcef6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e3e1dd6f2a21d7997b5fa281b76b709&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9b48425c0c345b3be14ed824c6579b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289071</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Softmax">
      <data key="d0">Softmax</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Softmax is an approach of policy parametrization used in action preferences.&lt;SEP&gt;A function used in the Actor network's output layer for distinct classes.&lt;SEP&gt;Softmax is a method used for the Actor's output to generate a probability distribution over distinct classes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2e3e1dd6f2a21d7997b5fa281b76b709&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5dae035c707962e5992f8ffb5b5c0c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288793</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advantage">
      <data key="d0">Advantage</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Advantage, denoted as $A(s,a)$, is a performance metric used in Advantage Actor Critic, defined by subtracting $V(s)$ from $Q(s,a)$. It quantifies how much better or worse taking a particular action is compared to acting according to the policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2e3e1dd6f2a21d7997b5fa281b76b709</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285941</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Value Function V(s)">
      <data key="d0">Value Function V(s)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Value Function $V(s)$ represents the value of a state and is used in the calculation of 'Advantage'.&lt;SEP&gt;The Value function V(s) represents the expected return from a state.&lt;SEP&gt;An estimate for the expected return Gt, used in Temporal Difference learning to update estimates incrementally.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2e3e1dd6f2a21d7997b5fa281b76b709&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289245</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mnih et al. 2016">
      <data key="d0">Mnih et al. 2016</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">This refers to a publication that describes the 'Advantage' function for indicating how much better or worse taking an action is compared to acting according to the policy.&lt;SEP&gt;Mnih et al. 2016 refers to a publication that discusses the Actor-Critic model and A2C. (Paper: Asynchronous Methods for Deep Reinforcement Learning)</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e3e1dd6f2a21d7997b5fa281b76b709</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285941</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Value">
      <data key="d0">Q-Value</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Q-value, often represented as Q(s, a) or Q_π(s_t, a_t), is a fundamental concept in reinforcement learning. It quantifies the expected return or utility of taking a specific action (a or a_t) from a particular state (s or s_t). This value is generally learned through training and serves as a numerical representation found in a Q-table or as a learned parameter/network.

The Q-value estimates the reward received from a state and action pair, followed by a greedy policy until the end of an episode. It is crucial for decision-making, particularly in algorithms like Q-Learning, where it indicates the estimated utility of performing a certain action from a given state. In the Actor-Critic method, the Q-value is used by the Critic component to evaluate the value of a state-action pair.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2ceb1ac6e54fec53c601d8b38f2b338d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f94c2e92a45229c63d7fd85dceb68356&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e3e1dd6f2a21d7997b5fa281b76b709&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8823695e17798553515e243bdbfda2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-742edca401668538e3a7a9b93ec14162&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e5324f5a16fd6822c647598c8e749b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca0b752852f034cec7766c7e6e92af67</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293091</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State's Value Function">
      <data key="d0">State's Value Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The state's Value function, denoted as V(s), is a concept referenced in Section 4.5.2 and used in the definition of 'Advantage'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2e3e1dd6f2a21d7997b5fa281b76b709</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Parametrization">
      <data key="d0">Policy Parametrization</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Policy parametrization defines how a policy is expressed, such as softmax in action preferences, and requires the policy to be differentiable with respect to its parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2e3e1dd6f2a21d7997b5fa281b76b709</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MC Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">MC Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">MC (Monte Carlo) algorithms are methods that encounter similar issues as PG regarding delayed updates to weights.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2e3e1dd6f2a21d7997b5fa281b76b709</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285944</data>
    </node>
