    <node id="Computer Simulations">
      <data key="d0">Computer Simulations</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Computer simulations are used in engineering product design to provide insights into performance under different requirements and constraints.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Virtual Prototyping">
      <data key="d0">Virtual Prototyping</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Virtual prototyping is a process where product performance details are obtained without building a physical structure, heavily relying on computer simulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Data-Driven Statistical Learning Methods">
      <data key="d0">Data-Driven Statistical Learning Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">These methods are used to reduce computational burden in simulations by developing surrogate models or reducing the number of simulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maxwells Equations">
      <data key="d0">Maxwells Equations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Maxwell's equations are a set of partial differential equations that, together with the Lorentz force law, form the foundation of classical electromagnetism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probabilistic Model">
      <data key="d0">Probabilistic Model</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A probabilistic model is introduced to enhance prediction accuracy and quantify uncertainty, utilizing Monte Carlo dropout.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monte Carlo Dropout">
      <data key="d0">Monte Carlo Dropout</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Monte Carlo dropout is a technique used to improve accuracy and quantify uncertainty in probabilistic models.&lt;SEP&gt;Monte Carlo Dropout is a technique that approximates Bayesian inference in Gaussian processes, enabling sampling from an approximation of the posterior distribution over weights by performing dropout during the test phase.&lt;SEP&gt;A practical implementation that approximates Bayesian inference, providing a computationally efficient way to sample from an approximate posterior distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;Monte Carlo Dropout is a technique used to compute uncertainty maps by retrieving 10 Monte Carlo samples and calculating the standard deviation over softmax outputs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92cc2b02ae3a119d92f0d1a7f6617885&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288907</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Finite Element Solution">
      <data key="d0">Finite Element Solution</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A conventional computational method used to validate the accuracy of the proposed deep learning methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reinforcement Learning Based Agent">
      <data key="d0">Reinforcement Learning Based Agent</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">This agent, trained on a Topology Optimization environment, uses tree search algorithms to yield manufacturable solutions efficiently.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topology Optimization Method">
      <data key="d0">Topology Optimization Method</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A novel topology optimization method that transforms the material distribution problem into a search problem for a movement sequence of a controller in design space, reinforcing connectivity between cells of the discretised design domain.&lt;SEP&gt;A new method proposed in the work that transforms material distribution into a movement sequence search problem for a controller.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-efb0bf44fcf7466c35982fd3bb8ae7ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Material Distribution Problem">
      <data key="d0">Material Distribution Problem</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A problem in design that the Topology Optimization method transforms into a movement sequence search problem.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of problem into which topology optimization transforms the design optimization problem.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Movement Sequence Search Problem">
      <data key="d0">Movement Sequence Search Problem</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">This is the transformed problem from material distribution, solved by a controller in the design space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Controller Moving in Design Space">
      <data key="d0">Controller Moving in Design Space</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">An entity used in the Topology Optimization method to search movement sequences.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Data-Driven Reinforcement Learning Based Agent">
      <data key="d0">Data-Driven Reinforcement Learning Based Agent</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of tree search algorithm trained on the proposed Topology Optimization environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topology Optimization Based Environment">
      <data key="d0">Topology Optimization Based Environment</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The environment on which the data-driven Reinforcement Learning based agent is trained.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285984</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Reinforcement Learning Based Agent">
      <data key="d0">Deep Reinforcement Learning Based Agent</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">After training, this agent can yield manufacturable solutions without extensive iterative analysis, reducing simulation needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285984</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Engineering">
      <data key="d0">Engineering</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The broad field in which product design relies on computer simulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a90b78ebd0801e79ae02815c13eb5b33</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Goodfellow Et Al. 2014">
      <data key="d0">Goodfellow Et Al. 2014</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Reference for previous work on Deep Neural Networks and initialization techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;Goodfellow Et Al. 2014 is a referenced publication for the concept of greedy layer-wise pretraining.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNN Weights">
      <data key="d0">DNN Weights</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Parameters within a Deep Neural Network that are trained.&lt;SEP&gt;DNN weights are trained in a supervised manner and, when combined with network architecture, are needed for transferring knowledge to a target task.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Supervised Manner">
      <data key="d0">Supervised Manner</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A training approach where the network learns from labeled data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prior Knowledge">
      <data key="d0">Prior Knowledge</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Prior Knowledge is an identified part of a pre-trained network that is useful for a new task and represents common knowledge between source and target tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;A part of the pre-trained network identified as useful for a new task; also referred to as common knowledge between source and target tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New Task">
      <data key="d0">New Task</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A task for which a pre-trained network is adapted.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Common Knowledge">
      <data key="d0">Common Knowledge</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Shared information or understanding between a source and target task.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Greedy Pre-Training">
      <data key="d0">Greedy Pre-Training</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Greedy pre-training is a technique used in the knowledge transfer procedure, combining with fine-tuning for successful transfer of knowledge. It involves freezing prior knowledge and substituting new layers.&lt;SEP&gt;A method that freezes the prior knowledge part of a pre-trained network and substitutes the rest with new layers, training them in a supervised manner.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285987</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Frozen Layers">
      <data key="d0">Frozen Layers</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Layers in which weights remain unchanged during the training procedure, meaning no gradient flows through them during backpropagation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285987</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New Layers">
      <data key="d0">New Layers</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Layers in a neural network that are initialized randomly or with specific techniques and are trained in a supervised manner during pre-training.&lt;SEP&gt;New layers substitute the rest of the network when the prior knowledge part is frozen during greedy pre-training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285987</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Training Procedure">
      <data key="d0">Training Procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The process of adjusting a neural network's weights to learn from data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Initialization Techniques">
      <data key="d0">Initialization Techniques</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Methods used to set the initial weights of a neural network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fine-Tuning">
      <data key="d0">Fine-Tuning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Fine-tuning is a technique combined with greedy pre-training to achieve successful transfer of knowledge.&lt;SEP&gt;A process similar to supervised learning where the entire network, including previously frozen parts, is trained with a reduced learning rate to optimize all layers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Source Model">
      <data key="d0">Source Model</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The origin of the highly optimized weights that can be compromised by large changes during fine-tuning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Computational Load">
      <data key="d0">Computational Load</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The amount of work or processing required by a system, which transfer learning aims to reduce.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Data Requirement">
      <data key="d0">Data Requirement</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The amount of data needed for a task, which transfer learning aims to reduce.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Training Cost">
      <data key="d0">Training Cost</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The resources, such as time and computational power, expended during training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285989</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Target Task">
      <data key="d0">Target Task</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The specific task for which a transfer learning model is being adapted.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285990</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Benchmark">
      <data key="d0">Benchmark</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A standard against which other models or methods are measured.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285990</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Model Performance">
      <data key="d0">Model Performance</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The effectiveness or accuracy of a model, which is tracked as training data is reduced.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285990</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Data Requirement Threshold">
      <data key="d0">Data Requirement Threshold</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The point at which reducing training data leads to a significant drop in model performance, used for successful knowledge transfer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285990</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hypothesis Test of Statistical Significance">
      <data key="d0">Hypothesis Test of Statistical Significance</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A statistical method used to determine if there is a significant difference between two means.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285990</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alpha-Value (Coverage)">
      <data key="d0">Alpha-Value (Coverage)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A statistical threshold, set to 0.05 or 5%, used in hypothesis testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285990</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Classical Formula Based Statistical Methods">
      <data key="d0">Classical Formula Based Statistical Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Traditional statistical procedures used for hypothesis testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285990</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Resampling Based Technique">
      <data key="d0">Resampling Based Technique</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A statistical method for hypothesis testing described in Chapter 5 of Bruce, Bruce, and Gedeck 2020.&lt;SEP&gt;A non-parametric method for hypothesis testing, robust to assumptions about underlying data distribution, used instead of classical formula-based methods. An example is the permutation test.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289197</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bruce, Bruce, And Gedeck 2020">
      <data key="d0">Bruce, Bruce, And Gedeck 2020</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A publication referenced for a resampling-based technique in hypothesis testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Permuted Data">
      <data key="d0">Permuted Data</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Data formed by combining samples from both pre-trained and trained weights, used in resampling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Difference In Weights Between The Two Sets">
      <data key="d0">Difference In Weights Between The Two Sets</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A quantity of interest calculated during resampling-based hypothesis testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Distribution Due To Random Variation">
      <data key="d0">Distribution Due To Random Variation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The expected spread of data points resulting from chance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285993</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Green Color">
      <data key="d0">Green Color</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Denotes layers where there is no significant difference in mean values between source and target models, as shown in Figure 3.19.&lt;SEP&gt;The green color in the diagrams indicates magnetic material within the design domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6248c24886e0bd2c4ecb6bd221bb01dd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288769</data>
    </node>
    <node id="P-Value">
      <data key="d0">P-Value</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A statistical measure used in hypothesis testing; failure to cross a threshold results in failing to reject the null hypothesis.&lt;SEP&gt;The probability of obtaining observed results (or more extreme results) if the null hypothesis were true, used to determine statistical significance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289197</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Null Hypothesis">
      <data key="d0">Null Hypothesis</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The hypothesis that there is no significant difference between the mean values of layers from source and target models.&lt;SEP&gt;A statement of no effect or no difference, proposing no statistically significant difference between population means (e.g., of source and target weights). It is denoted as H0.&lt;SEP&gt;The null hypothesis, denoted as H₀, states that there is no significant difference between two population means, specifically that μs equals μt.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9df755969eb90a4aeda5af51acf960d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293018</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Red Color">
      <data key="d0">Red Color</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Denotes the largest change in weights for a layer in the network in Figure 3.19.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285993</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yellow Color">
      <data key="d0">Yellow Color</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Denotes changes in weights that are between the extremes of 'red' and 'green' in Figure 3.19.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285993</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Source Neural Networks">
      <data key="d0">Source Neural Networks</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The neural network from which knowledge is transferred.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Target Neural Networks">
      <data key="d0">Target Neural Networks</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The neural network to which knowledge is transferred and adapted.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285995</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Domain">
      <data key="d0">Domain</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The specific area or context of the data.&lt;SEP&gt;In machine learning, a domain is composed of a feature space X and a marginal probability distribution P(x), representing the input observations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285995</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Label Space">
      <data key="d0">Label Space</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The set of possible output categories or values for a task.&lt;SEP&gt;A label space has two components, Y and P(y|x), where the probabilistic function P(y|x) makes predictions on instances from X.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285995</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Output Layer">
      <data key="d0">Output Layer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The final layer of a neural network, which was replaced in the pre-trained network due to a significantly different label space for the target.&lt;SEP&gt;The final layer in the network architecture, corresponding to Q(s,a) for the critic and π(s,a) for the actor.&lt;SEP&gt;The Output Layer is the rightmost element of the neural network diagram, enclosed in a light green rounded rectangle, containing a single circular node labeled 'O₁'.&lt;SEP&gt;The Output Layer is the final component of the Feedforward Neural Network, consisting of a single shaded circular node (`O1`) that produces the estimated Value Function or Action-Value Function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca632ae39508b7c6fdf1ad04973857b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb622c9ce0600f3539c3ed7199e0c0ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Time-Distributed Dense Layer">
      <data key="d0">Time-Distributed Dense Layer</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific type of output layer that was replaced in the pre-trained network.&lt;SEP&gt;The Time-Distributed Dense Layer is a yellow-colored layer in the decoder section, receiving input from "LSTM #3" and leading to the "Power Factor Map" output.&lt;SEP&gt;A layer in the neural network architecture often used in sequence models, which applies the same Dense layer to every temporal slice of an input.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288724</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cold-Start Supervised Learning Process">
      <data key="d0">Cold-Start Supervised Learning Process</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A supervised learning process that starts training a model from scratch without any pre-training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285995</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 3.20">
      <data key="d0">Figure 3.20</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A figure showing that transfer learning saves 50-55% in training time compared to coldstart supervised learning.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 3.20 is a 2D line graph that compares training curves between supervised learning and transfer learning tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-248a0b72513cd485685d4b3e3864e01e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289384</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pf Map">
      <data key="d0">Pf Map</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A map related to power factor, where Normalized RMSE is measured.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285995</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Neural Network Weights">
      <data key="d0">Deep Neural Network Weights</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Weights within a Deep Neural Network that are trained in a supervised manner.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Training Time">
      <data key="d0">Training Time</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The duration required to train a model, which transfer learning aims to decrease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weight Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Weight Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The pattern of how weights are distributed across a layer from the pre-trained network, compared with the target network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285998</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hypothesis Test Of Statistical Significance">
      <data key="d0">Hypothesis Test Of Statistical Significance</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A statistical method used to compare the means of weights between two networks, aiming to quantify weight changes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285998</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alpha Value">
      <data key="d0">Alpha Value</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A statistical parameter (coverage) set to 0.05 or 5% in hypothesis testing.&lt;SEP&gt;A predetermined threshold (e.g., 0.05) used to assess statistical significance; if the p-value falls below this, the null hypothesis is rejected.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289199</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Random Variation">
      <data key="d0">Random Variation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The natural fluctuation in weights observed in two neural networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285998</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Significant Difference">
      <data key="d0">Significant Difference</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A statistically determined difference between values, often indicated by a p-value crossing a threshold.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285998</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ipm Motor">
      <data key="d0">Ipm Motor</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of motor whose genetic variations are used in Internal Knowledge Transfer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285998</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coldstart Supervised Learning">
      <data key="d0">Coldstart Supervised Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A supervised learning process without pre-training, used as a benchmark for comparison with transfer learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760285998</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Normalized Rmse">
      <data key="d0">Normalized Rmse</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A metric used to quantify the error over a test set, typically lying in the range of 0.25-1.0%.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a73228cc9dfee94628a2eb077e2a4a4b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286000</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Actor-Critic Model">
      <data key="d0">Actor-Critic Model</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Actor-Critic model is a type of reinforcement learning algorithm that combines aspects of policy-based and value-based methods.&lt;SEP&gt;The Actor-Critic Model is an architecture within reinforcement learning, illustrated as a flowchart-like structure with an AGENT and an ENVIRONMENT.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9b48425c0c345b3be14ed824c6579b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289348</data>
    </node>
    <node id="A(s,a)">
      <data key="d0">A(s,a)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A(s,a) is the Advantage function, which compares acting from a state s' to acting according to the policy. It is useful for gradient calculation in the Advantage Actor-Critic model.&lt;SEP&gt;A(s,a) represents the advantage function in a reinforcement learning context, which quantifies how much better a specific action `a` at state `s` is compared to the expected value of being in state `s`.&lt;SEP&gt;A(s,a) represents the advantage function, which describes the extra value of taking a specific action 'a' in a given state 's' over the average value of that state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dae820ad2360b2e0540cedd11e75bb15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e90fd88a3c123b4352d139141e81b16</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SeqTO-vz">
      <data key="d0">SeqTO-vz</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">SeqTO-vz is a system that determines the possible actions available to the agent.&lt;SEP&gt;SeqTO-vz is a specific application domain or scenario where the A2C algorithm is used for agent control or decision-making.&lt;SEP&gt;SeqTO-vz is the context for the agent's actions, implying the specific environment or problem domain where these actions are being taken.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27909faa34224de8c011e982c98fcef6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289177</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Table 5.4">
      <data key="d0">Table 5.4</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Table 5.4 lists eight excitation scenarios for a C-core, used for testing the generalizability of the A2C algorithm.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 5.4 is referenced in the caption as containing different excitation scenarios for the topology optimization of a C-core actuator.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289162</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Table 5.5">
      <data key="d0">Table 5.5</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Table 5.5 presents the results obtained over scenario cases for different excitation for the C-core, comparing computational efficiency and optimal accuracy of various algorithms.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 5.5 is a reference in the document that tabulates different excitation data for the C-core and includes a comparison of computational efficiency and optimal accuracy of RL-based algorithms.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 5.5 is a foundational element for understanding the Results &amp; Discussion section, presenting the performance of the A2C algorithm across the defined scenarios.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 5.5 is a table presenting a comparative analysis of force magnitude and computational efficiency across various optimization algorithms for C-core excitation scenarios.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c88cccaef550c4d3cde6f591d2fe5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6504e2cf679305513217b8548174a68b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288777</data>
    </node>
    <node id="On-Off TO">
      <data key="d0">On-Off TO</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">On-Off TO is a methodology on which the GA algorithm operates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286000</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tabular Q-Learning">
      <data key="d0">Tabular Q-Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Tabular Q-Learning is a reinforcement learning method employed with SeqTO-v1 to verify results.&lt;SEP&gt;Tabular Q-learning is a method of Reinforcement Learning where state values or Q-values are directly stored in a table, without parameters, implying an exact lookup rather than an approximation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.19">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.19</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.19 illustrates the network architecture for the Actor &amp; Critic, which takes state representation and outputs probability distribution over actions.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5.19 is a caption that describes the image's content as "Network Architecture for A2C".</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5dae035c707962e5992f8ffb5b5c0c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.20">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.20</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.20 displays the conventional geometry of the C-core used in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5.20 illustrates the design of a C-core actuator, with part (a) showing the conventional design and part (b) showing half of the geometry for simulation due to symmetry.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289162</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RL Based Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">RL Based Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">RL Based Algorithms refers to algorithms that use Reinforcement Learning techniques, including Sequence-based TO (-v1 &amp; -v2).&lt;SEP&gt;Reinforcement Learning based algorithms used for computational efficiency and optimal accuracy comparisons, including A2C and QL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sequence-Based TO (-v1 &amp; -v2)">
      <data key="d0">Sequence-Based TO (-v1 &amp; -v2)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Different versions of Sequence-based topology optimization methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Sequence-based TO (Topology Optimization) is a methodology used with RL-based algorithms, appearing in two versions, -v1 and -v2, for achieving optimal accuracy and computational efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286003</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GA+ Filters">
      <data key="d0">GA+ Filters</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">GA+ filters are an optimization method used for comparison, building upon the Genetic Algorithm.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic Algorithm with filters, a method introduced by Midha et al. in 2019, used for comparison.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286003</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Performance Parameter">
      <data key="d0">Performance Parameter</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Performance Parameter specifically refers to the force on the armature in a C-core, used to compare the effectiveness of different optimization methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286003</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Accuracy">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Accuracy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Optimal Accuracy is a metric used to compare different RL based algorithms and optimization methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286003</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Excitation Cases">
      <data key="d0">Excitation Cases</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Excitation Cases are various scenarios for the C-core where the performance of algorithms is tested and compared.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286003</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mnih et al.">
      <data key="d0">Mnih et al.</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mnih et al.'s work from 2016 is referenced regarding policy and the A2C model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286003</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weights (θ)">
      <data key="d0">Weights (θ)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Weights (θ) are parameters that are updated for gradient calculation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286003</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advantage Actor-Critic (A2C)">
      <data key="d0">Advantage Actor-Critic (A2C)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A developed form of the Actor-Critic model, abbreviated to A2C, known for its advantage function.&lt;SEP&gt;Advantage Actor-Critic (A2C) is a sophisticated reinforcement learning algorithm that refines the Actor-Critic framework by using an Advantage function to improve robustness and reduce variance.&lt;SEP&gt;A2C is an algorithm within reinforcement learning that extends Actor-Critic methods to improve stability and learning efficiency, partly by using the Advantage function instead of raw Q-values.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific algorithm within the Actor-Critic framework that uses the Advantage function for more stable and efficient policy updates.&lt;SEP&gt;The Advantage Actor-Critic (A2C) is a model illustrated by a block diagram, showing the interaction between its Critic and Actor components. It represents an algorithm or system architecture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27909faa34224de8c011e982c98fcef6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288804</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State-Value Function">
      <data key="d0">State-Value Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The State-Value Function needs to be learned by an agent and used for both present and next states.&lt;SEP&gt;The state-value function, $V(\cdot)$, estimates the expected return from a given state. In A2C, an agent learns only this function and uses it twice.&lt;SEP&gt;The state-value function, denoted as $V(s)$ or $V(s')$, represents the expected return when starting in state $s$ or $s'$ and following a particular policy. It is defined in Section 4.5.2 (not provided in text).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288807</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.18">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.18</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.18 illustrates the Advantage Actor-Critic model.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5.18 is an image depicting the Advantage Actor-Critic (A2C) model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27909faa34224de8c011e982c98fcef6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289333</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Equation 5.16">
      <data key="d0">Equation 5.16</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Equation 5.16 is a mathematical expression used in the discussion of the A2C model's complexity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286006</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advantage Function">
      <data key="d0">Advantage Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Advantage function A(s,a) is derived from the immediate reward and state value of the next state.&lt;SEP&gt;The advantage function quantifies how much better or worse performing a specific action in a given state is compared to the average expected return from that state.&lt;SEP&gt;The Advantage function, denoted as $A(s, a)$, quantifies the advantage of taking a specific action $a$ in a given state $s$, relative to the expected value of that state under the current policy. It is central to Reinforcement Learning algorithms like A2C.&lt;SEP&gt;The Advantage function is an extension of the Q-value function used in reinforcement learning to reduce variance in policy gradient methods, specifically in A2C algorithms.&lt;SEP&gt;The Advantage Function $A(s, a)$ is defined as $Q(s, a) - V(s)$, and its calculation is simplified using the Q-value equation. It helps improve the stability and learning efficiency in actor-critic methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288807</data>
    </node>
    <node id="A2C Experiment Setup">
      <data key="d0">A2C Experiment Setup</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A section describing the experimental setup for the A2C algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286006</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Train Scenarios">
      <data key="d0">Train Scenarios</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Specific excitation settings (e.g., (1.0 A, 450 Turns), (1.0 A, 500 Turns), (1.5 A, 500 Turns)) used to train the A2C agent.&lt;SEP&gt;Scenarios where the agent interacts, collects, and trains on data.&lt;SEP&gt;Train Scenarios are the initial C-core excitation conditions used for training optimization algorithms, explicitly identified as '1.0 A, 250T', '1.0A, 500T', and '1.5A, 500T'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c88cccaef550c4d3cde6f591d2fe5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Test Scenarios">
      <data key="d0">Test Scenarios</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Five additional excitation scenarios to which the A2C agent is generalized after training on the train scenarios.&lt;SEP&gt;Scenarios used to check the generalizability of the agent.&lt;SEP&gt;Test Scenarios are a group of C-core excitation conditions, implicitly described as those preceded by 'LSTL', ranging from '0.5A, 250T' to '2.0A, 500T', used to evaluate generalization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3cc8202063f67533ab2523d99cadd60f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c88cccaef550c4d3cde6f591d2fe5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288787</data>
    </node>
