    <node id="Yilmaz and Krein 2008">
      <data key="d0">Yilmaz and Krein 2008</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Yilmaz and Krein 2008 refers to a publication that describes MEC modeling as a popular method for electric machines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286016</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Uddin and Sozer 2017">
      <data key="d0">Uddin and Sozer 2017</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Uddin and Sozer 2017 is a reference cited for MEC modeling considered an analytical technique when combined with Maxwell's equations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286016</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radun 2000">
      <data key="d0">Radun 2000</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Radun 2000 is a reference cited for MEC modeling considered an analytical technique when combined with Maxwell's equations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286016</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hameyer and Belmans 1999">
      <data key="d0">Hameyer and Belmans 1999</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Hameyer and Belmans 1999 is a reference cited for Finite Difference Methods having difficulties modeling complex geometries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286017</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mohammadi, Ghorbanian, and Lowther 201">
      <data key="d0">Mohammadi, Ghorbanian, and Lowther 201</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Mohammadi, Ghorbanian, and Lowther 201 is a reference cited for data-driven models predicting global performance parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286018</data>
    </node>
    <node id="System of Differential Equations">
      <data key="d0">System of Differential Equations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The system of differential equations is used for electromagnetic analysis of electric devices and is nonlinear due to saturation in magnetic materials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286018</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Saturation">
      <data key="d0">Saturation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Saturation is a property in magnetic materials that causes the system of differential equations for electromagnetic analysis of electric devices to be nonlinear.&lt;SEP&gt;Saturation is a dominant effect for 500 Amp-Turns and beyond, implying the agent learns to manage material non-linearity due to flux distribution and excitation levels.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical phenomenon where the magnetic material reaches a point where it can no longer significantly increase its magnetization, dominant at 500 Amp-Turns excitation.&lt;SEP&gt;Saturation is a condition occurring in magnetic materials, dominant beyond a certain threshold (e.g., 500 Amp-Turns).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad8c5c524179d51552952772945627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-081d5d2ff62504474fc8532dd817fb39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f2486e70169df5ba045508cbd9276f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288748</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Linear Dependence of Inductances">
      <data key="d0">Non-Linear Dependence of Inductances</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The non-linear dependence of inductances on rotor-to-stator angles is reflected in the non-linear relationship between current and fluxes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286018</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rotor-to-Stator Angles">
      <data key="d0">Rotor-to-Stator Angles</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Rotor-to-stator angles influence the non-linear dependence of inductances.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286018</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Induced Voltage">
      <data key="d0">Induced Voltage</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Induced voltage is proportional to state variables such as flux, current, and speed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286018</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Analytic Solution">
      <data key="d0">Analytic Solution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An analytic solution is a form of solution that is impossible to find for the machine system of differential equations due to nonlinearities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286018</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Linear Magnetic Materials">
      <data key="d0">Non-Linear Magnetic Materials</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-linear magnetic materials contribute to the complexity of electromagnetic modeling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286018</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complex Geometry">
      <data key="d0">Complex Geometry</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Complex geometry, such as that of an electric machine, adds to the complexity of finding a field solution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286020</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 2.1">
      <data key="d0">Figure 2.1</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 2.1 is an unspecified visual aid that categorizes algorithms for electromagnetic modeling and simulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 2.1 is titled "Different electromagnetic modeling techniques" and is described as a monochrome flowchart diagram.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b33ad32faa20d67bef064cd7c0c2009c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Closed-Form Expressions">
      <data key="d0">Closed-Form Expressions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Closed-form expressions are mathematical formulas that analytical methods try to find for magnetic fields and losses in a motor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286021</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Fields">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Fields</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnetic fields are phenomena for which analytical methods seek closed-form expressions and whose estimation is significant for motors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286021</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Losses in a Motor">
      <data key="d0">Losses in a Motor</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Losses in a motor are phenomena for which analytical methods seek closed-form expressions and whose estimation is significant.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286021</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Iron Saturation">
      <data key="d0">Iron Saturation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Iron saturation is an effect that analytical algorithms face challenges in incorporating into their analysis, but is significant for estimating magnetic fields and losses of motors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286021</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin and Proximity Effects">
      <data key="d0">Skin and Proximity Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Skin and proximity effects occur in windings and are phenomena that analytical algorithms are incapable of modeling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286021</data>
    </node>
    <node id="End Winding Inductance">
      <data key="d0">End Winding Inductance</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">End winding inductance is a phenomenon that analytical algorithms are incapable of modeling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286021</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fast Prototyping">
      <data key="d0">Fast Prototyping</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Fast prototyping is an application where analytical models are employed due to their low computational requirements and ease of parameterization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286021</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Global Performance Quantities">
      <data key="d0">Global Performance Quantities</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Global performance quantities, such as torque and forces, are used to evaluate designs when fast prototyping utilizing analytic models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286023</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Forces">
      <data key="d0">Forces</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Forces are global performance quantities used to evaluate designs in fast prototyping with analytic models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286023</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physics-Based Models">
      <data key="d0">Physics-Based Models</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Physics-based models are founded on established physical principles and lead to more accurate simulations by modeling the interaction of electromagnetic fields with objects and the environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286023</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electromagnetic Fields">
      <data key="d0">Electromagnetic Fields</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Electromagnetic fields are phenomena whose interaction with physical objects and the environment is modeled by physics-based models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286023</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nonlinearity of Magnetic Materials">
      <data key="d0">Nonlinearity of Magnetic Materials</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The nonlinearity of magnetic materials necessitates combining MEC modeling with numerical techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286023</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-Core Magnetic Devices">
      <data key="d0">C-Core Magnetic Devices</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">C-core magnetic devices are a type of device for which the MEC method can calculate magnetic flux.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286023</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Devices with Moving Parts">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Devices with Moving Parts</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnetic devices with moving parts, such as electric machines, can be modeled using the MEC method.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286023</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surrogate or Data-Driven Models">
      <data key="d0">Surrogate or Data-Driven Models</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Surrogate or data-driven models are a class of models not based on first principles, approximating input-output relationships and reducing the bottom-up approach of first principle models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286023</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Experimental or Finite Element (FE) Model Data">
      <data key="d0">Experimental or Finite Element (FE) Model Data</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Experimental or Finite Element (FE) model data is used to develop data-driven models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286023</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Average and Ripple Torque">
      <data key="d0">Average and Ripple Torque</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Average and ripple torque are global performance parameters that data-driven models, like those using ML, can quickly predict.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74285f9f1c2db4a1aaef011e90f166c0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
