      <data key="d5">1760288838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Field Saturation">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Field Saturation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnetic field saturation occurs in the iron placed by the agent, posing a conundrum for design optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5526ac278068ab9cbf72c11fd3817672</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286054</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SeqTO Methodology">
      <data key="d0">SeqTO Methodology</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The SeqTO methodology is a proposed optimization technique used to optimize the rotor of Synchronous Reluctance Motors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5526ac278068ab9cbf72c11fd3817672</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286056</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stator">
      <data key="d0">Stator</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Stator is a stationary component within an electric motor, often depicted as the outermost part in cross-sections, such as in IPM motors and SynRM (Synchronous Reluctance Motors). Its primary function is to influence the rotor's movement. In many motor types, it achieves this by generating a rotating magnetic field, typically by exciting a 3-phase stator winding.

Visually, the Stator is often represented as an outer curved or ring-shaped section. It commonly features multiple internal slots or trapezoidal shapes. These slots are where windings are placed, which are essential for its electromagnetic function. Examples show the Stator in various colors, including light gray with orange trapezoidal shapes (representing windings or slots), dark teal with golden-brown or orange windings, or orange-brown material filling its slots.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff320ec3e3a24e3c69d31630b6cba885&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b7e7c8729e5eef7f4ff08e29b1d0014&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b7b73719952876f4f9c0e6b4efe0027&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5526ac278068ab9cbf72c11fd3817672&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e55225b1f173db3033115aeae970db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rotating MMF">
      <data key="d0">Rotating MMF</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Rotating MMF (magnetomotive force) is generated in the stator by exciting a 3 phase stator winding, which causes the rotor to move.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5526ac278068ab9cbf72c11fd3817672</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286056</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Saliency">
      <data key="d0">Saliency</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Saliency refers to the property of rotor poles exhibiting internal magnetic barriers (slots, airgaps) or external notches, affecting reluctance torque.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5526ac278068ab9cbf72c11fd3817672</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286057</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Simple Salient Pole Rotor">
      <data key="d0">Simple Salient Pole Rotor</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The simple salient pole rotor is one of the three types of synchronous reluctance rotors mentioned in the literature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5526ac278068ab9cbf72c11fd3817672</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286057</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transverse Laminated Rotor">
      <data key="d0">Transverse Laminated Rotor</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The transverse laminated rotor is one of the three types of synchronous reluctance rotors mentioned in the literature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5526ac278068ab9cbf72c11fd3817672</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286057</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Axially Laminated Rotor">
      <data key="d0">Axially Laminated Rotor</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The axially laminated rotor is one of the three types of synchronous reluctance rotors mentioned in the literature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5526ac278068ab9cbf72c11fd3817672</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286057</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reluctance Torque">
      <data key="d0">Reluctance Torque</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Reluctance torque is affected by the profile of notches/barriers/slots in the rotor structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Reluctance torque is the principle by which synchronous reluctance motors operate, generated through the creation of magnetic barriers by specific rotor designs.&lt;SEP&gt;Reluctance torque is the performance metric maximized by finding an optimal configuration of magnetic flux barriers within the rotor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5526ac278068ab9cbf72c11fd3817672&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06d562d92283cc555ded41962ea3fb9e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288898</data>
    </node>
