    <node id="Deep Learning for Electric Machine Performance Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Deep Learning for Electric Machine Performance Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An application of deep learning for the analysis of electric machine performance, detailed in section 1.2.1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-efb0bf44fcf7466c35982fd3bb8ae7ba</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286059</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physics-Based Modeling">
      <data key="d0">Physics-Based Modeling</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Modeling techniques like Finite Element Methods and Magnetic Equivalent Circuits used for magnetic field prediction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-efb0bf44fcf7466c35982fd3bb8ae7ba</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286061</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Finite Element Methods">
      <data key="d0">Finite Element Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A physics-based modeling method used for magnetic field prediction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-efb0bf44fcf7466c35982fd3bb8ae7ba</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286061</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Uncertainty Prediction">
      <data key="d0">Uncertainty Prediction</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A concept in magnetic field prediction, including uncertainty maps and a theoretical basis.&lt;SEP&gt;Uncertainty Prediction is a field in Deep Learning focused on quantifying the model's confidence in its predictions.&lt;SEP&gt;Uncertainty Prediction is a concept in deep learning that involves quantifying the confidence of a model's predictions, moving beyond mere point predictions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-efb0bf44fcf7466c35982fd3bb8ae7ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289186</data>
    </node>
    <node id="David A. Lowther">
      <data key="d0">David A. Lowther</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">David A. Lowther is a research supervisor who provided guidance, patience, and encouragement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-efb0bf44fcf7466c35982fd3bb8ae7ba</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286062</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Machine Performance Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Machine Performance Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The process of analyzing the performance of electric machines, specifically using deep learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-efb0bf44fcf7466c35982fd3bb8ae7ba</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286062</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNNs">
      <data key="d0">DNNs</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">DNNs (Deep Neural Networks) are used as surrogates for FE analysis, particularly for Stages 1 and 3 of Figure 3.17. They are a popular methodology for model-based knowledge transfer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286062</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 3.17">
      <data key="d0">Figure 3.17</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 3.17 is a visual representation illustrating Stages 1 and 3 for which DNNs act as surrogates for FE analysis, and is also referenced for the knowledge transfer procedure.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 3.17 is a flowchart illustrating the process for calculating performance maps, specifically focusing on efficiency and power factor for electric motors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289416</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feature Space X">
      <data key="d0">Feature Space X</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The feature space X is a component of a domain, where each input observation in the dataset belongs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286062</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marginal Probability Distribution P(x)">
      <data key="d0">Marginal Probability Distribution P(x)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The marginal probability distribution P(x) is a component of a domain, associated with the input observations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286062</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Label Space L">
      <data key="d0">Label Space L</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Label Space L has two components: Y and P(y|x), where P(y|x) can make predictions on instances from X.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286063</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Source Domain (Ds, Ls)">
      <data key="d0">Source Domain (Ds, Ls)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">In Transfer Learning, a model is trained on a source domain and its corresponding labels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286064</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Target Domain (Dt, Lt)">
      <data key="d0">Target Domain (Dt, Lt)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">In Transfer Learning, knowledge is transferred to a target domain and its corresponding labels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286064</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Efficiency Map Prediction Estimation">
      <data key="d0">Efficiency Map Prediction Estimation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Efficiency Map Prediction Estimation is a source task in the Internal Knowledge Transfer scenario.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286064</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IPM Geometry">
      <data key="d0">IPM Geometry</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">IPM Geometry refers to the physical design of a machine, remaining the same for the Internal Knowledge Transfer scenario.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286064</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FSCW Surface-mounted PM Motor">
      <data key="d0">FSCW Surface-mounted PM Motor</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">FSCW Surface-mounted PM motor is a specific type of motor whose design space is used in the External Knowledge Transfer scenario.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286064</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multi-Task Learning">
      <data key="d0">Multi-Task Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Multi-Task Learning is a learning paradigm that aims to jointly improve the general performance of all constituent tasks without source or auxiliary information, requiring training data for all tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286064</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Greedy Pre-training">
      <data key="d0">Greedy Pre-training</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Greedy Pre-training is a technique used in Knowledge Transfer Procedure to achieve successful transfer of knowledge, often followed by fine-tuning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286064</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Belief Networks">
      <data key="d0">Deep Belief Networks</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep Belief Networks are a type of neural network where greedy layer-wise pretraining is widely used.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286064</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Autoencoders">
      <data key="d0">Autoencoders</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Autoencoders are a type of neural network where greedy layer-wise pretraining is widely used.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286066</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ML Tasks">
      <data key="d0">ML Tasks</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">ML tasks involve predicting using a machine learning model, with similarity between tasks being an important factor in Transfer Learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286066</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pan And Yang 2009">
      <data key="d0">Pan And Yang 2009</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Pan And Yang 2009 is a referenced publication for the definition of notation used in machine learning task prediction and model-based knowledge transfer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286066</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Input Observation">
      <data key="d0">Input Observation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An input observation is an element within the feature space X of a domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286067</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Y">
      <data key="d0">Y</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Y is a component of the label space.&lt;SEP&gt;Y is a dimension label indicating the total vertical height of the central limb of the transformer core.&lt;SEP&gt;Y represents the corresponding target labels or ground truth for the input data X, denoting the correct class for each pixel or region in an input image for pixel classification or segmentation.&lt;SEP&gt;Y denotes the target training data (labels or outputs) observed.&lt;SEP&gt;Y represents labels associated with the observed data X, used in the posterior distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;Y is a variable in the equation, representing data or another random variable.&lt;SEP&gt;The symbol 'Y' is a variable within the equation, likely representing a set of output data or target variables.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26c769a26f512a088ad02de1c81c6ddf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-916831dfb3bd404b7a6bf4cf512b41b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c59eba9d5ca580dfd954f743c22649a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a5cf1e19ea0a8639d0d309beab7d404</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293003</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probabilistic Function P(y|x)">
      <data key="d0">Probabilistic Function P(y|x)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A probabilistic function P(y|x) is a component of the label space that makes predictions on instances from X.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286067</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Point">
      <data key="d0">Design Point</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A design point is represented by geometrical features (CAD image or design vector) and excitation points, with associated efficiency values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286067</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Efficiency Values">
      <data key="d0">Efficiency Values</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Efficiency values are labels for design points, used in the context of supervised learning and Transfer Learning.&lt;SEP&gt;Efficiency values ($\eta^*$) are specific measurements used in motor designs, partitioned into two classes based on an 85% threshold.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74dead31275ed48a2a31d5ae16d58cb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289171</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Geometrical Features">
      <data key="d0">Geometrical Features</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Geometrical features, such as CAD images or design vectors, represent a design point.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286067</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CAD Image">
      <data key="d0">CAD Image</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A CAD image is a type of geometrical feature that can represent a design point.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286068</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Vector">
      <data key="d0">Design Vector</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A design vector is a type of geometrical feature that can represent a design point.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Excitation Points">
      <data key="d0">Excitation Points</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Excitation points are components that, along with geometrical features, represent a design point.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Source Domain">
      <data key="d0">Source Domain</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">In Transfer Learning, a source domain (Ds) is where a model is initially trained with its corresponding labels (Ls).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Target Domain">
      <data key="d0">Target Domain</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">In Transfer Learning, a target domain (Dt) is where knowledge from a source model is transferred to, with its corresponding labels (Lt).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FSCW Surface-Mounted PM Motor">
      <data key="d0">FSCW Surface-Mounted PM Motor</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">FSCW Surface-Mounted PM Motor is a different design space for which efficiency maps are predicted in External Knowledge Transfer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mou Et Al. 2016">
      <data key="d0">Mou Et Al. 2016</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Mou Et Al. 2016 is a referenced publication that observed that very dissimilar tasks fail in the Transfer Learning process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Source Task">
      <data key="d0">Source Task</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">In Transfer Learning, a source task provides auxiliary information for learning a new task.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Auxiliary Information">
      <data key="d0">Auxiliary Information</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Auxiliary information is used in Transfer Learning from a source task to learn a new task.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Knowledge Transfer Procedure">
      <data key="d0">Knowledge Transfer Procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The steps involved in transferring knowledge, either internally or externally, within a learning system.&lt;SEP&gt;The Knowledge Transfer Procedure describes the steps to transfer knowledge from a source to a target task, primarily using Stage 3 of Figure 3.17.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286071</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3 of Figure 3.17 is primarily used for Transfer Learning due to its higher data and computation requirements, unlike Stage 1 which can be trained in a supervised manner.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 3 is the Torque and Efficiency Calculation step where FE simulations are used to compute electromagnetic torque and efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 3 refers to the 'Torque and Efficiency Calculation (FEA Simulations)' block in the flowchart.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288915</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Source Training Data">
      <data key="d0">Source Training Data</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Source training data remains the same as that used in Section 3.3 to train the network in Section 3.4.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Section 3.3">
      <data key="d0">Section 3.3</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Section 3.3 is referenced as the source for the training data used to train the network in Section 3.4.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific section in the document where the flowchart's procedure for calculating efficiency maps is detailed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288912</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Greedy Layer-Wise Pretraining">
      <data key="d0">Greedy Layer-Wise Pretraining</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Greedy layer-wise pretraining is a technique widely used in training deep belief networks and autoencoders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d3e48fade88c3732bf1ffbbe240bea3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sequence-Based TO (-V1 &amp; -V2)">
      <data key="d0">Sequence-Based TO (-V1 &amp; -V2)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Sequence-based Topology Optimization methodologies used in conjunction with RL algorithms; -v1 and -v2 are specific versions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Midha (2018)">
      <data key="d0">Midha (2018)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Author of an original work published in 2018 whose results were not available for additional analyzed scenarios.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inference Check">
      <data key="d0">Inference Check</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A process performed as part of evaluating neural network-based A2C algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neural Network-Based A2C Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Neural Network-Based A2C Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of deep reinforcement learning algorithm, specifically Actor-Critic, used for optimization and generalization across scenarios.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QL Results">
      <data key="d0">QL Results</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Results obtained from the QL algorithm, which are independent runs for each excitation scenario.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286073</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NN (Function Approximator)">
      <data key="d0">NN (Function Approximator)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A neural network that acts as a function approximator, capable of extracting useful patterns and generalizing over similar scenarios.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286074</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-Core Excitation Scenario 1">
      <data key="d0">C-Core Excitation Scenario 1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific excitation scenario (Coil Current = 1.0 A, Coil Turns = 450) that is part of the 'train' cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286074</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.21">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.21</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A figure in the document that shows the force magnitude generated by the optimal design on the armature, along with the magnetic flux density distribution obtained from GA, QL, and A2C for a specific excitation scenario.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5.21 visually presents the force magnitude and magnetic flux density distribution from GA, QL, and A2C results.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5.21 is an image that presents a comparative analysis of three different optimization methodologies for designing an electromagnetic actuator.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3b984e4831b11f20f84bb8669cd7396b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289046</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.23">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.23</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A figure in the document that shows the sequence of actions corresponding to the optimal geometry obtained for a specific excitation using the A2C agent.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5.23 illustrates the sequence of actions for the optimal geometry derived using the A2C agent, specifically showing steps 12 to 15.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5.23 details a sequence for optimal geometry for excitation, showcasing various steps in a magnetic core design optimization process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6248c24886e0bd2c4ecb6bd221bb01dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288764</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GA (Park and Min 2009b)">
      <data key="d0">GA (Park and Min 2009b)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">GA (Genetic Algorithm) is an algorithm used for comparison, with results obtained from research by Park and Min in 2009b. It uses ON/OFF TO.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286074</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GA + Filters (Midha et al. 2019)">
      <data key="d0">GA + Filters (Midha et al. 2019)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An enhanced version of the Genetic Algorithm, including filters, presented by Midha et al. in 2019. It uses ON/OFF TO.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286074</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QL Algorithm">
      <data key="d0">QL Algorithm</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">QL (Q-Learning) is an algorithm compared with GA and A2C, noted for requiring the fewest function evaluations in many scenarios.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286074</data>
    </node>
    <node id="(1.25 A, 500 Turns)">
      <data key="d0">(1.25 A, 500 Turns)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An additional scenario analyzed for which original work results were not available and is part of the inference check of A2C algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286074</data>
    </node>
    <node id="(1.75 A, 500 Turns)">
      <data key="d0">(1.75 A, 500 Turns)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An additional scenario analyzed for which original work results were not available and is part of the inference check of A2C algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286076</data>
    </node>
    <node id="(2.0 A, 500 Turns)">
      <data key="d0">(2.0 A, 500 Turns)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An additional scenario analyzed for which original work results were not available and is part of the inference check of A2C algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286076</data>
    </node>
    <node id="(1.0 A, 450 Turns)">
      <data key="d0">(1.0 A, 450 Turns)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific C-core excitation scenario used as part of the 'train' cases for the A2C agent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286077</data>
    </node>
    <node id="(1.0 A, 500 Turns)">
      <data key="d0">(1.0 A, 500 Turns)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific C-core excitation scenario used as part of the 'train' cases for the A2C agent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286077</data>
    </node>
    <node id="(1.5 A, 500 Turns)">
      <data key="d0">(1.5 A, 500 Turns)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific C-core excitation scenario used as part of the 'train' cases for the A2C agent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-df545da4858de5af7705d69fba2707ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286077</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Optimization Methodologies">
      <data key="d0">Design Optimization Methodologies</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Design Optimization Methodologies are approaches used to optimize designs, including Topology Optimization.&lt;SEP&gt;Various techniques employed by designers to find a design that satisfies all criteria.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286077</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Haftka and Grandhi (1986)">
      <data key="d0">Haftka and Grandhi (1986)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A publication that defines size or parameter optimization as the most constrained design optimization methodology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286077</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bremicker et al. (1991)">
      <data key="d0">Bremicker et al. (1991)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A publication that states a major restriction in shape and size optimization is a fixed and pre-defined design topology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286077</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lei et al. (2017)">
      <data key="d0">Lei et al. (2017)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A publication indicating Topology Optimization offers the highest freedom in design changes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286077</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Garibaldi et al. (2019)">
      <data key="d0">Garibaldi et al. (2019)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A publication suggesting that well-tuned free-shape optimization, as in TO, can outperform other optimization routines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286079</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Faria et al. (2015)">
      <data key="d0">Faria et al. (2015)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A publication suggesting that well-tuned free-shape optimization, as in TO, can outperform other optimization routines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286079</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimization Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Optimization Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Optimization Algorithms are computational techniques used to find optimal solutions, including Genetic Algorithm and Tree Search Algorithms.&lt;SEP&gt;Methods used to solve topology optimization problems, categorized into deterministic and stochastic algorithms.&lt;SEP&gt;Optimization algorithms are methods used to find the best possible solution to a problem, compared in terms of force magnitude and computational efficiency in Table 5.5.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a38c88cccaef550c4d3cde6f591d2fe5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288777</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Allaire et al. (2019)">
      <data key="d0">Allaire et al. (2019)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A publication that defines the constituents of an optimal design problem, including a model, objective function, and admissible designs.&lt;SEP&gt;Allaire et al. (2019) refers to a publication by Allaire and colleagues in 2019, cited in relation to the jet engine mounting bracket.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c232c16db2cb946ab34105a48ebff79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289248</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deterministic Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Deterministic Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Optimization methods that use gradient information of the objective function, offering quick convergence but poor global search ability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286079</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stochastic Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Stochastic Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Optimization methods that use random search mechanisms, designed to converge to global solutions and escape local optima.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286079</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Evolutionary Algorithms (EA)">
      <data key="d0">Evolutionary Algorithms (EA)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A popular type of stochastic algorithm characterized by population, fitness, and variation to generate better solutions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286079</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Li (2019)">
      <data key="d0">Li (2019)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A publication discussing the limitations of deterministic methods in multi-objective optimization and the principles of global optimization methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286079</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Design Problem">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Design Problem</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A problem defined by a model to evaluate performance, an objective function to minimize/maximize, and admissible designs with constraints.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286079</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Geometric Variables">
      <data key="d0">Geometric Variables</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Parameters like thickness, width, and length used to define designs in sizing optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286081</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Population">
      <data key="d0">Population</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The current set of solutions in Evolutionary Algorithms that are optimized to generate better solutions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286081</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fitness">
      <data key="d0">Fitness</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A quantifiable measure used in Evolutionary Algorithms to compare solutions, calculated using a fitness function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286082</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Variation">
      <data key="d0">Variation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Transformations performed on the current generation of solutions in Evolutionary Algorithms to create an improved next generation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-75b68f2c0143d85fc982bac91ab7a190</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
