    <node id="Law of Conservation of Energy">
      <data key="d0">Law of Conservation of Energy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Law of Conservation of Energy states that energy can neither be created nor destroyed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286110</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Synchronous Speed">
      <data key="d0">Synchronous Speed</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Synchronous speed is a fixed speed at which the rotor of a Synchronous Reluctance Motor (SynRM) moves continuously.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286110</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Permanent Magnet Motor">
      <data key="d0">Permanent Magnet Motor</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A permanent magnet motor is an alternative motor type that typically produces superior average torque compared to a SynRM.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286112</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rotor Flux Barriers">
      <data key="d0">Rotor Flux Barriers</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Rotor flux barriers are crucial components in SynRM design optimisation, forcing the main flux through desired iron paths within a fixed stator geometry.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286112</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Function of 'a'">
      <data key="d0">Function of 'a'</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The function 'a' represents the negative magnitude of the average torque generated by the motor, and its sequence of actions controls the movement of the controller.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286113</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Material Distribution 'a'">
      <data key="d0">Material Distribution 'a'</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The material distribution controlled by 'a' refers to air (p_j : 1 0), which results in a rotor's flux barrier.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286113</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Siemens MAGNET">
      <data key="d0">Siemens MAGNET</data>
      <data key="d1">software</data>
      <data key="d2">Siemens MAGNET is a software used for extracting geometry for baseline performance in motor design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286113</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Baseline SynRM">
      <data key="d0">Baseline SynRM</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The baseline SynRM is a conventional design that exhibits an average torque of 2.5 N-m.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286113</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4-Pole, 3-Phase Design">
      <data key="d0">4-Pole, 3-Phase Design</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A 4-pole, 3-phase design is a motor configuration that offers symmetry, allowing a quarter of its geometry to be considered for design space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286113</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2-D Design Space">
      <data key="d0">2-D Design Space</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The 2-D design space is a quarter of the geometry, discretized into 25 (5x5) cells, where the initial design is filled with iron and can switch to air.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286113</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GA Based Topological Optimization">
      <data key="d0">GA Based Topological Optimization</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">GA (Genetic Algorithm) Based Topological Optimization is a method used to obtain an optimal flux barrier topology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286113</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Topology">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Topology</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The optimal topology is the best structural arrangement obtained through optimization methods like GA, significantly improving average torque.&lt;SEP&gt;Optimal Topology refers to the best structural arrangement of the SynRM rotor, achieved through optimization processes like the Genetic Algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b7b73719952876f4f9c0e6b4efe0027&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289289</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ON/OFF Algorithm">
      <data key="d0">ON/OFF Algorithm</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The ON/OFF algorithm is an alternative method described in Midha et al. 2019, used to validate results from sequence-based controllers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286115</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2D Finite Element Simulation">
      <data key="d0">2D Finite Element Simulation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Transient 2D Finite Element simulation, often performed with software like MAGNET, is used to obtain the average torque.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286115</data>
    </node>
    <node id="M-19 26 Ga Material">
      <data key="d0">M-19 26 Ga Material</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">M-19 26 Ga material is a specific material used to fill the discretized design space in TD-Learning results for SynRM.&lt;SEP&gt;M-19 26 Ga material is mentioned as being used for the controller, implying a non-linear material model in the SynRM design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7b6f3bea48fce928809f6ddf7db422c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289055</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Law Of Conservation Of Energy">
      <data key="d0">Law Of Conservation Of Energy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Law Of Conservation Of Energy states that energy cannot be created or destroyed, influencing the movement of a rotor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286115</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stator Geometry">
      <data key="d0">Stator Geometry</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stator geometry refers to the fixed physical arrangement of the stator, which impacts how rotor flux barriers function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286115</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Objective Criterion">
      <data key="d0">Objective Criterion</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The objective criterion for optimization is similar to that in Section 4.4.1 and is related to the negative magnitude of the average torque generated by the motor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286115</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Siemens">
      <data key="d0">Siemens</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Siemens is a company from which the geometry for baseline performance was extracted using their MAGNET software.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286115</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Quarter 2-D Design Space">
      <data key="d0">Quarter 2-D Design Space</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Quarter 2-D Design Space is a discretized area used for optimization, representing a portion of the SynRM's geometry due to symmetry.&lt;SEP&gt;Quarter 2-D Design Space refers to a section of the rotor's design, discretized into 25 (5x5) cells for optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e55225b1f173db3033115aeae970db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288970</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Initial Design">
      <data key="d0">Initial Design</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The initial design refers to the $5 \times 5$ cells of the discretized design space, initially filled with iron.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b63cf0d438e93eb8fd05fc3ffbbb91e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286117</data>
    </node>
