    <node id="Markov Decision Process">
      <data key="d0">Markov Decision Process</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A mathematical framework for modeling decision making in situations where outcomes are partly random and partly under the control of a decision maker.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical framework for modeling decision making in situations where outcomes are partly random and partly under the control of a decision maker. Reinforcement Learning is effective for problems represented as an MDP.&lt;SEP&gt;A Markov Decision Process (MDP) is a framework used to model decision making in situations where outcomes are partly random and partly under the control of a decision maker. The image illustrates a state transition within an MDP.&lt;SEP&gt;A Markov Decision Process (MDP) is a mathematical framework for modeling decision making in situations where outcomes are partly random and partly under the control of a decision maker.&lt;SEP&gt;A Markov Decision Process (MDP) is the framework within which the 'state' ($s$) is defined and used in the context of the equation.&lt;SEP&gt;A Markov Decision Process (MDP) is an implicitly assumed environmental model for the application domain of the Advantage function, defining states, actions, rewards, and transitions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ad46225ae0d5f3835a9bda1ed8158d48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6ab3c95a34435be519e28c9335ef7bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289059</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Function">
      <data key="d0">Policy Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A function that defines the behavior of an agent in reinforcement learning.&lt;SEP&gt;The Policy Function is a concept discussed in Section 4.5.3, which is directly supported and illustrated by Table 4.2, specifically concerning deterministic policies.&lt;SEP&gt;The Policy Function ($\pi$) is a function that maps a state to an action or a probability distribution over actions, guiding an agent's behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;A Policy function, denoted as $\pi$ or $\pi(a|s)$, maps a state to the probabilities of selecting each possible action given that state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca0b752852f034cec7766c7e6e92af67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5072e2b75e9621e87c85e7af0ee60311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4bc9886b92f8e393e3ab3d494b9db33c</data>
