    <node id="Figure 5.24">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.24</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.24 (a)-(d) displays the optimal results from GA, QL, A2C, and A2C (re-trained), along with their magnetic flux density distributions.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5.24 displays the optimal geometry and magnetic flux density distribution for an excitation scenario.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c48c238aedc178e88e075043138eb72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.25">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.25</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.25 illustrates the transitional change in the design space due to agent-environment interaction for a specific excitation scenario.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5.25 is an image representing a sequence for optimal geometry for excitation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f2486e70169df5ba045508cbd9276f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1 x 1 Controller">
      <data key="d0">1 x 1 Controller</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A 1 x 1 controller is a type of control mechanism that achieves higher force generation than an A2C controller, possibly due to its ability to place a thin strip of iron close to the air gap.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286143</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thin Strip of Iron">
      <data key="d0">Thin Strip of Iron</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A thin strip of iron can be placed close to the air gap by a 1 x 1 controller to generate higher force.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286145</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Material Block">
      <data key="d0">Material Block</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A material block with a smallest width limited to 2 near the boundary of the design space in the SeqTO-v2 environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286145</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Space Boundary">
      <data key="d0">Design Space Boundary</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The design space boundary is a limit within which the pointer of a controller operates, affecting the placement of material blocks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286146</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-Core Excitation Scenario 2">
      <data key="d0">C-Core Excitation Scenario 2</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">C-Core Excitation Scenario 2 is a test case involving coiled current of 0.75 A and 500 coil turns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286146</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coil Current 0.75 A 500 Turns">
      <data key="d0">Coil Current 0.75 A 500 Turns</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">This data represents a specific excitation scenario with a coil current of 0.75 A and 500 coil turns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286146</data>
    </node>
    <node id="A2C Agent Training Phase">
      <data key="d0">A2C Agent Training Phase</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The A2C agent training phase is a period during which the agent learns from environmental interactions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286146</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Post-Processing Step">
      <data key="d0">Post-Processing Step</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A post-processing step involves re-training the agent by exposing it to specific excitation scenarios to improve performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286146</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Interaction With Learning">
      <data key="d0">Interaction With Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Interaction with learning refers to the process where the agent learns from an environment during re-training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286146</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Excitation Condition">
      <data key="d0">Excitation Condition</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An excitation condition is a specific state (e.g., current and turns) that an agent might or might not encounter during training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286146</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Computational Cost Reduction">
      <data key="d0">Computational Cost Reduction</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Computational cost reduction is the benefit achieved by methods like A2C in comparison to QL and GA, requiring fewer FEA evaluations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Agent Maneuverability">
      <data key="d0">Agent Maneuverability</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Agent maneuverability refers to the agent's ability to utilize the design domain, including placing elements at the boundary.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2c2f32fec14ff1bde53b19699374993</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760286148</data>
    </node>
