    <node id="Optimal Policy">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Policy</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-808c974f7871ba6b7b5d18f72afc85a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58674fc78b8e0f9b48c3a573d49dd684&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4bc9886b92f8e393e3ab3d494b9db33c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca0b752852f034cec7766c7e6e92af67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa18d38be749cd12e05de349dfa07cf</data>
      <data key="d2">The Optimal Policy is a fundamental concept in reinforcement learning, representing the best possible strategy an agent can employ within an environment. Its primary objective is to maximize the agent's total expected cumulative reward over time. This policy dictates the optimal action to take at every given state, effectively mapping a state to the probabilities of selecting each possible action.

In practical terms, the Optimal Policy is the strategy that yields the maximum value of the action-value function, often denoted as $Q(s, a)$, for a specific state $s$. Knowing the optimal $Q(s, a)$ function automatically provides the optimal policy, and conversely, it is the policy that maximizes the expected cumulative reward. The Optimal Policy, often symbolized as $\pi^*(s)$, is typically derived from the optimal Q-value function, especially in algorithms like Q-Learning, and is the strategy an agent learns to maximize its cumulative future reward.</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289287</data>
    </node>
