    </node>
    <node id="Electromagnetic Modeling Techniques Flowchart (image)">
      <data key="d0">Electromagnetic Modeling Techniques Flowchart (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This flowchart, titled "Figure 2.1: Different electromagnetic modeling techniques," visually categorizes electromagnetic models into Analytical Models, Numerical Models, and Magnetic Equivalent Circuits (MEC). It provides specific examples like Maxwell's Equations under Analytical Models, and Finite Element Analysis and Finite Difference under Numerical Models. The diagram serves as a clear visual guide to the modeling techniques discussed in the accompanying text, highlighting their hierarchical relationships as part of the thesis on electromagnetic modeling and simulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b33ad32faa20d67bef064cd7c0c2009c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FEM Computational Analysis Flowchart (image)">
      <data key="d0">FEM Computational Analysis Flowchart (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This flowchart illustrates the iterative computational analysis process in the Finite Element Method (FEM). It outlines steps from defining a physical problem and creating an electromagnetic model to finding a solution, analyzing results, and performing iterative refinements for both the analysis and the physical problem domain, ultimately leading to design improvement. The diagram visually supports the textual explanation of FEM as a numerical model for solving engineering problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce41e8cc5ef38832503f503eebaccf56</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Experimental and Simulation Result Comparison for FEA and MEC Methods (table)">
      <data key="d0">Experimental and Simulation Result Comparison for FEA and MEC Methods (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">This table compares the accuracy of Finite Element Analysis (FEA) and Magnetic Equivalent Circuit (MEC) methods against experimental results for force and torque calculations in electric machines. It shows percentage differences for various studies, demonstrating that both methods offer acceptable accuracy, though FEA often provides tighter error margins. The table reinforces the surrounding discussion regarding the trade-offs between FEA's higher accuracy and computational cost versus MEC's simplicity and speed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d146c06d89d470b7db435c475826c0e7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parameterized Coil Geometry in Fixed Air Box Diagram (image)">
      <data key="d0">Parameterized Coil Geometry in Fixed Air Box Diagram (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image is a 2D diagram depicting the parameterized geometry of a coil, represented as a solid orange circle, situated within a 'Fixed Air Box'. It illustrates the coil's variable radius 'R' and its center's X and Y coordinates relative to an 'Origin'. This visual representation corresponds to the 'simple coil in air' problem discussed on page 37 of the surrounding text, where the coil's dimensions and position are varied for simulation purposes in electromagnetic device analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6dd34d919cd23617da046186d8e9bc3a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="image_5390ef181278bd0fdfc761b1b9393a37">
      <data key="d0">image_5390ef181278bd0fdfc761b1b9393a37</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">```json
{
    "detailed_description": "The image, titled \"Figure 2.4: Parametrized Geometry-Transfo...</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c59eba9d5ca580dfd954f743c22649a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transformer Parameter Ranges (table)">
      <data key="d0">Transformer Parameter Ranges (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">This table details the minimum and maximum dimensional ranges (in mm) for four parameters ('X', 'y', 'W', 'yc') of the transformer problem. These varying dimensions are crucial for generating a diverse dataset for deep learning model training, ensuring the model can accurately estimate magnetic fields across different transformer geometries, as stated in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e98180b8e135e08cf842f2a94ed38a2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parametrized IPM Motor Cross-Sectional Geometry Segment (image)">
      <data key="d0">Parametrized IPM Motor Cross-Sectional Geometry Segment (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image is a detailed cross-sectional diagram of a quarter-segment of an IPM (Internal Permanent Magnet) motor, illustrating its parameterized geometry. It displays the stator with its windings and the rotor containing embedded permanent magnets, along with dimensions labeled X1 through X5. This visual representation is crucial for understanding the '4-Pole, 24-slot IPM motor' analyzed as the most complex problem in the accompanying text, providing insight into the physical structure and parameters considered in the simulations and analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff320ec3e3a24e3c69d31630b6cba885</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IPM Motor Parameters (table)">
      <data key="d0">IPM Motor Parameters (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">This table, 'IPM Motor Parameters', lists the geometric minimum and maximum dimensions (in mm) for five key components of a 4-Pole, 24-slot IPM motor: Stator back iron, Stator tooth width, Magnet inset depth, Magnet thickness, and Magnet width. It provides the crucial input parameters for the motor simulations and analysis, defining the design space for this 'most complex problem' discussed in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-997630dd5ed7d24f34fabba54a0e27c6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Artificial Feedforward Neural Network with Two Hidden Layers Diagram (image)">
      <data key="d0">Artificial Feedforward Neural Network with Two Hidden Layers Diagram (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This diagram, Figure 2.6, illustrates the architecture of an Artificial/Feedforward Neural Network with two hidden layers. It depicts an input layer with three neurons (I1, I2, I3), two hidden layers each with four neurons (H11-H14 and H21-H24), and an output layer with one neuron (O1). The layers are fully connected, with arrows indicating the unidirectional flow of data from input to output, visually representing the acyclic graph structure of neural networks as described in the accompanying text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca632ae39508b7c6fdf1ad04973857b9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Convolution Kernel Visualization (image)">
      <data key="d0">Convolution Kernel Visualization (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates the operation of a convolutional kernel, a core component of Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs). It depicts a 3x3 kernel sliding over a 5x5 input map (blue squares) to produce a 3x3 output map (cyan squares). The connections by black lines show how a local region of the input contributes to a single output value, visually explaining the 'convolutional layer' concept introduced in the text as computing connections with the local region of the input volume and forming 2-D activation maps.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d59e50443efd19bae755d63382e315bd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Encoder-Decoder Convolutional Neural Network Architecture Diagram (image)">
      <data key="d0">Encoder-Decoder Convolutional Neural Network Architecture Diagram (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates the architecture of an Encode-Decoder based Convolutional Neural Network (CNN), also known as a U-Net. It shows an 'Encoder' path with convolutional and down-pooling layers reducing spatial dimensions to 'Latent Info', and a 'Decoder' path with de-convolutional and up-pooling layers restoring dimensions. 'Skip Connections' link corresponding layers across the Encoder and Decoder, preserving spatial details, crucial for dense regression problems as explained in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f41a0abc7626a2c56bb77667264c7785</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Network and Training Dataset Features (table)">
      <data key="d0">Network and Training Dataset Features (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">This table quantifies the architecture of a Deep Learning model and its dataset partitioning. It details a 32-layer network with 16 trainable layers and 2.4 Million parameters, alongside a dataset composed of 30000 training, 10000 validation, and 5000 test samples. This information is critical for understanding the model's complexity and how it was trained and evaluated, directly supporting the technical descriptions in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a177157c3402545cf649cdb67022050a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comparison of Non-dilated and Dilated Convolutional Filters with Dilation Rate of 1 (image)">
      <data key="d0">Comparison of Non-dilated and Dilated Convolutional Filters with Dilation Rate of 1 (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image, Figure 2.9, illustrates two types of convolutional filters in a CNN: a non-dilated filter with padding (a) and a dilated filter with a dilation rate of 1 (b). Both diagrams show how a 3x3 output map (teal) connects to a 7x7 input map (blue and gray). The non-dilated filter samples contiguous pixels, whereas the dilated filter samples pixels with gaps, effectively expanding its receptive field without increasing kernel size. This visual aids the understanding of how dilated filters capture spatially distant relationships, crucial for improved model performance as discussed in the surrounding text on pages 42 and 44.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a4b902f2347e5fea9d43e606ce167ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Training Curves with Normalized Prediction Error for Electromagnetic Problems (image)">
      <data key="d0">Training Curves with Normalized Prediction Error for Electromagnetic Problems (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image displays three line charts illustrating the normalized prediction error versus training epochs for Coil, Transformer, and IPM Motor problems. It compares four neural network configurations: K=32 No Dilation, K=32 with Dilation, K=64 No Dilation, and K=64 with Dilation. The charts consistently show that models using dilation, especially 'K=64, with Dilation', achieve significantly lower prediction errors across all three problem types. This supports the concept that dilation enhances the network's ability to capture long-range dependencies, as discussed in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8854ea1ca7b618b176856798664355f4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Learning and FEA Magnetic Field Prediction Comparison (image)">
      <data key="d0">Deep Learning and FEA Magnetic Field Prediction Comparison (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This detailed figure illustrates the process from defining engineering problems using CAD models to predicting magnetic fields. It showcases the architecture of a deep learning network used for these predictions and directly compares its output (Deep Learning Results) with those obtained from traditional Finite Element Analysis (FEA Results). The visual similarity between deep learning and FEA results for a coil, transformer, and IPM motor highlights the deep learning model's accuracy, as stated in the surrounding context, which indicates a low normalized mean squared error.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8b78525002f5dfa42398879cc72242c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Error Distribution Heat Map of a Mechanical Component (image)">
      <data key="d0">Error Distribution Heat Map of a Mechanical Component (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This false-color heat map illustrates the distribution of errors (Ground Truth - Prediction) across what appears to be a mechanical component, possibly from a motor or similar device. The color intensity, detailed by the horizontal legend at the bottom, indicates the magnitude of the error. Yellow and red areas signify higher error values, while blues denote lower errors. The image highlights regions of significant discrepancy between predicted and true values, which, in the context, relates to the discussion of uncertainty prediction in deep learning models and the importance of quantifying confidence in results, especially for geometries not well-represented in training data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb99d0c44b7c6ee269cad3cce40b7be7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Posterior Distribution of Network Weights (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Posterior Distribution of Network Weights (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation represents the intractable posterior probability distribution of a deep learning model's weights given training data. It is the theoretical basis for quantifying model uncertainty. Since direct computation is infeasible, techniques like Monte Carlo Dropout are employed to approximate sampling from this distribution, allowing for the generation of uncertainty maps and assessment of prediction confidence in practical applications without explicit calculation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bayes' Theorem for Neural Network Weights (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Bayes' Theorem for Neural Network Weights (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation is Bayes' Theorem, foundational for Bayesian neural networks. It expresses the posterior probability distribution of a neural network's weights ($W$) given observed training data ($X, Y$). It shows how prior beliefs about weights ($p(W)$) are updated by data likelihood ($p(Y|X,W)$) to form the posterior ($p(W|X,Y)$), normalized by the intractable evidence term ($P(X)$). Its approximation via Monte-Carlo dropout is key to quantifying prediction uncertainty in the discussed context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kullback-Leibler Divergence for Variational Inference in Bayesian Neural Networks (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Kullback-Leibler Divergence for Variational Inference in Bayesian Neural Networks (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence between an approximate weight distribution $q(W)$ and the true posterior $p(W|X,Y)$ in Bayesian Neural Networks. Minimizing this divergence is the core objective of Variational Inference, a technique used to approximate the intractable true posterior. It aims to find the best tractable distribution $q(W)$ that quantifies uncertainty in the model's weights given the training data, forming the theoretical basis for uncertainty estimation in deep learning, particularly through methods like MC dropout.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bayesian Predictive Distribution in Neural Networks (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Bayesian Predictive Distribution in Neural Networks (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines the Bayesian predictive distribution, representing the probability of a new output given a new input and all training data, marginalizing over the uncertainty in neural network weights. It underpins how Bayesian models quantify prediction uncertainty, enabling applications like uncertainty mapping and improved decision-making in engineering through Monte Carlo (MC) dropout approximations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bayesian Predictive Posterior Distribution (Approximate) (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Bayesian Predictive Posterior Distribution (Approximate) (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines the predictive posterior distribution $p(y | x, X, Y)$ for a new input $x$ and output $y$. It integrates the model likelihood $p(y | x, W)$ over an *approximate* posterior distribution $q(W)$ of the model's weights $W$. This is crucial for quantifying prediction uncertainty in Bayesian Neural Networks, especially when the true posterior is intractable. It forms the basis for extracting uncertainty information like uncertainty maps by averaging predictions over various weight configurations, often approximated via techniques like Monte Carlo dropout.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Error and Uncertainty Maps for Electromagnetic Device (image)">
      <data key="d0">Error and Uncertainty Maps for Electromagnetic Device (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image displays three panels illustrating error distribution and uncertainty in the magnetic field prediction of an electromagnetic device. Panel (a) shows prediction error, highlighting significant discrepancies between predicted and ground truth values in an intricate geometric structure. Panel (b) presents a normalized uncertainty map generated via Bayesian inference (Monte Carlo dropout), with red boxes indicating areas of high uncertainty. Panel (c) magnifies these high-uncertainty regions, confirming a correlation between high prediction error and high model uncertainty as discussed in the accompanying text. A shared color bar quantifies the error and uncertainty levels from low (blue) to high (red).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92cc2b02ae3a119d92f0d1a7f6617885</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Annual CO2 Emission and Global Temperature Increase Trends (image)">
      <data key="d0">Annual CO2 Emission and Global Temperature Increase Trends (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image, Figure 3.1, presents two line graphs illustrating the historical trends of global CO2 emissions from 1751 to 2017 and global land-sea temperature increases from 1850 to 2019. Graph (a) shows an exponential rise in CO2 emissions, especially after the mid-20th century. Graph (b) reveals a significant increase in global temperatures, particularly since the 1970s. Both graphs visually support the accompanying text's narrative on global warming and the urgency of addressing greenhouse gas emissions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1360ac2945668789a7cee8bd946888f8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="US Greenhouse Gas Emissions by Sector Pie Chart (image)">
      <data key="d0">US Greenhouse Gas Emissions by Sector Pie Chart (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This detailed 3D pie chart, titled "Figure 3.2: US GHG Emissions by Sector," visually quantifies the percentage contribution of various sectors to US Greenhouse Gas (GHG) emissions. The largest contributor is Transportation (28.0%), followed by Electricity (27.0%) and Industry (22.0%). This chart supports the contextual discussion on global warming and sources of GHG emissions, particularly highlighting the transportation sector's significant role as described in the accompanying text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ab7f78faa6fc4f50c84b5c05263b97d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="US Transportation Sector GHG Emissions Pie Chart (image)">
      <data key="d0">US Transportation Sector GHG Emissions Pie Chart (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">A 3D exploded pie chart visualizing the breakdown of US Transportation Sector GHG Emissions by source. It highlights that Light-duty Vehicles (59.0%) and Medium- &amp; Heavy-duty Vehicles (23.0%) are the largest contributors, totaling 82% of emissions from the transportation sector, which is mentioned in the surrounding text as a target for reduction. Other sources include Aircraft (9.0%), Other (5.0%), Rail (2.0%), and Ships &amp; Boats (2.0%).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e01cf8f858f58505e96e0122d134e7dd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2010 Toyota Prius IPM Motor Efficiency Map (image)">
      <data key="d0">2010 Toyota Prius IPM Motor Efficiency Map (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This contour plot illustrates the efficiency map of the 2010 Toyota Prius Interior Permanent Magnet (IPM) motor, showing efficiency as a function of motor speed (RPM) and torque (N·m). Efficiency levels, ranging from 62% to 95%, are depicted by a color spectrum from blue to red, with the highest efficiencies concentrated in central operating ranges. A yellow curve indicates the motor's peak torque/power limits, bounding the operational envelope. This map is critical for evaluating electric vehicle performance and energy consumption.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-172d2c57a19dd33adf49cb4619a788cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flowchart for Efficiency Map Calculation (image)">
      <data key="d0">Flowchart for Efficiency Map Calculation (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image is a flowchart detailing a four-stage process for calculating efficiency maps, as described in scientific research. Inputs include motor geometry, materials, and simulation parameters. The stages involve 'Flux Linkage Calculation' (S1), 'Excitation Calculation' (S2), 'Torque and Efficiency Calculation' (S3), and finally, generating an 'Efficiency Map' (S4). The context highlights that stages S1 and S3, which rely on computationally intensive FE simulations, are prime candidates for replacement with deep learning (DL) models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reference Motor Design Independent Parameters (table)">
      <data key="d0">Reference Motor Design Independent Parameters (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">This table lists the independent parameters for the reference motor design being studied: a 24-slot, 4-pole motor operating at 33.33 Arms and 400Vdc. These parameters serve as the baseline for the Latin Hypercube sampling used to create a design space of 2900 motor variations, which are then used to train and validate neural networks for predicting efficiency maps, as outlined in the data collection and neural network sections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28be27ccbb95fc691bf7d1ab7fac43a1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parametrized_IPM_Motor_Geometry_Diagram_Figure_3_6 (image)">
      <data key="d0">Parametrized_IPM_Motor_Geometry_Diagram_Figure_3_6 (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This diagram displays a quarter-section of a parameterized IPM motor, illustrating key geometric dimensions labeled X1 through X12. It serves as a visual representation for the motor geometry used in the research, specifically outlining the design variables that are sampled to create a large dataset for training neural networks. The diagram shows the stator slots, rotor components, and critical dimensions like airgap thickness (X5) and bridge thickness (X12), essential for understanding the motor's design space in the context of performance map prediction using machine learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reference Design Parameters for a Parameterized IPM Motor (table)">
      <data key="d0">Reference Design Parameters for a Parameterized IPM Motor (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">This table lists 12 specific design parameters (X1-X12) and their values for a reference 24-slot 4-pole IPM motor. These values form the baseline for generating 2900 motor design variations through Latin Hypercube sampling, with a ±50% variation. This dataset is critical for training deep learning models to predict flux linkages and efficiency maps, a central component of the 'Data Collection' process described in Section 3.3 for surrogate modeling in electromagnetic FE simulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="image_5424e1029ce6367c755623d7973997a0">
      <data key="d0">image_5424e1029ce6367c755623d7973997a0</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">```json
{
    "detailed_description": "The image displays a block diagram illustrating a neural netw...</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17fa5f6a446284811fca7ceb6337e9ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RNN with Attention Mechanism and Time-Distributed Feedforward Network for Efficiency Prediction (image)">
      <data key="d0">RNN with Attention Mechanism and Time-Distributed Feedforward Network for Efficiency Prediction (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates the architecture of a deep learning model for predicting motor efficiency maps. It consists of an RNN with an attention mechanism, processing excitation and motor geometry inputs to generate a 'context vector' and weighted 'encoder hidden vectors'. These are then fed into a time-distributed feedforward network to output an 'efficiency sequence', addressing information loss in long input sequences as discussed in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0dd4b73a53d12770e110415d865cc0d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="End-to-End CNN Architecture for Efficiency Map Prediction (image)">
      <data key="d0">End-to-End CNN Architecture for Efficiency Map Prediction (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image illustrates the architecture of an end-to-end convolutional neural network (CNN) model for predicting efficiency maps. It depicts a deep learning process, starting with an image of a motor's cross-section, feeding it into a U-Net-like autoencoder with encoder-decoder sections and skip connections, then outputting an 'Efficiency Map.' The surrounding text on page 61 and 63 describes this as an alternative approach for full efficiency map prediction, contrasting it with a modular network. This network directly uses 2D structured input containing CAD geometry and material distribution to predict a uniform grid of efficiency values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-640da4d848d6502c665bfc0ea66dce13</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Supplementary Neural Network for Max Torque and Speed Prediction (image)">
      <data key="d0">Supplementary Neural Network for Max Torque and Speed Prediction (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This flowchart illustrates a supplementary neural network architecture for predicting maximum torque and speed. It integrates two input streams: "3D Geometric Parameters" processed by a Feedforward Neural Network, and a motor's CAD geometry processed by a Convolutional Neural Network. The outputs from both streams converge into another Feedforward Neural Network, which then outputs the predicted "Max Torque &amp; Max Speed." This network is critical for converting normalized torque and speed values to absolute values, relying on CAD geometry and 3D parameters as inputs, as mentioned in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b7e7c8729e5eef7f4ff08e29b1d0014</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DL_vs_FE_Flux_Linkage_Maps (image)">
      <data key="d0">DL_vs_FE_Flux_Linkage_Maps (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image presents two 3D surface plots showing flux linkage (d-axis) maps. The left plot is generated using a Deep Learning (DL) model, and the right plot is obtained from Finite Element (FE) analysis. The visual similarity between these two maps demonstrates the accuracy of the DL model in predicting dq flux linkage values, aligning with the surrounding text about the model's performance and low RMSE compared to ground truth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9f12687a170b3ffb0757382a02803b98</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Confusion Matrix for High Efficiency Classification (table)">
      <data key="d0">Confusion Matrix for High Efficiency Classification (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">This table defines the standard confusion matrix for a binary classification task. It illustrates how model predictions (efficiency &gt; 85% vs. &lt;= 85%) are categorized against actual target values, leading to True Positive, True Negative, False Positive, and False Negative outcomes. This matrix is crucial for evaluating the deep learning model's performance in identifying high-efficiency regions, specifically mentioned to achieve 90-96% accuracy, as detailed in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Classification Accuracy (CA) (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Classification Accuracy (CA) (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation calculates Classification Accuracy (CA), a key machine learning metric derived from the confusion matrix. It quantifies the proportion of correctly classified instances (true positives and true negatives) out of the total, evaluating how well a deep learning model distinguishes between high-efficiency ($\\eta^* &gt; 85\\%$) and low-efficiency ($\\eta^* \\leq 85\\%$) motor operating points. A high CA (90-96% observed) indicates reliable performance in identifying high-efficiency regions for motor design and operation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74dead31275ed48a2a31d5ae16d58cb0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Qualitative and Quantitative Efficiency Map Results for IPM Motors (image)">
      <data key="d0">Qualitative and Quantitative Efficiency Map Results for IPM Motors (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image, Figure 3.12, presents a comparative analysis of target and predicted efficiency maps for four different IPM motor designs. It visually displays contour plots of efficiency, with embedded motor diagrams, alongside quantitative confusion matrix results and classification accuracy. The figure demonstrates the high accuracy of a neural network in predicting motor efficiency, supporting the efficiency of the end-to-end network approach described in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Training Curves for Supervised Efficiency Map Prediction (image)">
      <data key="d0">Training Curves for Supervised Efficiency Map Prediction (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This line graph, titled 'Figure 3.13: Training curves for supervised efficiency map prediction,' illustrates the performance of three neural network architectures: Modular CNN-RNN, Modular ANN-RNN, and End-to-End CNN. It plots the 'Avg Node Percentage Error' against 'Epochs', showing how error decreases during training. The graph visually confirms the convergence of these models, with all three achieving low average node percentage errors (around 1-2%) after approximately 35-45 epochs, demonstrating their effectiveness in supervised efficiency map prediction as discussed in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf789c1974c43fd1722237522bebd446</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="End-to-End Model Target and Predicted IPM Motor Efficiency Maps (image)">
      <data key="d0">End-to-End Model Target and Predicted IPM Motor Efficiency Maps (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image displays two efficiency maps for an IPM motor: one representing the target (ground truth) and the other a prediction from an end-to-end neural network model. An inset diagram of an IPM motor rotor highlights the component being analyzed. The plots use a color scale to indicate efficiency levels, demonstrating the model's accuracy in predicting efficiency, as discussed in the surrounding text concerning the network's training and prediction capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee3a2fda6147e62f8093cb1da810ea6f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Normalized_Error_and_Uncertainty_Heatmaps_of_Motor_Performance (image)">
      <data key="d0">Normalized_Error_and_Uncertainty_Heatmaps_of_Motor_Performance (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image displays two side-by-side heatmaps: a normalized error distribution and a normalized uncertainty map, likely related to electric motor performance. The left heatmap uses a green-teal to red-blue gradient to show error values, while the right uses a dark blue to yellow-red gradient for uncertainty. Both feature a prominent curvilinear band, representing areas of changing error and uncertainty. These visualizations likely support discussions on model accuracy and reliability in the context of transfer learning for motor design as described in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c85d4390c7f5cda3bb7e9cc87cbcec0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parametrized FSCW PM Motor Geometry for Transfer Learning (image)">
      <data key="d0">Parametrized FSCW PM Motor Geometry for Transfer Learning (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image presents a 2D cross-section of a Fractional-Slot Concentrated Winding Permanent Magnet (FSCW PM) motor, detailing its parametrized geometry with labeled dimensions like 'X1' through 'X10'. It visually supports the 'Dataset Generation' section 3.7.1, where these parameters are varied to create training data for transfer learning in electric motor drive analysis. The illustration defines key design variables referenced in the context of simulating and optimizing motor performance using deep neural networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f476972defaf212918b90b259aa69f03</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Motor Independent Parameters (table)">
      <data key="d0">Motor Independent Parameters (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">This table lists the specific independent parameters '12 slots 10 poles 300 Arms 450Vdc' defining a particular electric motor configuration. It highlights the fixed design and operating conditions used in the context of generating datasets for transfer learning in electric motor drives, where these parameters are fed as inputs to DNNs to predict performance maps.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1f6cebd0187b0c1924a3e6491e8ebe8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Motor Geometry Reference Parameters (table)">
      <data key="d0">Motor Geometry Reference Parameters (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">This table lists the nominal geometric parameters (X1 to X10, with values ranging from 0.25 mm to 115 mm) for a reference motor design. These specific values form the baseline for generating synthetic datasets by varying each parameter by ±50%, crucial for training deep neural networks in the context of transfer learning for electric motor design optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flowchart for Performance Map Calculation (image)">
      <data key="d0">Flowchart for Performance Map Calculation (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">A flowchart detailing the four-stage process for calculating performance maps in electric motors, involving 'Flux Linkage Calculation (FEA Simulation)' (S1), 'Excitation Calculation (Analytical Equations)' (S2), 'Torque and Efficiency Calculation (FEA Simulations)' (S3), and 'Efficiency Map' generation (S4). The diagram, labeled Figure 3.17, highlights the integration of FEA simulations and analytical methods, with contextual information indicating that DNNs are used as surrogates for FE analysis in stages S1 and S3, and Stage S3 is particularly relevant for Transfer Learning discussions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transfer Learning Flow Diagram for IPM and FSC Motors (image)">
      <data key="d0">Transfer Learning Flow Diagram for IPM and FSC Motors (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates the transfer learning process for motor design, distinguishing between 'Internal Knowledge Transfer' for a 4-pole 24-slot IPM motor to predict a 'Power Factor Map' from a 'Pre-trained Neural Network' that generates an 'Efficiency Map', and 'External Knowledge Transfer' to a 10-pole 12-slot FSC motor for predicting an 'Efficiency Map'. It visually represents how a pre-trained network's knowledge can be adapted for related tasks and different motor types, as discussed in sections 3.7.3 and 3.8 of the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-193c3a719cf23865bd32e0d110afb409</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hypotheses for Weight Distribution Comparison in Transfer Learning (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Hypotheses for Weight Distribution Comparison in Transfer Learning (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation formally defines the null ($H_0$) and alternative ($H_a$) hypotheses for a statistical test comparing the mean weights ($\mu_s$, $\mu_t$) of a neural network layer between a pre-trained (source) model and a fine-tuned (target) model during transfer learning. It is used to determine if observed changes in layer weights are statistically significant, providing a basis for understanding how knowledge is adapted and which parts of the network undergo substantial modification for new tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neural Network Architecture with Transfer Learning Weight Changes and Power Factor Maps (image)">
      <data key="d0">Neural Network Architecture with Transfer Learning Weight Changes and Power Factor Maps (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image illustrates a neural network architecture, specifically an encoder-decoder model, for mapping from IPM motor parameters to a Power Factor Map. The layers of the network are color-coded (green, yellow, red) to indicate the extent of weight change during a transfer learning process from efficiency maps to power factor maps, with green for minimal change and red for significant change. The right side displays two contour plots, one with an IPM motor inset, representing power factor maps, showing normalized torque versus normalized mechanical torque. The figure visually supports the discussion on internal knowledge transfer and layer-wise weight adaptation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Training Curves Comparison Supervised vs. Transfer Learning (image)">
      <data key="d0">Training Curves Comparison Supervised vs. Transfer Learning (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This line graph, titled 'Figure 3.20', compares the 'Normalized Error (Validation)' over 'Epochs' for Supervised Learning, Transfer Learning, and a Running Average. It illustrates that Transfer Learning achieves lower validation errors faster than Supervised Learning, supporting the text's claim of reduced computational load and improved efficiency for tasks like mapping 'Efficiency map to Power factor map' in IPM motors. The graph directly visualizes the performance benefits of transfer learning in the context of neural network training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-248a0b72513cd485685d4b3e3864e01e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Encoder-Decoder LSTM Network Architecture and Efficiency Maps for Motor Analysis (image)">
      <data key="d0">Encoder-Decoder LSTM Network Architecture and Efficiency Maps for Motor Analysis (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image illustrates a neural network architecture, specifically an Encoder-Decoder LSTM model with a Feedforward Network component, designed to generate 'Efficiency Maps.' The diagram shows inputs like 'Geometrical Information' and 'Torque, Speed, Advance Angle, Current' feeding into LSTM layers. On the right, two efficiency contour plots are displayed, with one containing an inset cross-section of an electric motor. The caption and surrounding text (Section 3.8.2) indicate this figure demonstrates weight changes in the network for an 'Efficiency map to Efficiency map' transfer learning task from an IPM motor to a PM motor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="comparison of training curves for supervised and transfer learning (image)">
      <data key="d0">comparison of training curves for supervised and transfer learning (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This graph compares the Normalized Error (Validation) over Epochs for 'Running Average', 'Transfer Learning', and 'Supervised Learning' processes. It illustrates that Transfer Learning achieves a low validation error much faster than Supervised Learning, starting with an error similar to the Running Average, supporting the surrounding text's claims about transfer learning reducing training time and data requirements for efficiency map tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2bea7680cdfeea039ec6cf21875ea0c6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topology Optimization of GE Aircraft Mounted Bracket: Conventional, Analysis, and Optimized Designs (image)">
      <data key="d0">Topology Optimization of GE Aircraft Mounted Bracket: Conventional, Analysis, and Optimized Designs (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image, Figure 4.1, illustrates the application of Topology Optimization (TO) to a GE aircraft-mounted bracket. It displays three stages: (a) the original conventional heavy design, (b) a computational analysis showing material distribution based on stress (red/yellow indicates high stress, blue low stress), and (c) the final lightweight, organically shaped optimized design. The sequence visually represents TO's ability to significantly reduce material while maintaining structural integrity, as described in the accompanying text regarding GE's 84% weight reduction success.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d608386f6581f290b37ee1f8bb75e40</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Global Electricity Consumption Pie Chart (image)">
      <data key="d0">Global Electricity Consumption Pie Chart (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This pie chart, titled 'Global Electricity consumption' (Figure 4.2), visually represents the distribution of global electricity usage across various sectors. It highlights that electric machines account for the largest share at 43%, followed by lighting and heat both at 18%, electronics at 9%, electrolysis at 8%, and stand-by at 3%. This critical data supports the surrounding text discussing the significant energy consumption by electrical machines, their environmental impact through CO2 emissions, and the increasing importance of energy efficiency and topology optimization in their design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7dd7d2a24a4f09809aeb5eb6306158ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Design Problem Categories Diagram (image)">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Design Problem Categories Diagram (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image is a flowchart diagram illustrating three categories of optimal design problems: Size Optimization, Shape Optimization, and Topology Optimization. It visually represents the increasing 'Level of Complexity' from left to right, with corresponding geometric examples for each category. The diagram serves to clarify the distinctions between these optimization methodologies, as discussed in section 4.1.2 of the surrounding content.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66f6813286ff2495396939f94deff0c3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Homogenization Method Domain Discretization and 2D Unit Microstructure with Cavity (image)">
      <data key="d0">Homogenization Method Domain Discretization and 2D Unit Microstructure with Cavity (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates the homogenization method for topology optimization by showing the progressive discretization of a 'Design Domain'. It depicts how a larger region is broken down into an array of microstructures within an oval, and then zooms into a single 2D unit microstructure. The unit microstructure is a black square with a central white square cavity, labeled with three design variables: horizontal dimension 'x', vertical dimension 'y', and angular orientation 'θ'. This visual representation supports the description of homogenization in the accompanying text, particularly regarding the use of three design variables for microstructures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb6925d3d6ed02adeec99edaa647d90b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3D Printed Jet Engine Mounting Bracket with Lattice Structure (image)">
      <data key="d0">3D Printed Jet Engine Mounting Bracket with Lattice Structure (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image showcases a 3D printed jet engine mounting bracket, characterized by an intricate metallic lattice structure. The object, rendered in silvery-gray metal, features several circular attachment points and highlights the advancements in additive manufacturing. It serves as an example of components produced using homogenization methods in topology optimization, illustrating how complex microstructures can be realized through 3D printing to achieve optimized performance and reduced weight, as discussed in the context of materials design and electromagnetic devices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c232c16db2cb946ab34105a48ebff79</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="image_49b5fb40fdbbd6dc5592180053516b55">
      <data key="d0">image_49b5fb40fdbbd6dc5592180053516b55</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">```json
{
    "detailed_description": "The image presents a sequence of three grayscale panels illus...</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3639a9f481dea165d73df49859233cf4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Level Set Material Assignment Function (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Level Set Material Assignment Function (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines a binary material distribution (solid or void) based on the sign of a level set function $\Phi$. It is central to boundary-based topology optimization methods, directly delivering crisp '0/1' topologies and avoiding intermediate densities characteristic of other techniques. It delineates material boundaries implicitly to create optimized designs with clear material presence or absence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d40236fe707c1436e66df73317598df7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Level Set Representation of a Curve (image)">
      <data key="d0">Level Set Representation of a Curve (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image, Figure 4.7, illustrates the concept of a level set, crucial for boundary-based topology optimization. Panel (a) shows the 3D intersection of a conical surface (Φ(x,t)) and a plane, forming a circular curve (Φ=0). Panel (b) provides a top-down 2D view, demonstrating how different regions are defined by the level set function: Φ=0 marks the boundary, Φ&lt;0 denotes the interior material, and Φ&gt;0 or Φ&lt;0 (outside) indicates the void or background material, establishing a clear distinction between design domain and structure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Controller Movement in Grid-Based Design Domain (image)">
      <data key="d0">Controller Movement in Grid-Based Design Domain (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image illustrates the operation of a 'controller' in a grid-based design domain through a three-step sequence. A 3x3 controller, marked by a red outline and a central black cell, deposits gray material as it moves 'RIGHT' and then 'DOWN' within a 10x10 grid. The image demonstrates how the controller sequentially fills cells, leaving a trail of material, which is a key concept for the proposed Sequence-based Topology Optimization (SeqTO) methodology discussed in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d610c2b7090b4469e2b4b784c49c5807</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-core EM actuator schematic with dimensions (image)">
      <data key="d0">C-core EM actuator schematic with dimensions (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This detailed 2D cross-sectional diagram illustrates a C-core electromagnetic (EM) actuator with its core, coils, and armature labeled and dimensioned. The image serves as a visual reference for the actuator's structure, important for the subsequent optimization discussions in the context. Key dimensions and component placements are shown, along with a symmetry line, supporting the explanation of the EM actuator's design and its role in the study's GA optimization problem. The core is made of soft magnetic material, and the armature's interaction with the magnetic flux is central to the design problem.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d94fd82540e01e83a3a7b0febcf3d7ea</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Material Volume Constraint for C-core Actuator (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Material Volume Constraint for C-core Actuator (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines a dual-sided inequality constraint on the total number of cells assigned electrical steel in the discretized design domain of a C-core electromagnetic actuator. It sets a minimum ($K1$) and maximum ($K2$) limit on the volume of electrical steel, serving as a material usage and design space exploration constraint for the Genetic Algorithm optimization. It ensures that generated designs are physically meaningful and adhere to practical design specifications, influencing the search for an optimal material distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-Core Actuator Discretized Design Space Diagram (image)">
      <data key="d0">C-Core Actuator Discretized Design Space Diagram (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image displays a 2D discretized design space for a C-core electromagnetic actuator, featuring two reddish-pink 'COIL' sections and a gray 'ARMATURE' block, alongside a grid-based 'DESIGN SPACE.' It illustrates the setup for optimizing the core's material distribution, where the grid cells can be filled with air or electrical steel, as part of a Genetic Algorithm optimization process. A coordinate system is present in the lower left, and a dashed line at the top indicates a boundary or line of symmetry, underscoring the computational efficiency approach mentioned in the context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-57172dfb9b0c90234ff04b1a5519b71e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-core Controller Interaction with Discretized Design Domain (image)">
      <data key="d0">C-core Controller Interaction with Discretized Design Domain (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This diagram visually represents the layout of a C-core controller interacting with a discretized design domain for topology optimization. It labels key components like the 'Controller,' 'COILs,' 'Pointer,' 'Design domain,' and 'ARMATURE,' along with the 'Performance measure: Avg |B|.' The grid illustrates the 2D design space where material distribution is optimized, with a pointer indicating the controller's current action location. This serves as a foundational visual for understanding the optimization problem setup described in the surrounding text, particularly the concept of a topology optimization controller traversing a material distribution vector.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-26404cee26f7623e6d969e264821542f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comparison of Evolutionary Algorithms and Reinforcement Learning (table)">
      <data key="d0">Comparison of Evolutionary Algorithms and Reinforcement Learning (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">This table compares Reinforcement Learning (RL) and Evolutionary Algorithms (EAs) across three key aspects: agent interaction, learning from outcomes, and problem suitability. It highlights RL's single-agent, iterative learning from both positive and negative actions, and its fit for Markov Decision Processes (MDPs), contrasting with EAs' population-based approach, focus on optimal solutions, and suitability for non-differentiable loss functions. The table elucidates the 'stark difference' between these paradigms, justifying the focus on RL for sequence-based topology optimization discussed in the surrounding text, particularly leading into the explanation of MDPs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ad46225ae0d5f3835a9bda1ed8158d48</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State Transition Diagram for Markov Decision Process (image)">
      <data key="d0">State Transition Diagram for Markov Decision Process (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image is a black and white schematic illustrating a state transition in a Markov Decision Process (MDP). It depicts an initial state, S_t, branching into three possible next states, S^1_{t+1}, S^2_{t+1}, and S^3_{t+1}, via corresponding actions, a^1_t, a^2_t, and a^3_t. The diagram visually represents how a decision (action) at a given state leads to a new state, fundamental to the concept of MDPs discussed in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e6ab3c95a34435be519e28c9335ef7bd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deterministic Policy Mapping (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Deterministic Policy Mapping (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines a deterministic policy $\pi$ as a function that maps a specific state $s$ to a unique action $a$. It is a core component of Markov Decision Processes in Reinforcement Learning, dictating the agent's behavior unequivocally for any given state in applications like sequence-based Topology Optimization. This policy forms the strategy by which an agent makes decisions to achieve optimization goals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-core controller design domain with performance measure (image)">
      <data key="d0">C-core controller design domain with performance measure (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image depicts a C-core controller setup, visually representing a design domain as a grid. It illustrates components like 'COIL' and 'ARMATURE,' along with a 'Pointer' indicating an active cell within the design space. The 'Performance measure: Avg |B|' signifies the average magnetic field strength as a key optimization criterion, which the context explains is a more robust performance indicator than force for C-core designs, especially when material is sparse.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31fcb301590d668517d7e9583a508d90</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Finite Horizon Return Equation (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Finite Horizon Return Equation (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines the 'Return' ($G_t$) as the sum of all subsequent immediate rewards ($R_k$) from time $t+1$ to a final time step $N$ within a finite-horizon episode. It is a fundamental concept in Reinforcement Learning, representing the cumulative objective an agent aims to maximize. In the context of C-core design optimization, it quantifies the total performance achieved by an agent over an entire design episode, balancing immediate gains with long-term success.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discrete Design Domain with Coils and Armature (image)">
      <data key="d0">Discrete Design Domain with Coils and Armature (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image, Figure 4.14, illustrates a discrete design domain as a state space for Topology Optimization (TO), crucial for understanding the Markov property in Reinforcement Learning. It depicts a grid with 'COIL' elements, an 'INITIAL POSITION', and an 'ARMATURE', alongside an irregular gray structure, representing material distribution within the design space. This specific state representation is used with RL and GA algorithms, distinguishing it from other state spaces discussed in Chapter 5.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8803108c64e008ff20b50a70b77f8af</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grid-based Electromagnetic Device Design State Representation (image)">
      <data key="d0">Grid-based Electromagnetic Device Design State Representation (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image illustrates a grid-based state representation for Topology Optimization (TO), crucial for Reinforcement Learning and Genetic Algorithms. It shows 'COIL' regions in red, 'INITIAL POSITION' for design, and 'Air Gap' in blue, with gray cells representing material distribution. This visual example corresponds to 'Figure 4.14' mentioned in the context as a simplified state representation used for discrete design domains in electromagnetic problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discrete Design Domain with Electromagnetic Field Visualization (image)">
      <data key="d0">Discrete Design Domain with Electromagnetic Field Visualization (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image presents two panels illustrating discrete design domains and a magnetic field simulation, combined by a plus sign. The left panel shows a grid-based spatial arrangement with 'COIL' regions and 'INITIAL POSITION' on a background of gray and white cells, representing a discretized configuration for a magnetic system. The right panel displays a contour plot of magnetic flux density within a complex 'C' shaped structure, using a color gradient from blue to red (low to high |B|) derived from an electromagnetic simulation. This figure, along with other similar ones, demonstrates different approaches for state representation in Topography Optimization, especially those incorporating electromagnetic considerations, which are crucial for advanced Reinforcement Learning algorithms discussed in Chapter 5.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7b76c5d19e7bcbbd5f11b71588e066f2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reinforcement Learning Agent Movement in Topology Optimization Grid (image)">
      <data key="d0">Reinforcement Learning Agent Movement in Topology Optimization Grid (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image illustrates three sequential steps ((a), (b), (c)) of a reinforcement learning agent moving 'UP' within a grid-based topology optimization scenario. The grid includes fixed 'COIL' and 'ARMATURE' elements and varying material distributions. A black square represents the agent's position, highlighted by a red outline, moving one cell upwards in each step. Header details like 'Reward: 64.94', 'Material left: 84', and 'Lives left: 3' remain constant across the steps, while the 'Step #' progresses, demonstrating discrete state transitions in an episodic task relevant to MDPs and value functions as discussed in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1474f4155ebb5657d8ca7f3c0d29b49</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grid-based spatial environment illustrating controller movement and design error (image)">
      <data key="d0">Grid-based spatial environment illustrating controller movement and design error (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image, Figure 4.18, is a conceptual grid model showing a 'controller' (black square) within a 'designated design space' (gray squares) between 'COIL' and 'ARMATURE' elements. Sub-image (a) shows the controller within this space, while sub-image (b) illustrates the controller having moved 'RIGHT' and outside the designated design space, highlighted by a red border. This visual conceptually demonstrates a design error, which relates to the discussed reinforcement learning value functions and how an agent's actions can lead to undesirable states.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-906a75be5f88ad37713839d335d1720c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action-Value Function (Q-function) Definition (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Action-Value Function (Q-function) Definition (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines the Action-Value Function, $Q(s, a)$, as the expected cumulative discounted future reward (return $G_t$) an agent receives by starting in state $s$, taking action $a$, and subsequently following a given policy $\pi$. It is fundamental to value-based reinforcement learning, providing a theoretical target for algorithms aiming to learn optimal decision-making strategies in sequential environments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="True Undiscounted Return for Episodic Tasks (equation)">
      <data key="d0">True Undiscounted Return for Episodic Tasks (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines $G_t$, the 'true return' at time step $t$, as the simple sum of all subsequent rewards $R_{t+1}$ through $R_n$ in an episodic reinforcement learning task. It represents the total future cumulative reward from the next step until the episode's end. It is a fundamental concept for value functions and is directly utilized by Monte Carlo methods to evaluate state-action pairs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recursive Return Definition (Undiscounted) (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Recursive Return Definition (Undiscounted) (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines the return at time $t$ ($G_t$) as the sum of the immediate reward at $t+1$ ($R_{t+1}$) and the return from $t+1$ onwards ($G_{t+1}$). It is a recursive definition fundamental to bootstrapping methods like Temporal Difference (TD) learning, allowing for incremental updates of value functions without waiting for episode termination, in contrast to Monte Carlo methods. While presented here without a discount factor, it forms the basis for calculating discounted returns in TD learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="unknown_entity (unknown)">
      <data key="d0">unknown_entity (unknown)</data>
      <data key="d1">unknown</data>
      <data key="d2">The table, titled 'Table 4.8: Comparison of On/Off TO with Seq based TO using GA &amp; TD-learning', pre</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0d5c4a49fbb5bedfe6eb2f200271d99</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action-Value Function Updates (MC and TD/Q-learning) (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Action-Value Function Updates (MC and TD/Q-learning) (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation presents two distinct update rules for the action-value function $Q_\pi(s, a)$. The first is a Monte Carlo (MC) update that adjusts the estimate towards the complete observed return $G_t$ at the end of an episode. The second is a Temporal Difference (TD) or Q-learning update that 'bootstraps' by using the immediate reward $R_{t+1}$ and the estimated maximal value of the *next* state, allowing for incremental mid-episode learning. It contrasts episodic, high-variance MC updates with faster, bootstrapped TD updates, a cornerstone of model-free reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Learning Bellman Optimality Equation (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Q-Learning Bellman Optimality Equation (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation is the Bellman optimality equation for the action-value function in Q-Learning. It defines how the optimal Q-value for a state-action pair is the sum of the immediate reward and the discounted maximum Q-value of the next state-action pair. It is central to how an agent learns an optimal policy by iteratively updating its estimate of the optimal action-value function through bootstrapping and maximizing over future actions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deterministic Policy Function (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Deterministic Policy Function (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines a deterministic policy, mapping a specific state (s) to a single action (a). It represents the learned behavior of an agent in reinforcement learning, dictating the exact action to take in any given state. This policy is often derived from an optimal Q-function, where the action chosen is the one that maximizes the expected return. It is a fundamental component in policy improvement and control in reinforcement learning, especially for applications requiring unambiguous decision-making after learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deterministic Policy Behavior Diagram (image)">
      <data key="d0">Deterministic Policy Behavior Diagram (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This diagram visually represents a deterministic policy by showing specific mappings from states to actions. Three states (S_0, S_1, S_2) are depicted on the left, and four actions (a_0, a_1, a_2, a_3) are shown on the right. Arrows illustrate that each state maps to a single, specific action, which aligns with the surrounding text's explanation of a deterministic policy, where 'a policy maps each state to a single action'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d164a774e2261a7031fb600ad6568887</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deterministic Policy Behavior Example (table)">
      <data key="d0">Deterministic Policy Behavior Example (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">This table illustrates a deterministic policy, mapping specific states (80, 81, 82) to unique actions (a0, a2). It directly supports the explanation of 'Policy function' in the surrounding content, showing how a deterministic policy assigns a single action to each state, differentiating it from a stochastic policy and playing a role in optimal decision-making.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4bc9886b92f8e393e3ab3d494b9db33c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Table Example (table)">
      <data key="d0">Q-Table Example (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">This table, 'Table 4.3: Example of a Q-table', illustrates the action-value function (Q-values) for different states (S0, S1, S2) and actions (a, a_1, a2, a3). It is a core component of Q-Learning, demonstrating how an agent evaluates actions to form optimal policies. High Q-values, such as 8.2 for S0-a and 11.1 for S2-a2, indicate preferred actions, supporting the text's explanation of policy evaluation and improvement based on maximizing reward signals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca0b752852f034cec7766c7e6e92af67</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Deterministic Policy Derived from Q-function (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Deterministic Policy Derived from Q-function (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines a deterministic policy $\\pi(s)$ by selecting the action $a$ that maximizes the action-value function $Q(s, a)$ for a given state $s$. It is fundamental to Q-Learning and policy improvement in Reinforcement Learning, representing the 'exploitation' aspect wherein an agent chooses the best-known action based on its current understanding of expected future rewards. This policy is the direct outcome of successful Q-function estimation, guiding optimal behavior post-learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58674fc78b8e0f9b48c3a573d49dd684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tree Diagram for Sequence-Based Controller with Grid States (image)">
      <data key="d0">Tree Diagram for Sequence-Based Controller with Grid States (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This tree diagram, Figure 4.20, visualizes decision-making paths for a sequence-based controller. It illustrates an agent navigating a grid-like environment, starting from a 'START' state and exploring four possible actions: 'Down', 'Up', 'Left', and 'Right'. Each path shows resulting grid states or indicates 'Not Feasible' actions. The diagram exemplifies the branching factor and complexity of state-action spaces in Markov Decision Processes, as discussed in the context of Q-learning and the exploration-exploitation dilemma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="equation_abd4a16f6cca780e693f862a7f6322fc">
      <data key="d0">equation_abd4a16f6cca780e693f862a7f6322fc</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">```json
{
    "detailed_description": "The provided equation is the fundamental update rule for the ...</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epsilon-Greedy Action Selection Policy (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Epsilon-Greedy Action Selection Policy (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines the epsilon-greedy action selection policy, a fundamental mechanism in reinforcement learning. It dictates how an agent chooses an action at a given state. With probability $1-\epsilon$, the agent exploits its current knowledge by selecting the action that maximizes its estimated Q-value; with probability $\epsilon$, it explores by picking a random action. This policy balances exploration and exploitation, enabling the agent to discover optimal strategies while leveraging learned information, crucial for effective learning in dynamic environments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-Core Design and Symmetrical Half for Simulation (image)">
      <data key="d0">C-Core Design and Symmetrical Half for Simulation (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image presents two diagrams of an electromagnetic C-core actuator. Diagram (a) illustrates the full conventional C-core design with its core, coils, and armature, along with key dimensions. Diagram (b) shows a discretized half of this geometry, which is used for simulation, highlighting the design space and how elements like the core and coils are represented in a grid. This visual aids understanding of the 'C-core - Topology Optimization Results' section on page 107, particularly regarding the setup and simulation of such actuators.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca13e3ef7d4bf929b84d6b30574e3485</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-core Topology Optimization Results using Genetic Algorithm with SeqTO-v1 (table)">
      <data key="d0">C-core Topology Optimization Results using Genetic Algorithm with SeqTO-v1 (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">This table presents C-core topology optimization results using Genetic Algorithm (GA) on the SeqTO-v1 environment. It details armature force for various controller sizes (1x1, 3x3) and material types (linear, non-linear), along with GA parameters like design variables, population size, and stalled generations. It quantifies that finer granularity (1x1 controller) and linear materials generally yield higher forces, and the SeqTO-v1 approach often surpasses comparative results from Midha et al. (2019), notably achieving 55N for '1x1 non-linear' which is an 8% increase over 51N for '3x3 non-linear'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimized C-Core Geometry for Linear Environment (SeqTO-v1 GA) (image)">
      <data key="d0">Optimized C-Core Geometry for Linear Environment (SeqTO-v1 GA) (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image displays the optimized geometry of a C-core electromagnet within a grid, generated using SeqTO-v1 with a Genetic Algorithm (GA) for a $3 \times 3$ linear environment. It shows the C-core (gray cells) and two coil regions (red blocks labeled 'COIL'), with an 'INITIAL POSITION' reference point. A separate gray block labeled 'ARMATURE' is positioned to the right. The diagram illustrates a technical design for maximizing force in an electromagnetic actuator, consistent with the surrounding text discussing topology optimization results for C-cores with linear material properties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-abb64932da4baf92322bc61ecf0df44f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="optimized C-core magnetic field distribution for 3x3 linear environment (image)">
      <data key="d0">optimized C-core magnetic field distribution for 3x3 linear environment (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image displays a scientific visualization of magnetic field distribution within an optimized C-core geometry. It features a shaded plot representing magnetic field strength using a red-to-blue color gradient and overlaid black lines for magnetic flux, as detailed in the 'Shaded Plot |B| smoothed' legend. The strongest fields (red/orange) are localized in narrow gaps and sharp corners of the core. As per the surrounding text, this visualization corresponds to the 'Optimized geometry’s magnetic field distribution for 3x3 Linear environment (SeqTO-v1 with GA)', part of a C-core topology optimization study from Section 4.6.1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d8330ecbeee1c4ebe7ea64bfd7fbb45b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimized Geometry for 1x1 Non-linear Environment (SeqTO-v1 with GA) (image)">
      <data key="d0">Optimized Geometry for 1x1 Non-linear Environment (SeqTO-v1 with GA) (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image displays the optimized geometry of an electromagnetic device obtained using a 1x1 controller in a non-linear environment with the SeqTO-v1 and Genetic Algorithm (GA) method. It features a grid showing the distribution of material (grey cells) around 'COIL' elements and an 'ARMATURE'. The fine granularity of the 1x1 controller is evident in the detailed material arrangement, which, according to the text, resulted in a superior performance (55 N force) compared to a 3x3 controller design (51 N).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0691c9f7251ff59c05b1bc277ea93e1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimized Geometry Magnetic Field Distribution for 1x1 Non-linear Environment (SeqTO-v1 with GA) (image)">
      <data key="d0">Optimized Geometry Magnetic Field Distribution for 1x1 Non-linear Environment (SeqTO-v1 with GA) (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image displays a scientific visualization of the magnetic field distribution in an optimized geometry, likely an electromagnet, within a 1x1 non-linear SeqTO-v1 environment. The C-shaped core, visualized with a heatmap from blue to red, indicates varying magnetic field strengths. Black contour lines outline magnetic flux paths. The detailed structure, particularly the stepped airgap, is a result of optimization using a Genetic Algorithm, aiming for efficient flux channeling and higher performance, as discussed in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6719c866b8100236897a8ad6ae78d11f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal C-core design and force curve for SeqTO-v1 with TD-learning (image)">
      <data key="d0">Optimal C-core design and force curve for SeqTO-v1 with TD-learning (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image displays the optimal C-core geometry generated by the SeqTO-v1 algorithm with TD-learning, indicating the step-by-step material placement. It also includes a graph showing the force on the armature in Newtons versus the step number, illustrating a monotonic increase in force, particularly after Step 7, aligning with the expected greedy action sequence described in the surrounding content. The visual demonstrates the output and performance of the topology optimization method.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rotor Designs for Synchronous Reluctance Motors (image)">
      <data key="d0">Rotor Designs for Synchronous Reluctance Motors (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image displays three conventional rotor designs for synchronous reluctance motors: salient pole type (a), axial lamination type (b), and transverse lamination type (c). Each illustration highlights distinct lamination techniques or structural arrangements used to create magnetic saliency, essential for generating reluctance torque. The figures demonstrate the various ways flux barriers can be implemented in rotor design, which is key for optimizing motor performance as discussed in the accompanying text on SynRM optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06d562d92283cc555ded41962ea3fb9e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SynRM Rotor Topology Optimization Problem (equation)">
      <data key="d0">SynRM Rotor Topology Optimization Problem (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines a constrained optimization problem for Synchronous Reluctance Motor (SynRM) rotor topology. It seeks to minimize a function representing negative average torque, effectively maximizing torque, by controlling the distribution of air (flux barriers) within a discretized rotor geometry. The optimization is subject to constraints on the total volume of air, ensuring practical and feasible designs. This mathematical framework is central to improving SynRM efficiency and power density, directly contributing to advanced motor design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SynRM Design Stages: Conventional, Discretized, and Initial Controller Position (image)">
      <data key="d0">SynRM Design Stages: Conventional, Discretized, and Initial Controller Position (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image, Figure 4.28, illustrates three stages of Synchronous Reluctance Machine (SynRM) rotor design. It shows a conventional single-barrier SynRM, a discretized rotor geometry used for optimization, and the discretized rotor with a controller at its starting position indicating initial air-filled cells. The image provides visual context for the 'SynRM characteristics' and the setup for topology optimization discussed in the surrounding text, particularly for the 'baseline performance' geometry and the 'discretized design space'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02e55225b1f173db3033115aeae970db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SynRM Fixed Parameters (table)">
      <data key="d0">SynRM Fixed Parameters (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">Table 4.5 lists the 10 fixed geometric, material, and electrical parameters defining a baseline synchronous reluctance motor (SynRM). These parameters, including a 24-slot stator, 4 poles, specific diameters, M-19 26 Ga core material, air barriers, and 18A RMS current density, are crucial for establishing the motor's initial configuration and performance (2.5 N-m average torque) against which topology optimization results are compared. The table directly supports the experimental setup described in the surrounding text for improving SynRM torque.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SynRM Optimized Geometry and Magnetic Flux Distribution with GA and SeqTO-v1 (image)">
      <data key="d0">SynRM Optimized Geometry and Magnetic Flux Distribution with GA and SeqTO-v1 (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image presents two visualizations of a Synchronous Reluctance Motor (SynRM) rotor. The left side, "(a) Optimal geometry," displays the topological optimization result of the SynRM rotor using a Genetic Algorithm (GA) with SeqTO-v1 on a 5x5 discretized space. White cells represent introduced air barriers for optimal flux. The right side, "(b) Magnetic |B| field distribution," shows the corresponding magnetic flux density and field lines, highlighting how the optimized geometry influences the magnetic field. This design achieves significant torque improvement according to the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b7b73719952876f4f9c0e6b4efe0027</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SynRM Topology Optimization GA Results (SeqTO-v1) (table)">
      <data key="d0">SynRM Topology Optimization GA Results (SeqTO-v1) (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">This table details the Genetic Algorithm (GA) results for SynRM topology optimization using SeqTO-v1. It specifies the '5x5 (Non-linear)' environment, '25' design variables, a 'PopulationSize' of '50' (implied by context), '25' stall generations, and an 'AverageTorque' of '3.40 N-m'. This optimized torque represents a 36% improvement over the conventional design's 2.5 N-m, demonstrating the effectiveness of the GA-based SeqTO-v1 in achieving enhanced motor performance as discussed in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f5a390cc7143789eccd25f5f3623694</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SynRM TD-learning with SeqTO-v1 Results on Rotor Structure (table)">
      <data key="d0">SynRM TD-learning with SeqTO-v1 Results on Rotor Structure (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">This table details the results from applying TD-learning with Sequence-based Topology Optimization (SeqTO-v1) to a Synchronous Reluctance Motor (SynRM). It shows that using a 5x5 non-linear environment, an episode length of 25, a learning rate of 0.8, and a torque gradient reward function (∇T), an average torque of 3.40 N-m was achieved. This significantly improves upon the conventional design's 2.5 N-m, validating the effectiveness of the proposed optimization method.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7b6f3bea48fce928809f6ddf7db422c4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SeqTO-v1 TD-Learning SynRM Optimized Geometry and Magnetic Flux Distribution (image)">
      <data key="d0">SeqTO-v1 TD-Learning SynRM Optimized Geometry and Magnetic Flux Distribution (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image displays the optimized geometry of a Synchronous Reluctance Motor (SynRM) rotor and its corresponding magnetic flux density distribution. Panel (a) shows the physical structure generated by Sequence-based Topology Optimization (SeqTO-v1) with TD-learning, featuring strategically placed air barriers within a 5x5 discretized design space. Panel (b) illustrates the magnetic field distribution, revealing how the optimized geometry channels magnetic flux. This visual evidence supports the computational efficiency and performance improvement of the TD-learning approach for SynRM design, as discussed in the surrounding text regarding average torque enhancement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b56c95146eb3d0e8591ffefd6ab679ea</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="image_e1227624f8b362cdfb78ae0e2aad935e">
