    <node id="Deep Reinforcement Learning Conceptual Diagram (image)">
      <data key="d0">Deep Reinforcement Learning Conceptual Diagram (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This diagram, Figure 5.1, visually demonstrates the foundational relationship where Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) is derived from the combination of Reinforcement Learning (RL) and Deep Learning (DL). It uses a simple, clear, bracketed structure to show DRL as the convergence of these two distinct fields, supporting the textual explanation that RL coupled with neural networks (Deep Learning) forms DRL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-294177d434050aa2bd996039510efe9d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Reinforcement Learning Applications in Gaming and Robotics (image)">
      <data key="d0">Deep Reinforcement Learning Applications in Gaming and Robotics (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image, Figure 5.2, illustrates three prominent applications of Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL). Panel (a) shows the Atari game 'Breakout', demonstrating DRL's prowess in video games. Panel (b) depicts a Go board with black and white stones, representing DRL's success in complex strategy games like Go. Panel (c) shows robotics, with a robotic arm manipulating objects, highlighting DRL's application in real-world physical tasks. The image visually supports the accompanying text discussing the versatility and impact of DRL across various AI domains.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d5f6d6c8243089f59fe677475645a52</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Survey of Machine Learning Papers in Topology Optimization (table)">
      <data key="d0">Survey of Machine Learning Papers in Topology Optimization (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">This table surveys ten ML papers in Topology Optimization, detailing their learning architectures, online learning capability, role as a design optimizer, generalizability, and inclusion of manufacturability. It reveals a dominance of CNNs, limited online learning, and a universal absence of explicit manufacturability integration, confirming the surrounding text's critique of supervised learning's generalizability and motivating the need for improved approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3676b77d4e3057ccce502057364cbae</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="equation_204216cee43478254f995c37d3f6a6fa">
      <data key="d0">equation_204216cee43478254f995c37d3f6a6fa</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">```json
{
    "detailed_description": "The equation presented, $\\hat { v } ( s , w ) \\approx v _ {...</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Example Q-table for Tabular Q-learning (table)">
      <data key="d0">Example Q-table for Tabular Q-learning (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">This table illustrates a Q-table, a core component of Tabular Q-learning, displaying Q-values for different state-action pairs (S0-S2, a0-a3). It highlights the discrete nature of tabular methods and their ability to discriminate between actions. The table explicitly exemplifies the mechanism where the highest Q-value for a given state indicates the optimal action, implicitly demonstrating the 'greedy' policy discussed in the text. It serves as a contrast to the advanced function approximators (Deep RL) introduced in the surrounding context, showcasing the limitations of tabular methods for high-dimensional or continuous state/action spaces.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2ceb1ac6e54fec53c601d8b38f2b338d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tabular Q-value Linear Approximation (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Tabular Q-value Linear Approximation (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines the estimated action-value function for a state-action pair using a linear function approximator. Crucially, by employing one-hot encoding for the state-action features, it mathematically demonstrates that a tabular representation is a special case of linear function approximation, where each state-action pair's Q-value is directly represented by a unique weight. This highlights the limitations of tabular methods in terms of generalization, particularly for large or continuous state/action spaces, and motivates the use of more advanced function approximators.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-70c2a865bc92e157d3426a926d7ddd22</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feedforward Neural Network for Value Function Approximation (image)">
      <data key="d0">Feedforward Neural Network for Value Function Approximation (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This detailed diagram illustrates a feedforward neural network architecture comprising input, hidden, and output layers. It processes 'State (s)' or 'State-action pair (s, a)' as input to estimate either the 'Q(s, a) (action-value function)' or 'V(s) (state-value function)', as discussed in the context of deep reinforcement learning for nonlinear function approximation. The network visually represents how input features are transformed through weighted connections across layers to produce a single scalar output, supporting the conceptual explanation provided in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-eb622c9ce0600f3539c3ed7199e0c0ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sequential Grid States and Reward Evolution (image)">
      <data key="d0">Sequential Grid States and Reward Evolution (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image, Figure 5.4, presents three sequential grid states (a, b, c) illustrating a Reinforcement Learning agent's interaction with an environment. Each state details the 'Step #', 'Reward', 'Material left', and 'Lives left'. A small black square, representing a key material element or agent, shifts its position within a grid featuring 'COIL' and 'ARMATURE' components. The image demonstrates how slight changes in state representation can lead to a significant decrease in received reward, posing a challenge for learning models in balancing generalizability and discrimination, as discussed in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5f031035d088ffa5bcc3e370fe22c958</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squared Error of Action-Value Estimation (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Squared Error of Action-Value Estimation (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines the squared error for a single state-action pair, representing the discrepancy between the true optimal action-value function ($Q^*$) and its neural network-based estimate ($\hat{Q}$). It serves as the fundamental objective function component for training value-based Reinforcement Learning agents using supervised learning principles, guiding the optimization of neural network weights to minimize the difference between estimated and optimal action values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="equation_8363e12835330258a1e3537af0ddf36c">
      <data key="d0">equation_8363e12835330258a1e3537af0ddf36c</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">```json
{
    "detailed_description": "This equation presents a sequence of state-action pairs and t...</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gradient of Squared Error Loss for Action-Value Function Approximation (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Gradient of Squared Error Loss for Action-Value Function Approximation (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation calculates the scaled gradient of the squared difference between a target action-value function $Q_{\pi}(s, a)$ and an estimated action-value function $\hat{Q}(s, a, w)$ parameterized by $w$. Scaled by learning rate $\alpha$, it represents the core learning signal for updating neural network weights in value-based reinforcement learning algorithms like Q-learning, driving the estimated values towards the target values and minimizing prediction error.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stochastic Gradient Descent Update for Action-Value Function Approximation (Return-Based) (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Stochastic Gradient Descent Update for Action-Value Function Approximation (Return-Based) (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation describes a stochastic gradient descent update rule for adjusting the weights of a neural network that approximates the action-value function. It aims to reduce the error between the network's current prediction ($\hat{Q}$) and the observed cumulative return ($G_t$) from an experience trajectory. This update is a cornerstone of value-based Deep Reinforcement Learning algorithms, allowing the network to learn optimal action values from environmental interactions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Learning Mean Squared Error with Bootstrapped Target (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Q-Learning Mean Squared Error with Bootstrapped Target (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines the Mean Squared Error (MSE) loss function for Q-learning with neural network function approximation. It quantifies the difference between the network's predicted action-value for a state-action pair and a bootstrapped target, which combines the immediate reward with the estimated maximum future action-value from the next state. Minimizing this error through gradient descent enables the neural network to learn an optimal action-value function, crucial for decision-making in reinforcement learning tasks where the state space is large.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="equation_753c2dfed760d049d8ab557c3562aed6">
      <data key="d0">equation_753c2dfed760d049d8ab557c3562aed6</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">```json
{
    "detailed_description": "The provided equation describes a weight update rule for a ne...</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Off-Policy Deep Q-Network (DQN) Weight Update Rule (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Off-Policy Deep Q-Network (DQN) Weight Update Rule (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation describes the weight update rule for a Deep Q-Network (DQN) using separate behavior and target networks in an off-policy reinforcement learning setting. It leverages a modified Temporal Difference (TD) error to update the behavior network's weights, where the target Q-value is estimated by a more stable, delayed-update target network. This method enhances learning stability and performance, enabling effective approximation of the action-value function in complex environments crucial for various deep RL applications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Network Setup Approaches Diagram (image)">
      <data key="d0">Q-Network Setup Approaches Diagram (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This diagram, 'Figure 5.5: Different approaches of setting up the Q-Network', visually explains two methods for structuring Q-Networks in reinforcement learning, distinguishing between an approach where a single network outputs Q-values for all possible actions directly from a given state (a), and another where the network takes both the state and a specific action as input to predict the Q-value for that state-action pair (b). This figure supports the textual explanation of Q-learning with neural networks, particularly the concept and implementation of stacking actions in one neural network to predict action values, as discussed in the surrounding text on page 128.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9a7d0fc487b66b91a1d8bae6610798e3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Learning Cycle Diagram (image)">
      <data key="d0">Q-Learning Cycle Diagram (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This detailed flowchart illustrates the Q-Learning Cycle, divided into 'Experience Collection &amp; Learning' and 'Decision Making' sections interacting with an 'Environment'. It depicts the flow of state, reward, and action, the roles of the Experience Buffer, Target Q-Network, Behavior Q-Network, Q-Learning algorithm, and the epsilon-greedy policy. The diagram highlights knowledge sharing and weight updates between the target and behavior policies, crucial for off-policy learning as discussed in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-11cc1c9381d2b807e25d94ba609ca1ea</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="image_a946df9a82cb2e76fdfb4ad12da3e8f0">
      <data key="d0">image_a946df9a82cb2e76fdfb4ad12da3e8f0</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">```json
{
    "detailed_description": "The image presents a technical diagram divided into two main ...</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-678d567f82303d2bd1928259fd243036</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Q-Learning C-core Optimization and Convergence (image)">
      <data key="d0">Deep Q-Learning C-core Optimization and Convergence (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image presents the optimal C-core geometry derived using Deep Q-Learning (DQL) and its convergence characteristics. Part (a) illustrates the step-by-step construction of the C-core, including coils and armature, highlighting the optimal magnetic material distribution. Part (b) displays a line graph showing the exponentially averaged reward increasing significantly before stabilizing, indicating the DQL algorithm's successful convergence over approximately 200 episodes. The visuals support the explanation of DQL's application in optimizing magnetic designs mentioned in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02a0921c09c7af3fdaeac2a38ff90bbb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discrete Design Domain as State Space for C-Core Problem (image)">
      <data key="d0">Discrete Design Domain as State Space for C-Core Problem (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image depicts the discrete design domain as the state space for an electromagnetic device, specifically a C-core, featuring two red 'COIL' blocks and a gray 'linear magnetic material' structure against a white grid. The 'INITIAL POSITION' of the magnetic material is marked. A 'ARMATURE' element is shown to the right. This representation, ideal for tabular Q-Learning as mentioned in the context, illustrates the state encoding used in earlier discussions (Chapter 4, Section 4.5.1).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec0d779e474774183c36eade5f33db6b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discrete Design Domain with Magnetic Field Distribution in Air Gap (image)">
      <data key="d0">Discrete Design Domain with Magnetic Field Distribution in Air Gap (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.10 illustrates a discrete design domain for magnetic material optimization, specifically for a C-core problem, showing the spatial arrangement of coils, magnetic material (gray), and air (white). The diagram highlights an air gap region on the right, which is crucial for monitoring the magnetic field magnitude, |B| airgap, as a state space variable in a reinforcement learning environment (SeqTO-v2) for generating complex electromagnetic structures. This visual representation is part of a modified state representation discussed in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5eebe68238bf8a33757041bc7dd3ffe9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discrete Design Domain with Magnetic Field Distribution as State Space (image)">
      <data key="d0">Discrete Design Domain with Magnetic Field Distribution as State Space (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image illustrates an advanced state representation for the SeqTO-v2 environment, combining a discrete design domain with the corresponding magnetic field distribution. The left panel shows a grid-based material layout with coils and an initial position, while the right panel displays a complex magnetic field plot with a color-coded magnitude scale and flux lines. A plus sign visually connects these two representations, emphasizing their integration into a single, comprehensive state space for improved Q-Learning algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0534c4408a52c608a5a24ccf9951f5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Evolved Action Space of SeqTO-v2 Controller (table)">
      <data key="d0">Evolved Action Space of SeqTO-v2 Controller (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">This table details the expanded action space of the SeqTO-v2 controller, listing eight distinct actions: 'Soft Up', 'Soft Down', 'Soft Right', 'Soft Left', 'Up', 'Down', 'Right', and 'Left'. This expansion from four to eight actions, particularly the introduction of 'soft' actions, directly addresses limitations of previous controllers by enabling more flexible and granular structural generation (e.g., varying block sizes like 3x1 or 1x3) in a single step, crucial for creating complex designs efficiently in the new SeqTO-v2 environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SeqTO-v2 Controller Agent Movement and Data Block Placement Demonstration (image)">
      <data key="d0">SeqTO-v2 Controller Agent Movement and Data Block Placement Demonstration (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image illustrates the agent's movement and data block placement capabilities with the SeqTO-v2 controller within a 9x9 grid. It shows the differentiation between 'Right', 'Soft Right', 'Soft Down', and 'Down' actions. The actions include placing both 3x3 blocks and smaller 'soft' blocks like 3x1 and 1x3, demonstrating the expanded action space discussed in the text, where the controller can place from 3 to 9 cells in a single step using 'soft' actions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-596bd8f0cbcfc00de61fa772b9317edc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Error in Coil Space Illustration (image)">
      <data key="d0">Design Error in Coil Space Illustration (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image, Figure 5.13, illustrates a 'design error' in a grid-based reinforcement learning environment. It shows a controller placing a component (represented by a black square within a 3x3 block) in an invalid area. The left panel (a) shows a valid placement, while the right panel (b) shows the component shifting into the 'COIL' space, which the accompanying text indicates is a 'bad action' for the agent, demonstrating how history can provide useful information for decision-making beyond strict Markov properties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1b166c346deb9e14c2fe057bc33cdbf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Learning Algorithm Training Flow (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Q-Learning Algorithm Training Flow (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation visually represents the conceptual training flow of the Q-Learning algorithm. It signifies that the overall training process involves optimizing internal parameters ($\theta$) to learn the action-value function ($Q(s,a)$), from which the agent's policy ($\pi$) is subsequently derived. This highlights Q-Learning's value-based approach, contrasting it with direct policy optimization methods like Policy Gradient.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-808c974f7871ba6b7b5d18f72afc85a7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Gradient Training Process Flow (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Policy Gradient Training Process Flow (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation visually summarizes the core operating principle of Policy Gradient algorithms. It illustrates that the 'Training' procedure directly modifies the neural network 'parameters (weights)' denoted by $\\theta$, which in turn define and determine the 'policy' $\\pi$. Essentially, training optimizes $\\theta$ to shape $\\pi$, contrasting with Q-Learning's indirect approach via Q-values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1cc3c9a8202b9032fe06ba473d7c76d8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Learning vs. Policy Gradient Method Comparison Diagram (image)">
      <data key="d0">Q-Learning vs. Policy Gradient Method Comparison Diagram (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image, 'Figure 5.14: Q-learning vs Policy Gradient Method', visually contrasts the pipeline of Q-Learning (a) and Policy Gradient (b) algorithms. Each diagram shows an 'Input (s, a)', the respective learning mechanism ('Q-Learning' or 'Policy Gradient'), and their distinct outputs: Q-Learning produces an 'Output ĝ(s, a, w)' (estimated Q-value), while Policy Gradient produces an 'Output π(a | s, θ)' (policy defining action probabilities). This diagram highlights the fundamental difference in what each algorithm directly optimizes, as discussed in the surrounding text on page 135 and 137.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d057a6f40f6119ac37674e31ce09f40a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Gradient Parameter Update Rule (Gradient Ascent) (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Policy Gradient Parameter Update Rule (Gradient Ascent) (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines the iterative update rule for the parameters (weights) of a policy network in Policy Gradient Reinforcement Learning. It's a gradient ascent step: the policy parameters $\\theta$ are adjusted in the direction that maximizes a performance measure $J$, scaled by a learning rate $\\alpha$. This rule is central to how Policy Gradient algorithms directly learn optimal behavior by optimizing policy parameters, distinguishing them from value-based methods like Q-Learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Performance Measure Approximation (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Policy Performance Measure Approximation (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines an approximation of the Policy Gradient (PG) algorithm's performance measure, $J(\theta)$, as the total accumulated reward, $G(t)$. It is analytically expressed as the expected value of state-action values $Q(s_0, a)$ under the policy's action distribution $p(a|s_0)$ for an initial state $s_0$. It highlights the objective of PG algorithms: to optimize policy parameters to achieve higher expected returns, conceptually linking value-based and policy-based reinforcement learning through the Q-function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb28e5c877704a09cf7eb4cbeb6522fe</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Gradient of Performance Measure (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Policy Gradient of Performance Measure (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines the gradient of the performance measure $J$ with respect to a policy's parameters $\boldsymbol{\theta}$ in Policy Gradient reinforcement learning. It indicates how policy parameters should be adjusted to maximize cumulative rewards, driven by total accumulated reward ($G_t$) and the gradient of the log-probability of chosen actions. This forms the core update rule for neural network weights representing the policy, contrasting with value-based methods like Q-learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Gradient Parameter Update Rule (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Policy Gradient Parameter Update Rule (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines the core update rule for policy parameters (neural network weights) in Policy Gradient algorithms. It uses gradient ascent to adjust parameters, making actions that led to high returns more probable. By leveraging the gradient of the log-policy and the observed episodic return, it directly optimizes the agent's stochastic policy to maximize accumulated rewards, thus bypassing explicit Q-value estimation and enabling more robust learning and inherent exploration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Gradient Implementation Flowchart (image)">
      <data key="d0">Policy Gradient Implementation Flowchart (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This diagram visually explains the Policy Gradient (PG) algorithm, showing how an input state 's' is processed by the "Policy Gradient" module to directly output eight distinct action probabilities, 'p(aᵢ, s)'. These probabilities correspond to actions like 'SOFT UP', 'DOWN', and 'LEFT', illustrating PG's mechanism of directly determining action preferences rather than evaluating action values, as discussed in the surrounding text concerning robust learning and the absence of a Q-Value function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66f8a9943508aa952a9845858b8d3374</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comparison of Q-Learning Greedy Policy vs. Actor-Critic Probabilistic Policy Actions (image)">
      <data key="d0">Comparison of Q-Learning Greedy Policy vs. Actor-Critic Probabilistic Policy Actions (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image displays two bar charts comparing action selection policies. Chart (a) illustrates a greedy Q-Learning (QL) policy where Action 2 is chosen due to its highest Q-value. Chart (b) shows a probabilistic policy, specifically A2C, where all actions have a non-zero probability of being sampled, with Action 2 and 3 having the highest probabilities. The charts demonstrate the difference between deterministic greedy action selection and probabilistic exploration in reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c2c4cc1ff144256a7c660b31a82de7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Softmax Policy Equation for Probabilistic Action Selection (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Softmax Policy Equation for Probabilistic Action Selection (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines a stochastic policy using the softmax function, where the probability of selecting an action 'a' in state 's' is proportional to the exponentiated preference score $f(s, a, \theta)$. Paramount in Policy Gradient and Actor-Critic methods, it enables agents to learn probabilistic behaviors, crucial for exploration and gradient-based optimization in Reinforcement Learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Generic Actor-Critic Model Diagram (image)">
      <data key="d0">Generic Actor-Critic Model Diagram (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates the architecture of a generic Actor-Critic (AC) model, central to reinforcement learning. It depicts two main components, the 'AGENT' and the 'ENVIRONMENT', highlighting their interactive loop. Within the 'AGENT', nodes for 'Critic', 'Input', 'Learning element', 'Actor', and 'Output' show the flow of information; the 'Critic' evaluates inputs, guiding a 'Learning element' to inform the 'Actor's policy, which then generates 'Output' actions for the 'ENVIRONMENT'. This visual explains how the Actor-Critic method functions as described in the accompanying text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b48425c0c345b3be14ed824c6579b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Actor-Critic Policy Parameter Update Rule (Q-value approximation) (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Actor-Critic Policy Parameter Update Rule (Q-value approximation) (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines an iterative update rule for the policy parameters in the Actor-Critic reinforcement learning framework. It uses stochastic gradient ascent, scaling the gradient of the log-policy by the Q-value function, which acts as the 'critic's' evaluation of an action's goodness. This mechanism allows the 'actor' to improve its policy by increasing the likelihood of actions leading to higher Q-values, addressing the delayed feedback issue of pure policy gradient methods and forming the basis for subsequent A2C algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advantage Function Definition (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Advantage Function Definition (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines the advantage function $A(s, a)$ as the difference between the action-value function $Q(s, a)$ and the state-value function $V(s)$. It quantifies how much better a specific action is compared to the average expected outcome from a given state, providing a crucial baseline for more stable and efficient policy gradient updates in the Advantage Actor-Critic (A2C) reinforcement learning algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Value Bellman Expectation Equation (A2C Form) (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Q-Value Bellman Expectation Equation (A2C Form) (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines the Q-value function $Q(s,a)$ as the sum of immediate reward $r(s,a)$ and the discounted value of the next state $\gamma V(s')$. It is a fundamental component of reinforcement learning, especially in Advantage Actor-Critic (A2C) algorithms. It simplifies the computation of the Advantage function, which quantifies how much better an action is compared to the current policy, by allowing it to be derived solely from the state-value function $V(s)$, thereby enhancing learning efficiency and stability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advantage Function Definition for A2C (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Advantage Function Definition for A2C (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines the Advantage function $A(s, a)$, a critical component of the Advantage Actor-Critic (A2C) algorithm in reinforcement learning. It quantifies how much better or worse performing action $a$ in state $s$ is, compared to the average expected outcome for that state under the current policy. By subtracting the state-value function $V(s)$ from an estimate of the Q-value ($r(s, a) + \gamma V(s')$), it provides a low-variance signal for policy gradient updates, enabling more stable and efficient learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advantage Actor-Critic (A2C) Model Architecture Diagram (image)">
      <data key="d0">Advantage Actor-Critic (A2C) Model Architecture Diagram (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This diagram, Figure 5.18, illustrates the architecture of the Advantage Actor-Critic (A2C) model. It shows the flow of state ('s') and action ('a') inputs through the 'Value Prediction (Critic)' and 'Policy Gradient (Actor)' components. The Critic estimates the Q-value ('Q(s,a)'), which, along with the state, influences the Actor to output a probability distribution over eight possible actions (e.g., 'SOFT UP', 'UP'). This visual representation clarifies the interaction between the Critic and Actor networks in the A2C algorithm as described in the accompanying text (Section 5.6 and preceding paragraphs).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27909faa34224de8c011e982c98fcef6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="A2C Neural Network Architecture Diagram (image)">
      <data key="d0">A2C Neural Network Architecture Diagram (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image is a simplified architectural diagram of a neural network used for an Advantage Actor-Critic (A2C) algorithm. It illustrates the data flow from a multi-channel input through a convolutional layer, a flattening operation, dense layers, and then branches into two outputs: one for the 'Value function' (Critic) and another for action probabilities (Actor). The diagram visually represents the network structure described in the accompanying text, highlighting its multi-output design for an agent in an optimal design problem.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5dae035c707962e5992f8ffb5b5c0c7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Table 5.4: C-core Excitation Scenarios for A2C Algorithm Training and Testing (table)">
      <data key="d0">Table 5.4: C-core Excitation Scenarios for A2C Algorithm Training and Testing (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">This table categorizes eight C-core excitation scenarios into 'NIVYL' (Train) and 'LSEL' (Test) groups, detailing coil current and turns. It explicitly defines the training and testing datasets for evaluating the A2C algorithm's generalizability, as discussed in the 'Experiment Setup - A2C' section. The table highlights the specific configurations used to train the A2C agent and those reserved to test its performance on unseen data, providing the foundation for subsequent performance analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6504e2cf679305513217b8548174a68b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conventional C-core Actuator Design and Symmetrical Simulation Setup (image)">
      <data key="d0">Conventional C-core Actuator Design and Symmetrical Simulation Setup (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image, Figure 5.20, illustrates the conventional design of a C-core actuator (a) and its symmetrical simulation setup (b). Diagram (a) details the C-core, coils, and armature with explicit dimensions. Diagram (b) shows a meshed representation of half the geometry, indicating its use in finite element simulations, as described in the accompanying text regarding C-core optimization using A2C, GA, and Q-Learning algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Table 5.5: Comparison of Force magnitude and FE calls for various optimization algorithms (table)">
      <data key="d0">Table 5.5: Comparison of Force magnitude and FE calls for various optimization algorithms (table)</data>
      <data key="d1">table</data>
      <data key="d2">This table compares force magnitudes and Finite Element (FE) calls of Conventional, GA, GA+Filter, QL (SeqTO-v1), and A2C (SeqTO-v2 with and without re-training) algorithms across eight C-core excitation scenarios. It highlights that while QL can achieve high force magnitudes for individual scenarios, the A2C agent, especially after re-training with transfer learning, offers significantly superior computational efficiency (drastically fewer FE calls) while maintaining comparable or excellent force magnitudes, particularly for 'test' scenarios. This supports A2C's effectiveness for generalization and overall resource optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a38c88cccaef550c4d3cde6f591d2fe5</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Geometry and Magnetic Flux Density Distribution Comparison for C-Core Excitation Scenario 1 (image)">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Geometry and Magnetic Flux Density Distribution Comparison for C-Core Excitation Scenario 1 (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image compares the optimal geometry and magnetic flux density distribution for a C-core excitation scenario (1.0 A, 250 Turns) obtained using GA, SeqTO-QL, and SeqTO-RL optimization methods. It highlights differences in core design and magnetic performance, showing SeqTO-QL achieves the highest force with fewer FE calls. The varying designs, particularly near the air gap, are visually contrasted, along with their respective force magnitudes and computational costs, demonstrating the strengths of each method.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3b984e4831b11f20f84bb8669cd7396b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Convergence Results for C-core Excitation Scenario 1 (image)">
      <data key="d0">Convergence Results for C-core Excitation Scenario 1 (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This line graph illustrates the 'Exponentially Average Reward' versus 'Episodes Count' for a reinforcement learning agent. It depicts the convergence of the reward over 5000 training episodes, showing an initial rapid increase followed by fluctuations that gradually stabilize around a reward of 14. This graph specifically represents the convergence results for the 'C-core Excitation Scenario 1' with '1.0 Amps, 250 Turns', providing insight into the agent's learning progression under these specific electrical parameters as mentioned in the surrounding context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a91ba976c2a6f12065edbafc2b708ae2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sequential Design Evolution of C-Core Excitation Scenario 2 (image)">
      <data key="d0">Sequential Design Evolution of C-Core Excitation Scenario 2 (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image illustrates the step-by-step evolution of an optimal geometry design for a C-core under a specific excitation scenario (Coil Current = 0.75 A, Coil Turns = 500), as determined by a reinforcement learning agent. Each sub-image shows the state of the design domain, material remaining, lives left, and the agent's action at a particular step, showcasing the agent's interaction with the environment to achieve an optimal design. This sequence highlights the ability of the A2C agent to adapt and improve even when encountering an 'unseen' excitation condition during initial training stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6248c24886e0bd2c4ecb6bd221bb01dd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="image_01e7a339bf9543d80267239df45cea29">
      <data key="d0">image_01e7a339bf9543d80267239df45cea29</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">```json
{
    "detailed_description": "The image presents a comparative analysis of optimal geometri...</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c48c238aedc178e88e075043138eb72</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal C-core Geometry Topology Optimization Sequence for 0.75A 500 Turns Excitation (image)">
      <data key="d0">Optimal C-core Geometry Topology Optimization Sequence for 0.75A 500 Turns Excitation (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image displays 45 sequential steps of an intelligent agent's topology optimization process for a C-core design, specifically for an excitation of 0.75 A and 500 Turns. Each step shows the evolving C-core geometry (lime green), a fixed component (dark teal bar), and the agent's action point (yellow square), with metrics for step number, material left, and lives left. The sequence illustrates the agent's strategy in forming an optimal core design, demonstrating material reduction and eventual convergence to a C-core shape, relevant to tackling material non-linearity under specific excitation levels as discussed in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1f2486e70169df5ba045508cbd9276f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Convergence Results for 0.75 Amps 500 Turns C-core Excitation (image)">
      <data key="d0">Convergence Results for 0.75 Amps 500 Turns C-core Excitation (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This line graph displays the 'Exponentially Average Reward' against 'Episodes Count' for an algorithm under C-core excitation conditions of 0.75 Amps and 500 turns. The red line shows an initial sharp increase in reward, stabilizing between 28 and 31 after approximately 2500 episodes. The graph demonstrates the algorithm's convergence and effective policy formation, supporting the understanding of its performance in magnetics research, specifically regarding material non-linearity in C-core designs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad8c5c524179d51552952772945627</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal C-core Geometries and Flux Distributions for Excitation Scenario 3 (image)">
      <data key="d0">Optimal C-core Geometries and Flux Distributions for Excitation Scenario 3 (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image displays the optimal C-core geometries and magnetic flux density distributions for an excitation scenario with 1.5A coil current and 500 coil turns, as determined by three different optimization algorithms: GA-On/Off, Seq TO-QL, and Seq TO-RL. Each algorithm's output includes a discretized core design, a corresponding B-distribution plot, and performance metrics (Force and FE Calls). The designs consistently resemble conventional C-core geometry, validating the algorithms' ability to find sensible core shapes, especially under conditions where material saturation is dominant.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-081d5d2ff62504474fc8532dd817fb39</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Geometry Sequence for C-Core Excitation 1.5A 500 Turns (image)">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Geometry Sequence for C-Core Excitation 1.5A 500 Turns (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image displays 37 sequential frames of a magnetic core geometry optimization process for a 1.5A, 500 Turns excitation scenario. Each frame, labeled with step number, material left, and lives left, shows the A2C algorithm developing an optimal L-shaped magnetic material configuration. The progression illustrates the agent's strategy in positioning material, starting from a small block and forming a C-core shape, relating to the document's discussion of different excitation scenarios and optimization algorithms like A2C, GA, and QL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68f5932d6d7a0e838c4c7ec90c8f340d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Convergence Results for 1.5 Amps, 500 Turns Plot (image)">
      <data key="d0">Convergence Results for 1.5 Amps, 500 Turns Plot (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This line graph visualizes the convergence behavior of a reinforcement learning algorithm for a C-core excitation scenario of 1.5 Amps and 500 Turns. It shows the 'Exponentially Average Reward' on the y-axis against 'Episodes Count' on the x-axis. The red line rapidly increases initially, then stabilizes between 70 and 80, demonstrating quick learning and sustained high performance over 3000 episodes, indicating convergence. This figure supports the analysis of optimal design strategies for different excitation values presented in the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4b98982b6cac8028635bf8c32451b12e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal geometry and B distribution for 2.0A, 500 Turns excitation scenario across various optimization algorithms (image)">
      <data key="d0">Optimal geometry and B distribution for 2.0A, 500 Turns excitation scenario across various optimization algorithms (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image compares the optimal geometries and B-field distributions for an excitation scenario of 2.0 A and 500 Turns, using genetic algorithm (GA), QL-based SeqTO-v1, and A2C-based SeqTO-v2. It shows the resulting C-core designs, magnetic flux, and associated 'Force' and 'FE Calls' values, demonstrating that for higher excitation values (such as 750 Amp-Turns onward), the optimal design largely resembles the conventional C-core geometry, consistent with findings from other optimization algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2a5c42907185fecc94ee85f1b33ec380</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Geometry and B Distribution Comparison for Excitation Scenario (1.75A, 500 Turns) (image)">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Geometry and B Distribution Comparison for Excitation Scenario (1.75A, 500 Turns) (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image compares optimal geometries and magnetic field distributions achieved by different sequential topology optimization (SeqTO) methods under an excitation scenario of 1.75 A and 500 turns. It features four panels, each detailing the optimized geometry, the corresponding magnetic field distribution, and performance metrics (Force and FE Calls) for 'Seq TO - QL', 'Seq TO - RL', and 'Seq TO - RL (Re-Train)'. The visual analysis highlights differences in material distribution and magnetic field patterns, alongside quantitative comparisons of force generation and computational cost.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2785b4e150ada39f3077e573325eeb4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Geometries and B Field Distributions for Excitation Scenarios using SeqTO-QL and SeqTO-RL (image)">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Geometries and B Field Distributions for Excitation Scenarios using SeqTO-QL and SeqTO-RL (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This image displays a comparative analysis of optimal geometries and magnetic field distributions achieved through different sequential topology optimization (SeqTO) methods: Q-Learning (QL) and Reinforcement Learning (RL), specifically in three scenarios: SeqTO-QL, SeqTO-RL, and SeqTO-RL (Re-Train). Each scenario presents a material geometry, its corresponding magnetic field distribution, and performance metrics including force and finite element (FE) calls, highlighting the efficiency gains of re-training the RL model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a11edf0b881aae28423c1b0d33893b2d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal C-Core Actuator Geometries and B-Field Distributions for Various Optimization Algorithms (image)">
      <data key="d0">Optimal C-Core Actuator Geometries and B-Field Distributions for Various Optimization Algorithms (image)</data>
      <data key="d1">image</data>
      <data key="d2">This multi-panel figure compares optimal C-core actuator geometries and magnetic field distributions (B) obtained using different optimization algorithms: Genetic Algorithm (GA), Q-Learning (QL) with SeqTO-v1, and A2C-based Reinforcement Learning (RL) with SeqTO-v2, including a retrained RL version. For an excitation of 0.25 A and 500 Turns, the figure illustrates the resulting force and the computational cost (FE Calls) of each method, demonstrating the efficiency and performance trade-offs between conventional and deep learning-based approaches for topology optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="A2C Stochastic Policy Function (equation)">
      <data key="d0">A2C Stochastic Policy Function (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines the A2C (Actor-Critic) agent's stochastic policy, $\\pi_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}(a | \\boldsymbol{s})$, using a softmax function. It calculates the probability of selecting action 'a' in state 's', based on a parameterized score function f(s, a). This probabilistic approach allows for exploration and explains the generation of multiple optimal designs in topology optimization for electromagnetic actuators, contrasting with deterministic policies like Q-Learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Action Selection in Q-Learning (Greedy Policy) (equation)">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Action Selection in Q-Learning (Greedy Policy) (equation)</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines the optimal action ($a^*$) in Q-Learning under a greedy policy. It states that the agent selects the action 'a' that maximizes the Q-value function ($Q(s, a)$) for a given state $s$. It highlights the deterministic nature of Q-Learning's action selection, contrasting with the stochastic policies of algorithms like A2C, and explains why Q-Learning typically yields a single optimal design while stochastic policies can produce multiple optimal geometries in topology optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neural Network Architecture Diagram">
      <data key="d0">Neural Network Architecture Diagram</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Neural Network Architecture Diagram is a flow chart depicting the network's structure, including input, encoder, context vector, attention mechanism, decoder, and output layers.&lt;SEP&gt;A diagram that primarily illustrates a flow from inputs to an output, framed as a "Feedforward Network," a central "Encoder-Decoder" structure, and an "Efficiency Map" as the final output.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288721</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heat Map-Style Contour Plots">
      <data key="d0">Heat Map-Style Contour Plots</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Heat Map-Style Contour Plots are a type of visualization used to display data, in this case, likely representing power factor values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288721</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Internal Permanent Magnet (IPM) Motor">
      <data key="d0">Internal Permanent Magnet (IPM) Motor</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">An Internal Permanent Magnet (IPM) Motor is an electrical motor type, depicted in an inset technical drawing within one of the contour plots.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288721</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Geometrical Information">
      <data key="d0">Geometrical Information</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Geometrical Information is one of the input parameters for the neural network.&lt;SEP&gt;Input data representing the physical dimensions or structural properties of an object, fed into the Feedforward Network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288721</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Torque, Speed, Advance Angle, Current">
      <data key="d0">Torque, Speed, Advance Angle, Current</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Torque, Speed, Advance Angle, Current are input parameters for the neural network.&lt;SEP&gt;These are operational parameters that serve as inputs directly to the Encoder part of the LSTM network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288721</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LSTM #1">
      <data key="d0">LSTM #1</data>
      <data key="d1">architecture</data>
      <data key="d2">LSTM #1 is the first Long Short-Term Memory layer within the Encoder block of the neural network.&lt;SEP&gt;LSTM #1 is a green-colored layer within the ENCODER section of the neural network, receiving input from "Geometrical Information" and "Torque, Speed, Advance Angle, Current."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288724</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LSTM #2">
      <data key="d0">LSTM #2</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">LSTM #2 is a yellow-colored Long Short-Term Memory layer in the encoder section, receiving input from "LSTM #1."&lt;SEP&gt;LSTM #2 is the second Long Short-Term Memory layer within the Encoder block of the neural network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288724</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Context Vector &amp; Attention Mechanism">
      <data key="d0">Context Vector &amp; Attention Mechanism</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Context Vector &amp; Attention Mechanism represents an intermediate process between the encoder and decoder sections of the neural network.&lt;SEP&gt;A component in the neural network architecture that processes information from the Encoder before feeding it to the Decoder, allowing the model to focus on relevant parts of the input.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288724</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LSTM #3">
      <data key="d0">LSTM #3</data>
      <data key="d1">architecture</data>
      <data key="d2">LSTM #3 is a Long Short-Term Memory layer within the Decoder block of the neural network.&lt;SEP&gt;LSTM #3 is a red-colored layer within the DECODER section, feeding into the "Time-distributed Dense layer."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288724</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Image Content Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Image Content Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Image Content Analysis refers to a method or process focused on examining, interpreting, and describing the visual characteristics and information present within an image. This analytical approach aims to detail what is visually depicted, encompassing elements, colors, and layout.

The analysis provides a comprehensive understanding of an image's content, including both visual and sometimes textual elements. This can involve identifying specific components, labels, graphs, and graphical representations. Furthermore, Image Content Analysis extends to contextual information often associated with images, such as paths, captions, and footnotes, particularly in scientific figures or structured documents. It serves to provide a detailed visual description, ensuring a thorough understanding of the image's significance and data representations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff320ec3e3a24e3c69d31630b6cba885&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b98982b6cac8028635bf8c32451b12e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c85d4390c7f5cda3bb7e9cc87cbcec0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17fa5f6a446284811fca7ceb6337e9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a5c42907185fecc94ee85f1b33ec380&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-193c3a719cf23865bd32e0d110afb409&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb99d0c44b7c6ee269cad3cce40b7be7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92cc2b02ae3a119d92f0d1a7f6617885&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a7d0fc487b66b91a1d8bae6610798e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8b78525002f5dfa42398879cc72242c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5dae035c707962e5992f8ffb5b5c0c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-248a0b72513cd485685d4b3e3864e01e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abb64932da4baf92322bc61ecf0df44f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a0921c09c7af3fdaeac2a38ff90bbb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Normalized Torque (Tm)">
      <data key="d0">Normalized Torque (Tm)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Normalized Torque (Tm) is represented on the Y-axis of the contour plots, ranging from 0 to 1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Normalized Torque (Nm)">
      <data key="d0">Normalized Torque (Nm)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Normalized Torque (Nm) is represented on the X-axis of the contour plots, ranging from 0 to 1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Color-Coding Scheme">
      <data key="d0">Color-Coding Scheme</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The color-coding scheme (green, yellow, red) indicates the changes in weights for each layer during a transfer learning process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transfer Learning Process">
      <data key="d0">Transfer Learning Process</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Transfer Learning Process involves adapting a pre-trained model to a new task, with color-coding indicating changes in layer weights.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flow Diagram">
      <data key="d0">Flow Diagram</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The flow diagram represents a neural network architecture, illustrating a deep learning model for mapping from input parameters to a Power Factor Map.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contour Plots">
      <data key="d0">Contour Plots</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Contour plots are a visual representation used in the image to display efficiency maps.&lt;SEP&gt;The contour plots are two heat map-style plots on the right of Figure 3.19, displaying normalized torque (Tm) versus normalized torque (Nm) with color-coded regions, likely representing power factor values.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of graphical representation showing three-dimensional relationships on a two-dimensional surface, particularly used here to display efficiency maps.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee3a2fda6147e62f8093cb1da810ea6f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Time-distributed Dense Layer">
      <data key="d0">Time-distributed Dense Layer</data>
      <data key="d1">architecture</data>
      <data key="d2">The Time-distributed Dense Layer is a yellow-colored layer in the DECODER section, receiving input from LSTM #3 and outputting the "Power Factor Map."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288728</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TM (Normalized Torque)">
      <data key="d0">TM (Normalized Torque)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">TM represents normalized torque and is displayed on the Y-axis of the contour plots.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288729</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NM (Normalized Torque)">
      <data key="d0">NM (Normalized Torque)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">NM represents normalized torque and is displayed on the X-axis of the contour plots.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfe7a5106e28df4677808f1339897e29</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288729</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Process">
      <data key="d0">Design Process</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Design Process is the left side of the 'V' in the V-Design and Manufacturing Cycle, where product requirements are specified and engineering design and optimization occur.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c0af18e12848c44ab0d9b01471789474</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288729</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Validation">
      <data key="d0">Validation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Validation is the right side of the 'V' in the V-Design and Manufacturing Cycle, encompassing manufacturing and testing stages such as fabrication, assembly, and system testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c0af18e12848c44ab0d9b01471789474</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288729</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Simulation-Based Design">
      <data key="d0">Simulation-Based Design</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Simulation-Based Design is a horizontal blue line at the bottom of the 'V-Design and Manufacturing Cycle' that connects the design and validation phases, emphasizing the role of advanced tools and modeling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c0af18e12848c44ab0d9b01471789474</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288729</data>
    </node>
    <node id="System-Level Requirements">
      <data key="d0">System-Level Requirements</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">System-Level Requirements initiate the design process in the V-Design and Manufacturing Cycle and are revisited if system testing indicates they are not satisfied.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c0af18e12848c44ab0d9b01471789474</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288731</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Subsystem-Level Requirements">
      <data key="d0">Subsystem-Level Requirements</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Subsystem-Level Requirements are derived from system-level requirements, detailing the needs for individual components or parts of the larger system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c0af18e12848c44ab0d9b01471789474</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288731</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Subsystem Sizing">
      <data key="d0">Subsystem Sizing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Subsystem Sizing is a step in the design process where the dimensions and specifications of subsystems are determined.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c0af18e12848c44ab0d9b01471789474</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288732</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Detailed Modeling, Simulation, and Optimization for Subsystems">
      <data key="d0">Detailed Modeling, Simulation, and Optimization for Subsystems</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">This central step in the V-Design and Manufacturing Cycle bridges the design and implementation/validation phases, involving electromagnetic, thermal, structural, and vibro-acoustic design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c0af18e12848c44ab0d9b01471789474</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288732</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electromagnetic Design">
      <data key="d0">Electromagnetic Design</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Electromagnetic Design is a type of design integrated into the "Detailed modeling, simulation, and optimization for subsystems" step.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c0af18e12848c44ab0d9b01471789474</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288732</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thermal Design">
      <data key="d0">Thermal Design</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Thermal Design is a specific type of design concern addressed during the detailed modeling and optimization phase for subsystems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c0af18e12848c44ab0d9b01471789474</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288732</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Structural Design">
      <data key="d0">Structural Design</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Structural Design is a specific type of design that focuses on the physical integrity and strength of subsystems, part of the detailed modeling and optimization phase.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c0af18e12848c44ab0d9b01471789474</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288732</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vibro-Acoustic Design">
      <data key="d0">Vibro-Acoustic Design</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Vibro-Acoustic Design is a specific type of design that deals with vibration and sound characteristics of subsystems, included in the detailed modeling and optimization phase.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c0af18e12848c44ab0d9b01471789474</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288732</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Subsystem Fabrication">
      <data key="d0">Subsystem Fabrication</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Subsystem Fabrication is a stage in the validation process where individual components or parts of the system are manufactured.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c0af18e12848c44ab0d9b01471789474</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288734</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Integration and Assembly">
      <data key="d0">Integration and Assembly</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Integration and Assembly is a step in the validation phase that follows Subsystem Fabrication.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c0af18e12848c44ab0d9b01471789474</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288734</data>
    </node>
    <node id="System Testing">
      <data key="d0">System Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">System Testing is the final stage in the validation process where the integrated system is tested to ensure it meets the initial requirements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c0af18e12848c44ab0d9b01471789474</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288734</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V-Design And Manufacturing Cycle">
      <data key="d0">V-Design And Manufacturing Cycle</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The V-Design and Manufacturing Cycle is a V-model diagram that illustrates the process for electric motor drives, showing design on the left and validation on the right, connected by simulation-based design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c0af18e12848c44ab0d9b01471789474</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288734</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Detailed Modeling, Simulation, And Optimization For Subsystems">
      <data key="d0">Detailed Modeling, Simulation, And Optimization For Subsystems</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">This stage is a central part of the V-Design and Manufacturing Cycle, involving in-depth analysis and refinement of subsystem designs through computational methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c0af18e12848c44ab0d9b01471789474</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288734</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Integration And Assembly">
      <data key="d0">Integration And Assembly</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Integration and Assembly is a stage in the validation process where fabricated subsystems are put together to form the complete system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c0af18e12848c44ab0d9b01471789474</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288736</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feedback Loop">
      <data key="d0">Feedback Loop</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Feedback Loop, indicated by a red arrow labeled 'Not Satisfied', connects System Testing back to System-Level Requirements, signifying a corrective mechanism when requirements are not met.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c0af18e12848c44ab0d9b01471789474</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288736</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Cycle Improvement">
      <data key="d0">Design Cycle Improvement</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Design Cycle Improvement is a goal mentioned in the context of the thesis, aiming to enhance the efficiency and effectiveness of the design process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c0af18e12848c44ab0d9b01471789474</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288736</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Samples">
      <data key="d0">Design Samples</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Design samples refer to two distinct IPM motor designs evaluated in the figure, each with target and prediction efficiency maps.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288737</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Targets">
      <data key="d0">Targets</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Targets represent the ground truth efficiency maps, likely derived from Finite Element (FE) simulations, used as a reference for comparison.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288737</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Confusion Matrices">
      <data key="d0">Confusion Matrices</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Confusion matrices are quantitative performance metrics that include values for True Negatives (TN), False Positives (FP), False Negatives (FN), and True Positives (TP), along with Classification Accuracy (CA).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288738</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IPM Motor Stator">
      <data key="d0">IPM Motor Stator</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">An IPM (Internal Permanent Magnet) motor stator is a component of an IPM motor, visible in cutaway diagrams embedded within the target efficiency plots, showing internal structures like windings and stator teeth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288738</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IPM Motor Rotor">
      <data key="d0">IPM Motor Rotor</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">An IPM (Internal Permanent Magnet) motor rotor is a component of an IPM motor, visible in cutaway diagrams embedded within the target efficiency plots, showing internal structures like permanent magnets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288739</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Windings">
      <data key="d0">Windings</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Windings are represented by four thin, orange, rectangular strips positioned vertically, acting as coils made of copper within the transformer.&lt;SEP&gt;Windings are internal components of an IPM motor stator, depicted in gold in the embedded cutaway diagrams.&lt;SEP&gt;Coiled wires, shown as brown sections in the stator of the motor cross-section, used to generate magnetic fields.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c59eba9d5ca580dfd954f743c22649a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288739</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stator Teeth">
      <data key="d0">Stator Teeth</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Stator teeth are internal components of an IPM motor stator, depicted in gray in the embedded cutaway diagrams.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288739</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neural Network Model">
      <data key="d0">Neural Network Model</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A neural network model is the method used to generate prediction efficiency maps for IPM motor designs, demonstrating high accuracy and speed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288739</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Speed (Nm)">
      <data key="d0">Speed (Nm)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Speed (Nm) is a parameter represented on the x-axis of the efficiency maps, ranging from 0 to 1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288741</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Torque (Tm)">
      <data key="d0">Torque (Tm)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Torque (Tm) is a parameter represented on the y-axis of the efficiency maps, ranging from 0 to 1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288741</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Color Bar">
      <data key="d0">Color Bar</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A color bar is a legend positioned horizontally at the bottom of the image, showing the range of values represented by different colors in the heat map, from 0 to 1.&lt;SEP&gt;The Color Bar is a unified legend placed below the panels of Figure 2.13, transitioning from dark blue (0) to red (1) to indicate data values for both error and uncertainty.&lt;SEP&gt;A color bar is a common visual element shared by both contour plots, indicating the scale of the mapped data.&lt;SEP&gt;A color bar at the bottom of the figure provides a legend for the efficiency maps, indicating efficiency levels from 0 to 0.9.&lt;SEP&gt;A Color Bar is a visual scale accompanying each heatmap, mapping numerical values to the color spectrum displayed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c85d4390c7f5cda3bb7e9cc87cbcec0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee3a2fda6147e62f8093cb1da810ea6f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb99d0c44b7c6ee269cad3cce40b7be7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92cc2b02ae3a119d92f0d1a7f6617885</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288741</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X-axis">
      <data key="d0">X-axis</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The x-axis of each contour plot is labeled "Nm" and spans from 0 to 1 with major ticks at 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8, and 1.&lt;SEP&gt;The horizontal axis on a graph, labeled "Nm" (Newton-meters) for torque in the efficiency maps.&lt;SEP&gt;The X-axis is part of a coordinate system, represented by a red arrow pointing to the right, indicating the horizontal orientation of the design space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0691c9f7251ff59c05b1bc277ea93e1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288741</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Y-axis">
      <data key="d0">Y-axis</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The y-axis of each contour plot is labeled "Tm" and spans from 0 to 1 with major ticks at 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8, and 1.&lt;SEP&gt;The vertical axis on a graph, labeled "Tm" (possibly normalized speed or other operational parameter) in the efficiency maps.&lt;SEP&gt;The Y-axis is part of a coordinate system, represented by a green arrow pointing upwards, indicating the vertical orientation of the design space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0691c9f7251ff59c05b1bc277ea93e1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288741</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nm">
      <data key="d0">Nm</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Nm is the label for the x-axis in the efficiency maps, representing a motor characteristic that increases along the axis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288741</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tm">
      <data key="d0">Tm</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Tm is the label for the y-axis in the efficiency maps, representing a motor characteristic that increases along the axis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288741</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TN (True Negatives)">
      <data key="d0">TN (True Negatives)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">TN (True Negatives) is a value within the confusion matrix that quantifies correctly identified low-efficiency regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288743</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FP (False Positives)">
      <data key="d0">FP (False Positives)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">FP (False Positives) is a value within the confusion matrix that quantifies incorrectly identified high-efficiency regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288743</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FN (False Negatives)">
      <data key="d0">FN (False Negatives)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">FN (False Negatives) is a value within the confusion matrix that quantifies incorrectly identified low-efficiency regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288743</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TP (True Positives)">
      <data key="d0">TP (True Positives)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">TP (True Positives) is a value within the confusion matrix that quantifies correctly identified high-efficiency regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288743</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CA (Classification Accuracy)">
      <data key="d0">CA (Classification Accuracy)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">CA (Classification Accuracy) is a percentage derived from the confusion matrix, indicating the model's overall accuracy in classifying efficiency regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ce70f04fc1f66c450a145e83381de84</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288743</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.26">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.26</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.26 is a line graph titled "Convergence results for 0.75 Amps, 500 Turns" showing the Exponentially Average Reward against Episodes Count.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad8c5c524179d51552952772945627</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288743</data>
    </node>
    <node id="0.75 Amps">
      <data key="d0">0.75 Amps</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">0.75 Amps is a specific C-core excitation condition for coil current, used in the convergence results shown in Figure 5.26.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad8c5c524179d51552952772945627</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288743</data>
    </node>
    <node id="500 Turns">
      <data key="d0">500 Turns</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">500 turns refers to the winding specification for the C-core, relating to the excitation current of 1.0 Amps to maximize force on the armature.&lt;SEP&gt;500 Turns is a specific C-core excitation condition for coil turns, used in the convergence results shown in Figure 5.26.&lt;SEP&gt;500 Turns is the number of turns specified for the excitation of the particular sequence shown in the image.&lt;SEP&gt;500 Turns represents a specific excitation scenario for which convergence results are depicted.&lt;SEP&gt;500 Turns refers to the number of coil turns specified in the excitation scenario for the C-core actuator.&lt;SEP&gt;500 Turns is the number of turns for the excitation scenario.&lt;SEP&gt;500 Turns represents the number of turns used in the excitation scenario.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4b98982b6cac8028635bf8c32451b12e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a0921c09c7af3fdaeac2a38ff90bbb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a5c42907185fecc94ee85f1b33ec380&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11edf0b881aae28423c1b0d33893b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ad8c5c524179d51552952772945627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f2486e70169df5ba045508cbd9276f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288745</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exponentially Average Reward">
      <data key="d0">Exponentially Average Reward</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Exponentially Average Reward is a metric plotted on the y-axis of the line graph in Figure 5.8 (b), indicating the performance of the Deep Q-learning algorithm over episodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Exponentially Average Reward is a metric represented on the y-axis of the graph, ranging from 6 to 14, indicating a measure of performance or return over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Exponentially Average Reward is a metric plotted on the Y-axis of the graph, showing the reward obtained by the algorithm over time.&lt;SEP&gt;The Exponentially Average Reward is a dependent variable on the y-axis of the graph, ranging from 10 to 80, indicating the performance metric of the reinforcement learning algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4b98982b6cac8028635bf8c32451b12e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ad8c5c524179d51552952772945627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a91ba976c2a6f12065edbafc2b708ae2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a0921c09c7af3fdaeac2a38ff90bbb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288745</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Episodes Count">
      <data key="d0">Episodes Count</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Episodes Count is the x-axis of the Exponentially Average Reward graph, ranging from 0 to 800, representing the number of training iterations for the Deep Q-learning algorithm.&lt;SEP&gt;Episodes Count is a metric represented on the x-axis of the graph, ranging from 0 to 5000, indicating the number of training episodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Episodes Count is a variable plotted on the X-axis of Figure 5.26, representing the number of training episodes for an algorithm.&lt;SEP&gt;Episodes Count is an independent variable on the x-axis of the graph, ranging from 0 to 3000, representing the number of training iterations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4b98982b6cac8028635bf8c32451b12e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ad8c5c524179d51552952772945627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a91ba976c2a6f12065edbafc2b708ae2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a0921c09c7af3fdaeac2a38ff90bbb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288746</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-Core Excitation Conditions">
      <data key="d0">C-Core Excitation Conditions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">C-Core Excitation Conditions refer to parameters like coil current and coil turns that define the environment for the algorithm's performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad8c5c524179d51552952772945627</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288746</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coil Current = 1.5A Coil Turns = 500">
      <data key="d0">Coil Current = 1.5A Coil Turns = 500</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">This is a specific C-core Excitation Scenario 3 mentioned in the text, distinct from the conditions shown in Figure 5.26.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad8c5c524179d51552952772945627</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288746</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reinforcement Learning Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Reinforcement Learning Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Reinforcement learning algorithms exhibit oscillations in reward values that tend to stabilize over time, as seen in the graph.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad8c5c524179d51552952772945627</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288748</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Algorithm Performance">
      <data key="d0">Algorithm Performance</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Algorithm performance refers to how well an algorithm functions, as illustrated by the convergence results shown in the graph.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad8c5c524179d51552952772945627</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288748</data>
    </node>
