      <data key="d0">Coil Current (0.75A)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Coil Current (0.75A) is a specific C-core excitation condition used in the experiment depicted by the graph.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad8c5c524179d51552952772945627</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288748</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Effective Policy">
      <data key="d0">Effective Policy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An Effective Policy is developed by the agent through learning, leading to stabilized rewards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad8c5c524179d51552952772945627</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288748</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.4">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.4</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.4 is a schematic representation of domain discretization and a 2-D Unit microstructure with cavity, used in the homogenization method.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb6925d3d6ed02adeec99edaa647d90b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288750</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2-D Unit Microstructure">
      <data key="d0">2-D Unit Microstructure</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A 2-D Unit Microstructure is a small, representative part of a material's internal structure, shown with a cavity, and described by three design variables in a 2D design space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb6925d3d6ed02adeec99edaa647d90b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288751</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Macroscopic Design Domain">
      <data key="d0">Macroscopic Design Domain</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Macroscopic Design Domain is a larger area from which a selected region is extracted for discretization. It is represented as a trapezoidal shape labeled "Design Domain" with the Greek letter Ω (Omega) inside.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb6925d3d6ed02adeec99edaa647d90b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288751</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microscopic Unit Cell">
      <data key="d0">Microscopic Unit Cell</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Microscopic Unit Cell is a single unit microstructure, specifically a square cavity within a larger black square, representing the detailed view of a discretized element.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb6925d3d6ed02adeec99edaa647d90b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288751</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Three Design Variables">
      <data key="d0">Three Design Variables</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Three Design Variables (x, y, and θ) are parameters used to describe a microstructure in a 2D design space within the homogenization method.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb6925d3d6ed02adeec99edaa647d90b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288751</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X-Dimension">
      <data key="d0">X-Dimension</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The X-Dimension is one of the three design variables, indicating the horizontal dimension of the white cavity within the unit microstructure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb6925d3d6ed02adeec99edaa647d90b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288752</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Y-Dimension">
      <data key="d0">Y-Dimension</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Y-Dimension is one of the three design variables, indicating the vertical dimension of the white cavity within the unit microstructure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb6925d3d6ed02adeec99edaa647d90b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288752</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Theta Angle">
      <data key="d0">Theta Angle</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Theta Angle (θ) is one of the three design variables, representing the orientation or rotation of the microstructure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb6925d3d6ed02adeec99edaa647d90b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288753</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cavity">
      <data key="d0">Cavity</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A cavity is a square-shaped void within the 2-D Unit Microstructure, defined by dimensions x and y.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb6925d3d6ed02adeec99edaa647d90b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288753</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Greek Letter Omega (Ω)">
      <data key="d0">Greek Letter Omega (Ω)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Greek Letter Omega (Ω) is used to label the Design Domain in the schematic representation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb6925d3d6ed02adeec99edaa647d90b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288753</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Graphical Elements">
      <data key="d0">Graphical Elements</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Graphical Elements are rendered in black and white in the image, emphasizing the technical and scientific style of the diagram.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb6925d3d6ed02adeec99edaa647d90b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288753</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Selected Region">
      <data key="d0">Selected Region</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Selected Region is indicated by a dotted-line oval within the Design Domain, representing an area chosen for further analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb6925d3d6ed02adeec99edaa647d90b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288754</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grid of Smaller, Uniformly Sized Squares">
      <data key="d0">Grid of Smaller, Uniformly Sized Squares</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A Grid of Smaller, Uniformly Sized Squares fills an oval in the middle element, implying a discretization of the selected region into microstructures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb6925d3d6ed02adeec99edaa647d90b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Square Cavity">
      <data key="d0">Square Cavity</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A Square Cavity is depicted within a larger black square, representing the void in the unit microstructure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb6925d3d6ed02adeec99edaa647d90b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dimensions x and y">
      <data key="d0">Dimensions x and y</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Dimensions x and y are labels with arrows indicating the horizontal and vertical dimensions of the white cavity within the unit microstructure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb6925d3d6ed02adeec99edaa647d90b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Angle Theta (θ)">
      <data key="d0">Angle Theta (θ)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Angle Theta (θ) is an arc with an arrow denoting an angle, typically representing the orientation or rotation of the microstructure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb6925d3d6ed02adeec99edaa647d90b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.26">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.26</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.26 is a visual representation titled 'Optimal solution 3x3 controller (SeqTO-v1) with TD-learning, on a C-core design domain'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288756</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Component (a)">
      <data key="d0">Component (a)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Component (a) is a grid-based diagram within Figure 4.26, showing the optimal geometry and an action sequence in a 3x3 linear environment for a C-core design.&lt;SEP&gt;Component (a) within Figure 5.8 depicts a grid-based representation for the optimal geometry of a C-core, along with the sequence of steps taken to achieve it.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a0921c09c7af3fdaeac2a38ff90bbb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288757</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Component (b)">
      <data key="d0">Component (b)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Component (b) is a 2D line graph within Figure 4.26, illustrating the 'Force on Armature [N]' against 'Step Number' over an episode.&lt;SEP&gt;Component (b) within Figure 5.8 is a line graph titled "Exponentially Average Reward" versus "Episodes Count," illustrating the convergence behavior of the Deep Q-learning algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a0921c09c7af3fdaeac2a38ff90bbb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288757</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grid-Based Diagram">
      <data key="d0">Grid-Based Diagram</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The grid-based diagram, labeled as Component (a), depicts the optimal geometry and action sequence for the C-core design, using a matrix of square cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288757</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Step Sequence">
      <data key="d0">Step Sequence</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Step Sequence includes labeled grey cells from 'STEP 1' to 'STEP 17', representing the progression of actions or changes in the C-core design.&lt;SEP&gt;The Step Sequence (STEP 1 through STEP 17) in Component (a) of Figure 5.8 outlines the visual representation of the construction of the C-core geometry.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a0921c09c7af3fdaeac2a38ff90bbb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288759</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2D Line Graph">
      <data key="d0">2D Line Graph</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A 2D line graph is a visual representation used to compare training curves, showing 'Epochs' on the x-axis and 'Normalized Error (Validation)' on the y-axis.&lt;SEP&gt;The 2D line graph, labeled as Component (b), shows the relationship between Force on Armature and Step Number.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-248a0b72513cd485685d4b3e3864e01e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288760</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Force on Armature">
      <data key="d0">Force on Armature</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Force on Armature, measured in Newtons (N), is a variable plotted on the y-axis of Component (b), representing the force exerted on the armature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288760</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Step Number">
      <data key="d0">Step Number</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Step number represents the progression of the agent's actions within the reinforcement learning process, changing from 12 to 14 across the diagrams.&lt;SEP&gt;Step Number is a variable ranging from 0 to 18, displayed on the x-axis of the 2D line graph in Component (b), representing steps within an episode.&lt;SEP&gt;Step Number, abbreviated as 'S:', indicates the chronological progress within the optimization sequence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1474f4155ebb5657d8ca7f3c0d29b49&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6248c24886e0bd2c4ecb6bd221bb01dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288760</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Initial Position">
      <data key="d0">Initial Position</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Initial Position refers to a specific, labeled gray area within a discrete design domain, typically indicating a starting configuration or a reference point for an agent or a design process. It is often described as a gray rectangular cell. In some contexts, it is situated between two 'COIL' sections, specifically in the second and third columns of the second row, or above the central empty space between coils. It can also be found in Component (a) of figures, such as Figure 5.8, where it marks the starting point for a sequence of steps, for example, in a C-core's construction sequence. This gray square is specifically labeled "INITIAL POSITION" within linear magnetic material, serving as a crucial starting point in the design process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec0d779e474774183c36eade5f33db6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b76c5d19e7bcbbd5f11b71588e066f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8803108c64e008ff20b50a70b77f8af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0534c4408a52c608a5a24ccf9951f5f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abb64932da4baf92322bc61ecf0df44f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a0921c09c7af3fdaeac2a38ff90bbb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ARMATURE">
      <data key="d0">ARMATURE</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The ARMATURE is a key component of the C-core electromagnetic (EM) actuator, consistently described as a rectangular block. It is typically depicted as vertically oriented, tall, and narrow, positioned to the right of the C-core and separated from it by an air gap. While primarily characterized as a fixed component of the C-core structure, its design and function suggest it is also a moving part that interacts with the C-core or an object whose movement or interaction is being optimized. Different descriptions vary slightly in its depicted shade, referring to it as gray, light gray, or dark gray. It is a functional area within the design domain or grid, often located on the far right. The ARMATURE experiences force, and its interaction is influenced by the magnetic field generated by the COIL elements, with its optimization being crucial for maximizing this force.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec0d779e474774183c36eade5f33db6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0691c9f7251ff59c05b1bc277ea93e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f031035d088ffa5bcc3e370fe22c958&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca13e3ef7d4bf929b84d6b30574e3485&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31fcb301590d668517d7e9583a508d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1474f4155ebb5657d8ca7f3c0d29b49&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57172dfb9b0c90234ff04b1a5519b71e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1b166c346deb9e14c2fe057bc33cdbf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26404cee26f7623e6d969e264821542f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d94fd82540e01e83a3a7b0febcf3d7ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abb64932da4baf92322bc61ecf0df44f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monotonic Increase in Reward Function">
      <data key="d0">Monotonic Increase in Reward Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The monotonic increase in reward function, or force, is a characteristic observed in the 2D line graph, signifying the impact of the topology optimization sequence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288762</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topology Optimization Sequence">
      <data key="d0">Topology Optimization Sequence</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Topology Optimization Sequence is a process that leads to the observed monotonic increase in reward function or force.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288762</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Solution 3x3 Controller (SeqTO-v1)">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Solution 3x3 Controller (SeqTO-v1)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Optimal Solution 3x3 Controller (SeqTO-v1) is a technical concept representing an optimized control system for a 3x3 linear environment using Sequential Topology Optimization (SeqTO-v1).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288762</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Component (A)">
      <data key="d0">Component (A)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Component (a) is a visual element within Figure 4.26, displaying a grid-based diagram representing the optimal geometry and an action sequence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288762</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Component (B)">
      <data key="d0">Component (B)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Component (b) is a visual element within Figure 4.26, which is a 2D line graph.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288762</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COIL">
      <data key="d0">COIL</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The COIL refers to a fixed component within various electromagnetic designs, consistently depicted as two distinct, often vertically oriented, rectangular elements. These components are primarily characterized by their reddish or pink hues, including red, light red, reddish-pink, salmon, or magenta, and may exhibit a textured appearance, such as circular textures or a textured rectangular region, indicating windings.

The COILs are strategically placed within the vertical arms or inner cavity of a C-core structure, or more generally within a discrete design domain or grid, and are crucial for the operation of electromagnetic actuators or similar systems. Their primary function is to generate a magnetic field, acting as electromagnetic coils or windings. In some representations, the COILs appear as prominent light red rectangular blocks or tall, narrow vertical bar components.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0691c9f7251ff59c05b1bc277ea93e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f031035d088ffa5bcc3e370fe22c958&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca13e3ef7d4bf929b84d6b30574e3485&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31fcb301590d668517d7e9583a508d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1474f4155ebb5657d8ca7f3c0d29b49&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57172dfb9b0c90234ff04b1a5519b71e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0534c4408a52c608a5a24ccf9951f5f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8803108c64e008ff20b50a70b77f8af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1b166c346deb9e14c2fe057bc33cdbf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26404cee26f7623e6d969e264821542f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d94fd82540e01e83a3a7b0febcf3d7ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abb64932da4baf92322bc61ecf0df44f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288763</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action Sequence (Component A)">
      <data key="d0">Action Sequence (Component A)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Action Sequence in Component (a) depicts the steps involved in the topology optimization process, labeled 'STEP 1' through 'STEP 17'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288764</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Force On Armature [N]">
      <data key="d0">Force On Armature [N]</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Force on Armature [N] is a measurement of force in Newtons displayed on the y-axis of the 2D line graph in Component (b).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e33feda9b614873c4d42a0aa2ba3f7c8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288764</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Core Design">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Core Design</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnetic core design is the process of optimizing the structure and material distribution of a magnetic core for specific excitation properties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6248c24886e0bd2c4ecb6bd221bb01dd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288766</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sequential Steps">
      <data key="d0">Sequential Steps</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Sequential steps are distinct stages in the optimization process, each showing the state of the magnetic core at a different point.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6248c24886e0bd2c4ecb6bd221bb01dd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288767</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yellow Square">
      <data key="d0">Yellow Square</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A small yellow square acts as a pointer, indicating the agent's current position and interaction point within the design domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6248c24886e0bd2c4ecb6bd221bb01dd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288767</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Geometry for Excitation">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Geometry for Excitation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Optimal geometry for excitation refers to the most efficient configuration for generating an excitation, specified with parameters like 1.0 A and 250 Turns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6248c24886e0bd2c4ecb6bd221bb01dd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288767</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Material Left">
      <data key="d0">Material Left</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Material left is a metric displayed in the header of each diagram, indicating remaining material, with a constant value of 84 in this sequence.&lt;SEP&gt;Material Left is a metric indicating the remaining material, decreasing steadily from 84 to 66 across the steps in Figure 5.4.&lt;SEP&gt;Material Left, abbreviated as 'M:', quantifies the amount of magnetic material remaining in the design at each step.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1474f4155ebb5657d8ca7f3c0d29b49&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6248c24886e0bd2c4ecb6bd221bb01dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f031035d088ffa5bcc3e370fe22c958</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288767</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lives Left">
      <data key="d0">Lives Left</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Lives left is a metric in the reinforcement learning scenario, with a consistent value of 3 across the displayed steps, possibly signifying episodes or attempts.&lt;SEP&gt;Lives Left is a metric that remains constant at 3 across all steps depicted in Figure 5.4.&lt;SEP&gt;Lives Left, abbreviated as 'L:', suggests a remaining capacity or iterations in the optimization process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1474f4155ebb5657d8ca7f3c0d29b49&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6248c24886e0bd2c4ecb6bd221bb01dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f031035d088ffa5bcc3e370fe22c958</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288768</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Visual Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Visual Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A detailed method of analyzing the visual elements of an image, describing components, colors, labels, and their spatial relationships.&lt;SEP&gt;Visual analysis is the method used to dissect and understand the content, structure, and implications of the image provided.&lt;SEP&gt;Visual Analysis is a detailed examination of the visual characteristics and components of an image.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2a5c42907185fecc94ee85f1b33ec380&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6248c24886e0bd2c4ecb6bd221bb01dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abb64932da4baf92322bc61ecf0df44f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288768</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Purple Color">
      <data key="d0">Purple Color</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The purple color in the diagrams represents the background or empty space within the design domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6248c24886e0bd2c4ecb6bd221bb01dd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288768</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lighter Blue Rectangular Region">
      <data key="d0">Lighter Blue Rectangular Region</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The lighter blue rectangular region on the left side of each square diagram likely represents a coil in the magnetic core design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6248c24886e0bd2c4ecb6bd221bb01dd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288769</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yellow Square Pointer">
      <data key="d0">Yellow Square Pointer</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A small yellow square, or 'pointer,' indicates the agent's current position and interaction point within the design domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6248c24886e0bd2c4ecb6bd221bb01dd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288769</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Normalized Prediction Error">
      <data key="d0">Normalized Prediction Error</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Normalized Prediction Error is a metric used to calculate the performance of a model, specifically over the validation dataset, and is presented as a percentage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8854ea1ca7b618b176856798664355f4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288769</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Normalized Percentage Error">
      <data key="d0">Normalized Percentage Error</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Normalized Percentage Error is a metric displayed on the y-axis of the training curve charts, ranging from 0 to 4.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8854ea1ca7b618b176856798664355f4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288771</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transformer Problem">
      <data key="d0">Transformer Problem</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The transformer problem is a complex model that considers geometry, material, and field distribution, which is influenced by varying structural dimensions.&lt;SEP&gt;The Transformer Problem is one of the electromagnetic problems used to test the training curves and performance of neural network models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e98180b8e135e08cf842f2a94ed38a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8854ea1ca7b618b176856798664355f4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288771</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IPM Motor Problem">
      <data key="d0">IPM Motor Problem</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The IPM Motor Problem is one of the electromagnetic problems used to test the training curves and performance of neural network models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8854ea1ca7b618b176856798664355f4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288772</data>
    </node>
    <node id="K=32 No Dilation">
      <data key="d0">K=32 No Dilation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">K=32 No Dilation represents a configuration of a neural network model without dilation and K value of 32. It consistently shows the worst performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8854ea1ca7b618b176856798664355f4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288772</data>
    </node>
    <node id="K=64 No Dilation">
      <data key="d0">K=64 No Dilation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">K=64 No Dilation represents a configuration of a neural network model without dilation and K value of 64.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8854ea1ca7b618b176856798664355f4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288772</data>
    </node>
    <node id="K=32 with Dilation">
      <data key="d0">K=32 with Dilation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">K=32 with Dilation represents a configuration of a neural network model with dilation and K value of 32, showing improved error reduction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8854ea1ca7b618b176856798664355f4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288773</data>
    </node>
    <node id="K=64 with Dilation">
      <data key="d0">K=64 with Dilation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">K=64 with Dilation represents a configuration of a neural network model with dilation and K value of 64. It consistently achieves the lowest error rates across all problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8854ea1ca7b618b176856798664355f4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288773</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neural Network Models">
      <data key="d0">Neural Network Models</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Neural Network Models are the subject of the performance analysis, with different configurations tested for electromagnetic problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8854ea1ca7b618b176856798664355f4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288774</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Validation Dataset">
      <data key="d0">Validation Dataset</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Validation Dataset is a subset of the data, comprising 10,000 samples, used to tune hyperparameters and monitor model performance.&lt;SEP&gt;The validation dataset is a subset of data used to evaluate the performance of a model during training and calculate the Normalized Prediction Error.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a177157c3402545cf649cdb67022050a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8854ea1ca7b618b176856798664355f4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288774</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Training Curve">
      <data key="d0">Training Curve</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A training curve visually represents the change in Normalized Prediction Error over epochs for a neural network model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8854ea1ca7b618b176856798664355f4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288774</data>
    </node>
    <node id="K=32 No Dilation Configuration">
      <data key="d0">K=32 No Dilation Configuration</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">This configuration of a neural network model uses K=32 and no dilation, and consistently shows the highest error across all problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8854ea1ca7b618b176856798664355f4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288774</data>
    </node>
    <node id="K=64 No Dilation Configuration">
      <data key="d0">K=64 No Dilation Configuration</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">This configuration of a neural network model uses K=64 and no dilation, showing better performance than K=32 No Dilation, but worse than dilated configurations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8854ea1ca7b618b176856798664355f4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288775</data>
    </node>
    <node id="K=32 With Dilation Configuration">
      <data key="d0">K=32 With Dilation Configuration</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">This configuration of a neural network model uses K=32 with dilation, providing a significant improvement in error reduction compared to non-dilated configurations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8854ea1ca7b618b176856798664355f4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288775</data>
    </node>
    <node id="K=64 With Dilation Configuration">
      <data key="d0">K=64 With Dilation Configuration</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">This configuration of a neural network model uses K=64 with dilation, consistently achieving the lowest normalized percentage error across all problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8854ea1ca7b618b176856798664355f4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288775</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-Core Excitation Scenarios">
      <data key="d0">C-Core Excitation Scenarios</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">C-core excitation scenarios refer to a potential application domain or use case for the Advantage Actor-Critic algorithm in engineering, suggesting a control or optimization problem.&lt;SEP&gt;C-core excitation scenarios describe the conditions of a C-core, specified by coil current (Amperes) and coil turns, used as test cases for optimization algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a38c88cccaef550c4d3cde6f591d2fe5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288777</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conventional Method">
      <data key="d0">Conventional Method</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Conventional Method represents a baseline or traditional approach for determining force magnitude, with values ranging from 2.52 N to 105 N.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a38c88cccaef550c4d3cde6f591d2fe5</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288777</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GA On/Of (Park et al.)">
      <data key="d0">GA On/Of (Park et al.)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">GA On/Of (Park et al.) is a Genetic Algorithm-based optimization method developed by Park and Min (2009b), characterized by high FE calls.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a38c88cccaef550c4d3cde6f591d2fe5</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288778</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Filter (Midha et al.)">
      <data key="d0">Filter (Midha et al.)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Filter (Midha et al.) is a method that combines Genetic Algorithms with filters, developed by Midha et al. (2019), for which force magnitudes are displayed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a38c88cccaef550c4d3cde6f591d2fe5</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288779</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QL SeqTO (1x1)">
      <data key="d0">QL SeqTO (1x1)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">QL SeqTO (1x1) is a Tabular Q-Learning algorithm using Sequence-based TO (SeqTO-v1) with a 1x1 environment, generally having lower FE calls than GA.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a38c88cccaef550c4d3cde6f591d2fe5</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288779</data>
    </node>
    <node id="A2C 1SeqTO-v2">
      <data key="d0">A2C 1SeqTO-v2</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A2C 1SeqTO-v2 is an Actor-Critic reinforcement learning agent using Sequence-based TO (SeqTO-v2) for initial training, with moderate FE calls.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a38c88cccaef550c4d3cde6f591d2fe5</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288780</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LSTL">
      <data key="d0">LSTL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">LSTL (Learned Solution Transfer Learning) is a prefix identifying a group of scenarios (0.5A, 250T to 2.0A, 500T).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a38c88cccaef550c4d3cde6f591d2fe5</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288781</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GA On/Of (Park Et Al.)">
      <data key="d0">GA On/Of (Park Et Al.)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">GA On/Of (Park Et Al.) is an optimization method based on the Genetic Algorithm, developed by Park and Min in 2009b.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a38c88cccaef550c4d3cde6f591d2fe5</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288781</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Park And Min (2009b)">
      <data key="d0">Park And Min (2009b)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Park and Min (2009b) refers to a research publication describing the Genetic Algorithm-based method used for comparison.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a38c88cccaef550c4d3cde6f591d2fe5</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288781</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GA+Filters (Midha Et Al. 2019)">
      <data key="d0">GA+Filters (Midha Et Al. 2019)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">GA+Filters (Midha Et Al. 2019) is a hybrid optimization method combining Genetic Algorithms with filters, developed by Midha et al. in 2019.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a38c88cccaef550c4d3cde6f591d2fe5</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288782</data>
    </node>
