    <node id="Input">
      <data key="d0">Input</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Input node within the AGENT receives information, likely representing the state 's', from the ENVIRONMENT.&lt;SEP&gt;The Input is the starting point of the neural network diagram, specified as having "4 Channels" and a "Dimension: 21 x 35".</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5dae035c707962e5992f8ffb5b5c0c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9b48425c0c345b3be14ed824c6579b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288788</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4 Channels">
      <data key="d0">4 Channels</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">4 Channels describes a characteristic of the Input block, suggesting a multi-channel input, likely representing spatial data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5dae035c707962e5992f8ffb5b5c0c7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288788</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dimension: 21 x 35">
      <data key="d0">Dimension: 21 x 35</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Dimension: 21 x 35 specifies the spatial dimensions of the input and the Convolution layer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5dae035c707962e5992f8ffb5b5c0c7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288789</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Convolution Layer">
      <data key="d0">Convolution Layer</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Convolution Layer is a processing step in the network architecture, represented by 16 overlapping grey rectangles, also having "16 Channels" and "Dimension: 21 x 35".</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5dae035c707962e5992f8ffb5b5c0c7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288789</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16 Channels">
      <data key="d0">16 Channels</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">16 Channels describes a characteristic of the Convolution Layer, indicating the number of output channels after convolution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5dae035c707962e5992f8ffb5b5c0c7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288789</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flatten">
      <data key="d0">Flatten</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Flatten is an operation that reshapes the data from the Convolution Layer into a one-dimensional vector, changing its dimension to "1 x 256".</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5dae035c707962e5992f8ffb5b5c0c7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288789</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dimension: 1 x 256">
      <data key="d0">Dimension: 1 x 256</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Dimension: 1 x 256 specifies the reshaped dimensions of the data after the Flatten operation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5dae035c707962e5992f8ffb5b5c0c7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288789</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dense Layer">
      <data key="d0">Dense Layer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Dense Layer, also known as a fully connected layer, is a component of the neural network architecture with a dimension of 1 x 128.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5dae035c707962e5992f8ffb5b5c0c7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288790</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dimension: 1 x 128">
      <data key="d0">Dimension: 1 x 128</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Dimension: 1 x 128 specifies the output dimensions of the first Dense Layer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5dae035c707962e5992f8ffb5b5c0c7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288790</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Output (1 x 1)">
      <data key="d0">Output (1 x 1)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Output (1 x 1) refers to the final output of the Critic Network, representing the Value Function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5dae035c707962e5992f8ffb5b5c0c7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288791</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Output (1 x 8)">
      <data key="d0">Output (1 x 8)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Output (1 x 8) refers to the final output of the Actor Network, representing a probability distribution over eight actions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5dae035c707962e5992f8ffb5b5c0c7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288791</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flatten Operation">
      <data key="d0">Flatten Operation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Flatten Operation is a process within the neural network that transforms the data's dimension from 21 x 35 x 16 (implied) to 1 x 256.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5dae035c707962e5992f8ffb5b5c0c7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288792</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Input Block">
      <data key="d0">Input Block</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Input Block is the starting point of the neural network diagram, labeled "4 Channels" and "Dimension: 21 x 35", representing spatial data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5dae035c707962e5992f8ffb5b5c0c7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288793</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Table 3.1">
      <data key="d0">Table 3.1</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Table 3.1 is titled "Dependent &amp; Independent Parameters of the reference design" and lists design parameters and their corresponding values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288793</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reference Design">
      <data key="d0">Reference Design</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The reference design pertains to a 24-slot 4-pole parameterized IPM motor and has specific dependent and independent parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288794</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parameter">
      <data key="d0">Parameter</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Parameter is a column header in Table 2.3, listing specific components or dimensions of the IPM motor.&lt;SEP&gt;A parameter is a variable that defines aspects of a design, with values provided in millimeters or degrees.&lt;SEP&gt;Parameter refers to the geometric variables of the motor design, such as X1, X2, X3, X4, X5, X6, X7, X8, X9, and X10.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-997630dd5ed7d24f34fabba54a0e27c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288794</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Value">
      <data key="d0">Value</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Values are measurements associated with the parameters, given in millimeters (mm) or degrees (°).&lt;SEP&gt;Value represents the specific measurement for each parameter, given in millimeters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288794</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X1">
      <data key="d0">X1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">X1 is a parametrized dimension that measures the radial depth of a stator winding.&lt;SEP&gt;X1 is a geometric parameter defining the width of a stator slot opening in the IPM motor design.&lt;SEP&gt;X1 is a design parameter with a value of 18 mm.&lt;SEP&gt;X1 is a tangential dimension of a stator tooth.&lt;SEP&gt;X1 is a geometric parameter of the motor design with a nominal value of 13 mm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff320ec3e3a24e3c69d31630b6cba885&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f476972defaf212918b90b259aa69f03</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288794</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X2">
      <data key="d0">X2</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">X2 is a parametrized dimension that measures the circumferential width between two adjacent stator windings at their inner edge.&lt;SEP&gt;X2 is a geometric parameter related to the depth or angular position of stator slots in the IPM motor design.&lt;SEP&gt;X2 is a design parameter with a value of 5.8 mm.&lt;SEP&gt;X2 is a radial dimension of a stator tooth.&lt;SEP&gt;X2 is a geometric parameter of the motor design with a nominal value of 25 mm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff320ec3e3a24e3c69d31630b6cba885&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f476972defaf212918b90b259aa69f03</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288795</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X3">
      <data key="d0">X3</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">X3 is a parametrized dimension that indicates the radial width of the permanent magnet assembly.&lt;SEP&gt;X3 is a geometric parameter related to the depth or angular position of stator slots in the IPM motor design.&lt;SEP&gt;X3 is a design parameter with a value of 3mm.&lt;SEP&gt;X3 is a small tangential dimension situated between two magnet segments.&lt;SEP&gt;X3 is a geometric parameter of the motor design with a nominal value of 4.3 mm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff320ec3e3a24e3c69d31630b6cba885&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f476972defaf212918b90b259aa69f03</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288795</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X4">
      <data key="d0">X4</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">X4 is a parametrized dimension that denotes the perpendicular distance from one side of the permanent magnet to the inner boundary of the white triangular section.&lt;SEP&gt;X4 is a geometric parameter related to the depth or angular position of stator slots in the IPM motor design.&lt;SEP&gt;X4 is a design parameter with a value of 1mm.&lt;SEP&gt;X4 is a small, tangential dimension located between the outer edge of the magnets and the stator teeth.&lt;SEP&gt;X4 is a geometric parameter of the motor design with a nominal value of 7 mm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff320ec3e3a24e3c69d31630b6cba885&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f476972defaf212918b90b259aa69f03</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288796</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X5">
      <data key="d0">X5</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">X5 is a parametrized dimension measuring the total length of the permanent magnet assembly along its diagonal axis.&lt;SEP&gt;X5 is a design parameter with a value of 1mm.&lt;SEP&gt;X5 is a small, tangential dimension located between the outer edge of the magnets and the stator teeth.&lt;SEP&gt;X5 is a geometric parameter of the motor design with a nominal value of 1.0 mm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff320ec3e3a24e3c69d31630b6cba885&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f476972defaf212918b90b259aa69f03</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288796</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X6">
      <data key="d0">X6</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">X6 is a design parameter with a value of 100mm.&lt;SEP&gt;X6 is a parameter labeled 'stack height', referring to a dimension perpendicular to the plane of the motor cross-section.&lt;SEP&gt;X6 is a geometric parameter of the motor design with a nominal value of 115 mm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f476972defaf212918b90b259aa69f03</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288796</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X7">
      <data key="d0">X7</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">X7 is a horizontal dimension indicating a distance from the motor's center to a vertical line defining an internal feature.&lt;SEP&gt;X7 is a design parameter with a value of 50mm.&lt;SEP&gt;X7 is a parameter representing a radial dimension, extending outwards from the origin to the inner edge of a blue segment on the motor geometry.&lt;SEP&gt;X7 is a parameter mentioned in the table that has an empty value cell.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f476972defaf212918b90b259aa69f03</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288796</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X8">
      <data key="d0">X8</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">X8 is a dimension describing the position or width/thickness of a light gray element, likely a permanent magnet or magnetic barrier, within the rotor.&lt;SEP&gt;X8 is a design parameter with a value of 18 mm.&lt;SEP&gt;X8 is a radial dimension of one of the red magnet segments.&lt;SEP&gt;X8 is a geometric parameter of the motor design with a nominal value of 12.5 mm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f476972defaf212918b90b259aa69f03</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288797</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X9">
      <data key="d0">X9</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">X9 is a dimension describing the position or width/thickness of a light gray element, likely a permanent magnet or magnetic barrier, within the rotor.&lt;SEP&gt;X9 is a design parameter with a value of 4mm.&lt;SEP&gt;X9 is a horizontal dimension, indicated by a double-headed arrow spanning a red magnet segment.&lt;SEP&gt;X9 is a geometric parameter of the motor design with a nominal value of 60 mm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f476972defaf212918b90b259aa69f03</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288797</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X10">
      <data key="d0">X10</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">X10 is a dimension associated with the width or length of a darker gray, rounded element, possibly the motor's rotor or central shaft.&lt;SEP&gt;X10 is a design parameter with a value of 15 mm.&lt;SEP&gt;X10 is a small radial dimension.&lt;SEP&gt;X10 is a geometric parameter of the motor design with a nominal value of 0.25 mm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f476972defaf212918b90b259aa69f03</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288797</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X11">
      <data key="d0">X11</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">X11 is a dimension that appears to indicate a thickness or gap near the lighter gray element within the motor.&lt;SEP&gt;X11 is an angular design parameter with a value of 45°.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288797</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X12">
      <data key="d0">X12</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">X12 is a design parameter with a value of 1.5 mm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Latin Hypercube Sampling Strategy">
      <data key="d0">Latin Hypercube Sampling Strategy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Latin Hypercube sampling strategy is a method used to generate 2900 samples of a parameterized IPM motor by varying reference design parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288799</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CNN Model">
      <data key="d0">CNN Model</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) model discussed in Section 3.4.1 that takes CAD geometry as input.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288799</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Table 3.1: Dependent &amp; Independent Parameters of the Reference Design">
      <data key="d0">Table 3.1: Dependent &amp; Independent Parameters of the Reference Design</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Table 3.1 presents the dependent and independent parameters and their corresponding values for a reference design in a two-column-pair format.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288800</data>
    </node>
    <node id="24-slot 4-pole Parameterized IPM Motor">
      <data key="d0">24-slot 4-pole Parameterized IPM Motor</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">This is the type of motor being designed and analyzed, whose variations are generated based on the reference design parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288801</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Variations">
      <data key="d0">Design Variations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">These are the different versions of the motor created by perturbing the reference design parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc05caebd47f0bf32bdc510804373755</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288801</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Equation: Q(s, a) = r(s, a) + γV(s')">
      <data key="d0">Equation: Q(s, a) = r(s, a) + γV(s')</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">This is the mathematical formula defining the Q-value function, establishing a fundamental relationship between the Q-value, immediate reward, and the value of the successor state in reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288802</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Successor State Value">
      <data key="d0">Successor State Value</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The successor state value, $\gamma V(s')$, represents the discounted expected future reward from the subsequent state $s'$, where $V(s')$ is the state-value function for the next state and $\gamma$ is the discount factor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288802</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action (a)">
      <data key="d0">Action (a)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Action (a) represents a fundamental concept in various computational models, particularly in reinforcement learning and design optimization. It signifies a choice made by an agent within a given state to interact with its environment. This interaction can involve influencing the material distribution in a design domain, such as making design choices or placing materials in the context of device optimization.

Within the framework of reinforcement learning, Action (a) is a specific action that an agent can take from a given state, often denoted as $s$. It is chosen from a set of possible actions. In the Q-Learning equation, Action (a) explicitly represents this choice made by the agent. Furthermore, in the context of the A2C (Advantage Actor-Critic) model, Action (a) serves as an input to the Critic component, typically represented as a single data point.&lt;SEP&gt;The variable $a$ represents an action taken from a given state.&lt;SEP&gt;Action (a) is a variable within the equation, representing a specific move or operation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7724d4c011c9981c316498400dd34c58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27909faa34224de8c011e982c98fcef6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f95cef6c8e603e27b162387295b8935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9121d5ef96392cd935214b547cd6dd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58674fc78b8e0f9b48c3a573d49dd684&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-367b8cf81a01ff1207d6410c92e1e959</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293131</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discount Factor (γ)">
      <data key="d0">Discount Factor (γ)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The discount factor, denoted as $\gamma$, is a scalar value between 0 and 1 that prioritizes immediate rewards over future rewards in reinforcement learning.&lt;SEP&gt;The Discount Factor (γ) is a value between 0 and 1 that determines the present value of future rewards in the Q-Learning equation.&lt;SEP&gt;The variable $\gamma$ (gamma) is the discount factor, a value between 0 and 1 that determines the present value of future rewards. It discounts future rewards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7724d4c011c9981c316498400dd34c58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9121d5ef96392cd935214b547cd6dd2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293106</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State-Value Function V(s')">
      <data key="d0">State-Value Function V(s')</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The state-value function for the next state $s'$, $V(s')$, represents the expected return when starting in state $s'$ and following a particular policy. It is implicitly defined in Section 4.5.2.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288803</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Addition (+)">
      <data key="d0">Addition (+)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Addition is a mathematical operation used to update parameters by adding the scaled gradient term.&lt;SEP&gt;Addition is a mathematical operation used in the equation to combine the immediate reward and the discounted future reward.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288803</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multiplication">
      <data key="d0">Multiplication</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An operation implying the product of the likelihood P(y|x, W) and the approximate posterior Q(W), a standard step in calculating joint probability or weighting a function by probability density.&lt;SEP&gt;Multiplication (×) is a mathematical operation used to combine the Q-value of an action with its probability.&lt;SEP&gt;Multiplication is a mathematical operation used for scalar multiplication of the learning rate and return with the gradient term.&lt;SEP&gt;Multiplication is implicitly used in the equation for the discounting operation, specifically $(\gamma \times V(s'))$.&lt;SEP&gt;Multiplication is a mathematical operation used for scalar scaling of terms, such as the gradient term by the learning rate and Q-value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb28e5c877704a09cf7eb4cbeb6522fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288804</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advantage Function A(s, a)">
      <data key="d0">Advantage Function A(s, a)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Advantage function, defined as $A(s, a) = Q(s, a) - V(s)$, simplifies the calculation of advantage by only requiring the state-value function V(s) to be learned.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bellman Expectation Equation">
      <data key="d0">Bellman Expectation Equation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An equation that describes the relationship between the value of a state and the values of its successor states under a given policy.&lt;SEP&gt;The equation $Q ( s , a ) = r ( s , a ) + \gamma V ( s ^ { \prime } )$ is a form of the Bellman expectation equation for the Q-value function under an arbitrary policy, adapted to connect Q-value to immediate reward and state value of the subsequent state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State (s)">
      <data key="d0">State (s)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The entity "State (s)" refers to a comprehensive representation of an environment's current conditions, particularly within the context of reinforcement learning and Markov Decision Processes (MDPs). It encapsulates sufficient information about the current material distribution within a design domain, or other relevant environmental factors. This includes aspects such as magnetic field distribution in an air gap or even the entire geometry, and its dimensionality can be high and continuous.

Crucially, the state (s) represents a specific observation or configuration of the environment in which an agent interacts, influencing the actions available to the agent and the rewards it might receive. It serves as an input to a neural network, forming part of the state-value function V(s), and is also specifically utilized as an input to both the Critic and Actor components of models like A2C, typically represented as a vector or sequence of information. In essence, the state (s) is a crucial encoding of the environment's current condition, often reflecting the current optimal design or other environmental factors pertinent to the agent's operation.&lt;SEP&gt;The State (s) represents the current situation or configuration in an environment within the Q-Learning equation.&lt;SEP&gt;State (s) refers to the current condition or configuration of an environment.&lt;SEP&gt;The variable $s$ represents the current state in a sequential decision-making problem.&lt;SEP&gt;State (s) is a variable within the equation, representing a particular condition or configuration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7724d4c011c9981c316498400dd34c58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27909faa34224de8c011e982c98fcef6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f95cef6c8e603e27b162387295b8935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb622c9ce0600f3539c3ed7199e0c0ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9121d5ef96392cd935214b547cd6dd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58674fc78b8e0f9b48c3a573d49dd684&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-367b8cf81a01ff1207d6410c92e1e959</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293131</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SARSA">
      <data key="d0">SARSA</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A model-free on-policy reinforcement learning algorithm for learning a Markov decision process policy.&lt;SEP&gt;SARSA is an on-policy Reinforcement Learning algorithm that learns an action-value function by taking into account the next state, next action, and the reward.&lt;SEP&gt;A Temporal Difference learning algorithm that utilizes the recursive definition of return for incremental updates, often for policy evaluation and improvement.&lt;SEP&gt;SARSA (State-Action-Reward-State-Action) is a variant of Temporal Difference learning that is an on-policy learning algorithm, similar to the one described by the equation.&lt;SEP&gt;SARSA (State-Action-Reward-State-Action) is an on-policy reinforcement learning algorithm that learns the Q-value function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288806</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Value Network">
      <data key="d0">Value Network</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A value network is a component in reinforcement learning algorithms, often part of a critic, used to estimate the value of states or state-action pairs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288806</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Value Of The Successor State">
      <data key="d0">Value Of The Successor State</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The value of the successor state, represented as $V(s')$, is the discounted expected future reward from the subsequent state $s'$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288806</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discount Factor Gamma">
      <data key="d0">Discount Factor Gamma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A factor (γ) that typically reduces the weight of future rewards in the calculation of the return, though not explicitly present in the simplified equation Gt.&lt;SEP&gt;The discount factor, denoted as $\gamma$, is a scalar value between 0 and 1 (exclusive) used to prioritize immediate rewards over future rewards in reinforcement learning calculations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288807</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Actor-Critic Methods">
      <data key="d0">Actor-Critic Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Actor-Critic methods are extensions of Policy Gradient that combine an actor (policy network) and a critic (value network) to guide learning.&lt;SEP&gt;Actor-Critic Methods are a class of reinforcement learning algorithms that combine elements of policy gradients (the "actor") and value-based methods (the "critic").&lt;SEP&gt;Actor-Critic Methods (e.g., A2C, A3C, PPO) are advanced policy gradient algorithms where the return `Gt` might be replaced by a more sophisticated advantage function.&lt;SEP&gt;Actor-Critic Methods are a class of reinforcement learning algorithms that combine policy-based (actor) and value-based (critic) approaches. A2C is an extension of these methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb28e5c877704a09cf7eb4cbeb6522fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827b20ce2e241ba2e3eb418463078226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288808</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 3.16">
      <data key="d0">Figure 3.16</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 3.16 is an image displaying the parametrized motor geometry of a Fractional-Slot Concentrated Winding Permanent Magnet (FSCW PM) motor.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 3.16 is a visual representation mentioned in the surrounding text, related to the reference designs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f476972defaf212918b90b259aa69f03</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288808</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parametrized Motor Geometry">
      <data key="d0">Parametrized Motor Geometry</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Parametrized Motor Geometry refers to the detailed, two-dimensional cross-section of a motor where various dimensions are represented by parameters such as X1 through X10.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f476972defaf212918b90b259aa69f03</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288809</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Table 3.3">
      <data key="d0">Table 3.3</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Table 3.3 lists the dependent and independent parameters of the FSCW PM Motor.</data>
