    </node>
    <node id="15.2°">
      <data key="d0">15.2°</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">15.2° is an angled dimension indicating an angular measurement of a slot opening.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f476972defaf212918b90b259aa69f03</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Actor-Critic Framework">
      <data key="d0">Actor-Critic Framework</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Actor-Critic Framework is a reinforcement learning paradigm that combines policy gradient methods with value function approximation, using an 'Actor' to learn the policy and a 'Critic' to estimate the value function.&lt;SEP&gt;A framework in Reinforcement Learning that combines policy-based and value-based methods, utilizing an actor (policy) and a critic (value function estimator).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Gradient Theorem">
      <data key="d0">Policy Gradient Theorem</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Policy Gradient Theorem is a foundational concept in Reinforcement Learning that provides a mathematical basis for policy gradient algorithms.&lt;SEP&gt;The Policy Gradient Theorem is a theoretical foundation in reinforcement learning that provides a method for optimizing parameterized policies by following the gradient of the expected return.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Parameters ($\theta$)">
      <data key="d0">Policy Parameters ($\theta$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Policy Parameters ($\theta$) are a vector of parameters defining the policy $\pi$ at a given time step, which are updated iteratively to improve the policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Learning Rate ($\alpha$)">
      <data key="d0">Learning Rate ($\alpha$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A hyperparameter, labeled 'lr', between 0 and 1 that controls the step size of the Q-value update. It determines the extent to which new information overrides old information.&lt;SEP&gt;A positive scalar hyperparameter that controls the step size of the weight updates, influencing the aggressiveness of updates.&lt;SEP&gt;The Learning Rate ($\alpha$) is a positive scalar hyperparameter that controls the step size of parameter updates in optimization.&lt;SEP&gt;The Learning Rate ($\alpha$) is a positive scalar that controls the magnitude of parameter updates, determining how quickly policy parameters adjust based on observed experience.&lt;SEP&gt;The learning rate ($\alpha$) is a hyperparameter that determines the step size at each iteration while moving toward a minimum of a loss function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39c26f40d41eeedc15f07ee76f39c83c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action-Value Function ($Q(s_t, a_t)$)">
      <data key="d0">Action-Value Function ($Q(s_t, a_t)$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Action-Value Function ($Q(s_t, a_t)$) represents the expected return achieved by taking action $a_t$ in state $s_t$ and then following the policy $\pi$ thereafter, serving as an estimate of the 'goodness' of an action.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288813</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State ($s_t$)">
      <data key="d0">State ($s_t$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The State ($s_t$) refers to the environment's condition at time step $t$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action ($a_t$)">
      <data key="d0">Action ($a_t$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Action ($a_t$) is the specific action taken by the agent in state $s_t$ at time step $t$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy ($\pi(a_t | s_t ; \theta_t)$)">
      <data key="d0">Policy ($\pi(a_t | s_t ; \theta_t)$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Policy ($\pi(a_t | s_t ; \theta_t)$) is a probability distribution over actions given a state, parameterized by $\theta_t$, representing the likelihood of taking action $a_t$ in state $s_t$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gradient Operator ($\nabla_\theta$)">
      <data key="d0">Gradient Operator ($\nabla_\theta$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Gradient Operator ($\nabla_\theta$) is a mathematical operator used to compute the direction of steepest ascent for functions with respect to the parameters $\theta$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mathematical Equation Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Mathematical Equation Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mathematical Equation Analysis refers to the comprehensive process of dissecting and interpreting mathematical expressions to understand their meaning, variables, operations, and various applications within a specific context. This analysis aims to provide an in-depth explanation of a given equation, detailing its components, implications, underlying mathematical meaning, and its overall significance. It involves examining the equation to define its relationship to other concepts and its application domain.

This analytical process is frequently applied within the field of Reinforcement Learning, where it serves to clarify complex mathematical equations pertinent to the discipline. Specific examples include the analysis of weight update rules for neural networks, often in the context of off-policy Q-learning, deep Q-networks (DQN) with separate behavior and target networks, and gradient-based optimization. It also elucidates concepts such as epsilon-greedy exploration and policies for action selection, and the relationship between return at time *t* and future rewards or returns.

Mathematical Equation Analysis can also be used to explain specific inequality constraints within optimization problems, such as those related to Synchronous Reluctance Motor rotor topology. This analysis may focus on particular equations like $\hat{v}(s, w) \approx v_{\pi}(s)$ or $a^* = \arg \max_a Q(s, a)$, providing their interpretation, variable definitions, contextual significance, and often presenting them in formats such as LaTeX.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70c2a865bc92e157d3426a926d7ddd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-808c974f7871ba6b7b5d18f72afc85a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58674fc78b8e0f9b48c3a573d49dd684&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d40236fe707c1436e66df73317598df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Natural Logarithm ($\ln \pi(a_t | s_t ; \theta_t)$)">
      <data key="d0">Natural Logarithm ($\ln \pi(a_t | s_t ; \theta_t)$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Natural Logarithm ($\ln \pi(a_t | s_t ; \theta_t)$) of the policy's probability, applied to simplify gradient calculations and often referred to as the 'score function'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Addition">
      <data key="d0">Addition</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A mathematical operation used to combine the immediate reward with the discounted expected future reward.&lt;SEP&gt;Addition is a mathematical operation used to update parameters by adding the scaled gradient term.&lt;SEP&gt;Addition is a mathematical operation used to update parameters, specifically $\theta_t + \dots$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Equation">
      <data key="d0">Equation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An Equation is a fundamental mathematical statement that asserts the equality of two expressions. Frequently representing a mathematical formula, equations are crucial across various disciplines, including machine learning, control theory, optimization, and reinforcement learning. They explicitly label content as a mathematical expression, often presented in LaTeX format.

Equations serve diverse purposes, from defining complex concepts like the Kullback-Leibler divergence or binary material distributions in topology optimization, to representing inequality constraints within optimization problems. In reinforcement learning, equations define policies such as a deterministic policy for optimal action or the common "epsilon-greedy policy" for action selection, and represent mathematical expressions under analysis, function as learning signals, or form components of update rules tied to the gradient of a squared error loss, such as the Mean Squared Error (MSE) used as a loss function in Q-learning. More advanced applications include defining the gradient of performance measure with respect to policy parameters, update rules for policy parameters in the Actor-Critic framework, and the definition of the Advantage function. Equations define Reinforcement Learning (RL) stochastic policies, particularly in Actor-Critic (A2C), and can represent variables like `a*`, which maximizes a function `Q(s, a)`. Furthermore, equations are used to calculate metrics such as Classification Accuracy and define mathematical constraints between specified variables.

An equation can also define a relationship between an action's value at a given state and the discounted value of the next state, ultimately contributing to a mathematical optimization problem. Specific examples include the equation for material distribution in topology optimization, an equation representing a sequence of state-action pairs and their corresponding Q-values (Q-pi), and an equation `$$ J ( \\\\theta ) = G ( t ) \\\\approx \\\\sum _ { a } \\\\Big ( Q ( s _ { 0 } , a ) \\\\times p ( a | s _ { 0 } ) \\\\Big ) $$`. Equations also represent mathematical relationships and expressions concerning conditional probability, often in the context of a policy or action selection mechanism in an algorithmic context. Notable examples include an update rule for a weight vector 'w' based on a temporal difference error, future and current Q-values, and a gradient of the behavioral Q-function; a mathematical relationship where G_t is an equivalent of R_(t+1) plus G_(t+1); and an update rule for a parameter theta, used in reinforcement learning. Equations are also used to define the calculation of `p(y|x,X,Y)` by integrating the product of `p(y|x,W)` and `p(W|X,Y)` over `W`, and present statistical hypothesis tests comparing two population means using null and alternative hypotheses.&lt;SEP&gt;The equation represents a mathematical expression, likely used in machine learning or control systems, describing the update rule for a parameter theta over time.&lt;SEP&gt;The equation represents a mathematical expression, likely used in machine learning or optimization, involving a difference squared and a gradient.&lt;SEP&gt;The equation represents an approximation where the estimated value function for a state and weight vector is approximately equal to the true value function for that state under policy pi.&lt;SEP&gt;The equation represents a mathematical relationship, likely a sum or series, in the context of LaTeX.&lt;SEP&gt;The equation defines a strategy for choosing an action $A_t$ based on Q-values, incorporating both exploitation and exploration.&lt;SEP&gt;The equation represents a mathematical concept involving states, actions, and a function 'f'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-86d0a1a94798eb7b1674502b0ae73cf4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6871b523f5d81edec8c74e56e7c813&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a062a30b9c7d57e6fe1cf2ad874832e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8823695e17798553515e243bdbfda2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d0e0689a5d95559de772960193f788e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50e280b998ddabea3a4713c685bb83d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a5cf1e19ea0a8639d0d309beab7d404&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2ac9217313164d00979e7afd6541254&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-367b8cf81a01ff1207d6410c92e1e959&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-215b04d60447db6b473faf768914326f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-916831dfb3bd404b7a6bf4cf512b41b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b9ed49570cc7fd3b04f001bb2371e8b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3c16dd3211cf28f9ec77da01f786ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0604f5005a60d070243b26410ba10b20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b2d0224b72ce534931ab18bf45eae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70c2a865bc92e157d3426a926d7ddd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f95cef6c8e603e27b162387295b8935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6cf4c742d5c3ecf6b0e985a5914f0f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19afc62597694db2255ea53cb7e6f242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-620c2ed72be858c05e569ae1452705a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58674fc78b8e0f9b48c3a573d49dd684&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e5324f5a16fd6822c647598c8e749b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed1035840471dadb1efd9b1057b1d2f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dae820ad2360b2e0540cedd11e75bb15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9df755969eb90a4aeda5af51acf960d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2a1300fe0e15e6c2632ac8232faa468&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd1039c6984ae2b3caedcb109bdc272a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb650db56653b515d55eb1ef7514257&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9242b5eeb7a8558a1b50bf53c2ccacba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26c769a26f512a088ad02de1c81c6ddf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14506e8e02aeb799dcd49d4b24c5b529&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e90fd88a3c123b4352d139141e81b16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d40236fe707c1436e66df73317598df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39c26f40d41eeedc15f07ee76f39c83c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f7dc16108c864fd058d1379ed9f298&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be1e8d82c79e97d6340503568a2610b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-679341b128f5b4ef1cbba0b4763e7aa8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293131</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Logarithm (ln)">
      <data key="d0">Logarithm (ln)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Logarithm (ln) is a mathematical operation applied to policy probability to simplify gradient calculations, resulting in the score function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gradient ($\nabla_\theta$)">
      <data key="d0">Gradient ($\nabla_\theta$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Gradient ($\nabla_\theta$) is a mathematical operation used to compute the direction of steepest ascent for the log-policy concerning the parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Robotic Control">
      <data key="d0">Robotic Control</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Robotic control is an application area where reinforcement learning policies can deterministically guide robot actions.&lt;SEP&gt;A practical use case for DQNs, demonstrating successful application of the error function in control tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;Robotic Control is a practical application field for policy gradient algorithms, requiring learning complex control policies.&lt;SEP&gt;Robotic control is a practical application where agents use stochastic policies for decision-making.&lt;SEP&gt;Robotic control is an application area where agents learn optimal action sequences for managing robotic systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Autonomous Driving">
      <data key="d0">Autonomous Driving</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Autonomous driving is an application area where reinforcement learning is used for optimal sequential decision-making strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Autonomous Driving is an application domain where this type of weight update is fundamental for learning safe and efficient actions.&lt;SEP&gt;Autonomous Driving is an application area for deep reinforcement learning, involving decision-making in self-driving cars.&lt;SEP&gt;Autonomous Driving is a practical application field where agents learn to navigate and make decisions in real-world driving scenarios.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288817</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Gradient (PG)">
      <data key="d0">Policy Gradient (PG)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Policy Gradient (PG) is a basic reinforcement learning method that optimizes parameterized policies by directly adjusting them based on the gradient of returns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288817</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monte Carlo Methods">
      <data key="d0">Monte Carlo Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Monte Carlo methods are reinforcement learning algorithms that aim to learn optimal policies.&lt;SEP&gt;Monte Carlo (MC) methods are reinforcement learning approaches that sum all rewards until the end of an episode to calculate the true return Gt.&lt;SEP&gt;Monte Carlo (MC) Methods are an approach in reinforcement learning that directly use the observed Return $G_t$ from completed episodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Monte Carlo methods are a class of Reinforcement Learning algorithms that learn value functions by averaging returns from complete episodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Learning approaches in reinforcement learning that learn from complete episodes of experience by using the 'true' observed return $G_t$.&lt;SEP&gt;Monte Carlo methods are a class of algorithms that rely on repeated random sampling to obtain numerical results, often used in reinforcement learning to estimate returns based on entire episodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Monte Carlo methods are a class of computational algorithms that rely on repeated random sampling to obtain numerical results, often used in reinforcement learning for estimating returns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288817</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advantage Function ($A(s,a)$)">
      <data key="d0">Advantage Function ($A(s,a)$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Advantage Function ($A(s,a)$) is a refinement of the Q-value used in Actor-Critic algorithms to improve robustness and reduce variance by estimating the advantage of an action over the average action in a given state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288817</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Game Playing">
      <data key="d0">Game Playing</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Game Playing is a significant application area where various machine learning techniques, particularly reinforcement learning, are extensively utilized. This domain serves as a practical application for agents to learn optimal strategies, encompassing both deterministic and stochastic policies for decision-making. Deep reinforcement learning has enabled agents to achieve superhuman performance in games, with notable examples including AlphaGo. The field also demonstrates the practical application of update rules, such as weight updates, which are fundamental for learning optimal strategies, and policy gradient algorithms, especially in complex environments like Atari games. Reinforcement learning algorithms are specifically employed to train agents to master games effectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288818</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Parameters">
      <data key="d0">Policy Parameters</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Policy Parameters, denoted by `theta`, are a vector representing the adjustable components (weights and biases) of a neural network that defines a policy.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy parameters, denoted by $\theta$, define the policy $\pi$ and are updated iteratively to improve the policy in reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288818</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Resource Management">
      <data key="d0">Resource Management</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Resource Management is an application area that uses reinforcement learning (RL) for optimal sequential decision-making strategies. It is particularly well-suited for RL, especially when dealing with large state and action spaces. Within this domain, deep reinforcement learning is often applied to optimize resource allocation, which includes balancing the exploration of various configurations with the exploitation of efficient ones. Furthermore, Resource Management is a practical application where energy consumption is often optimized, and RL agents employ stochastic policies for decision-making. In essence, it focuses on optimizing the allocation and use of resources through advanced learning techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gradient Ascent">
      <data key="d0">Gradient Ascent</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Gradient Ascent is an optimization method that drives parameters in the direction of steepest ascent of an error function.&lt;SEP&gt;Gradient ascent is an optimization algorithm typically used in Policy Gradient to maximize the performance measure J($\theta$) by updating parameters $\theta$.&lt;SEP&gt;Gradient ascent is an optimization algorithm used to find local maxima of a differentiable function by iteratively moving in the direction of the greatest increase of the function.&lt;SEP&gt;Gradient ascent is an iterative optimization algorithm for finding the maximum of a function by taking steps proportional to the positive of the gradient of the function at the current point.&lt;SEP&gt;Gradient ascent is a method used to maximize a function by iteratively moving in the direction of the greatest increase, utilized by the equation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gradient ascent is an optimization algorithm used to maximize an objective function by iteratively moving in the direction of the steepest ascent (given by the gradient).&lt;SEP&gt;The equation defines an iterative update rule, often used in optimization algorithms like gradient ascent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1cc3c9a8202b9032fe06ba473d7c76d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2ac9217313164d00979e7afd6541254</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292942</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Environment State">
      <data key="d0">Environment State</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The state of the environment at time step $t$, denoted by $s_t$, represents the current conditions or observations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Agent Action">
      <data key="d0">Agent Action</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The action taken by the agent in a given state at time step $t$, denoted by $a_t$, is the choice made by the learning agent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gradient Operator">
      <data key="d0">Gradient Operator</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The mathematical symbol $\nabla$, specifically $\nabla_w$, which computes the vector of partial derivatives, a multivariate calculus operation.&lt;SEP&gt;The gradient operator, $\nabla$, is a multivariate calculus operation that computes a vector of partial derivatives, indicating the direction of the greatest rate of increase of a scalar field.&lt;SEP&gt;The gradient operator ($\nabla$) computes partial derivatives of a scalar-valued function with respect to its vector-valued input.&lt;SEP&gt;The gradient operator $(\nabla)$ computes the vector of partial derivatives of a scalar-valued function with respect to each component of a parameter vector.&lt;SEP&gt;The Gradient Operator, `nabla`, is a vector operator that yields the vector of partial derivatives of a scalar function, signifying differentiation with respect to the parameter vector `theta`.&lt;SEP&gt;The gradient operator, $\nabla_\theta$, is a mathematical operator used to compute the direction and magnitude of the steepest ascent for a function with respect to its parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Log-Policy Probability">
      <data key="d0">Log-Policy Probability</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The log-policy probability, $\ln \pi(a_t | s_t ; \theta_t)$, is the natural logarithm of the policy's probability, used in policy gradient methods to simplify gradient calculations. This is often referred to as the 'score function'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Logarithm">
      <data key="d0">Logarithm</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The logarithm (ln) is a mathematical function applied to the policy probability to simplify gradient calculations in policy gradient methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-298487c8d447bf081bcb2234cb4ddb80</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288821</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.6">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.6</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.6 is an image presenting three grayscale panels illustrating the outcome of a density-based topology optimization method.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3639a9f481dea165d73df49859233cf4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288822</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Design From Density Method">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Design From Density Method</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Optimal Design from Density Method refers to the result of an initial optimization process, shown in Panel (b), which features a complex, pixelated design with a gradient of grayscale values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3639a9f481dea165d73df49859233cf4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288822</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Average Filtering">
      <data key="d0">Average Filtering</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Average Filtering is a technique applied to the optimal model to address intermediate densities, resulting in a visually smoother design as shown in Panel (c).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3639a9f481dea165d73df49859233cf4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288823</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Density-Based Topology Optimization">
      <data key="d0">Density-Based Topology Optimization</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Density-Based Topology Optimization is a method used to find an optimal material distribution within a design space by varying material densities.&lt;SEP&gt;Density-Based Topology Optimization is a method that infers material properties through continuous density fields and suffers from problems like intermediate density and grayscales.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3639a9f481dea165d73df49859233cf4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d40236fe707c1436e66df73317598df7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288823</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intermediate Densities">
      <data key="d0">Intermediate Densities</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Intermediate Densities, also known as "grayscales," are partial material densities that occur during density-based topology optimization methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3639a9f481dea165d73df49859233cf4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Filtered Optimal Model">
      <data key="d0">Filtered Optimal Model</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Filtered Optimal Model is a refined design achieved after applying a filtering process to address intermediate densities, resulting in a visually smoother and potentially more manufacturable version.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3639a9f481dea165d73df49859233cf4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Model">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Model</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Optimal Model is the result of initial optimization, characterized by a complex, pixelated design with a gradient of grayscale values indicating varying material densities, and illustrating the "grayscales material" problem.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3639a9f481dea165d73df49859233cf4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Density Method">
      <data key="d0">Density Method</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Density Method is a topology optimization method often resulting in intermediate density or grayscales material.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3639a9f481dea165d73df49859233cf4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intermediate Densities Problem">
      <data key="d0">Intermediate Densities Problem</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Intermediate Densities Problem, also known as "grayscales material," is a challenge in density-based topology optimization where optimization results show a range of grayscale values rather than clear black/white material distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3639a9f481dea165d73df49859233cf4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288825</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.16">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.16</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.16 is an image composed of two bar charts, labeled (a) and (b), which display policies related to reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c2c4cc1ff144256a7c660b31a82de7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288825</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Greedy QL Policy Always Chooses Action:2">
      <data key="d0">Greedy QL Policy Always Chooses Action:2</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Greedy QL policy is a Q-Learning policy that consistently selects action 2, as indicated by its highest Q-value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c2c4cc1ff144256a7c660b31a82de7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288826</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probabilistic Policy With A2C Samples All Actions">
      <data key="d0">Probabilistic Policy With A2C Samples All Actions</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Probabilistic policy with A2C is a policy that samples all actions, meaning that even actions with lower probabilities can still be chosen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c2c4cc1ff144256a7c660b31a82de7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288826</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chart (a)">
      <data key="d0">Chart (a)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chart (a) is a bar chart displaying 'Q(s, a) normalized' values for different actions in a greedy Q-Learning policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c2c4cc1ff144256a7c660b31a82de7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288826</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chart (b)">
      <data key="d0">Chart (b)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chart (b) is a bar chart presenting 'p(a)' (probability of action) for different actions within a probabilistic policy using A2C.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c2c4cc1ff144256a7c660b31a82de7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Actor-Critic (A2C)">
      <data key="d0">Actor-Critic (A2C)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Actor-Critic (A2C) is a policy gradient method used in reinforcement learning that allows for probabilistic sampling of actions, promoting exploration.&lt;SEP&gt;Actor-Critic (A2C) is a specific type of Reinforcement Learning algorithm that uses both an actor (policy) and a critic (value function) to learn optimal behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c2c4cc1ff144256a7c660b31a82de7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action 1">
      <data key="d0">Action 1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Action 1 is a discrete action label represented in both charts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c2c4cc1ff144256a7c660b31a82de7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action 2">
      <data key="d0">Action 2</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Action 2 is a discrete action label, which is the action always chosen by the greedy QL policy and has the highest Q-value/probability in the charts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c2c4cc1ff144256a7c660b31a82de7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action 3">
      <data key="d0">Action 3</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Action 3 is a discrete action label represented in both charts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c2c4cc1ff144256a7c660b31a82de7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action 4">
      <data key="d0">Action 4</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Action 4 is a discrete action label represented in both charts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c2c4cc1ff144256a7c660b31a82de7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288828</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probabilistic Policy With A2C">
      <data key="d0">Probabilistic Policy With A2C</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A probabilistic policy used with the A2C algorithm that samples all actions, as depicted in Chart (b).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c2c4cc1ff144256a7c660b31a82de7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action Probability">
      <data key="d0">Action Probability</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Action Probability is the likelihood of taking a specific action in a given state, as defined by a policy.&lt;SEP&gt;Action probability refers to the likelihood of an action being selected in a probabilistic policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c2c4cc1ff144256a7c660b31a82de7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Normalized Q-Values">
      <data key="d0">Normalized Q-Values</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Normalized Q-values are a measure of the expected utility of taking a given action in a given state, scaled for comparison.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c2c4cc1ff144256a7c660b31a82de7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reinforcement Learning Context">
      <data key="d0">Reinforcement Learning Context</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Reinforcement Learning Context is the framework within which the charts display relationships between actions and values or probabilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c2c4cc1ff144256a7c660b31a82de7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4-pole 24-slot IPM Motor">
      <data key="d0">4-pole 24-slot IPM Motor</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The 4-pole 24-slot IPM Motor is an Interior Permanent Magnet motor, which serves as a source for the "Pre-trained Neural Network" and the target of the "Modified Neural Network" in the context of creating a Power Factor Map.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-193c3a719cf23865bd32e0d110afb409</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288831</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Modified Neural Network (IPM)">
      <data key="d0">Modified Neural Network (IPM)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">This is a modified neural network connected to the "4-pole 24-slot IPM Motor" and receives internal knowledge transfer from a "Pre-trained Neural Network".</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-193c3a719cf23865bd32e0d110afb409</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288831</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pre-trained Neural Network">
      <data key="d0">Pre-trained Neural Network</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Pre-trained Neural Network is a central component in the diagram, receiving input from the "4-pole 24-slot IPM Motor" and serving as the source for knowledge transfer to modified neural networks for both IPM and FSC motors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-193c3a719cf23865bd32e0d110afb409</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288831</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Efficiency Map (Central IPM)">
      <data key="d0">Efficiency Map (Central IPM)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The Efficiency Map is a colored graph with Tm and Nm axes, generated from the "Pre-trained Neural Network", showing varying levels of efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-193c3a719cf23865bd32e0d110afb409</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288831</data>
    </node>
    <node id="10-pole 12-slot FSC Motor">
      <data key="d0">10-pole 12-slot FSC Motor</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The 10-pole 12-slot FSC Motor is a Flux Switching Permanent Magnet motor, which is the target of external knowledge transfer for its own "Modified Neural Network".</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-193c3a719cf23865bd32e0d110afb409</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288832</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Modified Neural Network (FSC)">
      <data key="d0">Modified Neural Network (FSC)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">This is a modified neural network connected to the "10-pole 12-slot FSC Motor" that receives external knowledge transfer from the "Pre-trained Neural Network".</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-193c3a719cf23865bd32e0d110afb409</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288832</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Efficiency Map (FSC)">
      <data key="d0">Efficiency Map (FSC)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The Efficiency Map is a description of a map (not explicitly visualized) generated from the "Modified Neural Network (FSC)".</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-193c3a719cf23865bd32e0d110afb409</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Motor Design">
      <data key="d0">Motor Design</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Motor Design is the engineering process detailed in the image analysis that involves applying neural networks for electrical machines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-193c3a719cf23865bd32e0d110afb409</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4-Pole 24-slot IPM Motor Diagram">
      <data key="d0">4-Pole 24-slot IPM Motor Diagram</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A circular cross-section diagram depicting an Interior Permanent Magnet (IPM) motor with a 4-pole rotor and 24-slot stator.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-193c3a719cf23865bd32e0d110afb409</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Modified Neural Network">
      <data key="d0">Modified Neural Network</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A neural network that has been adapted or retrained for a specific transfer learning task, either internal or external.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-193c3a719cf23865bd32e0d110afb409</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Efficiency Map (Central)">
      <data key="d0">Efficiency Map (Central)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A colored graph illustrating the efficiency of a motor design, featuring Tm (torque) and Nm (speed) axes, showing varying levels of efficiency across different operating points.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-193c3a719cf23865bd32e0d110afb409</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="10-pole 12-slot FSC Motor Diagram">
      <data key="d0">10-pole 12-slot FSC Motor Diagram</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A circular cross-section diagram depicting a Flux Switching Permanent Magnet (FSC) motor with a 10-pole rotor and 12-slot stator.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-193c3a719cf23865bd32e0d110afb409</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Efficiency Map (Right)">
      <data key="d0">Efficiency Map (Right)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A data output or representation of motor efficiency, conceptually similar to the central efficiency map but associated with the FSC motor and its modified neural network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-193c3a719cf23865bd32e0d110afb409</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288835</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.25">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.25</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.25 is a scientific visualization titled "Optimized geometry’s magnetic field distribution for 1x1 Non-linear environment (SeqTO-v1 with GA)".</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6719c866b8100236897a8ad6ae78d11f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288835</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimized Geometry">
      <data key="d0">Optimized Geometry</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Optimized geometry refers to the C-core structure that has been subjected to an optimization process to achieve a specific magnetic field distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;The Optimized Geometry refers to the structural arrangement of the C-core electromagnet designed for a linear environment, achieved through topology optimization.&lt;SEP&gt;Optimized geometry refers to the specific arrangement of material (grey filled cells) for a non-linear environment, designed using an optimization method to achieve better performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Optimized geometry refers to the design of a device, likely an electromagnet, that has been refined through an optimization process to achieve specific performance characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Optimized Geometry refers to the physical structure of the Synchronous Reluctance Motor (SynRM) rotor, achieved through a Genetic Algorithm.&lt;SEP&gt;Optimized Geometry refers to the structural design of the Synchronous Reluctance Motor (SynRM) rotor that has been enhanced for better performance, depicted in panel (a) of Figure 4.30.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d8330ecbeee1c4ebe7ea64bfd7fbb45b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0691c9f7251ff59c05b1bc277ea93e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6719c866b8100236897a8ad6ae78d11f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b7b73719952876f4f9c0e6b4efe0027&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b56c95146eb3d0e8591ffefd6ab679ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abb64932da4baf92322bc61ecf0df44f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288835</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Linear Environment">
      <data key="d0">Non-Linear Environment</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A non-linear environment is the specific context or condition for which the geometry is optimized, implying complex interactions.&lt;SEP&gt;A non-linear environment describes conditions where material properties exhibit non-linear behavior in response to a magnetic field, complexifying simulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6719c866b8100236897a8ad6ae78d11f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0691c9f7251ff59c05b1bc277ea93e1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288835</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-shaped Core Material">
      <data key="d0">C-shaped Core Material</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The C-shaped core material is an intricate structure within the optimized geometry, likely a ferromagnetic material that guides magnetic flux.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6719c866b8100236897a8ad6ae78d11f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288837</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stepped Airgap">
      <data key="d0">Stepped Airgap</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A stepped airgap is a narrow separation between the C-shaped core and an adjacent green region, filled with non-magnetic material.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6719c866b8100236897a8ad6ae78d11f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288837</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-shaped Core">
      <data key="d0">C-shaped Core</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The C-shaped Core is a central component of the optimized geometry, depicted in orange and yellow, guiding magnetic flux.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6719c866b8100236897a8ad6ae78d11f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Higher Performance Value">
      <data key="d0">Higher Performance Value</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Higher Performance Value refers to a superior outcome achieved by the 1x1 controller compared to the 3x3 controller in the optimization process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6719c866b8100236897a8ad6ae78d11f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Finer Granularity">
      <data key="d0">Finer Granularity</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A characteristic of the 1x1 controller, allowing for more precise design and improved performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Finer Granularity describes a more detailed and precise optimal design achieved by the 1x1 controller.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6719c866b8100236897a8ad6ae78d11f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0691c9f7251ff59c05b1bc277ea93e1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288839</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Gradient Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Policy Gradient Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Policy Gradient (PG) algorithms are a class of methods in Reinforcement Learning focused on directly learning and refining a policy through training.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy Gradient (PG) algorithms are a class of Reinforcement Learning algorithms that directly optimize the policy function.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy Gradient (PG) algorithms are a class of Reinforcement Learning algorithms used to optimize policy parameters to maximize a performance measure.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy Gradient Algorithms are a class of reinforcement learning algorithms that optimize a parameterized policy directly by estimating its gradient with respect to a performance measure.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy Gradient (PG) algorithms are reinforcement learning methods that update neural network weights using the gradient of a performance measure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb28e5c877704a09cf7eb4cbeb6522fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1cc3c9a8202b9032fe06ba473d7c76d8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288839</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Performance Measure J(θ)">
      <data key="d0">Performance Measure J(θ)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">J(θ) represents the performance measure that the learning algorithm aims to maximize by adjusting the policy parameters θ.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288839</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Parameters θ">
      <data key="d0">Policy Parameters θ</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">θ (theta) is a vector representing the parameters (weights and biases) of the neural network that defines the policy π, which are optimized during learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288840</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Learning Rate α">
      <data key="d0">Learning Rate α</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">α (alpha) is a positive scalar hyperparameter that controls the step size of the parameter update in optimization algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288840</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Total Accumulated Reward Gt">
      <data key="d0">Total Accumulated Reward Gt</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Gt represents the total accumulated reward (return) from time step t until the end of the current episode, serving as an estimator for the value of the action-state pair.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288840</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Log-Policy Gradient">
      <data key="d0">Log-Policy Gradient</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Log-Policy Gradient, denoted as ∇log(π(at | st; θ)), indicates the direction in which to change θ to increase the probability of selecting action at in state st.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288840</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy π(at | st; θ)">
      <data key="d0">Policy π(at | st; θ)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">π(at | st; θ) is the policy, represented as a probability distribution over actions given a state and the current parameters θ, which outputs the probability of selecting action at when in state st.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288841</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action at">
      <data key="d0">Action at</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">at represents the action taken by the agent at time step t.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288841</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State st">
      <data key="d0">State st</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">st represents the state of the environment at time step t.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288842</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multiplication (⋅)">
      <data key="d0">Multiplication (⋅)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Multiplication is a mathematical operation used for scalar multiplication of the learning rate and the return with the gradient term.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288842</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gradient (∇)">
      <data key="d0">Gradient (∇)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Gradient is a vector operator that yields the vector of partial derivatives of a scalar function with respect to its variables.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288842</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Logarithm (log)">
      <data key="d0">Logarithm (log)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Logarithm is a mathematical function applied to the policy's probability output, crucial for simplifying gradient calculation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288843</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stochastic Policies">
      <data key="d0">Stochastic Policies</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stochastic Policies are policies where action probabilities are non-zero for multiple actions, supporting exploration in Reinforcement Learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288843</data>
    </node>
    <node id="REINFORCE Algorithm">
      <data key="d0">REINFORCE Algorithm</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">REINFORCE Algorithm is a specific Policy Gradient algorithm that uses the total accumulated reward Gt as a weight for the log-policy gradient.&lt;SEP&gt;REINFORCE algorithm is a specific Policy Gradient method that leverages the log-probability term in its derivation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288843</data>
    </node>
    <node id="A3C">
      <data key="d0">A3C</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A3C (Asynchronous Advantage Actor-Critic) is an Actor-Critic Method, an extension of A2C for asynchronous training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288843</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Total Accumulated Reward">
      <data key="d0">Total Accumulated Reward</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Total Accumulated Reward, denoted by `Gt`, is the return from time step `t` until the end of the current episode, serving as an estimator for the value of an action-state pair.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288844</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gradient of Natural Logarithm">
      <data key="d0">Gradient of Natural Logarithm</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Gradient of Natural Logarithm, `nabla log(pi(at | st; theta))`, is also known as the 'score function' or 'log-policy gradient', indicating the direction to change policy parameters to increase action probability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288844</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy (pi)">
      <data key="d0">Policy (pi)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Policy `pi(at | st; theta)` is a probability distribution over actions given a state and current parameters, outputting the probability of selecting action `at` in state `st`.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288844</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action (at)">
      <data key="d0">Action (at)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Action `at` refers to a specific action taken at time step `t` by the learning agent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288844</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State (st)">
      <data key="d0">State (st)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The State `st` refers to the environment's state at time step `t`.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288845</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Logarithm Function">
      <data key="d0">Logarithm Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Logarithm Function, `log`, is the natural logarithm applied to the policy's probability output, simplifying gradient calculation.&lt;SEP&gt;The logarithm function (log) is a mathematical operation mentioned in the equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288845</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Performance Measure J">
      <data key="d0">Performance Measure J</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Performance Measure J is an objective function that the agent aims to maximize by adjusting its policy parameters. Its gradient drives the parameter update.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance Measure J (J(theta)) is the function implicitly optimized by adjusting policy parameters in the direction of its gradient to maximize a performance measure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288845</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Autonomous Navigation">
      <data key="d0">Autonomous Navigation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Autonomous Navigation is a practical application field for policy gradient algorithms, particularly in environments with continuous action spaces.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288845</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exploration vs Exploitation">
      <data key="d0">Exploration vs Exploitation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Exploration vs Exploitation refers to the fundamental dilemma in reinforcement learning concerning balancing trying new actions (exploration) and leveraging known good actions (exploitation).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b6b9bf681c07677d8af5a26068c52f6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288846</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 2.4: Parametrized Geometry-Transformer">
      <data key="d0">Figure 2.4: Parametrized Geometry-Transformer</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 2.4: Parametrized Geometry-Transformer is a 2D cross-sectional diagram illustrating a transformer core with windings and labeled dimensions, representing parameterized geometry.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c59eba9d5ca580dfd954f743c22649a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288847</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transformer Core">
      <data key="d0">Transformer Core</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Transformer Core is the primary structure of the transformer, depicted as an E-shaped block made of light gray material, consistent with M19 silicon steel.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c59eba9d5ca580dfd954f743c22649a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288847</data>
    </node>
    <node id="W">
      <data key="d0">W</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The entity designated as 'W' appears in various technical and scientific contexts, often representing a critical parameter or variable. In the domain of Deep Learning, particularly within Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), W consistently refers to the set of all convolutional weights (parameters) that define the model's transformation of input data. These weights are crucial parameters whose distribution is frequently analyzed and adjusted to accurately predict outcomes like Q(s, a).

Beyond neural networks, 'W' can also denote a variable, often representing a random variable or parameter in general equations, and specifically within the context of KL Divergence, it signifies a set of parameters or variables. In statistical and machine learning applications, 'W' frequently represents weights or parameters over which integrals are performed. Furthermore, 'W' can function as a dimension label, indicating the width of core material in various components, including limbs and spaces between windings and cores, highlighting its diverse application as a descriptive identifier in engineering and material science.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c59eba9d5ca580dfd954f743c22649a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26c769a26f512a088ad02de1c81c6ddf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-215b04d60447db6b473faf768914326f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-916831dfb3bd404b7a6bf4cf512b41b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a5cf1e19ea0a8639d0d309beab7d404</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293007</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X">
      <data key="d0">X</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">X is a dimension label indicating the total horizontal width of the top horizontal limb of the transformer core.&lt;SEP&gt;X represents the input data or features used for training the Deep Learning model, typically an image or a collection of images in the context of pixel classification.&lt;SEP&gt;X denotes the input training data observed.&lt;SEP&gt;X represents a variable in the context of a conditional probability distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;X is a variable in the equation, representing data or another random variable.&lt;SEP&gt;The symbol 'X' is a variable within the equation, likely representing a set of input data or observations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26c769a26f512a088ad02de1c81c6ddf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-916831dfb3bd404b7a6bf4cf512b41b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c59eba9d5ca580dfd954f743c22649a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a5cf1e19ea0a8639d0d309beab7d404</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293003</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Y_C">
      <data key="d0">Y_C</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Y_C is a dimension label indicating a vertical dimension within the right internal rectangular space, specifically the height of the right coil relative to the horizontal central axis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c59eba9d5ca580dfd954f743c22649a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288848</data>
    </node>
    <node id="M19 Silicon Steel Material (NL)">
      <data key="d0">M19 Silicon Steel Material (NL)</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">M19 Silicon Steel Material (NL) is the material composing the transformer core, as indicated in the contextual text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c59eba9d5ca580dfd954f743c22649a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288848</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Table 2.2">
      <data key="d0">Table 2.2</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Table 2.2 is a reference mentioned in the context that would contain dimension changes for the coils, which are visually defined by the diagram.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 2.2 is a table titled 'Transformer Parameters' that lists four parameters (X, y, W, and yc) along with their minimum and maximum values in millimeters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c59eba9d5ca580dfd954f743c22649a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e98180b8e135e08cf842f2a94ed38a2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transformer Depth">
      <data key="d0">Transformer Depth</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Transformer Depth is a third dimension, specifically 2.5mm, that is not shown in the 2D cross-section diagram.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c59eba9d5ca580dfd954f743c22649a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288850</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parametrized Geometry-Transformer">
      <data key="d0">Parametrized Geometry-Transformer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Parametrized Geometry-Transformer refers to the concept of a transformer design where geometry parameters can be adjusted or analyzed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c59eba9d5ca580dfd954f743c22649a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288851</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 2.4">
      <data key="d0">Figure 2.4</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 2.4 is a visual representation or diagram specified as a cross-sectional view of a Parametrized Geometry-Transformer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c59eba9d5ca580dfd954f743c22649a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288851</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Robotics Control">
      <data key="d0">Robotics Control</data>
      <data key="d1">application</data>
      <data key="d2">Robotics Control is a practical application of reinforcement learning where agents are trained to control robotic systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Robotics Control is a practical application where autonomous agents learn complex behaviors, like teaching a robot to walk or grasp objects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288851</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Financial Trading Strategies">
      <data key="d0">Financial Trading Strategies</data>
      <data key="d1">application</data>
      <data key="d2">Financial Trading Strategies is a practical application where the update rule can be used.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288851</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recommendation Systems">
      <data key="d0">Recommendation Systems</data>
      <data key="d1">application</data>
      <data key="d2">Recommendation systems personalize recommendations by exploring user preferences and exploiting popular items.&lt;SEP&gt;Recommendation Systems is a practical application where the update rule can be used.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288851</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Return Gt">
      <data key="d0">Return Gt</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Represents the total return from time step t onwards, defined as the cumulative reward function or total rewards an agent shall receive till the end of an episode. It is the sum of all rewards obtained from time t onwards until the episode terminates.&lt;SEP&gt;Return Gt is the total accumulated reward that serves as a measure of performance, used to estimate the gradient of J in many Policy Gradient algorithms.&lt;SEP&gt;Gt represents the return or total discounted reward from time step 't' onwards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8823695e17798553515e243bdbfda2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Improvement">
      <data key="d0">Policy Improvement</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The core task in reinforcement learning that Q-Learning addresses by iteratively updating the $Q(s,a)$ values to converge towards an optimal policy.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy improvement is a step where a policy is updated, potentially leading to a new deterministic policy.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy Improvement is the theoretical enhancement of an agent's policy with each update, leading to higher expected cumulative rewards, by moving parameters in the direction suggested by the gradient.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Direct Policy Search">
      <data key="d0">Direct Policy Search</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Direct Policy Search is a method where Policy Gradient algorithms directly search in the policy space using gradient-based updates, unlike value-based methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gradient-Based Optimization">
      <data key="d0">Gradient-Based Optimization</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Gradient-Based Optimization is a method used in Policy Gradient algorithms to iteratively adjust parameters of a neural network policy, moving them in the direction that maximizes a performance measure.&lt;SEP&gt;Gradient-Based Optimization is a class of optimization algorithms that use gradients to find the minimum or maximum of a function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neural Network Policy">
      <data key="d0">Neural Network Policy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Neural Network Policy is a parameterized function approximated by a neural network, whose weights, represented by $\theta$, determine the probability of selecting actions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Parameters ($\theta_{t+1}$)">
      <data key="d0">Policy Parameters ($\theta_{t+1}$)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">$\theta_{t+1}$ represents the updated set of parameters (weights) of the policy network after the $t$-th iteration of optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Parameters ($\theta_t$)">
      <data key="d0">Policy Parameters ($\theta_t$)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">$\theta_t$ represents the current set of parameters (weights) of the policy network at the $t$-th iteration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gradient of Performance Measure ($\nabla J (\theta_t)$)">
      <data key="d0">Gradient of Performance Measure ($\nabla J (\theta_t)$)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">$\nabla J (\theta_t)$ is the gradient of the performance measure $J$ with respect to the policy parameters $\theta$, evaluated at the current parameters $\theta_t$, indicating the direction of steepest ascent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Performance Measure ($J$)">
      <data key="d0">Performance Measure ($J$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Performance Measure ($J$) is a metric, similar to total accumulated reward, that an agent aims to maximize by adjusting its policy parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vector Addition">
      <data key="d0">Vector Addition</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Vector Addition is a mathematical operation where new parameters are obtained by adding a vector to current parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Scalar Multiplication">
      <data key="d0">Scalar Multiplication</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Scalar multiplication is a mathematical operation that scales a vector by a learning rate, adjusting the magnitude of the update.&lt;SEP&gt;Scalar Multiplication is a mathematical operation where a vector, like the gradient, is scaled by a scalar, like the learning rate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gradient Operator ($\nabla$)">
      <data key="d0">Gradient Operator ($\nabla$)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Gradient Operator ($\nabla$) computes the vector of partial derivatives of a scalar-valued performance measure with respect to each component of a parameter vector.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probability of Action Selection ($p(a)$)">
      <data key="d0">Probability of Action Selection ($p(a)$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Probability of Action Selection ($p(a)$) is a factor influencing the Performance Measure ($J$) and is derived from the policy $\pi$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288856</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Return ($G_t$)">
      <data key="d0">Return ($G_t$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Return ($G_t$) is a key concept in Reinforcement Learning and Markov Decision Processes, representing the sum of all future immediate rewards an agent expects to accumulate starting from a specific time step $t$. It is the objective function an RL agent aims to maximize.&lt;SEP&gt;The Return ($G_t$) is a fundamental concept in reinforcement learning, representing the total accumulated reward an agent receives from a specific time step $t+1$ until the end of an episode at time step $n$. It is the cumulative sum of all future rewards.&lt;SEP&gt;The return at time step $t$ is the cumulative reward from time $t$ onwards, typically represented as the sum of discounted future rewards.&lt;SEP&gt;Return ($G_t$) is defined as the total accumulated reward and serves as a measure of performance used in many Policy Gradient algorithms to estimate the gradient of $J$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288856</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action Probabilities">
      <data key="d0">Action Probabilities</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Action Probabilities are directly outputted by policies learned through Policy Gradient methods, allowing for more stable handling of continuous action spaces.&lt;SEP&gt;Action Probabilities are the outputs of the Policy Gradient algorithm, represented as p(aᵢ, s), indicating the likelihood of selecting a specific action 'aᵢ' given a state 's'.&lt;SEP&gt;Action probabilities are the outputs of the Actor component, denoted as p(a_i, s), representing the likelihood of different actions (SOFT UP, SOFT DOWN, SOFT LEFT, SOFT RIGHT, UP, DOWN, LEFT, RIGHT).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27909faa34224de8c011e982c98fcef6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66f8a9943508aa952a9845858b8d3374</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288857</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Value Function Approximations">
      <data key="d0">Value Function Approximations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Value Function Approximations are intermediate steps in value-based methods which Policy Gradient algorithms aim to avoid for direct optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a27902bb1d11b0f453eb766cb38c22a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288858</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PM Motor">
      <data key="d0">PM Motor</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">PM Motor refers to a Permanent Magnet motor, specified as the target motor in a transfer learning task.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Encoder-Decoder">
      <data key="d0">Encoder-Decoder</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An Encoder-Decoder is a central structure within the neural network, consisting of LSTM layers, a context vector, and an attention mechanism, responsible for processing input sequences and generating output sequences.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Motor Cross-Section">
      <data key="d0">Motor Cross-Section</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A Motor Cross-Section is an inset graphic within one of the efficiency maps, depicting the internal components of a motor, including a stator, windings, rotor, and permanent magnets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Section 3.8.2 External Knowledge Transfer">
      <data key="d0">Section 3.8.2 External Knowledge Transfer</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Section 3.8.2 External Knowledge Transfer is a textual context that confirms the figure's relation to transfer learning tasks and changes in network weights.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa9f53fdb95bf019f06cc136a7089a2d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.23">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.23</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.23 is a visualization representing the optimized geometry’s magnetic field distribution for a 3x3 Linear environment using SeqTO-v1 with GA.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d8330ecbeee1c4ebe7ea64bfd7fbb45b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-Core Geometry">
      <data key="d0">C-Core Geometry</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">C-Core Geometry is a specific configuration of a magnetic core, used in the visualization of magnetic field distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;C-core geometry refers to the shape of the magnetic core, which resembles a conventional "C" shape.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d8330ecbeee1c4ebe7ea64bfd7fbb45b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-081d5d2ff62504474fc8532dd817fb39</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288860</data>
