    <node id="F:\From_D_drive\Code\research_extender\output\Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis\auto\images\17c4d90b26b2853e55ba5ff3384cc7f8e039219298ea862620375f49b9e9f776.jpg">
      <data key="d0">F:\From_D_drive\Code\research_extender\output\Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis\auto\images\17c4d90b26b2853e55ba5ff3384cc7f8e039219298ea862620375f49b9e9f776.jpg</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The file path of the image being analyzed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17fa5f6a446284811fca7ceb6337e9ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288871</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 3.5">
      <data key="d0">Figure 3.5</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 3.5 is a reference figure that outlines the stages of the prediction process, where the DQ flux linkages prediction is Stage 1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17fa5f6a446284811fca7ceb6337e9ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Current And Advance Angle Vector">
      <data key="d0">Current And Advance Angle Vector</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">This is a 1-D vector input representing current excitation and advance angle, fed to a feedforward neural network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17fa5f6a446284811fca7ceb6337e9ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flattened 1D Vector">
      <data key="d0">Flattened 1D Vector</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">This is the output of the Convolutional Neural Network after it has been transformed into a one-dimensional vector.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17fa5f6a446284811fca7ceb6337e9ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288873</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Addition Operation">
      <data key="d0">Addition Operation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">This operation combines the processed features from both the Feedforward Neural Network and the Flattened 1D Vector.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical operation ($+$) that combines the immediate reward with the discounted future value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17fa5f6a446284811fca7ceb6337e9ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288873</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Agent Movement with SeqTO-v2 Controller">
      <data key="d0">Agent Movement with SeqTO-v2 Controller</data>
      <data key="d1">description</data>
      <data key="d2">Agent Movement with SeqTO-v2 Controller is the title of Figure 5.12, detailing a visual representation of how an agent moves and places data blocks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-596bd8f0cbcfc00de61fa772b9317edc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288873</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SeqTO-v2 Controller">
      <data key="d0">SeqTO-v2 Controller</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The SeqTO-v2 Controller is a system that governs the agent's movement and actions, including placing data blocks and executing different types of moves like 'Right', 'Soft Right', 'Soft Down', and 'Down'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-596bd8f0cbcfc00de61fa772b9317edc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288874</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grid-Based Environment">
      <data key="d0">Grid-Based Environment</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A grid-based environment is a structured space, composed of 9x9 cells, where the agent operates and places data blocks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-596bd8f0cbcfc00de61fa772b9317edc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288874</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Data Blocks">
      <data key="d0">Data Blocks</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Data Blocks are objects of varying sizes (e.g., 3x3, 3x1, 1x3) that the agent places in the grid, represented by shaded gray squares with red borders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-596bd8f0cbcfc00de61fa772b9317edc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Compass-Like Diagram">
      <data key="d0">Compass-Like Diagram</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Compass-Like Diagram is a visual element in the bottom-left corner of the image, representing potential movement directions ('Up', 'Down', 'Left', 'Right') as a legend.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-596bd8f0cbcfc00de61fa772b9317edc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Initial State">
      <data key="d0">Initial State</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Initial State is the condition shown in Panel 1, where the Controller is at the top-left of the grid and material is confined to its 3x3 block.&lt;SEP&gt;The Initial State is the starting point in the diagram, showing a 3x3 data block being placed by the agent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d610c2b7090b4469e2b4b784c49c5807&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-596bd8f0cbcfc00de61fa772b9317edc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Right Action">
      <data key="d0">Right Action</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Right Action is an action where a data block moves one cell to the right, and the agent's position also shifts one cell to the right, anchoring the block.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-596bd8f0cbcfc00de61fa772b9317edc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soft Right Action">
      <data key="d0">Soft Right Action</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Soft Right Action is an action where a smaller 3x1 data block is placed, demonstrating the controller's ability to place different block sizes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-596bd8f0cbcfc00de61fa772b9317edc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soft Down Action">
      <data key="d0">Soft Down Action</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Soft Down Action is an action where a 1x3 data block is placed, illustrating another 'soft' action for differently shaped blocks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-596bd8f0cbcfc00de61fa772b9317edc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Down Action">
      <data key="d0">Down Action</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Down Action is an action where a data block shifts one cell downwards, with the agent's position also moving down one cell while maintaining its relative position.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-596bd8f0cbcfc00de61fa772b9317edc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3x3 Block">
      <data key="d0">3x3 Block</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A 3x3 block represents a specific configuration of data, consisting of nine gray cells, placed by the agent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-596bd8f0cbcfc00de61fa772b9317edc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3x1 Block">
      <data key="d0">3x1 Block</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A 3x1 block is a specific size of structural element that the controller can place using soft actions.&lt;SEP&gt;A 3x1 Block is a specific size of data block (three gray cells) placed during the 'Soft Right' action.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-596bd8f0cbcfc00de61fa772b9317edc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1x3 Block">
      <data key="d0">1x3 Block</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A 1x3 block is a specific size of structural element that the controller can place using soft actions.&lt;SEP&gt;A 1x3 Block is a specific size of data block (three gray cells) placed during the 'Soft Down' action.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-596bd8f0cbcfc00de61fa772b9317edc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Data Block">
      <data key="d0">Data Block</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A data block is a piece of data of varying sizes (e.g., 3x3, 3x1, 1x3) that an agent places in the grid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-596bd8f0cbcfc00de61fa772b9317edc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288878</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Black Square">
      <data key="d0">Black Square</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The black square, highlighted with a red outline, indicates the position of an active element or the 'agent' that moves within the grid.&lt;SEP&gt;A small black square within the grid represents the agent's current position.&lt;SEP&gt;The black square represents the controller's position or the result of the controller's action within the design space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1474f4155ebb5657d8ca7f3c0d29b49&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1b166c346deb9e14c2fe057bc33cdbf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-596bd8f0cbcfc00de61fa772b9317edc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288878</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shaded Gray Squares with a Red Border">
      <data key="d0">Shaded Gray Squares with a Red Border</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">These squares indicate the data block being placed by the agent in the grid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-596bd8f0cbcfc00de61fa772b9317edc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flowchart-Like Diagram">
      <data key="d0">Flowchart-Like Diagram</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The overall composition of the image is a flowchart-like diagram, illustrating the progression of actions and transitions within the grid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-596bd8f0cbcfc00de61fa772b9317edc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maximum Torque">
      <data key="d0">Maximum Torque</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Maximum Torque is one of the target predictions of the neural network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b7e7c8729e5eef7f4ff08e29b1d0014</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maximum Speed">
      <data key="d0">Maximum Speed</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Maximum Speed is one of the target predictions of the neural network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b7e7c8729e5eef7f4ff08e29b1d0014</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flowchart">
      <data key="d0">Flowchart</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A visual representation in Figure 3.5 detailing the sequential process for calculating efficiency maps, presented in black and white.&lt;SEP&gt;The flowchart is a visual representation of the supplementary neural network architecture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b7e7c8729e5eef7f4ff08e29b1d0014&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3D Geometric Parameters">
      <data key="d0">3D Geometric Parameters</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">3D Geometric Parameters are one of the input sources for the neural network, processed by a Feedforward Neural Network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b7e7c8729e5eef7f4ff08e29b1d0014</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Image of Stator-Rotor Assembly">
      <data key="d0">Image of Stator-Rotor Assembly</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The image of a stator-rotor assembly for an electric motor, depicted in a cross-sectional view, is another input source for the neural network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b7e7c8729e5eef7f4ff08e29b1d0014</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feedforward Neural Network (First)">
      <data key="d0">Feedforward Neural Network (First)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The First Feedforward Neural Network processes the "3D Geometric Parameters" input.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b7e7c8729e5eef7f4ff08e29b1d0014</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feedforward Neural Network (Second)">
      <data key="d0">Feedforward Neural Network (Second)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Second Feedforward Neural Network receives input from both the "Flattened to 1D Vector" and the First Feedforward Neural Network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b7e7c8729e5eef7f4ff08e29b1d0014</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maximum Torque and Speed Prediction">
      <data key="d0">Maximum Torque and Speed Prediction</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Maximum Torque and Speed Prediction is the objective of the neural network architecture illustrated in Figure 3.10.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b7e7c8729e5eef7f4ff08e29b1d0014</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stator-Rotor Assembly Image">
      <data key="d0">Stator-Rotor Assembly Image</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Stator-Rotor Assembly Image is an input for the neural network, depicting an electric motor in cross-sectional view, processed by a Convolutional Neural Network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b7e7c8729e5eef7f4ff08e29b1d0014</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288883</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electric Motor Cross-Section">
      <data key="d0">Electric Motor Cross-Section</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Electric Motor Cross-Section is the specific content shown in the input image, featuring a stator and a rotor with embedded magnets.&lt;SEP&gt;The Electric Motor Cross-Section is a visual representation of a motor, showing its internal components like the stator, rotor, and magnets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-640da4d848d6502c665bfc0ea66dce13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b7e7c8729e5eef7f4ff08e29b1d0014</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288883</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Max Torque &amp; Max Speed">
      <data key="d0">Max Torque &amp; Max Speed</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Max Torque &amp; Max Speed are the final predicted outputs of the supplementary neural network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b7e7c8729e5eef7f4ff08e29b1d0014</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288883</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.24">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.24</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.24 is a technical diagram illustrating an optimized geometry for a non-linear environment using a 1x1 controller within the SeqTO-v1 with GA method.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0691c9f7251ff59c05b1bc277ea93e1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288884</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.22">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.22</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.22 shows the optimized geometry for a 3x3 Linear environment, obtained using SeqTO-v1 with GA.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 4.22 is another figure referenced in the text, representing a 3x3 controller, contrasted with Figure 4.24.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0691c9f7251ff59c05b1bc277ea93e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abb64932da4baf92322bc61ecf0df44f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288884</data>
    </node>
    <node id="8% Increase">
      <data key="d0">8% Increase</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">8% increase represents the performance difference between the 1x1 controller and the 3x3 controller.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0691c9f7251ff59c05b1bc277ea93e1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288884</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SeqTO-v1 With GA">
      <data key="d0">SeqTO-v1 With GA</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An optimization method that uses a 1x1 controller to achieve an optimized geometry for a non-linear environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0691c9f7251ff59c05b1bc277ea93e1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288885</data>
    </node>
    <node id="51 N">
      <data key="d0">51 N</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The performance achieved by the 3x3 controller.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0691c9f7251ff59c05b1bc277ea93e1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288885</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Learning Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Q-Learning Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Q-learning algorithms are a type of model-free reinforcement learning algorithm to learn an action-value function.&lt;SEP&gt;Q-Learning Algorithms are methods that aim to be improved by incorporating more numerical data into the state space within the SeqTO-v2 environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0534c4408a52c608a5a24ccf9951f5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288886</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electrical Coils">
      <data key="d0">Electrical Coils</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Electrical Coils are represented by two prominent red rectangular blocks labeled "COIL" in the discrete design domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0534c4408a52c608a5a24ccf9951f5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288886</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Field Magnitude">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Field Magnitude</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">An attribute indicated by "avg |B|", which is a performance measure in the design domain.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic Field Magnitude is indicated by a gradient color scheme from 0 to 6, with red representing higher magnitudes and dark blue representing lower magnitudes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0534c4408a52c608a5a24ccf9951f5f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678d567f82303d2bd1928259fd243036</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288886</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced State Representation Method">
      <data key="d0">Advanced State Representation Method</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Advanced State Representation Method is a technique discussed in the accompanying text, demonstrated by Figure 5.11, to encode numerical information into the state space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0534c4408a52c608a5a24ccf9951f5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288886</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INITIAL POSITION">
      <data key="d0">INITIAL POSITION</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A reference point within the grid, labeled in black, uppercase text, indicating a starting location for the system.&lt;SEP&gt;INITIAL POSITION refers to a specific gray square within the linear magnetic material, indicating a starting point within the design.&lt;SEP&gt;INITIAL POSITION marks a grey rectangular cell in the Discrete Design Domain, indicating a starting point within the structure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec0d779e474774183c36eade5f33db6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abb64932da4baf92322bc61ecf0df44f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0534c4408a52c608a5a24ccf9951f5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288888</data>
    </node>
    <node id="L-Shaped Metallic Structure">
      <data key="d0">L-Shaped Metallic Structure</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The L-Shaped Metallic Structure is a complex geometric configuration visualized in the magnetic field distribution, colored in a gradient to indicate magnetic field magnitude.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0534c4408a52c608a5a24ccf9951f5f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288888</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Table Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Table Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The method of analyzing the provided table's structure and content.&lt;SEP&gt;The Table Analysis section describes a table presenting geometric parameters and their nominal values for a reference motor design.&lt;SEP&gt;Table Analysis describes the process of examining and interpreting data presented in a tabular format.&lt;SEP&gt;Table Analysis is a section that describes a table's structure and contents, including specific data points and their implications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f5a390cc7143789eccd25f5f3623694&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6504e2cf679305513217b8548174a68b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28be27ccbb95fc691bf7d1ab7fac43a1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288888</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Independent Parameters">
      <data key="d0">Independent Parameters</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A concept describing specific motor design parameters that are considered independent variables.&lt;SEP&gt;Independent Parameters refers to fixed or controlled variables that define a specific motor configuration or operating condition in electric motor design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1f6cebd0187b0c1924a3e6491e8ebe8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28be27ccbb95fc691bf7d1ab7fac43a1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288889</data>
    </node>
    <node id="24 Slots 4 Poles 33.33 Arms 400Vdc">
      <data key="d0">24 Slots 4 Poles 33.33 Arms 400Vdc</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A data string representing specific motor design parameters: 24 slots, 4 poles, 33.33 Arms (armature current), and 400Vdc (DC link voltage).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28be27ccbb95fc691bf7d1ab7fac43a1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288889</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parameterized IPM Motor">
      <data key="d0">Parameterized IPM Motor</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of electric motor, specifically a 24-slot 4-pole design, that is the subject of a design space study.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28be27ccbb95fc691bf7d1ab7fac43a1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288889</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Independent Parameters Table">
      <data key="d0">Independent Parameters Table</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The Independent Parameters Table is a single-cell table that contains motor design parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28be27ccbb95fc691bf7d1ab7fac43a1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288891</data>
    </node>
    <node id="24 Slots 4 Poles">
      <data key="d0">24 Slots 4 Poles</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">24 Slots 4 Poles refers to a specific configuration of a parameterized IPM motor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28be27ccbb95fc691bf7d1ab7fac43a1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288891</data>
    </node>
    <node id="33.33 Arms">
      <data key="d0">33.33 Arms</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">33.33 Arms likely refers to the armature current, a crucial electrical operating condition for the motor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28be27ccbb95fc691bf7d1ab7fac43a1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288891</data>
    </node>
    <node id="400Vdc">
      <data key="d0">400Vdc</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">400Vdc indicates the DC link voltage, an electrical operating condition for the motor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28be27ccbb95fc691bf7d1ab7fac43a1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288891</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COIL Elements">
      <data key="d0">COIL Elements</data>
      <data key="d1">component</data>
      <data key="d2">COIL elements are rectangular blocks colored red within the grid, labeled 'COIL,' functioning as part of the electromagnetic device.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec0d779e474774183c36eade5f33db6b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288892</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grid-Based Representation">
      <data key="d0">Grid-Based Representation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Grid-Based Representation is the visual and conceptual structure used to define the discrete design domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec0d779e474774183c36eade5f33db6b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288893</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tabular Reinforcement Learning Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Tabular Reinforcement Learning Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Tabular Reinforcement Learning Algorithms are a type of algorithm for which the discrete design domain is ideal due to its integer encoding for state representation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec0d779e474774183c36eade5f33db6b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288893</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spatial Orientation">
      <data key="d0">Spatial Orientation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Spatial Orientation refers to the 3D coordinate system icon at the bottom-left corner of the grid, indicating the design's alignment in space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec0d779e474774183c36eade5f33db6b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288893</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COIL Element">
      <data key="d0">COIL Element</data>
      <data key="d1">component</data>
      <data key="d2">The COIL element refers to two red rectangular blocks within the design domain, labeled 'COIL', representing part of the electromagnetic device.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec0d779e474774183c36eade5f33db6b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288893</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3D Coordinate System Icon">
      <data key="d0">3D Coordinate System Icon</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A 3D Coordinate System Icon is present at the bottom-left corner of the grid, showing labeled axes (Y, Z, X) to indicate the spatial orientation of the design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec0d779e474774183c36eade5f33db6b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288893</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Integer Encoding">
      <data key="d0">Integer Encoding</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Integer Encoding is a method used for the state representation of the discrete design domain, making it ideal for tabular reinforcement learning algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec0d779e474774183c36eade5f33db6b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288895</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kolehmainen">
      <data key="d0">Kolehmainen</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Kolehmainen is the author cited for the information regarding conventional rotor designs in synchronous reluctance motors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06d562d92283cc555ded41962ea3fb9e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288896</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Salient Pole Type">
      <data key="d0">Salient Pole Type</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The salient pole type is a conventional rotor design for synchronous reluctance motors, characterized by multiple separate laminations stacked along the shaft, each with protruding salient poles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06d562d92283cc555ded41962ea3fb9e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288896</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Axial Lamination Type">
      <data key="d0">Axial Lamination Type</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The axial lamination type is a conventional rotor design for synchronous reluctance motors, consisting of multiple concentric layers arranged axially along the rotor's length.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06d562d92283cc555ded41962ea3fb9e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288896</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transverse Lamination Type">
      <data key="d0">Transverse Lamination Type</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The transverse lamination type is a conventional rotor design for synchronous reluctance motors, featuring a complex internal structure with laminations arranged to create curved patterns across its radial cross-section.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06d562d92283cc555ded41962ea3fb9e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288897</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Barriers">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Barriers</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnetic barriers are internal structures within synchronous reluctance motor rotors created by lamination techniques, crucial for their operation and the generation of reluctance torque.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06d562d92283cc555ded41962ea3fb9e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288897</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rotor Designs">
      <data key="d0">Rotor Designs</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Rotor designs refer to the structural configurations of the rotating component in synchronous reluctance motors, including salient pole, axial lamination, and transverse lamination types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06d562d92283cc555ded41962ea3fb9e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288898</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shaft">
      <data key="d0">Shaft</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A central cylindrical component extending horizontally through the rotor designs, indicating a rotating axis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06d562d92283cc555ded41962ea3fb9e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288898</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Domain For Half C-Core">
      <data key="d0">Design Domain For Half C-Core</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The conceptual area defined for optimizing a C-core, featuring COILs, a Controller, a Pointer, a Design Space, and an ARMATURE, as depicted in part (b) of the figure. It includes elements relevant to Topology Optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-678d567f82303d2bd1928259fd243036</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288898</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Core">
      <data key="d0">Core</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Core is a C-shaped, gray, solid component of the C-Core EM actuator. It has a horizontal upper section and two vertical arms, with a top horizontal section dimensioned at 21 mm long.&lt;SEP&gt;The C-shaped magnetic material component of the C-core actuator.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d94fd82540e01e83a3a7b0febcf3d7ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678d567f82303d2bd1928259fd243036</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288900</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Average Magnetic Field Magnitude">
      <data key="d0">Average Magnetic Field Magnitude</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Indicated by `avg |B|`, which refers to the average magnitude of the magnetic field over a specific area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-678d567f82303d2bd1928259fd243036</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288900</data>
    </node>
    <node id="q(W)">
      <data key="d0">q(W)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An approximate probability distribution for the weights $W$, typically chosen from a tractable family of distributions and optimized to minimize KL divergence to the true posterior.&lt;SEP&gt;q(W) represents a probability distribution over the variable W.&lt;SEP&gt;This represents the probability distribution of 'W', which could be a set of parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-916831dfb3bd404b7a6bf4cf512b41b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-215b04d60447db6b473faf768914326f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="p(W|X,Y)">
      <data key="d0">p(W|X,Y)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">p(W|X,Y) is the true posterior probability distribution of the weights W given observed input data X and target data Y, representing updated belief about weights after observing data.&lt;SEP&gt;p(W|X,Y) is a mathematical expression representing a conditional probability.&lt;SEP&gt;p(W|X,Y) represents a conditional probability distribution of W given X and Y.&lt;SEP&gt;p(W|X,Y) is the posterior probability of W given X and Y.&lt;SEP&gt;This represents a probability distribution of `W` given `X` and `Y`.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26c769a26f512a088ad02de1c81c6ddf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-916831dfb3bd404b7a6bf4cf512b41b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed1035840471dadb1efd9b1057b1d2f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a5cf1e19ea0a8639d0d309beab7d404</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bayesian Neural Networks (BNNs)">
      <data key="d0">Bayesian Neural Networks (BNNs)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Bayesian Neural Networks (BNNs) form the core framework where the posterior distribution p(W|X,Y) is sought, often confronting an intractable posterior.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of deep learning model that incorporates Bayesian inference to quantify prediction uncertainty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288902</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Uncertainty Quantification">
      <data key="d0">Uncertainty Quantification</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Uncertainty Quantification is the process of generating uncertainty maps to indicate where a model is confident or unsure, crucial for reliability assessment and selective prediction.&lt;SEP&gt;Uncertainty Quantification is a practical application of minimizing KL divergence, allowing for the estimation of uncertainty in model predictions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288904</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Model Robustness">
      <data key="d0">Model Robustness</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Model Robustness refers to improved model performance that accounts for parameter uncertainty, leading to less overfitting through Bayesian methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288904</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Decision Making">
      <data key="d0">Decision Making</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Decision Making involves using uncertainty maps to determine whether to trust a model's output or resort to traditional solvers when uncertainty is high.&lt;SEP&gt;Decision Making is a primary section of the Q-Learning Cycle responsible for selecting actions based on learned Q-values and feeding them back to the Environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11cc1c9381d2b807e25d94ba609ca1ea</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288905</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Approximate Posterior Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Approximate Posterior Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The approximate posterior distribution is a tractable representation of the true posterior, often achieved through minimization of KL divergence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288905</data>
    </node>
    <node id="True Posterior Distribution">
      <data key="d0">True Posterior Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The true posterior distribution is the actual probability distribution of model parameters given data, which is often intractable to compute directly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288905</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probability Distributions">
      <data key="d0">Probability Distributions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Probability Distributions are mathematical functions that describe the likelihood of different outcomes for a random variable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288905</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bayesian Neural Networks">
      <data key="d0">Bayesian Neural Networks</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Bayesian Neural Networks are a type of neural network that quantifies uncertainty in the model's weights after training by characterizing the entire distribution of possible weight values instead of single point estimates.&lt;SEP&gt;A framework where the posterior distribution of weights is sought, often using approximation methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288906</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Model Generalization">
      <data key="d0">Model Generalization</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The ability of a model to perform well on unseen data, potentially improved by Bayesian methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288907</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FEA/FDM">
      <data key="d0">FEA/FDM</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Traditional solvers like Finite Element Analysis (FEA) or Finite Difference Method (FDM) that can be resorted to when model uncertainty is high.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288907</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Approximate Bayesian Inference">
      <data key="d0">Approximate Bayesian Inference</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A method for approximating the posterior distribution of weights in Bayesian models, particularly in deep learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288907</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Convolutional Weights">
      <data key="d0">Convolutional Weights</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific type of weights in a neural network, represented by $W$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c2729bc0700da0250623cae161a1895</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288907</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flux Linkage Calculation (FEA Simulations)">
      <data key="d0">Flux Linkage Calculation (FEA Simulations)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The first stage (S1) of the efficiency map calculation, where electromagnetic FE simulations are run to obtain dq flux linkages.&lt;SEP&gt;Flux Linkage Calculation is the first stage (S1) of the process, which uses Finite Element Analysis (FEA) Simulations to determine λd and λq.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288907</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Excitation Calculation (Analytical Equations)">
      <data key="d0">Excitation Calculation (Analytical Equations)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The second stage (S2) of the efficiency map calculation, where optimal excitation conditions are found using different control strategies based on dq flux linkages.&lt;SEP&gt;Excitation Calculation is the second stage (S2) of the process, using analytical equations and control strategies to produce N*, Is*, and γ*.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288909</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Torque and Efficiency Calculation (FEA Simulations)">
      <data key="d0">Torque and Efficiency Calculation (FEA Simulations)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The third stage (S3) of the efficiency map calculation, where FE simulations are used to compute electromagnetic torque and efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;Torque and Efficiency Calculation is the third stage (S3) of the process, using Finite Element Analysis (FEA) Simulations to determine torque and efficiency from previous outputs. This stage is also relevant for Transfer Learning (TL).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288909</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Efficiency Map Generation">
      <data key="d0">Efficiency Map Generation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The fourth stage (S4) of the efficiency map calculation, where calculated points are post-processed and interpolated to generate the final efficiency map.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288909</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Simulation Parameters">
      <data key="d0">Simulation Parameters</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Simulation Parameters are inputs to the Flux Linkage Calculation stage.&lt;SEP&gt;Simulation Parameters are input settings for the Flux Linkage Calculation (FEA Simulations) in the flowchart.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="λd">
      <data key="d0">λd</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">λd (lambda d) is an output of the Flux Linkage Calculation stage and an input to the Excitation Calculation stage.&lt;SEP&gt;λd represents the direct axis flux linkage, an output from the Flux Linkage Calculation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="λq">
      <data key="d0">λq</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">λq (lambda q) is an output of the Flux Linkage Calculation stage and an input to the Excitation Calculation stage.&lt;SEP&gt;λq represents the quadrature axis flux linkage, an output from the Flux Linkage Calculation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="I*s">
      <data key="d0">I*s</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">I*s is an output of the Excitation Calculation stage representing optimal conditions, and an input to the Torque and Efficiency Calculation stage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288912</data>
    </node>
    <node id="γ*">
      <data key="d0">γ*</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">γ* (gamma star) is an output of the Excitation Calculation stage representing optimal conditions, and an input to the Torque and Efficiency Calculation stage.&lt;SEP&gt;γ* is an output from the Excitation Calculation (Analytical Equations), representing a specific motor angle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288912</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flowchart of Process for Calculating Efficiency Maps">
      <data key="d0">Flowchart of Process for Calculating Efficiency Maps</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Flowchart of the process for calculating efficiency maps illustrates a multi-stage procedure for determining system efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288912</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Excitation Calculation">
      <data key="d0">Excitation Calculation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Excitation Calculation is the second stage of the efficiency map process, using analytical equations to find optimal excitation conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288912</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FEA Simulations">
      <data key="d0">FEA Simulations</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">FEA Simulations are used in the Flux Linkage Calculation and Torque and Efficiency Calculation stages, which are computationally expensive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288913</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Analytical Equations">
      <data key="d0">Analytical Equations</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Analytical Equations are used in the Excitation Calculation stage to find optimal excitation conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288914</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1 is the Flux Linkage Calculation step where electromagnetic FE simulations are run to obtain dq flux linkages.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 1 refers to the 'Flux Linkage Calculation (FEA Simulations)' block in the flowchart.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288914</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 2 is the Excitation Calculation step where optimal excitation conditions are found using different control strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 2 refers to the 'Excitation Calculation (Analytical Equations)' block in the flowchart.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288915</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 4">
      <data key="d0">Stage 4</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 4 is the Efficiency Map generation step where calculated points are post-processed and interpolated to generate an efficiency map.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 4 refers to the 'Efficiency Map' block in the flowchart, representing the final output.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564505ebb053f0e31b0cfdaa812556ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288915</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State Input">
      <data key="d0">State Input</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The state input, labeled 's', is represented by a vertical stack of five empty rectangular boxes feeding into the Q-Network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9a7d0fc487b66b91a1d8bae6610798e3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288916</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Network Setup Approaches">
      <data key="d0">Q-Network Setup Approaches</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The image illustrates two different approaches to setting up a Q-Network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9a7d0fc487b66b91a1d8bae6610798e3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288916</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weight Update Rule">
      <data key="d0">Weight Update Rule</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The given equation represents a fundamental stochastic gradient descent-based weight update rule for a neural network within the context of Reinforcement Learning (RL).&lt;SEP&gt;The equation describes a weight update rule for a neural network in an off-policy Q-learning framework, specifically for a Deep Q-Network (DQN) architecture using separate behavior and target networks.&lt;SEP&gt;The weight update rule describes how the weights of a neural network are adjusted within a reinforcement learning algorithm. It is a variant of Temporal Difference (TD) learning, similar to SARSA.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288917</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stochastic Gradient Descent">
      <data key="d0">Stochastic Gradient Descent</data>
      <data key="d1">algorithm</data>
      <data key="d2">Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD) is an optimization method used to minimize the error between predictions and targets, akin to the process described by the weight update rule.&lt;SEP&gt;Stochastic Gradient Descent is an iterative optimization algorithm used to minimize an objective function by taking steps proportional to the negative of the gradient of the function at the current point, computed on randomly chosen subsets of the data.&lt;SEP&gt;An optimization algorithm used to adjust weights iteratively in the direction that minimizes a specific loss function.&lt;SEP&gt;The equation represents an update rule potentially for a stochastic gradient descent algorithm with a eligibility trace or similar reinforcement learning update.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39c26f40d41eeedc15f07ee76f39c83c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weight Vector 'w'">
      <data key="d0">Weight Vector 'w'</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The Weight Vector 'w' represents the parameters of the neural network function approximator, defining the mapping from state-action pairs to estimated Q-values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288917</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Learning Rate '$\\alpha$'">
      <data key="d0">Learning Rate '$\\alpha$'</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The Learning Rate '$\\alpha$' is a positive scalar hyperparameter that controls the step size of each weight update, influencing learning speed and stability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288917</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reward $R_{t+1}$">
      <data key="d0">Reward $R_{t+1}$</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The immediate reward received after taking action $a_t$ in state $s_t$ and transitioning to state $s_{t+1}$.&lt;SEP&gt;Reward $R_{t+1}$ is the scalar reward received at time step $t+1$ after taking action $a_t$ in state $s_t$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State $s_t$">
      <data key="d0">State $s_t$</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">State $s_t$ is the state of the environment at time step $t$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action $a_t$">
      <data key="d0">Action $a_t$</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Action $a_t$ is the action taken by the agent in state $s_t$ at time step $t$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State $s_{t+1}$">
      <data key="d0">State $s_{t+1}$</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">State $s_{t+1}$ is the state of the environment at time step $t+1$, resulting from taking action $a_t$ in state $s_t$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action $a_{t+1}$">
      <data key="d0">Action $a_{t+1}$</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Action $a_{t+1}$ is the action chosen by the current policy in state $s_{t+1}$ at time step $t+1$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TD Error">
      <data key="d0">TD Error</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The TD error, $\Big[ R_{t+1} + \operatorname*{max}_{a'} Q_\pi(s_{t+1}, a') - Q_\pi(s_t, a_t) \Big]$, represents the discrepancy between the current Q-value estimate and the bootstrapped target.&lt;SEP&gt;The difference between the TD Target and the current Q(s_t, a_t) estimate, quantifying the deviation from the experience-based target.&lt;SEP&gt;The TD Error is the discrepancy between the immediate reward plus the estimated value of the next state-action pair, and the current estimated value of the present state-action pair.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Target Value">
      <data key="d0">Target Value</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Target Value, $R_{t+1} + \\hat{Q}(s_{t+1}, a_{t+1}, w)$, serves as the desired outcome for the update of the action-value function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gradient '$\\nabla \\hat{Q}(s_t, a_t, w)$'">
      <data key="d0">Gradient '$\\nabla \\hat{Q}(s_t, a_t, w)$'</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The Gradient '$\\nabla \\hat{Q}(s_t, a_t, w)$' indicates the direction of steepest increase for $\\hat{Q}(s_t, a_t, w)$ in the weight space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288920</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gradient Descent">
      <data key="d0">Gradient Descent</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An optimization algorithm that uses the gradient to minimize the value estimation error by updating weights in the opposite direction of the error function's steepest ascent.&lt;SEP&gt;Gradient Descent is an optimization algorithm used to minimize a function by iteratively moving in the direction of steepest descent, confirmed by the explicit gradient term in the equation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gradient Descent is an optimization algorithm used to minimize a function by iteratively moving in the direction of steepest descent as defined by the negative of the gradient.&lt;SEP&gt;Gradient descent is an optimization algorithm used to minimize the loss function by iteratively adjusting model parameters.&lt;SEP&gt;Gradient descent is an optimization algorithm used to minimize a function by iteratively moving in the direction of steepest descent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288921</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reinforcement Learning Algorithm">
      <data key="d0">Reinforcement Learning Algorithm</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Reinforcement Learning Algorithm is the broader framework within which the weight update rule operates, aiming to optimize an agent's policy.&lt;SEP&gt;A reinforcement learning algorithm is a method used to train an agent to make decisions by maximizing a reward signal, as evidenced by the convergence results.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4b98982b6cac8028635bf8c32451b12e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288921</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alpha">
      <data key="d0">Alpha</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The variable $\alpha$ is the learning rate, a scalar hyperparameter that controls the step size of the weight update.&lt;SEP&gt;Alpha ($\alpha$) is the learning rate, a positive scalar hyperparameter that controls the step size of each weight update.&lt;SEP&gt;Alpha ($\alpha$) is a variable, often representing a learning rate or step size in optimization algorithms.&lt;SEP&gt;Alpha (α) represents the learning rate in the reinforcement learning equation.&lt;SEP&gt;Alpha (represented as α) is the learning rate (lr) in the Q-learning algorithm, determining the extent to which newly acquired information overrides old information.&lt;SEP&gt;Alpha (α) is a learning rate or step size in the equation.&lt;SEP&gt;Alpha ($\alpha$) is a scalar coefficient in the equation, often representing a learning rate or step size.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec4e495c59f004fcbe4475de4b2bcbd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19afc62597694db2255ea53cb7e6f242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e5324f5a16fd6822c647598c8e749b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d0e0689a5d95559de772960193f788e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2ac9217313164d00979e7afd6541254</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293067</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Temporal Difference Error">
      <data key="d0">Temporal Difference Error</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The term $[ R _ { t + 1 } + \hat { Q } _ { t a r } ( s _ { t } , a _ { t } , w _ { t a r } ) - \hat { Q } _ { b e h } ( s _ { t } , a _ { t } , w _ { b e h } ) ]$ represents the discrepancy between the agent's current estimate of the action-value function and a more 'stable' or 'accurate' target value.&lt;SEP&gt;The Temporal Difference (TD) error is the discrepancy between the immediate reward plus the estimated value of the next state-action pair and the current estimated value of the present state-action pair.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288922</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Off-Policy Methods">
      <data key="d0">Off-Policy Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Off-Policy Methods, such as dual-network updates, are contrasted with the on-policy nature of the presented equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08f59209c2113faa8129ee1fd7aa6b8e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 2.3">
      <data key="d0">Figure 2.3</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 2.3 is a visual representation titled "Parametrized Geometry - Coil" that illustrates the geometric setup of a coil within a fixed air box.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6dd34d919cd23617da046186d8e9bc3a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fixed Air Box">
      <data key="d0">Fixed Air Box</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Fixed Air Box is a square outline in the diagram representing an empty space with fixed dimensions, housing the coil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6dd34d919cd23617da046186d8e9bc3a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X Coordinate">
      <data key="d0">X Coordinate</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The X Coordinate is a variable parameter indicating the horizontal position of the coil's center relative to an origin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6dd34d919cd23617da046186d8e9bc3a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Y Coordinate">
      <data key="d0">Y Coordinate</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Y Coordinate is a variable parameter indicating the vertical position of the coil's center relative to an origin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6dd34d919cd23617da046186d8e9bc3a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radius">
      <data key="d0">Radius</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Radius, labeled 'R', is a variable parameter defining the size of the Coil, represented by a horizontal black arrow passing through its center.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6dd34d919cd23617da046186d8e9bc3a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288926</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2D Diagram">
      <data key="d0">2D Diagram</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The image is a 2D diagram used to illustrate the parameterized geometry of a coil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6dd34d919cd23617da046186d8e9bc3a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288927</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Page 37">
      <data key="d0">Page 37</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Page 37 contains textual description that the diagram visually supports, explaining the parameters of the Coil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6dd34d919cd23617da046186d8e9bc3a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288927</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X-coordinate">
      <data key="d0">X-coordinate</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The X-coordinate is a variable representing the horizontal position of the Coil's center, labeled 'X' in the diagram.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6dd34d919cd23617da046186d8e9bc3a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288927</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Y-coordinate">
      <data key="d0">Y-coordinate</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Y-coordinate is a variable representing the vertical position of the Coil's center, labeled 'y' in the diagram.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6dd34d919cd23617da046186d8e9bc3a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288928</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mahmoudi et al. (2015)">
      <data key="d0">Mahmoudi et al. (2015)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Mahmoudi et al. (2015) is the source attributed for the efficiency map data of the 2010 Toyota Prius IPM motor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-172d2c57a19dd33adf49cb4619a788cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288928</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Speed (RPM)">
      <data key="d0">Speed (RPM)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Speed (RPM) is an operational parameter of the motor, measured in revolutions per minute, ranging from 0 to 12000 RPM on the x-axis of the efficiency map.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-172d2c57a19dd33adf49cb4619a788cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Torque (N m)">
      <data key="d0">Torque (N m)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Torque (N m) is an operational parameter of the motor, measured in Newton-meters, ranging from 0 to 200 N·m on the y-axis of the efficiency map.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-172d2c57a19dd33adf49cb4619a788cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peak Torque/Power Curve">
      <data key="d0">Peak Torque/Power Curve</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Peak Torque/Power curve is a thick yellow line that outlines the maximum operational limits of the motor, delineating regions of non-operational or restricted areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-172d2c57a19dd33adf49cb4619a788cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Efficiency Percentages">
      <data key="d0">Efficiency Percentages</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Efficiency Percentages are values ranging from 62% to 95% that are visually correlated with colors on the efficiency map, indicating the motor's performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-172d2c57a19dd33adf49cb4619a788cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electric Motor Engineering">
      <data key="d0">Electric Motor Engineering</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Electric Motor Engineering is the field that uses technical diagrams like the efficiency map to understand and evaluate motor performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-172d2c57a19dd33adf49cb4619a788cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Motor Efficiency">
      <data key="d0">Motor Efficiency</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Motor efficiency is a measure of how effectively an electric motor converts electrical energy into mechanical energy, plotted as a function of speed and torque.&lt;SEP&gt;Motor efficiency is the specific topic being predicted and evaluated using the classification model and accuracy equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74dead31275ed48a2a31d5ae16d58cb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-172d2c57a19dd33adf49cb4619a788cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mahmoudi et al.">
      <data key="d0">Mahmoudi et al.</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mahmoudi et al. refers to the authors who published the data or analysis regarding the 2010 Toyota Prius IPM motor efficiency map in 2015.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-172d2c57a19dd33adf49cb4619a788cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Machine Learning Approaches">
      <data key="d0">Machine Learning Approaches</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Machine Learning Approaches are methods being researched to accelerate the generation of motor efficiency maps, which are otherwise computationally expensive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-172d2c57a19dd33adf49cb4619a788cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Energy Usage and Range Prediction">
      <data key="d0">Energy Usage and Range Prediction</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Energy usage and range prediction are critical applications of motor efficiency maps in vehicle dynamic simulations, particularly for Hybrid Electric Vehicles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-172d2c57a19dd33adf49cb4619a788cc</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Value-Based Methods">
      <data key="d0">Value-Based Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Value-based methods in Reinforcement Learning use value functions to formalize the long-term goal of an agent, contrasting with short-term reward functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Value-based methods are a type of Reinforcement Learning algorithm that learn to approximate optimal policies, utilizing weight update rules like the one provided.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="w">
      <data key="d0">w</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The variable `w` consistently refers to a weight vector or parameters within the context of machine learning, particularly in neural networks and approximation functions. These weights are crucial components that are adjusted during the learning process by algorithms. Specifically, `w` often represents the weights or parameters of a neural network function approximator, which are modified as the network learns. In reinforcement learning, `w` can denote the parameters used to approximate the action-value function Q(s, a, w), often seen in the form `&lt;w, x(s_i, a_j)&gt;` for approximating Q-values. Across its uses, `w` is understood to be the weight vector undergoing updates within an equation or learning algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb650db56653b515d55eb1ef7514257&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9242b5eeb7a8558a1b50bf53c2ccacba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ab45d957d12b26323f8399b12bc7b9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85055739dbffbeba952630d321118334&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39c26f40d41eeedc15f07ee76f39c83c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd1039c6984ae2b3caedcb109bdc272a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292989</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Hat (s, a, w)">
      <data key="d0">Q-Hat (s, a, w)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">$\hat{Q}(s_t, a_t, w)$ is the estimated action-value function, approximated by a neural network with weights $w$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nabla Q-Hat (s, a, w)">
      <data key="d0">Nabla Q-Hat (s, a, w)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">$\nabla \hat{Q}(s_t, a_t, w)$ represents the gradient of the estimated action-value function with respect to the weights $w$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Assignment Operator (←)">
      <data key="d0">Assignment Operator (←)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The assignment operator indicates that the value on the right-hand side is assigned to the variable on the left-hand side.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288934</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Addition Operator (+)">
      <data key="d0">Addition Operator (+)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The addition operator is used to update the weights by adding a scaled gradient term to the current weight.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288934</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Subtraction Operator (-)">
      <data key="d0">Subtraction Operator (-)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The subtraction operator is used to calculate the prediction error between the target return and the current Q-value estimate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288934</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Implicit Multiplication Operator (*)">
      <data key="d0">Implicit Multiplication Operator (*)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Implicit multiplication is used between the learning rate, the error term, and the gradient in the equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288934</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gradient Operator (∇)">
      <data key="d0">Gradient Operator (∇)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The gradient operator computes the vector of partial derivatives of $\hat{Q}$ with respect to each component of the weight vector $w$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288934</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Function Application (Q-Hat)">
      <data key="d0">Function Application (Q-Hat)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Function application refers to the neural network function that takes state, action, and weights as input to produce an estimated action value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288935</data>
    </node>
    <node id="latex">
      <data key="d0">latex</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Latex is the format in which the equation is presented.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288935</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action-Value Functions">
      <data key="d0">Action-Value Functions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Functions estimated in Reinforcement Learning to predict the expected return of taking a particular action in a given state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288936</data>
    </node>
    <node id="w (Weights)">
      <data key="d0">w (Weights)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A vector representing the trainable parameters of a neural network, adjusted by the learning algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288936</data>
    </node>
    <node id="alpha (Learning Rate)">
      <data key="d0">alpha (Learning Rate)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A scalar hyperparameter controlling the step size of the weight update, influencing learning aggressiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288936</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gt (Return)">
      <data key="d0">Gt (Return)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The total discounted future reward from a given state-action pair at time step t, serving as a target for the Q-function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288936</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-hat(st, at, w)">
      <data key="d0">Q-hat(st, at, w)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The estimated action-value function, approximated by a neural network with weights w, predicting expected return.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288936</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nabla Q-hat(st, at, w)">
      <data key="d0">Nabla Q-hat(st, at, w)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The gradient of the estimated action-value function with respect to the weights w, indicating the direction of steepest ascent for Q-hat.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288936</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Assignment Operator">
      <data key="d0">Assignment Operator</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The mathematical symbol $\longleftarrow$, indicating that the value on the right-hand side is assigned to the variable on the left-hand side.&lt;SEP&gt;The assignment operator ($\longleftarrow$) indicates that parameters are updated to a new value based on a calculation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288937</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Addition Operator">
      <data key="d0">Addition Operator</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The mathematical symbol $+$, used to update weights by adding a scaled gradient term.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288937</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Subtraction Operator">
      <data key="d0">Subtraction Operator</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The mathematical symbol $-$, used to calculate the 'temporal difference error' or 'prediction error'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288938</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multiplication Operator">
      <data key="d0">Multiplication Operator</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An implicit operation used between the learning rate, error term, and gradient.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288938</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Function Application">
      <data key="d0">Function Application</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The process by which the neural network function $\hat{Q}(\cdot)$ takes inputs (state, action, weights) to produce an estimated action value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79b78e3eb76f4c6790bb12bc1c7630ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electromagnetic Models">
      <data key="d0">Electromagnetic Models</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Electromagnetic Models is the central rectangular box at the root of the diagram, representing the umbrella concept.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b33ad32faa20d67bef064cd7c0c2009c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Numerical Models">
      <data key="d0">Numerical Models</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Numerical Models are a category of electromagnetic modeling techniques, depicted as one of three main branches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b33ad32faa20d67bef064cd7c0c2009c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288940</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maxwell Equation">
      <data key="d0">Maxwell Equation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Maxwell Equation is a specific example listed under Analytical Models, representing a method for electromagnetic modeling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b33ad32faa20d67bef064cd7c0c2009c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288941</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Finite Difference">
      <data key="d0">Finite Difference</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Finite Difference is a specific example listed under Numerical Models, representing a method for electromagnetic modeling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b33ad32faa20d67bef064cd7c0c2009c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288941</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flowchart Diagram">
      <data key="d0">Flowchart Diagram</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A Flowchart Diagram visually represents the hierarchical structure of electromagnetic modeling techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b33ad32faa20d67bef064cd7c0c2009c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288941</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Number Of Stator Slots">
      <data key="d0">Number Of Stator Slots</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Number of Stator Slots is a fixed parameter for the SynRM, specified as 24.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288942</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Number Of Poles">
      <data key="d0">Number Of Poles</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Number of Poles is a fixed parameter for the SynRM, specified as 4.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288942</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stator Outer Diameter">
      <data key="d0">Stator Outer Diameter</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Stator Outer Diameter is a fixed parameter for the SynRM, specified as 112 mm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Airgap Thickness">
      <data key="d0">Airgap Thickness</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Airgap Thickness is a fixed parameter for the SynRM, specified as 0.5 mm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rotor Outer Diameter">
      <data key="d0">Rotor Outer Diameter</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Rotor Outer Diameter is a fixed parameter for the SynRM, specified as 55 mm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stack Height">
      <data key="d0">Stack Height</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Stack Height is a fixed parameter for the SynRM, specified as 50 mm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rotor Inner Diameter">
      <data key="d0">Rotor Inner Diameter</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Rotor Inner Diameter is a fixed parameter for the SynRM, specified as 16 mm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288944</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RMS Current Density">
      <data key="d0">RMS Current Density</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The RMS Current Density is a fixed electrical operating parameter for the SynRM, specified as 18A.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288944</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Core Material">
      <data key="d0">Core Material</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The Core Material, represented by light gray rectangular cells, forms the primary structure of the C-core electromagnet.&lt;SEP&gt;The Core Material for the SynRM is M-19 26 Ga, a standard material for electrical machine laminations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-abb64932da4baf92322bc61ecf0df44f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288945</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Barrier Material">
      <data key="d0">Barrier Material</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">The Barrier Material for the SynRM is Air, which is significant for topology optimization where air (flux barriers) is introduced into the design space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288945</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Baseline SynRM Performance">
      <data key="d0">Baseline SynRM Performance</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Baseline SynRM Performance is defined by the fixed parameters in Table 4.5, exhibiting an average torque of 2.5 N-m, which serves as a benchmark for optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288945</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flux Barriers">
      <data key="d0">Flux Barriers</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Flux Barriers are modifications to the rotor's topology, specifically involving the distribution of air within an iron-filled design, which are optimized to improve torque.&lt;SEP&gt;Flux Barriers are part of the rotor design created by air cells, critical for the SynRM's optimized geometry.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b7b73719952876f4f9c0e6b4efe0027&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288945</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GA Approach">
      <data key="d0">GA Approach</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The GA Approach (Genetic Algorithm) is a method of topology optimization that achieves an average torque of 3.40 N-m by optimizing flux barriers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288946</data>
    </node>
    <node id="M-19 26 Ga">
      <data key="d0">M-19 26 Ga</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">M-19 26 Ga is the specified Core Material, a standard material for electrical machine laminations.&lt;SEP&gt;M-19 26 Ga refers to the blue-gray solid material used in the SynRM rotor, forming the core magnetic path.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b56c95146eb3d0e8591ffefd6ab679ea</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288946</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2.5 N-m">
      <data key="d0">2.5 N-m</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">2.5 N-m is the average torque of the baseline SynRM performance.&lt;SEP&gt;This value represents the average torque of a conventional SynRM design, used for comparison with the optimized design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7b6f3bea48fce928809f6ddf7db422c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288947</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3.40 N-m">
      <data key="d0">3.40 N-m</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">3.40 N-m is the average torque achieved by the GA approach after optimizing the flux barriers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288947</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topology Optimization Experiments">
      <data key="d0">Topology Optimization Experiments</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Topology optimization experiments are conducted to improve the performance of a Synchronous Reluctance Motor by modifying its rotor's flux barrier topology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288948</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Number of Stator Slots">
      <data key="d0">Number of Stator Slots</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The Number of Stator Slots is a fixed parameter for the SynRM, specified as 24.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288948</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Number of Poles">
      <data key="d0">Number of Poles</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The Number of Poles is a fixed parameter for the SynRM, specified as 4. This aligns with a 4-pole, 3-phase design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288948</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Average Torque of 2.5 N-m">
      <data key="d0">Average Torque of 2.5 N-m</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The Average Torque of 2.5 N-m represents the baseline performance of the SynRM before optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288948</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rotor's Flux Barrier Topology">
      <data key="d0">Rotor's Flux Barrier Topology</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Rotor's Flux Barrier Topology refers to the distribution of barrier material (air) within the core material of the rotor, modified during topology optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288949</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Average Torque of 3.40 N-m">
      <data key="d0">Average Torque of 3.40 N-m</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The Average Torque of 3.40 N-m is the improved torque achieved by the GA approach after optimizing the flux barriers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f519dcf3231dec106e81c35523ed6914</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288949</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Q-Networks">
      <data key="d0">Deep Q-Networks</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep Q-Networks (DQN) utilize experience replay buffers to store and sample data for training the Q-network.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep Q-Networks (DQNs) are a practical application of the gradient component, used to train value-based RL agents like those playing Atari games or controlling robots.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep Q-Networks (DQNs) are a type of Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) architecture that combine Q-learning with deep neural networks for approximating the action-value function, successfully applied to complex control tasks like playing Atari games.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep Q-Networks (DQN) is an algorithm that uses neural networks to approximate the Q-value function in Q-Learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-808c974f7871ba6b7b5d18f72afc85a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288951</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-value Approximation">
      <data key="d0">Q-value Approximation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Q-value approximation involves using methods like neural networks to accurately predict the action-value function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288951</data>
    </node>
    <node id="S_i">
      <data key="d0">S_i</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">S_i represents the state at time step `i` in a Reinforcement Learning context, providing a complete description of the environment at a particular moment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="A_i">
      <data key="d0">A_i</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A_i represents the action taken by the agent at time step `i`, which is a choice that influences the environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q_π(S_i, A_i)">
      <data key="d0">Q_π(S_i, A_i)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Q_π(S_i, A_i) is the action-value function (Q-value) for state `S_i` and action `A_i` under policy `π`, quantifying the expected return from that state-action pair.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="π">
      <data key="d0">π</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">π represents a policy in Reinforcement Learning, which maps states to probabilities of selecting actions.&lt;SEP&gt;π (pi) represents the policy in Reinforcement Learning, which is a function that maps states to actions, often probabilistically.&lt;SEP&gt;π represents the policy, which is parameterized by θ and dictates the agent's behavior by defining the probability of selecting actions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb28e5c877704a09cf7eb4cbeb6522fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-808c974f7871ba6b7b5d18f72afc85a7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q*(s, a)">
      <data key="d0">Q*(s, a)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Q*(s, a) is the true optimal action-value function, which the discussion revolves around estimating.&lt;SEP&gt;Q*(s, a) represents the optimal action-value function in a reinforcement learning context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85055739dbffbeba952630d321118334</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292948</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bellman Equation">
      <data key="d0">Bellman Equation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Bellman Equation is a necessary condition for optimality associated with dynamic programming and is used in the context of action-value function definitions.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bellman equation in RL refers to the implied expectation operation over future rewards from which Q-values are derived.&lt;SEP&gt;The second equation, Q_π(s_t, a_t) ← Q_π(s_t, a_t) + [R_{t+1} + max_{a'} Q_π(s_{t+1}, a') - Q_π(s_t, a_t)], represents a form of the Bellman equation for Q-learning, involving immediate reward and the maximum Q-value of the next state.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bellman Equation is a fundamental concept in dynamic programming that decomposes the value function into an immediate reward and the discounted value of the next state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18301e98e2477e8fce449561e9c20039&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8823695e17798553515e243bdbfda2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292921</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dataset of Experiences">
      <data key="d0">Dataset of Experiences</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Dataset of Experiences is formed by the sequence of state-action pairs and Q-value estimates, used for training a value function approximator.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288954</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Temporal Difference (TD) Learning">
      <data key="d0">Temporal Difference (TD) Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Temporal Difference (TD) Learning is a sequence-based decision-making algorithm that falls under Reinforcement Learning.&lt;SEP&gt;A method in reinforcement learning for estimating value functions, which relies on bootstrapping.&lt;SEP&gt;Temporal Difference (TD) Learning involves using bootstrap targets as estimates of $Q^*(s,a)$, directly building upon the concept of the error defined by MSE.&lt;SEP&gt;Temporal Difference (TD) learning is an algorithm that uses sequences of experiences to update Q-function estimates.&lt;SEP&gt;A learning method characterized by using estimations to update other estimations, exemplified by the structure of the equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ad46225ae0d5f3835a9bda1ed8158d48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288954</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Q-Networks (DQN)">
      <data key="d0">Deep Q-Networks (DQN)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep Q-Networks (DQN) utilize experience replay buffers to store and sample state-action-reward-next state tuples for training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9a600bc388389f7228161e154b865877</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288954</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Encode-Decoder based Convolutional Neural Network Architecture">
      <data key="d0">Encode-Decoder based Convolutional Neural Network Architecture</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Encode-Decoder based Convolutional Neural Network Architecture is a U-Net architecture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f41a0abc7626a2c56bb77667264c7785</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U-Net Architecture">
      <data key="d0">U-Net Architecture</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The U-Net Architecture is a specific type of Convolutional Neural Network known for its U-shaped appearance, suitable for tasks like image segmentation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f41a0abc7626a2c56bb77667264c7785</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Down Pooling">
      <data key="d0">Down Pooling</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A downsampling operation represented by dark purple trapezoidal blocks in the Encoder section, reducing dimensions sequentially.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f41a0abc7626a2c56bb77667264c7785</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288957</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONV Blocks">
      <data key="d0">CONV Blocks</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Light purple rectangular blocks representing convolutional layers in the Encoder section, typically followed by pooling layers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f41a0abc7626a2c56bb77667264c7785</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288957</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DE-CONV Layers">
      <data key="d0">DE-CONV Layers</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Light green rectangular blocks representing deconvolutional layers in the Decoder section, mirroring the CONV blocks of the Encoder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f41a0abc7626a2c56bb77667264c7785</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288957</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Up Pooling">
      <data key="d0">Up Pooling</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An upsampling operation represented by dark green trapezoidal blocks in the Decoder section, increasing dimensions sequentially.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f41a0abc7626a2c56bb77667264c7785</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288957</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Latent Info">
      <data key="d0">Latent Info</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A dashed grey arrow connecting the final block of the Encoder to the first block of the Decoder, representing the bottleneck or latent representation of the input.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f41a0abc7626a2c56bb77667264c7785</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288958</data>
    </node>
    <node id="400 x 400 Blocks">
      <data key="d0">400 x 400 Blocks</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">These blocks represent input dimensions for the initial Encoder layers and output dimensions for the final Decoder layers, indicating image size.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f41a0abc7626a2c56bb77667264c7785</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288958</data>
    </node>
    <node id="200 x 200 Blocks">
      <data key="d0">200 x 200 Blocks</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">These blocks represent intermediate dimensions within both the Encoder and Decoder sections after downsampling or before upsampling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f41a0abc7626a2c56bb77667264c7785</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288958</data>
    </node>
    <node id="100 x 100 Blocks">
      <data key="d0">100 x 100 Blocks</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">These blocks represent intermediate dimensions within both the Encoder and Decoder sections after further downsampling or before further upsampling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f41a0abc7626a2c56bb77667264c7785</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288959</data>
    </node>
    <node id="50 x 50 Blocks">
      <data key="d0">50 x 50 Blocks</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">These blocks represent the smallest dimensions, found at the bottleneck between the Encoder and Decoder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f41a0abc7626a2c56bb77667264c7785</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288959</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medium- &amp; Heavy-Duty Vehicles">
      <data key="d0">Medium- &amp; Heavy-Duty Vehicles</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Medium- &amp; Heavy-Duty Vehicles are a source of Greenhouse Gas emissions, contributing 23.0% of the total within the US transportation sector.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e01cf8f858f58505e96e0122d134e7dd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288962</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aircraft">
      <data key="d0">Aircraft</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Aircraft are a source of Greenhouse Gas emissions, contributing 9.0% of the total within the US transportation sector.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e01cf8f858f58505e96e0122d134e7dd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288962</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Other">
      <data key="d0">Other</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The 'Other' category represents various unspecified sources of greenhouse gas emissions, accounting for 5.0% within the US transportation sector.</data>
