    <node id="US Transportation Sector">
      <data key="d0">US Transportation Sector</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The US Transportation Sector is a segment of the United States economy comprising all industries that provide transportation services.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e01cf8f858f58505e96e0122d134e7dd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GHG Emissions by Source">
      <data key="d0">GHG Emissions by Source</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">GHG Emissions by Source is data representing various origins of Greenhouse Gas emissions within the US transportation sector.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e01cf8f858f58505e96e0122d134e7dd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Other Sources">
      <data key="d0">Other Sources</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Other Sources collectively represent 5.0% of Greenhouse Gas emissions within the US transportation sector.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e01cf8f858f58505e96e0122d134e7dd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State-Action Pair (s, a)">
      <data key="d0">State-Action Pair (s, a)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A combination of the environment's state 's' and an action 'a' taken in that state, used as input to a neural network, part of the action-value function Q(s, a).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-eb622c9ce0600f3539c3ed7199e0c0ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288965</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q(s, a) Function">
      <data key="d0">Q(s, a) Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The action-value function, which estimates the expected return for taking action 'a' in state 's' and then following a particular policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-eb622c9ce0600f3539c3ed7199e0c0ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288965</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V(s) Function">
      <data key="d0">V(s) Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The state-value function, which estimates the expected return when starting in state 's' and following a particular policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-eb622c9ce0600f3539c3ed7199e0c0ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288965</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Page 125">
      <data key="d0">Page 125</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific page referring to broader text that discusses neural networks implementing a nonlinear function of states.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-eb622c9ce0600f3539c3ed7199e0c0ee</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288965</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.28">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.28</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.28 is a visual representation titled "SynRM characteristics" that contains three quarter-section diagrams of a Synchronous Reluctance Machine (SynRM) rotor design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02e55225b1f173db3033115aeae970db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288966</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Synchronous Reluctance Machine (SynRM)">
      <data key="d0">Synchronous Reluctance Machine (SynRM)</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Synchronous Reluctance Machine (SynRM) is a type of electric motor whose characteristics and rotor design are illustrated in Figure 4.28.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02e55225b1f173db3033115aeae970db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288966</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conventional Single Barrier SynRM">
      <data key="d0">Conventional Single Barrier SynRM</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Conventional Single Barrier SynRM is a type of motor design depicted in diagram (a) of Figure 4.28, featuring a rotor with two distinct arc-shaped barriers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02e55225b1f173db3033115aeae970db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288967</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discretized Rotor Geometry">
      <data key="d0">Discretized Rotor Geometry</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Discretized Rotor Geometry represents a motor section shown in diagram (b) of Figure 4.28, where the rotor's design space is divided into a grid of small squares for topology optimization.&lt;SEP&gt;The SynRM rotor's design space divided into discrete cells for granular control over material distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02e55225b1f173db3033115aeae970db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288967</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Controller at Start Position">
      <data key="d0">Controller at Start Position</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Controller at Start Position is a stage in the SynRM design process, illustrated in diagram (c) of Figure 4.28, where initial 'air' elements are introduced into the discretized rotor grid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02e55225b1f173db3033115aeae970db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288967</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Copper Windings">
      <data key="d0">Copper Windings</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Copper windings are orange-colored components filling the slots of the stator in the SynRM illustrations, indicating their material.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02e55225b1f173db3033115aeae970db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288968</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flux Barrier">
      <data key="d0">Flux Barrier</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A flux barrier is a feature created by introducing 'air' elements into the rotor, visually represented by white squares in the discretized rotor grid, and forming a rudimentary barrier at the controller's start position.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02e55225b1f173db3033115aeae970db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288968</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2-D Design Space Quarter">
      <data key="d0">2-D Design Space Quarter</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The 2-D Design Space Quarter is a conceptual area within the rotor's design, discretized into 25 (5x5) cells for optimization purposes, with cells capable of switching between iron and air.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02e55225b1f173db3033115aeae970db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288968</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rotor Design">
      <data key="d0">Rotor Design</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Rotor Design refers to the structural arrangement of the rotating part of an electric motor, particularly studied for the SynRM.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02e55225b1f173db3033115aeae970db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288969</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Orange Windings">
      <data key="d0">Orange Windings</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Orange windings are components within the stator slots, indicating copper and representing the electrical coils in the motor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02e55225b1f173db3033115aeae970db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288969</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Light Gray Rotor">
      <data key="d0">Light Gray Rotor</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The light gray rotor is the inner rotating part of the motor in diagram (a), containing arc-shaped barriers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02e55225b1f173db3033115aeae970db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288969</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Arc-shaped Barriers">
      <data key="d0">Arc-shaped Barriers</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Arc-shaped barriers are white, non-magnetic elements within the light gray rotor in diagram (a), representing air or non-magnetic material.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02e55225b1f173db3033115aeae970db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288969</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medium Blue Rotor">
      <data key="d0">Medium Blue Rotor</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The medium blue rotor is the rotating part of the motor in diagram (b) and (c), completely filled in and overlaid with a grid to show discretization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02e55225b1f173db3033115aeae970db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288970</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Darker Blue Lines">
      <data key="d0">Darker Blue Lines</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Darker blue lines form a grid pattern over the medium blue rotor, indicating the discretization of the rotor's design space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02e55225b1f173db3033115aeae970db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288970</data>
    </node>
    <node id="White Squares">
      <data key="d0">White Squares</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">White squares are two adjacent elements within the discretized rotor grid in diagram (c), representing 'air' elements introduced by the controller.&lt;SEP&gt;White squares in the geometry panels represent voids or air.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a11edf0b881aae28423c1b0d33893b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e55225b1f173db3033115aeae970db</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288970</data>
