    <node id="Figure 5.2">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.2</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.2 is an image that illustrates the application of Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) in various fields.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d5f6d6c8243089f59fe677475645a52</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288978</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Panel (a)">
      <data key="d0">Panel (a)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Panel (a) is a visual representation titled "Intersection of a plane and and a surface," showing a 3D perspective of a conical surface intersected by a plane.&lt;SEP&gt;Panel (a) of Figure 4.30, titled "Optimal geometry," displays a quarter-section of a Synchronous Reluctance Motor rotor and stator, detailing its optimized structural design.&lt;SEP&gt;Panel (a) is a visual example within Figure 5.2, depicting an Atari game named 'Breakout' or a similar paddle-and-brick game.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5f6d6c8243089f59fe677475645a52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b56c95146eb3d0e8591ffefd6ab679ea</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288979</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breakout">
      <data key="d0">Breakout</data>
      <data key="d1">game</data>
      <data key="d2">Breakout is a classic Atari game, or a similar paddle-and-brick game, depicted in Panel (a) as an application of DRL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d5f6d6c8243089f59fe677475645a52</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288979</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Panel (b)">
      <data key="d0">Panel (b)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Panel (b) is a visual representation titled "Top View," providing a 2D overhead perspective of the level set concept with material distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;Panel (b) of Figure 4.30, titled "Magnetic |B| field distribution," visualizes the magnetic flux density within the same Synchronous Reluctance Motor structure using a color gradient.&lt;SEP&gt;Panel (b) is a visual example within Figure 5.2, illustrating a Go board with black and white stones in a mid-game state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5f6d6c8243089f59fe677475645a52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b56c95146eb3d0e8591ffefd6ab679ea</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288979</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Panel (c)">
      <data key="d0">Panel (c)</data>
      <data key="d1">part</data>
      <data key="d2">Panel (c) is a visual example within Figure 5.2, showing a two-part image illustrating robotics in action, specifically a robotic arm and gripper.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d5f6d6c8243089f59fe677475645a52</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Robotic Arm">
      <data key="d0">Robotic Arm</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The robotic arm is a light beige or white mechanism with dark gray or black accents, extending over a green fabric surface.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d5f6d6c8243089f59fe677475645a52</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Robotic Gripper">
      <data key="d0">Robotic Gripper</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A robotic gripper is shown in Panel (c) securely holding a light-colored cube, exemplifying DRL's application in robotics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d5f6d6c8243089f59fe677475645a52</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breakout Game">
      <data key="d0">Breakout Game</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Breakout Game, or a similar paddle-and-brick game, is an example of an Atari game depicted in the image.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d5f6d6c8243089f59fe677475645a52</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Atari Game Screen">
      <data key="d0">Atari Game Screen</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Atari Game Screen displays elements such as a score, lives, bricks, a paddle, and a ball.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d5f6d6c8243089f59fe677475645a52</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paddle">
      <data key="d0">Paddle</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The paddle is a small red object at the bottom-center of the Atari game screen, used to hit the ball.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d5f6d6c8243089f59fe677475645a52</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ball">
      <data key="d0">Ball</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The ball is a tiny red square bouncing upwards from the paddle in the Atari game.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d5f6d6c8243089f59fe677475645a52</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Go Board">
      <data key="d0">Go Board</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Go Board is a light brown, grid-lined surface with intersections where Go stones are placed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d5f6d6c8243089f59fe677475645a52</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Black Go Stones">
      <data key="d0">Black Go Stones</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Black Go Stones are circular pieces on the Go board, forming patterns in mid-game.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d5f6d6c8243089f59fe677475645a52</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="White Go Stones">
      <data key="d0">White Go Stones</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">White Go Stones are circular pieces on the Go board, clustered more on the left side.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d5f6d6c8243089f59fe677475645a52</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Star Points">
      <data key="d0">Star Points</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Star points, or Hoshi, are smaller dots at key intersections on the Go board, indicating strategic locations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d5f6d6c8243089f59fe677475645a52</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cylindrical Object">
      <data key="d0">Cylindrical Object</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A small white cylindrical object, possibly a bottle, rests on the green fabric below the robotic arm's gripper.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d5f6d6c8243089f59fe677475645a52</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Light-colored Cube">
      <data key="d0">Light-colored Cube</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The light-colored cube is an object being securely held by the robotic gripper, featuring various geometric cutouts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d5f6d6c8243089f59fe677475645a52</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288984</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Binary Material Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Binary Material Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Binary Material Distribution is a concept where material is assigned discrete values of 0 or 1, representing void or solid, based on a condition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d40236fe707c1436e66df73317598df7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288984</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Material Variable">
      <data key="d0">Material Variable</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Material Variable is a variable that takes discrete values of 0 or 1, representing the presence or absence of structural material.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d40236fe707c1436e66df73317598df7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SIMP">
      <data key="d0">SIMP</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">SIMP (Solid Isotropic Material with Penalization) is a density-based topology optimization method that can suffer from issues like intermediate density.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d40236fe707c1436e66df73317598df7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heaviside Step Function">
      <data key="d0">Heaviside Step Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Heaviside step function is a mathematical operation that conditionally discretizes the continuous output of the level set function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d40236fe707c1436e66df73317598df7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CAD-Ready Designs">
      <data key="d0">CAD-Ready Designs</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">CAD-Ready Designs are designs with distinct material regions that simplify manufacturing processes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d40236fe707c1436e66df73317598df7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grayscales">
      <data key="d0">Grayscales</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Grayscales refer to the problem of "intermediate density" in topology optimization, where material properties are not distinctly binary.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d40236fe707c1436e66df73317598df7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mesh Dependency">
      <data key="d0">Mesh Dependency</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mesh Dependency is a limitation or issue that can affect density-based topology optimization methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d40236fe707c1436e66df73317598df7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288987</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Level Set Function (Φ)">
      <data key="d0">Level Set Function (Φ)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Level Set Function is an implicit function of spatial coordinates determining whether a point belongs to a solid or void region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d40236fe707c1436e66df73317598df7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288987</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Boundary-Based Topology Optimization Methods">
      <data key="d0">Boundary-Based Topology Optimization Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Boundary-based topology optimization methods are a class of optimization methods that yield sharp boundaries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d40236fe707c1436e66df73317598df7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288987</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Table">
      <data key="d0">Table</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The table presents a set of geometric parameters and their corresponding nominal values for a reference motor design.&lt;SEP&gt;The table presents structured data with four columns: 'Type', 'Scenario', 'Coil current(A)', and 'No. of turns(T)'. It details experimental setups for an A2C algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6504e2cf679305513217b8548174a68b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NIVYL Scenarios">
      <data key="d0">NIVYL Scenarios</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">NIVYL scenarios are identified as 'Train' scenarios for the A2C algorithm, used for its training process. They consist of three configurations: (1.0 A, 250 Turns), (1.0 A, 500 Turns), and (1.5 A, 500 Turns).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6504e2cf679305513217b8548174a68b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LSEL Scenarios">
      <data key="d0">LSEL Scenarios</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">LSEL scenarios are identified as 'Test' scenarios for the A2C algorithm, used to evaluate its generalizability to unseen conditions. They encompass five configurations: (0.5 A, 250 Turns), (0.75 A, 500 Turns), (1.25 A, 500 Turns), (1.75 A, 500 Turns), and (2.0 A, 500 Turns).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6504e2cf679305513217b8548174a68b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288989</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coil Current (A)">
      <data key="d0">Coil Current (A)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Coil current is a parameter specified in Amperes within the table, ranging from 0.5 A to 2.0 A across different scenarios.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6504e2cf679305513217b8548174a68b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288989</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Number of Turns (T)">
      <data key="d0">Number of Turns (T)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Number of turns is a parameter specified in the table, indicating the number of coil turns, typically 250 or 500.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6504e2cf679305513217b8548174a68b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288989</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Training Regimes">
      <data key="d0">Training Regimes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Training regimes for the A2C algorithm are defined by the NIVYL scenarios outlined in the table.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6504e2cf679305513217b8548174a68b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288990</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing Regimes">
      <data key="d0">Testing Regimes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing regimes for the A2C algorithm are defined by the LSEL scenarios outlined in the table.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6504e2cf679305513217b8548174a68b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288990</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Number of Turns">
      <data key="d0">Number of Turns</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Number of Turns refers to the count of windings in a coil, a variable in the experimental scenarios, impacting magnetic field strength.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6504e2cf679305513217b8548174a68b</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288991</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Constrained Optimization Problem">
      <data key="d0">Constrained Optimization Problem</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A constrained optimization problem designed for the topology optimization of a Synchronous Reluctance Motor rotor, aiming to minimize a scalar function subject to an inequality constraint.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288991</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Scalar Function f(a)">
      <data key="d0">Scalar Function f(a)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The scalar function f(a) is the primary objective to be minimized, directly relating to the negative magnitude of the average torque generated by the motor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Variable A">
      <data key="d0">Variable A</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Variable 'a' represents a sequence of actions or a control parameter influencing the material distribution within the defined design space of the SynRM rotor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Air Barriers">
      <data key="d0">Air Barriers</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Air barriers, also known as flux barriers, are regions within the rotor geometry where discrete cells are filled with air ($p_j = 0$), manipulating magnetic reluctance paths.&lt;SEP&gt;Air Barriers are white cells within the rotor grid that represent air, forming flux barriers for optimized performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Air Barriers are white rectangular sections within the SynRM rotor that enhance the reluctance effect and are crucial for torque generation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b7b73719952876f4f9c0e6b4efe0027&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b56c95146eb3d0e8591ffefd6ab679ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Iron">
      <data key="d0">Iron</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Iron is the material that fills the discrete cells in the rotor geometry when $p_j = 1$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Variable pj">
      <data key="d0">Variable pj</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Variable $p_j$ signifies the material status of the j-th discrete cell in the rotor geometry, where $p_j = 0$ means air and $p_j = 1$ means iron.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288993</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Variable M">
      <data key="d0">Variable M</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Variable 'm' refers to the total number of discretized cells within the design space of the rotor. In this context, m = 25.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288993</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electrical Engineering">
      <data key="d0">Electrical Engineering</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The application domain of the mathematical equation is topology optimization in electrical engineering.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288995</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Flux Barriers">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Flux Barriers</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnetic flux barriers are configurations within the rotor that enhance reluctance torque by manipulating magnetic reluctance paths.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288995</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Computational Optimization Techniques">
      <data key="d0">Computational Optimization Techniques</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Computational optimization techniques are leveraged to systematically improve motor performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288996</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Efficiency, High-Power-Density Motors">
      <data key="d0">High-Efficiency, High-Power-Density Motors</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">High-efficiency, high-power-density motors are crucial for global energy consumption reduction and sustainable industrial practices, representing the goal of the optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288996</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Washing Machines">
      <data key="d0">Washing Machines</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Washing machines are practical applications that can benefit from enhanced motor designs resulting from this optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288996</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Industrial Motor Applications">
      <data key="d0">Industrial Motor Applications</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Industrial Motor Applications represent a broad category of practical applications that can benefit from enhanced motor designs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288996</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SynRM Rotor">
      <data key="d0">SynRM Rotor</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The rotating part of a Synchronous Reluctance Motor, whose material distribution is the subject of topology optimization.&lt;SEP&gt;The SynRM Rotor is the inner rotating component of the Synchronous Reluctance Motor, whose design is optimized and displayed in the image.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b56c95146eb3d0e8591ffefd6ab679ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Variable 'a'">
      <data key="d0">Variable 'a'</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A sequence of actions or a control parameter influencing the material distribution of the SynRM rotor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discrete Cell">
      <data key="d0">Discrete Cell</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A small, individual unit within the discretized rotor geometry; $p_j = 0$ signifies it's filled with air, $p_j = 1$ signifies iron.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288998</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Variable 'm'">
      <data key="d0">Variable 'm'</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The total number of discretized cells within the design space of the rotor, specified as 25 in a $5 \times 5$ grid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288998</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Summation Term">
      <data key="d0">Summation Term</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">$\sum_{j=1}^{n} (p_j = 0)$ represents the count of cells designated for air, where 'n' is expected to be equal to 'm'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288998</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parameters K1 and K2">
      <data key="d0">Parameters K1 and K2</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Problem-specific parameters defining the lower and upper bounds for the number of air cells allowed in the rotor design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288998</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topology Optimization in Electrical Engineering">
      <data key="d0">Topology Optimization in Electrical Engineering</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The application domain of the mathematical equation, focused on designing Synchronous Reluctance Motors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0011b0a6062b8a1499810eced96d7d94</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288999</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trainable Layers">
      <data key="d0">Trainable Layers</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Trainable Layers indicates the number of layers with adjustable parameters during the training process, which is 16.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a177157c3402545cf649cdb67022050a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760288999</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Number of Parameters">
      <data key="d0">Number of Parameters</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Number of Parameters quantifies the total number of adjustable parameters within the network, reported as 2.4 Million.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a177157c3402545cf649cdb67022050a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289000</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RMS Error Minimization">
      <data key="d0">RMS Error Minimization</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">RMS Error Minimization is the stated goal related to the model's performance, specifically the RMS error between prediction and FEA field distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a177157c3402545cf649cdb67022050a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Page 42">
      <data key="d0">Page 42</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Page 42 is a reference within the surrounding text that corroborates information about the Deep Learning model's layers.&lt;SEP&gt;Page 42 is the specific page where Figure 2.9 is referenced in the accompanying text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a177157c3402545cf649cdb67022050a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a4b902f2347e5fea9d43e606ce167ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Page 43">
      <data key="d0">Page 43</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Page 43 is a reference within the surrounding text that mentions the complexity the network needs to capture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a177157c3402545cf649cdb67022050a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289003</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Page 44">
      <data key="d0">Page 44</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Page 44 is a reference within the surrounding text that refers to the role of the validation dataset.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a177157c3402545cf649cdb67022050a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289003</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Number Of Layers">
      <data key="d0">Number Of Layers</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Number of Layers specifies the total count of layers within the Deep Learning model, which is 32.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a177157c3402545cf649cdb67022050a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289003</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Number Of Parameters">
      <data key="d0">Number Of Parameters</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Number of parameters quantifies the total adjustable parameters within the network, reported as 2.4 million.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a177157c3402545cf649cdb67022050a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289003</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Learning Network">
      <data key="d0">Deep Learning Network</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Deep Learning Network is a type of artificial neural network that includes multiple layers between the input and output layers, used for learning complex patterns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a177157c3402545cf649cdb67022050a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289003</data>
    </node>
    <node id="32 Layers">
      <data key="d0">32 Layers</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">32 Layers is the total number of layers in the Deep Learning model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a177157c3402545cf649cdb67022050a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="16 Trainable Convolutional Layers">
      <data key="d0">16 Trainable Convolutional Layers</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">16 Trainable Convolutional Layers are the layers within the Deep Learning model that have learnable parameters during training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a177157c3402545cf649cdb67022050a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="8 Pooling/up-sampling Layers">
      <data key="d0">8 Pooling/up-sampling Layers</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">8 Pooling/up-sampling Layers are non-trainable layers within the Deep Learning model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a177157c3402545cf649cdb67022050a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="8 Dropout Layers">
      <data key="d0">8 Dropout Layers</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">8 Dropout Layers are non-trainable layers within the Deep Learning model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a177157c3402545cf649cdb67022050a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.7">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.7</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.7 depicts the representation of a curve by a level set, illustrating concepts related to implicit functions and material distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289006</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Level Set">
      <data key="d0">Level Set</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A level set is a mathematical concept where a curve or surface is defined by the set of points where a function has a constant value, typically zero.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289006</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Curve Representation">
      <data key="d0">Curve Representation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Curve representation refers to how a curve is mathematically or visually defined, which in this context is through a level set an implicit function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289006</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plane">
      <data key="d0">Plane</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A plane is a flat, two-dimensional surface that can intersect other surfaces or objects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289006</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surface">
      <data key="d0">Surface</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A surface is a continuous two-dimensional object that forms the boundary of a three-dimensional object or separates two regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289007</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conical Surface">
      <data key="d0">Conical Surface</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A conical surface is a 3D shape resembling a cone, depicted as green-shaded and open-top in Panel (a).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289007</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Φ(x,t)">
      <data key="d0">Φ(x,t)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Φ(x,t) is a label used for a green-shaded, open-top conical surface in Panel (a) and represents an implicit function in the context of boundary-based topology optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289007</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Black Circular Curve">
      <data key="d0">Black Circular Curve</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Black Circular Curve is formed by the intersection of the plane and the conical surface, labeled as "Φ=0".</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289007</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Φ=0">
      <data key="d0">Φ=0</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Φ=0 represents a black circular curve on a plane in Panel (a) and the outermost boundary of the green ring in Panel (b), signifying the structural boundary definition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289008</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Φ&gt;0">
      <data key="d0">Φ&gt;0</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Φ&gt;0 represents a region just inside the boundary within the green ring in Panel (b), typically representing void material in the design domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289008</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Φ&lt;0">
      <data key="d0">Φ&lt;0</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Φ&lt;0 represents the central orange-brown circular area in Panel (b), typically representing solid material, and also a region in the lower right corner of the outer orange-brown field, indicating void or background material.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Boundary-Based Topology Optimization">
      <data key="d0">Boundary-Based Topology Optimization</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Boundary-Based Topology Optimization is a method that uses implicit functions and level sets to define structural boundaries for optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Solid Material">
      <data key="d0">Solid Material</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Solid Material is represented by regions where Φ&lt;0 in the context of level set functions for topology optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289010</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Void">
      <data key="d0">Void</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Void represents regions where Φ&gt;0 in the context of level set functions for topology optimization, indicating an absence of material.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289010</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Boundary-Based Topology Optimization Method">
      <data key="d0">Boundary-Based Topology Optimization Method</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Boundary-Based Topology Optimization Method is a technique that uses implicit functions to define structural boundaries, illustrated by Figure 4.7.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24ad555f52fcdc59c7c0057735a01366</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289010</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.29">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.29</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.29 is a line graph depicting convergence results for a reinforcement learning algorithm under specific excitation parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4b98982b6cac8028635bf8c32451b12e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289011</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1.5 Amps">
      <data key="d0">1.5 Amps</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">1.5 Amps represents a specific excitation scenario for which convergence results are depicted.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4b98982b6cac8028635bf8c32451b12e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289011</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 2.9">
      <data key="d0">Figure 2.9</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 2.9 is an image that illustrates different types of kernels for a Convolutional layer in a CNN, specifically comparing non-dilated and dilated filters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a4b902f2347e5fea9d43e606ce167ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289011</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Input Map">
      <data key="d0">Input Map</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The Input Map is represented as a larger, lower 5x5 grid in shades of blue, suggesting different intensity values or features, and serves as the data source for the convolutional process.&lt;SEP&gt;The Input Map represents the incoming data or features to a convolutional layer, shown in shades of blue in Figure 2.9.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a4b902f2347e5fea9d43e606ce167ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d59e50443efd19bae755d63382e315bd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Output Map">
      <data key="d0">Output Map</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The Output Map is represented as a smaller, elevated 3x3 grid in shades of cyan, showing activation values or features learned by the filter.&lt;SEP&gt;The Output Map, also referred to as a feature map, represents the result of a convolution operation, shown in cyan/teal in Figure 2.9.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a4b902f2347e5fea9d43e606ce167ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d59e50443efd19bae755d63382e315bd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Dilated Filter">
      <data key="d0">Non-Dilated Filter</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A Non-Dilated Filter is a type of convolutional filter that processes a compact, contiguous block of pixels in the input map, illustrated in part (a) of Figure 2.9.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a4b902f2347e5fea9d43e606ce167ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Padding">
      <data key="d0">Padding</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Padding refers to the boundaries of a larger input space, represented by a dashed white line forming a larger 7x7 grid around the 5x5 input grid.&lt;SEP&gt;Padding is the addition of extra values, often zeros, around the border of an input map to manage the spatial dimensions of the output feature map and prevent information loss at the edges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a4b902f2347e5fea9d43e606ce167ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d59e50443efd19bae755d63382e315bd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289014</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dilation Rate">
      <data key="d0">Dilation Rate</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Dilation Rate is a parameter that defines the spacing between the kernel's sampling points in a dilated convolution, enabling the filter to cover a wider receptive field.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a4b902f2347e5fea9d43e606ce167ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289014</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Receptive Field">
      <data key="d0">Receptive Field</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Receptive Field is a 3x3 section of the input map highlighted with a slightly desaturated blue-gray color, indicating the current area being processed by the convolutional kernel.&lt;SEP&gt;The Receptive Field refers to the area of the input feature map that a specified feature in the output feature map is sensitive to.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a4b902f2347e5fea9d43e606ce167ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d59e50443efd19bae755d63382e315bd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289014</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kernel">
      <data key="d0">Kernel</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Kernel, also known as a filter, is a small matrix used in a convolutional layer to extract features from the input data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a4b902f2347e5fea9d43e606ce167ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289015</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Geometric Shapes">
      <data key="d0">Geometric Shapes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Geometric Shapes are elements used in the visual analysis that constitute the figures, depicting the input and output maps and filters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a4b902f2347e5fea9d43e606ce167ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289015</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3D Isometric Perspective">
      <data key="d0">3D Isometric Perspective</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">3D Isometric Perspective is a visual technique used in the illustrations to give depth and dimension to the flat representations of the input and output maps.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a4b902f2347e5fea9d43e606ce167ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289015</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spatially Distant Relationships">
      <data key="d0">Spatially Distant Relationships</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Spatially Distant Relationships refer to connections or patterns between data points that are not immediately adjacent in the input map.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a4b902f2347e5fea9d43e606ce167ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289016</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Field Predictions">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Field Predictions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnetic Field Predictions are the ultimate goal or application for which the discussed neural network architectures are being used, highlighting the importance of capturing wider spatial relevance.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic Field Predictions are the subject of the deep learning and FEA results displayed in Figure 2.11.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8b78525002f5dfa42398879cc72242c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a4b902f2347e5fea9d43e606ce167ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289016</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Dilated Filter with Padding">
      <data key="d0">Non-Dilated Filter with Padding</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A non-dilated convolutional filter with padding processes a contiguous block of pixels in an input map.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a4b902f2347e5fea9d43e606ce167ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289016</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dilated Filter with Dilation Rate of 1">
      <data key="d0">Dilated Filter with Dilation Rate of 1</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A dilated filter with a dilation rate of 1 samples spaced-out pixels in the input map, effectively widening the receptive field without increasing parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a4b902f2347e5fea9d43e606ce167ec</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289017</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dumoulin and Visin">
      <data key="d0">Dumoulin and Visin</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Dumoulin and Visin are individuals credited for the concept illustrated in the convolution kernel visualization.&lt;SEP&gt;Dumoulin and Visin are researchers cited for their work on convolutional kernel types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a4b902f2347e5fea9d43e606ce167ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d59e50443efd19bae755d63382e315bd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289018</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GA ON/OFF">
      <data key="d0">GA ON/OFF</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">GA ON/OFF is a Topology Optimization methodology using a Genetic Algorithm (GA) with an ON/OFF or conventional design approach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0d5c4a49fbb5bedfe6eb2f200271d99</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289018</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GA SeqTO-v1">
      <data key="d0">GA SeqTO-v1</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">GA SeqTO-v1 is a Topology Optimization methodology using a Genetic Algorithm (GA) with a Sequence-based Topology Optimization (SeqTO-v1) approach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0d5c4a49fbb5bedfe6eb2f200271d99</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
