    </node>
    <node id="FE Simulations Reduction Factors">
      <data key="d0">FE Simulations Reduction Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">FE Simulations Reduction Factors refer to the statistical data indicating how much the number of FE simulations is reduced when using SeqTO-v1 methods compared to GA ON/OFF.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0d5c4a49fbb5bedfe6eb2f200271d99</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289026</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Efficiency of TD Learning with SeqTO-v1">
      <data key="d0">Efficiency of TD Learning with SeqTO-v1</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Efficiency of TD learning with SeqTO-v1 refers to the statistical data indicating the percentage by which TD SeqTO-v1 is more efficient than GA SeqTO-v1 in terms of FE simulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0d5c4a49fbb5bedfe6eb2f200271d99</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289026</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.27">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.27</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.27 displays the optimal geometry and B distribution for an excitation scenario (1.5 A, 500 Turns) using different optimization algorithms: GA, QL (SeqTO-v1), and A2C (SeqTO-v2).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-081d5d2ff62504474fc8532dd817fb39</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289027</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B-Distribution">
      <data key="d0">B-Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The magnetic flux density distribution within the C-core, visualized using a color bar and flux lines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-081d5d2ff62504474fc8532dd817fb39</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289028</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Force">
      <data key="d0">Force</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Force is an output parameter of electromagnetic actuators, which can be optimized through reinforcement learning.&lt;SEP&gt;Force is a performance metric for the electromagnetic actuator, measured in units not specified but compared across optimization methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Force is a metric used to evaluate the performance of different optimization methods in the context of magnetic actuators, measured in Newtons (N).&lt;SEP&gt;Force is a numerical result (in Newtons) indicating the performance metric for each optimized C-core design.&lt;SEP&gt;Force is a quantitative metric measured in Newtons (N) resulting from the optimization methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Force is a metric representing the physical force measured in Newtons (N) in each scenario.&lt;SEP&gt;Force is a performance metric measured in Newtons (N) for different optimization scenarios.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2a5c42907185fecc94ee85f1b33ec380&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b984e4831b11f20f84bb8669cd7396b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c48c238aedc178e88e075043138eb72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11edf0b881aae28423c1b0d33893b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-081d5d2ff62504474fc8532dd817fb39</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289029</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conventional C-Core Design Geometry">
      <data key="d0">Conventional C-Core Design Geometry</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A standard or typical shape for a C-core, which the optimized designs resemble.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-081d5d2ff62504474fc8532dd817fb39</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289029</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discretized C-Core Design">
      <data key="d0">Discretized C-Core Design</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The discretized C-core design is a visual representation where blue squares denote core material, white squares are voids, and orange patterned squares are coils.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-081d5d2ff62504474fc8532dd817fb39</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 3.13">
      <data key="d0">Figure 3.13</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 3.13 is a 2D line graph that illustrates the training curves for supervised efficiency map prediction, showing error decrease over epochs for various network architectures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf789c1974c43fd1722237522bebd446</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Training Curves">
      <data key="d0">Training Curves</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Training curves illustrate how the error of machine learning models changes over training epochs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf789c1974c43fd1722237522bebd446</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Supervised Efficiency Map Prediction">
      <data key="d0">Supervised Efficiency Map Prediction</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Supervised Efficiency Map Prediction is the process of training models to predict efficiency maps under supervision.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf789c1974c43fd1722237522bebd446</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Avg Node Percentage Error">
      <data key="d0">Avg Node Percentage Error</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Avg Node Percentage Error is a metric plotted on the y-axis, ranging from 0 to 30, representing the average error percentage per node in the prediction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf789c1974c43fd1722237522bebd446</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289031</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Modular CNN-RNN">
      <data key="d0">Modular CNN-RNN</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Modular CNN-RNN is a neural network model, represented by a blue line, that shows a decrease in average node percentage error during training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf789c1974c43fd1722237522bebd446</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289031</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Modular ANN-RNN">
      <data key="d0">Modular ANN-RNN</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Modular ANN-RNN is a neural network model, represented by an orange-red line, that shows a steeper initial decrease in average node percentage error during training compared to other models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf789c1974c43fd1722237522bebd446</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289031</data>
    </node>
    <node id="End-to-End CNN">
      <data key="d0">End-to-End CNN</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">End-to-End CNN is a neural network model, represented by a gold/yellow line, which converges at a low error after approximately 35 epochs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf789c1974c43fd1722237522bebd446</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Page 66">
      <data key="d0">Page 66</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Page 66 is a reference point in the surrounding context that provides additional details about the training of the End-to-End network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf789c1974c43fd1722237522bebd446</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Average Mean Squared Error">
      <data key="d0">Average Mean Squared Error</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Average Mean Squared Error is a metric used to measure the average squared difference between the estimated values and the actual value, found to be 1-1.5% per node for the End-to-End CNN.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf789c1974c43fd1722237522bebd446</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Section 3.5.1">
      <data key="d0">Section 3.5.1</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Section 3.5.1 is a section where the performance of the Modular network approach is discussed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf789c1974c43fd1722237522bebd446</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Panel 1">
      <data key="d0">Panel 1</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Panel 1 illustrates the initial state of the Controller within the Design Domain, positioned in the top-left corner of the 10x10 grid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d610c2b7090b4469e2b4b784c49c5807</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289035</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Panel 2">
      <data key="d0">Panel 2</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Panel 2 illustrates the state of the Controller after moving one step to the RIGHT from its position in Panel 1, leaving a material trail.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d610c2b7090b4469e2b4b784c49c5807</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289036</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Panel 3">
      <data key="d0">Panel 3</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Panel 3 illustrates the state of the Controller after moving one step DOWN from its position in Panel 2, further extending the material trail.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d610c2b7090b4469e2b4b784c49c5807</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289036</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action RIGHT">
      <data key="d0">Action RIGHT</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Action RIGHT refers to the movement of the Controller one step to the right within the Design Domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d610c2b7090b4469e2b4b784c49c5807</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289036</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action DOWN">
      <data key="d0">Action DOWN</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Action DOWN refers to the movement of the Controller one step down within the Design Domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d610c2b7090b4469e2b4b784c49c5807</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289037</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grid of Cells">
      <data key="d0">Grid of Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Grid of Cells is a 10x10 matrix that forms the Design Domain, where each cell can either be empty or filled with material.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d610c2b7090b4469e2b4b784c49c5807</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289037</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Material Trail">
      <data key="d0">Material Trail</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Material Trail refers to the accumulated gray cells, representing material left by the Controller as it moves across the Design Domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d610c2b7090b4469e2b4b784c49c5807</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289037</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sequence Based TO Methodology">
      <data key="d0">Sequence Based TO Methodology</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Sequence Based TO Methodology is a concept that the image illustrates, particularly through the sequential movement of the controller and material distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d610c2b7090b4469e2b4b784c49c5807</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GA On/Off">
      <data key="d0">GA On/Off</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">GA On/Off is an optimization method, likely a Genetic Algorithm with an on/off material distribution, that yielded a force of 32.5 N with 160,000 FE Calls.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c48c238aedc178e88e075043138eb72</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seq TO - QL">
      <data key="d0">Seq TO - QL</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Seq TO - QL is an optimization method based on Sequential Topology Optimization and Q-learning, yielding a force of 34.8 N with 105,000 FE Calls.&lt;SEP&gt;Seq TO - QL represents an optimization approach detailed in the image analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Seq TO - QL (Sequential Topology Optimization - Q-Learning) is an optimization method that produces a distinct L-shape geometry and strong yellow B fields.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a11edf0b881aae28423c1b0d33893b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2785b4e150ada39f3077e573325eeb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c48c238aedc178e88e075043138eb72</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seq TO - RL">
      <data key="d0">Seq TO - RL</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Seq TO - RL is an optimization method based on Sequential Topology Optimization and Reinforcement Learning, specifically A2C, producing a force of 32.7 N with 175,000 FE Calls.&lt;SEP&gt;Seq TO - RL represents an optimization approach detailed in the image analysis, appearing in two scenarios and a re-trained version.&lt;SEP&gt;Seq TO - RL (Sequential Topology Optimization - Reinforcement Learning) is an optimization method that produces geometries with slight variations and strong yellow B fields.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a11edf0b881aae28423c1b0d33893b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2785b4e150ada39f3077e573325eeb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c48c238aedc178e88e075043138eb72</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seq TO - RL (Re-Train)">
      <data key="d0">Seq TO - RL (Re-Train)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Seq TO - RL (Re-Train) is a fine-tuned A2C agent, a Reinforcement Learning-based optimization method, achieving a force of 34.6 N with 7,500 FE Calls.&lt;SEP&gt;Seq TO - RL (Re-Train) is a specific scenario of the Seq TO - RL optimization approach, showing re-trained results.&lt;SEP&gt;Seq TO - RL (Re-Train) is an optimization method that re-uses the Reinforcement Learning approach, producing geometries and B distributions visually similar to Seq TO - RL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a11edf0b881aae28423c1b0d33893b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2785b4e150ada39f3077e573325eeb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c48c238aedc178e88e075043138eb72</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289039</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Force (N)">
      <data key="d0">Force (N)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Force is a performance metric measured in Newtons, representing the output of the optimized designs.&lt;SEP&gt;Force is a performance metric measured in Newtons (N).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2785b4e150ada39f3077e573325eeb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c48c238aedc178e88e075043138eb72</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289039</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electromagnetic Actuator">
      <data key="d0">Electromagnetic Actuator</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">An electromagnetic actuator is a device for which the C-core discretized design space is designed, specifically focusing on optimizing its core material distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;The electromagnetic actuator, specifically a C-core solenoid, is the device being designed and optimized in the image.&lt;SEP&gt;An electromagnetic actuator is a device whose design is being optimized using various methods, featuring a C-core and coils.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3b984e4831b11f20f84bb8669cd7396b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c48c238aedc178e88e075043138eb72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57172dfb9b0c90234ff04b1a5519b71e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289040</data>
    </node>
    <node id="A2C based on SeqTO-v2">
      <data key="d0">A2C based on SeqTO-v2</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A2C based on SeqTO-v2 is an optimization method referring to Actor-Critic Reinforcement Learning which is categorized under Seq TO - RL.&lt;SEP&gt;A2C (Advantage Actor-Critic) based on SeqTO-v2 is an optimization method depicted in Figure 5.33 (caption c) for generating optimal C-core actuator designs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c48c238aedc178e88e075043138eb72</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289040</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QL based on SeqTO-v1">
      <data key="d0">QL based on SeqTO-v1</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">QL based on SeqTO-v1 is an optimization method referring to Q-Learning which is categorized under Seq TO - QL.&lt;SEP&gt;QL (Q-Learning) based on SeqTO-v1 is an optimization method depicted in Figure 5.33 (caption b) for generating optimal C-core actuator designs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c48c238aedc178e88e075043138eb72</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289040</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seq To - QL">
      <data key="d0">Seq To - QL</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Seq To - QL is an optimization method based on Sequential Topology Optimization with QL (Q-learning), achieving a force of 34.8 N with 105,000 FE Calls.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c48c238aedc178e88e075043138eb72</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289041</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seq To - RL">
      <data key="d0">Seq To - RL</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Seq To - RL is an optimization method based on Sequential Topology Optimization with RL (Reinforcement Learning), achieving a force of 32.7 N with 175,000 FE Calls.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c48c238aedc178e88e075043138eb72</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289042</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seq To - RL Re-Train">
      <data key="d0">Seq To - RL Re-Train</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Seq To - RL Re-Train is an optimization method representing a fine-tuned A2C agent based on Sequential Topology Optimization with Reinforcement Learning, achieving a force of 34.6 N with 7,500 FE Calls.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c48c238aedc178e88e075043138eb72</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289042</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.30">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.30</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.30 is a caption for an image that displays the optimized geometry and magnetic flux distribution for a discretized Synchronous Reluctance Motor (SynRM) using SeqTO-v1 with TD-learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b56c95146eb3d0e8591ffefd6ab679ea</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289042</data>
