    </node>
    <node id="r(s,a)">
      <data key="d0">r(s,a)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The immediate reward received from the environment after taking action $a$ in state $s$.&lt;SEP&gt;r(s,a) represents the immediate reward received after taking action 'a' in state 's'.&lt;SEP&gt;r(s,a) represents the immediate reward received after taking action 'a' in state 's'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e90fd88a3c123b4352d139141e81b16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7724d4c011c9981c316498400dd34c58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293109</data>
    </node>
    <node id="γ">
      <data key="d0">γ</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The discount factor, a scalar value between 0 and 1, used to weight immediate rewards more heavily than future rewards, determining the present value of future rewards.&lt;SEP&gt;γ (gamma) represents the discount factor, a value between 0 and 1, used to weight future rewards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e90fd88a3c123b4352d139141e81b16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V(s')">
      <data key="d0">V(s')</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The state-value function evaluated at the next state $s'$, representing the expected cumulative discounted future reward from state $s'$ onwards under the current policy.&lt;SEP&gt;V(s') represents the value of the next state (s'), indicating the expected cumulative reward from that state onwards.&lt;SEP&gt;V(s') represents the value function of the next state s', indicating the expected return from that state onward.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e90fd88a3c123b4352d139141e81b16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7724d4c011c9981c316498400dd34c58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293111</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Gradient (PG) Method">
      <data key="d0">Policy Gradient (PG) Method</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A policy-based Reinforcement Learning method that A2C improves upon by reducing variance in gradient estimates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289057</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gradient Calculation">
      <data key="d0">Gradient Calculation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Gradient calculation is a mathematical process used to determine the direction and magnitude of policy parameter adjustments in policy gradient methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Refers to the process of computing gradients for policy updates, where the Advantage function is considered more useful than the Q-value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="S">
      <data key="d0">S</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The current state of the environment, representing the complete observation of the system at a particular time step.&lt;SEP&gt;S is a variable used within the equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be1e8d82c79e97d6340503568a2610b8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292832</data>
    </node>
    <node id="A">
      <data key="d0">A</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A is one of the actions listed in the Q-table, associated with Q-values for different states.&lt;SEP&gt;The action taken by the agent in state S.&lt;SEP&gt;A is a variable or constant used within the equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca0b752852f034cec7766c7e6e92af67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be1e8d82c79e97d6340503568a2610b8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292832</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mnih et al. (2016)">
      <data key="d0">Mnih et al. (2016)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Referenced for their work in deep reinforcement learning, particularly regarding the use of the Advantage function in applications like playing Atari games.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289059</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-core Excitation Scenarios">
      <data key="d0">C-core Excitation Scenarios</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific application mentioned that suggests a control or optimization problem in engineering, where the Advantage function might be applied.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289059</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Improvement Signal">
      <data key="d0">Policy Improvement Signal</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The policy improvement signal is a theoretical concept that the Advantage function effectively decouples from the absolute scale of rewards, leading to more stable updates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289060</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mnih Et Al. (2016)">
      <data key="d0">Mnih Et Al. (2016)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Mnih et al. (2016) is a reference indicating the use of the Advantage function in deep reinforcement learning for tasks like playing Atari games.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22c061c45c3a0d2579ced8d3121f0d90</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289060</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-core Electromagnetic Actuator">
      <data key="d0">C-core Electromagnetic Actuator</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The C-core Electromagnetic Actuator is the object being designed and optimized, with its material distribution being a key factor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289061</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discretized Design Domain">
      <data key="d0">Discretized Design Domain</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Discretized Design Domain is the 2-D design space divided into 288 square cells where material properties are assigned.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289061</data>
    </node>
    <node id="p_j">
      <data key="d0">p_j</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">p_j is a binary variable representing the material property of the j-th cell, where $p_j=0$ indicates air and $p_j=1$ electrical steel.&lt;SEP&gt;p_j is a variable in the summation of the inequality constraint, and its value being 0 is a condition within the sum.&lt;SEP&gt;p_j is a variable in the equation, where p_j equals 1 for some cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14506e8e02aeb799dcd49d4b24c5b529&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50e280b998ddabea3a4713c685bb83d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="n">
      <data key="d0">n</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">n is a positive integer representing the total number of cells in the discretized design domain, specifically 288 square cells.&lt;SEP&gt;n is a positive integer representing the total number of cells in the discretized design domain, which is 288.&lt;SEP&gt;n is a variable representing an upper limit for the index 'j' in the summation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14506e8e02aeb799dcd49d4b24c5b529&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292788</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Objective Function f(a)">
      <data key="d0">Objective Function f(a)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Objective Function f(a) represents the negative magnitude of force, which is to be minimized in the optimization problem.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289062</data>
    </node>
    <node id="pj">
      <data key="d0">pj</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">p_j is the material property of the j-th cell in the discretized design domain, a binary variable where p_j=0 indicates air and p_j=1 indicates electrical steel.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289063</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Indicator Function">
      <data key="d0">Indicator Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The indicator function (p_j = 1) evaluates to 1 if the condition p_j = 1 is true, and 0 otherwise, used to count active cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289063</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Summation Operator">
      <data key="d0">Summation Operator</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The summation operator (∑) is a standard mathematical operation used to aggregate values over all n cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289063</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Less Than or Equal To Operator">
      <data key="d0">Less Than or Equal To Operator</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The less than or equal to operator (≤) is a relational operator defining the upper bound of the constraint.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289064</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Greater Than or Equal To Operator">
      <data key="d0">Greater Than or Equal To Operator</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The greater than or equal to operator (≥) is a relational operator defining the lower bound of the constraint.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289064</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Equality Check">
      <data key="d0">Equality Check</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The equality check (=) is used within the indicator function (p_j = 1) to determine if a cell contains electrical steel.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289064</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-Core Electromagnetic Actuator">
      <data key="d0">C-Core Electromagnetic Actuator</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A C-core electromagnetic actuator is the device being designed, with its material distribution optimized.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289064</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action Sequence a">
      <data key="d0">Action Sequence a</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The action sequence a is an optimization variable that indirectly defines the material distribution vector p.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289064</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Penalty Mechanism">
      <data key="d0">Penalty Mechanism</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A penalty mechanism is used if constraints are violated, likely within the Genetic Algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22d9bde16f3beda6b21defdcbea26a2a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289065</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Softmax Function">
      <data key="d0">Softmax Function</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The softmax function is a mathematical operation that converts a vector of arbitrary real values into a probability distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;Format: latex; The softmax function, also known as a normalized exponential function, transforms real-value scores into a probability distribution where output probabilities are between 0 and 1 and sum to 1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289066</data>
    </node>
    <node id="a">
      <data key="d0">a</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The entity "a" primarily represents an action within various contexts, particularly in artificial intelligence, reinforcement learning, and mathematical equations. It signifies a specific action that an agent can undertake from a given state, often denoted as 's', or more generally, a placeholder for an action at a specific time step, such as a_t.

More broadly, "a" can denote a generic action selected from the action space, which encompasses all possible actions an agent can perform. Within a design domain, the symbol 'a' specifically refers to an action taken by an agent that impacts the material distribution. In mathematical equations, 'a' functions as a variable or a constant. This variable often appears in the context of a Q-function, where it represents a specific action whose value is being evaluated.&lt;SEP&gt;The variable 'a' typically represents an action in a reinforcement learning context or a similar sequential decision-making process.&lt;SEP&gt;a represents the action in the context of reinforcement learning.&lt;SEP&gt;The variable 'a' represents an action that can be taken.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb28e5c877704a09cf7eb4cbeb6522fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9242b5eeb7a8558a1b50bf53c2ccacba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58674fc78b8e0f9b48c3a573d49dd684&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85055739dbffbeba952630d321118334&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-742edca401668538e3a7a9b93ec14162&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be1e8d82c79e97d6340503568a2610b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3c16dd3211cf28f9ec77da01f786ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293087</data>
    </node>
    <node id="s">
      <data key="d0">s</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The symbol or variable 's' consistently represents a "state" within various environments and contexts, particularly in the realm of artificial intelligence and optimization. In the context of Topology Optimization, 's' specifically denotes a state referring to the current material distribution within a design domain. More broadly, 's' signifies a particular state in an environment with which an agent interacts, encompassing configurations or observations. It serves as a placeholder for state s_t and represents the current state of the environment, which an agent observes. This can include parameters or operational conditions of an actuator within an environment. The variable 's' is frequently encountered in mathematical equations and is a common denotation for the state within reinforcement learning contexts or similar sequential decision-making processes, often appearing within Q-functions.&lt;SEP&gt;s represents the state in the context of reinforcement learning.&lt;SEP&gt;The variable 's' represents a state in a given system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9242b5eeb7a8558a1b50bf53c2ccacba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58674fc78b8e0f9b48c3a573d49dd684&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85055739dbffbeba952630d321118334&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-742edca401668538e3a7a9b93ec14162&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be1e8d82c79e97d6340503568a2610b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3c16dd3211cf28f9ec77da01f786ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c8e2c74dbd2fe78b4f64f43cd17e839</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293087</data>
    </node>
    <node id="theta">
      <data key="d0">theta</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The variable 'theta' represents the parameters of the policy function.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical variable, often representing a parameter vector in optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-620c2ed72be858c05e569ae1452705a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292710</data>
    </node>
    <node id="f(s, a, theta)">
      <data key="d0">f(s, a, theta)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">This notation represents a scalar 'preference' or 'score' for taking action 'a' in state 's', determined by a function parameterized by 'theta'.&lt;SEP&gt;f(s, a, theta) is a function that typically represents the score or utility of taking action 'a' in state 's' with parameters 'theta'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-367b8cf81a01ff1207d6410c92e1e959</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ai">
      <data key="d0">ai</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The variable 'ai' represents an arbitrary action from the set of all possible actions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289068</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exponentiation">
      <data key="d0">Exponentiation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Exponentiation is a mathematical operation that transforms raw scores into positive, unnormalized likelihoods within the policy equation.&lt;SEP&gt;The exponential function, $e^x$, applied to ensure positive components for probabilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289068</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Summation">
      <data key="d0">Summation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Summation, denoted by the symbol $\Sigma$, is a fundamental mathematical operation involving the addition of a sequence of terms. In various contexts, it serves several key purposes. Primarily, summation is utilized as the main mathematical operation within equations to represent an arithmetic series of rewards, specifically for calculating the total accumulated reward, often denoted as $G_t$. It is also implicitly involved in the calculation of return. Beyond reward accumulation, summation plays a crucial role in probability distributions, where it is used over all possible discrete actions to ensure that probabilities sum to unity. This function is further exemplified when a sum is taken over all possible actions $a_i$ in the action space, denoted as $\sum_{a_i}$. Additionally, summation is incorporated into the denominator of policy equations as a normalization factor. It is also employed to approximate functions like J(θ), where it involves the product of Q(s0, a) and p(a|s0) across different actions 'a'. An example includes a summation from j=1 to n of p_j=1.&lt;SEP&gt;Summation is a mathematical operation indicated by $\sum$, used to add a sequence of numbers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb28e5c877704a09cf7eb4cbeb6522fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f95cef6c8e603e27b162387295b8935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a062a30b9c7d57e6fe1cf2ad874832e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50e280b998ddabea3a4713c685bb83d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293131</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Division">
      <data key="d0">Division</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Division is a mathematical operation used to normalize the exponentiated preference of a specific action by the sum of exponentiated preferences over all actions, yielding a probability.&lt;SEP&gt;The final operation in the equation that normalizes the policy output to ensure it is a probability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Differentiability">
      <data key="d0">Differentiability</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Differentiability refers to the property of a function where its derivative exists at every point, crucial for gradient-based optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Actor Component">
      <data key="d0">Actor Component</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The actor component in Actor-Critic methods is responsible for determining the policy.&lt;SEP&gt;The Actor component of the A2C model is responsible for policy gradient, using state information and the Q-value estimate to produce action probabilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27909faa34224de8c011e982c98fcef6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Greedy Policies">
      <data key="d0">Greedy Policies</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Greedy policies always select the action with the highest estimated value, contrasting with stochastic policies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289070</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Differentiable Probability Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Differentiable Probability Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A differentiable probability distribution allows for continuous optimization using gradient descent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289070</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exploration vs. Exploitation">
      <data key="d0">Exploration vs. Exploitation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Exploration vs. Exploitation is a fundamental dilemma in Reinforcement Learning concerning discovering new actions versus using known optimal ones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289071</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PPO Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">PPO Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) algorithms are a type of Reinforcement Learning algorithm used in deep reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289071</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action Preferences">
      <data key="d0">Action Preferences</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Action preferences are scores or values indicating how desirable specific actions are in a given state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289071</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parameters Theta">
      <data key="d0">Parameters Theta</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Theta represents the parameters of the policy function, such as weights and biases of a neural network, adjusted during learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Partition Function">
      <data key="d0">Partition Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The partition function is the normalization constant in the denominator of the softmax equation, ensuring probabilities sum to one.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Proximal Policy Optimization">
      <data key="d0">Proximal Policy Optimization</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) is a Reinforcement Learning algorithm that uses a type of softmax policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Critic Component">
      <data key="d0">Critic Component</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The critic component in Actor-Critic methods evaluates the chosen actions or states.&lt;SEP&gt;The Critic component of the A2C model is responsible for value prediction, specifically estimating the Q-value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27909faa34224de8c011e982c98fcef6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epsilon-Greedy Methods">
      <data key="d0">Epsilon-Greedy Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Epsilon-Greedy methods are exploration strategies that combine greedy action selection with random exploration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b470eacdde6ba3189132ab26e9634120</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Space Grid">
      <data key="d0">Design Space Grid</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Design Space Grid is a large grid of white square cells with thin black outlines making up the 'DESIGN SPACE'. It extends across most of the image's width and vertically from just below the top boundary to near the bottom.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-57172dfb9b0c90234ff04b1a5519b71e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289074</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2D Coordinate System">
      <data key="d0">2D Coordinate System</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A 2D coordinate system is depicted in the bottom-left corner of the image, showing an x-axis (red arrow) and a y-axis (green arrow) with a blue sphere marking the origin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-57172dfb9b0c90234ff04b1a5519b71e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289074</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Controller Methodology">
      <data key="d0">Controller Methodology</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The controller methodology is applied to optimize the core's material distribution within the DESIGN SPACE of the C-core, alongside a Genetic Algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-57172dfb9b0c90234ff04b1a5519b71e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289075</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Material Distribution Optimization">
      <data key="d0">Material Distribution Optimization</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Material distribution optimization is the process within the DESIGN SPACE where materials like air or electrical steel are arranged to maximize the force on the ARMATURE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-57172dfb9b0c90234ff04b1a5519b71e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289075</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DESIGN SPACE">
      <data key="d0">DESIGN SPACE</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The DESIGN SPACE is the main grid area within the C-core discretized design, composed of 288 square cells where material distribution (air or electrical steel) is optimized.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-57172dfb9b0c90234ff04b1a5519b71e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289075</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Y-Axis">
      <data key="d0">Y-Axis</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Y-Axis is part of the coordinate system, indicated by a green arrow pointing upwards, defining the 2D design space.&lt;SEP&gt;The Y-axis runs vertically in the coordinate system, representing the other dimension of the 2D design space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-57172dfb9b0c90234ff04b1a5519b71e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26404cee26f7623e6d969e264821542f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289075</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Force Maximization">
      <data key="d0">Force Maximization</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Force maximization is the objective of the optimization process, specifically to maximize the force experienced by the ARMATURE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-57172dfb9b0c90234ff04b1a5519b71e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289075</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symmetrical Representation">
      <data key="d0">Symmetrical Representation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Symmetrical representation is a design choice (symmetry about the x-axis) utilized to reduce the computational load for the C-core design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-57172dfb9b0c90234ff04b1a5519b71e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289076</data>
    </node>
    <node id="J(θ)">
      <data key="d0">J(θ)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">J(θ) represents the performance measure of a policy, quantifying its effectiveness as a function of its parameters θ, aiming to be maximized by Policy Gradient algorithms.&lt;SEP&gt;J(θ) represents a mathematical function or objective, often used in optimization problems, and is approximated by a summation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb28e5c877704a09cf7eb4cbeb6522fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a062a30b9c7d57e6fe1cf2ad874832e7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="G(t)">
      <data key="d0">G(t)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">G(t) represents the total accumulated reward from step t until the end of an episode, also referred to as 'return', which serves as a measure of cumulative reward.&lt;SEP&gt;G(t) is a mathematical representation, likely a function of time or another variable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb28e5c877704a09cf7eb4cbeb6522fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a062a30b9c7d57e6fe1cf2ad874832e7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q(s0, a)">
      <data key="d0">Q(s0, a)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Q(s0, a) is the Q-value function, representing the expected cumulative future reward of taking action a in the initial state s0 and subsequently following the policy.&lt;SEP&gt;Q(s0, a) represents a value function or quality function, dependent on state 's0' and action 'a', typically seen in reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb28e5c877704a09cf7eb4cbeb6522fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a062a30b9c7d57e6fe1cf2ad874832e7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="p(a|s0)">
      <data key="d0">p(a|s0)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">p(a|s0) is the probability of selecting action a given the initial state s0, directly derived from the policy π parameterized by θ.&lt;SEP&gt;p(a|s0) represents the probability of taking action 'a' given state 's0', often seen in policy functions in reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb28e5c877704a09cf7eb4cbeb6522fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a062a30b9c7d57e6fe1cf2ad874832e7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="θ">
      <data key="d0">θ</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">θ (theta) represents parameters, which could be weights of a neural network defining a policy in Policy Gradient, or parameters of a Q-network approximating the Q-function in Q-Learning.&lt;SEP&gt;θ represents the parameters (weights) associated with the neural network of the policy (π), which are trainable variables optimized by the Policy Gradient algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb28e5c877704a09cf7eb4cbeb6522fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-808c974f7871ba6b7b5d18f72afc85a7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289078</data>
    </node>
    <node id="s0">
      <data key="d0">s0</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">s0 is the initial state of an episode or a specific starting state from which performance is being evaluated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb28e5c877704a09cf7eb4cbeb6522fe</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289078</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Approximation">
      <data key="d0">Approximation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The approximation symbol (≈) implies that the exact value of J(θ) is being approximated by the expression on the right.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb28e5c877704a09cf7eb4cbeb6522fe</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289079</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Values (Q(s, a))">
      <data key="d0">Q-Values (Q(s, a))</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Q-Values (Q(s, a)) represent the expected cumulative future reward of taking action a in state s and subsequently following the policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb28e5c877704a09cf7eb4cbeb6522fe</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289079</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State-Action Value Function">
      <data key="d0">State-Action Value Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The state-action value function represents the expected cumulative future reward of taking a specific action in a given state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb28e5c877704a09cf7eb4cbeb6522fe</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289079</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stator Back Iron (x1)">
      <data key="d0">Stator Back Iron (x1)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stator Back Iron (x1) is a specific component or dimension of the IPM motor with a design range from 5 mm to 35 mm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-997630dd5ed7d24f34fabba54a0e27c6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289080</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stator Tooth Width (x2)">
      <data key="d0">Stator Tooth Width (x2)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stator Tooth Width (x2) is a specific component or dimension of the IPM motor with a design range from 5 mm to 12 mm, implying critical importance or tighter design constraints.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-997630dd5ed7d24f34fabba54a0e27c6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289081</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnet Inset Depth (x3)">
      <data key="d0">Magnet Inset Depth (x3)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnet Inset Depth (x3) is a specific component or dimension of the IPM motor with a design range from 5 mm to 25 mm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-997630dd5ed7d24f34fabba54a0e27c6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289081</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnet Thickness (x4)">
      <data key="d0">Magnet Thickness (x4)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnet Thickness (x4) is a specific component or dimension of the IPM motor with the widest design range from 1 mm to 50 mm, indicating significant variability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-997630dd5ed7d24f34fabba54a0e27c6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289081</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnet Width (x5)">
      <data key="d0">Magnet Width (x5)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnet Width (x5) is a specific component or dimension of the IPM motor with a design range from 20 mm to 50 mm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-997630dd5ed7d24f34fabba54a0e27c6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289081</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DL Estimators">
      <data key="d0">DL Estimators</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">DL (Deep Learning) Estimators are generalized methods potentially used in conjunction with the IPM motor parameters, especially concerning 'arbitrary input' for ML networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-997630dd5ed7d24f34fabba54a0e27c6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289082</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Simulations">
      <data key="d0">Simulations</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Simulations are performed using a partial (one pole) model of the IPM motor to reduce computation time, utilizing the parameters listed in Table 2.3.</data>
