      <data key="d0">Section 4.5.1</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific section in the surrounding text that defines Markov Decision Processes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ad46225ae0d5f3835a9bda1ed8158d48</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289096</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Activation Values">
      <data key="d0">Activation Values</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Activation Values are represented by the varying shades of cyan in the output map, indicating features learned by the filter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d59e50443efd19bae755d63382e315bd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289097</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs)">
      <data key="d0">Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of artificial neural network commonly applied to visual imagery.&lt;SEP&gt;A class of deep neural networks commonly applied to visual imagery, where the equation is particularly relevant for quantifying uncertainty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d59e50443efd19bae755d63382e315bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289097</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.12">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.12</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.12 is a schematic diagram illustrating a state transition in a Markov Decision Process (MDP). Its caption is "State Transition $( s _ { t } , a _ { t } , s _ { t + 1 } )$".</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e6ab3c95a34435be519e28c9335ef7bd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289097</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Current State (S_t)">
      <data key="d0">Current State (S_t)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Current State (S_t) represents the state at time 't' in a Markov Decision Process, depicted as a large circle in the diagram.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e6ab3c95a34435be519e28c9335ef7bd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289099</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Subsequent States (S_t+1)">
      <data key="d0">Subsequent States (S_t+1)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Subsequent States (S_t+1) refer to the possible next states at time 't+1' in an MDP, specifically S^1_{t+1}, S^2_{t+1}, and S^3_{t+1}, visualized as target circles after an action.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e6ab3c95a34435be519e28c9335ef7bd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289099</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action (a_t)">
      <data key="d0">Action (a_t)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Action (a_t) represents the choices made at time 't' in a Markov Decision Process, which lead to a transition from the current state to a subsequent state. Specific actions mentioned are a^1_t, a^2_t, and a^3_t.&lt;SEP&gt;The decision made by the agent at time step 't' while in state s_t, denoted as a_t.&lt;SEP&gt;Action (a_t) refers to the specific step or decision taken by an agent at time t.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6ab3c95a34435be519e28c9335ef7bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8823695e17798553515e243bdbfda2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292784</data>
    </node>
    <node id="S^1_t+1">
      <data key="d0">S^1_t+1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">S^1_t+1 is one of the three possible subsequent states, reached by taking action a^1_t from Current State (S_t).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e6ab3c95a34435be519e28c9335ef7bd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289100</data>
    </node>
    <node id="S^2_t+1">
      <data key="d0">S^2_t+1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">S^2_t+1 is one of the three possible subsequent states, reached by taking action a^2_t from Current State (S_t).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e6ab3c95a34435be519e28c9335ef7bd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289100</data>
    </node>
    <node id="S^3_t+1">
      <data key="d0">S^3_t+1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">S^3_t+1 is one of the three possible subsequent states, reached by taking action a^3_t from Current State (S_t).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e6ab3c95a34435be519e28c9335ef7bd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289100</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Current State S_t">
      <data key="d0">Current State S_t</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Current State S_t represents the state at time 't' in a Markov Decision Process, illustrated as a large circle in the diagram.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e6ab3c95a34435be519e28c9335ef7bd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289101</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Next State S^1_{t+1}">
      <data key="d0">Next State S^1_{t+1}</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Next State S^1_{t+1} represents one of three possible subsequent states at time 't+1' in a Markov Decision Process, resulting from Action a^1_t.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e6ab3c95a34435be519e28c9335ef7bd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289101</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Next State S^2_{t+1}">
      <data key="d0">Next State S^2_{t+1}</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Next State S^2_{t+1} represents one of three possible subsequent states at time 't+1' in a Markov Decision Process, resulting from Action a^2_t.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e6ab3c95a34435be519e28c9335ef7bd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289101</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Next State S^3_{t+1}">
      <data key="d0">Next State S^3_{t+1}</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Next State S^3_{t+1} represents one of three possible subsequent states at time 't+1' in a Markov Decision Process, resulting from Action a^3_t.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e6ab3c95a34435be519e28c9335ef7bd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289102</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action a^1_t">
      <data key="d0">Action a^1_t</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Action a^1_t is the first action taken from Current State S_t, leading to Next State S^1_{t+1}.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e6ab3c95a34435be519e28c9335ef7bd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289102</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action a^2_t">
      <data key="d0">Action a^2_t</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Action a^2_t is the second action taken from Current State S_t, leading to Next State S^2_{t+1}.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e6ab3c95a34435be519e28c9335ef7bd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289102</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action a^3_t">
      <data key="d0">Action a^3_t</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Action a^3_t is the third action taken from Current State S_t, leading to Next State S^3_{t+1}.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e6ab3c95a34435be519e28c9335ef7bd</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289102</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epsilon-Greedy Policy">
      <data key="d0">Epsilon-Greedy Policy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The epsilon-greedy policy is an action selection strategy in reinforcement learning that balances exploration and exploitation.&lt;SEP&gt;The Epsilon-Greedy Policy is a strategy for action selection (exploration vs. exploitation) in reinforcement learning, where an agent chooses the best action with probability (1 - epsilon) and a random action with probability epsilon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0604f5005a60d070243b26410ba10b20</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293108</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action Selection Policy">
      <data key="d0">Action Selection Policy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An action selection policy defines how an agent chooses actions in an environment to achieve its goals.&lt;SEP&gt;An action selection policy dictates how an agent chooses an action at a given time step.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289103</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Time Step">
      <data key="d0">Time Step</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A discrete point in time in a sequential decision-making process, denoted as $t$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289103</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Network Weights">
      <data key="d0">Q-Network Weights</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Parameters of the neural network that approximates the Q-function, denoted as $w_{(beh)}$ or $w_{beh}$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discrete Probability Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Discrete Probability Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Discrete probability distribution describes the probability of the action selection being based on two mutually exclusive probabilistic outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Argmax Operator">
      <data key="d0">Argmax Operator</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The argmax operator ($\\\\operatorname{argmax}_a$) is a mathematical operator that returns the argument (action 'a') that maximizes the subsequent expression (Q(s, a)).&lt;SEP&gt;A fundamental optimization operator that identifies the argument (an action $a$) that maximizes a given function.&lt;SEP&gt;An operator that returns the argument that produces the maximum value of a given function, used to find the action $a$ that yields the highest estimated Q-value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58674fc78b8e0f9b48c3a573d49dd684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weight Parameter (w)">
      <data key="d0">Weight Parameter (w)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The weight parameter 'w' represents the parameters of the neural network that approximates the Q-function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289107</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epsilon (ε)">
      <data key="d0">Epsilon (ε)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Epsilon is a scalar parameter between 0 and 1 that represents the probability of choosing a random action, controlling the trade-off between exploration and exploitation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289107</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Experience Replay Buffer">
      <data key="d0">Experience Replay Buffer</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">An experience replay buffer is a storage mechanism for past experiences in reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289107</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Self-Driving Cars">
      <data key="d0">Self-Driving Cars</data>
      <data key="d1">application</data>
      <data key="d2">Self-driving cars are an application area for autonomous systems and reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289108</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Industrial Automation">
      <data key="d0">Industrial Automation</data>
      <data key="d1">application</data>
      <data key="d2">Industrial automation is an application area where deterministic policies can provide definitive actions for given perceived states.&lt;SEP&gt;Industrial automation is an application area for autonomous systems and reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289108</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Game AI">
      <data key="d0">Game AI</data>
      <data key="d1">application</data>
      <data key="d2">Game AI involves developing agents that can play games and discover optimal strategies using reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-023a66b0b7b2235f3cc11df422f013f1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289108</data>
    </node>
    <node id="False-Color Heat Map">
      <data key="d0">False-Color Heat Map</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The False-Color Heat Map is a visual representation method used to display data, in this case, error distribution, by mapping data values to colors.</data>
