    </node>
    <node id="Red-Framed Black Square">
      <data key="d0">Red-Framed Black Square</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">This represents an 'agent' or a significant 'material element' whose position changes across the grids in Figure 5.4. Its movement influences the reward received.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5f031035d088ffa5bcc3e370fe22c958</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289116</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reward Metric">
      <data key="d0">Reward Metric</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The Reward Metric is a quantitative value indicating the outcome of a particular step, displayed for each grid in Figure 5.4. It drastically drops from +69.23 to -640.68.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5f031035d088ffa5bcc3e370fe22c958</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289117</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Steps">
      <data key="d0">Steps</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Steps denote the progression through the sequential states, with numbers 12, 13, and 14 represented in the grids (a), (b), and (c) respectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5f031035d088ffa5bcc3e370fe22c958</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289117</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reinforcement Learning Environment">
      <data key="d0">Reinforcement Learning Environment</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Reinforcement Learning environment is a setting within which an agent acts, observes outcomes, and learns. The image provides a visual representation of such an environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5f031035d088ffa5bcc3e370fe22c958</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289117</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coordinate System Icon">
      <data key="d0">Coordinate System Icon</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A coordinate system icon in the bottom-left corner of each sub-image, featuring red for the X-axis, green for the Y-axis, and blue for the Z-axis, suggesting a technical or engineering context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5f031035d088ffa5bcc3e370fe22c958</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immediate Reward ($R_k$)">
      <data key="d0">Immediate Reward ($R_k$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Immediate Reward ($R_k$) is a scalar value received by an agent at time step $k$ that guides its learning process, representing the instantaneous performance or outcome of an action.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Time Step ($t$)">
      <data key="d0">Time Step ($t$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific Time Step ($t$) within an episode, from which the return ($G_t$) is calculated based on subsequent rewards.&lt;SEP&gt;The Time Step ($t$) denotes the current point in time from which the Return ($G_t$) is being calculated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289119</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Final Time Step ($N$)">
      <data key="d0">Final Time Step ($N$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Final Time Step ($N$) represents the end of an episode, indicating a finite horizon for the summation of rewards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289119</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Summation ($\sum$)">
      <data key="d0">Summation ($\sum$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Summation is the primary mathematical operation used in the equation to aggregate a sequence of rewards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289119</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Field Distribution Values">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Field Distribution Values</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnetic Field Distribution Values are a refined measure of reward in the context of a C-core design problem, used to guide an agent's learning instead of just force experienced by the armature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289119</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Value Functions ($Q(s,a)$ or $V(s)$)">
      <data key="d0">Value Functions ($Q(s,a)$ or $V(s)$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Value Functions are formalizations that build upon the concept of return to estimate the expected cumulative reward from a given state or state-action pair, often incorporating a discount factor for infinite-horizon problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289120</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal C-core Geometry">
      <data key="d0">Optimal C-core Geometry</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Optimal C-core Geometry is the desired outcome of the C-core design problem, achieved by an RL agent maximizing its expected return.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289120</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Return in Markov Decision Process and Reinforcement Learning">
      <data key="d0">Return in Markov Decision Process and Reinforcement Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Return in MDP and RL refers to the concept mathematically represented as a finite sum of immediate rewards an agent receives over a specific time horizon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289120</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$$ G _ { t } \doteq R _ { t + 1 } + R _ { t + 2 } + . . . + R _ { N } $$">
      <data key="d0">$$ G _ { t } \doteq R _ { t + 1 } + R _ { t + 2 } + . . . + R _ { N } $$</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">This is a mathematical equation in LaTeX format that defines the 'Return' ($G_t$) as the sum of subsequent rewards ($R_{t+1}$ to $R_N$).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289121</data>
    </node>
    <node id="R_k">
      <data key="d0">R_k</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">R_k denotes the 'immediate reward' received at time step k, guiding an agent's learning to maximize performance. Examples include 'force experienced by the armature' or 'magnetic field distribution values in the C-core armature'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289121</data>
    </node>
    <node id="t">
      <data key="d0">t</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">t represents a specific time step within an episode, from which the return G_t is calculated using rewards occurring after this time step.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289121</data>
    </node>
    <node id="N">
      <data key="d0">N</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">N represents the final time step or the end of an episode, at which point the summation for the return terminates, indicating a finite horizon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289121</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Finite-Horizon Episodic Task">
      <data key="d0">Finite-Horizon Episodic Task</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Finite-Horizon Episodic Task is a type of learning problem where the sequence of actions and rewards has a clear end point or a finite number of steps.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b95c8542f91fd870d1bfec5dbdecfb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289121</data>
    </node>
