      <data key="d5">1760289139</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Solution">
      <data key="d0">Solution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Solution refers to the outcome obtained after processing the Electromagnetic Model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce41e8cc5ef38832503f503eebaccf56</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289139</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Analysing Result">
      <data key="d0">Analysing Result</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Analysing Result is the process of evaluating the generated solution in the FEM workflow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce41e8cc5ef38832503f503eebaccf56</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289140</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Refine Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Refine Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Refine Analysis is a step in the FEM process aimed at improving the analysis based on initial results.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce41e8cc5ef38832503f503eebaccf56</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289140</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Improve Model">
      <data key="d0">Improve Model</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Improve Model is a step in the FEM process where the Electromagnetic Model is enhanced based on refined analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce41e8cc5ef38832503f503eebaccf56</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289141</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Improvement">
      <data key="d0">Design Improvement</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Design Improvement refers to enhancements made to a design based on the analysis of results from the FEM.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce41e8cc5ef38832503f503eebaccf56</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289141</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Change Problem Domain">
      <data key="d0">Change Problem Domain</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Change Problem Domain is a step where the original Physical Problem is re-evaluated or redefined.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce41e8cc5ef38832503f503eebaccf56</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289141</data>
    </node>
    <node id="System Equations">
      <data key="d0">System Equations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">System Equations are mathematical formulations used in FEM to represent the behavior of the system being analyzed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce41e8cc5ef38832503f503eebaccf56</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289141</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electric Motors and Generators">
      <data key="d0">Electric Motors and Generators</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Electric Motors and Generators are examples of artifacts whose design and analysis benefit from the Finite Element Method.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce41e8cc5ef38832503f503eebaccf56</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289142</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cartesian Coordinate System">
      <data key="d0">Cartesian Coordinate System</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Cartesian Coordinate System is indicated by a legend with a green 'Y' arrow and a red 'X' arrow, organizing the layout of the 2D grid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289143</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COIL Blocks">
      <data key="d0">COIL Blocks</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">COIL Blocks are two vertical red blocks, one on the far left and another to the right, spanning from row 1 to row 5, likely indicating fixed components or regions where coils are placed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289143</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INITIAL POSITION Label">
      <data key="d0">INITIAL POSITION Label</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The INITIAL POSITION label is horizontally aligned and centered above a single white grid cell, suggesting a specific starting point for an agent or a design process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gray L-shaped Structure">
      <data key="d0">Gray L-shaped Structure</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Gray L-shaped Structure represents material distribution, forming an initial design or a snapshot of a design during optimization, located within the design domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Field (|B|)">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Field (|B|)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Magnetic Field (|B|) is implied to be measured or influenced in the region of the Air Gap, consistent with an electromagnetic problem.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Page 98">
      <data key="d0">Page 98</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Page 98 is the location in the surrounding content that refers to different state representations for discrete design domains for TO and mentions Figure 4.14.&lt;SEP&gt;Page 98 contains a discussion about the discrete design domain state representation, relevant to Figure 4.17.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1474f4155ebb5657d8ca7f3c0d29b49</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289145</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Red Blocks">
      <data key="d0">Red Blocks</data>
      <data key="d1">object</data>
      <data key="d2">Two prominent vertical Red Blocks labeled "COIL" indicate fixed components or regions where coils are placed within the design domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289145</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gray Cells">
      <data key="d0">Gray Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">object</data>
      <data key="d2">Gray Cells represent material distribution, showing an initial design or a snapshot of a design during optimization within the grid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289145</data>
    </node>
    <node id="L-shaped Structure">
      <data key="d0">L-shaped Structure</data>
      <data key="d1">object</data>
      <data key="d2">The L-shaped Structure is formed by gray cells, representing a configuration of material distribution within the design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289145</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Markov Decision Processes">
      <data key="d0">Markov Decision Processes</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A Markov Decision Process (MDP) is a mathematical framework for modeling decision-making in situations where outcomes are partly random and partly under the control of a decision-maker.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289146</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State Representations for Discrete Design Domains">
      <data key="d0">State Representations for Discrete Design Domains</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">State Representations for Discrete Design Domains are different ways of visually or numerically representing the design space for Topology Optimization, such as the grid shown.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c8eeb9e91e5bd71f6909085d5d4a52e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289146</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.2">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.2</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.2 is a pie chart titled "Global Electricity consumption (Lei et al. 2017)" illustrating how electricity is used across various sectors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7dd7d2a24a4f09809aeb5eb6306158ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289146</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electric Machine Consumption">
      <data key="d0">Electric Machine Consumption</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Electric Machine Consumption accounts for 43% of the total global electricity consumption, represented by the largest slice in the pie chart.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7dd7d2a24a4f09809aeb5eb6306158ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289147</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lighting Consumption">
      <data key="d0">Lighting Consumption</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Lighting Consumption is a category of global electricity consumption, accounting for 18% of the total.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7dd7d2a24a4f09809aeb5eb6306158ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289147</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electronics Consumption">
      <data key="d0">Electronics Consumption</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Electronics Consumption is a category of global electricity consumption, accounting for 9% of the total.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7dd7d2a24a4f09809aeb5eb6306158ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289147</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electrolysis Consumption">
      <data key="d0">Electrolysis Consumption</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Electrolysis Consumption is a category of global electricity consumption, accounting for 8% of the total.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7dd7d2a24a4f09809aeb5eb6306158ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heat Consumption">
      <data key="d0">Heat Consumption</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Heat Consumption is a category of global electricity consumption, accounting for 18% of the total.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7dd7d2a24a4f09809aeb5eb6306158ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stand-By Consumption">
      <data key="d0">Stand-By Consumption</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stand-By Consumption is the smallest category of global electricity consumption, accounting for 3% of the total.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7dd7d2a24a4f09809aeb5eb6306158ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-core Design">
      <data key="d0">C-core Design</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The C-core Design is the subject of a sequential optimization process depicted in Figure 5.25.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1f2486e70169df5ba045508cbd9276f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289149</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lime Green Material">
      <data key="d0">Lime Green Material</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Lime Green Material represents the evolving C-core geometry within the diagrams.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1f2486e70169df5ba045508cbd9276f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289151</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dark Teal or Blue Vertical Bar">
      <data key="d0">Dark Teal or Blue Vertical Bar</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Dark Teal or Blue Vertical Bar represents a fixed component of the magnetic circuit not subject to optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1f2486e70169df5ba045508cbd9276f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289151</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small, Bright Yellow Square">
      <data key="d0">Small, Bright Yellow Square</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Small, Bright Yellow Square indicates the agent's current focus or action point in the topology optimization process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1f2486e70169df5ba045508cbd9276f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289151</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Circuit">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Circuit</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Magnetic Circuit consists of components, including the C-core and a fixed dark teal or blue vertical bar.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1f2486e70169df5ba045508cbd9276f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289152</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Amp-Turns">
      <data key="d0">Amp-Turns</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Amp-Turns is a unit of magnetomotive force, calculated from coil current and coil turns, and is mentioned in the context of saturation effect.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1f2486e70169df5ba045508cbd9276f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289152</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-Core Design">
      <data key="d0">C-Core Design</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The C-Core Design is the subject of the optimization process, gradually taking shape as lime green material in the diagrams.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1f2486e70169df5ba045508cbd9276f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289153</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Excitation Geometry">
      <data key="d0">Excitation Geometry</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Excitation geometry refers to the optimal geometric configuration for specific excitation conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1f2486e70169df5ba045508cbd9276f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289153</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Saturation Effect">
      <data key="d0">Saturation Effect</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Saturation Effect becomes dominant for 500 Amp-Turns and beyond, implying the agent learns to manage this material non-linearity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1f2486e70169df5ba045508cbd9276f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289153</data>
    </node>
    <node id="0.75 A">
      <data key="d0">0.75 A</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">0.75 A is the current specified for the excitation of the particular sequence shown in the image.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1f2486e70169df5ba045508cbd9276f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289153</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Variables">
      <data key="d0">Variables</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Variables are symbols used in mathematical equations to represent quantities that can change.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Euler's Number">
      <data key="d0">Euler's Number</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Euler's number, denoted by 'e', is the base of the natural logarithm, approximately 2.71828.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mathematical Operations">
      <data key="d0">Mathematical Operations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mathematical Operations include expected value (E), addition (+), and conditional probability.&lt;SEP&gt;Mathematical operations like exponentiation, summation, and division used in the equation to convert scores into a probability distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Gradient Methods">
      <data key="d0">Policy Gradient Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Policy Gradient Methods are a family of Reinforcement Learning algorithms that directly learn policy parameters, contrasted with Q-Learning.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy gradient methods are a category of Reinforcement Learning algorithms to which the described equation and stochastic policy are fundamental.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-808c974f7871ba6b7b5d18f72afc85a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289155</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action-Value Function (f(s,a))">
      <data key="d0">Action-Value Function (f(s,a))</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An action-value function ($f(\\boldsymbol{s}, a)$) is a real-valued score function, often generated by a neural network, that represents the preference for an action 'a' given a state 's'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9012606caf5dec3e73821be7188dc6cf</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289155</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 2.13">
      <data key="d0">Figure 2.13</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 2.13 is a multi-panel visual aid demonstrating the results of Bayesian inference in the context of magnetic field prediction, illustrating error distribution and uncertainty maps.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92cc2b02ae3a119d92f0d1a7f6617885</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289156</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Error Distribution Map">
      <data key="d0">Error Distribution Map</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Error Distribution Map, presented in panel (a) of Figure 2.13, visually represents the error in magnetic field prediction, using colors to indicate error levels in an electromagnetic device.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92cc2b02ae3a119d92f0d1a7f6617885</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289156</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Normalized Uncertainty Map from Bayesian Inference">
      <data key="d0">Normalized Uncertainty Map from Bayesian Inference</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Normalized Uncertainty Map, presented in panel (b) of Figure 2.13, displays uncertainty levels from Bayesian inference applied to magnetic field prediction, with colors indicating uncertainty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92cc2b02ae3a119d92f0d1a7f6617885</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289157</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Region of Uncertainty Map with High Uncertainty">
      <data key="d0">Region of Uncertainty Map with High Uncertainty</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Region of Uncertainty Map with High Uncertainty, presented in panel (c) of Figure 2.13, shows magnified sections corresponding to areas of elevated uncertainty from panel (b).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92cc2b02ae3a119d92f0d1a7f6617885</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289157</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Error">
      <data key="d0">Error</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Error represents the difference between prediction and ground truth, visually depicted in panel (a) of Figure 2.13.&lt;SEP&gt;Error is a concept represented by the MSE formula.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92cc2b02ae3a119d92f0d1a7f6617885&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-feffff69e0f56312f2846d9f57d0a4f7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292889</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Uncertainty">
      <data key="d0">Uncertainty</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Uncertainty refers to the confidence in prediction results, mapped in panels (b) and (c) of Figure 2.13, with high values indicating unreliability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92cc2b02ae3a119d92f0d1a7f6617885</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289157</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B_airgap">
      <data key="d0">B_airgap</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">B_airgap represents the magnetic flux density in the airgap, serving as the state space in the left panel.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7b76c5d19e7bcbbd5f11b71588e066f2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289159</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B Field Strength">
      <data key="d0">B Field Strength</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The magnitude of the magnetic field, often represented by |B|, used as a state space in simulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7b76c5d19e7bcbbd5f11b71588e066f2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289160</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contour Plot">
      <data key="d0">Contour Plot</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A visual representation of a simulated electromagnetic field, showing the magnetic flux density using a color gradient.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7b76c5d19e7bcbbd5f11b71588e066f2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289160</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topography Optimization">
      <data key="d0">Topography Optimization</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An optimization method that uses state representation, possibly in conjunction with RL or GA.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7b76c5d19e7bcbbd5f11b71588e066f2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289161</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagram (a)">
      <data key="d0">Diagram (a)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagram (a) illustrates Size Optimization with a C-shaped 2D object, showing parameters 'x1' and 'x2' for adjustment.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagram (a) is part of Figure 5.20, displaying a cross-sectional view of a conventional C-core design with key dimensions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66f6813286ff2495396939f94deff0c3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289162</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagram (b)">
      <data key="d0">Diagram (b)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagram (b) illustrates Shape Optimization using the C-shaped object, focusing on modifications to its internal and external corners with dashed lines.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagram (b) is part of Figure 5.20, depicting a simulation domain representing only half of the C-core actuator geometry due to symmetry, showing a meshed structure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66f6813286ff2495396939f94deff0c3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289162</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symmetry">
      <data key="d0">Symmetry</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Symmetry is a property of the C-core actuator geometry that allows for simulating only half of the design, reducing computational effort.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289163</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Simulation Calls">
      <data key="d0">Simulation Calls</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Simulation calls refer to the number of times simulations are run, relevant for evaluating computational efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Simulation Domain">
      <data key="d0">Simulation Domain</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The simulation domain is a meshed structure representing half of the C-core actuator geometry, used for computational analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FEA Mesh">
      <data key="d0">FEA Mesh</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">FEA Mesh is a grid structure used in Finite Element Analysis to discretize the simulation domain for numerical computation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289164</data>
    </node>
    <node id="6 mm Thickness">
      <data key="d0">6 mm Thickness</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The 6 mm thickness refers to a specific dimensional annotation for the top horizontal arm of the C-core.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289165</data>
    </node>
    <node id="6 mm Air Gap">
      <data key="d0">6 mm Air Gap</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The 6 mm air gap is the horizontal distance between the right vertical arm of the C-core and the armature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289165</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1 mm Air Gap">
      <data key="d0">1 mm Air Gap</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The 1 mm air gap is the vertical distance between the horizontal part of the C-core and the armature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289166</data>
    </node>
    <node id="30 mm Height">
      <data key="d0">30 mm Height</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The 30 mm height is the overall vertical dimension of the armature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289166</data>
    </node>
    <node id="18 mm Inner Space Height">
      <data key="d0">18 mm Inner Space Height</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The 18 mm inner space height is the vertical dimension of the inner space of the C-core.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289166</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3 mm Width">
      <data key="d0">3 mm Width</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The 3 mm width is likely the dimension along the x-axis for the C-core's horizontal base.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289167</data>
    </node>
    <node id="F:\From_D_drive\Code\research_extender\output\Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis\auto\images\00ea2586e9f48932b75aa18656e67fc50e08b7b49148815205bb4b8c043b2de8.jpg">
      <data key="d0">F:\From_D_drive\Code\research_extender\output\Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis\auto\images\00ea2586e9f48932b75aa18656e67fc50e08b7b49148815205bb4b8c043b2de8.jpg</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The file path for the image content analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3b83022ca44128f92a414d02c9b7fc66</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289167</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.17">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.17</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.17 presents a scenario for a negative Reward (R_t) in a reinforcement learning context for topology optimization (TO).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1474f4155ebb5657d8ca7f3c0d29b49</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289167</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grid-Based Diagrams">
      <data key="d0">Grid-Based Diagrams</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">These diagrams, labeled (a), (b), and (c), visualize steps in a reinforcement learning scenario, specifically for topology optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1474f4155ebb5657d8ca7f3c0d29b49</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289168</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UP Action">
      <data key="d0">UP Action</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The UP action is a specific action taken by the agent, indicated by arrows, causing the agent's position to move upwards in the grid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1474f4155ebb5657d8ca7f3c0d29b49</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289168</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discrete Design Domain State Representation">
      <data key="d0">Discrete Design Domain State Representation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Discrete design domain state representation is the method used to visualize the states within the topology optimization process, discussed on page 98.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1474f4155ebb5657d8ca7f3c0d29b49</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289168</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Page 100">
      <data key="d0">Page 100</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Page 100 mentions the concept of an agent moving through steps in an 'episodic task' and generating 'experience (s, a, s', r)' for TD-learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1474f4155ebb5657d8ca7f3c0d29b49</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289168</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reinforcement Learning Scenario">
      <data key="d0">Reinforcement Learning Scenario</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A reinforcement learning scenario is depicted with a sequence of grid-based diagrams for topology optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1474f4155ebb5657d8ca7f3c0d29b49</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289169</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Current Reward">
      <data key="d0">Current Reward</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The current reward is a metric displayed in the header of each diagram, with a constant value of 64.94 in this specific sequence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1474f4155ebb5657d8ca7f3c0d29b49</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289169</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Experience (s, a, s', r)">
      <data key="d0">Experience (s, a, s', r)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Experience (s, a, s', r) refers to the tuple of state, action, next state, and reward generated by the agent's interaction, used for TD-learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1474f4155ebb5657d8ca7f3c0d29b49</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289169</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Binary Classification">
      <data key="d0">Binary Classification</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of machine learning task where instances are divided into two distinct classes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74dead31275ed48a2a31d5ae16d58cb0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289170</data>
    </node>
    <node id="True Positives">
      <data key="d0">True Positives</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">True Positives (TP) represent instances where the model correctly predicts class 1 when the actual outcome is class 1. In the context, it means correctly identified high-efficiency motor operating points.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74dead31275ed48a2a31d5ae16d58cb0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289170</data>
    </node>
    <node id="True Negatives">
      <data key="d0">True Negatives</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">True Negatives (TN) represent instances where the model correctly predicts class 0 when the actual outcome is class 0. In the context, it means correctly identified low-efficiency motor operating points.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74dead31275ed48a2a31d5ae16d58cb0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289170</data>
    </node>
    <node id="False Positives">
      <data key="d0">False Positives</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">False Positives (FP) represent instances where the model incorrectly predicts class 1 when the actual outcome is class 0, also known as a Type I error. In the context, it means identifying a low-efficiency design as high-efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74dead31275ed48a2a31d5ae16d58cb0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289170</data>
    </node>
    <node id="False Negatives">
      <data key="d0">False Negatives</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">False Negatives (FN) represent instances where the model incorrectly predicts class 0 when the actual outcome is class 1, also known as a Type II error. In the context, it means identifying a high-efficiency design as low-efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74dead31275ed48a2a31d5ae16d58cb0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289171</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Classification Models">
      <data key="d0">Classification Models</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Classification models are used in machine learning to predict outcomes, specifically motor efficiency in this context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74dead31275ed48a2a31d5ae16d58cb0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289171</data>
    </node>
    <node id="85% Threshold">
      <data key="d0">85% Threshold</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The 85% threshold is the specific value used to partition efficiency values into two classes for classification.&lt;SEP&gt;The chosen value (85%) used to partition efficiency values into two classes for classification.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74dead31275ed48a2a31d5ae16d58cb0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Total Number Of Instances">
      <data key="d0">Total Number Of Instances</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The total number of instances represents the sum of True Positives, True Negatives, False Positives, and False Negatives, reflecting all evaluated points in an efficiency map.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74dead31275ed48a2a31d5ae16d58cb0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TP">
      <data key="d0">TP</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">TP, or True Positives, represents the number of instances where the model correctly predicts class 1 when the actual efficiency is greater than 85%.&lt;SEP&gt;TP stands for True Positives, representing cases where the model correctly identified the positive class.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a6871b523f5d81edec8c74e56e7c813&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74dead31275ed48a2a31d5ae16d58cb0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292681</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TN">
      <data key="d0">TN</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">TN, or True Negatives, represents the number of instances where the model correctly predicts class 0 when the actual efficiency is less than or equal to 85%.&lt;SEP&gt;TN stands for True Negatives, representing cases where the model correctly identified the negative class.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a6871b523f5d81edec8c74e56e7c813&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74dead31275ed48a2a31d5ae16d58cb0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292681</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FP">
      <data key="d0">FP</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">FP, or False Positives, represents the number of instances where the model incorrectly predicts class 1 when the actual efficiency is less than or equal to 85%.&lt;SEP&gt;FP stands for False Positives, representing cases where the model incorrectly identified a negative class as positive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a6871b523f5d81edec8c74e56e7c813&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74dead31275ed48a2a31d5ae16d58cb0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292681</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FN">
      <data key="d0">FN</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">FN, or False Negatives, represents the number of instances where the model incorrectly predicts class 0 when the actual efficiency is greater than 85%.&lt;SEP&gt;FN stands for False Negatives, representing cases where the model incorrectly identified a positive class as negative.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a6871b523f5d81edec8c74e56e7c813&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74dead31275ed48a2a31d5ae16d58cb0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292684</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Binary Classification Tasks">
      <data key="d0">Binary Classification Tasks</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Binary classification tasks involve categorizing data into one of two classes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74dead31275ed48a2a31d5ae16d58cb0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289174</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Type I Error">
      <data key="d0">Type I Error</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An error where the model incorrectly predicts a positive outcome when the actual outcome is negative, specifically when falsely predicting high efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;Type I error occurs when a model incorrectly identifies a low-efficiency design as high-efficiency, corresponding to a False Positive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74dead31275ed48a2a31d5ae16d58cb0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289174</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Type II Error">
      <data key="d0">Type II Error</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An error where the model incorrectly predicts a negative outcome when the actual outcome is positive, specifically when falsely predicting not high efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;Type II error occurs when a model incorrectly identifies a high-efficiency design as low-efficiency, corresponding to a False Negative.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74dead31275ed48a2a31d5ae16d58cb0</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289174</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Equation A(s, a) = Q(s, a) - V(s)">
      <data key="d0">Equation A(s, a) = Q(s, a) - V(s)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines the 'advantage function' in reinforcement learning, quantifying the advantage of performing a specific action in a given state relative to the expected value of that state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289174</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advantage Actor-Critic (A2C) Algorithm">
      <data key="d0">Advantage Actor-Critic (A2C) Algorithm</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A2C is a policy gradient method used in reinforcement learning that utilizes the advantage function to provide more stable and informative gradient signals for policy updates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289175</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Actor-Critic (AC) Method">
      <data key="d0">Actor-Critic (AC) Method</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">AC is a reinforcement learning method that A2C extends, involving an actor and a critic for policy learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289175</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action a">
      <data key="d0">Action a</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Action a represents a specific action available to the agent, a point in the action space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State s">
      <data key="d0">State s</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">State s represents a specific state in the environment, a point in the state space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action-Value Function Q(s, a)">
      <data key="d0">Action-Value Function Q(s, a)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Q(s, a) estimates the expected cumulative discounted future reward for taking action a in state s and then following the current policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State-Value Function V(s)">
      <data key="d0">State-Value Function V(s)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">V(s) estimates the expected cumulative discounted future reward from state s if actions are chosen according to the current policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Updates">
      <data key="d0">Policy Updates</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Policy updates are iterative adjustments to an agent's strategy based on learning signals, aiming to improve performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289177</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Gradient (PG) Methods">
      <data key="d0">Policy Gradient (PG) Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">PG methods are a class of reinforcement learning algorithms that optimize a policy directly by estimating the gradient of some performance measure with respect to the policy parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289177</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eight Actions">
      <data key="d0">Eight Actions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Eight actions refers to the number of actions an agent can choose from within a specific environment or application context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289177</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Actor-Critic Update Rule (eqn. 5.15)">
      <data key="d0">Actor-Critic Update Rule (eqn. 5.15)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Actor-Critic update rule is a mechanism for adjusting the actor's policy based on feedback from the critic.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289178</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-function">
      <data key="d0">Q-function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Q-function is an alternative name for the Action-Value Function Q(s, a), representing the expected reward for a state-action pair.&lt;SEP&gt;The Q-function, denoted as Q(S, A, w), is a core component of the equation, representing state-action value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-feffff69e0f56312f2846d9f57d0a4f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292891</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Current Policy">
      <data key="d0">Current Policy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Current Policy is a set of rules or preferences that dictate how an agent chooses actions in different states.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289179</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SeqTO-$vz$">
      <data key="d0">SeqTO-$vz$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">SeqTO-$vz$ is an application domain or context where the Advantage Actor-Critic (A2C) algorithm is applied.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289179</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Actor-Critic Update Rule">
      <data key="d0">Actor-Critic Update Rule</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Actor-Critic Update Rule (equation 5.15) is a specific method used in Actor-Critic algorithms to modify the policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="On-Policy Model">
      <data key="d0">On-Policy Model</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An On-Policy Model is a type of reinforcement learning model that updates the policy based on data generated by the current policy itself.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de032669e5f79b5807cd5cdbda09f710</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.33">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.33</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.33 illustrates optimal geometry and B distribution for an excitation scenario (0.25 A, 500 Turns) for various optimization methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="0.25 A">
      <data key="d0">0.25 A</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">0.25 A is the current specified in the excitation scenario for the C-core actuator.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289181</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Geometry Plot">
      <data key="d0">Geometry Plot</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A geometry plot is a grid-based representation showing the optimal C-core actuator geometry, depicting material presence (blue, orange) or absence (white).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289181</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Flux Density Distribution Plot">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Flux Density Distribution Plot</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A magnetic flux density distribution plot is a contour plot illustrating magnetic flux density inside and around the C-core actuator, with color representing density.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289181</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Resulting Metrics">
      <data key="d0">Resulting Metrics</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Resulting metrics are a textual display of quantitative results for each optimization method, including Force and FE Calls.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289181</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RL (Re-Train)">
      <data key="d0">RL (Re-Train)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">RL (Re-Train) refers to a retraining iteration of the Reinforcement Learning method for optimization.&lt;SEP&gt;RL (Re-Train) is an optimization algorithm described in the image as a variant of A2C based on SeqTO-v2.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2a5c42907185fecc94ee85f1b33ec380&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289182</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Flux Density (B) Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Flux Density (B) Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnetic Flux Density (B) Distribution is a contour plot illustrating the magnetic flux density within and around a C-core actuator.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289182</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sequential Topology Optimization">
      <data key="d0">Sequential Topology Optimization</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Sequential Topology Optimization is an environment or method for generating optimal C-core actuator designs, with versions v1 and v2.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289183</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimization Method">
      <data key="d0">Optimization Method</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An optimization method is an algorithm used to find the best possible design or solution for a given problem, such as C-core actuator design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289183</data>
    </node>
    <node id="General Algorithmic Search">
      <data key="d0">General Algorithmic Search</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">General Algorithmic Search (GA) is an optimization method used for generating optimal C-core actuator designs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289183</data>
    </node>
    <node id="A2C Reinforcement Learning">
      <data key="d0">A2C Reinforcement Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A2C Reinforcement Learning is an optimization method used for generating optimal C-core actuator designs, based on SeqTO-v2.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c8bce8440f0bbb7239189a9a4010d7a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289183</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bayes' Theorem">
      <data key="d0">Bayes' Theorem</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Bayes' Theorem is a mathematical formula that combines prior beliefs about parameters with the likelihood of observing data to derive a posterior distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;Bayes' Theorem is a fundamental equation in probability theory for calculating conditional probabilities, and it is applied here to the context of a neural network's weights.&lt;SEP&gt;This equation is a form of Bayes' Theorem, which describes the probability of an event, based on prior knowledge of conditions that might be related to the event.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a5cf1e19ea0a8639d0d309beab7d404</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292907</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Input Data (X)">
      <data key="d0">Input Data (X)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Input Data (X) is typically a matrix or tensor of training features, such as images for image segmentation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289184</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Target Output Data (Y)">
      <data key="d0">Target Output Data (Y)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Target Output Data (Y) corresponds to the desired labels or ground truth for the Input Data (X), such as segmentation masks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289184</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Posterior Probability Distribution (p(W|X, Y))">
      <data key="d0">Posterior Probability Distribution (p(W|X, Y))</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Posterior Probability Distribution (p(W|X, Y)) is the probability distribution of the weights W given the observed input X and target Y, representing the updated belief about the model parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289185</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Likelihood Function (p(Y|X, W))">
      <data key="d0">Likelihood Function (p(Y|X, W))</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Likelihood Function (p(Y|X, W)) represents the probability of observing the target outputs Y given the input X and a specific set of weights W.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289185</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prior Probability Distribution (p(W))">
      <data key="d0">Prior Probability Distribution (p(W))</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Prior Probability Distribution (p(W)) reflects initial belief or knowledge about the weights before observing any training data, often assumed to be a Gaussian distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289185</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Evidence Term (P(X))">
      <data key="d0">Evidence Term (P(X))</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Evidence Term (P(X)), also known as marginal likelihood or model evidence, is the probability of observing the input data X, which is a normalizing constant and is very difficult to compute.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289185</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probability Theory">
      <data key="d0">Probability Theory</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Probability Theory is the branch of mathematics concerned with probability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289187</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gaussian Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Gaussian Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Gaussian distribution is a common type of probability distribution often assumed as a simple prior for weights in Bayesian neural network implementations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289187</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Target Output Data">
      <data key="d0">Target Output Data</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Target output data, denoted by Y, corresponds to the desired labels or ground truth for the input X, such as segmentation masks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289188</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Posterior Probability Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Posterior Probability Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The posterior probability distribution of the weights $p(W | X, Y)$ represents the updated belief about the model parameters after observing training data, which Bayesian neural networks aim to infer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289188</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Likelihood Function">
      <data key="d0">Likelihood Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The likelihood function, $p(Y | X, W)$, describes the probability of observing target outputs Y given input X and a specific set of weights W, indicating how well the neural network explains the observed data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289188</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prior Probability Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Prior Probability Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The prior probability distribution, $p(W)$, reflects initial beliefs about the weights before observing any training data, often assumed to be a Gaussian distribution in Bayesian neural networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289189</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monte-Carlo Dropout">
      <data key="d0">Monte-Carlo Dropout</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Monte-Carlo dropout is an approximate Bayesian inference method used for quantifying prediction confidence in deep learning models, particularly for estimating intractable posterior distributions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd6ce2b178e669e439db6c5cb7f00f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289189</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.18">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.18</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.18 is a composite visual divided into two primary sub-images, (a) and (b), illustrating a conceptual model on a grid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-906a75be5f88ad37713839d335d1720c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289190</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Designated Design Space">
      <data key="d0">Designated Design Space</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The designated design space is an area within the grid, outlined by a red border in sub-image (b), representing an acceptable operational region for the controller.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-906a75be5f88ad37713839d335d1720c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289190</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sub-Image (a)">
      <data key="d0">Sub-Image (a)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Sub-image (a) depicts the initial state within Figure 4.18, showing the controller within a shaded gray area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-906a75be5f88ad37713839d335d1720c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289190</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sub-Image (b)">
      <data key="d0">Sub-Image (b)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Sub-image (b) depicts the subsequent state within Figure 4.18, showing the controller moved outside the designated design space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-906a75be5f88ad37713839d335d1720c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289190</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COIL Block">
      <data key="d0">COIL Block</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The COIL Block is a prominent vertical pink-red rectangular block, labeled "COIL," present in both sub-images. There are both left and right COIL blocks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-906a75be5f88ad37713839d335d1720c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289191</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ARMATURE Block">
      <data key="d0">ARMATURE Block</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The ARMATURE Block is a tall vertical gray rectangular block, labeled "ARMATURE," present in both sub-images.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-906a75be5f88ad37713839d335d1720c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289191</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X-Y Coordinate System">
      <data key="d0">X-Y Coordinate System</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A small X-Y coordinate system with red (X) and green (Y) axes visible in the bottom-left corner of both sub-images, suggesting spatial context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-906a75be5f88ad37713839d335d1720c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289191</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$Q(s,a)$">
      <data key="d0">$Q(s,a)$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Q(s,a) is a type of value function in reinforcement learning, used to evaluate actions taken in specific states.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-906a75be5f88ad37713839d335d1720c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289192</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$V(s)$">
      <data key="d0">$V(s)$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">V(s) is a type of value function in reinforcement learning, used to evaluate the value of specific states.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-906a75be5f88ad37713839d335d1720c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289192</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sub-image (a)">
      <data key="d0">Sub-image (a)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Sub-image (a) depicts an initial state of a conceptual model, showing a controller within a designated gray area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-906a75be5f88ad37713839d335d1720c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289192</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sub-image (b)">
      <data key="d0">Sub-image (b)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Sub-image (b) depicts a subsequent state where the controller has moved out of its designated design space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-906a75be5f88ad37713839d335d1720c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289193</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gray Square Area">
      <data key="d0">Gray Square Area</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A rectangular area of gray squares in the upper left quadrant of the grid, representing a designated space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-906a75be5f88ad37713839d335d1720c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289193</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Black Square Controller">
      <data key="d0">Black Square Controller</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Black Square Controller represents a specific 'controller' position within the grid space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-906a75be5f88ad37713839d335d1720c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289193</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State Transition (s to s')">
      <data key="d0">State Transition (s to s')</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The visual demonstration of movement from sub-image (a) to sub-image (b), representing a change from one state (s) to another (s').</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-906a75be5f88ad37713839d335d1720c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289193</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Statistical Hypothesis Test">
      <data key="d0">Statistical Hypothesis Test</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A method for making decisions about a population based on sample data, specifically used here to compare the means of two distinct groups within the context of transfer learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289194</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Null Hypothesis (H0)">
      <data key="d0">Null Hypothesis (H0)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A statement of no effect or no difference, proposing that there is no statistically significant difference between the population mean of the source weights and the population mean of the target weights.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289194</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alternative Hypothesis (Ha)">
      <data key="d0">Alternative Hypothesis (Ha)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A statement representing what the researcher is trying to prove, asserting that there is a statistically significant difference between the population means of the source and target weights.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289195</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Two-Tailed Hypothesis Test">
      <data key="d0">Two-Tailed Hypothesis Test</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of statistical test where any difference, positive or negative, from the null hypothesis is considered significant.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289195</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Population Mean (μs)">
      <data key="d0">Population Mean (μs)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The true population mean of the weight distribution for a layer from a pre-trained (source) network, representing the underlying average weight value before transfer learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289195</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Population Mean (μt)">
      <data key="d0">Population Mean (μt)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The true population mean of the weight distribution for the corresponding layer in a target network, representing the underlying average weight value after transfer learning and fine-tuning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289195</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pre-Trained Network (Source Model)">
      <data key="d0">Pre-Trained Network (Source Model)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A neural network that has been initially trained on a source task, from which knowledge is transferred during transfer learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289196</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Target Network (Target Model)">
      <data key="d0">Target Network (Target Model)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A neural network that is fine-tuned on a new, target task, adapting knowledge from a pre-trained network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289196</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Layer Weights">
      <data key="d0">Layer Weights</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The parameters within a neural network layer, whose changes are assessed by the hypothesis test.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289196</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Statistical Significance">
      <data key="d0">Statistical Significance</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The likelihood that a result or relationship is caused by something other than mere random chance, determined by comparing a p-value to an alpha-value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289196</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alpha-Value (α)">
      <data key="d0">Alpha-Value (α)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A pre-determined threshold, typically 0.05, used to decide whether to reject the null hypothesis in a hypothesis test.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289197</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Change in Weights">
      <data key="d0">Change in Weights</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The modification of synaptic weights within a neural network during the transfer learning process, indicating adaptation to a new task.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289197</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alternative Hypothesis">
      <data key="d0">Alternative Hypothesis</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A statement that the researcher is trying to prove, asserting a statistically significant difference between population means (e.g., of source and target weights). It is denoted as Ha.&lt;SEP&gt;The alternative hypothesis, denoted as Ha, states that there is a significant difference between two population means, specifically that μs does not equal μt.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9df755969eb90a4aeda5af51acf960d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293018</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Population Mean of Source Weights">
      <data key="d0">Population Mean of Source Weights</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The true population mean of the weight distribution for a layer from a pre-trained network (source model), denoted by μs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289198</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Population Mean of Target Weights">
      <data key="d0">Population Mean of Target Weights</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The true population mean of the weight distribution for the corresponding layer in the target network (target model), denoted by μt.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289198</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pre-trained Network">
      <data key="d0">Pre-trained Network</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A source model that has been trained on a large dataset for a specific task before being adapted for a new task.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289199</data>
    </node>
    <node id="P-value">
      <data key="d0">P-value</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The outcome of a hypothesis test used to decide whether to reject the null hypothesis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289199</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Difference in Sample Means">
      <data key="d0">Difference in Sample Means</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The quantity of interest in resampling procedures, representing the difference between the means of samples from source and target groups.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2ac5f9702a1eb6fb3fd66eadf964396</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289200</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State 's'">
      <data key="d0">State 's'</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">State 's' represents the input to the Policy Gradient algorithm, from which action probabilities are determined.&lt;SEP&gt;State 's' is likely the input received by the AGENT from the ENVIRONMENT.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b48425c0c345b3be14ed824c6579b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66f8a9943508aa952a9845858b8d3374</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289201</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOFT UP">
      <data key="d0">SOFT UP</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">SOFT UP is one of the eight possible actions, specifically labeled for action a₁.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66f8a9943508aa952a9845858b8d3374</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289201</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SOFT DOWN">
      <data key="d0">SOFT DOWN</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">SOFT DOWN is one of the eight possible actions, specifically labeled for action a₂.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66f8a9943508aa952a9845858b8d3374</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289201</data>
    </node>
