      <data key="d5">1760289206</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Geometry Panel">
      <data key="d0">Geometry Panel</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A Geometry Panel is a visual representation within the image that illustrates a discretized material distribution using white, blue, and brick-red squares.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a11edf0b881aae28423c1b0d33893b2d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289207</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B Distribution Panel">
      <data key="d0">B Distribution Panel</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A B Distribution Panel is a visual representation within the image that visualizes magnetic field lines and intensity using color gradients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a11edf0b881aae28423c1b0d33893b2d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289207</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy ($\pi$)">
      <data key="d0">Policy ($\pi$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A rule used by an agent to choose actions based on the current state.&lt;SEP&gt;A policy ($\pi$) is the agent's strategy for choosing actions in different states, directly influencing the expectation of future rewards in the Q-function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289208</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State ($s$)">
      <data key="d0">State ($s$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A state ($s$) refers to the current situation or configuration of the environment in Reinforcement Learning, used as a condition for the Q-function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289208</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action ($a$)">
      <data key="d0">Action ($a$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An action ($a$) is a specific move an agent can take at a given state, also a condition for the Q-function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289208</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Expectation Operator ($\mathbb{E}$)">
      <data key="d0">Expectation Operator ($\mathbb{E}$)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The expectation operator accounts for the stochasticity inherent in reinforcement learning environments by calculating the average value of $G_t$ over many possible future trajectories, weighted by their probabilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289208</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conditioning (|)">
      <data key="d0">Conditioning (|)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Conditioning is a mathematical concept where an expectation is evaluated given specific conditions, such as the current state and action in the Q-function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289209</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Policy ($\pi^*$)">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Policy ($\pi^*$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An optimal policy ($\pi^*$) is a strategy that maximizes the true $Q(s,a)$ values for all states and actions, allowing an agent to achieve the highest possible cumulative future reward.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289209</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Q-function ($Q^*(s,a)$)">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Q-function ($Q^*(s,a)$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The optimal Q-function is derived from an optimal policy, representing the maximum possible cumulative future reward for any state-action pair.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289209</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discount Factor ($\gamma$)">
      <data key="d0">Discount Factor ($\gamma$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Discount Factor ($\gamma$) is a parameter that modifies the basic definition of Return ($G_t$) by weighing future rewards less heavily, leading to a 'discounted return'.&lt;SEP&gt;The discount factor is a parameter used in Reinforcement Learning to weigh immediate rewards against future rewards in the calculation of return $G_t$.&lt;SEP&gt;A hyperparameter between 0 and 1 that determines the present value of future rewards. It influences how farsighted or myopic an agent is.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289209</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State-Value Function ($V(s)$)">
      <data key="d0">State-Value Function ($V(s)$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The State-Value Function ($V(s)$) is the expected value of all possible future rewards, across all possible actions, given an agent is in state $s$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289210</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monte Carlo (MC) Methods">
      <data key="d0">Monte Carlo (MC) Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Monte Carlo (MC) methods are a class of algorithms in Reinforcement Learning that directly use the empirically observed Return ($G_t$) from completed episodes to update Value Functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Monte Carlo methods in Reinforcement Learning estimate value functions by waiting until the end of an episode to observe the true realized return ($G_t$).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289210</data>
    </node>
    <node id="True Return">
      <data key="d0">True Return</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">True Return, also known as absolute return, is the quantitative representation of the long-term gain or future cumulative reward an agent is expected to receive from a given point in time. It is represented by $G_t$ in its undiscounted form.&lt;SEP&gt;The true return refers to the actual cumulative reward observed from an episode in Reinforcement Learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289210</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Diagnosis">
      <data key="d0">Medical Diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical diagnosis is an application area where reinforcement learning can be used for optimal sequential decision-making strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289211</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q ( s , a ) $\doteq \mathbb { E } _ { \pi } [ G _ { t } | s _ { t } = s , a _ { t } = a ]$">
      <data key="d0">Q ( s , a ) $\doteq \mathbb { E } _ { \pi } [ G _ { t } | s _ { t } = s , a _ { t } = a ]$</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The mathematical equation defining the Action-Value Function (Q-function).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289211</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reward Functions">
      <data key="d0">Reward Functions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Reward functions define the immediate feedback an agent receives for its actions, contrasting with the long-term goal formalized by value functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289212</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Decision Making">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Decision Making</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Optimal Decision Making refers to the process of choosing actions that maximize the expected cumulative future reward, a core goal in Reinforcement Learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289212</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Q-Function ($Q^*(s,a)$)">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Q-Function ($Q^*(s,a)$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The optimal Q-function ($Q^*(s,a)$) represents the maximum possible expected cumulative reward for taking action $a$ in state $s$ and subsequently following an optimal policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a19e84f4051b2d1d639403e518dc4ebb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289213</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Finite Element (FE)">
      <data key="d0">Finite Element (FE)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Finite Element (FE) analysis is a method used to obtain one of the flux linkage maps, as depicted in part (b) of Figure 3.11.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9f12687a170b3ffb0757382a02803b98</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289213</data>
    </node>
    <node id="λ_d [mWb]">
      <data key="d0">λ_d [mWb]</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">λ_d [mWb] is a variable representing flux linkage in milliwebers, depicted on the three-dimensional surface plots.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9f12687a170b3ffb0757382a02803b98</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289214</data>
    </node>
    <node id="I_q [A_RMS]">
      <data key="d0">I_q [A_RMS]</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">I_q [A_RMS] is a variable representing current in amperes RMS, depicted on the three-dimensional surface plots.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9f12687a170b3ffb0757382a02803b98</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289214</data>
    </node>
    <node id="I_d [A_RMS]">
      <data key="d0">I_d [A_RMS]</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">I_d [A_RMS] is a variable representing current in amperes RMS, depicted on the three-dimensional surface plots.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9f12687a170b3ffb0757382a02803b98</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289215</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RMSE of 1-2%">
      <data key="d0">RMSE of 1-2%</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The RMSE of 1-2% indicates a close match between the network's output and ground truth, specifically referring to the Deep Learning model's predictions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9f12687a170b3ffb0757382a02803b98</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289215</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Finite Element (FE) Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Finite Element (FE) Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Finite Element (FE) Analysis is a computational method used to obtain one of the flux linkage maps presented in the image, serving as a basis for comparison with the DL method.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9f12687a170b3ffb0757382a02803b98</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289216</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Root Mean Square Error (RMSE)">
      <data key="d0">Root Mean Square Error (RMSE)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) is a statistical measure used to quantify the difference between values predicted by a model and the actual values, noted as 1-2% for the network's output compared to ground truth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9f12687a170b3ffb0757382a02803b98</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289216</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Input (s, a)">
      <data key="d0">Input (s, a)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">This represents the input data for both Q-Learning and Policy Gradient algorithms, consisting of a state 's' and an action 'a' pair.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d057a6f40f6119ac37674e31ce09f40a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289216</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Output ĝ(s, a, w)">
      <data key="d0">Output ĝ(s, a, w)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">This is the output of the Q-Learning algorithm, representing the estimated action-value function (Q-value) for a given state 's' and action 'a' with weights 'w'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d057a6f40f6119ac37674e31ce09f40a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289216</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Output π(a | s, θ)">
      <data key="d0">Output π(a | s, θ)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">This is the output of the Policy Gradient algorithm, denoting the policy that gives the probability of selecting an action 'a' given a state 's' and parameters 'θ'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d057a6f40f6119ac37674e31ce09f40a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289216</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.14">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.14</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.14 is a visual representation that compares the Q-learning and Policy Gradient methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d057a6f40f6119ac37674e31ce09f40a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289217</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State And Action Pair (s, a)">
      <data key="d0">State And Action Pair (s, a)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The State And Action Pair (s, a) represents the input to both Q-Learning and Policy Gradient algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d057a6f40f6119ac37674e31ce09f40a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289217</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Estimated Action-Value Function Q-Value">
      <data key="d0">Estimated Action-Value Function Q-Value</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Estimated Action-Value Function Q-Value, denoted as "ĝ(s, a, w)", is the output of the Q-Learning algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d057a6f40f6119ac37674e31ce09f40a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289218</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Function (π(a | s, θ))">
      <data key="d0">Policy Function (π(a | s, θ))</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Policy Function, denoted as "π(a | s, θ)", is the output of the Policy Gradient algorithm, indicating the probability of an action given a state and parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d057a6f40f6119ac37674e31ce09f40a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289218</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.31">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.31</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.31 is a visual representation titled “Optimal geometry and B distribution for excitation scenario (1.75 A, 500 Turns)”.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2785b4e150ada39f3077e573325eeb4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289218</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SeqTO-QL">
      <data key="d0">SeqTO-QL</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">SeqTO-QL is an optimization approach representing Quadrature Learning combined with Sequential Topology Optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2785b4e150ada39f3077e573325eeb4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289218</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SeqTO-RL">
      <data key="d0">SeqTO-RL</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">SeqTO-RL is an optimization approach representing Reinforcement Learning combined with Sequential Topology Optimization, appearing in two different scenarios.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2785b4e150ada39f3077e573325eeb4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289218</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SeqTO-RL (Re-Train)">
      <data key="d0">SeqTO-RL (Re-Train)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">SeqTO-RL (Re-Train) is an optimization approach that re-trains the Reinforcement Learning model within Sequential Topology Optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2785b4e150ada39f3077e573325eeb4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289218</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimized Material">
      <data key="d0">Optimized Material</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The optimized material is represented by blue blocks within the 2D grid-based geometries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2785b4e150ada39f3077e573325eeb4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Empty Space">
      <data key="d0">Empty Space</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Empty space or a different material, depicted in light gray, forms part of the geometries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2785b4e150ada39f3077e573325eeb4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fixed Components">
      <data key="d0">Fixed Components</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Fixed components, depicted in textured orange, are parts of the structure that do not change during optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2785b4e150ada39f3077e573325eeb4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289220</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2D Grid-Based Geometry">
      <data key="d0">2D Grid-Based Geometry</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The 2D grid-based geometry is a visual representation of material distribution, typically L-shaped, composed of rectangular blocks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2785b4e150ada39f3077e573325eeb4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289220</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Field (B) Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Field (B) Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The magnetic field (B) distribution is visualized using a heatmap and streamlines, showing areas of varying field strength and direction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2785b4e150ada39f3077e573325eeb4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289220</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Force Measurement">
      <data key="d0">Force Measurement</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Force measurement is a performance metric, indicating the magnitude of force generated, e.g., 97.2 N.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b2785b4e150ada39f3077e573325eeb4</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289221</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reward ($R_{t+k}$)">
      <data key="d0">Reward ($R_{t+k}$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Reward ($R_{t+k}$) represents individual rewards received by an agent at specific time steps within an episode. $R_n$ is the reward received at the final time step $n$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289221</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Episodic Tasks">
      <data key="d0">Episodic Tasks</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Episodic Tasks are a type of task in Reinforcement Learning characterized by a clear start and end, allowing for the calculation of a finite sum of rewards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289221</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Expected Return">
      <data key="d0">Expected Return</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Expected Return is a theoretical estimation of the cumulative future rewards, which Value Functions aim to approximate.&lt;SEP&gt;Expected Return, denoted as Gt, is the total discounted reward an agent expects to receive from time 't' onward.&lt;SEP&gt;Expected Return refers to the anticipated total reward from a given state-action pair, following a specific policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18301e98e2477e8fce449561e9c20039&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6444ddfe2f3b1042c888bb109eee4098</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292921</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Off-Line Policy Evaluation">
      <data key="d0">Off-Line Policy Evaluation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Off-Line Policy Evaluation is a practical application where the Return ($G_t$) is calculated to quantify the utility of specific state-action pairs or states.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289222</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Return $G_t$">
      <data key="d0">Return $G_t$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Return $G_t$ is a concept in reinforcement learning representing the sum of future rewards from a specific time step $t$ until the end of an episode.&lt;SEP&gt;The total discounted reward accumulated from time step $t$ until the end of an episode, used in Monte Carlo methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289222</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reward $R_k$">
      <data key="d0">Reward $R_k$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Reward $R_k$ represents an individual reward received by an agent at a specific time step $k$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289222</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Time Step $t$">
      <data key="d0">Time Step $t$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Time Step $t$ denotes the current specific point in time from which the return is being calculated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289222</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Time Step $n$">
      <data key="d0">Time Step $n$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Time Step $n$ is defined as the final time step of an episodic task, marking the end of an episode.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discount Factor $\gamma$">
      <data key="d0">Discount Factor $\gamma$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Discount Factor $\gamma$ is a parameter that modifies the basic definition of Return by weighing future rewards less heavily.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Undiscounted Return">
      <data key="d0">Undiscounted Return</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Undiscounted Return is the basic form of Return, calculated as a simple sum of future rewards without a discount factor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discounted Return">
      <data key="d0">Discounted Return</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Discounted Return is a modified form of Return that incorporates a discount factor ($\gamma$) to value immediate rewards more than future rewards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-34ed3729fa06a3c6f2093a4d16a2c572</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289224</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Motor Configuration">
      <data key="d0">Motor Configuration</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Motor Configuration refers to the specific arrangement of a motor defined by its structural characteristics and electrical operating conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1f6cebd0187b0c1924a3e6491e8ebe8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289224</data>
    </node>
    <node id="12 Slots">
      <data key="d0">12 Slots</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">12 Slots is a specific structural characteristic of a motor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1f6cebd0187b0c1924a3e6491e8ebe8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289224</data>
    </node>
    <node id="10 Poles">
      <data key="d0">10 Poles</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">10 Poles is a specific structural characteristic of a motor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1f6cebd0187b0c1924a3e6491e8ebe8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289225</data>
    </node>
    <node id="300 Arms">
      <data key="d0">300 Arms</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">300 Arms represents the electrical current operating condition of a motor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1f6cebd0187b0c1924a3e6491e8ebe8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289225</data>
    </node>
    <node id="450Vdc">
      <data key="d0">450Vdc</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">450Vdc represents the electrical voltage operating condition of a motor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1f6cebd0187b0c1924a3e6491e8ebe8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289225</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Motor Setup">
      <data key="d0">Motor Setup</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Motor Setup is defined by the combination of specific data points like slots, poles, current, and voltage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1f6cebd0187b0c1924a3e6491e8ebe8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289225</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Section 3.7.1">
      <data key="d0">Section 3.7.1</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Section 3.7.1, titled 'Dataset Generation,' details how different datasets of motor designs and performance maps are needed for transfer learning.&lt;SEP&gt;Section 3.7.1 describes the data generation process for motor designs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f6cebd0187b0c1924a3e6491e8ebe8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289226</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Neural Networks (DNNs)">
      <data key="d0">Deep Neural Networks (DNNs)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep Neural Networks (DNNs) are a type of machine learning model that receive motor geometries as inputs.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep Neural Networks are used as surrogates for FE analysis in Stages 1 and 3 of the process for calculating performance maps.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f6cebd0187b0c1924a3e6491e8ebe8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289227</data>
    </node>
    <node id="12 Slots 10 Poles 300 Arms 450Vdc">
      <data key="d0">12 Slots 10 Poles 300 Arms 450Vdc</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">This represents a specific set of operational and structural parameters for a motor, including 12 slots, 10 poles, 300 Arms, and 450Vdc.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1f6cebd0187b0c1924a3e6491e8ebe8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289228</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Evolved Action Space">
      <data key="d0">Evolved Action Space</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The evolved action space refers to the expanded set of actions available to the controller in the SeqTO-v2 environment, detailed in Table 5.3.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289230</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soft Up">
      <data key="d0">Soft Up</data>
      <data key="d1">action</data>
      <data key="d2">Soft Up is an action specified as index 1 in the evolved action space, indicating a less aggressive or more granular upward movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289231</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soft Down">
      <data key="d0">Soft Down</data>
      <data key="d1">action</data>
      <data key="d2">Soft Down is an action specified as index 2 in the evolved action space, indicating a less aggressive or more granular downward movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289231</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soft Right">
      <data key="d0">Soft Right</data>
      <data key="d1">action</data>
      <data key="d2">Soft Right is an action specified as index 3 in the evolved action space, indicating a less aggressive or more granular rightward movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289231</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soft Left">
      <data key="d0">Soft Left</data>
      <data key="d1">action</data>
      <data key="d2">Soft Left is an action specified as index 4 in the evolved action space, indicating a less aggressive or more granular leftward movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289231</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Up">
      <data key="d0">Up</data>
      <data key="d1">action</data>
      <data key="d2">Up is an action specified as index 5 in the evolved action space, indicating a standard upward movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289231</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Down">
      <data key="d0">Down</data>
      <data key="d1">action</data>
      <data key="d2">Down is an action specified as index 6 in the evolved action space, indicating a standard downward movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289232</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Right">
      <data key="d0">Right</data>
      <data key="d1">action</data>
      <data key="d2">Right is an action specified as index 7 in the evolved action space, indicating a standard rightward movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289232</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Left">
      <data key="d0">Left</data>
      <data key="d1">action</data>
      <data key="d2">Left is an action specified as index 8 in the evolved action space, indicating a standard leftward movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289232</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 4, Section 4.3">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 4, Section 4.3</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 4, Section 4.3 is a prior section that mentioned four actions, indicating a previous, smaller action space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289233</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Block Placement">
      <data key="d0">Block Placement</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Block Placement refers to the controller's ability to place structural elements of varying sizes and orientations, enabled by the 'Soft' actions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289233</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3x2 Block">
      <data key="d0">3x2 Block</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A 3x2 block is a specific size of structural element that the controller can place using soft actions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289234</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2x3 Block">
      <data key="d0">2x3 Block</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A 2x3 block is a specific size of structural element that the controller can place using soft actions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289234</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electromagnetic Simulations">
      <data key="d0">Electromagnetic Simulations</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Electromagnetic Simulations are computational processes that the expanded action space aims to limit the number of.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289234</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blocks of Varying Sizes">
      <data key="d0">Blocks of Varying Sizes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Blocks of varying sizes (e.g., 3x1, 1x3, 3x2, 2x3) are structural elements that the controller can now place.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc7e662d13f8f799c0011dbabf78f253</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289234</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.30">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.30</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.30 presents a comparative analysis of optimal geometries and magnetic field distributions for an excitation scenario.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2a5c42907185fecc94ee85f1b33ec380</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289234</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2.0 A">
      <data key="d0">2.0 A</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">2.0 A represents the current used in the excitation scenario shown in the image.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2a5c42907185fecc94ee85f1b33ec380</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289235</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discrete Cellular Structure">
      <data key="d0">Discrete Cellular Structure</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A discrete cellular structure is a visual representation of material placement in the image.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2a5c42907185fecc94ee85f1b33ec380</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289235</data>
    </node>
    <node id="False-Color Plot">
      <data key="d0">False-Color Plot</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">False-Color Plot is a visual element in the image illustrating magnetic flux density distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2a5c42907185fecc94ee85f1b33ec380</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289236</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Captions">
      <data key="d0">Captions</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Captions are textual descriptions accompanying an image, providing context or explanation, such as Figure 5.30: Optimal geometry and B distribution for excitation scenario (2.0 A, 500 Turns) for (a) GA (b) QL based on SeqTO-v1 (c) A2C based on SeqTO-v2.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2a5c42907185fecc94ee85f1b33ec380</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289236</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Footnotes">
      <data key="d0">Footnotes</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Footnotes are additional notes or references provided at the bottom of a page, and in this case, there are none.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2a5c42907185fecc94ee85f1b33ec380</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289236</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Newtons">
      <data key="d0">Newtons</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Newtons (N) are the units of measurement for force.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2a5c42907185fecc94ee85f1b33ec380</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289236</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Equivalent Circuit (MEC)">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Equivalent Circuit (MEC)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">MEC is a simulation method used to estimate force and torque, providing reduced complexity and faster computation, but with variable accuracy depending on the study or application.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d146c06d89d470b7db435c475826c0e7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289237</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Force/Torque Comparison">
      <data key="d0">Force/Torque Comparison</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">This concept refers to the percentage difference between simulation results (FEA and MEC) and experimental measurements for force and torque.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d146c06d89d470b7db435c475826c0e7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289237</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liu and Chiang (2006)">
      <data key="d0">Liu and Chiang (2006)</data>
      <data key="d1">reference</data>
      <data key="d2">This reference describes a study that compared FEA and MEC results with experimental data, showing 6% force difference for MEC and 10% for FEA.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d146c06d89d470b7db435c475826c0e7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289238</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hur et al. (1997)">
      <data key="d0">Hur et al. (1997)</data>
      <data key="d1">reference</data>
      <data key="d2">This reference describes a study that provided detailed force comparisons for 2D MEC, 2D FEA, and 3D MEC against experimental results, with 3D MEC showing a 1% difference.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d146c06d89d470b7db435c475826c0e7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289238</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leplat et al. (1996)">
      <data key="d0">Leplat et al. (1996)</data>
      <data key="d1">reference</data>
      <data key="d2">This reference describes a study focusing on torque comparison, showing 16.9% torque difference for MEC and 11.1% for FEA against experimental results.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d146c06d89d470b7db435c475826c0e7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289238</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kim et al. (2004)">
      <data key="d0">Kim et al. (2004)</data>
      <data key="d1">reference</data>
      <data key="d2">This reference describes a study that presented ranges for force comparisons, with 3-5% for FEA and 5-35% for MEC against experimental results.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d146c06d89d470b7db435c475826c0e7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289239</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Experimental Results">
      <data key="d0">Experimental Results</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Experimental results are direct measurements used as a benchmark for validating simulations, representing the actual physical outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d146c06d89d470b7db435c475826c0e7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289239</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electric Machine Studies">
      <data key="d0">Electric Machine Studies</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">These are research endeavors focused on analyzing and comparing different methods for simulating and measuring performance in electric machines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d146c06d89d470b7db435c475826c0e7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289239</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Section 2.2.3 'Comparison Between MEC &amp; FEA'">
      <data key="d0">Section 2.2.3 'Comparison Between MEC &amp; FEA'</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">This section of the surrounding text discusses the comparison between MEC and FEA simulations against experimental measurements, stating that both methods show acceptable accuracy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d146c06d89d470b7db435c475826c0e7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289240</data>
    </node>
