      <data key="d5">1760289240</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Equivalent Circuit">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Equivalent Circuit</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnetic Equivalent Circuit (MEC) is a computational method used for simulation. It provides reduced complexity and faster computation compared to FEA.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d146c06d89d470b7db435c475826c0e7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ref Column">
      <data key="d0">Ref Column</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The "Ref" column in the table lists academic references for each study.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d146c06d89d470b7db435c475826c0e7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Method Column">
      <data key="d0">Method Column</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The "Method" column in the table indicates that each entry compares both FEA and MEC methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d146c06d89d470b7db435c475826c0e7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289242</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Force/Torque Comparison Column">
      <data key="d0">Force/Torque Comparison Column</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The "Force/Torque Comparison" column in the table details the percentage difference between simulation results and experimental measurements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d146c06d89d470b7db435c475826c0e7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289242</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gt">
      <data key="d0">Gt</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Gt represents the return at time step t. It signifies the total rewards an agent receives until the end of the episode, a cumulative reward function.&lt;SEP&gt;Gt likely represents the return or target value at time t.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd1039c6984ae2b3caedcb109bdc272a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292984</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rt+1">
      <data key="d0">Rt+1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Rt+1 denotes the immediate scalar reward received at time step t+1 from the environment after an action.&lt;SEP&gt;Rt+1 is the immediate reward received at time step t+1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec4e495c59f004fcbe4475de4b2bcbd1</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293034</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gt+1">
      <data key="d0">Gt+1</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Gt+1 represents the return at time step t+1, similar to Gt but starting from the next time step.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289243</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Episodic Reinforcement Learning">
      <data key="d0">Episodic Reinforcement Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Episodic Reinforcement Learning is a setting where tasks have a defined beginning and end, allowing for the calculation of total returns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289243</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reward Signal">
      <data key="d0">Reward Signal</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Reward Signal is the positive or negative feedback an agent receives from the environment, which is maximized through learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289243</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Control/Policy Improvement">
      <data key="d0">Control/Policy Improvement</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A process in reinforcement learning where the policy is updated to become better or optimal based on the improved value function estimates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289243</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Equation Gt">
      <data key="d0">Equation Gt</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The equation $G_t \doteq R_{t+1} + G_{t+1}$ defines the recursive relationship between the return at time t and future rewards/returns in an episodic reinforcement learning setting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289244</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reward Rt+1">
      <data key="d0">Reward Rt+1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Denotes the immediate scalar feedback from the environment received at time step t+1 after taking an action at time t and transitioning to a new state.&lt;SEP&gt;Rt+1 represents the immediate reward received at time step t+1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8823695e17798553515e243bdbfda2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292788</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Return Gt+1">
      <data key="d0">Return Gt+1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Represents the return at time step t+1, similar to Gt but starting from the next time step. It is the sum of all rewards obtained from time t+1 onwards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289245</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TD(0)">
      <data key="d0">TD(0)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An algorithm derived from the concepts discussed, specifically using the recursive relationship for incremental updates without a discount factor explicitly shown in the simplified equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289246</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Equation 4.6">
      <data key="d0">Equation 4.6</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An earlier equation from which the Gt equation is modified, implying a progression in the mathematical formulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289246</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Equation 4.5">
      <data key="d0">Equation 4.5</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An equation mentioned in the context that is updated in a similar fashion as for the Gt equation, suggesting a parallel in mathematical updates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289246</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Equation 4.3">
      <data key="d0">Equation 4.3</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An equation describing the value function that can also be updated in a similar fashion, indicating a shared approach to updating value functions in TD learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-87755691ed2aadfe9652dad2d067f4a9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289246</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.5">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.5</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.5 is an image showing an example of a 3D printed lattice structure of a jet engine mounting bracket.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c232c16db2cb946ab34105a48ebff79</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289246</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3D Printed Lattice Structure">
      <data key="d0">3D Printed Lattice Structure</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A 3D printed lattice structure refers to a complex, porous microstructure consisting of interconnected elements, often created through additive manufacturing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c232c16db2cb946ab34105a48ebff79</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jet Engine Mounting Bracket">
      <data key="d0">Jet Engine Mounting Bracket</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A jet engine mounting bracket is a component used in jet engines, exemplified here by a 3D printed version with a lattice structure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c232c16db2cb946ab34105a48ebff79</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Selective Laser Sintering (SLS)">
      <data key="d0">Selective Laser Sintering (SLS)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Selective Laser Sintering (SLS) is an additive manufacturing process that produces objects with a matte, slightly granular finish.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c232c16db2cb946ab34105a48ebff79</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289248</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Direct Metal Laser Sintering (DMLS)">
      <data key="d0">Direct Metal Laser Sintering (DMLS)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Direct Metal Laser Sintering (DMLS) is an additive manufacturing process that produces objects with a matte, slightly granular finish, similar to SLS.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c232c16db2cb946ab34105a48ebff79</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289249</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Titanium">
      <data key="d0">Titanium</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Titanium is a silvery-gray metallic material that could be used in the 3D printed bracket.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c232c16db2cb946ab34105a48ebff79</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289249</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aluminum Alloy">
      <data key="d0">Aluminum Alloy</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Aluminum Alloy is a silvery-gray metallic material that could be used in the 3D printed bracket.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4c232c16db2cb946ab34105a48ebff79</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289249</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Morgan et al.">
      <data key="d0">Morgan et al.</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Morgan et al. refers to the authors of a 2014 publication referenced in the image caption.&lt;SEP&gt;Researchers or a research group referenced for their work in 2014.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d608386f6581f290b37ee1f8bb75e40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c232c16db2cb946ab34105a48ebff79</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289250</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exponentially Average Reward Graph">
      <data key="d0">Exponentially Average Reward Graph</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">This graph plots the "Exponentially Average Reward" against "Episodes Count," illustrating the learning progress and convergence of the Deep Q-learning algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02a0921c09c7af3fdaeac2a38ff90bbb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289251</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1.0 Amps">
      <data key="d0">1.0 Amps</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">1.0 Amps is the specific excitation current used in the C-core problem, as mentioned in the caption for component (a) and section 5.3.4.&lt;SEP&gt;1.0 Amps is a specific current value, part of the excitation scenario described in the caption for which the convergence results are presented.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a91ba976c2a6f12065edbafc2b708ae2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a0921c09c7af3fdaeac2a38ff90bbb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289252</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Section 5.3.4">
      <data key="d0">Section 5.3.4</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Section 5.3.4 introduces the C-core problem, detailing its design objective to maximize force on the armature with specific current and turns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02a0921c09c7af3fdaeac2a38ff90bbb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289252</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Section 5.3.5">
      <data key="d0">Section 5.3.5</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Section 5.3.5 provides context for the optimal geometry shown in Figure 5.8, mentioning its environment, material, and convergence behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02a0921c09c7af3fdaeac2a38ff90bbb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289252</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Steps 1-17">
      <data key="d0">Steps 1-17</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Steps 1 through 17 represent the sequence of actions taken by the Deep Q-Learning algorithm to construct the optimal C-core geometry as shown in Figure 5.8 (a).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02a0921c09c7af3fdaeac2a38ff90bbb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289252</data>
    </node>
    <node id="950 Episodes">
      <data key="d0">950 Episodes</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">950 Episodes is the approximate number of episodes required for the Deep Q-learning algorithm to converge, as stated in Section 5.3.5.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02a0921c09c7af3fdaeac2a38ff90bbb</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289253</data>
    </node>
