      <data key="d0">H₁₄</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">H₁₄ is a circular node within Hidden Layer #1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca632ae39508b7c6fdf1ad04973857b9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289257</data>
    </node>
    <node id="O₁">
      <data key="d0">O₁</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">O₁ is a circular node within the Output Layer, representing the network's output.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca632ae39508b7c6fdf1ad04973857b9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289257</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neuron Connections">
      <data key="d0">Neuron Connections</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Neuron Connections are represented by thin black lines with arrowheads indicating the direction of data flow between layers in the neural network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca632ae39508b7c6fdf1ad04973857b9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289257</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Nodes are circular elements in the neural network diagram, representing individual neurons.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca632ae39508b7c6fdf1ad04973857b9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289258</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Function (Q-value function)">
      <data key="d0">Q-Function (Q-value function)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Q-function, represented as $Q(s_t, a_t)$ or $Q_{\pi}(s_t, a_t)$, quantifies the expected return for taking a specific action in a given state and subsequently following a particular policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289259</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Equation 1">
      <data key="d0">Equation 1</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The first equation "$Q(s_t, a_t) \\doteq \\mathbb{E}_{\\pi} [R_{t+1} + G_{t+1} | s_t = s, a_t = a]$" defines the action-value function, representing the expected return when starting from state $s_t$, taking action $a_t$, and then following policy $\\pi$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289259</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Expected Value ($\mathbb{E}_{\pi}$)">
      <data key="d0">Expected Value ($\mathbb{E}_{\pi}$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A mathematical operation representing the average outcome of a random variable, conditioned on the agent following policy $\pi$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289259</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immediate Reward ($R_{t+1}$)">
      <data key="d0">Immediate Reward ($R_{t+1}$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The reward received by an agent at time step $t+1$ after executing action $a_t$ in state $s_t$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289259</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Return ($G_{t+1}$)">
      <data key="d0">Return ($G_{t+1}$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The total discounted future reward accumulated from time step $t+1$ until the end of an episode.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289259</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State ($s_t$ or s)">
      <data key="d0">State ($s_t$ or s)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The condition of the environment at a specific time $t$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289260</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action ($a_t$ or a)">
      <data key="d0">Action ($a_t$ or a)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A move or decision made by an agent in a given state at time $t$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289260</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Markov Decision Processes (MDPs)">
      <data key="d0">Markov Decision Processes (MDPs)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Markov Decision Processes are a mathematical framework for modeling decision making in situations where outcomes are partly random and partly under the control of a decision maker.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289261</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Equation 2">
      <data key="d0">Equation 2</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The second equation "$Q_{\\pi}(s) = R_{t+1} + Q_{\\pi}(s_{t+1}, a_{t+1})$" appears to be a simplified or conceptually presented form of the Bellman equation, hinting at bootstrapping.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289261</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q(st, at)">
      <data key="d0">Q(st, at)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The action-value function representing the expected return when starting from state st, taking action at, and then following a policy π.&lt;SEP&gt;Q(st, at) represents the Q-value for a state-action pair (st, at) in the context of reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec4e495c59f004fcbe4475de4b2bcbd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293034</data>
    </node>
    <node id="s_t">
      <data key="d0">s_t</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">s_t represents the state of the environment at time t.&lt;SEP&gt;s_t represents the state at time step t.&lt;SEP&gt;s_t refers to the state observed at time step t within a reinforcement learning environment.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical variable, likely representing a state at time t.&lt;SEP&gt;s_t represents the state at time t.&lt;SEP&gt;s_t represents the state at time t.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-620c2ed72be858c05e569ae1452705a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6444ddfe2f3b1042c888bb109eee4098&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18301e98e2477e8fce449561e9c20039</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292921</data>
    </node>
    <node id="a_t">
      <data key="d0">a_t</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">a_t represents the action taken by the agent at time t.&lt;SEP&gt;a_t represents the action taken at time step t.&lt;SEP&gt;a_t refers to the action taken at time step t within a reinforcement learning environment.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical variable, likely representing an action at time t.&lt;SEP&gt;a_t represents the action taken at time t.&lt;SEP&gt;a_t represents the action taken at time t.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-620c2ed72be858c05e569ae1452705a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6444ddfe2f3b1042c888bb109eee4098&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18301e98e2477e8fce449561e9c20039</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292921</data>
    </node>
    <node id="E_pi">
      <data key="d0">E_pi</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">E_pi represents the expected value, conditioned on the agent following policy π, averaging over stochastic transitions and probabilistic actions.&lt;SEP&gt;The expectation under policy pi.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18301e98e2477e8fce449561e9c20039&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292923</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Pi">
      <data key="d0">Policy Pi</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Policy Pi is a function that maps states to probabilities of selecting each possible action.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy $\pi$ is a rule that maps states to actions, determining the agent's behavior in an environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Policy Pi ($\pi ( a _ { t } | s _ { t } ; \boldsymbol { \theta } ) $) is a probability distribution over actions given a state $s_t$, parameterized by $\boldsymbol { \theta }$, which outputs the probability of selecting action $a_t$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289262</data>
    </node>
    <node id="R_t+1">
      <data key="d0">R_t+1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">R_t+1 represents the immediate reward received by the agent at time t+1.&lt;SEP&gt;Representing the reward received at time t+1.&lt;SEP&gt;R_t+1 represents the reward received at time t+1.&lt;SEP&gt;R_t+1 represents the reward at the next time step (t+1).&lt;SEP&gt;R_t+1 is a variable or quantity at time 't+1'.&lt;SEP&gt;R_t+1 is the reward received after taking action a_t in state s_t and transitioning to the next state.&lt;SEP&gt;R_t+1 is the first reward in a sequence, contributing to the sum G_t.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-86d0a1a94798eb7b1674502b0ae73cf4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb650db56653b515d55eb1ef7514257&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec4e495c59f004fcbe4475de4b2bcbd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18301e98e2477e8fce449561e9c20039&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39c26f40d41eeedc15f07ee76f39c83c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f7dc16108c864fd058d1379ed9f298</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293089</data>
    </node>
    <node id="G_t+1">
      <data key="d0">G_t+1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">G_t+1 represents the return (total discounted future reward) accumulated from time step t+1 until the end of the episode.&lt;SEP&gt;G_t+1 represents the discounted sum of future rewards from time t+1 onwards.&lt;SEP&gt;G_t+1 is a variable or quantity at time 't+1'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1f7dc16108c864fd058d1379ed9f298&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18301e98e2477e8fce449561e9c20039&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292979</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Value Function Estimation">
      <data key="d0">Value Function Estimation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Value Function Estimation is a process in reinforcement learning concerned with estimating the value of states or state-action pairs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d6428f3e33b06568645767afedeaa69a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289263</data>
    </node>
    <node id="End-to-End Efficiency Map Prediction">
      <data key="d0">End-to-End Efficiency Map Prediction</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">End-to-End Efficiency Map Prediction is the task performed by the deep learning model, which generates an efficiency map from an input image.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-640da4d848d6502c665bfc0ea66dce13</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289264</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Input Image">
      <data key="d0">Input Image</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Input Image is a square image of an electric motor's cross-section, which serves as the initial input to the deep learning model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-640da4d848d6502c665bfc0ea66dce13</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289264</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U-Net-like Autoencoder">
      <data key="d0">U-Net-like Autoencoder</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The U-Net-like Autoencoder is the structural framework of the deep learning model, characterized by an encoder-decoder design with skip connections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-640da4d848d6502c665bfc0ea66dce13</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289264</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Convolutional Neural Network Layers">
      <data key="d0">Convolutional Neural Network Layers</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) Layers are visually represented as layered rectangles within the Encoder and Decoder sections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-640da4d848d6502c665bfc0ea66dce13</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289265</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Max Pooling Layer">
      <data key="d0">Max Pooling Layer</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Max Pooling Layer is a component of the encoder, indicated by a trapezoidal shape, used for down-sampling feature maps.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-640da4d848d6502c665bfc0ea66dce13</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289265</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Up Sampling Layer">
      <data key="d0">Up Sampling Layer</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Up Sampling Layer is a component of the decoder, indicated by an upward-pointing trapezoid, used for increasing feature map dimensions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-640da4d848d6502c665bfc0ea66dce13</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289265</data>
    </node>
    <node id="De-convolutional Layers">
      <data key="d0">De-convolutional Layers</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">De-convolutional Layers (DE-CONV) are components within the Decoder, indicated by light green rectangles, used for reconstruction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-640da4d848d6502c665bfc0ea66dce13</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289265</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) Layers">
      <data key="d0">Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) Layers</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">CNN Layers are represented by layered rectangles within the encoder and decoder, performing feature extraction and reconstruction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-640da4d848d6502c665bfc0ea66dce13</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289266</data>
    </node>
    <node id="De-convolutional (DE-CONV) Layers">
      <data key="d0">De-convolutional (DE-CONV) Layers</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">De-convolutional Layers are components of the decoder, represented by light green rectangles, used for reconstructing the output.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-640da4d848d6502c665bfc0ea66dce13</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289266</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Off-Policy Q-learning">
      <data key="d0">Off-Policy Q-learning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of Reinforcement Learning framework that allows the agent to learn an optimal policy from experiences generated by a different behavior policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289267</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Behavior Network">
      <data key="d0">Behavior Network</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The behavior network is one of two neural networks in the DQN architecture, responsible for generating actions during experience collection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289267</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reward ($R_{t+1}$)">
      <data key="d0">Reward ($R_{t+1}$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The immediate scalar reward received by the agent after taking an action at time $t$ and transitioning to the next state, a crucial component of the 'target' value.&lt;SEP&gt;The reward ($R_{t+1}$) is a feedback signal received at time t+1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39c26f40d41eeedc15f07ee76f39c83c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Estimated Action-Value ($\hat { Q } _ { t a r } ( s _ { t } , a _ { t } , w _ { t a r } )$">
      <data key="d0">Estimated Action-Value ($\hat { Q } _ { t a r } ( s _ { t } , a _ { t } , w _ { t a r } )$</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The estimated action-value (Q-value) for state $s_t$ and action $a_t$, evaluated by the target Q-network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289268</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Estimated Action-Value ($\hat { Q } _ { b e h } ( s _ { t } , a _ { t } , w _ { b e h } )$">
      <data key="d0">Estimated Action-Value ($\hat { Q } _ { b e h } ( s _ { t } , a _ { t } , w _ { b e h } )$</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The estimated action-value (Q-value) for state $s_t$ and action $a_t$, evaluated by the behavior Q-network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289268</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gradient ($\nabla \hat { Q } _ { b e h } ( s _ { t } , a _ { T } , w _ { b e h } )$">
      <data key="d0">Gradient ($\nabla \hat { Q } _ { b e h } ( s _ { t } , a _ { T } , w _ { b e h } )$</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Represents the gradient of the output of the behavior Q-network with respect to its weights, evaluated at state $s_t$ and action $a_T$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289268</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exploration-Exploitation Trade-off">
      <data key="d0">Exploration-Exploitation Trade-off</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The exploration-exploitation trade-off refers to the dilemma in reinforcement learning between trying new actions (exploration) and choosing known good actions (exploitation).&lt;SEP&gt;The exploration-exploitation trade-off is a fundamental challenge in reinforcement learning where an agent must choose between exploring new actions and exploiting known good actions.&lt;SEP&gt;A fundamental dilemma in Reinforcement Learning concerning how much an agent should explore new actions versus exploiting known good actions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289269</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$\epsilon$-greedy">
      <data key="d0">$\epsilon$-greedy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A common strategy for balancing exploration and exploitation in Reinforcement Learning, influencing how actions are chosen by the behavior policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289269</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Financial Trading">
      <data key="d0">Financial Trading</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Financial Trading is an application area for deep reinforcement learning, used for developing automated trading strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289269</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Off-Policy Q-Learning Framework">
      <data key="d0">Off-Policy Q-Learning Framework</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An off-policy Q-learning framework is a type of reinforcement learning setup where the policy used to collect experiences is different from the policy being learned.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289270</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Asynchronous Update Mechanism">
      <data key="d0">Asynchronous Update Mechanism</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An asynchronous update mechanism is a technique used in DQN to enhance the stability and performance of Q-learning by updating target network weights less frequently.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289270</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Function Approximation Techniques">
      <data key="d0">Function Approximation Techniques</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Function approximation techniques, such as neural networks, are used to estimate complex functions in reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289270</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gradient-Descent-Like Update Rule">
      <data key="d0">Gradient-Descent-Like Update Rule</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A gradient-descent-like update rule adjusts weights iteratively in the direction that minimizes a specific loss function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289271</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Learning Rate Alpha">
      <data key="d0">Learning Rate Alpha</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The learning rate ($\alpha$) is a positive scalar hyperparameter that controls the step size of weight updates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289271</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reward R Sub T Plus 1">
      <data key="d0">Reward R Sub T Plus 1</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The reward $R_{t+1}$ is the immediate scalar reward received by the agent after taking an action in a state and transitioning to the next state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289272</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Function Evaluation">
      <data key="d0">Function Evaluation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Function evaluation refers to the process of calculating the output of neural networks for specific state-action pairs and weight configurations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289272</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Backpropagation Algorithm">
      <data key="d0">Backpropagation Algorithm</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The backpropagation algorithm is used for calculating gradients in neural networks and is essential for updating network parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289272</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Model-Free RL Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Model-Free RL Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Model-Free RL algorithms are a category of reinforcement learning algorithms that do not require a model of the environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289272</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exploration-Exploitation Trade-Off">
      <data key="d0">Exploration-Exploitation Trade-Off</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Exploration-Exploitation Trade-Off, often implemented with $\epsilon$-greedy, dictates how actions are chosen by the behavior policy during experience collection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289272</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Unknown True Optimal Value Function">
      <data key="d0">Unknown True Optimal Value Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The unknown true optimal value function refers to the challenge in RL where the ideal function to approximate is not known beforehand.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289273</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Computational Cost of Full Returns">
      <data key="d0">Computational Cost of Full Returns</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The computational cost of full returns refers to the expense of calculating complete cumulative rewards, which TD learning aims to reduce.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289273</data>
    </node>
    <node id="On-Policy Methods">
      <data key="d0">On-Policy Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">On-policy methods are reinforcement learning approaches where the policy used to collect data is the same as the policy being learned.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-467bb160a8b38c86d752abe8ffcdc6b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289274</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Predictive Posterior Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Predictive Posterior Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">This represents the probability distribution of a new output 'y' given a new input 'x' and all the previously observed training data (inputs 'X' and targets 'Y'). It is the quantity of primary interest for making predictions with uncertainty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289274</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Output Variable Y">
      <data key="d0">Output Variable Y</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The output or target variable being predicted for a new input 'x'. This could be a segmentation mask, a class label, or a continuous value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289274</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Input Variable X">
      <data key="d0">Input Variable X</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A new input sample for which a prediction is to be made, representing an unseen data point.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289275</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Training Input Data X">
      <data key="d0">Training Input Data X</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The set of all input training data samples, implicitly containing known relationships or features that define the model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289275</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Target Output Values Y">
      <data key="d0">Target Output Values Y</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The set of all corresponding target (ground truth) output values for the training data 'X'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289275</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Model Parameters W">
      <data key="d0">Model Parameters W</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Represents the entire set of model parameters (weights) of a neural network, specifically convolutional weights, which are learned during training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289276</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Likelihood P(y|x, W)">
      <data key="d0">Likelihood P(y|x, W)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The likelihood or model distribution, representing the probability of observing output 'y' given a specific input 'x' and a particular set of model weights 'W'. This is the output of the neural network given its parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289276</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Approximate Posterior Distribution Q(W)">
      <data key="d0">Approximate Posterior Distribution Q(W)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An approximation of the true posterior distribution over the model weights 'W', used when the true posterior is intractable. It is learned, for example, through Variational Inference (VI).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289276</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Integration">
      <data key="d0">Integration</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A fundamental operation in Bayesian inference representing marginalization over uncertain parameters 'W', calculating the expectation of the likelihood with respect to the approximate posterior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289276</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Field Distribution Prediction">
      <data key="d0">Field Distribution Prediction</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A prediction task, potentially related to FEA software, focusing on estimating the distribution of a particular field.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289277</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intractable True Posterior P(W|X,Y)">
      <data key="d0">Intractable True Posterior P(W|X,Y)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The true posterior distribution over model weights 'W' given training data 'X' and 'Y', which is difficult or impossible to calculate directly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289277</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monte Carlo (MC) dropout">
      <data key="d0">Monte Carlo (MC) dropout</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A method that can be viewed as performing Monte Carlo sampling from the posterior distribution over various dropout models to approximate the integral in the predictive posterior distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289278</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kullback-Leibler (KL) Divergence">
      <data key="d0">Kullback-Leibler (KL) Divergence</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An objective function used in Variational Inference to measure the difference between an approximate distribution $q(W)$ and the true posterior $p(W|X, Y)$.&lt;SEP&gt;A measure of how one probability distribution diverges from a second, expected probability distribution. In VI, it's used to quantify the distance between an approximate distribution and the true posterior.&lt;SEP&gt;A metric used to measure the difference between two probability distributions, particularly for minimizing the difference between Q(W) and P(W|X,Y) in VI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289278</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Decision-Making Improvement">
      <data key="d0">Decision-Making Improvement</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The enhancement of choices made based on model outputs by considering the prediction uncertainty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289278</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Classification Accuracy Improvement">
      <data key="d0">Classification Accuracy Improvement</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The enhancement of the correctness of predictions in classification tasks, often through techniques like MC dropout.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289279</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regions of Interest Identification">
      <data key="d0">Regions of Interest Identification</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The process of pinpointing specific areas where the model struggled, providing insights for further data collection or model refinement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289279</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Output Variable 'Y'">
      <data key="d0">Output Variable 'Y'</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The output or target variable 'y' that the model is trying to predict for a new input 'x'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289279</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Input Variable 'X'">
      <data key="d0">Input Variable 'X'</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A new input sample 'x' for which a prediction is sought.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289279</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Training Data 'X'">
      <data key="d0">Training Data 'X'</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The set of all input training data samples, implicitly containing known relationships or features that define the model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289280</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Target Output Values 'Y'">
      <data key="d0">Target Output Values 'Y'</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The set of corresponding target (ground truth) output values for the training data 'X'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289280</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Model Parameters 'W'">
      <data key="d0">Model Parameters 'W'</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The entire set of model parameters (weights) of a neural network, specifically convolutional weights, which the network learns during training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289281</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Likelihood (or Model Distribution)">
      <data key="d0">Likelihood (or Model Distribution)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The probability of observing output 'y' given a specific input 'x' and a particular set of model weights 'W', representing the output of the neural network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289281</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Approximate Posterior Distribution q(W)">
      <data key="d0">Approximate Posterior Distribution q(W)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An approximation of the true posterior distribution over the model weights 'W', used when the true posterior is intractable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289281</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bayesian Neural Networks (Bnns)">
      <data key="d0">Bayesian Neural Networks (Bnns)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of neural network that incorporates Bayesian inference, particularly for tasks like image segmentation or field distribution prediction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289281</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intractable Posterior">
      <data key="d0">Intractable Posterior</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A true posterior distribution that cannot be computed exactly, which the equation addresses through approximation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289281</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Model Improvement">
      <data key="d0">Model Improvement</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A practical use case where high uncertainty flags regions for data collection or model refinement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289281</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Robust Decision-Making">
      <data key="d0">Robust Decision-Making</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The broader framework enabled by the equation, providing not just predictions but also a measure of their reliability in critical applications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4cb77e8b268b787c61c98919fbdbaff</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289282</data>
    </node>
    <node id="First Update Rule">
      <data key="d0">First Update Rule</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The First Update Rule is a Monte Carlo (MC) update rule for the action-value function, adjusting the estimated Q-value towards the actual return Gt observed until the end of the episode.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289283</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monte Carlo Update Rule">
      <data key="d0">Monte Carlo Update Rule</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A Monte Carlo Update Rule is a learning rule that adjusts the estimated action-value function based on the actual 'return' observed from an episode's completion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289283</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Error Term">
      <data key="d0">Error Term</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The difference $\Big[ G_t - Q_\pi(s_t, a_t) \Big]$ between the observed return and the current estimate, used to reduce this error in updates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289283</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Second Update Rule">
      <data key="d0">Second Update Rule</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Second Update Rule is a Temporal Difference (TD) learning update, specifically a form of Q-learning, which updates the current action-value estimate based on a bootstrapped estimate of the future return.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289284</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maximal Estimated Action-Value">
      <data key="d0">Maximal Estimated Action-Value</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The maximum estimated action-value for the next state $s_{t+1}$ over all possible actions $a'$, denoted as $\operatorname*{max}_{a'} Q_\pi(s_{t+1}, a')$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289284</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Off-Policy Control">
      <data key="d0">Off-Policy Control</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A control method that allows learning an optimal policy while following a different, exploratory policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289284</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bellman Optimality Equation">
      <data key="d0">Bellman Optimality Equation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Bellman Optimality Equation is a fundamental equation in optimal control and dynamic programming that provides a recursive definition for the optimal value function.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bellman optimality equation is a fundamental concept in dynamic programming and reinforcement learning, used to define the optimal value function or action-value function.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bellman Optimality Equation is a recursive equation that defines the optimal value function in dynamic programming and reinforcement learning, asserting that the optimal value of a state under an optimal policy is the sum of the immediate reward and the discounted optimal value of the next state.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bellman Optimality Equation is a fundamental concept in optimal control theory and dynamic programming, which provides a necessary and sufficient condition for optimality associated with dynamic optimization problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289285</data>
    </node>
    <node id="s_{t+1}">
      <data key="d0">s_{t+1}</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">s_{t+1} represents the state observed at time step t+1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289285</data>
    </node>
    <node id="a'">
      <data key="d0">a'</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">a' is a dummy variable representing any possible action that can be taken in state $s_{t+1}$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9d328bba2f8dc5a62b0adcf5df1aa5c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289285</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Size Optimization">
      <data key="d0">Size Optimization</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Size Optimization is a type of optimal design problem where the dimensions of an object are adjusted. It is characterized by adjusting parameters like horizontal length 'x1' and vertical thickness 'x2'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66f6813286ff2495396939f94deff0c3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289286</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Level Of Complexity">
      <data key="d0">Level Of Complexity</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Level Of Complexity is an indicator that increases from Size Optimization to Topology Optimization, signifying greater design freedom.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66f6813286ff2495396939f94deff0c3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289286</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-Shaped 2D Object">
      <data key="d0">C-Shaped 2D Object</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The C-Shaped 2D Object is a geometric representation used in the diagrams to illustrate the different types of optimization, specifically shown with a rectangular cross-section. It is subject to modifications in its size, shape, and topology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66f6813286ff2495396939f94deff0c3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289286</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagram (c)">
      <data key="d0">Diagram (c)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagram (c) illustrates Topology Optimization using the C-shaped object, depicting irregular, grey-shaded regions for material removal or addition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66f6813286ff2495396939f94deff0c3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289287</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Level of Complexity">
      <data key="d0">Level of Complexity</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Level of Complexity is an indicator that increases from size optimization to topology optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66f6813286ff2495396939f94deff0c3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289287</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-shaped 2D Object">
      <data key="d0">C-shaped 2D Object</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The C-shaped 2D object is a geometric diagram used as an example to illustrate different optimization categories.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66f6813286ff2495396939f94deff0c3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289287</data>
    </node>
    <node id="x1">
      <data key="d0">x1</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">x1 is a horizontal length parameter of the C-shaped 2D object, adjusted during size optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66f6813286ff2495396939f94deff0c3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289288</data>
    </node>
    <node id="x2">
      <data key="d0">x2</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">x2 is a vertical thickness parameter of the C-shaped 2D object, adjusted during size optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66f6813286ff2495396939f94deff0c3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289288</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Geometric Diagrams (a) (b) (c)">
      <data key="d0">Geometric Diagrams (a) (b) (c)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Geometric Diagrams (a), (b), and (c) are distinct visual representations illustrating the effects of sizing, shaping, and topology optimization respectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66f6813286ff2495396939f94deff0c3</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289288</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.29">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.29</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.29 contains two visualizations of a Synchronous Reluctance Motor (SynRM) design, labeled (a) and (b), depicting its optimized geometry and magnetic flux distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b7b73719952876f4f9c0e6b4efe0027</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289289</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Flux Density (|B|)">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Flux Density (|B|)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnetic Flux Density (|B|) is a measure represented by a color gradient and contour lines in the right image, illustrating magnetic field strength.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b7b73719952876f4f9c0e6b4efe0027</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289289</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Section 4.8.1 GA Results with SeqTO-v1">
      <data key="d0">Section 4.8.1 GA Results with SeqTO-v1</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Section 4.8.1 GA Results with SeqTO-v1 is the contextual section where the figures are discussed, indicating the results obtained from the Genetic Algorithm using SeqTO-v1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b7b73719952876f4f9c0e6b4efe0027</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289289</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic B Field Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic B Field Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnetic B Field Distribution illustrates the magnetic flux distribution within the SynRM structure, showing the magnitude of the magnetic flux density, |B|, using a color gradient and contour lines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b7b73719952876f4f9c0e6b4efe0027</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289290</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shaded Plot B Smoothed f:0ms">
      <data key="d0">Shaded Plot B Smoothed f:0ms</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Shaded Plot B Smoothed f:0ms is a legend with a color scale, found in the top-right corner of the right image, indicating the magnitude of the magnetic flux density, |B|.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b7b73719952876f4f9c0e6b4efe0027</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289290</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low Magnitude">
      <data key="d0">Low Magnitude</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Low Magnitude is represented by dark blue (4.75066e-06) on the color scale of the Shaded Plot |B| smoothed f:0ms legend.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b7b73719952876f4f9c0e6b4efe0027</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289290</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High Magnitude">
      <data key="d0">High Magnitude</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The High Magnitude is represented by yellow (2.6381) on the color scale for the Shaded Plot |B| smoothed f:0ms legend.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b7b73719952876f4f9c0e6b4efe0027</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289291</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Topology Obtained">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Topology Obtained</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Optimal Topology Obtained refers to the best structural arrangement for the SynRM rotor, achieved through the optimization process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b7b73719952876f4f9c0e6b4efe0027</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289291</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Flux Barrier Topology">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Flux Barrier Topology</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Optimal Flux Barrier Topology describes the best arrangement of air barriers within the SynRM rotor to guide magnetic flux.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b7b73719952876f4f9c0e6b4efe0027</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289291</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conventional Design">
      <data key="d0">Conventional Design</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Conventional Design refers to a standard SynRM design used as a comparison point for the optimized design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b7b73719952876f4f9c0e6b4efe0027</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289291</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Iron Paths">
      <data key="d0">Iron Paths</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Iron Paths are regions within the SynRM, predominantly blue-grey, where magnetic field lines are densely packed due to high flux density.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b7b73719952876f4f9c0e6b4efe0027</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289291</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$Q_{\pi}(s, a)$">
      <data key="d0">$Q_{\pi}(s, a)$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The true or target action-value function under policy $\pi$, representing the expected return under specific conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289292</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$\hat{Q}(s, a, w)$">
      <data key="d0">$\hat{Q}(s, a, w)$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The estimated action-value function, parameterized by weights $w$, often predicted by a neural network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289292</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$w$">
      <data key="d0">$w$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The vector of weights (parameters) of the neural network that estimates the action-value function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289292</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$\nabla$">
      <data key="d0">$\nabla$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The gradient operator, typically with respect to the weights $w$, indicating the direction of the greatest rate of increase of a scalar field.&lt;SEP&gt;Nabla (del) is a differential operator, representing the gradient in vector calculus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-19afc62597694db2255ea53cb7e6f242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293070</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$\alpha$">
      <data key="d0">$\alpha$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The learning rate, a positive constant that scales the magnitude of the update, crucial for step size in optimization.&lt;SEP&gt;Alpha is a variable in the equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-19afc62597694db2255ea53cb7e6f242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293067</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squared Error Loss">
      <data key="d0">Squared Error Loss</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Squared Error Loss is a loss function that quantifies the discrepancy between a target action-value function and an estimated action-value function, by squaring their difference.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289294</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$s$">
      <data key="d0">$s$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The current state of the agent in the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;A state from the environment's state space in reinforcement learning.&lt;SEP&gt;This variable represents the current state of the environment as observed by the agent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289294</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$a$">
      <data key="d0">$a$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A generic action from the set of all possible actions.&lt;SEP&gt;An action from the action space available in state $s$.&lt;SEP&gt;This variable denotes a generic action from the set of all possible actions that the agent can undertake.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289294</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Latex">
      <data key="d0">Latex</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Latex is a document preparation system used for typesetting the mathematical equation.&lt;SEP&gt;Latex is the format in which the mathematical equation is presented, often used for typesetting complex mathematical expressions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70c2a865bc92e157d3426a926d7ddd22</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289295</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Learning Signal">
      <data key="d0">Learning Signal</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A learning signal is a component in a learning process, represented by the equation, that drives updates in parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289295</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Update Rule Component">
      <data key="d0">Update Rule Component</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An update rule component is an element of an algorithm that defines how parameters are adjusted during learning, specifically related to the gradient of a squared error loss in reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289295</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Estimated Action-Value Function">
      <data key="d0">Estimated Action-Value Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Estimated Action-Value Function, denoted as $\\hat{Q}(s_i, a_j, w)$, predicts the expected cumulative future reward when starting in a given state, taking a specific action, and following a policy represented by parameters.&lt;SEP&gt;The Estimated Action-Value Function ($\hat{Q}(s, a, w)$) is an approximation by a non-linear function approximator, specifically a neural network with weights $w$. It provides an estimate of the expected total discounted future reward from state $s$ taking action $a$.&lt;SEP&gt;The estimated action-value function, $\hat{Q}(s, a, w)$, is parameterized by weights $w$ and approximates the true action value, often predicted by a neural network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70c2a865bc92e157d3426a926d7ddd22</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289296</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discrepancy">
      <data key="d0">Discrepancy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Discrepancy refers to the difference between the desired and predicted action values, which is quantified by the squared error.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289296</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Scalar Alpha">
      <data key="d0">Scalar Alpha</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Scalar Alpha, denoted by $\alpha$, is a learning rate, a positive constant that scales the magnitude of the update in gradient descent-based optimization algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289296</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Value Estimation Error">
      <data key="d0">Value Estimation Error</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Value Estimation Error is the error that the learning process aims to minimize by updating weights, making the estimated action-value function a better approximation of the target.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289296</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Subtraction">
      <data key="d0">Subtraction</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A mathematical operation used to calculate the difference (error) between the target Q-value and the predicted Q-value.&lt;SEP&gt;Subtraction is a mathematical operation used to calculate the temporal difference error or prediction error.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289297</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squaring">
      <data key="d0">Squaring</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A mathematical operation applied to the error term in MSE, ensuring errors are positive and penalizing larger errors more significantly.&lt;SEP&gt;Squaring is a mathematical operation used to form a squared error, a common loss function that penalizes larger errors more significantly and ensures positive values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289297</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Residual">
      <data key="d0">Residual</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The residual, $Q_{\pi}(s, a) - \hat{Q}(s, a, w)$, is analogous to the error term in regression within the context of supervised learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289298</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Robots">
      <data key="d0">Robots</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Robots are a practical application context where Deep Q-Networks can be used to control their behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289298</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Learning Equation">
      <data key="d0">Q-Learning Equation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The equation defines the update rule for the optimal action-value function, $Q(s, a)$, used in Q-Learning, a fundamental off-policy reinforcement learning algorithm.&lt;SEP&gt;The Q-Learning Equation is a fundamental formula in reinforcement learning used to update the Q-value for a given state-action pair, representing the expected future reward.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9121d5ef96392cd935214b547cd6dd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292684</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Action-Value Function">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Action-Value Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The $Q(s, a)$ function represents the expected return if an agent is in state $s$, takes action $a$, and then follows an optimal policy thereafter.&lt;SEP&gt;The Optimal Action-Value Function ($Q^*(s, a)$) represents the true value of the action-value function, signifying the expected total discounted future reward starting from a state $s$, taking action $a$, and thereafter following an optimal policy. It is often unknown at the beginning of training in most RL problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289298</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reward (r)">
      <data key="d0">Reward (r)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The immediate reward received by the agent after taking action $a$ in state $s$ and transitioning to the next state $s'$.&lt;SEP&gt;The Reward (r) is the immediate value received after taking an action in a state within the Q-Learning equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9121d5ef96392cd935214b547cd6dd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292684</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discount Factor (gamma)">
      <data key="d0">Discount Factor (gamma)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A scalar value between 0 and 1 that controls the importance of future rewards relative to immediate rewards, stabilizing learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289299</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Next State (s')">
      <data key="d0">Next State (s')</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The state reached after taking action $a$ in state $s$.&lt;SEP&gt;The Next State (s') refers to the state reached after taking action 'a' from state 's' in the Q-Learning equation.&lt;SEP&gt;The variable $s'$ represents the next state, reached after taking action $a$ in state $s$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7724d4c011c9981c316498400dd34c58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9121d5ef96392cd935214b547cd6dd2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293106</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Possible Action (a')">
      <data key="d0">Possible Action (a')</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A possible action that can be taken from the next state $s'$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289299</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maximization Operator">
      <data key="d0">Maximization Operator</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A mathematical operation, $\operatorname*{max}_{a'}$, that selects the largest value among a set of Q-values for different actions $a'$ from state $s'$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289300</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multiplication Operation">
      <data key="d0">Multiplication Operation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A mathematical operation ($\gamma \cdot \operatorname*{max}$) that applies the discount factor to the maximum future value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289300</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Temporal Difference Learning (TD-learning)">
      <data key="d0">Temporal Difference Learning (TD-learning)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A learning method where the value of one state is updated towards an estimate of the value in the next state; Q-Learning is a specific form of TD update.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289300</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bellman (1957)">
      <data key="d0">Bellman (1957)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A researcher associated with the development of dynamic programming principles from which RL algorithms were developed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289300</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stochastic Exploration Policy">
      <data key="d0">Stochastic Exploration Policy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A stochastic exploration policy, such as epsilon-greedy, allows an agent to balance exploring new actions and exploiting known good actions during learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bellman Expectation Equations">
      <data key="d0">Bellman Expectation Equations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Bellman expectation equations average over actions according to a fixed policy, distinguishing them from Bellman optimality equations which seek the value of the best possible future actions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$\epsilon$-Greedy">
      <data key="d0">$\epsilon$-Greedy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Epsilon-greedy is a stochastic exploration policy used in Q-Learning to balance exploration and exploitation by sometimes taking random actions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Normalized Error (Validation)">
      <data key="d0">Normalized Error (Validation)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Normalized Error (Validation) is a measure on the y-axis of the graph, ranging from 0.025 to 0.055, indicating the error rate during validation.&lt;SEP&gt;Normalized Error (Validation) is represented on the y-axis of Figure 3.22, ranging from 0.0025 to 0.0225.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2bea7680cdfeea039ec6cf21875ea0c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-248a0b72513cd485685d4b3e3864e01e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289302</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Running Average">
      <data key="d0">Running Average</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Running Average is a learning methodology shown by a red line on the graph, tracking closely with the Transfer Learning curve initially.&lt;SEP&gt;Running Average is a concept that appears as a red line in Figure 3.22, remaining mostly flat and near the bottom of the graph.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2bea7680cdfeea039ec6cf21875ea0c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-248a0b72513cd485685d4b3e3864e01e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289303</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Efficiency Map (IPM to PM)">
      <data key="d0">Efficiency Map (IPM to PM)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Efficiency map (IPM to PM) refers to a specific type of efficiency map used in the comparison of training curves in Figure 3.22.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2bea7680cdfeea039ec6cf21875ea0c6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289303</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Normalized Error">
      <data key="d0">Normalized Error</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Normalized Error (Validation) is represented on the y-axis of Figure 3.22, ranging from 0.0025 to 0.0225.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2bea7680cdfeea039ec6cf21875ea0c6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289303</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PM">
      <data key="d0">PM</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">PM is a concept used in the title of Figure 3.22, indicating another domain or type of efficiency map to which IPM is compared or mapped.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2bea7680cdfeea039ec6cf21875ea0c6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289303</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weight Vector">
      <data key="d0">Weight Vector</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Weight Vector, $w$, is a vector of real-valued parameters that are learned during the reinforcement learning process and adjusted to improve the accuracy of action-value predictions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-70c2a865bc92e157d3426a926d7ddd22</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289303</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feature Vector">
      <data key="d0">Feature Vector</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Feature Vector, $x(s_i, a_j)$, or basis function vector, is associated with a specific state-action pair $(s_i, a_j)$. In this context, it is a one-hot encoding vector.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-70c2a865bc92e157d3426a926d7ddd22</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289304</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inner Product">
      <data key="d0">Inner Product</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The inner product, denoted as $&lt;w, x(s_i, a_j)&gt;$, is a fundamental operation in linear algebra, calculated as the sum of the products of corresponding components of two vectors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-70c2a865bc92e157d3426a926d7ddd22</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289305</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-value Function">
      <data key="d0">Q-value Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Q-value function, denoted as Q(s, a), represents the expected cumulative discounted reward of taking action 'a' in state 's', and thereafter following an optimal policy.&lt;SEP&gt;The Q-value function, denoted as $\\hat{Q}(s_i, a_j, w)$, represents the estimated action-value for a given state-action pair, used in reinforcement learning.&lt;SEP&gt;The Q-value Function ($Q _ { \\pi } ( s , a )$) is a concept related to the state-value function, representing the expected return of taking action $a$ in state $s$ and following policy $\pi$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70c2a865bc92e157d3426a926d7ddd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289305</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inner Product Operation">
      <data key="d0">Inner Product Operation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Inner product is a mathematical operation defined as the sum of the products of corresponding components of two vectors, used within the equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-70c2a865bc92e157d3426a926d7ddd22</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289305</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Permanent Magnet">
      <data key="d0">Permanent Magnet</data>
      <data key="d1">component</data>
      <data key="d2">The Permanent Magnet is a long, slender, dark grey rectangular bar diagonally oriented within the rotor, flanked by triangular white shapes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff320ec3e3a24e3c69d31630b6cba885</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289306</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Central Shaft">
      <data key="d0">Central Shaft</data>
      <data key="d1">component</data>
      <data key="d2">The Central Shaft is indicated by a solid black, roughly quarter-circle shape at the innermost section of the motor diagram.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff320ec3e3a24e3c69d31630b6cba885</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289306</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parametrized Geometry">
      <data key="d0">Parametrized Geometry</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The geometry of the IPM motor is depicted with explicit dimensions (X1, X2, X3, X4, X5) that are relevant for analysis or optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff320ec3e3a24e3c69d31630b6cba885</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289306</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4-Pole, 24-Slot IPM Motor">
      <data key="d0">4-Pole, 24-Slot IPM Motor</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The 4-Pole, 24-Slot IPM Motor is a specific configuration of an Internal Permanent Magnet motor, explicitly mentioned in relation to the diagram's context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff320ec3e3a24e3c69d31630b6cba885</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289306</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 2.5">
      <data key="d0">Figure 2.5</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 2.5 is the title associated with the image, indicating its position and labeling within a larger document.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff320ec3e3a24e3c69d31630b6cba885</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289307</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$$\pi (s) = a$$">
      <data key="d0">$$\pi (s) = a$$</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">This is a mathematical equation representing a mapping from a state 's' to a specific action 'a', defining a deterministic policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289307</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$\pi$">
      <data key="d0">$\pi$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">$\pi$ represents the policy function, which maps a state to an action in a deterministic manner in this equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289307</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Function Mapping">
      <data key="d0">Function Mapping</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Function mapping is a mathematical operation where a function takes an input (e.g., state) and produces an output (e.g., action).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289307</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exploration and Exploitation">
      <data key="d0">Exploration and Exploitation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Exploration and Exploitation describes the trade-off an agent faces in reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289308</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Control / Policy Improvement Objective">
      <data key="d0">Control / Policy Improvement Objective</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Control / Policy Improvement objective in reinforcement learning aims to find a policy that maximizes the value function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289308</data>
    </node>
    <node id="pi(s) = a">
      <data key="d0">pi(s) = a</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">This is the mathematical equation $\pi(s) = a$, which represents a mapping from a state $s$ to a specific action $a$ in reinforcement learning, defining a deterministic policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Function">
      <data key="d0">Q-Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Q-function (or action-value function) estimates the expected return for taking a given action in a given state and following a policy thereafter.&lt;SEP&gt;The Q-function, denoted as Q(s, a), estimates the expected return for taking action 'a' in state 's' and following policy 'π' afterward.&lt;SEP&gt;The Q-function, denoted as Q(s, a; w), is used to evaluate the expected return of taking an action 'a' in a state 's' with parameters 'w'.&lt;SEP&gt;The Q-function, denoted as $Q(s,a)$, represents the expected total reward for taking action $a$ in state $s$ and then following an optimal policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7724d4c011c9981c316498400dd34c58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9242b5eeb7a8558a1b50bf53c2ccacba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6444ddfe2f3b1042c888bb109eee4098</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293106</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Navigation Task">
      <data key="d0">Navigation Task</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A navigation task is a practical use case where a deterministic policy might dictate specific movements for an agent based on its location.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-146167a8b3388ece49f9a67ad320dd0c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289309</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X Parameters">
      <data key="d0">X Parameters</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Labels (X1, X2,..., X12) representing various geometric parameters and dimensions within the IPM motor diagram.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289310</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Airgap Thickness (X5)">
      <data key="d0">Airgap Thickness (X5)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A critical design parameter labeled X5, representing the small gap between the stator and rotor, influencing motor performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289311</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stack Height (X6)">
      <data key="d0">Stack Height (X6)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An axial dimension of the motor, labeled X6, which is not explicitly shown in the 2D diagram but is a relevant parameter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289311</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bridge Thickness (X12)">
      <data key="d0">Bridge Thickness (X12)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A design parameter labeled X12, indicating the thickness of a magnetic bridge within the rotor or magnet embedment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289311</data>
    </node>
    <node id="91.5mm Fixed Dimension">
      <data key="d0">91.5mm Fixed Dimension</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A constant geometric feature indicating the outer radius of the motor stator or the overall motor radius.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289311</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neural Network Training">
      <data key="d0">Neural Network Training</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A process for which a dataset, generated from variations of the motor's geometry using Latin Hypercube sampling, is specifically intended.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289311</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X5: Airgap Thickness">
      <data key="d0">X5: Airgap Thickness</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">X5, referred to as "airgap thickness," is a critical design parameter defining the gap between the rotor and stator, visible as an annotation on the diagram.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289312</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X6: Stack Height">
      <data key="d0">X6: Stack Height</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">X6, referred to as "stack height," is an axial dimension of the motor not explicitly shown in the 2D diagram.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289312</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X12: Bridge Thickness">
      <data key="d0">X12: Bridge Thickness</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">X12, referred to as "bridge thickness," is a visible annotation pointing to a thin section of material, likely a magnetic bridge, separating parts of the rotor or magnet embedment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289312</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2D Engineering Drawing Style">
      <data key="d0">2D Engineering Drawing Style</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The 2D Engineering Drawing Style is the visual representation method used for the technical diagram.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-beaf090538cf5ed83b97d616d5275826</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289313</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.1">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.1</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.1 is a diagram illustrating the relationship between Reinforcement Learning (RL), Deep Learning (DL), and their combination to form Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-294177d434050aa2bd996039510efe9d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289313</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.19">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.19</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.19 is an image that depicts a deterministic policy behavior in reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d164a774e2261a7031fb600ad6568887</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289314</data>
    </node>
    <node id="S_0">
      <data key="d0">S_0</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">S_0 is a label for one of the individual states within the 'States' rectangle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d164a774e2261a7031fb600ad6568887</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289315</data>
    </node>
    <node id="S_1">
      <data key="d0">S_1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">S_1 is a label for one of the individual states within the 'States' rectangle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d164a774e2261a7031fb600ad6568887</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289315</data>
    </node>
    <node id="S_2">
      <data key="d0">S_2</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">S_2 is a label for one of the individual states within the 'States' rectangle.</data>
