    <node id="Value Prediction (Critic)">
      <data key="d0">Value Prediction (Critic)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Value Prediction (Critic) is the process performed by the Critic component in the A2C model, which takes states and actions as input.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27909faa34224de8c011e982c98fcef6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289333</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-value (Q(s,a))">
      <data key="d0">Q-value (Q(s,a))</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The Q-value (Q(s,a)) is an estimated output from Value Prediction (Critic) and serves as an input to the Policy Gradient (Actor).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27909faa34224de8c011e982c98fcef6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289333</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Policy Gradient (Actor)">
      <data key="d0">Policy Gradient (Actor)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Policy Gradient (Actor) is the process performed by the Actor component in the A2C model, which receives the state and Q-value estimate to produce action probabilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27909faa34224de8c011e982c98fcef6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289333</data>
    </node>
    <node id="p(a_i, s)">
      <data key="d0">p(a_i, s)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">p(a_i, s) represents the probability of taking a specific action 'a_i' given a state 's', and is an output of the Policy Gradient (Actor).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27909faa34224de8c011e982c98fcef6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289334</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action Labels">
      <data key="d0">Action Labels</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Action Labels are specific descriptions of actions output by the Actor, including 'SOFT UP', 'SOFT DOWN', 'SOFT LEFT', 'SOFT RIGHT', 'UP', 'DOWN', 'LEFT', and 'RIGHT'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27909faa34224de8c011e982c98fcef6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289334</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advantage Actor Critic Model">
      <data key="d0">Advantage Actor Critic Model</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Advantage Actor-Critic (A2C) model is an algorithm described by a block diagram, illustrating the interaction between its Critic and Actor components for producing action probabilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27909faa34224de8c011e982c98fcef6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289334</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State `s`">
      <data key="d0">State `s`</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The input `s` represents the state in the A2C model, depicted as a vector or sequence of information given to both the Critic and Actor components.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27909faa34224de8c011e982c98fcef6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289334</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action `a`">
      <data key="d0">Action `a`</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The input `a` represents the action in the A2C model, serving as an input to the Critic component.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27909faa34224de8c011e982c98fcef6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289335</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Value Estimation">
      <data key="d0">Q-Value Estimation</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The Q-value estimation is an output from the Critic component, represented as Q(s,a), and serves as an input to the Actor component.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27909faa34224de8c011e982c98fcef6</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289335</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Agriculture Sector">
      <data key="d0">Agriculture Sector</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A sector contributing to US GHG emissions, represented by a dark gray slice of 10.0%.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ab7f78faa6fc4f50c84b5c05263b97d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289336</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Industry Sector">
      <data key="d0">Industry Sector</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A sector contributing to US GHG emissions, represented by a light purple slice of 22.0%.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ab7f78faa6fc4f50c84b5c05263b97d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289336</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electricity Sector">
      <data key="d0">Electricity Sector</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A sector contributing to US GHG emissions, represented by a vibrant blue slice of 27.0%.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ab7f78faa6fc4f50c84b5c05263b97d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289336</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Residential Sector">
      <data key="d0">Residential Sector</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A sector contributing to US GHG emissions, represented by a bright green slice of 6.0%.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ab7f78faa6fc4f50c84b5c05263b97d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289337</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Commercial Sector">
      <data key="d0">Commercial Sector</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A sector contributing to US GHG emissions, represented by an orange-yellow slice of 7.0%.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ab7f78faa6fc4f50c84b5c05263b97d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289337</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Greenhouse Gas Emissions">
      <data key="d0">Greenhouse Gas Emissions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Greenhouse Gas Emissions (GHG) are gases that trap heat in the atmosphere, and their distribution by sector is illustrated in the pie chart.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ab7f78faa6fc4f50c84b5c05263b97d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289337</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2018">
      <data key="d0">2018</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">2018 is the year for which the transportation sector's total U.S. GHG emissions are specified.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ab7f78faa6fc4f50c84b5c05263b97d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289338</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pi (Policy)">
      <data key="d0">Pi (Policy)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The policy, denoted by $\\pi(s)$, is a function that maps a state $s$ to a specific action $a$. When it maps each state to a single action, it is a deterministic policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58674fc78b8e0f9b48c3a573d49dd684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289339</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Function (Q(s,a))">
      <data key="d0">Q-Function (Q(s,a))</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The action-value function that estimates the expected total discounted future reward for taking action $a$ in state $s$ and then following an optimal policy thereafter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58674fc78b8e0f9b48c3a573d49dd684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289339</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Expected Future Reward">
      <data key="d0">Expected Future Reward</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The quantified expected cumulative reward obtained by taking an action in a state and subsequently following a specific policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58674fc78b8e0f9b48c3a573d49dd684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289339</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pi (s)">
      <data key="d0">Pi (s)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Pi (s) represents the policy, a function that maps a state 's' to a specific action 'a'.&lt;SEP&gt;Pi (s) represents a policy function in the given equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58674fc78b8e0f9b48c3a573d49dd684&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-742edca401668538e3a7a9b93ec14162</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293087</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nominal Values">
      <data key="d0">Nominal Values</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Nominal values are the specific measurements assigned to each geometric parameter in the reference motor design, serving as the baseline for variations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289340</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reference Motor Design">
      <data key="d0">Reference Motor Design</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The reference motor design is defined by the parameters and values listed in the table, serving as a baseline for variations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289341</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parameter Column">
      <data key="d0">Parameter Column</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Parameter column in the table lists the design variables of the motor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289341</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Value Column">
      <data key="d0">Value Column</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Value column in the table lists the specific measurements for each parameter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289341</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Millimeters (mm)">
      <data key="d0">Millimeters (mm)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Millimeters (mm) is the unit of measurement used for all physical dimension values in the table.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289341</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Data Generation">
      <data key="d0">Data Generation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Data generation is a process described in Section 3.7.1, where parameters are varied by ±50% of their nominal values to create datasets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289342</data>
    </node>
    <node id="±50% Variation">
      <data key="d0">±50% Variation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">±50% variation describes the range by which each parameter of the reference designs was varied from its nominal value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289342</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transfer Learning Problem">
      <data key="d0">Transfer Learning Problem</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The transfer learning problem is a research area that utilizes datasets of motor designs and performance maps.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289343</data>
    </node>
    <node id="13 mm">
      <data key="d0">13 mm</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">13 mm is the nominal value for parameter X1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289343</data>
    </node>
    <node id="115 mm">
      <data key="d0">115 mm</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">115 mm is the nominal value for parameter X6.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289343</data>
    </node>
    <node id="25 mm">
      <data key="d0">25 mm</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">25 mm is the nominal value for parameter X2.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289343</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4.3 mm">
      <data key="d0">4.3 mm</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">4.3 mm is the nominal value for parameter X3.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289344</data>
    </node>
    <node id="7 mm">
      <data key="d0">7 mm</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">7 mm is the nominal value for parameter X4.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289344</data>
    </node>
    <node id="12.5 mm">
      <data key="d0">12.5 mm</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">12.5 mm is the nominal value for parameter X8.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289344</data>
    </node>
    <node id="60 mm">
      <data key="d0">60 mm</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">60 mm is the nominal value for parameter X9.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289345</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1.0 mm">
      <data key="d0">1.0 mm</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">1.0 mm is the nominal value for parameter X5.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289345</data>
    </node>
    <node id="0.25 mm">
      <data key="d0">0.25 mm</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">0.25 mm is the nominal value for parameter X10.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289345</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Millimeters">
      <data key="d0">Millimeters</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Millimeters (mm) is the unit of measurement for all physical dimensions listed in the table.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289345</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Datasets of Motor Designs and Performance Maps">
      <data key="d0">Datasets of Motor Designs and Performance Maps</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">These datasets are essential for the transfer learning problem and are created by varying nominal parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c99e142741706e208af18fcf2be391e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289345</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 5.17">
      <data key="d0">Figure 5.17</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.17 is a black and white diagram illustrating the architecture of a generic Actor-Critic (AC) model in the context of reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b48425c0c345b3be14ed824c6579b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289346</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Actor-Critic (AC) Model">
      <data key="d0">Actor-Critic (AC) Model</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Actor-Critic (AC) Model is an architecture within reinforcement learning depicted in Figure 5.17, comprising an Agent and an Environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b48425c0c345b3be14ed824c6579b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289346</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Input Node">
      <data key="d0">Input Node</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Input Node is a component within the Agent that receives state 's' from the Environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b48425c0c345b3be14ed824c6579b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289346</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Learning Element">
      <data key="d0">Learning Element</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Learning Element is a node within the Agent that receives feedback from the Critic and guides the Actor in refining its policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b48425c0c345b3be14ed824c6579b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289347</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Output Node">
      <data key="d0">Output Node</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Output Node is a component within the Agent that sends action 'a' from the Actor back to the Environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b48425c0c345b3be14ed824c6579b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289347</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGENT">
      <data key="d0">AGENT</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The AGENT is a primary component in the Actor-Critic model, encompassing internal nodes like Critic, Input, Learning element, and Actor, which process information and generate actions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b48425c0c345b3be14ed824c6579b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289348</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENVIRONMENT">
      <data key="d0">ENVIRONMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The ENVIRONMENT is a primary component in the Actor-Critic model, interacting with the AGENT by sending states as input and receiving actions as output.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b48425c0c345b3be14ed824c6579b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289348</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Output">
      <data key="d0">Output</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Output node within the AGENT sends the determined action 'a' from the Actor back to the ENVIRONMENT.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b48425c0c345b3be14ed824c6579b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289348</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action 'a'">
      <data key="d0">Action 'a'</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Action 'a' is the output generated by the AGENT and sent back to the ENVIRONMENT.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9b48425c0c345b3be14ed824c6579b7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289348</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1961-1990 Average Temperature">
      <data key="d0">1961-1990 Average Temperature</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The 1961-1990 average temperature is a baseline used for calculating the global land-sea temperature increase.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1360ac2945668789a7cee8bd946888f8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289350</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Graph (a)">
      <data key="d0">Graph (a)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Graph (a) is the left line graph in Figure 3.1, specifically illustrating "Annual CO2 emission" globally from 1751 to 2017.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1360ac2945668789a7cee8bd946888f8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289350</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Graph (b)">
      <data key="d0">Graph (b)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Graph (b) is the right line graph in Figure 3.1, specifically illustrating "Global land-sea temperature increase relative to 1961-1990 average temperature" from 1850 to 2019.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1360ac2945668789a7cee8bd946888f8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289351</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Climate Change">
      <data key="d0">Climate Change</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Climate Change is a broad environmental phenomenon encompassing global warming and related climate shifts, a central theme in discussions around CO2 emissions and temperature rise.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1360ac2945668789a7cee8bd946888f8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289352</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 4.20">
      <data key="d0">Figure 4.20</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 4.20 is a tree diagram illustrating a sequence-based controller, found in the image path F:\From_D_drive\Code\research_extender\output\Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis\auto\images\adeca819d36436cac467bd4bba1ff6b9e25086de31254e854efb2e5925dce29a.jpg, with the caption "The tree diagram for the sequence based controller."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289352</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sequence-Based Controller">
      <data key="d0">Sequence-Based Controller</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A sequence-based controller is a system whose operation is illustrated by branching actions and states, as depicted in the tree diagram (Figure 4.20), within the context of reinforcement learning or agent-environment interaction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289352</data>
    </node>
    <node id="START Node">
      <data key="d0">START Node</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The START node is the initial point of the tree diagram, representing the beginning state of the sequence-based controller.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289352</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action Paths">
      <data key="d0">Action Paths</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Action Paths are the branches extending from a node in the tree diagram, representing possible actions an agent can take.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289352</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Down Branch">
      <data key="d0">Down Branch</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Down Branch is one of the four main action paths from the START node, leading to a subsequent state represented by an 8x8 grid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289353</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Up Branch">
      <data key="d0">Up Branch</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Up Branch is an action path from the START node that leads to an "Not Feasible" outcome.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289353</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Left Branch">
      <data key="d0">Left Branch</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Left Branch is an action path from the START node that leads to an "Not Feasible" outcome.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289353</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Right Branch">
      <data key="d0">Right Branch</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Right Branch is one of the four main action paths from the START node, leading to a subsequent state represented by an 8x8 grid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289354</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Not Feasible Label">
      <data key="d0">Not Feasible Label</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The "Not Feasible" label in the diagram indicates an invalid or undesirable action path.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289354</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grid Representation">
      <data key="d0">Grid Representation</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Grid Representation is a visual element within the diagram, consisting of 8x8 squares, depicting the agent's state or position.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289354</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Darker Gray Squares">
      <data key="d0">Darker Gray Squares</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Darker Gray Squares within the grid representation indicate the agent's current location or configuration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289354</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Red Outlines">
      <data key="d0">Red Outlines</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Red Outlines in the grid representation highlight potential next moves or target cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289354</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Branching Factor">
      <data key="d0">Branching Factor</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Branching factor refers to the number of possible actions or choices available at each node in a tree diagram, which is four in the context of the sequence-based controller shown in Figure 4.20.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289355</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epsilon-Greedy Approach">
      <data key="d0">Epsilon-Greedy Approach</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The epsilon-greedy approach is an efficient learning strategy used in Q-learning to balance exploration and exploitation by sometimes choosing random actions (exploration) and sometimes choosing the best-known action (exploitation).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289355</data>
    </node>
    <node id="L-Shaped Darker Gray Area">
      <data key="d0">L-Shaped Darker Gray Area</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The L-Shaped Darker Gray Area is a specific configuration of darker gray squares in a grid, representing a particular state of the agent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289355</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tree Diagram">
      <data key="d0">Tree Diagram</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A tree diagram is a hierarchical structure used to illustrate concepts such as a sequence-based controller, showcasing branching paths and states.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289356</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Main Paths">
      <data key="d0">Main Paths</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The main paths are four possible actions branching from the START node: "Down," "Up," "Left," and "Right," in the context of the sequence-based controller.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289356</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State Representation (Grid)">
      <data key="d0">State Representation (Grid)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A state representation (grid) is an 8x8 grid composed of light gray squares used to visually represent the agent's state or position, with darker gray squares indicating current location or configuration and red outlines suggesting next moves.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289357</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Not Feasible Path">
      <data key="d0">Not Feasible Path</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Not Feasible Path is an action path that is invalid or leads to an undesirable outcome, indicated by a solid blue circular node with "Not Feasible" in red text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289357</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State-Action Space">
      <data key="d0">State-Action Space</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">State-action space refers to the set of all possible states an agent can be in and all possible actions it can take, whose complexity is demonstrated by the tree diagram of the sequence-based controller.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5c17ed6446ce59a36ae88843222657c2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289357</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SeqTO-v1 Method">
      <data key="d0">SeqTO-v1 Method</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The SeqTO-v1 method is a basis for the Genetic Algorithm used in the topology optimization of Synchronous Reluctance Motors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f5a390cc7143789eccd25f5f3623694</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289357</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EnvironmentType">
      <data key="d0">EnvironmentType</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">EnvironmentType is a column in the table that specifies the discretization of the design space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f5a390cc7143789eccd25f5f3623694</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289357</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Variables(m)">
      <data key="d0">Design Variables(m)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Design Variables(m) is a column header representing the number of design variables used in the Genetic Algorithm, linked to the dimensionality of the controller.&lt;SEP&gt;Design Variables(m) is a column in the table that represents the number of geometric and excitation parameters being optimized, with a value of '25'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f5a390cc7143789eccd25f5f3623694&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289357</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PopulationSize">
      <data key="d0">PopulationSize</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">PopulationSize is a column in the table that indicates the size of the population used in the Genetic Algorithm, with values '25||50'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f5a390cc7143789eccd25f5f3623694</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289358</data>
    </node>
    <node id="StallGeneration">
      <data key="d0">StallGeneration</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">StallGeneration is a column in the table that indicates the number of generations without significant improvement after which the Genetic Algorithm stops, with a value of '25'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f5a390cc7143789eccd25f5f3623694</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289359</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AverageTorque">
      <data key="d0">AverageTorque</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">AverageTorque is a column in the table that presents the primary performance metric for the Synchronous Reluctance Motor, showing '3.40 N-m'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f5a390cc7143789eccd25f5f3623694</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289359</data>
    </node>
    <node id="25 Design Variables">
      <data key="d0">25 Design Variables</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">25 Design Variables is the numerical value for the Design Variables(m), representing the optimized parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f5a390cc7143789eccd25f5f3623694</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289359</data>
    </node>
    <node id="50 Population Size">
      <data key="d0">50 Population Size</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">50 Population Size is the relevant population size for the Genetic Algorithm, as clarified by the surrounding text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f5a390cc7143789eccd25f5f3623694</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289359</data>
    </node>
    <node id="25 StallGeneration">
      <data key="d0">25 StallGeneration</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">25 StallGeneration is the value indicating the number of generations without significant improvement for the Genetic Algorithm in this specific SynRM optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f5a390cc7143789eccd25f5f3623694</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289360</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3.40 N-m Average Torque">
      <data key="d0">3.40 N-m Average Torque</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">3.40 N-m Average Torque is the optimized average torque achieved by the SynRM, which is a significant improvement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f5a390cc7143789eccd25f5f3623694</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289360</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Torque of the Conventional Design">
      <data key="d0">Torque of the Conventional Design</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Torque of the Conventional Design refers to the torque value of a standard SynRM design, specified as 2.5 N-m.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f5a390cc7143789eccd25f5f3623694</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289361</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Variables">
      <data key="d0">Design Variables</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Design variables, denoted as 'm', represent the number of variables used in the Genetic Algorithm, linked to the dimensionality of the controller.&lt;SEP&gt;The 'Design Variables' represent the number of geometric and excitation parameters being optimized, with a value of '25'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f5a390cc7143789eccd25f5f3623694&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289361</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Population Size">
      <data key="d0">Population Size</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Population size indicates the number of individuals used in the Genetic Algorithm's search process, often expressed in multiples of 'm'.&lt;SEP&gt;The 'PopulationSize' refers to the number of individuals (solutions) in each generation of the Genetic Algorithm, with an implied relevant value of '50'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f5a390cc7143789eccd25f5f3623694&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289361</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stall Generation">
      <data key="d0">Stall Generation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The 'StallGeneration' indicates the number of generations without significant improvement after which the Genetic Algorithm stops, with a value of '25' for this specific SynRM optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f5a390cc7143789eccd25f5f3623694</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289361</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5 x 5 Discretized Space">
      <data key="d0">5 x 5 Discretized Space</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The '5 x 5 discretized space' refers to the grid resolution for the optimization problem.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f5a390cc7143789eccd25f5f3623694</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289362</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Torque (2.5 N-m) of Conventional Design">
      <data key="d0">Torque (2.5 N-m) of Conventional Design</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">This refers to the torque value of a conventional SynRM design, which is 2.5 N-m and is used as a baseline for comparison.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f5a390cc7143789eccd25f5f3623694</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289362</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Excitation Input Sequence">
      <data key="d0">Excitation Input Sequence</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">This is the primary input to the RNN with Attention Mechanism, consisting of various parameters like N*i, T*emi, I*si, γ*i, and N*basei.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0dd4b73a53d12770e110415d865cc0d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289364</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RNN Blocks">
      <data key="d0">RNN Blocks</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">These are interconnected units (E1, E2, E3, up to En) that process the input sequence in the RNN with Attention Mechanism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0dd4b73a53d12770e110415d865cc0d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289364</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Motor Geometry Information">
      <data key="d0">Motor Geometry Information</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Motor Geometry Information is an input used to initialize the context vector in the RNN architecture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0dd4b73a53d12770e110415d865cc0d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289364</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Encoder Hidden Vector">
      <data key="d0">Encoder Hidden Vector</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The Encoder Hidden Vector is an output from the RNN blocks, combined with the score and context vector.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0dd4b73a53d12770e110415d865cc0d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289364</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Score">
      <data key="d0">Score</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The Score is generated within the attention mechanism, used to weight the encoder output, and combined with the encoder hidden vector and context vector.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0dd4b73a53d12770e110415d865cc0d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289365</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Efficiency Sequence">
      <data key="d0">Efficiency Sequence</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">An Efficiency Sequence represents a sequence of predicted efficiency values, which is the final output of the neural network architecture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0dd4b73a53d12770e110415d865cc0d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289366</data>
    </node>
    <node id="J">
      <data key="d0">J</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">J is a scalar 'performance measure' or 'performance criteria' that quantifies an agent's performance, defined as the measure of all rewards seen by the agent, aiming to maximize accumulated rewards.&lt;SEP&gt;A variable often used to represent a cost function or objective function in mathematical contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;J is a function, typically representing a cost or objective function in an optimization problem.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-620c2ed72be858c05e569ae1452705a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2ac9217313164d00979e7afd6541254&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292942</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$\boldsymbol{\theta}$">
      <data key="d0">$\boldsymbol{\theta}$</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">$\boldsymbol{\theta}$ is a vector representing the parameters (weights) associated with the neural network that constitutes the policy $\pi$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} J$">
      <data key="d0">$\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} J$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">$\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} J$ is the gradient of the performance measure J with respect to the policy parameters $\boldsymbol{\theta}$, indicating the rate of change of J relative to each parameter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$\pi ( a _ { t } | s _ { t } ; \boldsymbol { \theta } )$">
      <data key="d0">$\pi ( a _ { t } | s _ { t } ; \boldsymbol { \theta } )$</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">$\pi ( a _ { t } | s _ { t } ; \boldsymbol { \theta } )$ represents the policy, which is a probability distribution over actions given a state $s_t$, parameterized by $\boldsymbol{\theta}$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$\nabla l o g ( \pi ( a _ { t } | s _ { t } ; \boldsymbol { \theta } ) ) $">
      <data key="d0">$\nabla l o g ( \pi ( a _ { t } | s _ { t } ; \boldsymbol { \theta } ) ) $</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">$\nabla l o g ( \pi ( a _ { t } | s _ { t } ; \boldsymbol { \theta } ) ) $ is the gradient of the logarithm of the policy's probability, also known as the 'score function', which links the policy's choice to its parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289368</data>
    </node>
    <node id="REINFORCE">
      <data key="d0">REINFORCE</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">REINFORCE is a Policy Gradient algorithm that utilizes the log-probability term in its derivations to adjust policy parameters based on returns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289368</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Theta">
      <data key="d0">Theta</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Theta ($\boldsymbol { \theta }$) is a vector representing the parameters (weights) associated with the neural network that constitutes the policy $\pi$.&lt;SEP&gt;Theta (θ) commonly represents a vector of parameters in mathematical and statistical models.&lt;SEP&gt;Theta ($\theta$) is a variable, often representing parameters in an optimization context.&lt;SEP&gt;Theta ($\theta$) is a variable, typically used in equations to represent parameters or states.&lt;SEP&gt;Theta (θ) represents the parameters of the policy in the reinforcement learning equation.&lt;SEP&gt;Theta (θ) is a parameter in the equation, updated from time t to t+1.&lt;SEP&gt;Theta ($\theta$) represents a parameter or set of parameters in the equation, often updated during training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f94c2e92a45229c63d7fd85dceb68356&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-620c2ed72be858c05e569ae1452705a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e5324f5a16fd6822c647598c8e749b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d0e0689a5d95559de772960193f788e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b9ed49570cc7fd3b04f001bb2371e8b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2ac9217313164d00979e7afd6541254</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293059</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nabla J">
      <data key="d0">Nabla J</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Nabla J ($\nabla _ { \boldsymbol { \theta } } J$) is the gradient of the performance measure J with respect to the policy parameters $\boldsymbol { \theta }$, indicating the rate of change of J.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289368</data>
    </node>
    <node id="G t">
      <data key="d0">G t</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">G t ($G _ { t }$) is the total accumulated reward from time step t until the end of an episode, serving as an indicator of an action sequence's desirability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289369</data>
    </node>
    <node id="a t">
      <data key="d0">a t</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">a t ($a _ { t }$) represents the action taken at a specific time step t.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289369</data>
    </node>
    <node id="s t">
      <data key="d0">s t</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">s t ($s _ { t }$) represents the state observed at a specific time step t.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289369</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nabla Log Policy">
      <data key="d0">Nabla Log Policy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Nabla Log Policy ($\nabla l o g ( \pi ( a _ { t } | s _ { t } ; \boldsymbol { \theta } ) ) $) is the gradient of the logarithm of the policy's probability, also known as the 'score function'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289370</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nabla Operator">
      <data key="d0">Nabla Operator</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Nabla operator ($\nabla$) represents the gradient, yielding a vector of partial derivatives when applied to a scalar function with respect to a vector.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289370</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probability Distribution Function">
      <data key="d0">Probability Distribution Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The probability distribution function ($\pi$), also referred to as the policy, is a mathematical function used in the equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289371</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Return (G t)">
      <data key="d0">Return (G t)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Return ($G_t$) refers to the total accumulated reward, which the performance measure J is similar to.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289371</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$J($\boldsymbol{\theta}$)$">
      <data key="d0">$J($\boldsymbol{\theta}$)$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">J($\boldsymbol{\theta}$) refers to the expected return and is being optimized in the given equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289371</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LaTeX Format">
      <data key="d0">LaTeX Format</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">LaTeX is the specified format for the mathematical equation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-890e76382b57a11ba9855a8134986055</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289371</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Learning Temporal-Difference Update Rule">
      <data key="d0">Q-Learning Temporal-Difference Update Rule</data>
      <data key="d1">equation</data>
      <data key="d2">The Q-Learning Temporal-Difference Update Rule defines the core update rule for Q-learning, a model-free reinforcement learning algorithm. It iteratively estimates the optimal action-value function Q(s,a) by adjusting current estimates based on immediate rewards and discounted maximum future Q-values, scaled by a learning rate. This incremental, experience-driven update allows an agent to learn an optimal policy without explicit knowledge of environment dynamics, balancing exploration with exploitation to maximize cumulative reward over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289371</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State (s_t)">
      <data key="d0">State (s_t)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The current situation or configuration of the agent at time step 't', denoted as s_t.&lt;SEP&gt;State (s_t) refers to the current situation or configuration of an agent within its environment at time t.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8823695e17798553515e243bdbfda2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292784</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reward (R_{t+1})">
      <data key="d0">Reward (R_{t+1})</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The immediate scalar feedback received by the agent at time step t+1 after taking action a_t in state s_t and transitioning to state s_{t+1}.&lt;SEP&gt;Reward (R_{t+1}) is the immediate reward received after taking action a_t in state s_t and transitioning to state s_{t+1}.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8823695e17798553515e243bdbfda2</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292784</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State (s_{t+1})">
      <data key="d0">State (s_{t+1})</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The next situation or configuration the agent transitions to after taking action a_t in state s_t, denoted as s_{t+1}.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289373</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dummy Variable (a')">
      <data key="d0">Dummy Variable (a')</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A placeholder representing any possible action in the next state, s_{t+1}, used in the maximization operation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289373</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TD Target">
      <data key="d0">TD Target</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The TD target, also known as the bootstrap target, is an estimate of the true expected return from a state and action, calculated as the immediate reward plus the discounted maximum estimated Q-value for the next state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289373</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Model-Free Reinforcement Learning">
      <data key="d0">Model-Free Reinforcement Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An approach in reinforcement learning where the agent learns an optimal policy without explicit knowledge of the environment's dynamics, such as state transition probabilities or reward functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289374</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Temporal-Difference Learning">
      <data key="d0">Temporal-Difference Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Temporal-Difference (TD) learning is a class of model-free reinforcement learning methods that learn by bootstrapping from the agent's current estimate of the value function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289374</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$\epsilon$-greedy Policy">
      <data key="d0">$\epsilon$-greedy Policy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A common action selection strategy in reinforcement learning that balances exploration and exploitation, taking random actions with probability $\epsilon$ and greedy actions with probability $1-\epsilon$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289374</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Learning Rate ($ \alpha $)">
      <data key="d0">Learning Rate ($ \alpha $)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The learning rate ($\alpha$) is a hyperparameter in Q-learning that controls the step size of the update, determining the extent to which new information overrides old information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289374</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reward ($ R_{t+1} $)">
      <data key="d0">Reward ($ R_{t+1} $)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The reward ($R_{t+1}$) is the immediate scalar value received by the agent after taking an action and transitioning to a new state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289375</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discount Factor ($ \gamma $)">
      <data key="d0">Discount Factor ($ \gamma $)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The discount factor ($ \gamma $) is a hyperparameter that determines the present value of future rewards, influencing whether the agent prioritizes immediate or long-term rewards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289375</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State ($ s_t $)">
      <data key="d0">State ($ s_t $)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The state ($s_t$) represents the current situation of the agent in the environment at time step $t$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289375</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action ($ a_t $)">
      <data key="d0">Action ($ a_t $)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The action ($a_t$) is a choice made by the agent at time step $t$ while in a specific state $s_t$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289375</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$ \epsilon $-Greedy Policy">
      <data key="d0">$ \epsilon $-Greedy Policy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An $\epsilon$-greedy policy is an action selection strategy used in reinforcement learning that balances exploration and exploitation, choosing random actions with probability $\epsilon$ and greedy actions with probability $1-\epsilon$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-258e29a004ed4a9ecd38f72b2c0d91d8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289376</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bellman Equation Updates">
      <data key="d0">Bellman Equation Updates</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Bellman equation updates are iterative calculations involved in the training phase of Q-Learning to learn the Q-value function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-808c974f7871ba6b7b5d18f72afc85a7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289376</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Argmax Operation">
      <data key="d0">Argmax Operation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The argmax operation is a mathematical selection function used to derive an optimal policy from learned Q-values by choosing the action that maximizes the Q-value for each state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-808c974f7871ba6b7b5d18f72afc85a7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289376</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Value-Based Approach">
      <data key="d0">Value-Based Approach</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Value-Based Approach is a characteristic of Q-Learning where the policy is indirectly derived from value estimates (Q-values).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-808c974f7871ba6b7b5d18f72afc85a7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289377</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Q-Value Function">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Q-Value Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Optimal Q-Value Function ($Q^*(s, a)$) is the ideal action-value function that represents the maximum expected reward for taking an action in a given state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-808c974f7871ba6b7b5d18f72afc85a7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289377</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Latency">
      <data key="d0">Latency</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Latency is the time delay experienced in a system or network. (Note: "latency" is used as a placeholder in the initial prompt for this example, but it's not present in the provided text. I will only identify entities from the text provided.)</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-808c974f7871ba6b7b5d18f72afc85a7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289377</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Overarching Learning Procedure">
      <data key="d0">Overarching Learning Procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Overarching Learning Procedure represents the iterative process by which a Reinforcement Learning agent acquires knowledge.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-808c974f7871ba6b7b5d18f72afc85a7</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289377</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epsilon-Greedy Exploration">
      <data key="d0">Epsilon-Greedy Exploration</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Epsilon-greedy exploration is an action selection policy in reinforcement learning that balances exploration and exploitation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289378</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$A_t$">
      <data key="d0">$A_t$</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The action selected by the agent at time step $t$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289378</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$Q_t(s,a)$">
      <data key="d0">$Q_t(s,a)$</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The current estimate of the action-value function (Q-function) at time $t$ for a given state $s$ and action $a$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289378</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exploration Parameter ($\epsilon$)">
      <data key="d0">Exploration Parameter ($\epsilon$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The exploration parameter, $\\epsilon$, is a probability value between 0 and 1 that quantifies the likelihood of an agent choosing to explore (select a random action) versus exploiting (selecting the greedy action).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289379</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Set of Possible Actions ($a_1, \ldots, a_k$)">
      <data key="d0">Set of Possible Actions ($a_1, \ldots, a_k$)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Represents the set of all $k$ possible actions available to the agent in the current state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289380</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probability of Greedy Action ($1-\epsilon$)">
      <data key="d0">Probability of Greedy Action ($1-\epsilon$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The probability of selecting the greedy action.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289380</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probability of Random Action ($\epsilon$)">
      <data key="d0">Probability of Random Action ($\epsilon$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The probability of selecting a random action from the available set.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289380</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Piecewise Function Definition">
      <data key="d0">Piecewise Function Definition</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A mathematical definition indicating distinct action selection mechanisms based on probability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289380</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probability Selection">
      <data key="d0">Probability Selection</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Probability selection is a mathematical operation where the choice between two branches of a piecewise definition is determined probabilistically by $\\epsilon$ and $1 - \\epsilon$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289380</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action-Value Function (Q-function)">
      <data key="d0">Action-Value Function (Q-function)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Action-Value Function, or Q-function, represented as $Q_t(s, a)$, estimates the expected cumulative future reward for taking action $a$ in state $s$ at time $t$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289381</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Set of All Possible Actions ($a_1, \ldots, a_k$)">
      <data key="d0">Set of All Possible Actions ($a_1, \ldots, a_k$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The set of all possible actions, $(a_1, \ldots, a_k)$, represents the $k$ discrete actions available to the agent in the current state, from which a random action is chosen during exploration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289382</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probability of Selecting Greedy Action ($1 - \epsilon$)">
      <data key="d0">Probability of Selecting Greedy Action ($1 - \epsilon$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The probability $1 - \\epsilon$ indicates the likelihood of the agent selecting the greedy action, i.e., the action that maximizes the Q-function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289382</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probability of Selecting Random Action ($\epsilon$)">
      <data key="d0">Probability of Selecting Random Action ($\epsilon$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The probability $\\epsilon$ indicates the likelihood of the agent selecting a random action from the set of all possible actions, facilitating exploration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289382</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conditional (Piecewise) Definition">
      <data key="d0">Conditional (Piecewise) Definition</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The conditional (piecewise) definition is a mathematical operation used in the equation to define two distinct action selection mechanisms based on probability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289382</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Optimization Operator ($\arg\\max$)">
      <data key="d0">Optimization Operator ($\arg\\max$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The $\\arg\\max$ operator is a fundamental optimization operator that identifies the argument, in this case, an action $a$, that maximizes a given function $Q_t(s,a)$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289383</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QL-Based Controller">
      <data key="d0">QL-Based Controller</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A QL-based controller is a system that uses Q-learning to guide an agent's actions within an environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289383</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topology Optimization Results">
      <data key="d0">Topology Optimization Results</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Topology optimization results pertain to scenarios, such as those for electromagnetic actuators, where the $\\epsilon$-greedy strategy might be applied.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289383</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electromagnetic Actuators">
      <data key="d0">Electromagnetic Actuators</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Electromagnetic actuators are devices, such as the C-core example, that operate with coil current, turns, and force, and can be subject to reinforcement learning for optimal control.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289384</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-Core Example">
      <data key="d0">C-Core Example</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The C-core example refers to a specific type of electromagnetic actuator with coil current, turns, and force, used to illustrate application domains.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289384</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comparison Of Training Curves">
      <data key="d0">Comparison Of Training Curves</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The comparison of training curves illustrates the efficiency of different learning methodologies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-248a0b72513cd485685d4b3e3864e01e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289384</data>
    </node>
