    <node id="Efficiency Map to Power Factor Map">
      <data key="d0">Efficiency Map to Power Factor Map</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">This is the specific task or mapping goal for which the learning methodologies are being compared.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-248a0b72513cd485685d4b3e3864e01e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289386</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Computation Load For Training">
      <data key="d0">Computation Load For Training</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">This concept refers to the resources and time required for training a model, which Transfer Learning is stated to reduce.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-248a0b72513cd485685d4b3e3864e01e</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289387</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-Core Type Magnetic Material System">
      <data key="d0">C-Core Type Magnetic Material System</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The C-Core Type Magnetic Material System is a specific type of magnetic material system whose cross-section is depicted in the Discrete Design Domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5eebe68238bf8a33757041bc7dd3ffe9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289387</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$|B|_{airgap}$">
      <data key="d0">$|B|_{airgap}$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">refers to the magnetic flux density in the air gap, which is considered the state space in the discrete design domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5eebe68238bf8a33757041bc7dd3ffe9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289387</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Airgap Flux Density">
      <data key="d0">Airgap Flux Density</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Airgap Flux Density, denoted as $| B | _ { a i r g a p }$, is described as the state space within the discrete design domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5eebe68238bf8a33757041bc7dd3ffe9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289387</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$a^* = \arg \operatorname*{max}_a Q(s, a)$">
      <data key="d0">$a^* = \arg \operatorname*{max}_a Q(s, a)$</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines the optimal action in a deterministic policy context, specifically in Q-Learning, where $a^*$ is chosen by maximizing the Q-function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289387</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$a^*$">
      <data key="d0">$a^*$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An optimal action chosen from the set of all possible actions, aiming to achieve the maximum value of the Q-function for a given state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289388</data>
    </node>
    <node id="$Q(s, a)$">
      <data key="d0">$Q(s, a)$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Q-value function estimates the expected cumulative future reward for taking action $a$ in state $s$ and then following an optimal policy thereafter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289388</data>
    </node>
    <node id="argmax Operator">
      <data key="d0">argmax Operator</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The `argmax` operator returns the value of the variable for which the function $Q(s, a)$ attains its maximum value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289389</data>
    </node>
    <node id="A2C (Actor-Critic) Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">A2C (Actor-Critic) Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A2C (Actor-Critic) Algorithms are a type of reinforcement learning algorithm that typically use stochastic policies, contrasting with the deterministic policy often seen in Q-Learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289389</data>
    </node>
    <node id="a*">
      <data key="d0">a*</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Represents the optimal action to be taken by the agent, derived from maximizing the Q-function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289390</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Argmax">
      <data key="d0">Argmax</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A mathematical operator which returns the argument (variable) that yields the maximum value of a function.&lt;SEP&gt;Argmax is a mathematical operation that returns the argument for which the given expression has its maximum value.&lt;SEP&gt;Argmax is a mathematical operation that returns the argument for which the Q-value is maximized.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9242b5eeb7a8558a1b50bf53c2ccacba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-742edca401668538e3a7a9b93ec14162&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293087</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-values">
      <data key="d0">Q-values</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Measures the "quality" or desirability of taking a particular action in a given state, foundational to the Q-Learning algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289390</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Line of Symmetry">
      <data key="d0">Line of Symmetry</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A dashed-dot horizontal line running through the center of the C-core and armature, indicating a central axis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca13e3ef7d4bf929b84d6b30574e3485</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289391</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Half-Geometry">
      <data key="d0">Half-Geometry</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A representation of only one lower half of the C-core and armature, used for simulation due to symmetry.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca13e3ef7d4bf929b84d6b30574e3485</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289391</data>
    </node>
    <node id="L-Shaped Region">
      <data key="d0">L-Shaped Region</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A discretized representation of the core material in the half-geometry.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca13e3ef7d4bf929b84d6b30574e3485</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289391</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topology Optimization (TO) Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Topology Optimization (TO) Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Topology Optimization (TO) Algorithms are methods mentioned in the surrounding text to fill the design space in discretized representations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca13e3ef7d4bf929b84d6b30574e3485</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289391</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Controllers">
      <data key="d0">Controllers</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Elements such as '3x3' that are used to fill the design space, influencing the granularity of discretization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca13e3ef7d4bf929b84d6b30574e3485</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289392</data>
    </node>
    <node id="b6f5e2af2f03fc7e5efe994fc3d2753139ba687b436fe4c78ce0411348222bc1.jpg">
      <data key="d0">b6f5e2af2f03fc7e5efe994fc3d2753139ba687b436fe4c78ce0411348222bc1.jpg</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The file path of the image being analyzed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca13e3ef7d4bf929b84d6b30574e3485</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289392</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Controller Sizes">
      <data key="d0">Controller Sizes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Controller Sizes, such as 1x1 and 3x3, are parameters discussed in the context of topology optimization, relating to the granularity of discretization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca13e3ef7d4bf929b84d6b30574e3485</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289393</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Predictive Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Predictive Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The predictive distribution in a Bayesian neural network setting represents the probability of observing a new output given new input and training data, accounting for parameter uncertainty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289393</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bayesian Neural Network">
      <data key="d0">Bayesian Neural Network</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Bayesian Neural Network is a type of neural network that incorporates Bayesian inference to quantify uncertainty in its predictions, primarily by modeling a distribution over its weights instead of fixed values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289393</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Model Parameters">
      <data key="d0">Model Parameters</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Model parameters are the collection of weights ($W$) in a neural network, specifically the convolutional weights, representing internal learned features.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289393</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New Output ($y$)">
      <data key="d0">New Output ($y$)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A new output or prediction for a given input $x$, such as a pixel's class label in image segmentation or a value in a field distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289393</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New Input ($x$)">
      <data key="d0">New Input ($x$)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A data point for which a prediction is desired, e.g., an image or a specific spatial coordinate in a field.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289394</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Observed Training Input Data ($X$)">
      <data key="d0">Observed Training Input Data ($X$)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The observed training input data, typically a matrix or set of input samples used for training a neural network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289394</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Observed Training Target Data ($Y$)">
      <data key="d0">Observed Training Target Data ($Y$)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The observed training target data, corresponding to $X$, typically a vector or set of output labels/values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289394</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weights ($W$)">
      <data key="d0">Weights ($W$)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The collection of all model parameters, specifically the convolutional weights of the neural network, which is a high-dimensional vector or matrix representing internal learned features.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289395</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Likelihood $p(y|x, W)$">
      <data key="d0">Likelihood $p(y|x, W)$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The likelihood of obtaining prediction $y$ for input $x$ given a specific set of weights $W$, representing a standard output of a neural network for a given input and fixed weights.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289395</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Posterior Probability Distribution $p(W|X, Y)$">
      <data key="d0">Posterior Probability Distribution $p(W|X, Y)$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The posterior probability distribution over the weights $W$ given the entire observed training dataset ($X, Y$), capturing uncertainty about the 'true' weights after observing training data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289396</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Posterior Predictive Distribution $p(y|x, X, Y)$">
      <data key="d0">Posterior Predictive Distribution $p(y|x, X, Y)$</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The ultimate goal of Bayesian inference in this context, representing the probability of $y$ given $x$ and all training data, marginalizing out the uncertainty in $W$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289396</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Integration $\int \cdot dW$">
      <data key="d0">Integration $\int \cdot dW$</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A mathematical operation denoting integration over the entire parameter space of $W$, used for marginalization in Bayesian neural networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289396</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multiplication $\times$">
      <data key="d0">Multiplication $\times$</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard mathematical operation for multiplying probability density functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0a01577276faacfde89d458d90e662</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289396</data>
    </node>
