    <node id="Eps Greedy Policy">
      <data key="d0">Eps Greedy Policy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Eps Greedy Policy is a method that takes Q-values for state-action pairs and outputs the chosen action `a_t` to the Environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-11cc1c9381d2b807e25d94ba609ca1ea</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289407</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action a_t">
      <data key="d0">Action a_t</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Action `a_t` is the chosen action output by the Eps Greedy Policy and fed into the Environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-11cc1c9381d2b807e25d94ba609ca1ea</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289407</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Next State s_t+1">
      <data key="d0">Next State s_t+1</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Next State `s_t+1` is the state returned by the Environment after an action `a_t` is performed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-11cc1c9381d2b807e25d94ba609ca1ea</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289407</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reward r_t">
      <data key="d0">Reward r_t</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">`r_t` represents the reward received from the environment after an action `a_t` has been taken.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-11cc1c9381d2b807e25d94ba609ca1ea</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Current State s">
      <data key="d0">Current State s</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Current State `s` is the input representing the current condition fed into the Q-Network (Behaviour).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-11cc1c9381d2b807e25d94ba609ca1ea</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Knowledge Sharing">
      <data key="d0">Knowledge Sharing</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Knowledge sharing refers to the transfer of learned parameters or information from the Q-Learning component to both the Q-Network (Target) and Q-Network (Behaviour).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-11cc1c9381d2b807e25d94ba609ca1ea</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weight Update">
      <data key="d0">Weight Update</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Weight Update refers to the process of updating the weights of the Q-Network (Behaviour) based on information from the Q-Learning component.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-11cc1c9381d2b807e25d94ba609ca1ea</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EPS Greedy Policy">
      <data key="d0">EPS Greedy Policy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The 'eps greedy Policy' is a decision-making component that selects an action based on Q-values, balancing exploration and exploitation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-11cc1c9381d2b807e25d94ba609ca1ea</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289409</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Table 4.4">
      <data key="d0">Table 4.4</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Table 4.4 is titled "C-core results obtained from Genetic Algorithm using the SeqTO-v1 TO environment, along with a comparison of armature force wrt GA optimized results + filters (Midha et al. 2019)." It presents a detailed comparison of C-core topology optimization results.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289409</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SeqTO-v1 TO Environment">
      <data key="d0">SeqTO-v1 TO Environment</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The SeqTO-v1 TO environment is a topology optimization environment used in conjunction with the Genetic Algorithm to obtain C-core results.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289409</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pop. Size">
      <data key="d0">Pop. Size</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Pop. Size is a column header indicating the population size used in the Genetic Algorithm, often expressed in multiples of 'm'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289410</data>
    </node>
    <node id="StallGen.">
      <data key="d0">StallGen.</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">StallGen. refers to the number of 'stalled generations' in the Genetic Algorithm after which training stops.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289410</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ForceGASeqTO-v1">
      <data key="d0">ForceGASeqTO-v1</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">ForceGASeqTO-v1 is a column showing the optimal armature force achieved by the Genetic Algorithm using the SeqTO-v1 topology optimization environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289410</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ForceGA On/Off(Midha et al.)">
      <data key="d0">ForceGA On/Off(Midha et al.)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">ForceGA On/Off(Midha et al.) is a column providing comparative armature force reported by Midha et al. (2019) using an ON/OFF based Topology Optimization algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289411</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Controller Size">
      <data key="d0">Controller Size</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Controller size, such as '3 x 3' or '1 x 1', describes the configuration of the experimental environment and impacts the granularity of the optimal structure and design variables.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289411</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Linear Material">
      <data key="d0">Linear Material</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Linear material, specifically pure iron, is a material type used in C-core optimization that consistently yields higher armature forces.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289411</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Linear Material">
      <data key="d0">Non-Linear Material</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-Linear material, specifically electrical steel M-19 26 Ga, is a material type used in C-core optimization that yields lower armature forces compared to linear materials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289411</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ON/OFF Based Topology Optimization Algorithm">
      <data key="d0">ON/OFF Based Topology Optimization Algorithm</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The ON/OFF Based Topology Optimization Algorithm is a method used by Midha et al. (2019) and forms the basis for comparative armature force data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289411</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-core Actuators">
      <data key="d0">C-core Actuators</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">C-core actuators are devices for which topology optimization is performed, as discussed in the surrounding context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289412</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Section 4.6 C-core - Topology Optimization Results">
      <data key="d0">Section 4.6 C-core - Topology Optimization Results</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Section 4.6 C-core - Topology Optimization Results is a part of the surrounding text that discusses the results of C-core topology optimization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289412</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4.6.1 GA with SeqTO-v1">
      <data key="d0">4.6.1 GA with SeqTO-v1</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">4.6.1 GA with SeqTO-v1 is a subsection that details the application of Genetic Algorithm with the SeqTO-v1 environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289412</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Page 107">
      <data key="d0">Page 107</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Page 107 is a specific page in a document that explicitly references Table 4.4 and discusses the performance values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289413</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Material Type">
      <data key="d0">Material Type</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Material type, either 'Linear' (pure iron) or 'Non-Linear' (electrical steel M-19 26 Ga), significantly impacts the achievable armature force in C-core designs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289414</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stalled Generations">
      <data key="d0">Stalled Generations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stalled generations refer to the number of generations without improvement after which the Genetic Algorithm is considered to have converged and training stops.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289414</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ForceGA On/Off(Midha Et Al.)">
      <data key="d0">ForceGA On/Off(Midha Et Al.)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">ForceGA On/Off(Midha et al.) provides a comparative armature force reported by Midha et al. (2019) using an ON/OFF based Topology Optimization algorithm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289414</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Section 4.6 C-Core - Topology Optimization Results">
      <data key="d0">Section 4.6 C-Core - Topology Optimization Results</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">This section in a document discusses the results of topology optimization for C-core designs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289414</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4.6.1 GA With SeqTO-v1">
      <data key="d0">4.6.1 GA With SeqTO-v1</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">This subsection within a larger document focuses on the application of the Genetic Algorithm with the SeqTO-v1 environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289414</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1 X 1 Controller">
      <data key="d0">1 X 1 Controller</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The 1 x 1 controller is a type of controller size that allows for finer granularity and can achieve higher performance compared to a 3 x 3 controller.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289414</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3 X 3 Controller">
      <data key="d0">3 X 3 Controller</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The 3 x 3 controller is a type of controller size that uses fewer design variables compared to a 1 x 1 controller.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af2139266a69c7c4fede66d615ca0684</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289415</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Finite Element Analysis (FEA) Simulations">
      <data key="d0">Finite Element Analysis (FEA) Simulations</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">FEA Simulations are used in the Flux Linkage Calculation (S1) and Torque and Efficiency Calculation (S3) stages of the process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lambda D">
      <data key="d0">Lambda D</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Lambda D represents the direct axis flux linkage, an output from the Flux Linkage Calculation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lambda Q">
      <data key="d0">Lambda Q</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Lambda Q represents the quadrature axis flux linkage, an output from the Flux Linkage Calculation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7fda188f6e0be572dc98e8f2a6ca4e9</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mean Squared Error (MSE)">
      <data key="d0">Mean Squared Error (MSE)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mean Squared Error (MSE) is a specific error metric defined as the squared difference between an optimal, true action-value function and an estimated action-value function. It is a fundamental component for training a learning system, particularly neural networks, in reinforcement learning.&lt;SEP&gt;The Mean Squared Error is an objective function for regression tasks, calculating the squared difference between a target value and a prediction. In this context, it quantifies the error in Q-learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289418</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rt">
      <data key="d0">Rt</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The immediate reward received at time step t, a scalar value reflecting the desirability of a transition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289418</data>
    </node>
    <node id="max a' Q-hat(St+1, At+1, w)">
      <data key="d0">max a' Q-hat(St+1, At+1, w)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">This term represents the estimated maximum future action-value from the next state St+1, used to construct the target value for the current state-action pair.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289418</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-hat(St, At, w)">
      <data key="d0">Q-hat(St, At, w)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The estimated action-value (Q-value) for the current state St and action At, predicted by a neural network with weights w.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289418</data>
    </node>
    <node id="St">
      <data key="d0">St</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The state of the environment at time step t, representing the current observation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289419</data>
    </node>
    <node id="At">
      <data key="d0">At</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The action taken by the agent at time step t, leading from St to St+1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289419</data>
    </node>
    <node id="St+1">
      <data key="d0">St+1</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The state of the environment at time step t+1, resulting from taking action At in state St.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289419</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maximization">
      <data key="d0">Maximization</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A crucial operator in Q-learning, used to select the action a' in the next state St+1 that yields the highest estimated Q-value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289420</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Q-Networks (DQNs)">
      <data key="d0">Deep Q-Networks (DQNs)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A practical application of the error function for Reinforcement Learning, successfully applied to control tasks like Atari games.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289420</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Off-policy Learning">
      <data key="d0">Off-policy Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Off-policy learning refers to reinforcement learning methods where the agent learns about an optimal policy (target policy) while following a different policy (behavior policy) for data collection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289420</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sequential Decision-making Problems">
      <data key="d0">Sequential Decision-making Problems</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Problems where DQNs are used to learn an optimal policy by estimating action-values from environmental interactions, especially when the state space is too large for tabular methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289420</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adam">
      <data key="d0">Adam</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Adam is an optimization algorithm that can be used instead of the classical stochastic gradient descent procedure to update network weights in training deep learning models. It combines the advantages of two other extensions of stochastic gradient descent: Adaptive Gradient Algorithm (AdaGrad) and Root Mean Square Propagation (RMSProp).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a24ed737fb6aa8500ccd706bf246c499</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289420</data>
    </node>
    <node id="p(W | X, Y)">
      <data key="d0">p(W | X, Y)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">This is a mathematical equation representing the posterior probability distribution of convolutional network weights, W, given observed training data (input features X and target labels Y).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289421</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bayesian Statistics">
      <data key="d0">Bayesian Statistics</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Bayesian Statistics is a statistical framework in which the posterior distribution is a fundamental quantity that encapsulates knowledge about parameters after observing data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289421</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probability Density Function (PDF)">
      <data key="d0">Probability Density Function (PDF)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A PDF is a function whose value at any given sample (or point) in the sample space (the set of possible values taken by the random variable) can be interpreted as providing a relative likelihood that the value of the random variable would be equal to that sample.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289421</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probability Mass Function (PMF)">
      <data key="d0">Probability Mass Function (PMF)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A PMF is a function that gives the probability that a discrete random variable is exactly equal to some value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289422</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epistemic Uncertainty">
      <data key="d0">Epistemic Uncertainty</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Epistemic Uncertainty refers to the uncertainty in a model that can be reduced with more data or better models, often captured by the probabilistic representation of weights.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289422</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reliability Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Reliability Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Reliability Assessment is the process of quantifying confidence in Deep Learning model predictions, particularly important for safety-critical applications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289422</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Selective Prediction">
      <data key="d0">Selective Prediction</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Selective Prediction is a strategy where models abstain from making predictions in highly uncertain regions or flag them for human review.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289422</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Uncertainty Prediction in Deep Learning">
      <data key="d0">Uncertainty Prediction in Deep Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A field of study focusing on quantifying the confidence of predictions made by Deep Learning models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289423</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Image Segmentation Tasks">
      <data key="d0">Image Segmentation Tasks</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A computer vision task that involves partitioning an image into multiple segments or parts, often to identify objects or regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289424</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Posterior Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Posterior Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">In Bayesian statistics, the posterior distribution represents updated beliefs about a parameter after observing data, derived from Bayes' theorem.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289424</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Convolutional Network Weights">
      <data key="d0">Convolutional Network Weights</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Parameters within a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) that determine how input data is transformed through network layers. Represented by 'W'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289424</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Input Features">
      <data key="d0">Input Features</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The raw data or features used for training a Deep Learning model. Represented by 'X'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289424</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Target Labels">
      <data key="d0">Target Labels</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The ground truth or correct classifications corresponding to the input features. Represented by 'Y'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289425</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prior Distribution">
      <data key="d0">Prior Distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">In Bayesian statistics, the prior distribution represents initial beliefs about a parameter before observing any data. Represented by 'p(W)'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289425</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Likelihood">
      <data key="d0">Likelihood</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The probability of observing the data given a specific set of parameters. Represented by 'p(X, Y | W)'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289425</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Model Uncertainty">
      <data key="d0">Model Uncertainty</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The uncertainty in a model's parameters or structure, also known as epistemic uncertainty, reflecting uncertainty due to limited data or model limitations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-289a72546a02d10d2aa4147957dfa515</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289426</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C-Core Electromagnet">
      <data key="d0">C-Core Electromagnet</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The C-Core Electromagnet is the device being optimized, characterized by its C-shaped core structure, coils, and armature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-abb64932da4baf92322bc61ecf0df44f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289426</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grid System">
      <data key="d0">Grid System</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Grid System is the underlying structure of the diagram, composed of cells that represent different components or materials within the C-core.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-abb64932da4baf92322bc61ecf0df44f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289426</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Force Generation">
      <data key="d0">Force Generation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Force Generation is the objective of the optimization process, aiming to maximize the force experienced by the armature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-abb64932da4baf92322bc61ecf0df44f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289427</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B-H Characteristics">
      <data key="d0">B-H Characteristics</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to the magnetic properties of a material, specifically how magnetic field (B) relates to magnetizing force (H).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-abb64932da4baf92322bc61ecf0df44f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289427</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High Efficiency Classification">
      <data key="d0">High Efficiency Classification</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The process of classifying efficiency values into two categories: high efficiency (greater than 85%) and not high efficiency (less than or equal to 85%).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289427</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Classification Model">
      <data key="d0">Classification Model</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A model designed to categorize data into predefined classes, specifically evaluated for its ability to classify efficiency values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289428</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Target 0">
      <data key="d0">Target 0</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Represents an actual efficiency value (η*) less than or equal to 85% (η* ≤ 85%).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289428</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Target 1">
      <data key="d0">Target 1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Represents an actual efficiency value (η*) greater than 85% (η* &gt; 85%).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289428</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prediction 0">
      <data key="d0">Prediction 0</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Represents a predicted efficiency value less than or equal to 85%.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289429</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prediction 1">
      <data key="d0">Prediction 1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Represents a predicted efficiency value greater than 85%.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289429</data>
    </node>
    <node id="True Negative (TN)">
      <data key="d0">True Negative (TN)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Instances where the model correctly predicted that the efficiency was not high (Target: 0, Prediction: 0).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289429</data>
    </node>
    <node id="False Positive (FP)">
      <data key="d0">False Positive (FP)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Instances where the model incorrectly predicted high efficiency when the actual efficiency was not high (Target 0, Prediction 1), also known as a Type I error.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289429</data>
    </node>
    <node id="False Negative (FN)">
      <data key="d0">False Negative (FN)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Instances where the model incorrectly predicted that the efficiency was not high when the actual efficiency was high (Target 1, Prediction 0), also known as a Type II error.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289429</data>
    </node>
    <node id="True Positive (TP)">
      <data key="d0">True Positive (TP)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Instances where the model correctly predicted high efficiency (Target: 1, Prediction: 1).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289430</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2x2 Matrix">
      <data key="d0">2x2 Matrix</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A standard structural format for the Confusion Matrix.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289430</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Efficiency Value">
      <data key="d0">Efficiency Value</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A quantitative measure of how effectively resources are utilized, partitioned into two classes based on an 85% threshold.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289430</data>
    </node>
    <node id="85% Efficiency Threshold">
      <data key="d0">85% Efficiency Threshold</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The chosen boundary for partitioning efficiency values into two classes: less than or equal to 85% and greater than 85%.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289431</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Target Class 0">
      <data key="d0">Target Class 0</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Represents an actual efficiency value (η*) less than or equal to 85% (η* ≤ 85%).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289431</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Target Class 1">
      <data key="d0">Target Class 1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Represents an actual efficiency value greater than 85% (η* &gt; 85%).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289431</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Predicted Class 0">
      <data key="d0">Predicted Class 0</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Represents a predicted efficiency value less than or equal to 85%.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289431</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Predicted Class 1">
      <data key="d0">Predicted Class 1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Represents a predicted efficiency value greater than 85%.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289431</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Efficiency Value (η*)">
      <data key="d0">Efficiency Value (η*)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A metric used to partition data into two classes based on a threshold, chosen here as 85%.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289432</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Page 63">
      <data key="d0">Page 63</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to the surrounding context where the confusion matrix is discussed as a performance evaluator.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289432</data>
    </node>
    <node id="100 Randomly Selected Designs">
      <data key="d0">100 Randomly Selected Designs</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The dataset used to evaluate the deep learning model, over which the Classification Accuracy ranged between 90-96%.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-852a5b40a3022884df146775f2a7b320</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289433</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Reinforcement Learning (Deep RL)">
      <data key="d0">Deep Reinforcement Learning (Deep RL)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep Reinforcement Learning (Deep RL) is a branch of RL where the MSE equation is foundational for algorithms like Q-learning, particularly when neural networks are used.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289434</data>
    </node>
    <node id="(G_t) Return">
      <data key="d0">(G_t) Return</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">(G_t) Return is an estimate used in Reinforcement Learning to address the unknown nature of the Optimal Action-Value Function ($Q^*(s,a)$).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289434</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Q-Network (DQN) Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Deep Q-Network (DQN) Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep Q-Network (DQN) Algorithms are practical applications of the MSE equation's underlying principle for training neural networks in Reinforcement Learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289434</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gradient of the Loss Function">
      <data key="d0">Gradient of the Loss Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Gradient of the Loss Function is used to make updates to decrease the error in a neural network, involving adjustments to the neural network weights.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289435</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Error (MSE)">
      <data key="d0">Error (MSE)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Error (MSE) is a specific error metric, the Mean Squared Error, defining the squared difference between an optimal action-value function and an estimated action-value function parametrised by a neural network.&lt;SEP&gt;Error (MSE) stands for Mean Squared Error, a common metric used to quantify the difference between values predicted by a model or estimator and the true values.&lt;SEP&gt;Error (MSE) stands for Mean Squared Error and is a mathematical expression used to quantify the difference between an estimated value and the actual value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-feffff69e0f56312f2846d9f57d0a4f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85055739dbffbeba952630d321118334</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760292948</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Q-Network Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Deep Q-Network Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep Q-Network (DQN) Algorithms are practical applications and extensions that use the principle of minimizing the error equation for training neural networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289435</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Target Plot">
      <data key="d0">Target Plot</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Target Plot is a contour plot labeled (a) in Figure 3.14, representing an efficiency map for an IPM motor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee3a2fda6147e62f8093cb1da810ea6f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289436</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prediction Plot">
      <data key="d0">Prediction Plot</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Prediction Plot is a contour plot labeled (b) in Figure 3.14, representing an efficiency map for an IPM motor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee3a2fda6147e62f8093cb1da810ea6f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289436</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inset Diagram">
      <data key="d0">Inset Diagram</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">An inset diagram of an IPM motor is centrally located above the Target plot.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee3a2fda6147e62f8093cb1da810ea6f</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289436</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State-Value Function Approximation">
      <data key="d0">State-Value Function Approximation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">This equation defines state-value function approximation, where a parameterized function $\\hat { v } ( s , w )$ dependent on state $s$ and weights $w$ estimates the true state value $v _ { \\pi } ( s )$ under policy $\\pi$. It is crucial for Deep Reinforcement Learning, enabling generalization across high-dimensional and continuous state spaces, thus overcoming the 'curse of dimensionality' prevalent in traditional tabular methods for complex optimization tasks like electromagnetic design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289437</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Equation: $\\hat { v } ( s , w ) \\approx v _ { \\pi } ( s )$">
      <data key="d0">Equation: $\\hat { v } ( s , w ) \\approx v _ { \\pi } ( s )$</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The equation presented describes the fundamental concept of function approximation within the domain of Reinforcement Learning, specifically in the context of Deep Reinforcement Learning. It posits that an estimated value function, parameterized by weights, approximates the true value function for a given state under a particular policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289437</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Estimated Value Function ($\\hat { v } ( s , w )$)">
      <data key="d0">Estimated Value Function ($\\hat { v } ( s , w )$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The 'estimated value function' for state s, parameterized by w, represents a function approximator's output aiming to predict the action-value function $\\hat { Q } ( s , a , w )$ or the state-value function $\\hat { V } ( s , w )$. The given equation specifically refers to the state-value function approximation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289437</data>
    </node>
    <node id="True State-Value Function ($v _ { \\pi } ( s )$)">
      <data key="d0">True State-Value Function ($v _ { \\pi } ( s )$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The 'true state-value function' for state s under a policy $\\pi$ represents the expected total discounted future reward starting from state s and following policy $\\pi$. This is the ideal, often unknown, quantity that the function approximator strives to estimate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Approximation ($\\approx$)">
      <data key="d0">Approximation ($\\approx$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The approximation symbol ($\\approx$) highlights that the parameterized model will not usually perfectly reproduce the true value function but rather provide a close estimate, especially given the complexities of high-dimensional state spaces.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electromagnetic Device Design Optimization">
      <data key="d0">Electromagnetic Device Design Optimization</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Electromagnetic Device Design Optimization refers to the application domain and context where Deep Reinforcement Learning is used to optimize the design of electromagnetic devices, particularly concerning novel geometries and excitations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Action Value Function Predictor ($\\hat { Q } ( s , a , w )$)">
      <data key="d0">Action Value Function Predictor ($\\hat { Q } ( s , a , w )$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Action Value Function Predictor is an approximated function that estimates the value of taking a specific action 'a' in a given state 's', parameterized by 'w'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="State-value Function ($\\hat { V } ( s , w )$)">
      <data key="d0">State-value Function ($\\hat { V } ( s , w )$)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The State-value Function is an approximated function that estimates the value of a state 's', parameterized by 'w', often within the context of a Markov Decision Process (MDP).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289439</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Estimated Value Function">
      <data key="d0">Estimated Value Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Estimated Value Function, denoted as $\\hat { v } ( s , w )$, is a parameterized model used to approximate the true value function for a given state under a particular policy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289439</data>
    </node>
    <node id="True State-Value Function">
      <data key="d0">True State-Value Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The True State-Value Function ($v _ { \\pi } ( s )$) for state $s$ under a policy $\\pi$ represents the expected total discounted future reward, serving as the ideal and often unknown quantity that the approximator strives to estimate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289439</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Approximation Symbol">
      <data key="d0">Approximation Symbol</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The approximation symbol ($\approx$) indicates that the parameterized model provides a close estimate rather than a perfect reproduction of the true value function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289440</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Functional Notation">
      <data key="d0">Functional Notation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Functional Notation is used in the equation $\\hat { v } ( s , w )$, implying a function that takes $s$ and $w$ as inputs and outputs a scalar value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289440</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep RL Setup">
      <data key="d0">Deep RL Setup</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep RL Setup is a concept that the equation is central to, focusing on advanced function approximators for extending Reinforcement Learning to real-world engineering problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289441</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electromagnetic Device Design">
      <data key="d0">Electromagnetic Device Design</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Electromagnetic Device Design is an application domain where the equation is used for optimizing devices with novel geometries and excitations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6aa75c1dcbd4f8e8da642ba3e55f2a1c</data>
      <data key="d4">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1760289441</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Support Vector Machines">
      <data key="d0">Support Vector Machines</data>
