    <node id="H_0">
      <data key="d0">H_0</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">H_0 represents the null hypothesis in a statistical test, stating that the population mean of sample group (mu_s) is equal to the population mean of target group (mu_t).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b9df755969eb90a4aeda5af51acf960d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293018</data>
    </node>
    <node id="H_a">
      <data key="d0">H_a</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">H_a represents the alternative hypothesis in a statistical test, stating that the population mean of sample group (mu_s) is not equal to the population mean of target group (mu_t).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b9df755969eb90a4aeda5af51acf960d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293018</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mu_s">
      <data key="d0">Mu_s</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mu_s represents the population mean of the sample group.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b9df755969eb90a4aeda5af51acf960d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293018</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mu_t">
      <data key="d0">Mu_t</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mu_t represents the population mean of the target group.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b9df755969eb90a4aeda5af51acf960d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293018</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Statistical Hypothesis Testing">
      <data key="d0">Statistical Hypothesis Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Statistical Hypothesis Testing is a method used to test a claim or hypothesis about a population parameter based on sample data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b9df755969eb90a4aeda5af51acf960d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293018</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mu Sub S">
      <data key="d0">Mu Sub S</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Mu Sub S, or μs, represents the population mean of a first group or sample.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b9df755969eb90a4aeda5af51acf960d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293018</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mu Sub T">
      <data key="d0">Mu Sub T</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Mu Sub T, or μt, represents the population mean of a second group or sample.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b9df755969eb90a4aeda5af51acf960d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293021</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gamma">
      <data key="d0">Gamma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Gamma (represented as γ) is the discount factor in the Q-learning algorithm, which determines the importance of future rewards.&lt;SEP&gt;Gamma (γ) is the discount factor, a value between 0 and 1, used to determine the present value of future rewards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7724d4c011c9981c316498400dd34c58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec4e495c59f004fcbe4475de4b2bcbd1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293111</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Max Q(st+1, a')">
      <data key="d0">Max Q(st+1, a')</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Max Q(st+1, a') represents the maximum Q-value for the next state (st+1) across all possible actions (a'), indicating the highest possible future reward.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec4e495c59f004fcbe4475de4b2bcbd1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293034</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Learning Rate (lr)">
      <data key="d0">Learning Rate (lr)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The learning rate (lr) is denoted by alpha (α) and controls the step size when updating the Q-value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec4e495c59f004fcbe4475de4b2bcbd1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293034</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reward Theory">
      <data key="d0">Reward Theory</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Reward theory is the understanding of how immediate and future rewards influence learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec4e495c59f004fcbe4475de4b2bcbd1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293036</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Q-Learning Update Rule">
      <data key="d0">Q-Learning Update Rule</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Q-Learning update rule is a fundamental equation in reinforcement learning used to iteratively improve the Q-value estimates for state-action pairs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec4e495c59f004fcbe4475de4b2bcbd1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293036</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alpha (α)">
      <data key="d0">Alpha (α)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Alpha (α) is the learning rate, a hyperparameter that determines how much new information overrides old information in the Q-value update.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec4e495c59f004fcbe4475de4b2bcbd1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293036</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gamma (γ)">
      <data key="d0">Gamma (γ)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Gamma (γ) is the discount factor, a hyperparameter that discounts future rewards to prioritize immediate rewards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ec4e495c59f004fcbe4475de4b2bcbd1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1760293036</data>
    </node>
