    <edge source="$\hat{Q}(s, a, w)$" target="$w$">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The estimated action-value function $\hat{Q}(s, a, w)$ is parameterized by the weights $w$.</data>
      <data key="d8">estimation,parameterization</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760291853</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="$w$" target="$\nabla$">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The gradient operator $\nabla$ is typically applied with respect to the weights $w$ of the neural network.</data>
      <data key="d8">differentiation,operator application</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760291850</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="$w$" target="$\alpha$">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The learning rate $\alpha$ scales the magnitude of the update for the weights $w$ in optimization algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d8">optimization,scaling</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760291852</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squared Error Loss" target="Learning Signal">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The learning signal is specifically related to the gradient of a squared error loss.</data>
      <data key="d8">component,derivation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760291855</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Squared Error Loss" target="Discrepancy">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The squared error loss quantifies the discrepancy between desired and predicted action values.</data>
      <data key="d8">measurement,quantification</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c35bf46c88c50e423b73a91a41f7f95a</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760291857</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="$s$" target="$Q_t(s,a)$">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">$s$ is a variable used within $Q_t(s,a)$.</data>
      <data key="d8">component of</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760292298</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="$s$" target="$Q(s, a)$">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Q-value function $Q(s, a)$ takes the state $s$ as one of its arguments.</data>
      <data key="d8">is a function of</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760292337</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="$s$" target="$a^* = \arg \operatorname*{max}_a Q(s, a)$">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">$s$ represents the current state for which the optimal action is determined in the equation $a^* = \arg \operatorname*{max}_a Q(s, a)$.</data>
      <data key="d8">is context for</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760292340</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="$a$" target="$Q_t(s,a)$">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">$a$ is a variable used within $Q_t(s,a)$.</data>
      <data key="d8">component of</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-dbd39005d698bcd623fe368243dbe14d</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760292299</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="$a$" target="$a^* = \arg \operatorname*{max}_a Q(s, a)$">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">$a$ is a generic action variable within the equation $a^* = \arg \operatorname*{max}_a Q(s, a)$.</data>
      <data key="d8">is variable in</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760292339</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="$a$" target="$Q(s, a)$">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Q-value function $Q(s, a)$ takes the action $a$ as one of its arguments.</data>
      <data key="d8">is a function of</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-29e0300f86edc3c358dfc2a9374e88a8</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760292340</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Estimated Action-Value Function" target="Weight Vector">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Estimated Action-Value Function ($\hat{Q}$) is defined using the Weight Vector ($w$) as one of its parameters.</data>
      <data key="d8">dependency,prediction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-70c2a865bc92e157d3426a926d7ddd22</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760291918</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Estimated Action-Value Function" target="Inner Product">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Estimated Action-Value Function is derived from the Inner Product of the Weight Vector and the Feature Vector.</data>
      <data key="d8">calculation,definition</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-70c2a865bc92e157d3426a926d7ddd22</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760291921</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Estimated Action-Value Function" target="Mean Squared Error (MSE)">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Mean Squared Error (MSE) quantifies the difference between the Optimal Action-Value Function and the Estimated Action-Value Function.</data>
      <data key="d8">difference,quantification</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760292610</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Estimated Action-Value Function" target="Error (MSE)">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Error (MSE) is calculated based on the difference from the Estimated Action-Value Function.</data>
      <data key="d8">difference,measurement</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760292617</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Q-Learning Equation" target="Optimal Action-Value Function">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Q-Learning Equation defines the update rule for the Optimal Action-Value Function, $Q(s,a)$.</data>
      <data key="d8">definition,update rule</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760291887</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Q-Learning Equation" target="Reward (r)">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Immediate Reward (r) is a component of the Q-Learning Equation.&lt;SEP&gt;The Q-Learning Equation incorporates the immediate Reward (r) in its update rule.</data>
      <data key="d8">calculation,component</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c9121d5ef96392cd935214b547cd6dd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1760292688</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Q-Learning Equation" target="Discount Factor (gamma)">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Discount Factor ($\gamma$) is used in the Q-Learning Equation to weight future rewards.</data>
      <data key="d8">calculation,component</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760291889</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Q-Learning Equation" target="Next State (s')">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Next State (s') is a variable within the Q-Learning Equation.&lt;SEP&gt;The Q-Learning Equation considers the Next State (s') to estimate future rewards.</data>
      <data key="d8">component,context,prediction,variable</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c9121d5ef96392cd935214b547cd6dd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1760292689</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Q-Learning Equation" target="Possible Action (a')">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Possible Action (a') is a variable within the Q-Learning Equation.</data>
      <data key="d8">context,variable</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760291893</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Q-Learning Equation" target="Maximization Operator">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Q-Learning Equation uses the Maximization Operator ($\operatorname*{max}_{a'}$) to select the maximum future value.</data>
      <data key="d8">mathematical operation,uses</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760291894</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Q-Learning Equation" target="Multiplication Operation">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Q-Learning Equation uses the Multiplication Operation to apply the discount factor.</data>
      <data key="d8">mathematical operation,uses</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0e759e174fdc4ef458e871149c5bf316</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760291896</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Q-Learning Equation" target="Next Action (a')">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Q-Learning Equation evaluates the Next Action (a') to find the maximum future Q-value.</data>
      <data key="d8">component,optimization</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c9121d5ef96392cd935214b547cd6dd2</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1760292690</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Q-Learning Equation" target="LaTeX">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Q-Learning Equation is presented using LaTeX for clear and precise mathematical notation.</data>
      <data key="d8">formatting,representation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c9121d5ef96392cd935214b547cd6dd2</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1760292691</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Optimal Action-Value Function" target="Mean Squared Error (MSE)">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Mean Squared Error (MSE) quantifies the difference between the Optimal Action-Value Function and the Estimated Action-Value Function.</data>
      <data key="d8">difference,quantification</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760292609</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Optimal Action-Value Function" target="(G_t) Return">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The (G_t) Return is used to estimate the unknown Optimal Action-Value Function ($Q^*(s,a)$).</data>
      <data key="d8">estimation,substitution</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760292612</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Optimal Action-Value Function" target="Error (MSE)">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Error (MSE) is calculated based on the difference from the Optimal Action-Value Function.</data>
      <data key="d8">difference,measurement</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f74efd7c2930be98c8e7ee58bb7f2201</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760292615</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Next State (s')" target="Value Function (V(s'))">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Value Function is calculated for the next state $s'$.</data>
      <data key="d8">dependency</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-7724d4c011c9981c316498400dd34c58</data>
      <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d11">1760293113</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Normalized Error (Validation)" target="Running Average">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Running Average exhibits a certain Normalized Error (Validation) over time.&lt;SEP&gt;The Running Average fluctuates around 0.003 to 0.004 in Normalized Error (Validation).</data>
      <data key="d8">measurement,performance metric,range,y-axis label</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2bea7680cdfeea039ec6cf21875ea0c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-248a0b72513cd485685d4b3e3864e01e</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760291913</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Weight Vector" target="Inner Product">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Inner Product involves the Weight Vector ($w$) as one of its components.&lt;SEP&gt;The weight vector is a component used in the inner product calculation.</data>
      <data key="d8">component,operation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-70c2a865bc92e157d3426a926d7ddd22</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760291922</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Weight Vector" target="Q-value Function">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Q-value function is parameterized by the weight vector $w$.</data>
      <data key="d8">dependency,parameterization</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-70c2a865bc92e157d3426a926d7ddd22</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760291925</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Feature Vector" target="Inner Product">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Inner Product involves the Feature Vector ($x(s_i, a_j)$) as one of its components.&lt;SEP&gt;The feature vector is a component used in the inner product calculation.</data>
      <data key="d8">component,operation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-70c2a865bc92e157d3426a926d7ddd22</data>
      <data key="d10">Khan_Arbaaz_ECE_Thesis.pdf</data>
      <data key="d11">1760291923</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inner Product" target="Q-value Function">
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Q-value function is defined as the inner product of the weight vector and the feature vector.</data>
